    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Guyton1972_Angiotensin MODEL0911342562 (REK/ANX1 -> ANC) | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-094 (17.7: The Adrenal Glands): 'Renin then catalyzes the conversion of... angiotensinogen... to... angiotensin I. Angiotensin I is converted in the lungs to angiotensin II by angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE).'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-148 (25.5: Microscopic Anatomy of the Kidney): 'Active renin... cleaves several amino acids from angiotensinogen to produce angiotensin I. Angiotensin I is not biologically active until converted to angiotensin II by angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) from the lungs.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-152 (25.9: Endocrine Regulation of Kidney Function): 'Renin is an enzyme...It enzymatically converts angiotensinogen...into angiotensin I. ACE...enzymatically converts inactive angiotensin I into active angiotensin II.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-154 (25.11: The Urinary System and Homeostasis): 'Renin converts angiotensinogen into angiotensin I, and ACE produced in the lung converts angiotensin I into biologically active angiotensin II' (lines 45-46); OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-41.5 (41.5: Hormonal Control of Osmoregulatory Functions): 'Renin acts on angiotensinogen, which is made in the liver and converts it to angiotensin I. Angiotensin converting enzyme (ACE) converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "renin cleaves angiotensinogen -> AngI -> (ACE) AngII"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/angiotensin_II> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldosterone> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aangiotensin_II%3Apositive%3Aaldosterone> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/angiotensin_II> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldosterone> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Angiotensin II and potassium stimulate aldosterone secretion principally by increasing the transcription of CYP11B2 through common intracellular signaling pathways\"; \"the control of adrenal aldosterone secretion by angiotensin II was confirmed\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 15, p. 490"
    ; pmo:mechanism "angiotensin II binds AT1R on zona glomerulosa → phospholipase C → intracellular Ca2+ → calmodulin kinases → CYP11B2 transcription → aldosterone synthesis"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/angiotensin_II> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adh> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aangiotensin_II%3Apositive%3Aadh> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/angiotensin_II> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adh> ; pmo:sign "+"
