    ; pmo:mechanism "angiotensin II promotes release of vasopressin (ADH) from posterior pituitary, contributing to volume retention"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/angiotensin_II> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/total_peripheral_resistance> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aangiotensin_II%3Apositive%3Atotal_peripheral_resistance> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/angiotensin_II> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/total_peripheral_resistance> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "circulation.cellml non_renal_systemic_arterial_resistance_multiplier (ANU/ARM); Guyton circulatory dynamics | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-094 (17.7: The Adrenal Glands): 'Initiating vasoconstriction of the arterioles, decreasing blood flow.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-114 (20.5: Homeostatic Regulation of the Vascular System): 'Angiotensin II is a powerful vasoconstrictor, greatly increasing blood pressure.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-148 (25.5: Microscopic Anatomy of the Kidney): 'Angiotensin II is a systemic vasoconstrictor that helps to regulate blood pressure by increasing it.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-152 (25.9: Endocrine Regulation of Kidney Function): 'Angiotensin II is a potent vasoconstrictor that plays an immediate role in the regulation of blood pressure. It acts systemically to cause vasoconstriction.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-153 (Regulation of Fluid Volume and Composition): 'renin activity leads to an almost immediate rise in blood pressure as activated angiotensin II produces vasoconstriction'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-154 (25.11: The Urinary System and Homeostasis): 'The immediate and short-term effect of angiotensin II is to raise blood pressure by causing widespread vasoconstriction' (lines 46-47); OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-157 (26.4: Electrolyte Balance): 'Angiotensin II causes vasoconstriction and an increase in systemic blood pressure.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.3 (37.3: Regulation of Body Processes): 'Angiotensin II in addition to being a potent vasoconstrictor also causes an increase in ADH and increased thirst.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-41.5 (41.5: Hormonal Control of Osmoregulatory Functions): 'Angiotensin II raises blood pressure by constricting blood vessels.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "AngII-induced arteriolar vasoconstriction"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/angiotensin_II> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sodium_water_reabsorption> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aangiotensin_II%3Apositive%3Asodium_water_reabsorption> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/angiotensin_II> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sodium_water_reabsorption> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "kidney.cellml angiotensin_induced_Na_reabsorption_into_distal_tubules | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-094 (17.7: The Adrenal Glands): 'Stimulating kidney tubules to reabsorb NaCl and water, increasing blood volume.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-152 (25.9: Endocrine Regulation of Kidney Function): 'It [AngII] reduces both GFR and renal blood flow, thereby limiting fluid loss and preserving blood volume'; sodium retention via aldosterone stimulation.; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-41.5 (41.5: Hormonal Control of Osmoregulatory Functions): aldosterone 'stimulates the renal tubules to reabsorb more sodium' triggered by Ang II; Ang II also directly decreases GFR reducing filtered load."
    ; pmo:mechanism "AngII increases proximal/distal tubular Na reabsorption"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldosterone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sodium_water_reabsorption> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aaldosterone%3Apositive%3Asodium_water_reabsorption> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldosterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sodium_water_reabsorption> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Guyton1972_Electrolytes MODEL0912160001 (AMK -> Na handling); kidney.cellml Na reabsorption | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-090 (17.3: Hormones): table entry 'Aldosterone: Increases blood Na+ levels'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-094 (17.7: The Adrenal Glands): 'aldosterone increases the excretion of K+ and the retention of Na+, which in turn increases blood volume and blood pressure.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-114 (20.5: Homeostatic Regulation of the Vascular System): 'Aldosterone increases the reabsorption of sodium into the blood by the kidneys. Since water follows sodium, this increases the reabsorption of water.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-116 (20.7: Development of Blood Vessels and Fetal Circulation): Review question 17 confirms 'aldosterone prompts the kidneys to reabsorb sodium' as the correct answer in the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone mechanism.; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-147 (25.4: Gross Anatomy of the Kidney): 'The adrenal cortex directly influences renal function through the production of the hormone aldosterone to stimulate sodium reabsorption.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-148 (25.5: Microscopic Anatomy of the Kidney): 'Aldosterone stimulates Na+ reabsorption by the kidney, which also results in water retention and increased blood pressure.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-150 (25.7: Tubular Reabsorption): aldosterone stimulates principal cells to manufacture luminal Na+ channels and Na+/K+ ATPase pumps in DCT/collecting duct, increasing Na+ reabsorption; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-152 (25.9: Endocrine Regulation of Kidney Function): 'It [aldosterone] promotes Na+ reabsorption by the nephron, promoting the retention of water.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-153 (Regulation of Fluid Volume and Composition): 'Aldosterone stimulates the uptake of Na+ on the apical cell membrane of cells in the DCT and collecting ducts'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-154 (25.11: The Urinary System and Homeostasis): aldosterone 'results in renal reabsorption of Na+ and its associated osmotic recovery of water. The reabsorption of Na+ helps to raise and maintain blood pressure over a longer term' (lines 49-50); 'Aldosterone stimulates Na+ recovery by the collecting duct' (line 345); OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-156 (26.3: Water Balance): 'Aldosterone increases the reabsorption of sodium in the distal tubules of the nephrons in the kidneys, and water follows this reabsorbed sodium back into the blood.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-157 (26.4: Electrolyte Balance): 'aldosterone increases the...reabsorption of sodium in the distal tubule...Aldosterone causes an increase in blood sodium levels and blood volume.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.3 (37.3: Regulation of Body Processes): 'aldosterone maintains proper water balance by enhancing Na+ reabsorption'; 'The hormone aldosterone stimulates the reabsorption of water and sodium ions in the kidney.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-41.5 (41.5: Hormonal Control of Osmoregulatory Functions): 'Almost all of the sodium in the blood is reclaimed by the renal tubules under the influence of aldosterone. Because sodium is always reabsorbed by active transport and water follows sodium to maintain osmotic balance, aldosterone manages not only sodium levels but also the water levels in body fluids.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "aldosterone-driven distal Na reabsorption (ENaC)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sodium_water_reabsorption> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aadh%3Apositive%3Asodium_water_reabsorption> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sodium_water_reabsorption> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Guyton1972_AntidiureticHormone MODEL0911309080 -> renal water handling | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-090 (17.3: Hormones): 'ADH... Stimulates water reabsorption by kidneys'; also 'The hormone causes the kidneys to reabsorb more water and reduce the volume of urine produced'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-091 (17.4: The Pituitary Gland and Hypothalamus): 'Its effect is to increase epithelial permeability to water, allowing increased water reabsorption.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-114 (20.5: Homeostatic Regulation of the Vascular System): 'ADH signals its target cells in the kidneys to reabsorb more water, thus preventing the loss of additional fluid in the urine.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-148 (25.5: Microscopic Anatomy of the Kidney): 'When stimulated by ADH, these [collecting duct] cells will insert aquaporin channel proteins into their membranes... allowing water to pass from the duct lumen through the cells and into the interstitial spaces to be recovered by the vasa recta.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-150 (25.7: Tubular Reabsorption): ADH (vasopressin) stimulates insertion of aquaporin channels into apical membrane of principal cells in collecting ducts, increasing water reabsorption; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-152 (25.9: Endocrine Regulation of Kidney Function): 'ADH...promotes the recovery of water, decreases urine volume, and maintains plasma osmolarity and blood pressure...by stimulating the movement of aquaporin proteins into the apical cell membrane of principal cells of the collecting ducts.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-153 (Regulation of Fluid Volume and Composition): 'ADH also causes activation of aquaporin channels in the collecting ducts to affect the recovery of water to help restore vascular volume'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-154 (25.11: The Urinary System and Homeostasis): 'ADH stimulation of aquaporin channels allows for regulation of water recovery in the collecting ducts' (line 38); 'ADH promotes water recovery by the collecting ducts by stimulating the insertion of aquaporin water channels into cell membranes' (lines 358-359); OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-156 (26.3: Water Balance): 'ADH also causes the epithelial cells that line the renal collecting tubules to move water channel proteins, called aquaporins... large increase in water passage from the urine through the walls of the collecting tubules, leading to more reabsorption of water into the bloodstream.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.3 (37.3: Regulation of Body Processes): 'ADH changes the kidneys to become more permeable to water by temporarily inserting water channels, aquaporins, into the kidney tubules. Water moves out of the kidney tubules through the aquaporins, reducing urine volume.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-41.5 (41.5: Hormonal Control of Osmoregulatory Functions): 'It [ADH] acts by inserting aquaporins in the collecting ducts and promotes reabsorption of water.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ADH increases collecting-duct water reabsorption"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mean_arterial_pressure> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adh> .
