<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/blood_volume> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/anp> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ablood_volume%3Apositive%3Aanp> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/blood_volume> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/anp> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Guyton1972_AtrialNatriureticPeptide MODEL0911272039 (PLA/PRA -> ANPC) | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-098 (17.11: Organs with Secondary Endocrine Functions): 'When the body experiences an increase in blood volume or pressure, the cells of the heart's atrial wall stretch … produce and secrete … atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP)'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-114 (20.5: Homeostatic Regulation of the Vascular System): 'Secreted by cells in the atria of the heart, atrial natriuretic hormone (ANH) is secreted when blood volume is high enough to cause extreme stretching of the cardiac cells.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-153 (Regulation of Fluid Volume and Composition): 'Cardiomyocytes of the atria also respond to greater stretch (as blood pressure rises) by secreting ANH'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.5 (37.5: Endocrine Glands): 'These cells release the hormone atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) in response to increased blood volume. High blood volume causes the cells to be stretched, resulting in hormone release.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-41.5 (41.5: Hormonal Control of Osmoregulatory Functions): 'It [ANP] is released by cells in the atrium of the heart in response to high blood pressure' (atrial stretch from elevated blood volume/pressure triggers ANP release)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "atrial stretch (raised atrial pressure) releases ANP"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/anp> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sodium_excretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aanp%3Apositive%3Asodium_excretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/anp> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sodium_excretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Guyton1972_AtrialNatriureticPeptide MODEL0911272039; kidney natriuretic effect | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-098 (17.11: Organs with Secondary Endocrine Functions): 'ANP signals the kidneys to reduce sodium reabsorption, thereby decreasing the amount of water reabsorbed … reducing blood volume'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-152 (25.9: Endocrine Regulation of Kidney Function): 'Natriuretic hormones are peptides that stimulate the kidneys to excrete sodium...natriuretic peptides inhibit both Na+ and water recovery.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-153 (Regulation of Fluid Volume and Composition): 'More Na+ is lost, and as water follows, total blood volume and pressure decline'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-154 (25.11: The Urinary System and Homeostasis): 'ANH is released by the cardiomyocytes when blood pressure increases, causing Na+ and water loss' (lines 369); OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-41.5 (41.5: Hormonal Control of Osmoregulatory Functions): 'ANP prevents sodium reabsorption by the renal tubules, decreasing water reabsorption (thus acting as a diuretic) and lowering blood pressure.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ANP promotes natriuresis (raises GFR, inhibits Na reabsorption)"
    .
