<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/blood_volume> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/venous_return> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ablood_volume%3Apositive%3Avenous_return> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/blood_volume> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/venous_return> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "circulation.cellml venous_blood_volume/venous_average_pressure -> rate_of_blood_flow_from_veins_to_right_atrium (QVO) | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-110 (19.6: Development of the Heart) — chapter review 19.4: 'Venous return is determined by activity of the skeletal muscles, blood volume, and changes in peripheral circulation.' (lines 413–414).; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-112 (20.3: Blood Flow, Blood Pressure, and Resistance): 'As blood volume decreases, pressure and flow decrease. As blood volume increases, pressure and flow increase.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-156 (26.3: Water Balance): 'Decreased blood volume resulting from water loss... baroreceptors detect a decrease in blood pressure that results from decreased blood volume.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "raised blood volume raises mean systemic filling pressure -> venous return"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/venous_return> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cardiac_output> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Avenous_return%3Apositive%3Acardiac_output> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/venous_return> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cardiac_output> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "circulation.cellml Guyton curves: right_atrial_pressure -> right/left_ventricular_output (QLO/QAO) | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-112 (20.3: Blood Flow, Blood Pressure, and Resistance): 'venoconstriction increases the return of blood to the heart... increases the preload or stretch of the cardiac muscle and increases contraction.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-116 (20.7: Development of Blood Vessels and Fetal Circulation): Chapter review §20.2 states venoconstriction and the skeletal muscle pump promote venous return to the heart, implying more venous return drives cardiac output.; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-40.3 (40.3: Mammalian Heart and Blood Vessels): 'The atria are the chambers that receive blood, and the ventricles are the chambers that pump blood.' Deoxygenated venous blood enters the right atrium and is pumped by the right ventricle; oxygenated blood from the left atrium is pumped by the left ventricle into the aorta — the cardiac cycle coordinates filling and ejection so that venous inflow drives cardiac output."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Frank-Starling — venous return / atrial filling sets stroke volume"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sympathetic_tone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/total_peripheral_resistance> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asympathetic_tone%3Apositive%3Atotal_peripheral_resistance> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sympathetic_tone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/total_peripheral_resistance> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Guyton1972_Autonomics MODEL0911270005 (AU -> arterial resistance multiplier) | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-081 (15.3: Autonomic Reflexes and Homeostasis): postganglionic sympathetic fibers release norepinephrine onto adrenergic receptors in blood vessel walls → smooth-muscle constriction → increased blood pressure; sympathetic system determines systemic vascular tone.; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-082 (15.4: Central Control): vasomotor nerves (preganglionic sympathetic fibers) constrict blood vessels, raising systemic vascular resistance; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-083 (15.5: Drugs that Affect the Autonomic System): 'nicotine will preferentially cause the vascular tone to become more sympathetic, which means blood pressure will be increased' — confirms sympathetic drive increases vascular resistance / arterial pressure; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-111 (20.2: Structure and Function of Blood Vessels): vasoconstriction via smooth muscle contraction narrows arteriole lumen and increases blood pressure; arterioles are the primary site of resistance and regulation of blood pressure"
    ; pmo:mechanism "sympathetic arteriolar vasoconstriction"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sympathetic_tone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/heart_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asympathetic_tone%3Apositive%3Aheart_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sympathetic_tone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/heart_rate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Guyton & Hall — sympathetic cardiac nerves increase rate; BioModels MODEL2101280001 (Schölzel 2015) | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-059 (10.8: Cardiac Muscle Tissue): pacemaker cells respond to ANS signals to speed up heart rate; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-075 (13.4: Circulation and the Central Nervous System): orthostatic reflex — 'Heart rate increases—a reflex of the sympathetic division of the autonomic nervous system—and this raises blood pressure.' Directly confirms sympathetic activation raises heart rate.; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-080 (15.2: Divisions of the Autonomic Nervous System): 'One causes heart rate to increase' (sympathetic division); 'the sympathetic system would increase heart rate.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-081 (15.3: Autonomic Reflexes and Homeostasis): norepinephrine on SA-node adrenergic receptors causes faster depolarization, increasing heart rate; sympathetic speeds heart from ~100 bpm to 120–140 bpm.; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-082 (15.4: Central Control): preganglionic sympathetic cardiac accelerator nerves increase heart rate; sympathetic activation during exercise raises heart rate to 120-140 bpm; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-083 (15.5: Drugs that Affect the Autonomic System): 'the autonomic system does not cause the heart to beat, it just speeds it up (sympathetic) or slows it down (parasympathetic)'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-109 (19.5: Cardiac Physiology): 'The cardioaccelerator regions stimulate activity via sympathetic stimulation … increases in physical activity … increase sympathetic stimulation as needed in order to increase blood flow [and HR].'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-114 (20.5: Homeostatic Regulation of the Vascular System): 'When blood pressure drops too low...trigger an increase in sympathetic stimulation of the heart, causing cardiac output to increase.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-33.2 (33.2: Animal Primary Tissues): 'Cardiac muscle is not under voluntary control but can be influenced by the autonomic nervous system to speed up or slow down' — sympathetic stimulation increases heart rate; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-35.4 (35.4: The Peripheral Nervous System): 'Examples of functions controlled by the sympathetic nervous system include an accelerated heart rate'."
    ; pmo:mechanism "direct cardiac sympathetic nerve activity (in addition to humoral NE) raises SA-node firing; lumps the fast neural chronotropic limb of the reflex."
    .
