    ; dcterms:source "kidney.cellml perfusion_pressure -> glomerular_pressure -> glomerular_filtration_rate | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-149 (25.6: Physiology of Urine Formation): 'Glomerular filtration occurs when glomerular hydrostatic pressure exceeds the luminal hydrostatic pressure of Bowman's capsule'; GFR is set by net filtration pressure driven by glomerular capillary (perfusion-dependent) hydrostatic pressure; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-151 (25.8: Regulation of Renal Blood Flow): 'Only a 10 mm Hg pressure differential across the glomerulus is required for normal GFR, so very small changes in afferent arterial pressure significantly increase or decrease GFR.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-155 (26.2: Body Fluids and Fluid Compartment): 'As hydrostatic pressure in the kidneys increases, the amount of water leaving the capillaries also increases, and more urine filtrate is formed.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-41.2 (41.2: The Kidneys and Osmoregulatory Organs): 'The glomerulus forces small solutes out of the blood by pressure.' Glomerular filtration rate is driven by hydrostatic pressure in the glomerular capillaries, which depends on renal perfusion pressure.; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-5.2 (5.2: Passive Transport): 'blood pressure forces large amounts of water and accompanying dissolved substances, or solutes, out of the blood and into the renal tubules. The diffusion rate in this instance is almost totally dependent on pressure.' — supports blood pressure driving glomerular filtration (GFR)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "higher perfusion -> higher glomerular pressure -> higher filtration"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sodium_excretion> .
