    ; pmo:mechanism "the branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase complex irreversibly decarboxylates the branched-chain alpha-keto acids; the upstream branched-chain amino acids are the SUBSTRATE pool, so ∂[BCAA]/∂(BCKDH activity) < 0 — LoF (activity DOWN) => BCAA accumulate (MSUD)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urea_cycle_enzyme_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_urea_synthesis> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aurea_cycle_enzyme_activity%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_urea_synthesis> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urea_cycle_enzyme_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_urea_synthesis> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 18 (the urea cycle; OTC catalyzes carbamoyl-phosphate + ornithine -> citrulline); OTC deficiency OMIM 311250 | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-41.4 (Nitrogenous Wastes): urea cycle enzymes catalyze the conversion of ammonia to urea in the liver, so higher enzyme activity drives higher urea synthesis"
    ; pmo:mechanism "the urea cycle (CPS1, ornithine transcarbamylase OTC, ASS1, ASL, arginase) converts ammonia + aspartate nitrogen to urea; urea-cycle enzyme activity sets the urea synthesis flux (LoF => urea synthesis falls)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_urea_synthesis> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ammonia> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahepatic_urea_synthesis%3Anegative%3Aplasma_ammonia> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_urea_synthesis> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ammonia> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 18 (ureagenesis as ammonia detoxification); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (hepatic ammonia clearance); OTC deficiency OMIM 311250 | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-141 (24.5: Protein Metabolism): 'In the urea cycle, ammonium is combined with CO2, resulting in urea and water. The urea is eliminated through the kidneys in the urine' — urea synthesis removes ammonia from circulation"
    ; pmo:mechanism "urea synthesis is the principal route of ammonia disposal; higher hepatic ureagenesis lowers the circulating ammonia load, so reduced urea synthesis raises plasma ammonia (hyperammonemia of urea-cycle disorders)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ammonia> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glutamine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_ammonia%3Apositive%3Aplasma_glutamine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ammonia> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glutamine> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (glutamine as the principal circulating ammonia carrier); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 18 (glutamine synthetase ammonia fixation); urea-cycle disorders OMIM 311250"
    ; pmo:mechanism "excess ammonia is buffered by glutamine synthetase amidation of glutamate to glutamine (the major non-toxic ammonia carrier); rising ammonia raises plasma glutamine (elevated glutamine is an early marker of hyperammonemia)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ammonia> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_urea_synthesis> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_ammonia%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_urea_synthesis> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ammonia> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_urea_synthesis> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 18 (ammonia/carbamoyl phosphate is the first urea-cycle substrate; flux responds to nitrogen load); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-141 (24.5: Protein Metabolism): 'The urea cycle is a set of biochemical reactions that produces urea from ammonium ions in order to prevent a toxic level of ammonium in the body... ammonium ions are produced from the breakdown of amino acids... enters into the urea cycle to be eliminated'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ammonia (as carbamoyl phosphate via CPS1) is the entry substrate of the urea cycle; higher ammonia load drives higher ureagenesis when urea-cycle capacity is intact (substrate-driven flux). This closes a negative-feedback loop with hepatic_urea_synthesis -(-)-> plasma_ammonia."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mean_arterial_pressure> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/baroreceptor_firing_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amean_arterial_pressure%3Apositive%3Abaroreceptor_firing_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mean_arterial_pressure> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/baroreceptor_firing_rate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Guyton & Hall — baroreceptor reflex (stretch raises afferent firing); BioModels MODEL2101280001 (Schölzel 2015 / Seidel-Herzel 1998) | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-075 (13.4: Circulation and the Central Nervous System): 'The bases of the common carotids contain stretch receptors that immediately respond to the drop in blood pressure upon standing... Heart rate increases—a reflex of the sympathetic division of the autonomic nervous system—and this raises blood pressure.' Confirms carotid baroreceptors respond to arterial pressure changes, raising baroreceptor firing.; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-109 (19.5: Cardiac Physiology): 'baroreceptors are stretch receptors … Rates of firing from the baroreceptors represent blood pressure … With increased pressure and stretch, the rate of baroreceptor firing increases.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-114 (20.5: Homeostatic Regulation of the Vascular System): 'When blood pressure increases, the baroreceptors are stretched more tightly and initiate action potentials at a higher rate.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-153 (Regulation of Fluid Volume and Composition): 'blood pressure can be measured... measured by baroreceptors in the aorta and carotid sinuses. When blood pressure increases, baroreceptors send more frequent action potentials'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-36.2 (36.2: Somatosensation): 'Baroreceptors detect pressure changes in an organ. They are found in the walls of the carotid artery and the aorta where they monitor blood pressure'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "rising arterial pressure stretches the carotid-sinus / aortic-arch vessel wall; mechanosensitive baroreceptor disposition -> increased afferent action-potential firing carried via glossopharyngeal/vagal afferents to the NTS."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/baroreceptor_firing_rate> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sympathetic_tone> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abaroreceptor_firing_rate%3Anegative%3Asympathetic_tone> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/baroreceptor_firing_rate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sympathetic_tone> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Guyton & Hall — baroreceptor inhibition of vasomotor centre; Boron & Boulpaep — arterial baroreflex; BioModels MODEL2101280001 (Schölzel 2015) | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-075 (13.4: Circulation and the Central Nervous System): 'Heart rate increases—a reflex of the sympathetic division of the autonomic nervous system'—corroborates that baroreceptor activation (in response to pressure drop) leads to sympathetic activation of heart rate; when pressure drops, baroreceptors fire less, sympathetic tone rises.; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-114 (20.5: Homeostatic Regulation of the Vascular System): 'When blood pressure rises too high, the baroreceptors fire at a higher rate...Sympathetic stimulation of the peripheral arterioles will also decrease, resulting in vasodilation.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-153 (Regulation of Fluid Volume and Composition): 'If pressure decreases, fewer action potentials travel to the central nervous system, resulting in more sympathetic stimulation-producing vasoconstriction'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "baroreceptor afferents excite NTS -> caudal VLM inhibition of the rostral VLM vasomotor centre, withdrawing sympathetic vasoconstrictor/cardiac outflow."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/baroreceptor_firing_rate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/parasympathetic_tone> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abaroreceptor_firing_rate%3Apositive%3Aparasympathetic_tone> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/baroreceptor_firing_rate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/parasympathetic_tone> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Guyton & Hall — baroreceptor activation of vagal centre; Boron & Boulpaep — parasympathetic cardiac control; BioModels MODEL2101280001 (Schölzel 2015) | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-114 (20.5: Homeostatic Regulation of the Vascular System): 'When blood pressure rises too high, the baroreceptors fire at a higher rate and trigger parasympathetic stimulation of the heart.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "baroreceptor afferents via NTS excite the nucleus ambiguus / dorsal vagal motor neurons, increasing vagal efferent (cardioinhibitory) tone."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sympathetic_tone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/norepinephrine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asympathetic_tone%3Apositive%3Anorepinephrine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sympathetic_tone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/norepinephrine> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep — sympathetic norepinephrine release; Guyton & Hall — autonomic transmitters | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.4 (37.4: Regulation of Hormone Production): neuronal signaling from sympathetic nervous system directly stimulates adrenal medulla to release epinephrine and norepinephrine; StatPearls NBK507716 (Physiology, Catecholamines, opened): NE is secreted by sympathetic nerve endings, with a2-autoreceptor feedback modulating its release"
    ; pmo:mechanism "postganglionic sympathetic nerve activity drives noradrenaline release at cardiac and vascular adrenergic terminals (raising synaptic/plasma NE)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sympathetic_tone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/epinephrine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asympathetic_tone%3Apositive%3Aepinephrine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sympathetic_tone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/epinephrine> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Guyton & Hall — adrenal medullary secretion by sympathetic stimulation; Boron & Boulpaep | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-080 (15.2: Divisions of the Autonomic Nervous System): sympathetic preganglionic fibers project to adrenal medulla chromaffin cells, which 'release signaling molecules into the bloodstream' (epinephrine and norepinephrine).; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.4 (37.4: Regulation of Hormone Production): sympathetic nervous system directly stimulates adrenal medulla to release epinephrine and norepinephrine in response to stress (neural stimulus example); StatPearls NBK507716 (Physiology, Catecholamines, opened): epinephrine is synthesized in adrenal-medulla chromaffin cells and released into the bloodstream, rapidly under sympathetic/stress drive"
    ; pmo:mechanism "preganglionic sympathetic drive to the adrenal medulla evokes chromaffin-cell catecholamine secretion, raising circulating epinephrine."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/norepinephrine> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/total_peripheral_resistance> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Anorepinephrine%3Apositive%3Atotal_peripheral_resistance> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/norepinephrine> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/total_peripheral_resistance> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Guyton & Hall — alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction; Boron & Boulpaep — adrenergic vascular tone | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-081 (15.3: Autonomic Reflexes and Homeostasis): norepinephrine from sympathetic postganglionic fibers binds adrenergic receptors on systemic blood vessels causing constriction and increased blood pressure.; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-094 (17.7: The Adrenal Glands): 'it triggers vasoconstriction to blood vessels serving less essential organs such as the gastrointestinal tract, kidneys, and skin.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-35.4 (35.4: The Peripheral Nervous System): sympathetic norepinephrine release causes vasoconstriction, moving blood from skin to heart/muscles/brain, increasing peripheral resistance.; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-41.5 (41.5: Hormonal Control of Osmoregulatory Functions): 'These hormones [epinephrine and norepinephrine] function by acting directly on the smooth muscles of blood vessels to constrict them.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "norepinephrine activates arteriolar alpha-1 adrenoceptors -> vascular smooth muscle contraction -> vasoconstriction raising systemic resistance."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/norepinephrine> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/heart_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Anorepinephrine%3Apositive%3Aheart_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/norepinephrine> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/heart_rate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep — beta-1 chronotropy; Guyton & Hall — sympathetic cardiac stimulation | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-081 (15.3: Autonomic Reflexes and Homeostasis): norepinephrine binds adrenergic receptors on SA-node cells, causing faster depolarization and increased heart rate.; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-094 (17.7: The Adrenal Glands): 'These hormones increase the heart rate, pulse, and blood pressure.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-109 (19.5: Cardiac Physiology): 'NE shortens the repolarization period, thus speeding the rate of depolarization and contraction, which results in an increase in HR.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-35.4 (35.4: The Peripheral Nervous System): 'The physiological effects of this norepinephrine release include...increasing heart rate'."
    ; pmo:mechanism "norepinephrine at SA-node beta-1 adrenoceptors increases pacemaker If/Ca current, raising the rate of spontaneous depolarisation (positive chronotropy)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/epinephrine> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/heart_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aepinephrine%3Apositive%3Aheart_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/epinephrine> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/heart_rate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Guyton & Hall — adrenal medullary catecholamines augment heart rate; Boron & Boulpaep | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-081 (15.3: Autonomic Reflexes and Homeostasis): adrenal medullary epinephrine (and norepinephrine) binds adrenergic receptors; referenced in the same passage as the sympathetic control of heart rate pacemaker cells.; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-094 (17.7: The Adrenal Glands): 'These hormones increase the heart rate, pulse, and blood pressure to prepare the body to fight the perceived threat or flee from it.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-109 (19.5: Cardiac Physiology): 'The catecholamines, epinephrine and NE … binding to the beta-1 receptors … The rate of depolarization is increased by this additional influx of positively charged ions, so the threshold is reached more quickly.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-138 (24.2: Overview of Metabolic Reactions): 'adrenaline/epinephrine... increases heart rate'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.5 (37.5: Endocrine Glands): 'Epinephrine and norepinephrine increase heart rate, breathing rate, cardiac muscle contractions, blood pressure, and blood glucose levels.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "circulating epinephrine acts on SA-node beta-1 (and beta-2) adrenoceptors, reinforcing positive chronotropy."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/parasympathetic_tone> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/heart_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aparasympathetic_tone%3Anegative%3Aheart_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/parasympathetic_tone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/heart_rate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Guyton & Hall — vagal slowing of heart rate; Boron & Boulpaep — muscarinic SA-node control; BioModels MODEL2101280001 (Schölzel 2015) | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-059 (10.8: Cardiac Muscle Tissue): pacemaker cells respond to ANS signals to slow down heart rate; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-080 (15.2: Divisions of the Autonomic Nervous System): 'the other causes heart rate to decrease' (parasympathetic division).; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-081 (15.3: Autonomic Reflexes and Homeostasis): ACh on muscarinic receptors causes SA-node hyperpolarization, slowing heart rate to 60–80 bpm; parasympathetic system is the basis of resting (parasympathetic) cardiac tone.; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-082 (15.4: Central Control): dorsal motor nucleus of vagus nerve decreases heart rate; parasympathetic system slows heart rate from ~100 bpm to resting ~70 bpm via vagal M2 muscarinic signalling at the SA node; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-083 (15.5: Drugs that Affect the Autonomic System): 'the autonomic system does not cause the heart to beat, it just speeds it up (sympathetic) or slows it down (parasympathetic)'; also confirmed in review: 'sympathetic system increases heart rate, whereas the parasympathetic system decreases heart rate'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-109 (19.5: Cardiac Physiology): 'ACh slows HR by opening chemical- or ligand-gated potassium ion channels to slow the rate of spontaneous depolarization … parasympathetic stimulation normally slows HR.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-114 (20.5: Homeostatic Regulation of the Vascular System): 'When blood pressure rises too high...trigger parasympathetic stimulation of the heart. As a result, cardiac output falls.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-33.2 (33.2: Animal Primary Tissues): 'Cardiac muscle is not under voluntary control but can be influenced by the autonomic nervous system to speed up or slow down' — parasympathetic stimulation slows heart rate; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-35.4 (35.4: The Peripheral Nervous System): 'Effects of acetylcholine release on target organs include slowing of heart rate'."
    ; pmo:mechanism "vagal acetylcholine at SA-node M2 muscarinic receptors opens GIRK K+ channels and slows pacemaker depolarisation (negative chronotropy), lowering heart rate."
    .
