    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (sequential hepatic bilirubin handling: uptake -> conjugation -> excretion); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (heme catabolism / bilirubin glucuronidation)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "uptake delivers unconjugated bilirubin into the hepatocyte, supplying substrate for UGT1A1; higher uptake raises the achievable conjugation flux (substrate supply)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ugt1a1_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_bilirubin_conjugation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Augt1a1_activity%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_bilirubin_conjugation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ugt1a1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_bilirubin_conjugation> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (bilirubin glucuronidation by UGT1A1); Harrison's, 'Jaundice' — Gilbert syndrome (OMIM 143500) and Crigler-Najjar type I (OMIM 218800) are UGT1A1 hypomorph/LoF causing unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia"
    ; pmo:mechanism "UGT1A1 (bilirubin UDP-glucuronosyltransferase) catalyzes glucuronidation of bilirubin; enzyme activity is rate-limiting for the conjugation flux"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_bilirubin_conjugation> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_unconjugated_bilirubin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahepatic_bilirubin_conjugation%3Anegative%3Aplasma_unconjugated_bilirubin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_bilirubin_conjugation> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_unconjugated_bilirubin> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 'Jaundice' (impaired conjugation -> unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia; Gilbert OMIM 143500, Crigler-Najjar OMIM 218800); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology"
    ; pmo:mechanism "conjugation consumes intracellular unconjugated bilirubin; higher conjugation flux lowers the unconjugated pool that equilibrates with plasma (UGT1A1 LoF therefore RAISES plasma unconjugated bilirubin)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_bilirubin_conjugation> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_conjugated_bilirubin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahepatic_bilirubin_conjugation%3Apositive%3Aplasma_conjugated_bilirubin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_bilirubin_conjugation> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_conjugated_bilirubin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (formation of bilirubin diglucuronide); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (conjugated bilirubin formation); Crigler-Najjar type I / Gilbert syndrome (UGT1A1 LoF/hypomorph, OMIM 218800/143500) present with reduced plasma conjugated bilirubin alongside unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, confirming conjugation sets the conjugated-bilirubin production term"
    ; pmo:mechanism "conjugation produces bilirubin glucuronides (conjugated/direct bilirubin); higher conjugation flux raises the conjugated pool available for canalicular excretion and, at steady state, the small fraction refluxing to plasma"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_conjugated_bilirubin_excretion> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_conjugated_bilirubin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahepatic_conjugated_bilirubin_excretion%3Anegative%3Aplasma_conjugated_bilirubin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_conjugated_bilirubin_excretion> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_conjugated_bilirubin> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (canalicular MRP2/ABCC2 export of conjugated bilirubin); Harrison's, 'Jaundice' — Dubin-Johnson syndrome (ABCC2/MRP2 LoF; OMIM 237500) causes conjugated hyperbilirubinemia"
    ; pmo:mechanism "MRP2/ABCC2 actively transports conjugated bilirubin across the canalicular membrane into bile; higher excretion clears the hepatocyte conjugated pool and lowers plasma conjugated bilirubin (MRP2 LoF therefore RAISES plasma conjugated bilirubin)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_conjugated_bilirubin_excretion> obo:RO_0002411 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bile_acids> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahepatic_conjugated_bilirubin_excretion%3Aunknown%3Aplasma_bile_acids> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_conjugated_bilirubin_excretion> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bile_acids> ; pmo:sign "?"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (distinct canalicular transporters: MRP2 for organic anions/conjugated bilirubin vs BSEP for bile acids); flagged '?' per PhysioMap policy (do not fabricate a determinate sign)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ambiguous: MRP2/ABCC2 export of bilirubin glucuronides is not the principal bile-acid pump (BSEP/ABCB11 handles bile acids), but cholestatic loss of canalicular secretory function broadly reduces biliary clearance and can raise plasma bile acids; the net partial w.r.t. the bilirubin-excretion node alone is not determinate"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_albumin_synthesis> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_albumin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahepatic_albumin_synthesis%3Apositive%3Aplasma_albumin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_albumin_synthesis> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_albumin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (hepatic protein synthesis; albumin); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (hypoalbuminemia in chronic liver failure / cirrhosis as a marker of synthetic dysfunction) | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-155 (26.2: Body Fluids and Fluid Compartment): 'a decrease in osmotic pressure in chronic and severe liver disease, where the liver is unable to manufacture plasma proteins ... A decrease in the normal levels of plasma proteins results in a decrease of colloid osmotic pressure.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-40.2 (Components of the Blood): 'Human serum albumin is the most abundant protein in human blood plasma and is synthesized in the liver.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "albumin is synthesized almost exclusively by hepatocytes and secreted into plasma; synthesis rate sets the steady-state plasma albumin concentration (reduced hepatic synthetic function in liver failure -> hypoalbuminemia)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_albumin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_unconjugated_bilirubin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_albumin%3Apositive%3Aplasma_unconjugated_bilirubin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_albumin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_unconjugated_bilirubin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (albumin binding of unconjugated bilirubin); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (albumin transport of bilirubin); clinical correlate: low albumin / displacers raise free bilirubin (kernicterus risk)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "unconjugated bilirubin is water-insoluble and circulates tightly bound to albumin; higher plasma albumin increases the bound-bilirubin carrying capacity, raising measured total plasma unconjugated bilirubin (and conversely lowering free bilirubin)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_sodium_concentration> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_osmolality> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_sodium_concentration%3Apositive%3Aplasma_osmolality> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_sodium_concentration> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_osmolality> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep; Guyton & Hall; osmolality ~= 2*[Na+] + glucose/18 + BUN/2.8"
    ; pmo:mechanism "sodium (with accompanying anions) is the principal extracellular osmole"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_osmolality> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adh> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_osmolality%3Apositive%3Aadh> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_osmolality> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adh> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "StatPearls NBK526069 (Physiology, Vasopressin): hypothalamic osmoreceptors respond to changes as small as ~2 mOsm/L; slight elevations in osmolarity trigger ADH secretion. Bourque 2008 Nat Rev Neurosci; Boron & Boulpaep; HumMod ADH module | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-090 (17.3: Hormones): 'If blood osmolarity is too high... osmoreceptors signal the hypothalamus to release ADH'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-091 (17.4: The Pituitary Gland and Hypothalamus): 'In response to high blood osmolarity... the osmoreceptors signal the posterior pituitary to release antidiuretic hormone (ADH).'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-114 (20.5: Homeostatic Regulation of the Vascular System): 'The primary trigger prompting the hypothalamus to release ADH is increasing osmolarity of tissue fluid, usually in response to significant loss of blood volume.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-150 (25.7: Tubular Reabsorption): 'With mild dehydration, plasma osmolarity rises slightly. This increase is detected by osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus, which stimulates the release of ADH from the posterior pituitary.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-156 (26.3: Water Balance): 'If blood osmolality increases above its ideal value, the hypothalamus transmits signals that result in... the hypothalamus of a dehydrated person also releases antidiuretic hormone (ADH) through the posterior pituitary gland.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.3 (37.3: Regulation of Body Processes): 'Dehydration or physiological stress can cause an increase of osmolarity above 300 mOsm/L, which in turn, raises ADH secretion'; osmoreceptors in hypothalamus detect rising electrolyte concentration and trigger ADH release.; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-5.2 (5.2: Passive Transport): 'Osmoreceptors are specialized cells in the brain that monitor solute concentration in the blood. If the solute levels increase beyond a certain range, a hormone releases that slows water loss through the kidney and dilutes the blood to safer levels.' — supports osmolality-driven ADH release."
    ; pmo:mechanism "hypothalamic osmoreceptors stimulate ADH (vasopressin) release as osmolality rises; hypertonic-saline infusion (a controlled osmotic stimulus) raises plasma ADH"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adh> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_osmolality> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aadh%3Anegative%3Aplasma_osmolality> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_osmolality> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "StatPearls NBK526069 (Physiology, Vasopressin): ADH inserts collecting-duct water channels, increasing renal water reabsorption and lowering plasma osmolarity toward baseline. Boron & Boulpaep; Guyton & Hall — closes osmoregulatory negative feedback | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-145 (25.2: Physical Characteristics of Urine): ADH increases collecting-duct aquaporin-2 insertion, driving water reabsorption that retains free water and dilutes plasma osmolality; insufficient ADH causes polyuria and failure to dilute plasma."
    ; pmo:mechanism "ADH increases renal collecting-duct water reabsorption (aquaporin-2), retaining free water that dilutes plasma; desmopressin/vaptan pharmacology corroborates the direction"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adh> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_sodium_concentration> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aadh%3Anegative%3Aplasma_sodium_concentration> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_sodium_concentration> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "StatPearls NBK526069 (Physiology, Vasopressin): SIADH causes excess water reabsorption and thus dilutional hyponatremia. Boron & Boulpaep; clinical SIADH hyponatremia; HumMod"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ADH-driven free-water retention produces dilutional hyponatremia (hallmark of SIADH); vaptan reversal of SIADH corroborates the direction"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_osmolality> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/water_intake> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_osmolality%3Apositive%3Awater_intake> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_osmolality> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/water_intake> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "PMC10507174 (OVLT/SFO regulation of thirst review): increased plasma osmolality is sensed by OVLT and SFO neurons to drive thirst and water intake; OVLT ablation alters drinking. Bourque 2008; Boron & Boulpaep (osmotic thirst); Guyton & Hall | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-156 (26.3: Water Balance): 'If blood osmolality increases above its ideal value, the hypothalamus transmits signals that result in a conscious awareness of thirst.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "rising osmolality/hypernatremia is sensed by OVLT and SFO osmosensing neurons (Nax) that drive hypothalamic thirst and water intake; OVLT lesion alters drinking"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/water_intake> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_osmolality> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Awater_intake%3Anegative%3Aplasma_osmolality> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/water_intake> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_osmolality> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep; Guyton & Hall — closes osmoregulatory negative feedback"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ingested free water dilutes body fluids, lowering osmolality"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/water_intake> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_sodium_concentration> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Awater_intake%3Anegative%3Aplasma_sodium_concentration> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/water_intake> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_sodium_concentration> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "StatPearls NBK544365 (Physiology, Plasma Osmolality): free water dilutes plasma solutes including [Na+]. Boron & Boulpaep; Guyton & Hall (water-balance control of [Na+])"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ingested free water dilutes plasma solutes including sodium concentration (a water-balance variable) — the direct inverse of dehydration-driven concentration"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/water_intake> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/extracellular_fluid_volume> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Awater_intake%3Apositive%3Aextracellular_fluid_volume> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/water_intake> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/extracellular_fluid_volume> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep; Guyton & Hall — coupling to volume core"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ingested water is distributed into and expands the extracellular fluid"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/angiotensin_II> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/water_intake> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aangiotensin_II%3Apositive%3Awater_intake> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/angiotensin_II> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/water_intake> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "PMC10507174 (OVLT/SFO thirst review): ANG II induces thirst/drinking via the SFO; ICV/systemic ANG II is a potent dipsogen. Bourque 2008; Boron & Boulpaep (AngII-induced thirst); HumMod | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-114 (20.5: Homeostatic Regulation of the Vascular System): 'Angiotensin II also stimulates the thirst center in the hypothalamus, so an individual will likely consume more fluids, again increasing blood volume and pressure.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-156 (26.3: Water Balance): 'angiotensin II... helps stimulate thirst'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.3 (37.3: Regulation of Body Processes): 'Angiotensin II in addition to being a potent vasoconstrictor also causes an increase in ADH and increased thirst, both of which help to raise blood pressure.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "circulating angiotensin II acts on subfornical-organ AT1 receptors to induce thirst and drinking (ICV/systemic ANG II is a potent dipsogen; AT1 antagonists block it)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldosterone> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_potassium> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aaldosterone%3Anegative%3Aplasma_potassium> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldosterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_potassium> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "StatPearls NBK470339 (Physiology, Aldosterone): aldosterone increases distal/collecting-duct potassium secretion/excretion, lowering plasma potassium. Boron & Boulpaep; Guyton & Hall; HumMod aldosterone module"
    ; pmo:mechanism "aldosterone stimulates renal distal/collecting-duct K+ secretion (kaliuresis), lowering plasma K+; spironolactone->hyperkalemia and Addison/Conn corroborate the direction"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_potassium> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldosterone> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_potassium%3Apositive%3Aaldosterone> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_potassium> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldosterone> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The potassium effect is mediated through membrane depolarization and opening of calcium channels\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 15, p. 490"
    ; pmo:mechanism "elevated plasma [K+] → zona glomerulosa membrane depolarization → opening of calcium channels → increased intracellular Ca2+ → calmodulin kinase activation → CYP11B2 transcription → aldosterone synthesis"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcium> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pth> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_calcium%3Anegative%3Apth> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcium> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pth> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Peterson & Riggs 2010 bone-mineral model; Raposo 2002; HumMod parathyroid; textbook | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-035 (6.8: Calcium Homeostasis - Interactions of the Skeletal System and Other Organ): 'When calcium is not binding to these receptors, the cells release PTH' — parathyroid CaSR-mediated suppression of PTH when Ca2+ is adequate; release when Ca2+ falls.; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-093 (17.6: The Parathyroid Glands): 'A negative feedback loop regulates the levels of PTH, with rising blood calcium levels inhibiting further release of PTH'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-099 (17.12: Development and Aging of the Endocrine System): 'elevated blood calcium levels inhibit secretion of PTH' (Chapter Review §17.5); 'reduced dietary calcium levels, causing a compensatory increase in parathyroid hormone' (aging section).; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-137 (23.8: Chemical Digestion and Absorption - A Closer Look): 'Blood levels of ionic calcium determine the absorption of dietary calcium. When blood levels of ionic calcium drop, parathyroid hormone (PTH) secreted by the parathyroid glands stimulates...' — falling plasma calcium drives PTH secretion; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-152 (25.9: Endocrine Regulation of Kidney Function): 'Parathyroid hormone (PTH)...produced by the parathyroid glands in response to decreased circulating Ca++ levels.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-153 (Regulation of Fluid Volume and Composition): 'The parathyroid glands monitor and respond to circulating levels of Ca++ in the blood. When levels drop too low, PTH is released'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-157 (26.4: Electrolyte Balance): 'PTH is released from the parathyroid gland in response to a decrease in the concentration of blood calcium.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-33.3 (Homeostasis): 'If calcium levels decrease, specialized cells in the parathyroid gland sense this and release parathyroid hormone (PTH).'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.3 (37.3: Regulation of Body Processes): 'PTH is released in response to low blood Ca2+ levels'; 'PTH release is inhibited by rising blood calcium levels.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.5 (37.5: Endocrine Glands): 'PTH increases blood calcium concentrations when calcium ion levels fall below normal.' (inverse: high calcium suppresses PTH)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "parathyroid calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR) suppresses PTH secretion"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pth> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcium> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apth%3Apositive%3Aplasma_calcium> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pth> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcium> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Peterson & Riggs 2010 (bone + kidney Ca handling); HumMod; standard endocrinology | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-034 (Exercise, Nutrition, Hormones, and Bone Tissue): 'PTH stimulates osteoclast proliferation and activity. As a result, calcium is released from the bones into the circulation, thus increasing the calcium ion concentration in the blood.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-090 (17.3: Hormones): table entry 'PTH: Increases blood Ca2+ levels'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-093 (17.6: The Parathyroid Glands): 'PTH secretion causes the release of calcium from the bones by stimulating osteoclasts...PTH also inhibits osteoblasts...PTH causes increased reabsorption of calcium...in the kidney tubules'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-099 (17.12: Development and Aging of the Endocrine System): 'PTH stimulates demineralization of bones to increase blood calcium levels'.; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-137 (23.8: Chemical Digestion and Absorption - A Closer Look): 'PTH secreted by the parathyroid glands stimulates the release of calcium ions from bone matrices and increases the reabsorption of calcium by the kidneys' — PTH raises plasma calcium via bone and kidney; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-150 (25.7: Tubular Reabsorption): PTH induces Ca++ channel insertion in DCT luminal surface and calcitriol synthesis, both of which promote calcium recovery and raise plasma calcium; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-152 (25.9: Endocrine Regulation of Kidney Function): 'By ridding the blood of phosphate, higher circulating Ca++ levels are permitted.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-153 (Regulation of Fluid Volume and Composition): 'PTH is released to stimulate the DCT to reabsorb Ca++ from the forming urine'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-157 (26.4: Electrolyte Balance): 'PTH raises blood calcium levels by inhibiting the loss of calcium through the kidneys' and 'activates osteoclasts to break down bone matrix and release inorganic calcium-phosphate salts.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-166 (28.5: Changes During Pregnancy, Labor, and Birth): 'increased parathyroid levels mobilize calcium from maternal bones for fetal use.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-33.3 (Homeostasis): 'The effects of PTH are to raise blood levels of [calcium]' via increased intestinal absorption, renal reabsorption, and bone resorption.; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.3 (37.3: Regulation of Body Processes): 'PTH increases Ca2+ levels by targeting the skeleton, the kidneys, and the intestine.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.5 (37.5: Endocrine Glands): 'PTH increases blood calcium concentrations when calcium ion levels fall below normal.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "PTH drives osteoclastic bone resorption and distal renal Ca2+ reabsorption"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pth> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/calcitriol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apth%3Apositive%3Acalcitriol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pth> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/calcitriol> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Peterson & Riggs 2010; Raposo 2002; standard endocrinology | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-034 (Exercise, Nutrition, Hormones, and Bone Tissue): 'another function of PTH is to stimulate the synthesis of vitamin D [calcitriol]'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-035 (6.8: Calcium Homeostasis - Interactions of the Skeletal System and Other Organ): 'PTH stimulates the synthesis of vitamin D, which in turn, stimulates calcium absorption from any digested food in the small intestine.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-093 (17.6: The Parathyroid Glands): 'PTH initiates the production of the steroid hormone calcitriol...in the kidneys'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-098 (17.11: Organs with Secondary Endocrine Functions): 'the kidneys also play a role in regulating blood calcium levels through the production of calcitriol from vitamin D3, which is released in response to the secretion of parathyroid hormone (PTH)'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-137 (23.8: Chemical Digestion and Absorption - A Closer Look): 'PTH also upregulates the activation of vitamin D in the kidney' — PTH stimulates renal calcitriol synthesis; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-152 (25.9: Endocrine Regulation of Kidney Function): 'PTH...stimulates the hydroxylation of calcidiol to calcitriol (1,25-hydroxycholecalciferol, the active form of vitamin D).'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-153 (Regulation of Fluid Volume and Composition): 'PTH also stimulates the renal conversion of calcidiol into calcitriol, the active form of vitamin D'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-154 (25.11: The Urinary System and Homeostasis): 'PTH stimulates the final step in the formation of active vitamin D3' (line 361); 'Calcium recovery in the DCT is influenced by PTH and active vitamin D' (line 343); OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-157 (26.4: Electrolyte Balance): 'PTH also increases the gastrointestinal absorption of dietary calcium by converting vitamin D into dihydroxyvitamin D (calcitriol).'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.3 (37.3: Regulation of Body Processes): 'PTH triggers the formation of calcitriol, an active form of vitamin D, which acts on the intestines to increase absorption of dietary calcium.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.5 (37.5: Endocrine Glands): 'PTH... (3) stimulates synthesis and secretion of calcitriol by the kidneys, which enhances Ca2+ absorption by the digestive system.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "PTH stimulates renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase (CYP27B1) -> calcitriol synthesis"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/calcitriol> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcium> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acalcitriol%3Apositive%3Aplasma_calcium> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/calcitriol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcium> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Peterson & Riggs 2010 (gut Ca absorption); HumMod; textbook | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-034 (Exercise, Nutrition, Hormones, and Bone Tissue): 'calcitriol, the active form of vitamin D, is produced by the kidneys and stimulates the absorption of calcium and phosphate from the digestive tract'; 'vitamin D promotes intestinal absorption of calcium'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-035 (6.8: Calcium Homeostasis - Interactions of the Skeletal System and Other Organ): 'vitamin D ... stimulates calcium absorption from any digested food in the small intestine' — calcitriol raises intestinal Ca2+ absorption, increasing plasma calcium.; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-093 (17.6: The Parathyroid Glands): 'Calcitriol then stimulates increased absorption of dietary calcium by the intestines'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-098 (17.11: Organs with Secondary Endocrine Functions): 'Calcitriol — Aids in the absorption of Ca2+'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-137 (23.8: Chemical Digestion and Absorption - A Closer Look): 'which then facilitates intestinal calcium ion absorption' — calcitriol raises plasma calcium by increasing gut Ca absorption; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-144 (24.8: Nutrition and Diet) — Vitamin D 'Aids in calcium absorption, promoting bone growth'; deficiency causes 'Rickets, bone pain, muscle weakness'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-153 (Regulation of Fluid Volume and Composition): 'Calcitriol then stimulates the intestines to absorb more Ca++ from the diet'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-154 (25.11: The Urinary System and Homeostasis): 'Activated vitamin D is important for absorption of Ca++ in the digestive tract, its reabsorption in the kidney, and the maintenance of normal serum concentrations of Ca++ and phosphate' (lines 17-19); OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-157 (26.4: Electrolyte Balance): 'Calcium is absorbed through the intestines under the influence of activated vitamin D. A deficiency of vitamin D leads to a decrease in absorbed calcium.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-34.2 (34.2: Nutrition and Energy Production): Table 34.2 — 'Vitamin D: Critical for calcium absorption for bone development and strength.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.3 (37.3: Regulation of Body Processes): calcitriol 'acts on the intestines to increase absorption of dietary calcium' — increased intestinal absorption raises plasma calcium.; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.5 (37.5: Endocrine Glands): 'PTH... stimulates synthesis and secretion of calcitriol by the kidneys, which enhances Ca2+ absorption by the digestive system' — calcitriol raises plasma calcium via intestinal Ca2+ absorption."
    ; pmo:mechanism "calcitriol increases intestinal Ca2+ absorption (and synergises with PTH on bone)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/calcitriol> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pth> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acalcitriol%3Anegative%3Apth> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/calcitriol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pth> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; dcterms:source "Peterson & Riggs 2010; standard endocrinology (vitamin D - PTH feedback)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "calcitriol directly suppresses PTH gene transcription/secretion (VDR feedback)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pth> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_phosphate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apth%3Anegative%3Aplasma_phosphate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pth> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_phosphate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; dcterms:source "Peterson & Riggs 2010 (renal phosphate handling); HumMod; textbook | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-152 (25.9: Endocrine Regulation of Kidney Function): 'It [PTH] also blocks reabsorption of phosphate (PO3–), causing its loss in the urine.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-153 (Regulation of Fluid Volume and Composition): 'When Ca++ levels are low, PTH inhibits reabsorption of [phosphate] so that its blood level drops, allowing Ca++ levels to rise'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-154 (25.11: The Urinary System and Homeostasis): 'PTH... reduces phosphate reabsorption, resulting in higher circulating Ca++ levels' (lines 361-362); OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-157 (26.4: Electrolyte Balance): 'PTH also increases the loss of phosphate through the kidneys.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "PTH induces phosphaturia (downregulates proximal-tubule NaPi cotransporters)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/calcitriol> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_phosphate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acalcitriol%3Apositive%3Aplasma_phosphate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/calcitriol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_phosphate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; dcterms:source "Peterson & Riggs 2010 (gut phosphate); standard endocrinology"
    ; pmo:mechanism "calcitriol increases intestinal phosphate absorption"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_phosphate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pth> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_phosphate%3Apositive%3Apth> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_phosphate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pth> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; dcterms:source "Peterson & Riggs 2010; clinical CKD-MBD; standard endocrinology"
    ; pmo:mechanism "hyperphosphatemia raises PTH (lowers ionised Ca2+ and directly stimulates parathyroid)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_phosphate> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/calcitriol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_phosphate%3Anegative%3Acalcitriol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_phosphate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/calcitriol> ; pmo:sign "-"
