    ; pmo:mechanism "phosphate (via FGF23) suppresses renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase, lowering calcitriol"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcium> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/parathyroid_chief_pth_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_calcium%3Anegative%3Aparathyroid_chief_pth_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcium> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/parathyroid_chief_pth_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The parathyroid glands produce and secrete PTH... in response to low blood calcium levels\" and \"rising blood calcium levels inhibiting further release of PTH\" — OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e, section anp-093 (The Parathyroid Glands)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "chief-cell calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR) detects ionized Ca2+; a fall in plasma Ca2+ disinhibits PTH secretion while a rise suppresses it (inverse regulatory influence)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/venous_return> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/end_diastolic_volume> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Avenous_return%3Apositive%3Aend_diastolic_volume> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/venous_return> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/end_diastolic_volume> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; dcterms:source "Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology, 'The Heart as a Pump' (venous return sets EDV); legacy example-frank-starling.json (venous_return -> end_diastolic_volume) | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-108 (19.4: Cardiac Cycle): 'Approximately 70–80 percent of ventricular filling occurs by this method' (passive inflow from veins during diastole before atrial kick); OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-109 (19.5: Cardiac Physiology): 'Any sympathetic stimulation to the venous system will increase venous return to the heart, which contributes to ventricular filling, and EDV and preload.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-110 (19.6: Development of the Heart) — chapter review 19.4: 'Venous return determines preload … Preload then impacts both EDV and ESV.' (lines 413–415).; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-114 (20.5: Homeostatic Regulation of the Vascular System): 'As blood returns to the heart more quickly, preload rises and the Frank-Starling principle tells us that contraction of the cardiac muscle in the atria and ventricles will be more forceful.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "venous return / diastolic filling raises ventricular end-diastolic volume (quality: filling flow -> disposition: ventricular compliance -> process: heart process (diastolic filling) GO:0003015 -> quality: end-diastolic volume)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/end_diastolic_volume> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/stroke_volume> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aend_diastolic_volume%3Apositive%3Astroke_volume> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/end_diastolic_volume> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/stroke_volume> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; dcterms:source "Guyton & Hall, Frank-Starling law of the heart (ventricular function curve); Boron & Boulpaep 'The Heart as a Pump' (preload -> SV); legacy example-frank-starling.json | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-108 (19.4: Cardiac Cycle): 'At the end of atrial systole and just prior to ventricular contraction, the ventricles contain approximately 130 mL blood... Stroke volume will normally be in the range of 70–80 mL... there is still 50–60 mL of blood remaining in the ventricle following contraction (end systolic volume)'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-110 (19.6: Development of the Heart) — key terms: 'Frank-Starling mechanism: relationship between ventricular stretch and contraction in which the force of heart contraction is directly proportional to the initial length of the muscle fiber.' (lines 178–180); chapter review: 'SV is the difference between EDV and ESV.' (line 417).; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-114 (20.5: Homeostatic Regulation of the Vascular System): 'As blood returns to the heart more quickly, preload rises and the Frank-Starling principle tells us that contraction of the cardiac muscle in the atria and ventricles will be more forceful.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Frank-Starling law — greater diastolic fibre stretch (sarcomere length) increases myofilament overlap and calcium sensitivity, raising contractile force and ejected volume (quality: EDV/fibre length -> disposition: length-dependent activation GO:0055117 -> process: cardiac muscle contraction GO:0060048 -> quality: stroke volume)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/myocardial_contractility> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/stroke_volume> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amyocardial_contractility%3Apositive%3Astroke_volume> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/myocardial_contractility> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/stroke_volume> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology (contractility as a determinant of SV; Suga end-systolic elastance); Guyton & Hall (sympathetic inotropy shifts the function curve up)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "increased inotropic state raises systolic elastance / force at given preload and afterload, ejecting more volume (quality: contractility -> disposition: crossbridge cycling capacity -> process: regulation of cardiac muscle contraction GO:0055117 -> quality: stroke volume)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/afterload> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/stroke_volume> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aafterload%3Anegative%3Astroke_volume> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/afterload> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/stroke_volume> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; dcterms:source "Guyton & Hall (raised aortic/arterial pressure reduces stroke volume at constant filling); Boron & Boulpaep (afterload inversely related to SV); legacy example-frank-starling.json (afterload reduces stroke_volume)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "higher ejection load (systolic wall stress / aortic pressure) opposes shortening, raising end-systolic volume and reducing ejected volume at fixed preload and contractility (quality: afterload -> disposition: load-dependent shortening -> process: cardiac muscle contraction GO:0060048 -> quality: stroke volume)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mean_arterial_pressure> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/afterload> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amean_arterial_pressure%3Apositive%3Aafterload> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mean_arterial_pressure> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/afterload> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; dcterms:source "Guyton & Hall (aortic/arterial pressure is the principal afterload); Boron & Boulpaep (afterload ~ systolic wall stress, rises with arterial pressure)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "arterial pressure during ejection sets the load the ventricle ejects against; by Laplace systolic wall stress rises with chamber pressure (quality: arterial pressure -> disposition: ventricular wall mechanics -> quality: afterload)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/total_peripheral_resistance> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/afterload> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atotal_peripheral_resistance%3Apositive%3Aafterload> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/total_peripheral_resistance> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/afterload> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; dcterms:source "Guyton & Hall (peripheral resistance contributes to the impedance load on the ventricle); Boron & Boulpaep (afterload reflects aortic input impedance / TPR)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "higher systemic vascular resistance raises the pressure the ventricle must develop to eject, increasing afterload (quality: peripheral resistance -> disposition: impedance to ejection -> quality: afterload)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sympathetic_tone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/myocardial_contractility> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asympathetic_tone%3Apositive%3Amyocardial_contractility> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sympathetic_tone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/myocardial_contractility> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology (sympathetic / beta-adrenergic positive inotropy); Guyton & Hall (autonomic control of cardiac contractility) | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-109 (19.5: Cardiac Physiology): 'sympathetic stimulation is a positive inotrope … NE … increase metabolic rate and the force of contraction.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "beta-1 adrenergic stimulation increases cytosolic Ca2+ transients and crossbridge force (positive inotropy) (quality: sympathetic tone -> disposition: beta-adrenergic responsiveness -> process: regulation of heart contraction GO:0008016 -> quality: contractility)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/stroke_volume> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/end_diastolic_volume> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Astroke_volume%3Anegative%3Aend_diastolic_volume> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/stroke_volume> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/end_diastolic_volume> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep (EDV = ESV + venous filling; greater emptying lowers carried-over volume); Guyton & Hall (ventricular volume balance over the cardiac cycle)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "at a given filling rate, greater ejection lowers the residual (end-systolic) volume carried into the next diastole, reducing end-diastolic volume — the volume-draining arm that, with venous filling, closes the ventricular volume balance"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/myocardial_contractility> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ejection_fraction> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amyocardial_contractility%3Apositive%3Aejection_fraction> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/myocardial_contractility> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ejection_fraction> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep (EF rises with contractility); Guyton & Hall (inotropic state and ejection fraction)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "EF is a load-dependent index of contractility; raising inotropy increases the fraction of EDV ejected (quality: contractility -> process: regulation of cardiac muscle contraction GO:0055117 -> quality: ejection fraction)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/afterload> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ejection_fraction> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aafterload%3Anegative%3Aejection_fraction> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/afterload> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ejection_fraction> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep (afterload lowers EF); Guyton & Hall (raised arterial pressure reduces ejection fraction)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "increased ejection load reduces the fraction of end-diastolic volume ejected (quality: afterload -> disposition: load-dependent shortening -> quality: ejection fraction)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/end_diastolic_volume> obo:RO_0002411 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ejection_fraction> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aend_diastolic_volume%3Aunknown%3Aejection_fraction> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/end_diastolic_volume> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ejection_fraction> ; pmo:sign "?"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep (EF = SV/EDV; in dilated ventricles SV may rise while EF falls — net direction not determinate); Guyton & Hall (ventricular function curve)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "EF = SV / EDV: by Frank-Starling a larger EDV raises the numerator SV (+), but EDV is also the denominator (-); the net sign of EF w.r.t. EDV is genuinely ambiguous and context-dependent (depends on the slope of the function curve vs. the volume ratio)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/vessel_injury> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tissue_factor> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Avessel_injury%3Apositive%3Atissue_factor> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/vessel_injury> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tissue_factor> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology (hemostasis: TF exposure on injury); Wajima 2009 (TF-initiated extrinsic pathway), BioModels BIOMD0000000339"
    ; pmo:mechanism "vascular wall injury exposes subendothelial tissue factor (FIII) to plasma, increasing active TF available to bind FVIIa"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tissue_factor> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thrombin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atissue_factor%3Apositive%3Athrombin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tissue_factor> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thrombin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Wajima 2009, Clin Pharmacol Ther 86:290 (extrinsic-pathway thrombin generation), BioModels BIOMD0000000338/339; Boron & Boulpaep | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-104 (18.6: Hemostasis): 'factor III (thromboplastin) ... This enzyme complex leads to activation of factor X ... the enzyme prothrombinase converts factor II, the inactive enzyme prothrombin, into the active enzyme thrombin'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "TF:FVIIa complex activates FX -> FXa, which with FVa (prothrombinase) cleaves prothrombin (FII) to thrombin (FIIa)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thrombin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/platelet_activation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Athrombin%3Apositive%3Aplatelet_activation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thrombin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/platelet_activation> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep (thrombin-induced platelet activation, PAR receptors); Panteleev 2010 BioModels BIOMD0000000740 (platelet/surface-dependent thrombin generation) | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-005 (1.6: Homeostasis): 'As each step of clotting occurs, it stimulates the release of more clotting substances' — positive feedback amplification of clotting cascade; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-104 (18.6: Hemostasis): 'activated platelet level / procoagulant surface ... thrombin [activates] platelets (via PAR receptors)'; Jakubowski & Flatt, Fundamentals of Biochemistry Vol I §biochem-11.1.3 (Peripheral Proteins): '…or when thrombin activates platelet receptors' — thrombin-mediated platelet receptor activation corroborated; Jakubowski & Flatt, Fundamentals of Biochemistry Vol I §biochem-7.2.5 (Glycosaminoglycans - Heteropolysaccharides with Disaccharide Repeating Units): 'thrombin, which cleaves fibrinogen and activates platelets to form clots' — corroborates thrombin activating platelets"
    ; pmo:mechanism "thrombin cleaves platelet PAR-1/PAR-4 receptors, activating platelets and exposing procoagulant phosphatidylserine surface for assembly of tenase/prothrombinase complexes"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/platelet_activation> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thrombin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplatelet_activation%3Apositive%3Athrombin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/platelet_activation> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thrombin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Panteleev 2010 BioModels BIOMD0000000740; Xu 2002 BioModels MODEL1806130001 (activated-factor model with platelet activation); Boron & Boulpaep | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-104 (18.6: Hemostasis): 'chemicals released by the platelets increase the rate of these activation reactions ... factor VIII ... from the platelets and endothelial cells combines with factor IX ... to form an enzyme complex that activates factor X ... leading to the common pathway'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-40.2 (Components of the Blood): platelet contents 'activate other platelets and also interact with other coagulation factors' to drive coagulation cascade amplification."
    ; pmo:mechanism "activated platelet procoagulant surface localizes and accelerates tenase (FIXa:FVIIIa) and prothrombinase (FXa:FVa) complex assembly, amplifying thrombin generation"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thrombin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thrombin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Athrombin%3Apositive%3Athrombin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thrombin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thrombin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Wajima 2009 (FV/FVIII/FXI activation by thrombin), BioModels BIOMD0000000338; Boron & Boulpaep (thrombin amplification)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "thrombin feedback-activates cofactors FV->FVa and FVIII->FVIIIa and FXI->FXIa, accelerating its own further generation (intrinsic-tenase amplification)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thrombin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fibrin_clot> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Athrombin%3Apositive%3Afibrin_clot> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thrombin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fibrin_clot> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Wajima 2009 (fibrinogen->fibrin, FXIII), BioModels BIOMD0000000339; Naski 1991 BioModels MODEL1808210002 (thrombin-catalyzed fibrinogen conversion) | textbook corroboration: Jakubowski & Flatt, Fundamentals of Biochemistry Vol I §biochem-5.03.3 (Allostery within monomeric protein): thrombin is described as cleaving circulating fibrinogen into fibrin, which self-associates to form a fibrin clot; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-104 (18.6: Hemostasis): 'thrombin converts factor I, the soluble fibrinogen, into the insoluble fibrin protein strands. Factor XIII then stabilizes the fibrin clot'; Jakubowski & Flatt, Fundamentals of Biochemistry Vol I §biochem-11.1.3 (Peripheral Proteins): 'Clotting is initiated when the serine protease thrombin cleaves fibrinogen to form fibrin, which self-associates to form a fibrin clot'; Jakubowski & Flatt, Fundamentals of Biochemistry Vol I §biochem-4.7.6 (Fibrinogen): thrombin cleaves fibrinopeptides A and B from fibrinogen alpha and beta chains, exposing knob-A and knob-B sequences that bind holes on adjacent fibrinogen to drive polymerization into fibrin clots; Jakubowski & Flatt, Fundamentals of Biochemistry Vol I §biochem-7.2.5 (Glycosaminoglycans - Heteropolysaccharides with Disaccharide Repeating Units): 'thrombin, which cleaves fibrinogen and activates platelets to form clots' — corroborates thrombin promoting fibrin clot formation; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-33.3 (Homeostasis): 'The final factor, a protein called thrombin, converts inactive fibrinogen to fibrin' — thrombin drives clot formation.; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-40.2 (Components of the Blood): 'coagulation factors...convert fibrinogen, a water-soluble protein present in blood serum into fibrin (a non-water soluble protein), causing the blood to clot.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "thrombin cleaves fibrinogen to fibrin monomers and activates FXIII, which cross-links fibrin into a stable clot"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/antithrombin> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thrombin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aantithrombin%3Anegative%3Athrombin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/antithrombin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thrombin> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Wajima 2009 (antithrombin inhibition), BioModels BIOMD0000000340; Boron & Boulpaep (antithrombin anticoagulant system) | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-104 (18.6: Hemostasis): 'Antithrombin inactivates factor X and opposes the conversion of prothrombin (factor II) to thrombin in the common pathway'; Wong, Cells: Molecules and Mechanisms §wong-13.14 (Gap Junctions - Undeclared): 'Heparin... activating antithrombin III, which inhibits clotting' — corroborates antithrombin anticoagulant activity inhibiting thrombin; Jakubowski & Flatt, Fundamentals of Biochemistry Vol I §biochem-7.2.5 (Glycosaminoglycans - Heteropolysaccharides with Disaccharide Repeating Units): 'Heparin acts as a catalyst to accelerate the inhibition of the enzyme thrombin... antithrombin III [inhibits thrombin]' — corroborates antithrombin inhibiting thrombin activity"
    ; pmo:mechanism "antithrombin forms 1:1 inhibitory complexes with thrombin (and FXa, FIXa), neutralizing thrombin activity (heparin-accelerated serpin inhibition)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thrombin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/activated_protein_C> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Athrombin%3Apositive%3Aactivated_protein_C> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thrombin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/activated_protein_C> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep (thrombin-thrombomodulin -> protein C activation); Wajima 2009 (protein C / APC pathway), BioModels BIOMD0000000340 | textbook corroboration: Jakubowski & Flatt, Fundamentals of Biochemistry Vol I §biochem-5.03.3 (Allostery within monomeric protein): thrombin binding to endothelial thrombomodulin enables it to cleave protein C, generating activated protein C with anticoagulant properties"
    ; pmo:mechanism "thrombin bound to endothelial thrombomodulin activates protein C to APC; APC generation rises with thrombin"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/activated_protein_C> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thrombin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aactivated_protein_C%3Anegative%3Athrombin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/activated_protein_C> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thrombin> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Wajima 2009 (APC inactivation of FVa/FVIIIa), BioModels BIOMD0000000340; Nayak 2015 BioModels BIOMD0000000611; Boron & Boulpaep"
    ; pmo:mechanism "APC (with protein S) proteolytically inactivates FVa and FVIIIa, the cofactors of prothrombinase/tenase, reducing thrombin generation"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fibrin_clot> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasmin_fibrinolysis> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afibrin_clot%3Apositive%3Aplasmin_fibrinolysis> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fibrin_clot> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasmin_fibrinolysis> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep (fibrin-stimulated tPA/plasminogen activation); Wajima 2009 (plasmin/fibrinolysis sub-network), BioModels BIOMD0000000340"
    ; pmo:mechanism "fibrin provides the surface co-localizing tPA and plasminogen, accelerating plasmin generation (fibrin-enhanced fibrinolysis)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasmin_fibrinolysis> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fibrin_clot> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasmin_fibrinolysis%3Anegative%3Afibrin_clot> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasmin_fibrinolysis> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fibrin_clot> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Wajima 2009 (plasmin degradation of fibrin), BioModels BIOMD0000000340; Boron & Boulpaep (plasmin fibrinolysis) | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-104 (18.6: Hemostasis): 'the inactive protein plasminogen is converted into the active plasmin, which gradually breaks down the fibrin of the clot'; Jakubowski & Flatt, Fundamentals of Biochemistry Vol I §biochem-4.7.6 (Fibrinogen): plasmin cleaves fibrin clots, facilitating their removal; the long flexible domain structure of fibrinogen is required to be accessible to plasmin"
    ; pmo:mechanism "plasmin proteolytically degrades cross-linked fibrin into fibrin-degradation products (D-dimer), dissolving the clot"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fibrin_clot> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/blood_viscosity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afibrin_clot%3Apositive%3Ablood_viscosity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fibrin_clot> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/blood_viscosity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep / Guyton & Hall (clot impeding flow); rheology of fibrin networks (clot raises apparent viscosity)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "a fibrin/platelet clot burden raises local effective whole-blood viscosity and impedes flow within the affected vessel segment"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_branched_chain_amino_acids> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/muscle_protein_synthesis_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_branched_chain_amino_acids%3Apositive%3Amuscle_protein_synthesis_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_branched_chain_amino_acids> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/muscle_protein_synthesis_rate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Maple syrup urine disease OMIM 248600; Norton LE & Layman DK, J Nutr 2006 (leucine regulates translation initiation of muscle protein synthesis via mTOR); Suryawan A et al., Am J Physiol Endocrinol Metab 2008 (BCAA stimulate mTORC1-mediated protein synthesis); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 18 (BCAA catabolism, BCKDH complex); StatPearls MSUD NBK557773. | intervention: do(BCKDH complex loss-of-function) — maple syrup urine disease OMIM 248600"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Branched-chain amino acids — especially leucine — are the primary activators of the mTORC1 kinase complex in skeletal muscle; mTORC1 phosphorylates 4E-BP1 and S6K1, stimulating cap-dependent translation and muscle protein synthesis. Elevated plasma BCAA (as in maple syrup urine disease, BCKDH LoF) drives supraphysiological mTORC1 activation and protein anabolism. do(BCKDH LoF) → plasma BCAA ↑ → mTORC1 ↑ → muscle protein synthesis rate ↑."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_serine> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_gluconeogenesis> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_serine%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_gluconeogenesis> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_serine> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_gluconeogenesis> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 14 (serine dehydratase: serine → pyruvate + NH3; pyruvate as gluconeogenic precursor); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (amino acid gluconeogenesis); OMMBID PHGDH deficiency (serine biosynthesis disorders) — low serine, impaired gluconeogenic substrate availability; Newsholme & Leech, Functional Biochemistry in Health and Disease (amino acid gluconeogenesis). | intervention: do(PHGDH loss-of-function) — serine biosynthesis deficiency; also: do(glycine cleavage system LoF) — nonketotic hyperglycinemia (NKH), OMIM 605899"
    ; pmo:mechanism "L-serine is a gluconeogenic amino acid; hepatic serine dehydratase (SDS) deaminates serine to pyruvate, which enters the gluconeogenic pathway (pyruvate carboxylase → OAA → PEPCK → …→ glucose-6-phosphate). Higher plasma serine provides more gluconeogenic substrate, raising hepatic gluconeogenesis flux. In PHGDH deficiency (serine biosynthesis defect), low plasma serine reduces gluconeogenic substrate supply. do(PHGDH LoF) → plasma serine ↓ → hepatic gluconeogenesis ↓."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_creatine> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_creatinine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_creatine%3Apositive%3Aplasma_creatinine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_creatine> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_creatinine> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Creatine deficiency syndromes — AGAT deficiency OMIM 612718, GAMT deficiency OMIM 601240; Schulze A, J Inherit Metab Dis 2003 (markedly reduced urinary creatinine in AGAT/GAMT deficiency); GeneReviews creatine deficiency (NBK3794): 'plasma creatinine is reduced'; Wyss M & Kaddurah-Daouk R, Physiol Rev 2000 (creatine-to-creatinine conversion rate proportional to creatine pool size). | intervention: do(AGAT loss-of-function) — AGAT deficiency OMIM 612718; do(GAMT loss-of-function) — GAMT deficiency OMIM 601240"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Creatinine is formed by the spontaneous, non-enzymatic, irreversible cyclization of phosphocreatine and creatine in muscle and other tissues. The daily creatinine production rate is directly proportional to the total body creatine pool; plasma creatinine concentration reflects this production at steady state. In creatine synthesis deficiency (AGAT or GAMT LoF), the total creatine pool is markedly reduced, resulting in very low plasma and urinary creatinine. do(AGAT LoF or GAMT LoF) → plasma creatine ↓ → creatinine production ↓ → plasma creatinine ↓."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/carbamoylphosphate_synthetase_1_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/blood_urea_nitrogen> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acarbamoylphosphate_synthetase_1_activity%3Apositive%3Ablood_urea_nitrogen> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/carbamoylphosphate_synthetase_1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/blood_urea_nitrogen> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "CPS1 deficiency OMIM 237300; ScienceDirect CPS1 deficiency overview — 'CPS1 deficiency causes hyperammonemia with low citrulline and reduced BUN'; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 18 (the urea cycle; CPS1 as the committed step, rate-limiting mitochondrial enzyme); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (hepatic nitrogen handling, urea synthesis); Batshaw ML et al., N Engl J Med 1984 (urea cycle disorders, low BUN in CPS1/OTC deficiency). | intervention: do(CPS1 loss-of-function) — CPS1 deficiency OMIM 237300; do(OTC loss-of-function) — OTC deficiency OMIM 311250; do(NAGS loss-of-function) — NAGS deficiency OMIM 237310"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Carbamoylphosphate synthetase 1 (CPS1) catalyzes the first committed and rate-limiting step of the urea cycle: NH3 + HCO3- + 2 ATP → carbamoyl phosphate + 2 ADP + Pi. CPS1 activity determines the rate of urea synthesis; urea is excreted by the kidney, and blood urea nitrogen (BUN) reflects the steady-state balance of urea production and renal excretion. CPS1 LoF abolishes carbamoyl phosphate production, blocking the entire urea cycle and markedly reducing BUN. Identical logic applies to NAGS LoF (NAGS makes the CPS1 activator N-acetylglutamate) and OTC LoF. do(CPS1 LoF) → urea cycle blocked → urea production ↓ → BUN ↓."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dmgdh_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Admgdh_activity%3Apositive%3Acellular_atp_level> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dmgdh_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "DMGDH deficiency OMIM 605850; Porter DH et al., J Biol Chem 1985 (DMGDH isolated from mitochondria; FAD reoxidation by ETF); PMC1275637 (AJHG 2005) — DMGDH deficiency with ~100-fold elevation of dimethylglycine; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (mitochondrial electron transport, ETF for fatty acid / amino acid oxidation). | intervention: do(DMGDH loss-of-function) — dimethylglycinuria / DMGDH deficiency OMIM 605850 [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Dimethylglycine dehydrogenase (DMGDH) is a mitochondrial FAD-dependent oxidoreductase that catalyzes the oxidative demethylation of N,N-dimethylglycine to sarcosine. The FADH2 generated is transferred to the mitochondrial electron-transfer flavoprotein (ETF), donating electrons to ETF-ubiquinone oxidoreductase and thence to ubiquinol-complex III of the respiratory chain, ultimately driving ATP synthesis. DMGDH LoF reduces this electron-donating flux, slightly lowering mitochondrial ATP production in liver cells where DMGDH is most active. do(DMGDH LoF) → FADH2 from DMG catabolism absent → reduced mitochondrial electron input → cellular ATP ↓ (slight)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_sah> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_albumin_synthesis> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_sah%3Anegative%3Ahepatic_albumin_synthesis> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_sah> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_albumin_synthesis> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Adenosylhomocysteinase deficiency / AHCY deficiency OMIM 180960; Stender S et al., N Engl J Med 2017 / Buist NRM et al., Am J Hum Genet 2006 (AHCY deficiency causes hepatic dysfunction including hypoalbuminemia); Clarke S & Banfield K, in: Carethers JM ed., OMMBID (SAH as a potent methyltransferase inhibitor, Ki competitive with SAM); Finkelstein JD, J Nutr Biochem 1990 (SAH inhibition of transmethylation reactions); Fowler B, Eur J Pediatr 1998 (clinical spectrum of AHCY deficiency: liver failure, hypoalbuminemia). | intervention: do(AHCY/SAH hydrolase loss-of-function) — adenosylhomocysteinase deficiency OMIM 180960"
    ; pmo:mechanism "S-adenosylhomocysteine (SAH) is a potent product inhibitor of all SAM-dependent methyltransferases (Ki ~0.5-1 µM, comparable to physiological concentrations); it competes with SAM at the methyl-donor binding site. Accumulation of SAH (as occurs in AHCY/SAH hydrolase deficiency) inhibits DNA methyltransferases and histone methyltransferases in hepatocytes, impairing transcriptional regulation of liver-specific genes including the albumin (ALB) gene. Clinical consequence: AHCY deficiency causes progressive liver disease including hypoalbuminemia. do(AHCY LoF) → SAH ↑ → global methyltransferase inhibition in hepatocytes → reduced albumin gene transcription → hepatic albumin synthesis ↓."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/slc6a19_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_tryptophan> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aslc6a19_activity%3Apositive%3Acsf_tryptophan> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/slc6a19_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_tryptophan> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Hartnup disease OMIM 234500 (SLC6A19 / B0AT1 LoF); Seow HF et al., Nat Genet 2004 (SLC6A19 mutations cause Hartnup disorder; intestinal neutral amino acid transport defect); Broer S et al., Biochem J 2008 (B0AT1/SLC6A19 is the major intestinal neutral amino acid transporter including tryptophan); Darras BT et al., in Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (Hartnup disease: pellagra-like symptoms from tryptophan/niacin deficiency; low plasma/CSF tryptophan); OMIM 234500 (clinical features include reduced plasma neutral amino acids). | intervention: do(SLC6A19 loss-of-function) — Hartnup disease OMIM 234500"
    ; pmo:mechanism "SLC6A19 (B0AT1) is the apical sodium-coupled neutral amino acid transporter in intestinal enterocytes and renal proximal tubule cells, responsible for the majority of dietary tryptophan absorption. Hartnup disease (SLC6A19 LoF) causes failure to absorb neutral amino acids including tryptophan in the intestine and failure to reabsorb them in the kidney; plasma tryptophan falls. Tryptophan crosses the blood-brain barrier by LAT1/SLC7A5, so reduced plasma tryptophan leads to reduced CSF tryptophan and downstream reduced serotonin synthesis. do(SLC6A19 LoF) → intestinal tryptophan malabsorption → plasma tryptophan ↓ → CSF tryptophan ↓."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_histidine> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_histamine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_histidine%3Apositive%3Aplasma_histamine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_histidine> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_histamine> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Histidinemia OMIM 235800 (HAL deficiency — elevated plasma histidine); Befus AD et al., in: Metzger H ed. Immunoglobulin E (mast cell histamine synthesis rate determined by histidine availability); Schayer RW, Physiol Rev 1959 (histidine is the sole precursor for all histamine in mammals; tissue histidine concentration influences histamine synthesis rate); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (histidine catabolism; HDC reaction). | intervention: do(HAL loss-of-function) — histidinemia OMIM 235800 [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Histamine is biosynthesized from L-histidine by the enzyme histidine decarboxylase (HDC; pyridoxal-5-phosphate-dependent decarboxylase) in mast cells, basophils, enterochromaffin-like cells, and neurons. Histidine is the sole substrate for HDC; the Km of HDC for histidine is in the physiological range (~2-5 mM), so elevated plasma histidine (as in histidinemia, HAL LoF) increases HDC substrate availability and drives more histamine synthesis. do(HAL LoF) → plasma histidine ↑ → HDC substrate ↑ → histamine production ↑ → plasma histamine ↑."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/haao_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_nadh> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahaao_activity%3Apositive%3Acellular_nadh> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/haao_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_nadh> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Shi H et al. NEJM 2017 (doi:10.1056/NEJMoa1616361) — biallelic HAAO loss-of-function causes congenital NAD deficiency disorder with congenital malformations rescued by niacin supplementation; patient plasma showed increased 3-HAA and decreased NAD/NADH. GeneReviews NBK593504 (CNDD): HAAO, KYNU, and NADSYN1 are the three CNDD-causing genes, all essential for de novo NAD synthesis from tryptophan. Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (kynurenine pathway → quinolinate → NaMN via QPRT → NAD+; HAAO catalyses the penultimate step). | intervention: do(HAAO biallelic LoF) = congenital NAD deficiency disorder; HAAO activity falls → NAD+ and NADH levels fall | intervention: do(HAAO biallelic LoF) — congenital NAD deficiency disorder (CNDD), OMIM via Shi NEJM 2017"
    ; pmo:mechanism "3-Hydroxyanthranilate 3,4-dioxygenase (HAAO) catalyses the committed step from 3-hydroxyanthranilic acid to alpha-amino-beta-carboxymuconate semialdehyde, which spontaneously cyclizes to quinolinate — the universal de novo precursor of the NAD+ pyridine ring. Biallelic HAAO LoF blocks de novo NAD+ biosynthesis from tryptophan (congenital NAD deficiency disorder, CNDD), reducing the total cellular NAD+ pool and consequently cellular NADH. Sign: more HAAO activity → more quinolinate flux → more NAD+ → more NADH (do(HAAO LoF) = NAD/NADH falls)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/p5c_dehydrogenase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aadc_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ap5c_dehydrogenase_activity%3Apositive%3Aaadc_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/p5c_dehydrogenase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aadc_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Farrant RD et al. J Biol Chem 2001 276:15108-15116 (PMID 11134058) — 'Pyridoxal phosphate de-activation by pyrroline-5-carboxylic acid: increased risk of vitamin B6 deficiency and seizures in hyperprolinemia type II'; mechanism confirmed as Knoevenagel condensation of P5C with PLP. PMC9492674 (Comm Biol 2022, Brolese et al.) — new biomarkers for hyperprolinemia type II confirm P5C accumulation and PLP trapping. Frontiers Psychiatry PMC6423912 — 'Intellectual disability associated with pyridoxine-responsive epilepsies': P5C-mediated PLP inactivation reduces AADC and other PLP-dependent enzyme activities. | intervention: do(ALDH4A1 biallelic LoF) = hyperprolinemia type II (OMIM 239500): P5C accumulates → PLP trapped → AADC activity reduced | intervention: do(ALDH4A1 biallelic LoF) — hyperprolinemia type II, OMIM 239500 [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Delta-1-pyrroline-5-carboxylate dehydrogenase (ALDH4A1) oxidises pyrroline-5-carboxylate (P5C) to glutamate. ALDH4A1 LoF (hyperprolinemia type II) causes P5C to accumulate. P5C spontaneously condenses with pyridoxal 5'-phosphate (PLP) in a Knoevenagel-type reaction, forming a stable aldamine adduct that inactivates PLP. Since aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (AADC/DDC) requires PLP as an essential cofactor, secondary functional B6 deficiency reduces AADC activity. Sign: more ALDH4A1 activity → less P5C → less PLP trapping → more AADC activity (do(ALDH4A1 LoF) = AADC activity falls)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldh7a1_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aadc_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aaldh7a1_activity%3Apositive%3Aaadc_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldh7a1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aadc_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "GeneReviews NBK1486 (Pyridoxine-Dependent Epilepsy, ALDH7A1): 'P6C inactivates PLP and leads to a lifelong hyperphysiologic pyridoxine demand'; AADC secondary deficiency is documented as a consequence. Mills PB et al. Biochem J 2006 — pyridoxine-dependent epilepsy (antiquitin/ALDH7A1 deficiency): P6C condensation with PLP, PLP-dependent enzyme (AADC, GABA-T) deficiency confirmed. OMIM 266100 (pyridoxine-dependent epilepsy). pdeonline.org/resources/PDE-review-MGM.pdf — mechanism of PLP trapping by P6C leading to secondary neurotransmitter synthesis defects. | intervention: do(ALDH7A1 biallelic LoF) = pyridoxine-dependent epilepsy, OMIM 266100: P6C accumulates → PLP trapped → AADC activity reduced | intervention: do(ALDH7A1 biallelic LoF) — pyridoxine-dependent epilepsy (PDE), OMIM 266100"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Alpha-aminoadipic semialdehyde dehydrogenase (ALDH7A1 / antiquitin) oxidises alpha-AASA to alpha-aminoadipate. ALDH7A1 LoF (pyridoxine-dependent epilepsy, PDE) causes alpha-AASA and its cyclic Schiff base piperideine-6-carboxylate (P6C) to accumulate. P6C condenses with pyridoxal 5'-phosphate (PLP) via a Knoevenagel adduction, forming a covalent P6C-PLP adduct that severely depletes bioavailable PLP. Aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (AADC/DDC) requires PLP as its obligatory cofactor; PLP depletion reduces AADC activity, causing secondary AADC deficiency with reduced synthesis of dopamine and serotonin. Sign: more ALDH7A1 → less P6C → less PLP trapping → more AADC activity (do(ALDH7A1 LoF) = AADC activity falls)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/kynureninase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_nadh> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Akynureninase_activity%3Apositive%3Acellular_nadh> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/kynureninase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_nadh> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Shi H et al. NEJM 2017 (doi:10.1056/NEJMoa1616361) — biallelic KYNU loss-of-function explicitly identified as a cause of congenital NAD deficiency disorder (CNDD) alongside HAAO and NADSYN1; 'affected patients had biallelic pathogenic variants in KYNU, HAAO, or NADSYN1'. GeneReviews NBK593504 (CNDD): KYNU is one of three CNDD genes. Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (kynurenine pathway: KYNU is obligate upstream of HAAO and QPRT in de novo NAD synthesis from tryptophan). | intervention: do(KYNU biallelic LoF) = congenital NAD deficiency disorder (CNDD): 3-HK not cleaved → no flux to quinolinate → NAD+/NADH falls | intervention: do(KYNU biallelic LoF) — congenital NAD deficiency disorder (CNDD) caused by kynureninase deficiency, Shi NEJM 2017"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Kynureninase (KYNU) catalyses the PLP-dependent cleavage of 3-hydroxykynurenine to 3-hydroxyanthranilate + alanine, a step required for flux through the tryptophan → kynurenine → 3-HK → 3-HAA → quinolinate (via HAAO) → NaMN (via QPRT) → NAD+ de novo biosynthesis pathway. KYNU LoF blocks this flux at 3-hydroxykynurenine, preventing generation of 3-hydroxyanthranilate and all downstream NAD+ precursors. The total cellular NAD+ pool (and therefore NADH) falls. Sign: more KYNU activity → more 3-HAA produced → more quinolinate → more NAD+/NADH (do(KYNU LoF) = NAD/NADH falls)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cystinosin_transport_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acystinosin_transport_activity%3Apositive%3Agfr> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cystinosin_transport_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Gahl WA et al. NEJM 2002 346:1456-1463 — nephropathic cystinosis: natural history of GFR decline; renal Fanconi syndrome is the earliest manifestation, progressing to ESRD. GeneReviews NBK1400 (cystinosis): 'Kidneys are initially affected with generalised proximal tubular dysfunction (renal Fanconi syndrome), then disease rapidly affects glomeruli and progresses towards end-stage renal failure'. PMC4374958 (Amer J Physiol Renal 2015) — endo-lysosomal dysfunction in cystinosis-deficient proximal tubular cells. OMIM 219800 (nephropathic cystinosis). | intervention: do(CTNS biallelic LoF) = nephropathic cystinosis, OMIM 219800: lysosomal cystine accumulates → Fanconi syndrome → progressive GFR loss | intervention: do(CTNS biallelic LoF) — nephropathic cystinosis, OMIM 219800"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Cystinosin (CTNS) is the H+-driven lysosomal cystine transporter that exports free cystine from lysosomes to the cytosol. CTNS LoF (nephropathic cystinosis) abolishes lysosomal cystine export, causing progressive intralysosomal cystine crystal accumulation in all cells, most critically in renal proximal tubular epithelial cells. Crystal accumulation causes oxidative stress, impaired autophagy, and tubular cell apoptosis, producing Fanconi syndrome (generalised proximal tubular dysfunction). Progressive tubular and glomerular injury then causes sustained GFR decline, with untreated patients reaching end-stage renal disease in the first two decades. Sign: more cystinosin activity → better lysosomal cystine clearance → preserved tubular function → higher GFR (do(CTNS LoF) = GFR falls)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/kynu_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_nadh> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Akynu_activity%3Apositive%3Acellular_nadh> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/kynu_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_nadh> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Shi H et al. NEJM 2017 (doi:10.1056/NEJMoa1616361) — biallelic KYNU LoF is one of three causes of congenital NAD deficiency disorder (CNDD); 'affected patients had biallelic pathogenic variants in KYNU, HAAO, or NADSYN1, all essential genes of NAD de novo synthesis'. GeneReviews NBK593504 (CNDD). OMIM 236800 (hydroxykynureninuria / KYNU deficiency). Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (kynurenine pathway to NAD+; KYNU step is obligate for flux from 3-HK to 3-HAA). | intervention: do(KYNU biallelic LoF) = hydroxykynureninuria / CNDD: 3-HK not cleaved → de novo NAD+ pathway blocked → cellular NADH reduced | intervention: do(KYNU biallelic LoF) — hydroxykynureninuria / congenital NAD deficiency disorder, OMIM 236800 / Shi NEJM 2017"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Kynureninase (KYNU, gene KYNU) catalyses the PLP-dependent hydrolysis of 3-hydroxykynurenine to 3-hydroxyanthranilate, a step required for de novo NAD+ biosynthesis from tryptophan via the kynurenine pathway. KYNU LoF blocks flux to 3-hydroxyanthranilate and all downstream de novo NAD+ precursors (quinolinate via HAAO → NaMN via QPRT → NAD+), reducing the total cellular NAD+ pool and hence cellular NADH. Sign: more KYNU activity → more 3-hydroxyanthranilate → more quinolinate → more de novo NAD+/NADH (do(KYNU biallelic LoF) = cellular NAD+/NADH falls). [Note: kynu_activity (PR:000009598) and kynureninase_activity (PR:Q16719) are two model nodes for the same KYNU enzyme encoded in separate fragments; the bridge mechanism is identical.]"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/suox_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asuox_activity%3Apositive%3Acellular_atp_level> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/suox_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "PMC5707142 (JCI 2017, Veldman et al.) — 'S-sulfocysteine/NMDA receptor-dependent signaling underlies neurodegeneration in molybdenum cofactor deficiency': SSC activates NMDA-R, causes dose-dependent depolarisation, increased intracellular Ca2+, calpain activation, and cell death; pharmacological NMDA blockade abolishes neurotoxicity. PMC7817995 (Frontiers Genetics 2021) — 'Sulfite alters the mitochondrial network in molybdenum cofactor deficiency': sulfite causes mitochondrial fragmentation and impairs oxidative phosphorylation, reducing cellular ATP. OMIM 272300 (isolated sulfite oxidase deficiency). Schwahn BC et al. JIMD 2024 consensus guidelines (doi:10.1002/jimd.12730). | intervention: do(SUOX biallelic LoF) = isolated sulfite oxidase deficiency, OMIM 272300: SSC accumulates → NMDA excitotoxicity → mitochondrial dysfunction → cellular ATP falls | intervention: do(SUOX biallelic LoF) — isolated sulfite oxidase deficiency, OMIM 272300"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Sulfite oxidase (SUOX) oxidises sulfite to sulfate, the terminal step of cysteine/methionine catabolism. SUOX LoF (isolated sulfite oxidase deficiency or molybdenum cofactor deficiency) causes sulfite to accumulate; sulfite reacts with cystine to produce S-sulfocysteine (SSC). SSC is a structural analogue of glutamate that acts as a potent NMDA receptor agonist, causing sustained calcium influx, activation of calpain and downstream apoptotic cascades, and mitochondrial network disruption. NMDA-driven excitotoxicity and mitochondrial dysfunction deplete intracellular ATP in neurons (Na+/K+-ATPase overload + impaired oxidative phosphorylation). Sign: more SUOX activity → less SSC → less NMDA excitotoxicity → higher cellular ATP (do(SUOX LoF) = SSC accumulates → cellular ATP falls)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/slc6a6_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cardiac_output> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aslc6a6_activity%3Apositive%3Acardiac_output> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/slc6a6_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cardiac_output> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "ScienceDirect 2008 (TauT/Slc6a6 KO mouse): 'taurine depletion caused by knocking out the taurine transporter gene leads to cardiomyopathy with cardiac atrophy'; OMIM 186854 (SLC6A6); Spandidos MMR 2020 (taurine transporter homeostasis review). | intervention: do(SLC6A6 loss-of-function) — hypotaurinemic retinal degeneration and cardiomyopathy: SLC6A6 activity DOWN -> systemic taurine depletion -> cardiomyocyte taurine deficiency -> dilated cardiomyopathy -> cardiac output DOWN [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "SLC6A6 (TauT) is the sodium- and chloride-dependent taurine transporter that maintains systemic taurine homeostasis by intestinal absorption and renal reabsorption. Taurine is the most abundant intracellular amino acid in cardiomyocytes and is required for Ca2+ handling, osmoprotection, and mitochondrial function in cardiac muscle. SLC6A6 deficiency depletes plasma and cardiomyocyte taurine to near-undetectable levels, causing dilated cardiomyopathy and reduced cardiac output. do(SLC6A6 LoF) -> systemic taurine depletion -> dilated cardiomyopathy -> cardiac output falls."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ggt1_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ggt> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aggt1_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_ggt> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ggt1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ggt> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Heisterkamp et al., European J Human Genetics 2018 / PMC5974402: patients with GGT1 homozygous deletion had 'markedly increased excretion of glutathione in urine and very low gamma-glutamyl transpeptidase activity in serum'; OMIM 231950 (glutathionuria) and OMIM 612346 (GGT1 gene); NORD gamma-glutamyl transpeptidase deficiency; PMC1852094 (gamma-glutamyl cycle, GGT1 role). | intervention: do(GGT1 loss-of-function) — gamma-glutamyl transpeptidase deficiency / glutathionuria OMIM 231950: GGT1 activity DOWN -> plasma GGT concentration/activity DOWN"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GGT1 (gamma-glutamyltransferase 1) is the dominant isoform of GGT expressed on the surface of hepatocytes and renal proximal tubular cells and shed into blood. Plasma GGT activity (the clinical serum GGT test) predominantly reflects GGT1 activity from liver and kidney. GGT1 deficiency (glutathionuria, OMIM 231950) causes markedly low serum GGT activity in all reported patients. do(GGT1 LoF) -> plasma GGT activity falls to near-undetectable levels."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta_galactosidase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alkaline_phosphatase> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abeta_galactosidase_activity%3Anegative%3Aalkaline_phosphatase> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta_galactosidase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alkaline_phosphatase> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "PMC11786200 (Persistent elevations of alkaline phosphatase as an early indicator of GM1 gangliosidosis; ALP significantly higher in infantile GM1 vs late-infantile/juvenile; associated with abnormal bone physiology in neonatal period); OMIM 230500 (GM1-gangliosidosis type I; GLB1 LoF; skeletal dysplasia); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, Lysosomal Storage Diseases (GM1 gangliosidosis: hepatosplenomegaly, skeletal dysplasia, early death). | intervention: GLB1 biallelic loss-of-function (GM1 gangliosidosis, OMIM 230500): do(beta_galactosidase_activity DOWN) -> GM1 ganglioside / keratan sulfate accumulation in bone -> osteoblast dysregulation -> elevated serum alkaline phosphatase [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GLB1 deficiency (GM1 gangliosidosis) causes lysosomal storage of GM1 ganglioside and keratan sulfate in bone cells, inducing osteoblast dysfunction and reactive periosteal bone remodeling; serum alkaline phosphatase rises as an early biomarker of this abnormal bone physiology. More beta-galactosidase activity prevents storage, reduces bone pathology, and keeps alkaline phosphatase at baseline."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_manganese> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_dopamine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_manganese%3Anegative%3Acsf_dopamine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_manganese> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_dopamine> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "PMC5799044 (Familial manganese-induced neurotoxicity due to mutations in SLC30A10 or SLC39A14; Mn accumulates in basal ganglia; dystonia); PMC5791243 (SLC39A14 mutations cause hypermanganesemia; CSF Mn 3-fold higher than plasma; brain Mn deposition); PNAS 2023 10.1073/pnas.2515785123 (Neuron-specific SLC30A10 KO identifies dopaminergic neurons as key targets of Mn-induced motor disease); PMC7677177 (Mn impacts dopamine neurotransmitter system; VMAT2 and DAT downregulation; Mn enters dopamine neurons via nifedipine-sensitive Ca2+ channels); OMIM 255800 (SLC30A10 LoF, HMNDYT1), OMIM 617013 (SLC39A14 LoF, HMNDYT2). | intervention: SLC30A10 or SLC39A14 loss-of-function causing hypermanganesemia (OMIM 255800 / OMIM 617013): do(plasma_manganese UP) -> CNS Mn accumulation -> dopaminergic neurotoxicity -> CSF dopamine DOWN | intervention: SLC30A10 or SLC39A14 loss-of-function hypermanganesemia (OMIM 255800 / OMIM 617013)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Elevated plasma manganese (as in SLC30A10 or SLC39A14 LoF hypermanganesemia) crosses the blood-brain barrier and accumulates in basal ganglia, with CSF manganese measured 3-fold higher than plasma in SLC39A14-mutant patients. Mn preferentially targets dopaminergic neurons of the substantia nigra and globus pallidus, downregulates dopamine transporters (VMAT2, DAT), and causes oxidative mitochondrial damage (BNIP3-mediated mitophagy), depleting dopamine synthesis and release. Clinical result is manganism (parkinsonian dystonia); CSF dopamine and HVA are reduced."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_cysteine> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aurinary_cysteine%3Anegative%3Agfr> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_cysteine> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "PMC3464901 (Cystinuria: an inborn cause of urolithiasis; complications include CKD and end-stage kidney disease); PMC4514483 (Update on cystinuria; recurrent stones and plugs of tubules result, with CKD a frequent complication); GeneReviews NBK619248 (Cystinuria; complications include recurrent UTI, CKD, hypertension, some individuals develop ESKD); OMIM 220100 (Cystinuria type A, SLC3A1 LoF). | intervention: SLC3A1 biallelic loss-of-function (cystinuria type A, OMIM 220100): do(SLC3A1 activity DOWN) -> urinary cystine UP -> cystine stone formation -> obstructive nephropathy -> GFR DOWN"
    ; pmo:mechanism "In cystinuria (SLC3A1 or SLC7A9 LoF), defective renal proximal-tubule cystine reabsorption leads to hyperexcretion of insoluble cystine in urine; cystine precipitates in the distal tubule and collecting system forming calculi. Recurrent cystine nephrolithiasis causes obstructive uropathy, tubular injury, recurrent UTI, and progressive chronic kidney disease, lowering GFR. Elevated urinary cystine thus negatively predicts GFR over time."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_glucose> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acsf_glucose%3Apositive%3Acellular_atp_level> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_glucose> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "PMC12002639 (Seizure and redox rescue in a model of glucose transport deficiency: GLUT1-DS seizures result from after-discharge neuronal firing caused by post-stimulus ATP reductions and impaired Na+/K+-ATPase); OMIM 606777 (GLUT1 deficiency syndrome, De Vivo disease; SLC2A1 LoF; hypoglycorrhachia); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (CSF glucose: normal 60-70% of plasma, reduced by GLUT1 deficiency). | intervention: SLC2A1 biallelic loss-of-function (GLUT1 deficiency syndrome, OMIM 606777): do(SLC2A1 activity DOWN) -> CSF glucose DOWN -> neuronal ATP DOWN -> seizures | intervention: SLC2A1 biallelic loss-of-function (GLUT1 deficiency syndrome, De Vivo disease, OMIM 606777) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CSF glucose is the primary metabolic substrate for neurons (transported via GLUT3 at the neuronal membrane). Low CSF glucose (hypoglycorrhachia) — as in GLUT1 deficiency syndrome — starves neurons of their principal energy source; experimental and clinical evidence shows that GLUT1-DS seizures result from post-stimulus ATP reductions and impaired Na+/K+-ATPase function. Higher CSF glucose increases neuronal glycolytic flux and oxidative phosphorylation, raising cellular ATP concentration."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mcc_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amcc_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_glucose> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mcc_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "OMIM 210200 (3-methylcrotonyl-CoA carboxylase 1 deficiency, MCC1D; clinical features include acute hypoglycemia, metabolic acidosis, secondary carnitine deficiency); MedlinePlus Genetics / JCI PMC199271 (MCC deficiency: very low plasma free carnitine due to 3-hydroxyisovaleryl-carnitine conjugation; very low plasma glucose is a presenting feature); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (leucine catabolism: 3-methylcrotonyl-CoA -> HMG-CoA -> acetyl-CoA + acetoacetate; carnitine-dependent FAO supports gluconeogenic substrates and ketogenesis during fasting). | intervention: MCC biallelic loss-of-function (MCC1D, OMIM 210200): do(MCC activity DOWN) -> leucine catabolism blocked -> 3-methylcrotonyl-carnitine accumulates -> carnitine deficiency -> impaired FAO -> fasting hypoglycemia -> plasma glucose DOWN | intervention: MCC biallelic loss-of-function (3-MCC deficiency, OMIM 210200)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "3-Methylcrotonyl-CoA carboxylase (MCC) catalyzes the fourth step of leucine catabolism (3-methylcrotonyl-CoA -> 3-methylglutaconyl-CoA). MCC deficiency causes 3-methylcrotonyl-CoA to accumulate and conjugate with carnitine, depleting the free carnitine pool; secondary carnitine deficiency impairs mitochondrial fatty acid beta-oxidation, removing the principal fasting fuel source and producing hypoketotic hypoglycaemia. More MCC activity preserves carnitine homeostasis, supports FAO during fasting, and maintains plasma glucose."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mttp_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/vldl_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amttp_activity%3Apositive%3Avldl_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mttp_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/vldl_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Sharp D et al. Nature 1993 (MTTP mutations cause abetalipoproteinemia); OMIM 200100 (abetalipoproteinemia; absence of apoB-containing lipoproteins; VLDL absent); JCI 1999 PMC199271 (hepatic Mttp KO in mice: striking reduction in VLDL triglycerides; confirms MTTP as rate-limiting for hepatic VLDL secretion); Circulation Research 2016 (Contemporary aspects of MTP biology: MTP adds TG to nascent chylomicrons and VLDL; MTP inhibition reduces VLDL secretion); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, Disorders of Lipoprotein Metabolism (abetalipoproteinemia: MTTP LoF -> complete absence of VLDL and LDL). | intervention: MTTP biallelic loss-of-function (abetalipoproteinemia, OMIM 200100): do(MTTP activity DOWN) -> VLDL assembly fails -> vldl_secretion DOWN"
    ; pmo:mechanism "MTTP (microsomal triglyceride transfer protein) transfers triglycerides, cholesteryl esters, and phospholipids onto nascent apoB-100 in the ER lumen, enabling VLDL particle assembly and secretion by hepatocytes. Without MTTP, apoB-100 is co-translationally ubiquitinated and degraded; no VLDL (or chylomicrons) are secreted. In abetalipoproteinemia (MTTP LoF, OMIM 200100), hepatic VLDL secretion is essentially zero. More MTTP activity directly enables more VLDL assembly and secretion."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatocyte_apoe_secretion> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_triglycerides> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahepatocyte_apoe_secretion%3Anegative%3Aplasma_triglycerides> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatocyte_apoe_secretion> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_triglycerides> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "JLR 1999 (ApoE participates in regulation of VLDL-TG secretion and clearance; apoE-deficient hepatocytes show 46% reduced VLDL-TG production AND reduced remnant clearance); Dysbetalipoproteinemia NBK567738 / OMIM 617347 (APOE2/2: impaired LDLR/LRP1 binding -> elevated plasma TG; type III HLP characterized by VLDL and IDL accumulation); Mahley RW & Rall SC, Annu Rev Genomics Hum Genet 2000 (ApoE: lipid transport and receptor-mediated clearance; apoE deficiency causes hypertriglyceridemia). | intervention: APOE null / APOE2 homozygosity (type III hyperlipoproteinemia, OMIM 617347): do(hepatocyte_apoe_secretion DOWN) -> impaired VLDL/IDL remnant clearance -> plasma_triglycerides UP | intervention: APOE null or APOE2/2 homozygosity (familial dysbetalipoproteinemia, OMIM 617347) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ApoE on VLDL and IDL remnants is the ligand recognized by hepatic LDL receptor (LDLR) and LRP1 for receptor-mediated endocytosis and clearance. More hepatic ApoE secretion results in better opsonization of circulating VLDL remnants, accelerating their hepatic clearance and lowering plasma triglycerides. ApoE-deficient mice show markedly elevated VLDL and IDL; APOE2/2 homozygotes (type III hyperlipoproteinemia / familial dysbetalipoproteinemia, OMIM 617347) have elevated plasma triglycerides due to impaired remnant clearance."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/abcg5_abcg8_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatocyte_cholesterol_content> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aabcg5_abcg8_activity%3Anegative%3Ahepatocyte_cholesterol_content> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/abcg5_abcg8_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatocyte_cholesterol_content> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Berge KE et al. Science 2000;290:1771-1775 (ABCG5/ABCG8 mutations cause sitosterolemia; intestinal hyperabsorption and impaired biliary excretion of sterols); PMC6143779 (Sitosterolemia, hypercholesterolemia, and coronary artery disease; elevated LDL cholesterol is major atherogenic driver in sitosterolemia; ABCG8 canalicular efflux essential for biliary cholesterol secretion); PMC7961684 (ABCG5/ABCG8: twenty years of discovery; intestinal + hepatic sterol efflux; LoF -> sterol accumulation in enterocytes and hepatocytes); OMIM 210250 (sitosterolemia 1; ABCG5 LoF). | intervention: ABCG5/ABCG8 biallelic loss-of-function (sitosterolemia 1, OMIM 210250): do(abcg5_abcg8_activity DOWN) -> biliary cholesterol/sterol efflux fails -> hepatocyte_cholesterol_content UP"
    ; pmo:mechanism "The ABCG5/ABCG8 heterodimer at the hepatocyte canalicular membrane is the major transporter pumping free cholesterol (and plant sterols) from hepatocytes into bile for elimination. Higher ABCG5/ABCG8 activity increases biliary cholesterol secretion, reducing intrahepatic cholesterol content. In sitosterolemia (ABCG5 or ABCG8 LoF, OMIM 210250), biliary cholesterol and sterol efflux fails; hepatocyte cholesterol and sitosterol content rise, driving feedback inhibition of LDL receptor expression and contributing to hypercholesterolaemia."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cholesterol_sulfate> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_cholesterol_synthesis> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_cholesterol_sulfate%3Anegative%3Ahepatic_cholesterol_synthesis> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cholesterol_sulfate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_cholesterol_synthesis> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "PMC12182261 / ScienceDirect S1016847825000330 (Cholesterol sulfate as a negative regulator of cellular cholesterol homeostasis: CS promotes proteasomal degradation of HMGCR via INSIG-mediated ubiquitination; suppresses SREBP2 activation; reduces LDL receptor endocytosis — a multifaceted negative feedback on cholesterol synthesis and uptake); OMIM 308100 (X-linked ichthyosis; STS LoF; elevated plasma cholesterol sulfate); Hernandez-Martin & Torrelo, Am J Clin Dermatol 2006 (XLI: STS deficiency causes CS accumulation in plasma and stratum corneum). | intervention: STS loss-of-function (X-linked ichthyosis, OMIM 308100): do(STS activity DOWN) -> plasma_cholesterol_sulfate UP -> HMGCR degradation enhanced -> hepatic_cholesterol_synthesis DOWN"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Cholesterol sulfate (CS) acts as a negative feedback regulator of hepatic cholesterol homeostasis: CS promotes INSIG-mediated ubiquitination and proteasomal degradation of HMG-CoA reductase (HMGCR), suppresses proteolytic activation of SREBP2 (the master transcription factor for cholesterol synthesis and uptake), and blocks LDL receptor endocytosis. Elevated plasma CS (as in STS/XLI deficiency, where CS accumulates because it cannot be hydrolyzed back to cholesterol) therefore feedbacks to suppress hepatic de novo cholesterol synthesis."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/elovl6_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_lipogenesis> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aelovl6_activity%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_lipogenesis> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/elovl6_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_lipogenesis> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Matsuzaka et al. (2002) J Lipid Res 43:911-920 (ELOVL6 mRNA co-induced with FAS/SCD1 by insulin/SREBP-1c); Nobuyuki et al. PMC4242452 — Elovl6 KO blocks stearate/oleate synthesis; Ohno et al. PMC7790921 (Role of fatty acid elongase Elovl6 in energy metabolism); Wakana et al. 2015 (de novo lipogenesis reduction in ELOVL6 KO liver). Inborn error of fatty acid elongation (ELOVL6 LoF) markedly reduces elongation of palmitate to stearate and the overall de novo lipogenic product profile. | intervention: genetic LoF of ELOVL6 (elongase deficiency): hepatic de novo lipogenesis rate falls; ELOVL6 overexpression: lipogenesis increases"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ELOVL6 is the rate-limiting elongase in hepatic de novo lipogenesis, catalysing the SREBP-1c-regulated chain elongation of palmitate (C16:0) to stearate (C18:0). As a lipogenic enzyme co-regulated with FAS and SCD1, ELOVL6 activity is a direct determinant of the de novo lipogenic flux; ELOVL6 knockout reduces hepatic C18 fatty acid production and blunts de novo lipogenesis."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_l_2_hydroxyglutarate> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alpha_ketoglutarate_dehydrogenase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acsf_l_2_hydroxyglutarate%3Anegative%3Aalpha_ketoglutarate_dehydrogenase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_l_2_hydroxyglutarate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alpha_ketoglutarate_dehydrogenase_activity> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "L-2-hydroxyglutaric aciduria (OMIM 236792, L2HGDH gene); Achouri et al. (2004) Biochem J — L-2-HG is a competitive inhibitor of alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase; L2hgdh Deficiency Accumulates l-2-Hydroxyglutarate with Progressive Leukoencephalopathy PMC4357467; Struys (2006) JIMD — elevated CSF L-2-HG; L-2-HG inhibits multiple alpha-KG-dependent dioxygenases and the alpha-KGDH complex. | intervention: genetic LoF of L2HGDH: CSF L-2-hydroxyglutarate rises; competitively inhibits mitochondrial alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase -> alpha-KGDH activity decreases"
    ; pmo:mechanism "L-2-hydroxyglutarate (L-2-HG) is a competitive inhibitor of alpha-ketoglutarate-dependent enzymes including the mitochondrial alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase complex (alpha-KGDH). L2HGDH LoF causes L-2-HG to accumulate in CSF, plasma, and urine; accumulated L-2-HG inhibits alpha-KGDH, impairing the TCA cycle at the 2-oxoglutarate step, reducing mitochondrial energy production and driving neurodegeneration."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hoga1_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahoga1_activity%3Apositive%3Agfr> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hoga1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Primary Hyperoxaluria Type 3 — GeneReviews NBK316514; HOGA1 gene MedlinePlus Genetics; Cellular degradation of HOGA1 leads to absolute deficiency in PH3; 4-Hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate Aldolase Inactivity in PH3 PMC3418427; PH3 cohort study PMC11753311 (childhood nephrolithiasis, risk of renal impairment). Disease: primary hyperoxaluria type 3 (OMIM 613616). | intervention: genetic LoF of HOGA1: urinary oxalate rises -> calcium oxalate nephro­calcinosis -> GFR decreases; HOGA1 activity +(+)-> GFR [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "HOGA1 (4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate aldolase) is a mitochondrial enzyme in the hydroxyproline degradation pathway; HOGA1 LoF causes primary hyperoxaluria type 3 (PH3), characterised by excess urinary oxalate excretion. Calcium oxalate crystals deposit in renal tubules (nephrocalcinosis) and form urolithiasis, progressively reducing glomerular filtration rate (GFR). Higher HOGA1 activity prevents oxalate overproduction and protects GFR."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_dihydrotestosterone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_androgen> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_dihydrotestosterone%3Apositive%3Aplasma_androgen> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_dihydrotestosterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_androgen> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "5-alpha-reductase type 2 deficiency (OMIM 264600) — DHT is markedly low; StatPearls NBK539904; Integrative review of SRD5A2 deficiency PMC7167369; Structure of SRD5A2 with finasteride PMC7591894 (DHT affinity for androgen receptor 2-5x testosterone); MedlinePlus Genetics SRD5A2 gene. SRD5A2 pharmacological inhibitors (finasteride) lower plasma DHT and total androgenic load. | intervention: SRD5A2 LoF: plasma DHT falls -> plasma androgen concentration decreases; SRD5A2 gain of function (BPH/prostate cancer context): DHT rises -> plasma androgen increases"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Dihydrotestosterone (DHT) is the most potent endogenous androgen (androgen-receptor affinity ~5x testosterone), contributing directly to circulating plasma androgen concentration. SRD5A2 LoF (5-alpha-reductase type 2 deficiency) markedly reduces plasma DHT while plasma testosterone rises; DHT is quantitatively a component of total plasma androgen concentration."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cyp11b1_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/zona_fasciculata_cortisol_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acyp11b1_activity%3Apositive%3Azona_fasciculata_cortisol_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cyp11b1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/zona_fasciculata_cortisol_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Congenital adrenal hyperplasia due to 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency (OMIM 202010); MedlinePlus CYP11B1; Clinical, genetic, structural basis of CAH PMC5347606; CYP11B1 mutations and functional consequences PMC3992560; Classic adrenal steroidogenesis PMC9991025. CYP11B1 encodes the enzyme that performs the 11-beta hydroxylation converting 11-deoxycortisol to cortisol in zona fasciculata. | intervention: CYP11B1 LoF (CAH due to 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency): cortisol secretion from zona fasciculata falls; ACTH stimulation increases CYP11B1 activity -> cortisol secretion increases"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CYP11B1 (steroid 11-beta-hydroxylase) catalyses the terminal step in cortisol biosynthesis in adrenal zona fasciculata: converting 11-deoxycortisol to cortisol. CYP11B1 activity is the immediate and direct determinant of cortisol output from zona fasciculata cells; LoF (CAH type due to 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency) abolishes cortisol production from this step."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cyp11b2_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/zona_glomerulosa_cell_aldosterone_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acyp11b2_activity%3Apositive%3Azona_glomerulosa_cell_aldosterone_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cyp11b2_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/zona_glomerulosa_cell_aldosterone_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "CYP11B2 aldosterone synthase deficiency (isolated hypoaldosteronism, OMIM 203400); Aldosterone synthase Wikipedia; PMC3976226 — clinical significance of CYP11B2 deficiency (salt-wasting, hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia, low aldosterone); PNAS 1992 — CYP11B2 mutations causing corticosterone methyloxidase II deficiency; PMC10118833 (congenital hyperreninemic hypoaldosteronism). CYP11B2 activity directly produces aldosterone in zona glomerulosa. | intervention: CYP11B2 LoF (aldosterone synthase deficiency): aldosterone secretion from zona glomerulosa falls -> salt wasting; angiotensin II / potassium stimulate CYP11B2 -> aldosterone secretion increases"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CYP11B2 (aldosterone synthase) is expressed exclusively in adrenal zona glomerulosa and catalyses all three terminal steps of aldosterone biosynthesis: 11-beta-hydroxylation of DOC to corticosterone, 18-hydroxylation to 18-hydroxycorticosterone, and 18-methyl oxidation to aldosterone. CYP11B2 activity is the sole determinant of zona glomerulosa aldosterone output; LoF causes isolated aldosterone synthase deficiency with salt-wasting."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/slc26a3_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_chloride> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aslc26a3_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_chloride> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/slc26a3_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_chloride> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Congenital chloride diarrhoea (OMIM 214700); SLC26A3 (DRA, CLD gene) — novel therapeutic target PMC12003007; slc26a3-deficient mice display chloride-losing diarrhea PMID 17001077; Update on SLC26A3 mutations in CLD (Wedenoja 2011, Hum Mutat); Frontiers Dysnatremia in GI disorders (hypochloraemia + hyponatraemia secondary to fecal chloride loss in CLD). | intervention: SLC26A3 LoF (congenital chloride diarrhoea): intestinal Cl absorption abolished -> stool chloride rises -> plasma chloride falls; SLC26A3 activity +(+)-> plasma chloride [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "SLC26A3 (DRA, Down-Regulated in Adenoma) is the apical Cl-/HCO3- exchanger in ileal and colonic enterocytes responsible for active chloride absorption. SLC26A3 LoF (congenital chloride diarrhoea, CLD) causes failure to absorb luminal chloride, massive fecal chloride loss (stool Cl > 90 mmol/L), and consequent hypochloraemia, hyponatraemia, and metabolic alkalosis. Higher SLC26A3 activity = more intestinal Cl absorption = higher plasma chloride."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_pyrimidine_nucleotide_pool> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_unconjugated_bilirubin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aerythrocyte_pyrimidine_nucleotide_pool%3Apositive%3Aplasma_unconjugated_bilirubin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_pyrimidine_nucleotide_pool> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_unconjugated_bilirubin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Hemolytic anemia due to pyrimidine 5' nucleotidase deficiency (OMIM 266120, NT5C3A gene); PMC3878480 (About P5N deficiency — jaundice/neonatal hyperbilirubinaemia listed as clinical feature); Blood ASH 2005 PMC: functional analysis of NT5C3 mutants — haemolytic anaemia; Orphanet 35120 (clinical features include jaundice). Mechanism: pyrimidine nucleotide accumulation -> basophilic stippling, haemolysis -> bilirubin release -> unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia. | intervention: NT5C3A LoF: erythrocyte pyrimidine nucleotide pool rises -> haemolytic anaemia -> plasma unconjugated bilirubin increases"
    ; pmo:mechanism "NT5C3A (pyrimidine 5'-nucleotidase 1) deficiency causes accumulation of pyrimidine nucleotides (UMP, CMP) in erythrocytes. Elevated erythrocyte pyrimidine nucleotides impair red cell energy metabolism, leading to chronic haemolytic anaemia. RBC haemolysis releases haemoglobin that is catabolised to unconjugated bilirubin (indirect bilirubin), causing jaundice/hyperbilirubinaemia. Elevated erythrocyte pyrimidine nucleotide pool -> haemolysis -> ↑plasma unconjugated bilirubin."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_itp> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/myocardial_contractility> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aerythrocyte_itp%3Anegative%3Amyocardial_contractility> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_itp> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/myocardial_contractility> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "ITPase deficiency causes a Martsolf-like syndrome with lethal infantile dilated cardiomyopathy (Mayr et al., PLOS Genetics 2019, PMID:30856165; PMC:PMC6428344). Cardiac involvement in 28.6% of patients with ITPA deficiency; rI misincorporation in RNA correlates with organ dysfunction severity, highest in embryonic heart. | intervention: do(ITPA loss-of-function): complete ITPase deficiency → ITP accumulates → rI misincorporated into cardiac RNA → cardiomyocyte dysfunction → reduced contractility [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ITPA deficiency → erythrocyte (and tissue) ITP accumulation → inosine (rI) misincorporation into cardiac RNA → proteotoxic RNA dysfunction and mitochondrial damage → dilated cardiomyopathy → reduced myocardial contractility; highest rI accumulation seen in embryonic heart (Martsolf-like syndrome, PMID:30856165)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/muscle_coenzyme_q10> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_complex_iii_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amuscle_coenzyme_q10%3Apositive%3Amitochondrial_complex_iii_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/muscle_coenzyme_q10> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_complex_iii_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "COQ2 mutations cause primary CoQ10 deficiency; CoQ10-deficient patients show decreased quinone-dependent Complex II+III and G3P+III activities (PMC:PMC4705639; Acosta et al., Biol Res 2016). CoQ10 deficiency → decreased electron transport → reduced mitochondrial respiratory chain activity (PMC:PMC4112527). Oral CoQ10 supplementation restores Complex III activity in COQ2 patients. | intervention: do(COQ2 loss-of-function): impaired 4-hydroxybenzoate polyprenylation → reduced CoQ10 biosynthesis → decreased muscle CoQ10 → reduced Complex III substrate availability → decreased Complex III activity"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Coenzyme Q10 (ubiquinone) is the obligate electron shuttle between Complex I/II and Complex III in the mitochondrial respiratory chain; COQ2 deficiency → reduced CoQ10 biosynthesis → reduced muscle CoQ10 → insufficient substrate for Complex III ubiquinol:cytochrome c oxidoreductase reaction → decreased Complex III activity and impaired oxidative phosphorylation"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_oleylethanolamide> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/food_intake> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_oleylethanolamide%3Anegative%3Afood_intake> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_oleylethanolamide> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/food_intake> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OEA levels increase upon refeeding and decrease during fasting in the rodent small intestine, indicating endogenous satiety role (PMC:PMC1764767). OEA inhibits eating via PPAR-alpha and vagal sensory fibers; systematic review confirms OEA reduces food intake (Tutunchi et al., Clin Exp Pharmacol Physiol 2020). The lipid messenger OEA links dietary fat intake to satiety (PMC:PMC2572640). FAAH deficiency promotes energy storage and enhanced food motivation (PMID:20029375). | intervention: do(FAAH loss-of-function / exogenous OEA elevation): plasma OEA increases → PPAR-alpha activation in intestinal mucosa → satiety signal to hypothalamus → reduced food intake [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Oleoylethanolamide (OEA) is an endogenous PPAR-alpha agonist and satiety signal produced in the proximal small intestine after fat ingestion; OEA activates PPAR-alpha → stimulates CCK release and vagal afferent signaling → activates hypothalamic satiety pathways (PVN) → reduces meal size and food intake; FAAH deficiency → elevated OEA → reduced food intake (though AEA dominates in FAAH-KO due to opposing effects)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_motilin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_tract_motility> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_motilin%3Apositive%3Agi_tract_motility> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_motilin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_tract_motility> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Motilin stimulates gastric and small-intestine motility, initiating the interdigestive MMC (StatPearls NBK545309; Ohno et al., Int J Peptides 2010, PMC:PMC2925306). Plasma motilin peaks are synchronized to strong peristaltic contractions in the stomach and duodenum. Motilin releases 5-HT and ACh from enteric neurons, amplifying contractile response (PMC:PMC8419268). Erythromycin mimics motilin (prokinetic mechanism). | intervention: do(elevated plasma motilin / motilin-secreting cell activation): plasma motilin rises → motilin receptor activation on GI smooth muscle → smooth-muscle contraction → increased GI tract motility (MMC initiation)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Motilin, a 22-amino-acid peptide secreted cyclically by duodenal Mo cells every 90-120 minutes during fasting, binds motilin receptors (GPCRs) on GI smooth muscle → activates phospholipase C → inositol trisphosphate → smooth-muscle contraction → initiates migrating motor complex (MMC) → increases GI tract peristaltic activity"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_tubular_ammonia_secretion> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_ph> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Arenal_tubular_ammonia_secretion%3Apositive%3Aarterial_ph> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_tubular_ammonia_secretion> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_ph> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Renal ammoniagenesis from glutamine is the primary component of net acid excretion and the major mechanism of acid-base homeostasis (PMC:PMC3041479; PMC:PMC4313553). Complete metabolism of each glutamine generates 2 NH4+ and 2 HCO3- (eClinpath renal ammonia physiology). Glutaminase (GLS) is induced 2-fold by metabolic acidosis, driving compensatory NH4+ excretion to restore arterial pH. Proximal tubule-specific glutamine synthetase deletion alters acidosis-stimulated ammonia metabolism (PMID:27009341). | intervention: do(increased renal_glutaminase_activity): increased glutamine catabolism → more NH4+ secreted into filtrate → more urinary ammonium excreted → more new HCO3- regenerated to blood → increased arterial pH [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Renal proximal tubule glutaminase catabolizes glutamine → glutamate + NH4+; complete glutamine catabolism generates 2 NH4+ and 2 HCO3-; NH4+ is secreted into the tubular lumen (apical NHE3) and ultimately excreted as urinary ammonium; each mole of excreted NH4+ represents one mole of newly generated bicarbonate returning to blood → raises plasma [HCO3-] → raises arterial pH (net acid excretion pathway)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intestinal_lipid_emulsification> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_free_fatty_acids> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aintestinal_lipid_emulsification%3Apositive%3Aplasma_free_fatty_acids> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intestinal_lipid_emulsification> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_free_fatty_acids> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Tso P & Balint JA 1986 Am J Physiol 250:G715-G726 — bile-salt emulsification is rate-limiting for pancreatic lipase action and FFA absorption; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §23.7 (Accessory Organs: Liver, Pancreas, Gallbladder): 'Bile acts as an emulsifier to break large fat globules into smaller fat globules'; Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology 3e Ch 45: fat emulsification → micellar solubilisation → FFA absorption into enterocytes → chylomicron assembly → plasma FFA. | intervention: do(intestinal_lipid_emulsification +) — e.g., exogenous bile salt replacement in cholestatic disease or bile-acid sequestrant withdrawal; do(intestinal_lipid_emulsification -) — biliary obstruction or bile-salt deficiency causes fat malabsorption and reduced plasma FFA."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Bile-salt-mediated emulsification of dietary triglycerides in the small intestinal lumen is required for pancreatic lipase access and micelle formation; greater emulsification increases the rate of fat hydrolysis and subsequent absorption of free fatty acids into the portal circulation, raising circulating FFA. In abetalipoproteinaemia (MTP deficiency) and Wolman disease (LIPA deficiency), impaired fat absorption despite adequate luminal lipolysis confirms the causal path from luminal emulsification/hydrolysis to circulating FFA."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_contraction_rate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_lactate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Askeletal_muscle_contraction_rate%3Apositive%3Aplasma_lactate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_contraction_rate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_lactate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Brooks GA 1986 J Appl Physiol 60:1078-1086 — lactate production proportional to exercise intensity / muscle contraction rate; McArdle disease (OMIM 232600): ischaemic forearm exercise test shows flat lactate curve confirming muscle glycolysis → plasma lactate; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §10.3 (Muscle Fiber Contraction and Relaxation): anaerobic glycolysis produces lactic acid when O2 supply is insufficient to meet ATP demand. | intervention: do(skeletal_muscle_contraction_rate +) — exercise or electrically-stimulated muscle contraction raises plasma lactate; do(skeletal_muscle_contraction_rate -) — rest or neuromuscular blockade reduces plasma lactate to baseline."
    ; pmo:mechanism "During high-intensity skeletal muscle contraction, pyruvate production from glycolysis exceeds the capacity of pyruvate dehydrogenase and the TCA cycle; lactate dehydrogenase (LDHA) reduces pyruvate to lactate, which is exported via MCT1/MCT4 monocarboxylate transporters into the circulation, raising plasma lactate. In McArdle disease (PYGM deficiency) the exercise-induced lactate rise is abolished, confirming the causal link from muscle contraction rate to plasma lactate via glycolysis."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/duodenal_lipase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_free_fatty_acids> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aduodenal_lipase_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_free_fatty_acids> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/duodenal_lipase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_free_fatty_acids> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lowe ME 2002 J Lipid Res 43:2007-2016 — pancreatic lipase is the principal enzyme for dietary fat digestion; OMIM 614337 (pancreatic lipase deficiency): severe steatorrhea, fat-soluble vitamin deficiency, low plasma FFA postprandially; Jakubowski & Flatt Fundamentals of Biochemistry Vol I §lipid digestion: pancreatic lipase + colipase hydrolyse TG → FFA + 2-MAG → absorbed → chylomicron → plasma FFA. | intervention: do(duodenal_lipase_activity -) — orlistat (gastric/pancreatic lipase inhibitor) blocks fat digestion, reducing plasma postprandial FFA; do(duodenal_lipase_activity +) — exogenous pancrelipase (pancreatic enzyme replacement) restores fat absorption and plasma FFA in exocrine pancreatic insufficiency."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Pancreatic lipase (PNLIP) hydrolyses dietary triglycerides at the sn-1 and sn-3 positions in the duodenal lumen, releasing 2-monoacylglycerol and free fatty acids. These are incorporated into mixed micelles and absorbed across the enterocyte brush border; FFA are re-esterified into triglycerides, assembled into chylomicrons, and secreted into lymph and then blood, raising plasma FFA. Congenital pancreatic lipase deficiency (OMIM 614337) causes severe fat malabsorption and reduced postprandial plasma FFA, confirming the causal link."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/monoamine_oxidase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_dopamine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amonoamine_oxidase_activity%3Anegative%3Acsf_dopamine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/monoamine_oxidase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_dopamine> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Robinson DS 1975 Fed Proc 34:103: MAO activity inversely correlates with CSF dopamine metabolites across age; Fowler JS et al 1997 PNAS 94:4938 — MAO-B inhibition raises brain dopamine levels. | intervention: do(monoamine_oxidase_activity -) — selegiline/rasagiline MAO-B inhibition raises synaptic and CSF dopamine; do(monoamine_oxidase_activity +) — no direct pharmacological up-regulation, but loss-of-function MAO mutations (Brunner syndrome, OMIM 300615) markedly elevate dopamine and CSF dopamine. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Monoamine oxidase (MAO-A and MAO-B) catalyses the oxidative deamination of dopamine in presynaptic neurons and astroglia to 3,4-dihydroxyphenylacetaldehyde, which is further oxidised to DOPAC and then HVA. Greater MAO activity accelerates dopamine catabolism, reducing intra-neuronal and synaptic dopamine concentrations and consequently CSF dopamine levels. MAO inhibition (by selegiline or phenelzine) raises CSF dopamine, confirming the inverse causal relationship."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cd4_t_cell_count> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/t_cell_immune_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acd4_t_cell_count%3Apositive%3At_cell_immune_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cd4_t_cell_count> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/t_cell_immune_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Fauci AS et al 1996 Ann Intern Med 124:654 — CD4 count is the primary surrogate marker for T cell immune activity in HIV disease; Glare EM & Quinn KM 2016: CD4+ count < 200 cells/μL defines AIDS by immunological collapse of T cell-mediated immunity; Mosmann TR & Coffman RL 1989 Ann Rev Immunol 7:145 — CD4+ Th cells are obligate activators of both cytotoxic T cell and humoral arms; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §21.3 (Adaptive Immune Response): 'helper T cells serve as the master regulators of the adaptive immune response'. | intervention: do(cd4_t_cell_count -) — HIV infection depletes CD4+ T cells, proportionally collapsing T cell immune activity; do(cd4_t_cell_count +) — antiretroviral therapy restores CD4+ count and T cell immune activity."
    ; pmo:mechanism "CD4+ helper T cells are the master coordinators of adaptive immune responses: they provide cytokine help (IL-2, IFN-γ, IL-4) to cytotoxic T cells, B cells, and macrophages, and are required for effective T cell-mediated immune activity. When CD4+ T cell count falls (as in HIV/AIDS), T cell-mediated immune activity collapses, leading to opportunistic infections. The causal direction is: CD4+ T cell number sets the ceiling on T cell immune activity."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/platelet_count> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/platelet_activation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplatelet_count%3Apositive%3Aplatelet_activation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/platelet_count> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/platelet_activation> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Hanson SR & Slichter SJ 1985 Blood 66:1105 — clinical thrombocytopenia (aplasia / ITP) reduces platelet activation and procoagulant activity proportionally; Italiano JE & Hartwig JA 2013 Platelets Ch 2 — circulating platelet count determines the haemostatic response to vessel injury. | intervention: do(platelet_count -) — chemotherapy-induced thrombocytopenia or ITP reduces platelet activation and bleeding risk; do(platelet_count +) — thrombopoietin (romiplostim) treatment raises platelet count and restores haemostatic platelet activation. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "The number of circulating platelets sets the upper limit on the pool of platelets available for activation at sites of vascular injury. Under a fixed stimulus (thrombin, collagen, ADP), more platelets in the blood produce more activated platelets contributing to the procoagulant surface. Severe thrombocytopenia (platelet count <50,000/μL) markedly reduces haemostatic platelet plug formation, confirming that platelet count causally constrains activated platelet output."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/helper_t_cell_activation> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/t_cell_immune_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahelper_t_cell_activation%3Apositive%3At_cell_immune_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/helper_t_cell_activation> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/t_cell_immune_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Mosmann TR & Coffman RL 1989 Ann Rev Immunol 7:145-173 — CD4+ helper T cells provide obligatory activating signals for cytotoxic T cell responses; Reith W et al 2005 J Exp Med 202:1183 — MHC class II deficiency (bare lymphocyte syndrome): absent helper T cell activation collapses T cell-mediated immunity (OMIM 209920); OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §21.3: 'helper T cells serve as the master regulators of the adaptive immune response' — activation of helper T cells is the gating event for T cell immune activity. | intervention: do(helper_t_cell_activation -) — MHC class II deficiency / CIITA mutation abolishes helper T cell activation and T cell immune activity; do(helper_t_cell_activation +) — checkpoint inhibitor (anti-PD-1) releases helper T cell inhibition, amplifying T cell immune activity."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Activated CD4+ helper T cells (Th1, Th2, Tfh) are the central coordinators of T cell-mediated adaptive immunity: they secrete IL-2 (driving cytotoxic T cell expansion), IFN-γ (activating macrophages), and provide cognate help to cytotoxic and regulatory T cells. Helper T cell activation is therefore an upstream causal determinant of T cell immune activity amplitude. In MHC class II deficiency (bare lymphocyte syndrome type II) absent helper T cell activation causes severe combined immunodeficiency with collapsed T cell immune activity."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/airway_resistance> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alveolar_ventilation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aairway_resistance%3Anegative%3Aalveolar_ventilation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/airway_resistance> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alveolar_ventilation> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "West JB Respiratory Physiology 9e Ch 1 — VA = minute ventilation − dead-space ventilation; resistance reduces effective flow at given driving pressure; GINA 2023 Guidelines — bronchospasm in asthma raises airway resistance, reduces FEV1/VA; Miller MR et al 2005 Eur Respir J 26:319 — spirometric measures of airway resistance inversely correlate with alveolar ventilation; pharmacological bronchodilators (salbutamol, do(airway_resistance -)) restore VA in obstructive disease. | intervention: do(airway_resistance +) — inhaled methacholine challenge or allergen bronchoprovocation raises airway resistance, reducing alveolar ventilation; do(airway_resistance -) — salbutamol/ipratropium bronchodilation reduces airway resistance, restoring alveolar ventilation."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Airway resistance (R) and alveolar ventilation (VA) are linked by the fundamental respiratory equation: VA = ΔP / R (for laminar flow), where ΔP is the driving pressure generated by respiratory muscles. When airway resistance rises (bronchospasm, mucus plugging, bronchiole smooth-muscle contraction), the same muscular effort produces less airflow per unit time, reducing effective alveolar ventilation. In asthma, bronchoconstriction raises airway resistance and reduces VA, causing hypercapnia and hypoxaemia."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alveolar_surface_tension> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tidal_volume> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aalveolar_surface_tension%3Anegative%3Atidal_volume> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alveolar_surface_tension> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tidal_volume> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Avery ME & Mead J, Am J Dis Child 1959;97:517-523 (PMID:13649082): premature infants deficient in surfactant have higher alveolar surface tension and lower lung compliance, resulting in reduced tidal volumes and respiratory failure. OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-124 (22.2): 'A type II alveolar cell ... secretes pulmonary surfactant ... reduces the surface tension of the alveoli' — loss of surfactant raises surface tension, impairing expansion. Intervention: do(surfactant deficiency) -> alveolar_surface_tension up -> tidal_volume down. | intervention: Surfactant deficiency (NRDS/RDS, OMIM 267450): do(surfactant down) -> alveolar_surface_tension up -> tidal_volume down. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Increased alveolar surface tension (as in surfactant deficiency / neonatal respiratory distress syndrome) reduces lung compliance; stiffer alveoli resist expansion, lowering tidal volume per breath. Surfactant replacement (DPPC/SP-B/SP-C) restores compliance and normalises tidal volume."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/t_cell_activation_rate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/t_cell_immune_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3At_cell_activation_rate%3Apositive%3At_cell_immune_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/t_cell_activation_rate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/t_cell_immune_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-123 (21.8): 'hormones such as cortisol and prednisone … suppress T cell immune mechanisms' — confirming T cell activation rate drives T cell immune activity. Jakubowski & Flatt Fundamentals of Biochemistry §biochem-4.8.4: calcineurin required for T cell function; FK506/cyclosporin inhibit calcineurin → prevent T cell activation. Murphy K, Janeway's Immunobiology 9e Ch10: T cell activation is the rate-limiting step for T cell-mediated immunity. Intervention: do(calcineurin_activity ↓) → t_cell_activation_rate ↓ → t_cell_immune_activity ↓ (basis of immunosuppression in transplantation). | intervention: Calcineurin inhibition (cyclosporin A, tacrolimus): do(calcineurin ↓) → t_cell_activation_rate ↓ → t_cell_immune_activity ↓."
    ; pmo:mechanism "T cell activation (antigen-MHC recognition, co-stimulation, cytokine signalling) directly drives and constitutes T cell-mediated immune activity. Calcineurin inhibitors (cyclosporin A, tacrolimus) block NFAT dephosphorylation, prevent T cell activation, and markedly suppress T cell-mediated immune activity — confirming the interventional direction."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sting_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/macrophage_nlrp3_inflammasome_assembly> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asting_activity%3Apositive%3Amacrophage_nlrp3_inflammasome_assembly> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sting_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/macrophage_nlrp3_inflammasome_assembly> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "OMIM 615934 (SAVI): gain-of-function STING1 mutations -> autoinflammatory disease with elevated NLRP3-pathway cytokines. Intervention: do(STING gain-of-function) -> sting_activity up -> macrophage_nlrp3_inflammasome_assembly up. | intervention: SAVI (STING gain-of-function, OMIM 615934): constitutive sting_activity -> NLRP3 priming and autoinflammation. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "STING (STING1) activation by cGAMP signals through TBK1/IKKε to activate NF-κB (in addition to IRF3/IFN-β). NF-κB drives transcriptional upregulation of NLRP3 and pro-IL-1β, providing the priming (Signal 1) required for NLRP3 inflammasome assembly. STING gain-of-function mutations (STING-associated vasculopathy with onset in infancy, SAVI; OMIM 615934) cause constitutive NLRP3-dependent autoinflammation."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_prostaglandin_e2> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nociceptive_afferent_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_prostaglandin_e2%3Apositive%3Anociceptive_afferent_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_prostaglandin_e2> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nociceptive_afferent_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Vane JR Nobel Lecture 1982: aspirin blocks COX → reduces PGE2 → reduces peripheral nociceptor sensitisation (analgesia). OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-122 (21.7): 'epinephrine … counteract the effects of anaphylaxis' (context: PGE2 sensitisation of nociceptors is COX-mediated). NSAID intervention: do(PGE2 ↓) → nociceptive_afferent_activity ↓ (analgesic effect). Intervention: do(plasma_prostaglandin_e2 ↑) → nociceptive_afferent_activity ↑; do(COX inhibitor) → plasma_prostaglandin_e2 ↓ → nociceptive_afferent_activity ↓. | intervention: NSAIDs (COX inhibitors, e.g. ibuprofen): do(plasma_prostaglandin_e2 ↓) → nociceptive_afferent_activity ↓ (analgesia). [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Prostaglandin E2 (PGE2) binds EP2 and EP4 receptors on peripheral nociceptive C fibres and Aδ fibres; Gs/cAMP signalling lowers the activation threshold of TRPV1 and Nav1.8 channels, causing peripheral sensitisation (hyperalgesia and allodynia). COX inhibitors (aspirin, ibuprofen) reduce PGE2 and ablate this sensitisation — direct interventional evidence for the positive causal direction."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_thromboxane_b2> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/platelet_activation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_thromboxane_b2%3Apositive%3Aplatelet_activation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_thromboxane_b2> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/platelet_activation> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Intervention: do(plasma_thromboxane_b2 ↑) → platelet_activation ↑; do(aspirin) → plasma_thromboxane_b2 ↓ → platelet_activation ↓ (antiplatelet effect). | intervention: Aspirin (irreversible COX-1 inhibition): do(plasma_thromboxane_b2 ↓) → platelet_activation ↓. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Thromboxane A2 (TXA2, measured as its stable hydrolysis product TXB2) activates the Gq-coupled TP (thromboxane-prostanoid) receptor on platelets, triggering shape change, granule secretion, and integrin αIIbβ3 activation, resulting in platelet aggregation and activation. Aspirin irreversibly inhibits platelet COX-1, reducing TXA2 synthesis and suppressing platelet activation — the basis of antiplatelet therapy."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/synaptic_acetylcholine> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alveolar_ventilation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asynaptic_acetylcholine%3Apositive%3Aalveolar_ventilation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/synaptic_acetylcholine> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alveolar_ventilation> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-38.4 (38.4: Muscle Contraction and Locomotion): 'ACh … causes postsynaptic Na+ channels to open … depolarization'; diaphragm NMJ contraction drives breathing. Intervention: do(neostigmine, ↑ synaptic_acetylcholine) → stronger diaphragm contraction → alveolar_ventilation ↑; do(botulinum toxin, blocks ACh release) → synaptic_acetylcholine ↓ → alveolar_ventilation ↓. | intervention: Botulinum toxin (blocks NMJ ACh release): do(synaptic_acetylcholine ↓) → alveolar_ventilation ↓ (respiratory failure); neostigmine/pyridostigmine (AChE inhibitors): do(synaptic_acetylcholine ↑) → alveolar_ventilation ↑. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Acetylcholine released at the NMJ binds nicotinic AChRs on the diaphragm and intercostal muscle end-plates, triggering action potentials and muscle contraction. Higher NMJ ACh concentration (or tonically elevated ACh as in AChE inhibitor poisoning) drives stronger and/or more frequent respiratory muscle contractions, increasing tidal volume and alveolar ventilation. Botulinum toxin blocks ACh release at NMJ → respiratory muscle paralysis → apnoea; AChE inhibitors (neostigmine) increase NMJ ACh → restore ventilation in neuromuscular blockade reversal."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/uterine_contraction_rate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nociceptive_afferent_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Auterine_contraction_rate%3Apositive%3Anociceptive_afferent_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/uterine_contraction_rate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nociceptive_afferent_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Intervention: do(oxytocin ↑, uterine_contraction_rate ↑) → nociceptive_afferent_activity ↑ (labour pain); do(tocolytic, uterine_contraction_rate ↓) → nociceptive_afferent_activity ↓. | intervention: Tocolytics (β2 agonists, nifedipine): do(uterine_contraction_rate ↓) → nociceptive_afferent_activity ↓ (reduced labour pain). [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Uterine smooth muscle contractions activate uterine mechanonociceptors (A-delta and C fibres) via mechanical distortion and ischaemia-related metabolites (bradykinin, PGE2, potassium). Stronger/more frequent contractions generate higher nociceptive afferent discharge, experienced as dysmenorrhoea or labour pain. NSAIDs and tocolytics that reduce uterine contractions also reduce associated pain — interventional confirmation."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/stomach_motility> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gastric_emptying_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Astomach_motility%3Apositive%3Agastric_emptying_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/stomach_motility> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gastric_emptying_rate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-134 (23.5: The Small and Large Intestines): gastric motility drives chyme through pylorus into duodenum — antral contraction rate determines gastric emptying rate. Intervention: do(metoclopramide, stomach_motility ↑) → gastric_emptying_rate ↑; do(opioids, stomach_motility ↓) → gastric_emptying_rate ↓ (gastroparesis). | intervention: Metoclopramide (prokinetic): do(stomach_motility ↑) → gastric_emptying_rate ↑; Opioids: do(stomach_motility ↓) → gastric_emptying_rate ↓ (opioid-induced gastroparesis). [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Gastric peristaltic contractions (antral pump) generate the intragastric pressure gradient that propels chyme through the pyloric sphincter into the duodenum. Higher contraction rate and amplitude increase the gastric emptying rate. Prokinetics (metoclopramide, domperidone, erythromycin) increase gastric motility and accelerate emptying; opioids and anticholinergics reduce gastric motility and delay emptying."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_melatonin> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_melatonin%3Anegative%3Acortisol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_melatonin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-091 (16.2: Central Control): pineal melatonin regulates circadian rhythm of cortisol — 'the pineal gland secretes melatonin'; melatonin and cortisol have reciprocal circadian rhythms. Intervention: do(plasma_melatonin ↑) → cortisol ↓; do(pinealectomy / melatonin receptor blockade) → plasma_melatonin ↓ → cortisol disinhibited. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Melatonin inhibits the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis at multiple levels: it suppresses CRH release from parvocellular neurons and inhibits pituitary ACTH secretion, reducing adrenal cortisol output. The inverse relationship between melatonin (night peak) and cortisol (morning peak) reflects this reciprocal circadian entrainment. Exogenous melatonin administration acutely suppresses cortisol."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pulmonary_arteriole_diameter> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pulmonary_vascular_resistance> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apulmonary_arteriole_diameter%3Anegative%3Apulmonary_vascular_resistance> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pulmonary_arteriole_diameter> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pulmonary_vascular_resistance> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-127 (22.5 Gas Exchange): 'a greater partial pressure of oxygen in the alveoli causes the pulmonary arterioles to dilate' — vasodilation (increased diameter) reduces PVR. Intervention: do(sildenafil, vasodilation → pulmonary_arteriole_diameter ↑) → pulmonary_vascular_resistance ↓; do(HPV, arteriole_diameter ↓) → pulmonary_vascular_resistance ↑. | intervention: Sildenafil (PDE5 inhibitor, pulmonary vasodilation): do(pulmonary_arteriole_diameter ↑) → pulmonary_vascular_resistance ↓; HPV: do(arteriole_diameter ↓) → PVR ↑. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Pulmonary vascular resistance follows Poiseuille's law (R ∝ 8ηL/πr⁴); arteriolar diameter is the dominant determinant. Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction (HPV) reduces pulmonary arteriole diameter → raises PVR (V/Q matching). Pulmonary vasodilators (sildenafil, iloprost) increase diameter → lower PVR. The alveolar PO2–driven arteriolar dilation in comp227 reduces PVR and redistributes blood to well-ventilated alveoli."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bronchiole_diameter> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alveolar_ventilation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abronchiole_diameter%3Apositive%3Aalveolar_ventilation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bronchiole_diameter> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alveolar_ventilation> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-124 (22.2): 'This muscular wall can change the size of the tubing to increase or decrease airflow through the tube'; 'bronchospasms … can inhibit air from entering the lungs.' Intervention: do(salbutamol/albuterol, bronchiole_diameter ↑) → alveolar_ventilation ↑; do(bronchoconstriction/asthma, bronchiole_diameter ↓) → alveolar_ventilation ↓. | intervention: Salbutamol/albuterol (β2 agonist bronchodilator): do(bronchiole_diameter ↑) → alveolar_ventilation ↑; asthma attack: do(bronchiole_diameter ↓) → alveolar_ventilation ↓. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Bronchiole luminal diameter determines airway resistance (Poiseuille: R ∝ 1/r⁴); larger diameter → lower airway resistance → higher airflow for a given pressure gradient → increased alveolar ventilation. Bronchodilators (β2 agonists, ipratropium) widen bronchioles and improve alveolar ventilation in asthma/COPD. Bronchoconstriction (asthma, histamine) reduces diameter and decreases ventilation."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/duodenal_chyme_acidity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/s_cell_secretin_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aduodenal_chyme_acidity%3Apositive%3As_cell_secretin_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/duodenal_chyme_acidity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/s_cell_secretin_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "StatPearls: Physiology, Secretin (NBK537116) — 'S-cells located in the duodenum and proximal jejunum secrete secretin in response to increased [H+] in the lumen'; Pearson A&P: 'A drop in pH below 4.5 in the duodenum stimulates the secretion of secretin'; Johnson & Grossman, Advances in Physiology Education 2009 (doi:10.1152/advan.00023.2009) — classical paper establishing duodenal acidification as the primary secretin stimulus. | intervention: do(duodenal_chyme_acidity↑) — as occurs when gastric acid hypersecretion (e.g. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome) delivers very acidic chyme to the duodenum, or experimentally by intraduodenal HCl infusion"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Acidic duodenal chyme (pH < 4.5 entering the duodenum from the stomach) directly stimulates intestinal S-cells via luminal H+ sensing, triggering secretin secretion into the portal circulation. This is the canonical stimulus-secretion coupling for secretin release."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/esophageal_luminal_pressure> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_tract_motility> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aesophageal_luminal_pressure%3Apositive%3Agi_tract_motility> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/esophageal_luminal_pressure> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_tract_motility> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "Mittal & Bhalla, J Clin Gastroenterol 2004; PMC2728598 — 'Secondary peristalsis is entirely due to peripheral mechanisms' triggered by esophageal distension; StatPearls: Physiology, Lower Esophageal Sphincter (NBK557452) — 'esophageal peristalsis promotes esophageal acid clearance by removing refluxate'; esophageal luminal pressure is the proximate mechanosensory stimulus for secondary peristalsis. | intervention: do(esophageal_luminal_pressure↑) — as occurs during swallowing or esophageal distension (e.g. achalasia manometry, esophageal balloon distension experiments)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Increased esophageal luminal pressure (distension by a swallowed bolus) activates mechanosensory afferents in the esophageal wall, which trigger secondary peristalsis through enteric and vagal neural circuits. Secondary peristalsis is entirely peripherally (ENS) driven by esophageal distension and represents a key component of GI motility (the rate of peristaltic propagation along the gut)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intracranial_pressure> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mean_arterial_pressure> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aintracranial_pressure%3Apositive%3Amean_arterial_pressure> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intracranial_pressure> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mean_arterial_pressure> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "StatPearls: Cushing Reflex (NBK549801) — 'The mechanism of this reflex is stimulation of sympathetic neurons in the medulla by brainstem compression... causing peripheral vasoconstriction and an increase in cardiac output, which serve to increase arterial blood pressure'; PMC5809772 — 'Intracranial Pressure Is a Determinant of Sympathetic Activity'; Wikipedia: Cushing reflex — characteristic Cushing triad (hypertension, bradycardia, irregular respiration). | intervention: do(intracranial_pressure↑) — as occurs in traumatic brain injury, subarachnoid hemorrhage, hydrocephalus, intracranial mass; clinically correlates with the Cushing triad sign of impending brainstem herniation"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Cushing reflex: acute elevation of intracranial pressure (ICP) compresses the brainstem and medullary vasomotor centers, activating the sympathetic nervous system, causing peripheral vasoconstriction and increased cardiac output → systemic hypertension. The purpose is to maintain cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP = MAP − ICP); rising ICP obligately drives MAP upward to preserve CPP."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/amyloid_protein_aggregate_amount> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il1beta> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aamyloid_protein_aggregate_amount%3Apositive%3Ail1beta> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/amyloid_protein_aggregate_amount> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il1beta> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "PMC8543248 — 'NLRP3 Inflammasome: A Starring Role in Amyloid-β- and Tau-Driven Pathological Events in Alzheimer's Disease'; PMC6231249 — 'Amyloid β directly interacts with NLRP3 to initiate inflammasome activation'; PMC3877570 — 'Oligomeric amyloid β induces IL-1β processing via production of ROS: implication in Alzheimer's disease'; APP/PSEN1 transgenic mice lacking NLRP3 show reduced Aβ deposition and IL-1β, confirming the causal chain (PMC12009708). | intervention: do(amyloid_protein_aggregate_amount↑) — as occurs in Alzheimer's disease (APP, PSEN1/2 gain-of-function mutations; APOE4 risk allele); also modeled by Aβ oligomer injection or APP/PS1 transgenic mouse overexpression"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Amyloid-beta oligomers and fibrils activate microglial NLRP3 inflammasome through multiple convergent pathways: (1) Aβ binds CD36, triggering TLR4-TLR6 heterodimer → NF-κB → pro-IL-1β and NLRP3 transcription; (2) Aβ disrupts lysosomal integrity → cathepsin B release → NLRP3 priming; (3) Aβ oligomers directly bind NLRP3/ASC; (4) Aβ-driven mitochondrial ROS → NLRP3 activation → caspase-1 cleavage of pro-IL-1β → mature IL-1β secretion. Net effect: Aβ aggregate accumulation raises circulating and brain-resident IL-1β."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/interferon_gamma> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/t_cell_immune_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ainterferon_gamma%3Apositive%3At_cell_immune_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/interferon_gamma> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/t_cell_immune_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "PMC4283431 — 'Gamma Interferon: From Antimicrobial Activity to Immune Regulation' — IFN-γ 'plays a major role in activating anticancer immunity, by promoting the activity of CD4 T helper type 1 cells, CD8 cytotoxic T lymphocytes, NK cells, dendritic cells, and macrophages, promoting antigen presentation'; Frontiers Immunology 2018 (doi:10.3389/fimmu.2018.00847) — IFN-γ enhances CTL motility and cytotoxicity (doi:10.1038/cddis.2017.67); PMC4154595 — IFN-γ receptor signaling activates JAK1/2-STAT1 → immune gene transcription. | intervention: do(interferon_gamma↑) — as occurs in viral infection, hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (HLH), macrophage activation syndrome; IFN-γ injection experiments and IFNγ−/− knockout mice confirm the causal role in T cell activation"
    ; pmo:mechanism "IFN-γ (predominantly secreted by NK cells and Th1/CTL cells) promotes activation and effector function of CD4+ Th1 and CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes: it upregulates MHC class I and II expression (enhancing antigen presentation), drives Th1 polarization, enhances CTL cytotoxicity, and activates macrophage microbicidal activity — collectively amplifying T cell-mediated immunity. Net causal arrow: IFN-γ↑ → T cell immune activity↑."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mcad_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_ketogenesis> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amcad_activity%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_ketogenesis> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mcad_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_ketogenesis> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Scriver's OMMBID / Saudubray 'Inborn Metabolic Diseases': MCAD deficiency (OMIM 201450) — cardinal feature is hypoketotic hypoglycaemia on fasting, demonstrating that MCAD activity is required for normal hepatic ketone-body production from medium-chain fatty acids. Lehninger Biochemistry Ch.17: beta-oxidation generates acetyl-CoA feeding ketogenesis. | intervention: MCAD deficiency (ACADM LoF, OMIM 201450) as the archetypal do() clamp: enzyme activity abolished → hepatic_ketogenesis reduced (hypoketotic state)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "MCAD (ACADM) catalyses the first step of medium-chain fatty-acid beta-oxidation, generating acetyl-CoA; acetyl-CoA is the obligate substrate for hepatic ketogenesis. MCAD deficiency (LoF) impairs FAO, reducing acetyl-CoA supply and thus hepatic ketogenesis, producing hypoketotic hypoglycaemia (the hallmark of MCAD deficiency). do(MCAD_activity UP) → more medium-chain acyl-CoA oxidised → more acetyl-CoA → more hepatic ketogenesis."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/scot_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ketone_bodies> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ascot_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_ketone_bodies> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/scot_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ketone_bodies> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Scriver's OMMBID / Saudubray 'Inborn Metabolic Diseases': SCOT (OXCT1) deficiency (OMIM 245050) — hallmark is persistent, non-fasting ketoacidosis because acetoacetate cannot be activated for peripheral oxidation; plasma 3-hydroxybutyrate and acetoacetate markedly elevated. Mechanism: Lehninger Ch.17 (ketone-body utilisation, SCOT role). | intervention: SCOT deficiency (OXCT1 LoF, OMIM 245050): peripheral ketolysis abolished → plasma acetoacetate and 3-hydroxybutyrate rise persistently"
    ; pmo:mechanism "SCOT (succinyl-CoA:3-oxoacid-CoA transferase, OXCT1) catalyses the rate-limiting step of peripheral ketone-body oxidation: activating acetoacetate to acetoacetyl-CoA for further catabolism. SCOT is absent from liver, so its activity determines extrahepatic ketone utilisation. do(SCOT_activity UP) → more acetoacetate activated and oxidised → plasma ketone bodies fall. SCOT deficiency (OMIM 245050) causes permanent non-fasting ketoacidosis because ketone bodies cannot be consumed peripherally."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_oxalate> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_oxalate%3Anegative%3Agfr> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_oxalate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (primary hyperoxaluria / oxalosis, renal failure); Scriver's OMMBID — PH1 (AGXT, OMIM 259900): oxalate nephropathy is the cause of renal failure; Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (renal handling of oxalate, nephrocalcinosis). | intervention: Primary hyperoxaluria type 1 (AGXT LoF, OMIM 259900): plasma and urinary oxalate chronically elevated → calcium-oxalate deposition in kidney → nephrocalcinosis → GFR progressively falls"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Oxalate is freely filtered at the glomerulus but is insoluble with calcium at physiological concentrations; hyperoxaluria (plasma oxalate elevated) leads to calcium-oxalate crystal deposition in renal tubules and interstitium (nephrocalcinosis/nephrolithiasis), causing progressive tubulointerstitial nephropathy and reduction in GFR. In primary hyperoxaluria type 1 (AGT/AGXT deficiency), severe oxalate overproduction leads to end-stage renal disease. do(plasma_oxalate UP) → renal crystal deposition → tubular injury → GFR falls."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bckdh_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abckdh_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_glucose> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bckdh_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Scriver's OMMBID / Saudubray — MSUD (BCKDHA/BCKDHB LoF, OMIM 248600): hypoglycaemia is a documented acute complication; alpha-KIC inhibits PDH (Danner & Doering, J Biol Chem 1977); succinyl-CoA from isoleucine/valine catabolism feeds gluconeogenesis (Lehninger Ch.18). Saudubray (2016) 'Inborn Metabolic Diseases' Ch.21: MSUD metabolic crises include hypoglycaemia. | intervention: MSUD (BCKDH LoF, OMIM 248600): BCKAs accumulate, PDH inhibited, gluconeogenic anaplerosis reduced → plasma glucose falls (hypoglycaemia)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "The BCKDH complex oxidatively decarboxylates branched-chain alpha-keto acids (BCKAs), generating succinyl-CoA (from isoleucine/valine, a gluconeogenic substrate) and acetyl-CoA (from leucine). In MSUD (BCKDH deficiency), accumulating BCKAs — particularly 4-methyl-2-oxopentanoate (alpha-ketoisocaproate) — inhibit pyruvate dehydrogenase and disrupt anaplerosis, impairing hepatic gluconeogenesis and causing hypoglycaemia. do(BCKDH_activity UP) → BCKAs cleared → less PDH inhibition + more succinyl-CoA anaplerosis → hepatic glucose production supported → plasma glucose rises."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/propionyl_coa_carboxylase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apropionyl_coa_carboxylase_activity%3Apositive%3Acellular_atp_level> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/propionyl_coa_carboxylase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Scriver's OMMBID / Saudubray — propionic acidemia (PCCA/PCCB, OMIM 606054): propionyl-CoA accumulates and inhibits mitochondrial complex I activity (Schwab et al. 2006 J Inherit Metab Dis); reduced TCA anaplerosis from succinyl-CoA deficit documented. Lehninger Ch.20: succinyl-CoA as TCA anaplerotic substrate from propionyl-CoA pathway. | intervention: Propionic acidemia (PCC LoF, OMIM 606054): propionyl-CoA accumulates → Complex I inhibition → cellular ATP level falls; TCA anaplerosis reduced"
    ; pmo:mechanism "PCC (propionyl-CoA carboxylase) converts propionyl-CoA to methylmalonyl-CoA, which is subsequently converted to succinyl-CoA for TCA-cycle anaplerosis. This pathway provides succinyl-CoA (an obligate TCA intermediate) from odd-chain fatty acids, isoleucine, valine, methionine and threonine catabolism, supporting NADH/FADH2 generation and ATP synthesis. In propionic acidemia (PCC deficiency), accumulating propionyl-CoA directly inhibits mitochondrial complex I (NADH:ubiquinone oxidoreductase) and N-acetylglutamate synthase, impairing ETC and urea-cycle function. do(PCC_activity UP) → propionyl-CoA cleared → more succinyl-CoA → TCA flux → more ATP; also relieves propionyl-CoA inhibition of Complex I."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gcdh_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Agcdh_activity%3Apositive%3Acellular_atp_level> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gcdh_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Scriver's OMMBID / Saudubray — glutaric aciduria type 1 (GCDH, OMIM 231670): mitochondrial dysfunction and energy deficit documented; GCDH electrons feed ETC via ETF/ETF-QO. Lehninger Ch.18: FAD-linked acyl-CoA dehydrogenases (including GCDH) donate electrons to ETF for ETC entry. Boy et al. (2017) J Inherit Metab Dis: mitochondrial ATP production impaired in GCDH deficiency. | intervention: Glutaric aciduria type 1 (GCDH LoF, OMIM 231670): ETF electron flux from lysine catabolism impaired; glutaryl-CoA/glutarate mitochondrial toxicity → cellular ATP level reduced"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GCDH (glutaryl-CoA dehydrogenase) catalyses the FAD-linked oxidative decarboxylation of glutaryl-CoA to crotonyl-CoA in lysine/hydroxylysine/tryptophan catabolism; its electrons are transferred via ETF (electron-transfer flavoprotein) to ETF-QO and then to ubiquinone in the mitochondrial ETC, contributing to cellular ATP synthesis. GCDH deficiency (glutaric aciduria type 1) impairs this electron-flux pathway; accumulated glutaryl-CoA and glutaric acid also exert mitochondrial toxicity reducing ETC efficiency. do(GCDH_activity UP) → more ETF-mediated electron flux → ETC → more ATP."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fumarate_hydratase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afumarate_hydratase_activity%3Apositive%3Acellular_atp_level> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fumarate_hydratase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Scriver's OMMBID / Saudubray — fumaric aciduria (FH/FUM1 LoF, OMIM 606812): severe encephalopathy and early death due to profound energy deficit; TCA cycle block at fumarate→malate demonstrated. Lehninger Ch.16: fumarate hydratase in TCA cycle. Briere et al. (2005) Cancer Res: FH loss and TCA block. | intervention: Fumaric aciduria (FH LoF, OMIM 606812): TCA cycle interrupted at fumarate, NADH/FADH2 production severely impaired → cellular ATP level markedly reduced"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Fumarate hydratase (fumarase, FH) catalyses the reversible hydration of fumarate to malate in the TCA cycle, an obligate step for continued cycle flux. FH deficiency (fumaric aciduria, OMIM 606812) blocks TCA cycle at this step, preventing downstream malate → oxaloacetate → citrate, disrupting NADH/FADH2 generation from TCA and severely impairing ATP synthesis. Accumulated fumarate also acts as a competitive inhibitor of alpha-KG-dependent dioxygenases. do(FH_activity UP) → TCA cycle flux maintained → NADH/FADH2 generated → ETC → more cellular ATP."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/isovaleryl_coa_dehydrogenase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aisovaleryl_coa_dehydrogenase_activity%3Apositive%3Acellular_atp_level> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/isovaleryl_coa_dehydrogenase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Scriver's OMMBID / Saudubray — isovaleric acidemia (IVD, OMIM 243500): mitochondrial toxicity of accumulated isovaleryl-CoA; IVD is an ETF-dependent dehydrogenase (same ETF pathway as GCDH, MCAD, SCHAD). Lehninger Ch.18: acyl-CoA dehydrogenases donate electrons to ETF for ETC entry. Ensenauer et al. (2004) Pediatr Res: mitochondrial dysfunction in isovaleric acidemia. | intervention: Isovaleric acidemia (IVD LoF, OMIM 243500): ETF electron flux from leucine catabolism impaired; isovaleryl-CoA mitochondrial toxicity → cellular ATP level reduced"
    ; pmo:mechanism "IVD (isovaleryl-CoA dehydrogenase) catalyses the FAD-linked dehydrogenation of isovaleryl-CoA in leucine catabolism; electrons are transferred via ETF to ETF-QO and then ubiquinone in the mitochondrial ETC, contributing to ATP synthesis. IVD deficiency (isovaleric acidemia) impairs this electron-flux pathway, and accumulated isovaleryl-CoA/isovalerate exerts mitochondrial toxicity. do(IVD_activity UP) → more ETF electron flux from leucine catabolism → ETC → more cellular ATP."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/succinate_dehydrogenase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_respiratory_chain_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asuccinate_dehydrogenase_activity%3Apositive%3Amitochondrial_respiratory_chain_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/succinate_dehydrogenase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_respiratory_chain_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Biochemistry Ch.18-19: succinate dehydrogenase = Complex II, directly couples TCA cycle to ETC. SDH subunit mutations (SDHA: OMIM 614165; SDHB: OMIM 185470): cause mitochondrial respiratory chain dysfunction, encephalomyopathy and neurodegeneration. Scriver's OMMBID: SDH deficiency reduces mitochondrial respiratory chain electron flux. Parfait et al. (2000) Eur J Hum Genet: isolated Complex II deficiency from SDHA mutations causes Leigh syndrome via ETC impairment. | intervention: SDH/Complex II deficiency (SDHA LoF, OMIM 614165): succinate-ubiquinone oxidoreductase activity abolished → mitochondrial respiratory chain electron flux reduced → overall ETC activity falls"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Succinate dehydrogenase (SDH) is mitochondrial respiratory chain complex II: it catalyses both the oxidation of succinate to fumarate in the TCA cycle (generating FADH2) AND the transfer of those electrons directly to ubiquinone (CoQ) in the inner mitochondrial membrane, making SDH an integral component of the ETC. SDH activity is therefore directly constitutive of mitochondrial respiratory chain function; reduced SDH activity reduces electron flux through ubiquinone → Complex III → Complex IV → oxygen, lowering the overall ETC/respiratory chain activity. do(SDH_activity UP) → more electrons to CoQ → more ETC activity."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/etfdh_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aetfdh_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_glucose> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/etfdh_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Multiple acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency (MADD; OMIM 231680); Wikipedia/GeneReviews NBK558236: 'profound hypoglycemia' is a cardinal feature of neonatal-onset MADD; PMC7212588 (2020 ETFDH case report): 'patients must strictly avoid fasting to prevent hypoglycemia'; Saudubray, Inborn Metabolic Diseases (MADD/glutaric aciduria type II). Intervention: do(ETFDH loss-of-function, MADD, OMIM 231680). | intervention: genetic LoF of ETFDH (electron-transfer flavoprotein dehydrogenase; MADD / glutaric aciduria type II, OMIM 231680)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ETFDH (electron-transfer flavoprotein dehydrogenase) is the terminal electron acceptor of multiple flavoprotein acyl-CoA dehydrogenases in fatty-acid and amino-acid oxidation. ETFDH deficiency (MADD / glutaric aciduria type II, OMIM 231680) impairs mitochondrial fatty-acid beta-oxidation. During fasting, FAO normally spares glucose and provides ketone-body precursors; ETFDH deficiency abolishes this alternative fuel supply, causing profound fasting hypoglycemia (hypoketotic hypoglycemia). Do(ETFDH LoF) -> impaired FAO -> no FAO-derived energy to spare glucose -> plasma glucose falls."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hibch_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_respiratory_chain_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahibch_activity%3Apositive%3Amitochondrial_respiratory_chain_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hibch_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_respiratory_chain_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "HIBCH deficiency (OMIM 250620); Reuter et al., Am J Med Genet 2014 (PMID 25251209): 'multiple mitochondrial respiratory chain enzyme deficiencies in skeletal muscle and fibroblasts'; Olsen et al., Clin Genet 2014 (PMC4222069): 'HIBCH mutations can cause Leigh-like disease with combined deficiency of multiple mitochondrial respiratory chain enzymes and pyruvate dehydrogenase'; Saudubray, Inborn Metabolic Diseases. Intervention: do(HIBCH loss-of-function, OMIM 250620). | intervention: genetic LoF of HIBCH (3-hydroxyisobutyryl-CoA hydrolase; OMIM 250620) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "3-Hydroxyisobutyryl-CoA hydrolase (HIBCH) hydrolyzes 3-hydroxyisobutyryl-CoA to free 3-hydroxyisobutyrate and CoA in valine catabolism. HIBCH deficiency (OMIM 250620) causes upstream accumulation of methacrylyl-CoA, a reactive alpha,beta-unsaturated thioester that alkylates CoA pools and mitochondrial proteins, causing secondary deficiency of multiple respiratory chain complexes (I, III, and IV documented in muscle and fibroblasts). Do(HIBCH LoF) -> methacrylyl-CoA accumulates -> inhibits mitochondrial respiratory chain -> mitochondrial_respiratory_chain_activity decreases. HIBCH active -> methacrylyl-CoA cleared -> respiratory chain intact."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/squalene_synthase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ldl_cholesterol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asqualene_synthase_activity%3Apositive%3Aldl_cholesterol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/squalene_synthase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ldl_cholesterol> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Squalene synthase deficiency (FDFT1; OMIM 618156); Coman et al., Am J Hum Genet 2018 ( / PMC6037199): 'patients show low blood total cholesterol and reduced low-density lipoprotein cholesterol (LDL-C)' as part of the clinical characterization; GeneReviews NBK553533 (squalene synthase deficiency: 'low total and LDL-cholesterol'). Intervention: do(FDFT1/squalene synthase loss-of-function, OMIM 618156). | intervention: genetic LoF of FDFT1 (squalene synthase; squalene synthase deficiency, OMIM 618156) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Squalene synthase (FDFT1) catalyzes the first committed step of the mevalonate pathway leading to cholesterol biosynthesis, condensing two farnesyl-pyrophosphate molecules to squalene. FDFT1 deficiency (squalene synthase deficiency, OMIM 618156) reduces de novo hepatic cholesterol synthesis, lowering circulating LDL-cholesterol. Do(FDFT1 LoF) -> less squalene -> less cholesterol synthesis -> LDL-cholesterol falls. FDFT1 active -> more squalene -> more cholesterol -> more LDL."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fumarylacetoacetase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afumarylacetoacetase_activity%3Apositive%3Agfr> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fumarylacetoacetase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Hereditary tyrosinemia type 1 (FAH; OMIM 276700); Lindstedt et al., Lancet 1992 (pioneering HT1 therapy paper); Bergeron et al., Pediatr Res 2000 (PMID 10665936): 'de Toni-Fanconi syndrome and apoptosis of renal tubular cells in Fah/Hpd double mutant mice'; Saudubray, Inborn Metabolic Diseases (tyrosinemia type I: renal tubular dysfunction, Fanconi syndrome, renal insufficiency); GeneReviews NBK1515 (tyrosinemia type 1: progressive renal tubular dysfunction and GFR decline). Intervention: do(FAH loss-of-function, hereditary tyrosinemia type 1, OMIM 276700). | intervention: genetic LoF of FAH (fumarylacetoacetase; hereditary tyrosinemia type 1, OMIM 276700) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Fumarylacetoacetase (FAH) catalyzes the final step of tyrosine catabolism; FAH deficiency (hereditary tyrosinemia type 1, OMIM 276700) causes accumulation of fumarylacetoacetate and its conversion to succinylacetone. Succinylacetone is a potent mitochondrial toxin in renal proximal tubular cells: it inhibits Na+-coupled sugar and amino acid cotransporters in brush-border membrane vesicles, impairs mitochondrial function, and causes apoptosis of tubular cells (Fanconi syndrome). Chronic tubular damage reduces GFR. Do(FAH LoF) -> succinylacetone accumulates -> renal tubular injury -> GFR declines. FAH active -> no toxic metabolite accumulation -> renal tubules intact -> GFR maintained."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pah_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_catecholamines> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apah_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_catecholamines> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pah_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_catecholamines> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Phenylketonuria (PAH; OMIM 261600); Scriver et al., OMMBID (PKU chapter): 'tyrosine, the precursor of melanin and catecholamines, is often low without appropriate dietary management'; NBK535378 (StatPearls PKU): 'deficiency of downstream products including catecholamine neurotransmitters'; PMC3470696 (ENU2 mice): 'impaired cortical dopamine synthesis due to reduced tyrosine precursor availability'; Blau et al., Physicians Guide to the Diagnosis, Treatment, and Follow-Up of Inherited Metabolic Diseases. Intervention: do(PAH loss-of-function, phenylketonuria, OMIM 261600). | intervention: genetic LoF of PAH (phenylalanine-4-hydroxylase; phenylketonuria / PKU, OMIM 261600)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Phenylalanine-4-hydroxylase (PAH) converts phenylalanine to tyrosine, which is the direct precursor for the catecholamine biosynthetic pathway (tyrosine -> DOPA -> dopamine -> norepinephrine -> epinephrine). PAH deficiency (phenylketonuria, OMIM 261600) causes phenylalanine accumulation and simultaneously reduces tyrosine availability. Reduced tyrosine substrate lowers the rate-limiting step of catecholamine synthesis (tyrosine hydroxylase), reducing production of dopamine, norepinephrine, and epinephrine. Do(PAH LoF) -> less tyrosine -> less catecholamine synthesis -> plasma catecholamines fall. PAH active -> sufficient tyrosine -> normal catecholamine production."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/uroporphyrinogen_iii_synthase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythropoietic_iron_demand> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Auroporphyrinogen_iii_synthase_activity%3Anegative%3Aerythropoietic_iron_demand> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/uroporphyrinogen_iii_synthase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythropoietic_iron_demand> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, The Porphyrias (CEP: severe hemolytic anemia, splenomegaly, elevated erythropoietic drive); GeneReviews NBK154652 (CEP: 'elevated erythrocyte uroporphyrin I and coproporphyrin I … hemolytic anemia'). OMIM 263700 (CEP, UROS LoF). Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, heme biosynthesis (UROS = uroporphyrinogen-III synthase step). | intervention: UROS loss-of-function (congenital erythropoietic porphyria, OMIM 263700) | intervention: UROS loss-of-function (CEP, OMIM 263700)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "UROS deficiency (congenital erythropoietic porphyria, CEP; OMIM 263700) causes massive accumulation of uroporphyrin I and coproporphyrin I in erythroid cells, leading to intramedullary and peripheral hemolysis. Chronic hemolytic anemia drives compensatory erythropoiesis, raising erythroferrone-mediated erythropoietic iron demand. do(UROS LoF) → CEP → hemolytic anemia → ↑ erythropoietic iron demand."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sulfide_quinone_oxidoreductase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asulfide_quinone_oxidoreductase_activity%3Apositive%3Acellular_atp_level> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sulfide_quinone_oxidoreductase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "SQOR deficiency (SQORD; OMIM 619221): SQOR LoF causes Leigh disease with profound mitochondrial dysfunction. Ziosi et al., Frontiers in Physiology 2017 (doi:10.3389/fphys.2017.00525): SQOR knockdown causes mitochondrial dysfunction and reduced electron transport chain activity. PMC7484123 (Pathogenic variants in SQOR are a potentially treatable cause of Leigh disease): 'mitochondrial complex IV activity reduced; ATP synthesis impaired'. Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (H2S as complex IV inhibitor; SQOR in H2S oxidation). | intervention: SQOR loss-of-function (SQORD, Leigh disease variant, OMIM 619221) | intervention: SQOR loss-of-function (SQORD, OMIM 619221)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "SQOR (sulfide:quinone oxidoreductase, mitochondrial; SQORD) is the first enzyme in the mitochondrial H2S oxidation pathway: it oxidizes H2S to a persulfide and transfers electrons to coenzyme Q, feeding the electron transport chain. SQOR deficiency (OMIM 619221) causes H2S to accumulate in cells; H2S is a potent inhibitor of cytochrome c oxidase (complex IV), blocking the terminal step of oxidative phosphorylation and severely reducing mitochondrial ATP synthesis. do(SQOR LoF) → H2S accumulates → complex IV inhibited → cellular ATP falls."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/neuraminidase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aneuraminidase_activity%3Apositive%3Agfr> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/neuraminidase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Sialidosis OMIM 256550 (NEU1 LoF); Genes 2025 PMC11855786 (sialidosis: multi-organ storage including kidney; renal involvement documented). Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, Lysosomal Storage Diseases (renal storage in oligosaccharidoses). Saudubray 'Inborn Metabolic Diseases' (sialidosis: renal involvement with proteinuria reported in severe forms). | intervention: NEU1 (neuraminidase-1) loss-of-function (sialidosis, OMIM 256550) | intervention: NEU1 loss-of-function (sialidosis, OMIM 256550)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Lysosomal neuraminidase-1 (NEU1) deficiency (sialidosis, OMIM 256550) causes accumulation of sialylated oligosaccharides and glycoproteins in multiple tissues including kidney podocytes and mesangial cells. Progressive lysosomal storage in glomerular cells leads to podocyte injury, proteinuria, and impaired glomerular filtration. do(NEU1 LoF) → sialic-acid-containing oligosaccharide storage in kidney → glomerular damage → GFR falls."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gla_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Agla_activity%3Apositive%3Agfr> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gla_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Fabry disease OMIM 301500 (GLA LoF); Schiffmann et al., NEJM 2001 (enzyme replacement in Fabry: renal function protection); Germain, Orphanet J Rare Dis 2010 (Fabry nephropathy: Gb3 accumulation in all renal cell types; GFR decline in males from 3rd-4th decade). Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, Lysosomal Storage Diseases (Fabry disease: renal involvement causes progressive renal failure). | intervention: GLA loss-of-function (Fabry disease, OMIM 301500)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Alpha-galactosidase A (GLA) deficiency (Fabry disease, OMIM 301500) causes globotriaosylceramide (Gb3) to accumulate in renal tubular cells, glomerular endothelial cells, mesangial cells, and podocytes. Progressive Gb3 storage leads to podocyte vacuolation, glomerulosclerosis, tubular atrophy, and interstitial fibrosis — the hallmark Fabry nephropathy — causing progressive reduction in GFR and end-stage renal disease in untreated males. do(GLA LoF) → Gb3 storage in kidney → nephropathy → GFR falls."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ferrochelatase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythropoietic_iron_demand> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aferrochelatase_activity%3Anegative%3Aerythropoietic_iron_demand> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ferrochelatase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythropoietic_iron_demand> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Erythropoietic protoporphyria OMIM 177000 (FECH LoF); Barman-Aksözen et al., Blood Adv 2017 (EPP: anemia due to defective heme synthesis, elevated erythropoietin, compensatory erythropoiesis); Matthes et al., J Hepatol 1994 (EPP: evidence of ineffective erythropoiesis and iron dysregulation). Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, heme biosynthesis (ferrochelatase = terminal iron-insertion step). | intervention: FECH loss-of-function (erythropoietic protoporphyria, OMIM 177000)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Ferrochelatase (FECH) catalyzes the final step of heme biosynthesis, inserting Fe2+ into protoporphyrin IX. FECH deficiency (erythropoietic protoporphyria, EPP; OMIM 177000) blocks heme synthesis in erythroid precursors; despite adequate iron, heme cannot be made. The functional heme deficit in erythroid cells activates compensatory erythropoietic signaling: erythropoiesis is ineffective, EPO rises, and erythropoietic iron demand (erythroferrone signal) increases to drive more iron acquisition even though the iron cannot be incorporated into heme. do(FECH LoF) → heme synthesis blocked → ineffective erythropoiesis → ↑ erythropoietic iron demand."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/porphobilinogen_deaminase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nociceptive_afferent_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aporphobilinogen_deaminase_activity%3Anegative%3Anociceptive_afferent_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/porphobilinogen_deaminase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nociceptive_afferent_activity> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Acute intermittent porphyria OMIM 176000 (HMBS/PBGD LoF); Albers & Fink, Prog Neurobiol 2004 (ALA neurotoxicity: GABA-receptor antagonism, oxidative stress, neuropathy mechanisms); Besur et al., Diagnostics 2014 (AIP: ALA accumulation causes acute neurovisceral attacks with severe abdominal pain and peripheral neuropathy). Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, The Porphyrias (AIP: severe acute abdominal pain, peripheral and autonomic neuropathy during attacks). | intervention: HMBS/PBGD loss-of-function (acute intermittent porphyria, OMIM 176000) | intervention: HMBS/PBGD loss-of-function (AIP, OMIM 176000)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Porphobilinogen deaminase (HMBS/PBGD) deficiency (acute intermittent porphyria, AIP; OMIM 176000) causes ALA (delta-aminolevulinic acid) and PBG to accumulate during metabolic attacks. ALA is a structural analogue of GABA and is directly neurotoxic: it inhibits GABAergic inhibition, activates glutamate receptors, generates reactive oxygen species, and causes autonomic and peripheral neuropathy. The cardinal presentation of AIP attacks is severe acute abdominal pain driven by visceral neuropathy plus somatic pain from peripheral nerve damage. do(PBGD LoF → ALA accumulation) → nociceptive afferent activity rises."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tissue_nonspecific_alp_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alkaline_phosphatase> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atissue_nonspecific_alp_activity%3Apositive%3Aalkaline_phosphatase> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tissue_nonspecific_alp_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alkaline_phosphatase> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Whyte 2016 PMC4824800 'Alkaline Phosphatase and Hypophosphatasia' (hypophosphatasia is defined by subnormal serum ALP activity due to ALPL LoF; ALP is the direct serum measurement of TNAP activity). Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 'Hypophosphatasia' (low ALP the diagnostic hallmark). OMIM 241500 (perinatal lethal HPP, ALPL LoF: 'subnormal serum ALP'). | intervention: ALPL (TNAP) loss-of-function (hypophosphatasia, OMIM 241500/146300) | intervention: ALPL loss-of-function (hypophosphatasia, OMIM 241500/146300)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Tissue-nonspecific alkaline phosphatase (TNAP, encoded by ALPL) is the enzyme whose serum/plasma activity IS measured as 'alkaline phosphatase' in clinical chemistry. TNAP is expressed in bone osteoblasts, liver, kidney, and intestine, and is released into the circulation. ALPL deficiency (hypophosphatasia; OMIM 241500/146300) directly and dramatically lowers circulating alkaline phosphatase activity — the hallmark and diagnostic criterion of hypophosphatasia. do(ALPL LoF) → less TNAP released into plasma → ↓ plasma alkaline phosphatase."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/coproporphyrinogen_oxidase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nociceptive_afferent_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acoproporphyrinogen_oxidase_activity%3Anegative%3Anociceptive_afferent_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/coproporphyrinogen_oxidase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nociceptive_afferent_activity> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Hereditary coproporphyria OMIM 121300 (CPOX LoF); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, The Porphyrias (HCP: acute neurological attacks with severe abdominal pain, identical to AIP; ALA and PBG elevated during attacks). American Porphyria Foundation, HCP (www.porphyriafoundation.org): 'acute attacks with severe abdominal pain and neurological symptoms, caused by ALA and PBG accumulation'. Albers & Fink, Prog Neurobiol 2004 (ALA neurotoxicity mechanism, common to all acute hepatic porphyrias). | intervention: CPOX loss-of-function (hereditary coproporphyria, OMIM 121300)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Coproporphyrinogen III oxidase (CPOX) deficiency (hereditary coproporphyria, HCP; OMIM 121300) causes accumulation of ALA and PBG upstream of the enzymatic block in addition to coproporphyrin III. The elevated ALA (neurotoxic GABA-analogue) causes autonomic and peripheral neuropathy; acute attacks are characterized by severe abdominal pain indistinguishable from acute intermittent porphyria. do(CPOX LoF → ALA/PBG accumulation) → nociceptive afferent activity rises."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/heme_oxygenase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_unconjugated_bilirubin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aheme_oxygenase_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_unconjugated_bilirubin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/heme_oxygenase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_unconjugated_bilirubin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, heme catabolism: 'heme oxygenase cleaves the α-methene bridge of heme to yield biliverdin IX; biliverdin reductase then reduces biliverdin to bilirubin'; this is the committed step for plasma unconjugated bilirubin. HMOX1 deficiency (OMIM 614034): patients show reduced plasma bilirubin and absence of CO production. Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (bile pigments: HMOX the rate-limiting step, bilirubin formed = 250-300 mg/day from heme catabolism). | intervention: HMOX1/HMOX2 activity modulation (HMOX1 deficiency OMIM 614034; or heme loading pharmacological model) | intervention: HMOX1/2 activity change (HMOX1 deficiency OMIM 614034)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Heme oxygenase (HMOX1/HMOX2) catalyzes the rate-limiting step in heme catabolism: it cleaves the porphyrin ring of heme to produce biliverdin (the immediate product) plus CO and Fe2+. Biliverdin is immediately reduced to unconjugated bilirubin by biliverdin reductase (BVR). Greater heme oxygenase activity produces more biliverdin and thus more unconjugated bilirubin entering the plasma. do(HMOX1 activity UP) → biliverdin production UP → plasma unconjugated bilirubin UP. HMOX1 deficiency (OMIM 614034) causes reduced bilirubin production."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/qdpr_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_tetrahydrobiopterin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aqdpr_activity%3Apositive%3Acsf_tetrahydrobiopterin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/qdpr_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_tetrahydrobiopterin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "DHPR/QDPR deficiency OMIM 261630; Blau et al., Physician's Guide to Inherited Metabolic Diseases (DHPR deficiency: 'CSF tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4) is markedly reduced'; 'failure to regenerate BH4 causes severe neurotransmitter deficiency'). PMC11743392 (UAE BH4 disorder cohort: CSF BH4 severely reduced in DHPR deficiency). Kaufman, Proc Natl Acad Sci 1975 (DHPR regenerates BH4 in the hydroxylase catalytic cycle). | intervention: QDPR (DHPR) loss-of-function (DHPR deficiency, OMIM 261630) | intervention: QDPR/DHPR loss-of-function (OMIM 261630)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Dihydropteridine reductase (QDPR) regenerates tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4) from quinonoid-dihydrobiopterin (qBH2) in the aromatic amino acid hydroxylase reactions (phenylalanine hydroxylase, tyrosine hydroxylase, tryptophan hydroxylase). QDPR deficiency (DHPR deficiency; OMIM 261630) abolishes BH4 regeneration; BH4 is consumed and not recycled, causing CSF BH4 to fall while biopterin (the fully oxidized form) accumulates. Low CSF BH4 impairs neurotransmitter synthesis (dopamine, norepinephrine, serotonin) causing severe progressive neurological disease. do(QDPR LoF) → BH4 not regenerated → CSF BH4 falls."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_tryptophan> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_serotonin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_tryptophan%3Apositive%3Aplasma_serotonin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_tryptophan> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_serotonin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "PMC9048412 (Enzyme Promiscuity in Serotonin Biosynthesis: TPH1 in enterochromaffin cells, tryptophan as sole substrate); PMC6098093 (Microbial tryptophan catabolites in health and disease: ~5% tryptophan to serotonin pathway in EC cells; substrate availability influences 5-HT output); Cell TEM 2024 (S1043-2760(24)00195-4) host-microbe serotonin metabolism — plasma tryptophan is a key determinant of enterochromaffin serotonin synthesis. | intervention: tryptophan loading (elevated plasma tryptophan) or conditions reducing bacterial tryptophan diversion | intervention: do(plasma_tryptophan elevated) — tryptophan loading, reduced gut bacterial catabolism, or impaired kynurenine-pathway shunting"
    ; pmo:mechanism "L-tryptophan is the obligate substrate for serotonin biosynthesis: intestinal enterochromaffin cells express tryptophan hydroxylase 1 (TPH1), the rate-limiting enzyme that hydroxylates tryptophan to 5-hydroxytryptophan (5-HTP), subsequently decarboxylated to serotonin (5-HT). ~5% of dietary tryptophan enters this pathway; plasma tryptophan availability is rate-limiting when TPH1 is unsaturated. Elevated plasma tryptophan (e.g. tryptophan-loading, reduced bacterial diversion) raises serotonin synthesis and plasma/platelet serotonin. do(plasma_tryptophan up) → plasma_serotonin up."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/methylmalonyl_coa_mutase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_lactate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amethylmalonyl_coa_mutase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_lactate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/methylmalonyl_coa_mutase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_lactate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "GeneReviews NBK1231 (Isolated Methylmalonic Acidemia: lactic acidemia is a defining metabolic crisis feature); PMC10715492 (New insights into MMA pathophysiology: methylmalonic acid inhibits respiratory chain; secondary lactic acidosis); OMIM 251000 (methylmalonic acidemia, MMUT type: lactate, ammonia elevated in acute decompensation). | intervention: do(MMUT LoF) — biallelic MMUT mutations causing methylmalonic acidemia (OMIM 251000) | intervention: do(MMUT loss-of-function) — biallelic mutations in MMUT gene causing isolated methylmalonic acidemia (OMIM 251000)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase (MMUT) converts methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA for TCA-cycle entry. MMUT deficiency (methylmalonic acidemia, MMA) causes accumulation of methylmalonic acid and 2-methylcitric acid, which inhibit the mitochondrial respiratory chain (complex I/II) and reduce aerobic oxidation flux; anaerobic glycolysis is upregulated, increasing pyruvate-to-lactate conversion. The resulting lactic acidosis is a hallmark metabolic crisis in MMA. do(MMUT activity down) → plasma_lactate up; do(MMUT activity up) → plasma_lactate down."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cftr_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_chloride> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acftr_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_chloride> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cftr_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_chloride> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "PMC11335532 (Metabolic alkalosis in CF: impaired bicarbonate excretion and sweat Cl loss → hypochloremia, pseudo-Bartter); PMC10575793 (Uncommon CF presentation: pseudo-Bartter syndrome, plasma Cl <95, metabolic alkalosis); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (sweat gland: CFTR reabsorbs ductal Cl; CF → salty sweat → systemic Cl depletion). OMIM 219700 (cystic fibrosis). | intervention: do(CFTR LoF) — biallelic CFTR mutations causing cystic fibrosis (OMIM 219700) | intervention: do(CFTR loss-of-function) — biallelic CFTR mutations (cystic fibrosis, OMIM 219700) causing chronic sweat chloride wasting and systemic hypochloremia"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CFTR is the apical chloride channel of sweat gland duct epithelium; it reabsorbs chloride from sweat lumen back into the body. Active CFTR retains chloride systemically; loss of CFTR (cystic fibrosis LoF) abolishes duct chloride reabsorption, causing chronic chloride depletion into sweat. This chronic sweat chloride loss causes hypochloremia (plasma Cl often <95 mmol/L), with secondary hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis — the pseudo-Bartter syndrome of CF. do(CFTR activity up) → more Cl retained → plasma_chloride higher; do(CFTR LoF) → plasma_chloride falls."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/slc9a3_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_sodium_concentration> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aslc9a3_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_sodium_concentration> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/slc9a3_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_sodium_concentration> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "PMC9356552 (Further delineation SLC9A3-related congenital sodium diarrhea: hyponatremia, DIAR8, OMIM 616868); PMC8819665 (inducible NHE3 knockout mouse: mimics congenital sodium diarrhea — hyponatremia, metabolic acidosis); PMID 35775128 (SLC9A3 LoF: polyhydramnios, hyponatremia, diarrhea). Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (NHE3 is the major intestinal Na+ absorber). | intervention: do(SLC9A3 LoF) — biallelic loss-of-function causing congenital secretory sodium diarrhea (OMIM 616868) | intervention: do(SLC9A3/NHE3 loss-of-function) — biallelic mutations in SLC9A3 causing congenital secretory sodium diarrhea (DIAR8, OMIM 616868) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "SLC9A3 (NHE3) is the dominant apical Na+/H+ exchanger in intestinal epithelial cells (jejunum >> duodenum > ileum), responsible for electroneutral sodium absorption from the gut lumen. NHE3 activity is the principal driver of intestinal Na+ absorption; its activity contributes directly to plasma sodium homeostasis. LoF mutations (congenital secretory sodium diarrhea, DIAR8) abolish intestinal Na+ absorption, causing massive fecal sodium loss, volume contraction, and hyponatremia. do(NHE3 activity up) → more Na+ absorbed → plasma_sodium_concentration rises."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alox12_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/platelet_activation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aalox12_activity%3Apositive%3Aplatelet_activation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alox12_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/platelet_activation> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "PMC3494251 (Investigations of human platelet-type 12-LOX: role of 12-HETE in platelet activation; ML355 inhibition attenuates aggregation); PMID 15142951 (platelet 12-LOX activation via glycoprotein VI, ITAM signaling); Blood 2010 (RUNX1 regulates ALOX12); PMID 33564083 (ALOX12 mutation — dominantly inherited bleeding diathesis: decreased 12-HETE → reduced platelet activation). | intervention: do(ALOX12 activity up, e.g. stimulation) → platelet activation up; do(ALOX12 LoF) → platelet activation impaired | intervention: do(ALOX12 activity modulation) — ALOX12 gain-of-function raises 12-HETE and platelet activation; LoF (dominant bleeding diathesis, PMID 33564083) impairs platelet activation [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ALOX12 (platelet-type 12-lipoxygenase, 12-LOX) converts arachidonic acid to 12(S)-HpETE, reduced to 12(S)-HETE, in platelets. 12(S)-HETE signals through the high-affinity GPCR GPR31 to amplify platelet activation: it potentiates agonist-stimulated Ca2+ signaling, granule secretion, integrin αIIbβ3 activation, and thrombus formation. Pharmacological 12-LOX inhibition (ML355) markedly attenuates human platelet aggregation; Alox12 knockout mice show impaired thrombus formation. do(ALOX12 activity up) → more 12-HETE → platelet_activation up."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tn_antigen_level> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atn_antigen_level%3Anegative%3Agfr> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tn_antigen_level> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "PMC4315396 (DNA methylation in Cosmc, Tn antigen, and pediatric IgAN: aberrant glycosylation of IgA1 is pathogenic); PMC2761879 (C1GALT1C1 mutations in IgA nephropathy: COSMC-dependent Gd-IgA1 drives mesangial damage); MDPI JCM 2021 10(19):4501 (IgAN pathogenesis: 4-hit cascade, Gd-IgA1 → mesangial deposition → GFR decline); PMC10235935 (Germline C1GALT1C1 chaperonopathy: global O-glycosylation defect including Tn antigen elevation). | intervention: do(Tn antigen elevated) — COSMC/C1GALT1C1 LoF raising Gd-IgA1 and glomerular injury | intervention: do(Tn antigen elevated, e.g. COSMC/C1GALT1C1 LoF) — Tn-antigen-driven galactose-deficient IgA1 causes IgA nephropathy with progressive GFR decline"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Elevated Tn antigen (GalNAc-Ser/Thr) on IgA1 hinge-region O-glycans reflects deficient Core 1 galactosylation. Galactose-deficient IgA1 (Gd-IgA1) forms immune complexes that deposit in the glomerular mesangium, activating mesangial cells and complement, producing glomerulonephritis (IgA nephropathy, IgAN). Progressive mesangial damage and glomerulosclerosis reduce GFR, with ~15–20% of IgAN patients reaching ESRD within 10 years. do(Tn antigen level up) → more Gd-IgA1 → mesangial immune deposit → glomerulonephritis → GFR falls."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/c27_3beta_hsd_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bile_acids> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ac27_3beta_hsd_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_bile_acids> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/c27_3beta_hsd_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bile_acids> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "OMIM 607765 (CBAS1: HSD3B7 LoF, congenital bile acid synthesis defect — primary bile acids absent, atypical bile acids in urine); JCEM 2003 PMID 12679481 (molecular genetics of HSD3B7 deficiency in 16 patients: loss of bile acid synthesis, liver disease, Rx cholic acid restores plasma bile acids); PMC9282059 (systematic review of HSD3B7 deficiency: cholestasis, lipid malabsorption, absent primary bile acids in plasma/bile). | intervention: do(HSD3B7 LoF) — biallelic mutations in HSD3B7 gene causing congenital bile acid synthesis defect 1 (OMIM 607765) | intervention: do(HSD3B7/C27-3β-HSD loss-of-function) — biallelic HSD3B7 mutations causing CBAS1 (OMIM 607765) with absent plasma primary bile acids [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "3β-hydroxy-Δ5-C27-steroid dehydrogenase (HSD3B7) catalyzes an early obligate step in primary bile acid synthesis (oxidation/isomerization of 7α-hydroxycholesterol intermediates toward cholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid). HSD3B7 deficiency (congenital bile acid synthesis defect 1, CBAS1) abolishes primary bile acid production; plasma and biliary primary bile acids (CDCA, CA) are severely reduced while atypical 3β-hydroxy-Δ5 bile acids accumulate in urine. More HSD3B7 activity → more primary bile acid synthesis → higher plasma bile acids. do(HSD3B7 activity down) → plasma_bile_acids down."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glyoxalase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aglyoxalase_activity%3Apositive%3Agfr> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glyoxalase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Diabetes 2014 63(1):50 (Critical role of methylglyoxal and glyoxalase 1 in diabetic nephropathy: Glo1 overexpression in rats reduces glomerular volume, podocyte loss, and GFR decline); Diabetologia 2013 PMID 24162587 (Glo1 overexpression attenuates early renal impairment in diabetic rat model); PMID 25818485 (Role of methylglyoxal and glyoxalase system: GLO1 deficiency → MG accumulation → AGE formation → renal and vascular pathology). | intervention: do(GLO1 activity reduced) — pharmacological GLO1 inhibition or increased glycolytic MG flux (diabetic hyperglycaemia) causes glomerular AGE accumulation and GFR decline | intervention: do(glyoxalase/GLO1 activity reduced) — GLO1 deficiency or inhibition allows methylglyoxal accumulation, causing glomerular AGE-mediated nephropathy and GFR decline [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Glyoxalase I (GLO1, encoded by GLO1) catalyzes the rate-limiting detoxification of methylglyoxal (MG), a reactive dicarbonyl byproduct of glycolysis, converting MG + glutathione to S-lactoylglutathione. GLO1 deficiency allows MG to accumulate in plasma and tissues; MG reacts with proteins, lipids, and DNA to form advanced glycation end-products (AGEs), particularly in long-lived glomerular structures (podocytes, mesangium, basement membrane). AGE accumulation in the glomerulus activates RAGE signaling, induces mesangial contraction and oxidative stress, damages podocytes, and reduces filtration surface area, causing a progressive decline in GFR — analogous to diabetic nephropathy (where hyperglycemia similarly elevates MG and reduces GLO1). do(GLO1 activity up) → MG detoxified → less glomerular AGE → GFR preserved."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glutathione_reductase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/red_cell_mass> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aglutathione_reductase_activity%3Apositive%3Ared_cell_mass> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glutathione_reductase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/red_cell_mass> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Orphanet ORPHA:90030 (Hemolytic anemia due to glutathione reductase deficiency — rare autosomal recessive, hemolytic crises on oxidative challenge); ScienceDirect (Familial Deficiency of Glutathione Reductase in Human Blood Cells — hemolysis, oxidative stress); PMID 2195189 (Impaired glutathione metabolism in hemolytic anemia — GSH depletion drives hemolysis). | intervention: do(GSR LoF) — biallelic GSR mutations causing hereditary hemolytic anemia (Orphanet ORPHA:90030) with GSSG accumulation and reduced red cell mass | intervention: do(glutathione_reductase/GSR loss-of-function) — biallelic GSR mutations causing hereditary hemolytic anemia (Orphanet ORPHA:90030) with oxidative hemolysis and reduced red cell mass [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Glutathione reductase (GSR) reduces oxidized glutathione (GSSG) back to reduced glutathione (GSH) in the NADPH-dependent glutathione redox cycle. This is the principal mechanism for regenerating the erythrocyte's main antioxidant, GSH. GSR deficiency causes GSSG accumulation and GSH depletion in red blood cells; compromised antioxidant capacity leaves hemoglobin and membrane proteins vulnerable to oxidative damage, triggering hemolytic crises (especially upon oxidative challenge, e.g. fava bean ingestion). Hereditary complete GSR deficiency causes chronic hemolytic anemia, reducing total red cell mass. do(GSR activity up) → GSH regenerated → antioxidant capacity maintained → red_cell_mass preserved."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lcat_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hdl_cholesterol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alcat_activity%3Apositive%3Ahdl_cholesterol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lcat_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hdl_cholesterol> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Glomset JA. The plasma lecithin:cholesterol acyltransferase reaction. J Lipid Res. 1968;9(2):155-167; OMIM 245900 (familial LCAT deficiency); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, Disorders of Lipoprotein Metabolism; PMC3373316 (LCAT deficiency, HDL-C very low). Intervention: LCAT loss-of-function (OMIM 245900): do(LCAT activity down) -> plasma HDL-cholesterol severely reduced. | intervention: LCAT loss-of-function (familial LCAT deficiency, OMIM 245900)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LCAT (lecithin-cholesterol acyltransferase) esterifies free cholesterol on nascent HDL particles, driving their maturation from small pre-beta HDL3 to large alpha-HDL2; the resulting cholesteryl-ester-enriched core stabilises the HDL particle. LCAT deficiency (familial LCAT deficiency, OMIM 245900; fish-eye disease, OMIM 136120) abolishes HDL maturation, producing a severe fall in plasma HDL-cholesterol with accumulation of abnormal lipid-poor nascent HDL discs."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dhcr7_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_cholesterol_synthesis> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Adhcr7_activity%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_cholesterol_synthesis> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dhcr7_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_cholesterol_synthesis> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Wassif CA et al. Nat Genet. 1998;18:107-110; Waterham HR et al. Ann Hum Genet. 2003;67:516-534 (DHCR7 mutations in SLOS); OMIM 270400 (Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, cholesterol biosynthesis (Kandutsch-Russell pathway: 7-DHC -> cholesterol via DHCR7). Intervention: DHCR7 loss-of-function (Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome, OMIM 270400): do(DHCR7 activity down) -> hepatic cholesterol synthesis severely reduced."
    ; pmo:mechanism "DHCR7 (7-dehydrocholesterol reductase) catalyses the final step of cholesterol biosynthesis (Kandutsch-Russell pathway): reduction of 7-dehydrocholesterol to cholesterol. DHCR7 loss-of-function (Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome, SLOS, OMIM 270400) blocks the last enzymatic step, drastically reducing de novo cholesterol synthesis in the liver and all tissues while 7-DHC and 8-DHC accumulate."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sterol_27_hydroxylase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bile_acids> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asterol_27_hydroxylase_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_bile_acids> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sterol_27_hydroxylase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bile_acids> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Björkhem I et al. J Clin Invest. 1994;94:1226-1234 (CTX: CYP27A1 LoF reduces CDCA and bile acids); OMIM 213700 (cerebrotendinous xanthomatosis, CYP27A1); PMC4264335 (CTX comprehensive review: reduced bile-acid synthesis). Intervention: CYP27A1 loss-of-function (cerebrotendinous xanthomatosis, OMIM 213700): do(CYP27A1 activity down) -> plasma bile acids reduced."
    ; pmo:mechanism "CYP27A1 (sterol 27-hydroxylase) is a mitochondrial cytochrome P450 that catalyses the first step of the acidic (alternative) bile-acid synthesis pathway by 27-hydroxylating cholesterol and bile-acid intermediates. Loss of CYP27A1 (cerebrotendinous xanthomatosis, CTX, OMIM 213700) impairs bile acid synthesis, particularly chenodeoxycholic acid (CDCA), reducing the total bile acid pool and plasma bile acids, while cholestanol and bile alcohols accumulate."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mevalonate_kinase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_cholesterol_synthesis> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amevalonate_kinase_activity%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_cholesterol_synthesis> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mevalonate_kinase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_cholesterol_synthesis> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Hoffmann GF et al. J Inherit Metab Dis. 1993;16:872-879 (mevalonic aciduria, MVK LoF, impaired cholesterol synthesis); OMIM 251170 (mevalonic aciduria); PMC4867845 (urinary mevalonate as diagnostic marker); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, isoprenoid/cholesterol biosynthesis (mevalonate kinase as second committed step). Intervention: MVK loss-of-function (mevalonic aciduria, OMIM 251170): do(MVK activity down) -> hepatic cholesterol synthesis impaired."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Mevalonate kinase (MVK) phosphorylates mevalonate to mevalonate-5-phosphate, the second committed step of the mevalonate/isoprenoid pathway, which is the sole biosynthetic route to cholesterol, dolichol, ubiquinone, and prenylated proteins. MVK deficiency (mevalonic aciduria, OMIM 251170; HIDS, OMIM 260920) blocks the pathway upstream of squalene synthesis, causing mevalonate accumulation (urinary excretion) and severely impaired cholesterol synthesis in all tissues."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/abca1_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hdl_cholesterol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aabca1_activity%3Apositive%3Ahdl_cholesterol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/abca1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hdl_cholesterol> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Bodzioch M et al. Nat Genet. 1999;22:347-351 (Tangier disease = ABCA1 mutations; near-absent HDL-C and apoA-I); OMIM 205400 (Tangier disease); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, Disorders of Lipoprotein Metabolism (ABCA1 as obligate HDL-forming transporter). Intervention: ABCA1 loss-of-function (Tangier disease, OMIM 205400): do(ABCA1 activity down) -> plasma HDL-cholesterol severely reduced."
    ; pmo:mechanism "ABCA1 (ATP-binding cassette transporter A1) mediates the cellular efflux of phospholipids and free cholesterol to lipid-poor apolipoprotein A-I (apoA-I), forming nascent HDL — the essential first step of reverse cholesterol transport. Without ABCA1-mediated lipidation, apoA-I is rapidly catabolised before it can acquire cholesterol, and virtually no HDL forms. Tangier disease (ABCA1 LoF, OMIM 205400) produces near-total HDL-cholesterol deficiency (<5 mg/dL) and massive cholesterol accumulation in tissue macrophages."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/msmo1_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_cholesterol_synthesis> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amsmo1_activity%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_cholesterol_synthesis> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/msmo1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_cholesterol_synthesis> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Boyden LM et al. Am J Hum Genet. 2014;94:309-315 (MSMO1/SC4MOL mutations; C4-methylsterol accumulation, cholesterol synthesis defect); OMIM 616834 (MCCPD, SC4MOL deficiency); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, post-squalene cholesterol biosynthesis (C4-demethylation steps). Intervention: MSMO1 loss-of-function (C4-methylsterol oxidase deficiency, OMIM 616834): do(MSMO1 activity down) -> hepatic cholesterol synthesis impaired."
    ; pmo:mechanism "MSMO1 (methylsterol monooxygenase 1; SC4MOL) catalyses the first oxidative step in the C4-demethylation sequence of post-squalene cholesterol biosynthesis, acting on 4,4-dimethylsterols (including lanosterol and subsequent intermediates). Loss of MSMO1 (C4-methylsterol oxidase deficiency / MCCPD, OMIM 616834) blocks the pathway between lanosterol and zymosterol, causing accumulation of 4-methylsterols and 4,4-dimethylsterols and a commensurate deficit in hepatic cholesterol synthesis."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_paf> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/platelet_activation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_paf%3Apositive%3Aplatelet_activation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_paf> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/platelet_activation> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Shimizu T et al. Physiol Rev. 2000;80:1669-1699 (PAF receptor on platelets: Gq pathway, Ca2+ mobilisation, platelet activation); PMC507371 (PAF-AH deficiency near active site; elevated plasma PAF); PMC2674695 (emerging roles of PAF-AH: deficiency raises PAF and inflammatory/platelet risk). Intervention: PAF-AH loss-of-function (PLA2G7, V279F; PMC507371): do(PAF-AH activity down) -> plasma PAF elevated -> platelet activation increased. | intervention: PAF-AH loss-of-function (PLA2G7 V279F; PAF-AH deficiency)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Platelet-activating factor (PAF; 1-alkyl-2-acetyl-sn-glycero-3-phosphocholine) binds its cognate G-protein-coupled receptor (PAFR) on platelets, triggering Gq-mediated phospholipase C activation, IP3-dependent Ca2+ mobilisation, PKC activation, and release of arachidonic acid/thromboxane A2, resulting in full platelet activation (shape change, granule secretion, GPIIb/IIIa activation). PAF-AH deficiency (PLA2G7 LoF; common V279F in East Asian populations) impairs PAF degradation, raising plasma PAF and thereby increasing platelet activation and prothrombotic risk."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/scd_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_lipogenesis> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ascd_activity%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_lipogenesis> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/scd_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_lipogenesis> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Ntambi JM et al. PNAS. 2002;99:11482-11487 (SCD1 knockout mice: reduced adiposity and hepatic lipogenesis); Miyazaki M et al. J Biol Chem. 2001;276:39455-39461 (SCD1 LoF: reduced hepatic lipogenesis and VLDL TG); PMC12173144 (SCD1 deficiency reduces liver lipogenesis). Intervention: SCD loss-of-function: do(SCD activity down) -> hepatic de novo lipogenesis reduced. | intervention: SCD1 loss-of-function (mouse model; human SCD1 deficiency leads to dyslipidaemia and reduced MUFA synthesis)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Stearoyl-CoA desaturase (SCD/SCD1) introduces the cis-delta-9 double bond in saturated fatty acyl-CoAs to produce monounsaturated fatty acids (principally oleoyl-CoA from stearoyl-CoA and palmitoleoyl-CoA from palmitoyl-CoA). MUFAs are the obligate substrates for triacylglycerol, phospholipid, and cholesteryl ester synthesis; SCD1 activity is therefore a rate-controlling step for de novo hepatic lipogenesis. SCD1 deficiency in mice reduces liver TG, VLDL secretion, and de novo lipogenesis; conversely, SCD1 upregulation (e.g. high-carbohydrate diet, insulin, SREBP-1c) drives hepatic steatosis."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dhcr24_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_cholesterol_synthesis> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Adhcr24_activity%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_cholesterol_synthesis> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dhcr24_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_cholesterol_synthesis> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Waterham HR et al. Am J Hum Genet. 2001;69:685-694 (desmosterolosis; DHCR24 LoF causes desmosterol accumulation and cholesterol deficit); OMIM 602398 (desmosterolosis); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, cholesterol biosynthesis (Bloch pathway: desmosterol -> cholesterol via DHCR24). Intervention: DHCR24 loss-of-function (desmosterolosis, OMIM 602398): do(DHCR24 activity down) -> hepatic cholesterol synthesis impaired."
    ; pmo:mechanism "DHCR24 (Delta(24)-sterol reductase / 3-beta-hydroxysterol Delta24-reductase) reduces the C24=C25 double bond of desmosterol to produce cholesterol, the final step of the Bloch pathway of cholesterol biosynthesis. DHCR24 loss-of-function (desmosterolosis, OMIM 602398) blocks this terminal step, causing desmosterol to accumulate to high levels in plasma and tissues while hepatic cholesterol synthesis is severely impaired."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ebp_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_cholesterol_synthesis> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aebp_activity%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_cholesterol_synthesis> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ebp_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_cholesterol_synthesis> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Braverman N et al. Hum Mol Genet. 1999;8:1773-1780 (EBP mutations cause X-linked CDPX2; 8(9)-cholestenol accumulation and cholesterol synthesis defect); OMIM 302960 (CDPX2, EBP LoF); PMC11700059 (novel EBP variant, MEND syndrome; impaired cholesterol biosynthesis). Intervention: EBP loss-of-function (CDPX2, OMIM 302960): do(EBP activity down) -> hepatic cholesterol synthesis impaired. | intervention: EBP loss-of-function (X-linked chondrodysplasia punctata type 2, OMIM 302960)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "EBP (emopamil-binding protein; 3-beta-hydroxysteroid-Delta(8),Delta(7)-isomerase) catalyses the isomerisation of Delta8-sterols (principally 8(9)-cholestenol) to Delta7-sterols (lathosterol) in the post-lanosterol cholesterol biosynthesis pathway. EBP loss-of-function (X-linked chondrodysplasia punctata type 2 / Conradi-Hunermann-Happle syndrome, OMIM 302960; MEND syndrome in males) causes accumulation of 8(9)-cholestenol and 8-dehydrocholesterol in plasma and severely impairs hepatic cholesterol synthesis downstream of the block."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nsdhl_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_cholesterol_synthesis> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ansdhl_activity%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_cholesterol_synthesis> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nsdhl_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_cholesterol_synthesis> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "König A et al. Am J Hum Genet. 2000;66:1210-1218 (NSDHL mutations in CHILD syndrome; monomethylsterol and C4-sterol accumulation, cholesterol synthesis defect); OMIM 308050 (CHILD syndrome, X-linked, NSDHL LoF); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, post-squalene sterol demethylation. Intervention: NSDHL loss-of-function (CHILD syndrome, OMIM 308050): do(NSDHL activity down) -> hepatic cholesterol synthesis impaired."
    ; pmo:mechanism "NSDHL (sterol-4-alpha-carboxylate 3-dehydrogenase, decarboxylating) catalyses the second step of C4-methylsterol demethylation in post-squalene cholesterol biosynthesis, acting on 4-monomethylsterol intermediates after MSMO1 removes the first methyl group. NSDHL loss-of-function (CHILD syndrome, OMIM 308050; CK syndrome in males) blocks the pathway, causing monomethylsterol accumulation and impaired hepatic cholesterol synthesis, with Hedgehog-pathway disruption contributing to severe developmental defects."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cyp7a1_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bile_acids> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acyp7a1_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_bile_acids> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cyp7a1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bile_acids> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Schwarz M et al. J Clin Invest. 1998;102:1690-1697 (CYP7A1 deficiency in humans: reduced bile acids, raised LDL-C); OMIM 603540 (bile acid synthesis defect 1, CYP7A1); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, Ch.26 (CYP7A1 rate-limiting for bile acid synthesis). Intervention: CYP7A1 loss-of-function (bile acid synthesis defect type 1, OMIM 603540): do(CYP7A1 activity down) -> plasma bile acids reduced. | intervention: CYP7A1 loss-of-function (bile acid synthesis defect congenital type 1, OMIM 603540)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CYP7A1 (cholesterol 7-alpha-hydroxylase) is the rate-limiting enzyme of the classical (neutral) bile-acid synthesis pathway, converting cholesterol to 7-alpha-hydroxycholesterol. CYP7A1 controls the primary route of hepatic cholesterol catabolism; its activity determines the size of the bile-acid pool and therefore plasma bile-acid concentration. CYP7A1 deficiency (bile acid synthesis defect congenital type 1, OMIM 603540) reduces the bile-acid pool, decreases fecal and plasma bile acids, and raises plasma LDL-cholesterol due to impaired hepatic LDL-receptor upregulation."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fads1_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_leukotriene_c4> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afads1_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_leukotriene_c4> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fads1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_leukotriene_c4> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "PMC3371240 (Fads1 knockout mouse): 'systemic disruption of Fads1 reciprocally altered DGLA and AA in mouse tissues, resulting in profound increase in 1-series and concurrent decrease in 2-series prostaglandins'; AA-dependent 5-LOX/LTC4 synthesis reduced. The Medical Biochemistry Page (eicosanoid metabolism): AA -> 5-LOX -> LTA4 -> LTC4. OMIM FADS1 (HGNC:3574). Arteriosclerosis Thrombosis Vascular Biology doi:10.1161/ATVBAHA.117.309660 (FADS1 and lipid mediators). | intervention: do(fads1_activity=0) = FADS1 LoF; reduces plasma arachidonic acid, reducing substrate for 5-LOX, lowering plasma leukotriene C4"
    ; pmo:mechanism "FADS1 (delta-5 desaturase) is the sole mammalian enzyme that converts dihomo-gamma-linolenic acid (DGLA) to arachidonic acid (AA; 20:4 n-6). AA is the obligate substrate for 5-lipoxygenase (5-LOX) -> leukotriene A4 -> LTC4 (via glutathione transferase). FADS1 LoF reduces plasma AA, decreasing AA available for 5-LOX, thereby reducing LTC4 synthesis. Conversely, higher FADS1 activity raises AA and thus LTC4. The edge fads1_activity ->(+)-> plasma_leukotriene_c4 reflects the substrate-supply bottleneck: the rate-limiting step for LTC4 in most cell types is AA availability, which is set by FADS1-dependent desaturation of the dietary linoleic-acid precursor."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fasn_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_lipogenesis> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afasn_activity%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_lipogenesis> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fasn_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_lipogenesis> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "ScienceDirect (Fatty acid synthase and liver TG metabolism, 2011): 'FAS is solely responsible for synthesizing palmitate'; 'FAS participates in liver TG metabolism by contributing de novo synthesized lipids for storage and secretion'. PMC2897101 (FASN in NAFLD): FASN overexpression drives steatosis via DNL. Nature Communications doi:10.1038/s41467-023-44364-y (fatty acid synthesis and PUFA use). NCT02948569 (FASN inhibitor trial, DNL reduction). Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, de novo lipogenesis chapter. | intervention: do(fasn_activity UP) raises hepatic de novo lipogenesis flux; do(fasn_activity DOWN/LoF) reduces palmitate synthesis and hepatic DNL"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Fatty acid synthase (FASN, PR:000007348) catalyzes the committed, rate-determining step of hepatic de novo lipogenesis (DNL): serial condensation of malonyl-CoA units onto acetyl-CoA to produce palmitate (C16:0). FASN is the only mammalian enzyme capable of synthesizing palmitate de novo; hepatic_lipogenesis is defined as the flux from acetyl-CoA to fatty acids/triglycerides, which is proportional to FASN activity. Raised FASN expression (e.g. in insulin-stimulated or NAFLD states) directly increases DNL flux; FASN inhibitors (e.g. TVB-2640) reduce hepatic DNL in clinical trials. The causal edge is the direct enzyme-product relationship: do(fasn_activity UP) -> hepatic_lipogenesis UP."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/vlcad_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_ketogenesis> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Avlcad_activity%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_ketogenesis> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/vlcad_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_ketogenesis> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 'Inherited disorders of fatty acid metabolism' — VLCAD deficiency (OMIM 201475): fasting hypoketotic hypoglycaemia with elevated C14-C18 acylcarnitines; Leslie & Cohen, Paediatrics in Review 2010; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, fatty-acid catabolism chapter. | intervention: do(VLCAD/ACADVL loss-of-function) — mitochondrial beta-oxidation of VLCFA/LCFA impaired"
    ; pmo:mechanism "VLCAD (ACADVL) catalyzes the first step of mitochondrial beta-oxidation for very-long-chain (C14-C20) and long-chain fatty acids; VLCAD deficiency impairs LCFA oxidation, reducing hepatic acetyl-CoA supply and thus hepatic ketogenesis — the hallmark is hypoketotic hypoglycaemia on fasting."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cpt2_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_ketogenesis> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acpt2_activity%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_ketogenesis> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cpt2_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_ketogenesis> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 'Inherited disorders of fatty acid metabolism' — CPT-II deficiency (OMIM 255110): elevated C16/C18 acylcarnitines, hypoketotic hypoglycaemia, hepatomegaly; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, carnitine shuttle mechanism. | intervention: do(CPT2 loss-of-function) — carnitine cycle blocked, LCFA cannot reach mitochondrial matrix"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CPT2 regenerates long-chain acyl-CoA from acylcarnitine at the inner mitochondrial membrane, completing the carnitine cycle and enabling LCFA entry into the beta-oxidation spiral; CPT-II deficiency blocks LCFA import into the mitochondrial matrix, impairs beta-oxidation and hepatic acetyl-CoA supply, reducing ketogenesis and causing hypoketotic hypoglycaemia."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/short_chain_acyl_coa_dehydrogenase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_ketogenesis> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ashort_chain_acyl_coa_dehydrogenase_activity%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_ketogenesis> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/short_chain_acyl_coa_dehydrogenase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_ketogenesis> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 'Inherited disorders of fatty acid metabolism' — SCAD deficiency (OMIM 201470): elevated butyrylcarnitine (C4) and urinary ethylmalonic acid; Gregersen N et al., J Inherit Metab Dis 2008; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, beta-oxidation spiral. | intervention: do(SCAD/ACADS loss-of-function) — short-chain fatty acid oxidation impaired, butyryl-CoA accumulates"
    ; pmo:mechanism "SCAD (ACADS) catalyzes the first step of mitochondrial beta-oxidation of short-chain (C4-C6) acyl-CoA esters, producing acetyl-CoA that feeds the hepatic ketogenesis pathway; SCAD deficiency causes butyryl-CoA and C4 acylcarnitine accumulation with a secondary reduction in acetyl-CoA available for ketogenesis. Accumulating butyryl-CoA also inhibits pyruvate dehydrogenase complex, further impairing hepatic energy metabolism."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/octn2_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_ketogenesis> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aoctn2_activity%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_ketogenesis> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/octn2_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_ketogenesis> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 'Disorders of fatty acid metabolism' — primary carnitine deficiency (OMIM 212140): systemic carnitine deficiency, hypoketotic hypoglycaemia, cardiomyopathy; Koizumi A et al., Am J Hum Genet 1999; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, carnitine transport. | intervention: do(OCTN2/SLC22A5 loss-of-function) — systemic carnitine deficiency, LCFA cannot enter mitochondria"
    ; pmo:mechanism "OCTN2 (SLC22A5) is the high-affinity sodium-dependent carnitine transporter responsible for renal carnitine reabsorption and cellular carnitine uptake. OCTN2 deficiency causes systemic primary carnitine deficiency: plasma, hepatic and muscle carnitine levels fall, impairing the carnitine cycle (CPT1/CACT/CPT2) and blocking long-chain fatty acid import into mitochondria; this impairs hepatic beta-oxidation and ketogenesis, producing hypoketotic hypoglycaemia on fasting."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lchad_mtp_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_ketogenesis> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alchad_mtp_activity%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_ketogenesis> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lchad_mtp_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_ketogenesis> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 'Inherited disorders of fatty acid metabolism' — LCHAD/MTP deficiency (OMIM 600890): hypoketotic hypoglycaemia, elevated 3-hydroxy-long-chain acylcarnitines; Ibdah JA et al., N Engl J Med 1999; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, mitochondrial trifunctional protein. | intervention: do(LCHAD/HADHA loss-of-function) — long-chain hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenation blocked, LCFA oxidation impaired"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LCHAD (3-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase domain of mitochondrial trifunctional protein, HADHA) catalyzes the third step of mitochondrial beta-oxidation for long-chain fatty acids; LCHAD/MTP deficiency impairs LCFA oxidation, reduces hepatic acetyl-CoA supply, and causes hypoketotic hypoglycaemia. 3-hydroxy long-chain acylcarnitines (C16-OH, C18-OH) accumulate as diagnostic markers."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ivd_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aivd_activity%3Apositive%3Acellular_atp_level> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ivd_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 'Organic acidaemias' — isovaleric acidemia (OMIM 243500): isovalerylcarnitine (C5) elevation, metabolic acidosis, hyperammonaemia, neurological crisis; Tanaka K, J Biol Chem 1968; Vockley J et al., Mol Genet Metab 2012; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, leucine catabolism. | intervention: do(IVD loss-of-function) — isovaleryl-CoA accumulates, mitochondrial TCA cycle/ETC inhibited"
    ; pmo:mechanism "IVD (isovaleryl-CoA dehydrogenase) catalyzes the third step of leucine catabolism, converting isovaleryl-CoA to 3-methylcrotonyl-CoA. IVD deficiency causes isovaleric acidemia: isovaleryl-CoA and isovalerate accumulate, inhibiting mitochondrial enzymes including pyruvate carboxylase and the alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase complex, impairing the TCA cycle and oxidative phosphorylation and reducing intracellular ATP levels. Episodes of metabolic decompensation present with encephalopathy and organic acidaemia."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/abcd1_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aabcd1_activity%3Apositive%3Acortisol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/abcd1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Moser HW et al., Neurology 2005;64:1376-88 (adrenal insufficiency in X-ALD, OMIM 300100); Engelen M et al., Lancet Neurol 2012;11:422-34 (X-ALD disease review); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 'X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy'; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, peroxisomal fatty acid transport. | intervention: do(ABCD1 loss-of-function) — VLCFA accumulate in adrenal cortex, steroidogenesis impaired, adrenal insufficiency"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ABCD1 (adrenoleukodystrophy protein/ALDP) transports very-long-chain acyl-CoA esters into peroxisomes for beta-oxidation; ABCD1 deficiency (X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy) causes VLCFA accumulation in adrenal cortex zona fasciculata cells, disrupting steroidogenic membrane function and eventually destroying adrenal cortex tissue, causing primary adrenal insufficiency with low plasma cortisol — present in 70-80% of X-ALD patients."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mut_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_respiratory_chain_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amut_activity%3Apositive%3Amitochondrial_respiratory_chain_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mut_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_respiratory_chain_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Kolker S et al., Hum Mol Genet 2004;13:2501-9 (methylmalonate inhibits succinate dehydrogenase, complex II); Mc Guire PJ et al., Mol Genet Metab 2009 (MMA mitochondrial dysfunction); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 'Organic acidaemias' (MMA, OMIM 251000); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, TCA cycle and ETC. | intervention: do(MMUT loss-of-function) — methylmalonyl-CoA/methylmalonate accumulate, complex II and TCA cycle inhibited"
    ; pmo:mechanism "MMUT (methylmalonyl-CoA mutase) converts methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA for TCA cycle entry. MMUT deficiency causes methylmalonic acidemia (MMA): methylmalonate and methylmalonyl-CoA accumulate and directly inhibit succinate dehydrogenase (respiratory complex II), as well as the alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase complex, impairing the TCA cycle and electron transport chain activity. Secondary mitochondrial dysfunction is a key contributor to the renal and neurological damage in MMA."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mlycd_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_ketogenesis> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amlycd_activity%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_ketogenesis> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mlycd_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_ketogenesis> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 'Inherited disorders of fatty acid metabolism' — malonyl-CoA decarboxylase deficiency (OMIM 248360); Santer R et al., J Inherit Metab Dis 2003; Wightman PJ et al., Hum Genet 2003; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, malonyl-CoA as CPT1 inhibitor and fatty acid synthesis regulation. | intervention: do(MLYCD loss-of-function) — malonyl-CoA accumulates, CPT1 inhibited, LCFA beta-oxidation and ketogenesis impaired"
    ; pmo:mechanism "MLYCD (malonyl-CoA decarboxylase) degrades malonyl-CoA to acetyl-CoA and CO2. MLYCD deficiency causes malonyl-CoA accumulation; elevated malonyl-CoA allosterically inhibits CPT1 (carnitine palmitoyltransferase 1), the rate-limiting enzyme of the carnitine cycle, blocking LCFA import into the mitochondrial matrix and impairing beta-oxidation. The resulting impairment of hepatic fatty acid oxidation reduces acetyl-CoA supply for ketogenesis, producing hypoketotic hypoglycaemia, as well as cardiomyopathy and skeletal myopathy."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_phytanic_acid> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_total_lipids> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_phytanic_acid%3Apositive%3Aplasma_total_lipids> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_phytanic_acid> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_total_lipids> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Refsum disease (PHYH deficiency, OMIM 266500): plasma phytanic acid levels rise from undetectable to 10-1000 mg/L and are distributed across VLDL (16%), LDL (35%), and HDL (14%) fractions (Eldjarn et al. 1966 Lancet; transport on lipoproteins: PMID:10070615). Plasma phytanic acid accumulation contributes to elevated total plasma lipid concentration in affected patients. Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 'Disorders of peroxisome metabolism', Refsum disease. | intervention: PHYH LoF elevating plasma_phytanic_acid (do(plasma_phytanic_acid) increased) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Phytanic acid is a 3-methyl-branched saturated fatty acid that cannot undergo beta-oxidation without prior alpha-oxidation by PHYH. In Refsum disease (PHYH deficiency, OMIM 266500), phytanic acid is not degraded and accumulates to high concentrations in plasma, where it circulates in all major lipoprotein fractions (VLDL, LDL, HDL). The elevated phytanic acid directly and additively raises total plasma lipid concentration, as phytanic acid is itself a lipid component of plasma."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/abat_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_alpha_ketoglutarate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aabat_activity%3Anegative%3Acsf_alpha_ketoglutarate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/abat_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_alpha_ketoglutarate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "GABA-transaminase deficiency (ABAT LoF, OMIM 613163): Jaeken et al. (1984) Neuropediatrics 15:165-168 (elevated CSF GABA in ABAT deficiency); Blau et al., Physician's Guide to Inherited Metabolic Diseases (GABA transamination uses alpha-ketoglutarate as amino-acceptor; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (GABA catabolism: GABA-T reaction stoichiometry). Intervention: do(ABAT LoF). | intervention: genetic loss-of-function of ABAT (GABA-transaminase deficiency, OMIM 613163)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "4-Aminobutyrate aminotransferase (ABAT/GABA-transaminase) catalyzes GABA + alpha-ketoglutarate -> succinic semialdehyde + glutamate, consuming alpha-KG as the amino-acceptor. Active ABAT consumes CSF alpha-KG; ABAT deficiency (GABA-transaminase deficiency, OMIM 613163) spares alpha-KG from this transamination, raising the CSF alpha-KG pool."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_n_acetylaspartate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_organic_acids> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aurinary_n_acetylaspartate%3Apositive%3Aurinary_organic_acids> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_n_acetylaspartate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_organic_acids> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Canavan disease (ASPA deficiency, OMIM 271900): Matalon et al. (1988) Am J Med Genet 29:463-471 (pathognomonic N-acetylaspartic aciduria); Saudubray, Inborn Metabolic Diseases (urinary NAA up to 60-fold above normal in Canavan disease, detectable on organic acid screen). Intervention: do(ASPA LoF). | intervention: genetic loss-of-function of ASPA (Canavan disease, OMIM 271900) raising urinary NAA"
    ; pmo:mechanism "N-acetylaspartate (NAA) is an N-acetylated amino acid derivative — a low-molecular-weight organic acid that is quantified on urinary organic acid screens (GC-MS). In Canavan disease (ASPA deficiency, OMIM 271900), NAA accumulates massively in brain and overflows into blood and urine, contributing directly to elevated urinary organic acid excretion. A higher urinary NAA level is a component of the total urinary organic-acid load."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pdh_complex_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_lactate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apdh_complex_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_lactate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pdh_complex_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_lactate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "PDH deficiency (OMIM 312170): Stacpoole (1986) Metabolism 35:830-843 (lactic acidosis in PDH deficiency); Robinson (2006) Mitochondrion 6:257-263 (PDH deficiency and lactic acidosis); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (pyruvate dehydrogenase reaction and lactic acidosis in PDH deficiency). Intervention: do(PDH complex LoF). | intervention: genetic loss-of-function of PDH complex (PDH deficiency, OMIM 312170) causing lactic acidosis"
    ; pmo:mechanism "The pyruvate dehydrogenase complex (PDH) irreversibly converts pyruvate to acetyl-CoA; when PDH activity is reduced, pyruvate that cannot enter the TCA cycle is instead reduced to lactate by lactate dehydrogenase (LDH). PDH deficiency (PDHA1 or PDHB mutations, OMIM 312170) causes lactic acidosis — elevated plasma and CSF lactate — because the pyruvate-to-acetyl-CoA flux is impaired and lactate accumulates. Active PDH reduces plasma lactate by clearing pyruvate into the TCA cycle."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pnmt_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/epinephrine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apnmt_activity%3Apositive%3Aepinephrine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pnmt_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/epinephrine> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (epinephrine biosynthesis: norepinephrine -PNMT-> epinephrine, last step in catecholamine pathway); Wurtman & Axelrod (1966) Sci Am 214:100-106 (PNMT induction by adrenal cortex; PNMT controls adrenal epinephrine); Blau et al., Physician's Guide to Inherited Metabolic Diseases (catecholamine biosynthesis enzymes and CSF metabolite patterns). Intervention: do(PNMT modulation). | intervention: perturbation or genetic modulation of PNMT activity controlling adrenal epinephrine synthesis"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Phenylethanolamine N-methyltransferase (PNMT) catalyzes the final step of catecholamine biosynthesis: N-methylation of norepinephrine to epinephrine in adrenal medullary chromaffin cells and PNMT-positive neurons (C1-C3 brainstem). PNMT activity directly controls adrenal epinephrine synthesis and secretion; higher PNMT activity yields more epinephrine in plasma. PNMT deficiency or suppression (e.g., in pheochromocytoma-associated NE excess) causes low plasma epinephrine."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/folr1_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_5hiaa> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afolr1_activity%3Apositive%3Acsf_5hiaa> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/folr1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_5hiaa> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Cerebral folate deficiency (FOLR1 deficiency/antibodies, OMIM 613068): Ramaekers et al. (2004) Brain 127:1360-1380 (FOLR1 deficiency causes low CSF 5-MTHF, low CSF 5-HIAA and HVA); Blau et al., Physician's Guide to Inherited Metabolic Diseases (cerebral folate deficiency: low CSF 5-MTHF associated with low BH4, 5-HIAA, HVA). Intervention: do(FOLR1 LoF). | intervention: genetic loss-of-function or autoantibody blockade of FOLR1 (cerebral folate deficiency, OMIM 613068)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Folate receptor alpha (FOLR1) mediates the transport of 5-methyltetrahydrofolate (5-MTHF) across the choroid plexus into cerebrospinal fluid. FOLR1 deficiency or blocking autoantibodies (cerebral folate deficiency, OMIM 613068) causes markedly low CSF 5-MTHF, which impairs folate-dependent regeneration of tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4) and disrupts the methylation cycle. BH4 is the obligatory cofactor for tryptophan hydroxylase (serotonin biosynthesis); reduced BH4 lowers serotonin production and CSF 5-HIAA. Active FOLR1 maintains adequate CSF 5-MTHF -> BH4 -> serotonin -> 5-HIAA."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ornithine_aminotransferase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_alpha_ketoglutarate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aornithine_aminotransferase_activity%3Anegative%3Acsf_alpha_ketoglutarate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ornithine_aminotransferase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_alpha_ketoglutarate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Gyrate atrophy (OAT deficiency, OMIM 258870): Valle & Simell (2001) in Scriver's OMMBID (OAT reaction: ornithine + alpha-KG -> P5C + glutamate, consumes alpha-KG); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (OAT as mitochondrial aminotransferase using alpha-KG); Blau et al., Physician's Guide to Inherited Metabolic Diseases (OAT deficiency: hyperornithinemia, impaired P5C/glutamate/proline synthesis). Intervention: do(OAT LoF). | intervention: genetic loss-of-function of OAT (gyrate atrophy of the choroid and retina, OMIM 258870)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Ornithine aminotransferase (OAT) catalyzes the reversible transamination of L-ornithine using alpha-ketoglutarate as the amino-acceptor: ornithine + alpha-KG -> pyrroline-5-carboxylate (P5C) + glutamate, consuming alpha-KG. Active OAT depletes the alpha-KG pool; OAT deficiency (gyrate atrophy of choroid and retina, OMIM 258870) spares alpha-KG from this transamination, allowing the CSF alpha-KG pool to rise. The reciprocal: do(OAT LoF) -> elevated ornithine AND elevated alpha-KG."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cystinosin_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acystinosin_activity%3Apositive%3Agfr> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cystinosin_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Nephropathic cystinosis (CTNS deficiency, OMIM 219800): Gahl et al. (2002) N Engl J Med 347:111-121 (cystinosis: lysosomal cystine accumulation -> Fanconi syndrome -> progressive renal failure, GFR decline); Town et al. (1998) Nat Genet 18:319-324 (CTNS cloning: cystinosin as lysosomal cystine transporter); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (cystinosis: renal Fanconi syndrome, end-stage renal disease). Intervention: do(CTNS LoF). | intervention: genetic loss-of-function of CTNS (nephropathic cystinosis, OMIM 219800)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Cystinosin (CTNS) is the lysosomal cystine transporter that exports cystine from lysosomes to the cytoplasm for further metabolism. Cystinosin deficiency (nephropathic cystinosis, OMIM 219800) causes cystine to accumulate in lysosomes of renal proximal tubular cells, leading to proximal tubular dysfunction (Fanconi syndrome), progressive tubular and glomerular damage, and chronic kidney disease with declining GFR. Active cystinosin maintains lysosomal cystine homeostasis and preserves renal tubular and glomerular function, sustaining GFR."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/d2hgdh_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_alpha_ketoglutarate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ad2hgdh_activity%3Apositive%3Acsf_alpha_ketoglutarate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/d2hgdh_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_alpha_ketoglutarate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "D-2-hydroxyglutaric aciduria type I (D2HGDH deficiency, OMIM 600721): Struys et al. (2005) Am J Hum Genet 76:358-360 (D2HGDH catalyzes D-2-HG -> alpha-KG; deficiency -> D-2-HG accumulation in CSF and urine); Kranendijk et al. (2012) J Inherit Metab Dis 35:571-587 (D2HGDH reaction: D-2-HG oxidation to alpha-KG). Intervention: do(D2HGDH LoF). | intervention: genetic loss-of-function of D2HGDH (D-2-hydroxyglutaric aciduria type I, OMIM 600721)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "D-2-hydroxyglutarate dehydrogenase (D2HGDH) catalyzes the FAD-dependent oxidation of D-2-hydroxyglutarate (D-2-HG) to alpha-ketoglutarate (alpha-KG): D-2-HG + FAD -> alpha-KG + FADH2. Active D2HGDH produces alpha-KG from the D-2-HG substrate, raising the intracellular and CSF alpha-KG pool. D2HGDH deficiency (D-2-hydroxyglutaric aciduria type I, OMIM 600721) blocks this conversion, causing D-2-HG accumulation and simultaneously reducing alpha-KG production from this pathway."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/purine_nucleoside_phosphorylase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/t_cell_immune_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apurine_nucleoside_phosphorylase_activity%3Apositive%3At_cell_immune_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/purine_nucleoside_phosphorylase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/t_cell_immune_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Markert et al., Semin Hematol 1998 (PNP deficiency OMIM 613179: deoxyguanosine accumulation -> dGTP -> T-cell death); Cohen et al., J Clin Invest 1978 (PMC372283: elevated dGTP in PNP-deficient T-cells); Blanco et al., J Exp Med 2000 (PMC2193200: mitochondrial DNA damage mechanism in PNP-deficient T-cells). MedlinePlus Genetics PNP deficiency. | intervention: do(PNP LoF, OMIM 613179): biallelic PNP mutations abolish PNP activity; deoxyguanosine not phosphorolysed; dGTP accumulates; T-cells die; t_cell_immune_activity falls. | intervention: do(PNP LoF, biallelic PNP mutations, OMIM 613179)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "PNP (purine nucleoside phosphorylase) phosphorolyses inosine and guanosine to hypoxanthine/guanine; loss of PNP (PNP deficiency, OMIM 613179) causes accumulation of deoxyguanosine -> dGTP in T lymphocytes. Excess dGTP inhibits ribonucleotide reductase and impairs mitochondrial DNA repair, triggering T-cell apoptosis and severe combined T-cell immunodeficiency. Higher PNP activity prevents dGTP accumulation, preserving T-cell viability and immune function. PNP deficiency is the prototypic model for dGTP-mediated T-cell killing."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thymidine_phosphorylase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_atp_synthase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Athymidine_phosphorylase_activity%3Apositive%3Amitochondrial_atp_synthase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thymidine_phosphorylase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_atp_synthase_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Pontarin et al., PLOS Genet 2011 (PMC3069123: limited dCTP availability explains mtDNA depletion in MNGIE: thymidine/dUrd accumulation -> imbalanced mitochondrial dNTP pool -> mtDNA depletion); Marti et al., Ann Neurol 2002 (MNGIE biochemistry: thymidine and deoxyuridine elevated, lactic acidosis, mitochondrial myopathy); OMIM 603041 (MtDNA depletion syndrome 1, MNGIE type). | intervention: do(TYMP LoF, OMIM 603041): biallelic TYMP mutations -> thymidine + dUrd accumulate -> mtDNA depletion -> mitochondrial ATP synthase complex assembly impaired. | intervention: do(TYMP LoF, MNGIE, OMIM 603041) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "TYMP (thymidine phosphorylase) catabolises thymidine and deoxyuridine to thymine and uracil. TYMP LoF (MNGIE, OMIM 603041) causes systemic accumulation of thymidine and deoxyuridine, creating severe imbalance in mitochondrial dNTP pools (dTTP excess, depleted dCTP). The unbalanced mtDNA precursor pool causes mtDNA depletion and multiple mtDNA deletions in affected tissues, impairing the structural integrity of the mitochondrial respiratory chain (ETC complexes I-V). Impaired ETC assembly specifically reduces Complex V (ATP synthase) activity and overall OXPHOS-coupled ATP production. Higher TYMP activity maintains balanced dNTP pools, preserving mtDNA integrity and mitochondrial ATP synthase function."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aicar_transformylase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aaicar_transformylase_activity%3Apositive%3Acellular_atp_level> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aicar_transformylase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Ramond et al., JIMD 2020 (three ATIC deficiency cases: severe neurological phenotype, AICAR and SAICAR accumulate, de novo purine synthesis blocked); Marie et al., Am J Hum Genet 2004 (OMIM 608688: ATIC deficiency causes neurologically devastating phenotype; de novo purine pathway impaired). Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (de novo purine synthesis: ATIC produces IMP, the precursor of AMP/ATP). | intervention: do(ATIC LoF, OMIM 608688): AICAR not converted to IMP; de novo purine synthesis blocked; cellular ATP pool reduced. | intervention: do(ATIC LoF, AICA-ribosiduria, OMIM 608688) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ATIC (bifunctional AICAR transformylase / IMP cyclohydrolase) catalyses the final two steps of de novo purine biosynthesis: AICAR -> FAICAR -> IMP. IMP is the immediate precursor of AMP and GMP, and ultimately of the intracellular adenine nucleotide pool (AMP -> ADP -> ATP). ATIC LoF (AICA-ribosiduria, OMIM 608688) blocks de novo purine synthesis at this penultimate step, preventing IMP formation from the de novo pathway and reducing the cellular adenine nucleotide pool; the accumulated AICAR is excreted in urine rather than converted to ATP. Higher ATIC activity increases de novo IMP production, supporting the cellular ATP pool."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adsl_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aadsl_activity%3Apositive%3Acellular_atp_level> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adsl_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Jaeken & Van den Berghe, Science 1984 (original ADSL deficiency description; succinyladenosine and SAICAr accumulate); Van den Berghe et al., Adv Exp Med Biol 1998 (ADSL role in the purine nucleotide cycle: SAMP->AMP step; ADSL deficiency impairs AMP regeneration); PMC4341013 (Adenylosuccinate lyase deficiency review: ADSL participates in purine nucleotide cycle controlling AMP/ATP ratio); OMIM 103050. Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (ADSL dual function: de novo purine synthesis + purine nucleotide cycle). | intervention: do(ADSL LoF, OMIM 103050): SAMP not converted to AMP; de novo IMP synthesis impaired; cellular ATP reduced."
    ; pmo:mechanism "ADSL (adenylosuccinate lyase) catalyses two steps in purine metabolism: (1) SAICAR -> AICAR in de novo purine synthesis (contributing to IMP/AMP/ATP production); (2) SAMP -> AMP in the purine nucleotide cycle (directly producing AMP from adenylosuccinate). The purine nucleotide cycle maintains the ATP/ADP/AMP ratio in muscle and other tissues. ADSL deficiency (OMIM 103050) impairs both reactions, reducing the AMP supply from SAMP and impairing de novo purine synthesis flux toward IMP, thereby lowering the cellular adenine nucleotide pool and ATP level. Higher ADSL activity supports both the de novo IMP pathway and the purine nucleotide cycle, raising cellular ATP."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adenosine_deaminase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/t_cell_immune_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aadenosine_deaminase_activity%3Apositive%3At_cell_immune_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adenosine_deaminase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/t_cell_immune_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Hershfield, Genet Med 2003 (ADA deficiency, OMIM 102700: deoxyadenosine -> dATP toxicity in T-cells); Hirschhorn, Ann NY Acad Sci 1993 (dATP mechanism of T-cell killing in ADA-SCID); PMC209335 (ADA deficiency increases thymic apoptosis via dATP accumulation); Blood 2009 (doi:10.1182/blood-2009-01-160440: ADA treatment approach confirms T-cell selective toxicity). | intervention: do(ADA LoF, ADA-SCID, OMIM 102700): deoxyadenosine not deaminated; dATP accumulates in T-cells; T-cells die by apoptosis; t_cell_immune_activity falls to near zero."
    ; pmo:mechanism "ADA (adenosine deaminase) deaminates deoxyadenosine (and adenosine) to deoxyinosine (and inosine). ADA deficiency (ADA-SCID, OMIM 102700) causes deoxyadenosine to accumulate and be phosphorylated to dATP by deoxynucleoside kinases, particularly in T lymphocytes (which have high deoxynucleoside kinase/low 5'-nucleotidase activity). Excess dATP inhibits ribonucleotide reductase (blocking DNA synthesis), inhibits SAH hydrolase (blocking transmethylation), and activates apoptosis signalling in thymocytes. The net effect is selective T-cell destruction, causing severe combined immunodeficiency. Higher ADA activity prevents dATP accumulation, preserving T-cell survival and immune activity."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aprt_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aaprt_activity%3Apositive%3Agfr> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aprt_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Edvardsson et al., Am J Kidney Dis 2013 (APRT deficiency OMIM 614723: 2,8-DHA crystal nephropathy, CKD progression, ESRD in ~15% at diagnosis); PMID 24940675 (2,8-DHA urolithiasis review confirming progressive GFR decline if untreated); PMC4560835 (2,8-DHA nephropathy: recurrent post-transplant allograft failure; crystal deposition confirmed). | intervention: do(APRT LoF, OMIM 614723): adenine not salvaged; 2,8-DHA crystals deposit in renal tubules; progressive nephropathy; GFR falls. | intervention: do(APRT LoF, 2,8-DHA urolithiasis/nephropathy, OMIM 614723) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "APRT (adenine phosphoribosyltransferase) salvages adenine (with PRPP) back to AMP; APRT deficiency (OMIM 614723) prevents adenine salvage, leaving free adenine to be oxidised by xanthine oxidase to 8-hydroxyadenine and then 2,8-dihydroxyadenine (2,8-DHA). 2,8-DHA is virtually insoluble in urine at any physiological pH, crystallising in the renal tubules and collecting system to form stones and cause 2,8-DHA crystal nephropathy. Progressive tubular and glomerular damage from crystal deposition leads to chronic kidney disease and declining GFR (approximately 15% present with ESRD at diagnosis). Higher APRT activity salvages adenine away from the 2,8-DHA-forming pathway, preventing crystal nephropathy and preserving GFR."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adenylate_kinase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ldh> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aadenylate_kinase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_ldh> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adenylate_kinase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ldh> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Beutler et al., JCI 1983 (PMID 6308059: AK1 deficiency causes nonspherocytic hemolytic anemia with erythrocyte ATP depletion); OMIM 612631 (CNSHA3: biallelic AK1 mutations -> chronic hemolytic anemia; elevated plasma LDH is a standard diagnostic marker of hemolysis). Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (hemolytic anemia chapter: LDH released from lysed erythrocytes; plasma LDH elevation is a universal marker of intravascular and extravascular hemolysis). | intervention: do(AK1 LoF, OMIM 612631): erythrocyte ATP depleted; premature haemolysis; plasma LDH rises. | intervention: do(AK1 LoF, nonspherocytic hemolytic anemia, OMIM 612631) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "AK1 (adenylate kinase 1) catalyses ATP + AMP <-> 2 ADP in erythrocytes, regenerating ADP for ATP synthesis and preventing AMP accumulation. AK1 deficiency (CNSHA3, OMIM 612631) depletes erythrocyte ATP by impairing AMP re-phosphorylation, causing energy-deficient, mechanically fragile red cells that undergo premature haemolysis. Lysed erythrocytes release their abundant intracellular LDH (predominantly the LDH-1/LDH-2 isoforms) into the plasma, raising plasma LDH — the hallmark of hemolytic anemia. Higher AK1 activity preserves erythrocyte ATP, prevents premature haemolysis, and keeps plasma LDH low."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nadsyn1_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_complex_i_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Anadsyn1_activity%3Apositive%3Amitochondrial_complex_i_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nadsyn1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_complex_i_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Szot JO et al. Am J Hum Genet 2020 (PMC7453336) — metabolic block in de novo and Preiss-Handler NAD pathways; Tran MT et al. Metabolomics 2023 (PMC10745350) — NADSYN1-deficient cells show TCA/glycolysis/ETC perturbations; Gaude E & Frezza C Cell Mol Life Sci 2014 (NAD+/NADH ratio governs Complex I NADH reoxidation). | intervention: do(NADSYN1 LoF, as in VCRL3 congenital NAD deficiency disorder) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "NADSYN1 (NAD synthetase 1) catalyses the terminal amidation step of de novo NAD+ biosynthesis (nicotinic-acid adenine dinucleotide → NAD+). NAD+ is the indispensable substrate for Complex I (NADH:ubiquinone oxidoreductase), the first and rate-limiting step of the mitochondrial electron transport chain, which regenerates NAD+ from NADH. NADSYN1 deficiency depletes cellular NAD+ — impairing the NADH/NAD+ redox balance and the availability of NAD+ for Complex I-dependent NADH oxidation, thus reducing Complex I flux and oxidative phosphorylation. NADSYN1 LoF → intracellular NAD+ ↓ → mitochondrial Complex I activity ↓."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_biotin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pyruvate_carboxylase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_biotin%3Apositive%3Apyruvate_carboxylase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_biotin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pyruvate_carboxylase_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Wolf B GeneReviews NBK1322 (biotinidase deficiency, OMIM 253260); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (biotin-dependent carboxylases: PC, ACC, PCC, MCC); StatPearls NBK560607 (biotinidase deficiency — secondary multiple carboxylase deficiency, lactic acidosis); Sweetman L & Williams JC in Scriver's Metabolic Basis of Inherited Disease (multiple carboxylase deficiency). | intervention: do(biotinidase LoF causing secondary biotin deficiency, OMIM 253260)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Biotin is covalently attached by holocarboxylase synthetase (HLCS) to the epsilon-amino group of a lysine residue in pyruvate carboxylase (PC), acetyl-CoA carboxylase, propionyl-CoA carboxylase, and 3-methylcrotonyl-CoA carboxylase. Without biotin, PC cannot bind its CO2 carrier and is catalytically inactive. Biotinidase deficiency (OMIM 253260) prevents recycling of biotin from biocytin, causing secondary biotin depletion and hence multiple carboxylase deficiency — PC LoF leads to impaired gluconeogenesis and pyruvate clearance, producing lactic acidosis and hyperammonaemia. do(biotinidase LoF → plasma biotin ↓) → pyruvate carboxylase activity ↓."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/muscle_lipoic_acid> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alpha_ketoglutarate_dehydrogenase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amuscle_lipoic_acid%3Apositive%3Aalpha_ketoglutarate_dehydrogenase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/muscle_lipoic_acid> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alpha_ketoglutarate_dehydrogenase_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Mayr JA et al. Am J Hum Genet 2011 (PMC3234378) — LIAS deficiency: neonatal epilepsy, lactic acidosis, reduced KGDH/PDH activities; Mayr JA et al. J Inherit Metab Dis 2014 (PMID:24777537) — lipoic acid biosynthesis defects review; Solmonson A & DeBerardinis RJ J Biol Chem 2018 (lipoate-dependent TCA enzymes); OMIM entry 607031 (LIAS). | intervention: do(LIAS LoF causing lipoic acid biosynthesis deficiency) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Lipoic acid (as the lipoamide prosthetic group) is covalently attached to the E2 dihydrolipoamide acyltransferase subunit of alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase (KGDH), pyruvate dehydrogenase (PDH), and branched-chain keto-acid dehydrogenase (BCKDH). Without lipoate, these complexes cannot transfer acyl groups and are inactive. LIAS (lipoic acid synthase) deficiency causes fatal neonatal-onset lactic acidosis and severely reduced KGDH and PDH activities, blocking TCA-cycle flux and pyruvate oxidation. do(LIAS LoF → muscle lipoic acid ↓) → alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase activity ↓."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_pyridoxal_phosphate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aadc_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acsf_pyridoxal_phosphate%3Apositive%3Aaadc_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_pyridoxal_phosphate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aadc_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "GeneReviews NBK581452 (PNPO deficiency, OMIM 610090); Alghamdi S et al. Clin Genet 2021 — 87-case PNPO deficiency review: CSF HVA/5-HIAA decreased pattern; Blau N et al. Neurology 2001 (CSF monoamine metabolite patterns in B6-responsive disorders); Pons R et al. Pediatr Res 2004 (PLP as AADC cofactor). | intervention: do(PNPO LoF causing pyridoxal-5'-phosphate-responsive seizures, OMIM 610090) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Pyridoxal-5'-phosphate (PLP) is the obligatory cofactor for aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (AADC/DDC), the enzyme that decarboxylates L-DOPA to dopamine and 5-hydroxytryptophan (5-HTP) to serotonin. PNPO (pyridoxine-5'-phosphate oxidase) deficiency impairs conversion of pyridoxine-5'-phosphate to PLP, depleting CSF PLP. Without adequate PLP the AADC holoenzyme cannot form, abolishing dopamine and serotonin synthesis and producing an AADC-like neurotransmitter deficiency pattern (CSF HVA ↓, CSF 5-HIAA ↓, urinary vanillactate ↑). do(PNPO LoF → CSF PLP ↓) → AADC activity ↓."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ige> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mast_cell_degranulation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_ige%3Apositive%3Amast_cell_degranulation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ige> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mast_cell_degranulation> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Janeway's Immunobiology 10th ed. Ch.9 (IgE-FcεRI crosslinking mechanism of Type I hypersensitivity; mast cell degranulation); Hyper-IgE syndrome (STAT3 LOF, OMIM 147060): extremely elevated serum IgE >2000 IU/mL, eczema, and recurrent infections — Grimbacher et al. N Engl J Med 1999;340:692; Abbas & Lichtman Cellular and Molecular Immunology 9th ed. Ch.13 (IgE sensitizes mast cells via FcεRI; allergen crosslinking → degranulation is the canonical Type I mechanism) | intervention: do(elevated plasma IgE, e.g., Hyper-IgE syndrome / STAT3 mutation) → mast cell degranulation increases | intervention: do(plasma_ige elevated, e.g., Hyper-IgE syndrome STAT3 LOF/GOF, OMIM 147060) → mast_cell_degranulation increases"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Circulating IgE binds constitutively to high-affinity FcεRI receptors on mast cells; elevated plasma IgE (sensitization) → greater FcεRI loading → allergen crosslinking of receptor-bound IgE triggers Lyn/Syk kinase cascade → mast cell degranulation. In Hyper-IgE syndrome (STAT3 LOF, OMIM 147060) markedly elevated plasma IgE causes chronic mast cell sensitisation with eczema and allergic features. Conversely, do(BTK/AICDA/CD40L deficiency) → absent IgE production → no FcεRI loading → no allergen-driven degranulation."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cd46> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_cd46%3Apositive%3Agfr> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cd46> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Noris & Remuzzi N Engl J Med 2009;361:1676 (atypical HUS: complement dysregulation, CD46/MCP mutations account for ~15% of aHUS; pathogenesis: uncontrolled C3b on glomerular endothelium → TMA → AKI/CKD). Richards et al. J Am Soc Nephrol 2003;14:2429 (CD46 mutations in familial aHUS, impaired factor I cofactor activity, loss of glomerular endothelial protection). OMIM 612922 (atypical HUS 2, MCP/CD46 gene) | intervention: do(CD46 LOF mutation, OMIM 612922) → GFR decreases | intervention: do(CD46 loss-of-function mutation, aHUS type 2, OMIM 612922) → GFR decreases"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CD46 (membrane cofactor protein, MCP) acts as a cofactor for complement factor I in cleaving and inactivating C3b and C4b deposited on host cell surfaces including glomerular endothelium. Adequate plasma/surface CD46 → C3b inactivation on glomerular endothelium → complement-mediated injury limited → GFR preserved. CD46 LOF mutations (aHUS type 2, OMIM 612922) → uncontrolled alternative-pathway C3b amplification on glomerular endothelium → thrombotic microangiopathy (TMA) → platelet-fibrin thrombi occlude glomerular capillaries → GFR falls. do(CD46 deficiency) → GFR decreases."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cfhr1> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_cfhr1%3Apositive%3Agfr> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cfhr1> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Zipfel et al. Mol Immunol 2007;44:3693 — CFHR1/CFHR3 deletion in 25% of aHUS patients; CFHR1 blocks MAC on endothelial surfaces (CFHR1 competes with properdin and inhibits terminal pathway at C5 convertase level). Skerka et al. Mol Immunol 2013;56:187 — CFHR1 deficiency associated with C3 glomerulopathy and aHUS via loss of terminal complement inhibition on glomerular membranes. Loirat & Fremeaux-Bacchi Orphanet J Rare Dis 2011;6:60 (aHUS genetics including CFHR1) | intervention: do(homozygous CFHR1/CFHR3 deletion) → GFR decreases | intervention: do(homozygous CFHR1/CFHR3 84-kb deletion — plasma CFHR1 absent) → GFR decreases"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Complement factor H-related protein 1 (CFHR1, encoded by CFHR1) circulates in plasma and inhibits terminal complement by competing with C5 convertase and blocking C5b-9 (membrane attack complex, MAC) formation on glomerular endothelial surfaces. Adequate CFHR1 → MAC formation suppressed on glomerular endothelium → endothelial integrity preserved → GFR maintained. CFHR1/CFHR3 chromosomal deletion (homozygous ~84-kb deletion on chr 1q31) is a major genetic risk factor for aHUS and C3 glomerulopathy → uncontrolled MAC-mediated glomerular endothelial lysis and C3-dense deposit disease → progressive GFR decline. do(CFHR1 deficiency) → GFR decreases."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/transaldolase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_albumin_synthesis> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atransaldolase_activity%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_albumin_synthesis> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/transaldolase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_albumin_synthesis> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "PMID 11283793 Wamelink et al. 2001 Am J Hum Genet (PMC1226089) 'Transaldolase deficiency: liver cirrhosis associated with a new inborn error in the pentose phosphate pathway' (liver cirrhosis, hepatomegaly, reduced hepatic synthetic function); OMIM 606003 (TRANSALDOLASE DEFICIENCY; TALDOD — hepatic disease with decreased albumin as clinical feature); Transaldolase haploinsufficiency PMC7317976 (hepatic vulnerability). Intervention: do(TALDO1 LoF) -> progressive liver disease -> hepatic albumin synthesis DOWN. | intervention: do(TALDO1 LoF, transaldolase deficiency, OMIM 606003) -> hepatic oxidative stress + polyol accumulation -> liver cirrhosis/fibrosis -> impaired hepatic synthetic function -> hepatic_albumin_synthesis DOWN [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Transaldolase (TALDO1) deficiency causes accumulation of sedoheptulose-7-phosphate and pentitol polyols (ribitol, D-arabitol, erythritol), leading to hepatocyte oxidative stress (reduced NADPH via impaired PPP flux) and progressive liver disease with fibrosis/cirrhosis. Hepatic synthetic function is impaired: decreased serum albumin and abnormal clotting factors were documented in 53% of the 34-patient cohort. TALDO1 activity therefore positively determines hepatic albumin synthesis rate; do(TALDO1 LoF) -> hepatic albumin synthesis DOWN."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glycogen_phosphorylase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_glucose_production> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aglycogen_phosphorylase_activity%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_glucose_production> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glycogen_phosphorylase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_glucose_production> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Glycogen storage disease type VI / Hers disease (OMIM 232700; PYGL deficiency): fasting hypoglycemia from impaired hepatic glycogenolysis is the cardinal feature, confirming PYGL activity positively drives hepatic_glucose_production. Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 15 (glycogen catabolism: glycogen phosphorylase rate-limits glycogenolysis to G-1-P -> hepatic glucose output). Scriver's OMMBID / Saudubray 'Inborn Metabolic Diseases' (GSD VI: hepatic phosphorylase deficiency -> hepatomegaly + fasting hypoglycemia). StatPearls 'Glycogen Storage Disease' (NCBI NBK459277: hepatic GSD types II/III/VI/IX -> fasting hypoglycemia via impaired glycogenolysis). Intervention: do(PYGL LoF, GSD VI, OMIM 232700) -> hepatic glycogenolysis impaired -> hepatic_glucose_production DOWN. | intervention: do(PYGL LoF, Hers disease, GSD VI, OMIM 232700) -> hepatic glycogenolysis blocked -> hepatic glucose production DOWN (fasting hypoglycemia)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Hepatic glycogen phosphorylase (PYGL) catalyzes phosphorolysis of alpha-1,4-glucosidic bonds in liver glycogen, releasing glucose-1-phosphate for conversion to free glucose. PYGL activity is therefore a positive determinant of hepatic glucose production via the glycogenolytic arm. In GSD VI (Hers disease, OMIM 232700), PYGL deficiency impairs hepatic glycogenolysis -> reduced hepatic glucose production -> fasting hypoglycemia (especially post-prandial fasting). PYGL activity UP -> glycogenolysis UP -> hepatic_glucose_production UP."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldolase_b_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aaldolase_b_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_glucose> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldolase_b_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Hereditary fructose intolerance OMIM 229600 (ALDOB deficiency; hypoglycemia after fructose/sucrose ingestion is the cardinal acute manifestation). Steinmann et al. in Scriver's OMMBID 'Disorders of Fructose Metabolism' (fructose-1-phosphate accumulation inhibits glycogen phosphorylase and FBPase -> combined glycogenolysis + gluconeogenesis failure -> hypoglycemia). PMC11105038 (Bouteldja & Timson 2024 'Recent advances in the pathogenesis of HFI': F1P inhibits hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis). Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 15 (fructose catabolism: ALDOB in hepatocytes; F1P accumulation mechanism). OMIM 612724 (ALDOB gene entry). Intervention: do(ALDOB LoF, HFI, OMIM 229600) -> F1P accumulates -> inhibits glycogenolysis + gluconeogenesis -> plasma_glucose DOWN (hypoglycemia after fructose exposure). | intervention: do(ALDOB LoF, hereditary fructose intolerance, OMIM 229600) -> fructose-1-phosphate accumulation -> inhibition of glycogenolysis (glycogen phosphorylase) and gluconeogenesis (FBPase) -> plasma_glucose DOWN (acute hypoglycemia)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Hepatic aldolase B (ALDOB) cleaves fructose-1-phosphate (the KHK product) into dihydroxyacetone phosphate and glyceraldehyde. ALDOB LoF (hereditary fructose intolerance, HFI, OMIM 229600) causes fructose-1-phosphate accumulation in hepatocytes: F1P competitively inhibits both glycogen phosphorylase (blocking glycogenolysis) and fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase (blocking gluconeogenesis), while also depleting inorganic phosphate and ATP. The combined effect is severe fasting hypoglycemia after fructose ingestion. ALDOB activity -> F1P clearance -> restored glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis -> plasma glucose maintained."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/phosphomannomutase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/antithrombin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aphosphomannomutase_activity%3Apositive%3Aantithrombin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/phosphomannomutase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/antithrombin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "OMIM 212065 (PMM2-CDG / CDG-Ia: coagulopathy including reduced antithrombin III documented as clinical feature). Bhatt et al. 2023 J Thromb Haemost (PMID 37224763) 'Coagulation abnormalities in a prospective cohort of 50 patients with PMM2-CDG' (antithrombin among coagulation proteins reduced in PMM2-CDG). Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (N-glycosylation: PMM2 -> mannose-1-P -> GDP-mannose -> dolichol-PP-oligosaccharide assembly). Intervention: do(PMM2 LoF, PMM2-CDG, OMIM 212065) -> GDP-mannose deficiency -> hypoglycosylation of SERPINC1 -> antithrombin activity DOWN. | intervention: do(PMM2 LoF, PMM2-CDG/CDG-Ia, OMIM 212065) -> GDP-mannose deficiency -> N-glycosylation defect of antithrombin (SERPINC1) -> antithrombin anticoagulant activity DOWN [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "PMM2 (phosphomannomutase 2) converts mannose-6-phosphate to mannose-1-phosphate, the precursor for GDP-mannose synthesis, which is essential for N-linked glycosylation. PMM2 LoF (PMM2-CDG / CDG-Ia, OMIM 212065) causes global N-glycosylation deficiency. Antithrombin III (SERPINC1) is an N-glycosylated serine protease inhibitor; its anticoagulant activity and plasma half-life depend on intact N-glycosylation. In PMM2-CDG patients, approximately 20% of antithrombin molecules have N-glycosylation defects, reducing functional antithrombin activity -> coagulopathy/thrombophilia. PMM2 activity UP -> adequate GDP-mannose -> complete N-glycosylation of antithrombin -> normal antithrombin activity."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_trypsinogen> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pancreatic_exocrine_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_trypsinogen%3Anegative%3Apancreatic_exocrine_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_trypsinogen> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pancreatic_exocrine_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "PRSS1-Related Hereditary Pancreatitis GeneReviews NBK84399: approximately half of individuals progress to chronic pancreatitis with pancreatic exocrine dysfunction; PMC3949028 (Human cationic trypsinogen PRSS1 variants and chronic pancreatitis): PRSS1 GoF R122H and N29I cause chronic pancreatitis leading to exocrine insufficiency; PMC6620583 (Elevated intracellular trypsin exacerbates acute pancreatitis and chronic pancreatitis): elevated intrapancreatic trypsin drives acinar damage; OMIM 167800 (Hereditary pancreatitis): exocrine pancreatic insufficiency with steatorrhoea documented. Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 23 (pancreatic zymogen secretion and activation). intervention: do(PRSS1 GoF, e.g. R122H or N29I) -> elevated plasma trypsinogen and pancreatitis -> decreased pancreatic exocrine secretion in chronic disease. | intervention: do(PRSS1 gain-of-function, e.g. R122H / N29I variant causing hereditary pancreatitis)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Elevated plasma trypsinogen (as in PRSS1 GoF hereditary pancreatitis) indicates premature intrapancreatic trypsinogen activation. Active intrapancreatic trypsin triggers autodigestion and acinar cell death; repeated or chronic attacks lead to irreversible pancreatic parenchymal destruction, fibrosis, and exocrine insufficiency. In chronic PRSS1-related pancreatitis, pancreatic exocrine secretion (enzyme + bicarbonate output) is progressively reduced as functional acinar cell mass is lost. do(PRSS1 GoF) -> elevated plasma trypsinogen -> pancreatitis -> pancreatic acinar destruction -> reduced exocrine secretion."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/carboxypeptidase_n_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bradykinin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acarboxypeptidase_n_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_bradykinin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/carboxypeptidase_n_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bradykinin> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "OMIM 212070 (Carboxypeptidase N Deficiency, CPND): CPN1 LoF mutations cause episodic angioedema from bradykinin accumulation; PMC10912455 (Hereditary angioedema with normal C1 inhibitor associated with carboxypeptidase N deficiency, JACI Global 2024): CPN1 variants in 4 unrelated families with HAE and plasma bradykinin accumulation; PMID 7437116 (Familial carboxypeptidase N deficiency): original description of CPN deficiency with reduced bradykinin inactivation; JCI 15490 (The plasma kallikrein-kinin system counterbalances the renin-angiotensin system): CPN (kininase I) as a bradykinin-inactivating enzyme. Williams Hematology: CPN as kininase I inactivates bradykinin by removing C-terminal Arg. intervention: do(CPN1 LoF, carboxypeptidase N deficiency OMIM 212070) -> reduced bradykinin inactivation -> plasma bradykinin rises -> angioedema. | intervention: do(CPN1 loss-of-function; carboxypeptidase N deficiency OMIM 212070) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Carboxypeptidase N (CPN; kininase I; CPN1 gene product) circulates in plasma as a constitutively active metallopeptidase that cleaves the C-terminal Arg residue from bradykinin (and kallidin, C3a, C4a, C5a), irreversibly inactivating it. CPN mediates ~10% of plasma bradykinin catabolism (alongside ACE/kininase II as the major degrading enzyme). When CPN activity is reduced (CPN deficiency, OMIM 212070, due to CPN1 LoF mutations), bradykinin clearance is impaired, plasma bradykinin accumulates, causing episodic bradykinin-mediated angioedema (HAE-nC1-INH). do(CPN activity decreased) -> bradykinin degradation reduced -> plasma bradykinin rises."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ceruloplasmin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_iron> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_ceruloplasmin%3Apositive%3Aplasma_iron> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ceruloplasmin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_iron> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "OMIM 604290 (aceruloplasminemia; CP LoF; hallmarks: absent serum ceruloplasmin ferroxidase activity, low serum iron, low transferrin saturation, high ferritin, tissue iron overload); Miyajima et al. 1987 / Yoshida et al. 1995 (aceruloplasminemia: low serum iron with brain and visceral iron accumulation); PMC6290325 (aceruloplasminemia review: absent Cp ferroxidase -> impaired iron export from cells, low transferrin saturation); PMC4050113 (ceruloplasmin-ferroportin system of iron traffic: Cp ferroxidase required for Fe3+ loading onto transferrin); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (ceruloplasmin as ferroxidase: Fe2+ -> Fe3+ for transferrin loading). | intervention: aceruloplasminemia (CP loss-of-function, autosomal-recessive, OMIM 604290) | intervention: aceruloplasminemia (CP/ceruloplasmin loss-of-function, OMIM 604290) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Ceruloplasmin (CP) is a multicopper ferroxidase that oxidises ferrous iron (Fe2+) to ferric iron (Fe3+) at the surface of hepatocytes, macrophages, and enterocytes exporting iron; only ferric iron can be loaded onto apotransferrin, so ceruloplasmin activity is required for iron to enter the plasma transferrin-bound pool. In aceruloplasminemia (CP LoF, OMIM 604290), absence of ferroxidase activity prevents iron loading onto transferrin, resulting in low serum (transferrin-bound) iron, low transferrin saturation, and paradoxical tissue iron overload (high ferritin). Intervention: do(CP LoF / aceruloplasminemia) -> absent ceruloplasmin ferroxidase activity -> failure to load Fe3+ onto transferrin -> low plasma (transferrin-bound) iron."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_selenium> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_selenium%3Apositive%3Afree_t3> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_selenium> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "PMC7783124 (Thyroid function in patients with selenium deficiency: significantly higher free T4/T3 ratio vs controls, directly demonstrating impaired T4->T3 conversion under selenium deficiency); PMID 25129137 / Springer 2014 (selenium deficiency inhibits T4->T3 conversion in thyroid via deiodinase suppression); PMC12672807 (comprehensive review: selenium is essential cofactor of DIO1/DIO2; selenium deficiency reduces deiodinase activity, lowers T3 and raises T4/T3 ratio); PMC8807339 (inherited disorder of thyroid hormone metabolism from SECISBP2 dysregulation: multisystem selenoprotein deficiency including DIO1/DIO2, with altered thyroid hormone profile, low T3); Williams Textbook of Endocrinology (selenium and deiodinases: DIO1/DIO2 are selenoenzymes; selenium deficiency impairs T4-to-T3 conversion); OMIM 609698 (SECISBP2 LoF: multisystem selenoprotein deficiency with abnormal thyroid hormone metabolism, low T3). | intervention: selenium deficiency states; SECISBP2 loss-of-function (OMIM 609698) | intervention: selenium deficiency; SECISBP2 loss-of-function (OMIM 609698) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Iodothyronine deiodinases DIO1 and DIO2 are selenoproteins that catalyse the 5'-deiodination of T4 to the active hormone T3; their catalytic selenocysteine residue requires selenium for incorporation during translation (via the SECISBP2/SBP2 machinery). When plasma selenium is low (selenium deficiency, or SECISBP2 LoF reducing selenoprotein synthesis), DIO1/DIO2 activity falls, impairing T4-to-T3 conversion and reducing circulating free T3 (with a raised free T4/T3 ratio). Intervention: do(selenium deficiency) -> low DIO1/DIO2 selenoprotein activity -> impaired T4->T3 conversion -> low free T3."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/breast_milk_zinc> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/t_cell_immune_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abreast_milk_zinc%3Apositive%3At_cell_immune_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/breast_milk_zinc> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/t_cell_immune_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "OMIM 608118 (transient neonatal zinc deficiency, TNZD; SLC30A2 LoF in lactating mothers; infants present with immune insufficiency, dermatitis, diarrhoea); PMC3669329 (Itsumura et al. 2013, compound heterozygous SLC30A2 mutations: >75% reduction in breast milk zinc causing zinc deficiency with immune dysfunction in breastfed infant); PMC10030689 (novel SLC30A2 mutations in transient neonatal zinc deficiency: immune insufficiency explicitly listed); Prasad 1998 / PMC7199614 (experimental human zinc deficiency: decreased thymulin activity in Th1, decreased mRNA of IL-2 and IFN-gamma, decreased NK and cytotoxic T-cell activity); ScienceDirect (zinc deficiency, depressed thymic hormones, and T lymphocyte dysfunction; thymic involution and lymphopenia as classic hallmarks); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (zinc deficiency: impaired cellular immunity, thymic involution, depressed cell-mediated immunity). | intervention: SLC30A2 loss-of-function in lactating mother (OMIM 608118) causing infant zinc deficiency | intervention: SLC30A2/ZnT2 loss-of-function in lactating mother (OMIM 608118) causing transient neonatal zinc deficiency"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Zinc is an obligate cofactor for thymulin (a thymic nonapeptide hormone) and is required for T-cell differentiation, thymic epithelial integrity, and IL-2-dependent T-cell proliferation. When breast milk zinc is low due to SLC30A2/ZnT2 LoF in the mother, the nursing infant develops transient neonatal zinc deficiency; zinc depletion causes thymic involution, depressed thymulin activity, lymphopenia, reduced IL-2 and IFN-gamma production, and impaired T-cell cytotoxic function. Infant T-cell mediated immune activity falls proportionally to the severity and duration of zinc deficiency. Intervention: do(SLC30A2 LoF in lactating mother) -> low breast milk zinc -> infant zinc deficiency -> thymic atrophy, reduced thymulin saturation -> low T-cell immune activity."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nis_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thyroid_follicular_t4_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Anis_activity%3Apositive%3Athyroid_follicular_t4_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nis_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thyroid_follicular_t4_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "OMIM 274400 (thyroid dyshormonogenesis 1, TDH1; SLC5A5/NIS LoF; congenital hypothyroidism with absent/severely reduced thyroidal radioiodine uptake and low T4); PMC8861941 (novel SLC5A5 mutation S356F: NIS LoF causes dyshormonogenic congenital hypothyroidism with absent radioiodine uptake, directly confirming NIS is required for T4 synthesis); PMC11693440 (silent SLC5A5 variant causing dyshormonogenic congenital hypothyroidism via NIS LoF: low T4 and elevated TSH); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (thyroidal iodide trapping by NIS is the first step in thyroid hormone biosynthesis; NIS LoF abolishes the iodide gradient required for thyroglobulin iodination); Williams Textbook of Endocrinology (NIS-mediated thyroid iodide uptake: rate-limiting for T4 synthesis; NIS loss causes hypothyroidism). | intervention: NIS (SLC5A5) loss-of-function (autosomal-recessive, OMIM 274400) | intervention: NIS (SLC5A5) loss-of-function (autosomal-recessive iodide transport defect, OMIM 274400)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "NIS (sodium/iodide symporter, SLC5A5) is the basolateral membrane glycoprotein of thyroid follicular cells that actively co-transports 2 Na+ per I- into the cell, concentrating iodide 20-40-fold above plasma — the first and rate-limiting step of thyroid hormone biosynthesis. Without sufficient thyroidal iodide uptake, the substrate for thyroid peroxidase-catalysed iodination of thyroglobulin is absent, and T4 (thyroxine) synthesis and secretion fall. NIS LoF (iodide transport defect, OMIM 274400) causes dyshormonogenic congenital hypothyroidism with absent radioiodine uptake, goitre, and very low thyroid hormone secretion. Intervention: do(NIS LoF) -> absent thyroidal iodide trapping -> insufficient iodide for thyroglobulin iodination -> reduced T4 synthesis and secretion."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_dhea> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_androgen> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_dhea%3Apositive%3Aplasma_androgen> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_dhea> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_androgen> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Labrie et al. (2003) J Steroid Biochem Mol Biol 85:377-382 (intracrinology; DHEA as precursor to peripheral androgens); PMID:25962158 Labrie et al. 2015 (DHEA conversion to activated steroid hormones in adrenal and ovary); Williams Textbook of Endocrinology (steroidogenesis chapter: DHEA -> androstenedione -> testosterone via peripheral 3β-HSD / 17β-HSD); CYP17A1 deficiency (OMIM:202110, 17α-hydroxylase/17,20-lyase deficiency) abolishes adrenal DHEA production and results in severely reduced circulating androgens, confirming interventional causal link. | intervention: CYP17A1 loss-of-function (17alpha-hydroxylase/17,20-lyase deficiency; OMIM:202110): adrenal DHEA production is abolished -> plasma DHEA and DHEA-S fall markedly -> plasma androgen concentration is severely reduced; reciprocally, DHEA replacement therapy raises circulating androgens. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Circulating DHEA is taken up by peripheral tissues (skin, muscle, bone, adipose, gonad) where 3β-HSD and 17β-HSD convert it to androstenedione and then testosterone, raising the plasma androgen pool; adrenal DHEA/DHEA-S are the quantitatively dominant circulating androgen precursors (responsible for ~50% of total androgens in women and a significant fraction in men)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aromatase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/granulosa_estradiol_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aaromatase_activity%3Apositive%3Agranulosa_estradiol_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aromatase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/granulosa_estradiol_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Bulun et al. PMC7952251 (Estrogen disorders: aromatase in granulosa cells); Simpson et al. (2002) TRENDS Endocrinol Metab 13:158-163 (aromatase biology); aromatase-excess syndrome (OMIM:139300) — gain-of-function CYP19A1 mutation causes elevated granulosa/adipose estradiol, gynecomastia and precocious feminization; aromatase deficiency (OMIM:107910) — LoF CYP19A1 causes absent estradiol production and virilization; Williams Textbook of Endocrinology (ovarian steroidogenesis: granulosa aromatase drives follicular estradiol output). | intervention: Aromatase deficiency (CYP19A1 LoF, OMIM:107910): granulosa-cell aromatase is absent -> estradiol secretion falls to essentially zero -> severe estrogen deficiency with virilization; aromatase inhibitor therapy (anastrozole, letrozole) similarly abolishes granulosa estradiol output; aromatase-excess syndrome (GoF) markedly raises estradiol."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Aromatase (CYP19A1) in ovarian granulosa cells catalyses the rate-limiting aromatization of androstenedione (supplied by theca cells) to estrone, and testosterone to 17β-estradiol; higher aromatase activity directly increases granulosa-cell estradiol secretion rate (two-cell two-gonadotropin model). FSH induces CYP19A1 transcription via cAMP/PKA in granulosa cells; granulosa-cell aromatase is the dominant source of circulating estradiol in pre-menopausal women."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_t4> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_t4%3Apositive%3Afree_t4> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_t4> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Samuels & Bowers (1991) Endocr Rev (thyroid hormone binding and free-hormone hypothesis); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (thyroid chapter: T4 distribution between bound and free fractions; TBG as major carrier); PMID:1623594 Chopra (1991) — free-T4 depends on total T4 and TBG concentration; congenital hypothyroidism (PAX8/NKX2-1 LoF): total T4 falls -> free T4 falls; levothyroxine (T4) therapy raises total T4 -> free T4 rises. | intervention: do(levothyroxine supplementation) raises total plasma T4 pool -> free T4 rises, TSH falls; do(hypothyroidism / ablation of thyroid output): total T4 falls -> free T4 falls; the sign is unambiguously + across all clinical thyroid disorders where both total T4 and free T4 are measured. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Total plasma T4 is in binding equilibrium with its carrier proteins (TBG ~70%, transthyretin ~20%, albumin ~10%); free T4 (the biologically active fraction, ~0.03% of total) is set by mass-action equilibrium between the total T4 pool and these binding proteins. At constant binding-protein concentration, higher total T4 raises free T4 proportionally; this equilibrium relationship is the pharmacokinetic basis of thyroid hormone signalling."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_vasoactive_intestinal_peptide> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_tract_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_vasoactive_intestinal_peptide%3Apositive%3Agi_tract_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_vasoactive_intestinal_peptide> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_tract_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Bloom et al. (1973) Lancet (original description of VIP-secreting pancreatic tumor causing watery diarrhoea); Said & Mutt (1970) Science (VIP discovery and secretory effects); VIPoma (Verner-Morrison syndrome; OMIM:601634) — autonomous VIP hypersecretion causes profuse secretory diarrhea >1 L/day (WDHA: watery diarrhoea, hypokalaemia, achlorhydria); StatPearls VIPoma (NBK507698); PMC6743256 Fung & Vanden Berghe (2020) review of VIP physiology in GI tract. | intervention: VIPoma (neuroendocrine tumour with autonomous VIP secretion; OMIM:601634): plasma VIP markedly elevated -> intestinal secretion profoundly increased -> secretory diarrhea, hypovolaemia, hypokalaemia; octreotide (somatostatin analogue) suppresses VIP secretion -> diarrhea and intestinal hypersecretion resolve, confirming causal link."
    ; pmo:mechanism "VIP binds VPAC1/VPAC2 receptors on intestinal epithelial cells and submucosal neurons; receptor activation raises intracellular cAMP via adenylyl cyclase -> PKA-dependent phosphorylation of CFTR and Na/K pumps -> active Cl- and water secretion into the intestinal lumen, plus stimulation of pancreatic bicarbonate and enzyme secretion. VIP also inhibits gastric acid secretion. Elevated plasma VIP directly drives intestinal secretion rate."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_urea_synthesis> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/blood_urea_nitrogen> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahepatic_urea_synthesis%3Apositive%3Ablood_urea_nitrogen> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_urea_synthesis> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/blood_urea_nitrogen> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "StatPearls 'Physiology, Urea Cycle' (NCBI Bookshelf NBK513323): urea is synthesised in liver and excreted by kidneys; serum urea depends on the balance of hepatic synthesis and renal elimination. PMC11612001 (Calvani et al. 2024, 'Down the road towards hepatic encephalopathy: urea synthesis — the liver workhorse of nitrogen metabolism'). eClinpath Urea review: 'more than 99% of urea synthesis occurs in the liver; serum levels depend predominantly on the balance between urea synthesis in the liver and its elimination by the kidney'. OTC deficiency OMIM 311250: low/normal BUN with hyperammonemia is the hallmark laboratory finding. | intervention: do(urea-cycle enzyme LoF, e.g. OTC/CPS1 deficiency, OMIM 311250) -> hepatic_urea_synthesis decreases -> blood_urea_nitrogen decreases"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Hepatic urea-cycle flux releases urea directly into the portal/systemic circulation; >99% of circulating urea is synthesised in the liver, so steady-state BUN reflects the balance between hepatic urea production rate and renal urea clearance. In urea-cycle disorders (OTC/CPS1/ASS1/ASL/ARG1 deficiency), reduced hepatic urea synthesis lowers BUN despite severe hyperammonemia — the classic 'low BUN with high ammonia' signature of UCDs. Interventionally: do(urea_cycle_enzyme deficiency) -> hepatic_urea_synthesis DOWN -> BUN DOWN."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_plasmalogen> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hdl_cholesterol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_plasmalogen%3Apositive%3Ahdl_cholesterol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_plasmalogen> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hdl_cholesterol> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Munn & Voelker (1998) BBRC 249:459-464 (PMID:9753636): HDL-mediated cholesterol efflux was reduced by 57% in plasmalogen-deficient RAW-mutant macrophages and RCDP2 patient fibroblasts; incubation with 1-O-hexadecyl-sn-glycerol to restore plasmalogens rescued efflux. RCDP type 2 (OMIM 222765, GNPAT LoF) as the disease-perturbation model: GNPAT LoF → DHAPAT activity abolished → plasmalogen synthesis blocked → plasma plasmalogens severely reduced → impaired HDL-mediated cholesterol efflux → reduced plasma HDL-cholesterol. Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry ch. 21 (ether-lipid/plasmalogen biosynthesis and membrane cholesterol homeostasis). | intervention: GNPAT or AGPS loss-of-function (RCDP type 2/3): enzyme LoF → plasma plasmalogen falls → HDL-mediated cholesterol efflux impaired → plasma HDL-cholesterol reduced | intervention: GNPAT loss-of-function (RCDP type 2, OMIM 222765) or AGPS loss-of-function (RCDP type 3, OMIM 600121): peroxisomal plasmalogen synthesis abolished → plasma plasmalogens severely reduced [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Plasmalogens are required for efficient HDL-mediated cholesterol efflux from cells. Plasmalogen-deficient macrophages and fibroblasts (from RCDP type 2 / GNPAT-LoF patients) show a ~57% reduction in HDL-mediated cholesterol efflux; restoring plasmalogen levels rescues efflux capacity. Plasmalogens facilitate the availability of the free-cholesterol pool at the plasma membrane and support the membrane dynamics required for ABCA1/SR-BI-mediated cholesterol transfer to HDL. Higher plasma (and membrane) plasmalogen concentration → larger free-cholesterol pool accessible for reverse cholesterol transport → higher HDL-cholesterol efflux capacity and circulating HDL-cholesterol."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_complex_ii_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_complex_iii_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amitochondrial_complex_ii_activity%3Apositive%3Amitochondrial_complex_iii_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_complex_ii_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_complex_iii_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Mitochondrial Complex II Deficiency Nuclear Type 1 (OMIM 252011, SDHA LoF): SDHA mutations abolish Complex II (SDH) activity, leading to reduced overall respiratory chain activity and lactic acidosis; patients show 20-30% residual SDH and succinate-CoQ reductase activity in muscle/fibroblasts. StatPearls Biochemistry: Electron Transport Chain (NBK526105): Complex II transfers electrons from FADH2 to ubiquinone (Q), producing ubiquinol (QH2) that feeds Complex III. Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry ch. 19 (electron transport chain: CoQ as mobile carrier between Complexes II/III and Complex III). | intervention: SDHA/SDHB/SDHC/SDHD loss-of-function (Mitochondrial Complex II Deficiency, OMIM 252011/619166/619167/619224): SDH LoF → Complex II activity reduced → ubiquinol production reduced → Complex III electron input decreased → Complex III activity reduced | intervention: SDHA/SDHB/SDHC/SDHD loss-of-function (Mitochondrial Complex II Deficiency, OMIM 252011): SDH subunit LoF → Complex II activity abolished or severely reduced"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Complex II (succinate:ubiquinone oxidoreductase / SDH) oxidizes succinate to fumarate and, via its covalently-bound FADH2, transfers two electrons through three iron-sulfur clusters to ubiquinone (CoQ), reducing it to ubiquinol (QH2). Ubiquinol is the mobile electron carrier that delivers electrons to Complex III (ubiquinol:cytochrome c oxidoreductase / bc1 complex). Complex II activity is therefore the rate-controlling step for ubiquinol supply to Complex III: higher Complex II activity → more ubiquinol produced → more electrons available for Complex III → higher Complex III electron-transfer activity. LoF of any SDH subunit (SDHA, SDHB, SDHC, SDHD) reduces ubiquinol production and consequently reduces Complex III flux and overall respiratory chain activity."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_acetaldehyde> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_lactate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_acetaldehyde%3Apositive%3Aplasma_lactate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_acetaldehyde> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_lactate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, Ch. 17 — ethanol metabolism: ethanol -> (ADH, consumes NAD+) -> acetaldehyde -> (ALDH2, consumes NAD+) -> acetate; NADH accumulation drives pyruvate -> lactate; alcohol-induced lactic acidosis is a recognized complication (elevated plasma lactate, reduced gluconeogenesis). ALDH2 deficiency (OMIM 610251, Asian flush / Acute Alcohol Sensitivity) — ALDH2*2 (Glu487Lys) LoF results in acetaldehyde accumulation; Cederbaum AI 'Alcohol metabolism: its biology and pathology' Alcohol Research & Health 2012 (PMC6527027) — NADH/NAD+ ratio increase underlies ethanol-associated lactic acidosis; PMC6875787 (ALDH2 deficiency, Alcohol Res 2019). Intervention: do(plasma_acetaldehyde ↑, e.g. ALDH2 LoF or ethanol challenge in ALDH2-deficient subjects) -> hepatic NADH excess -> LDH shifts toward lactate -> plasma lactate rises. | intervention: do(plasma_acetaldehyde ↑) via ALDH2 LoF (ALDH2*2 homozygosity, OMIM 610251) or pharmacological ALDH2 inhibition (disulfiram analogue) following ethanol challenge — acetaldehyde accumulates, hepatic NADH/NAD+ ratio rises, plasma lactate increases"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Acetaldehyde (the first oxidation product of ethanol, cleared by hepatic ALDH2) directly inhibits mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation in hepatocytes by disrupting Complex I and Complex III of the respiratory chain. This impairs NADH reoxidation, causing intrahepatic NADH/NAD+ ratio to rise. The excess NADH drives the lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) equilibrium strongly toward lactate: pyruvate + NADH + H+ -> lactate + NAD+. Additionally, NADH excess impairs hepatic gluconeogenesis (which requires NAD+ for glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase), further diverting pyruvate toward lactate rather than glucose. The net effect is plasma lactic acidosis. ALDH2 deficiency (reduced acetaldehyde clearance) exacerbates both acetaldehyde accumulation and the secondary lactic acidosis."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_urate> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_urate%3Anegative%3Agfr> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_urate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Conger JD. Acute uric acid nephropathy. Med Clin North Am. 1990;74(4):859-71. PMID 2195258. Mei Y et al. Excess Uric Acid Induces Gouty Nephropathy Through Crystal Formation: A Review of Recent Insights. Front Endocrinol (Lausanne). 2022;13:911968. PMC9329685. OMIM #300322 (Lesch-Nyhan syndrome; renal failure in ~66% of patients from urate stone disease/obstruction). Frontiers Pediatrics 2022 (PMC9806254): early-onset renal failure as presenting sign of Lesch-Nyhan disease. | intervention: do(HPRT deficiency [Lesch-Nyhan, OMIM 300322] or PRPS1 superactivity [OMIM 300661]) -> plasma_urate UP -> uric acid crystal deposition in renal tubules/collecting duct -> tubular obstruction -> GFR DOWN [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Elevated plasma urate (as in HPRT deficiency / Lesch-Nyhan disease, OMIM 300322, or PRPS1 superactivity, OMIM 300661) leads to supersaturation of uric acid in the renal tubular lumen, particularly in the acidic environment of the collecting duct and distal nephron. Uric acid crystallises in situ, causing intratubular obstruction, raised intrarenal pressure, compression of the renal venous microvasculature, and a consequent fall in renal blood flow and GFR. This is the established mechanism of acute uric acid nephropathy: do(hyperuricaemia) -> crystal-mediated tubular obstruction -> GFR decreases."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/comt_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/epinephrine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acomt_activity%3Anegative%3Aepinephrine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/comt_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/epinephrine> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (catecholamine catabolism: COMT O-methylates epinephrine -> metanephrine, the primary peripheral inactivation step); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (hepatic COMT as the main route of circulating catecholamine inactivation by O-methylation); Williams Textbook of Endocrinology (fractionated plasma metanephrines as COMT-product diagnostic markers in pheochromocytoma; elevated epinephrine substrate drives elevated metanephrine via COMT); pharmacological intervention: COMT inhibitors entacapone/tolcapone (approved for Parkinson's disease adjunct therapy) raise plasma catecholamines/dopamine by blocking this O-methylation step — do(comt_activity=low) -> plasma epinephrine rises; do(comt_activity=high) -> plasma epinephrine falls faster. | intervention: COMT inhibition (entacapone/tolcapone pharmacological block): do(comt_activity=low) -> plasma epinephrine rises; conversely, elevated COMT activity (e.g., higher-activity COMT Val158Met variant) -> faster epinephrine degradation -> lower plasma epinephrine."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT) O-methylates plasma epinephrine to metanephrine as the principal peripheral inactivation route (hepatic and adrenomedullary COMT); higher COMT catalytic activity converts more epinephrine to metanephrine, accelerating clearance of plasma epinephrine. This is the same enzymatic step that generates the metanephrine already encoded in comp99 (comt_activity -> plasma_metanephrine +); its substrate side is the negative effect on epinephrine. COMT inhibitors (entacapone, tolcapone) pharmacologically block this step, raising plasma catecholamines including epinephrine."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_methemoglobin> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hemoglobin_o2_saturation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aerythrocyte_methemoglobin%3Anegative%3Ahemoglobin_o2_saturation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_methemoglobin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hemoglobin_o2_saturation> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Hereditary methemoglobinemia types I and II (OMIM #250800): CYB5R3 LoF raises erythrocyte metHb to 10-70%; patients are cyanotic with reduced true oxyhemoglobin saturation (Percy & Lappin 2008 Lancet 371:2032). Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 'Methemoglobinaemia': metHb fraction directly reduces O2-carrying capacity and SaO2. Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology, 'Haemoglobin and O2 transport': functional Hb = total Hb minus metHb; SaO2 (true) falls proportionally to metHb fraction. Intervention: genetic LoF CYB5R3 (OMIM #250800) — erythrocyte metHb rises, SaO2 falls; pharmacological methemoglobin-inducing agents (dapsone, nitrites) phenocopy. | intervention: Genetic LoF: homozygous CYB5R3 mutation (OMIM #250800, recessive congenital methemoglobinemia types I/II) — erythrocyte methemoglobin rises to 10-70% of total haemoglobin, producing cyanosis and reduced arterial oxygen saturation (SaO2). [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Methemoglobin (Fe3+ haem) cannot bind molecular oxygen and does not participate in O2 transport. When the erythrocyte methemoglobin fraction rises, the proportion of functional oxyhaemoglobin (Fe2+ haem) available at any alveolar PO2 is correspondingly reduced, lowering arterial SaO2. Additionally, methemoglobin allosterically shifts the O2-dissociation curve of the remaining Fe2+ haem leftward (higher O2 affinity, impaired O2 unloading), but the dominant acute effect is the direct reduction in O2-carrying haemoglobin fraction. In hereditary methemoglobinemia (CYB5R3 LoF, OMIM 250800), metHb rises to 10-70% of total haemoglobin and patients present with cyanosis and reduced functional SaO2. do(CYB5R3 LoF) => erythrocyte_methemoglobin UP => hemoglobin_o2_saturation DOWN."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_selenium> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_glutathione_peroxidase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_selenium%3Apositive%3Aerythrocyte_glutathione_peroxidase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_selenium> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_glutathione_peroxidase_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "Interventional selenium supplementation and cellular depletion/repletion. Endpoint measured: erythrocyte glutathione peroxidase (GPX1) activity. Intervention: controlled human selenium supplementation (sodium selenite or selenomethionine) in low-selenium subjects, and cellular selenium depletion followed by re-addition. Meta-analysis of organic-selenium RCTs on erythrocyte GPx (PMID 22443071): pooled controlled supplementation raises erythrocyte GPx activity (SMD ~0.36) in healthy adults — interventional do(Se up) -> GPx up. Thomson/Robinson New Zealand selenium-supplementation series (PMID 4026872): erythrocyte GPx activity rises with selenium then plateaus, valid as a selenium-status indicator only below the plateau (saturable enzyme). Context: human RCTs / depletion studies and cell culture; SATURABLE — sign + holds only in the deficient (below-plateau) range. | intervention: controlled selenium supplementation within the below-plateau range, do(plasma_selenium up) -> erythrocyte glutathione peroxidase activity up"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Selenium is the obligate selenocysteine constituent of the glutathione peroxidase 1 (GPX1) catalytic centre; within the selenium-deficient (below-plateau) range selenium availability is rate-limiting for functional GPX1 selenoprotein synthesis, so raising plasma selenium raises erythrocyte glutathione peroxidase activity. Above the plateau (~90-100 ng/mL plasma Se) the erythrocyte selenoprotein pool is saturated and d(GPx)/d(Se) approaches zero. The additive sign graph cannot express this saturation, so the + sign is asserted ONLY for the below-plateau / selenium-deficient regime, where the relationship is monotone non-decreasing (never negative)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_aspartate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_n_acetylaspartate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_aspartate%3Apositive%3Aplasma_n_acetylaspartate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_aspartate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_n_acetylaspartate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "PMID 32545833; (Introduction; Antioxidants (Basel) 2020;9(6):522); “Aspartate N-acetyltransferase (NAT8L) is a neuronal enzyme producing N-acetylaspartate (NAA) from acetyl-CoA and aspartate” | Aspartate is the amino-acid substrate from which NAT8L synthesises N-acetylaspartate, so do(raise aspartate) raises NAA (sign +). [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "aspartate concentration (substrate availability) -> NAT8L N-acetyltransferase disposition -> N-acetylation process -> plasma N-acetylaspartate concentration"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/slc6a6_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_taurine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aslc6a6_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_taurine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/slc6a6_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_taurine> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "PMID 31345061; (Abstract; Biallelic mutation of human SLC6A6 encoding the taurine transporter TAUT (2019)); “taurine levels were severely reduced in plasma, skeletal muscle, and brain” | Biallelic SLC6A6 (TAUT) loss-of-function lowers plasma taurine, so SLC6A6 transport activity raises plasma taurine (sign +); the monogenic LoF is a clean do(). [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "SLC6A6 (TauT) transport activity (Na/Cl-coupled taurine uptake/renal reabsorption) -> taurine retention process -> plasma taurine concentration"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_methionine> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_methionine_sulfoxide> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_methionine%3Apositive%3Aplasma_methionine_sulfoxide> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_methionine> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_methionine_sulfoxide> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "PMID 30545068; (Introduction; Antioxidants (Basel) 2018;7(12):191); “Methionine side chains can also be oxidized by reactive oxygen species. Methionine oxidation, by the addition of an extra oxygen atom, leads to the generation of methionine sulfoxide.” | Methionine is the substrate that ROS oxidise to methionine sulfoxide, so do(raise methionine) raises the oxidisable pool and methionine sulfoxide (sign +). [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "methionine concentration (oxidizable thioether substrate) -> susceptibility to ROS oxidation -> oxidation process (addition of an oxygen atom) -> methionine sulfoxide concentration"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dystrophin_level> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_3_methylhistidine> .
