    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "PMID 34176136; (Meyer et al., HIBADH deficiency - a novel disorder of valine metabolism (J Inherit Metab Dis), abstract); “Under strict adherence to a low-valine diet a rapid decrease of 3HiB excretion in the urine was observed.” | Reducing the precursor valine lowers urinary 3-hydroxyisobutyrate (3HiB), i.e. valine causally increases urinary 3-hydroxyisobutyrate (sign +). [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "3-hydroxyisobutyrate is an obligatory intermediate of valine catabolism; raising valine flux increases 3-hydroxyisobutyrate production and urinary excretion, and lowering dietary valine (a do() on the precursor) reduces it - establishing plasma valine as a positive causal driver of urinary 3-hydroxyisobutyrate, the analyte that joins this valine-catabolism enzyme island to the core BCAA hub."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_blood_flow> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cardiac_output> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Askeletal_muscle_blood_flow%3Apositive%3Acardiac_output> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_blood_flow> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cardiac_output> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, 12th edition; (Ch. 20, Cardiac Output, Venous Return, and Their Regulation); “The venous return to the heart is the sum of all the local blood flows through all the individual tissue segments of the peripheral circulation. Therefore, it follows that cardiac output regulation is the sum of all the local blood flow regulations.” | Local tissue blood flow (here skeletal-muscle blood flow) sums into venous return and thereby sets cardiac output, supporting skeletal_muscle_blood_flow -> cardiac_output with sign +."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Skeletal-muscle vasodilation (e.g. driven by intracellular ATP/creatine-phosphate depletion during work) increases local muscle blood flow; because venous return is the sum of all local tissue blood flows, increasing muscle blood flow raises venous return and therefore cardiac output - the Guyton local-flow control of cardiac output."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/rnasel_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/blood_viral_load> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Arnasel_activity%3Anegative%3Ablood_viral_load> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/rnasel_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/blood_viral_load> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "PMID 9525594; (Title/abstract: RNase L mediates the antiviral effect of interferon through a selective reduction in viral RNA during encephalomyocarditis virus infection); “RNase L induction markedly enhanced the anti-EMCV activity of IFN via a reduction in EMCV RNA.” | Inducing RNase L activity reduces viral RNA/viral burden, supporting rnasel_activity -> blood_viral_load with sign - (interventional induction of the enzyme). [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "2-5A produced by OAS activates RNase L, whose endoribonuclease activity cleaves viral single-stranded RNA, degrading the viral genome and reducing viral replication; inducing/perturbing RNase L activity therefore lowers viral load, connecting the OAS/2-5A/RNase L antiviral island to the systemic blood viral load hub."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta_hexosaminidase_b_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_beta_hexosaminidase> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abeta_hexosaminidase_b_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_beta_hexosaminidase> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta_hexosaminidase_b_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_beta_hexosaminidase> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "PMID 28553389; (Sandhoff Disease without Hepatosplenomegaly Due to Hexosaminidase B Gene Mutation, abstract/introduction); “Sandhoff disease is a neurodegenerative disease caused due to deficiency of hexosaminidase (HEX) A and B.” | HEXB (beta-subunit) loss causes deficiency of both HexA and HexB, so loss of beta-hexosaminidase-B activity lowers total (serum) hexosaminidase, supporting beta_hexosaminidase_b_activity -> plasma_beta_hexosaminidase with sign +. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "The beta subunit (HEXB) is shared by both hexosaminidase isoenzymes (HexA = alpha-beta, HexB = beta-beta); biallelic HEXB loss-of-function (Sandhoff disease, a natural do()) abolishes both isoenzymes, so total serum beta-hexosaminidase falls - hence beta-hexosaminidase-B activity positively determines measured serum total hexosaminidase, joining the lysosomal HEX/GM2 island to the core serum analyte."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ppt1_activity> obo:RO_0002411 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_complex_iv_activity> .
