    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology, GI chapter (gastrin -> parietal acid secretion) | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-098 (17.11: Organs with Secondary Endocrine Functions): 'gastrin, a peptide hormone secreted in response to stomach distention that stimulates the release of hydrochloric acid'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-132 (23.3: Digestive System Processes and Regulation): 'Gastrin stimulates the secretion of gastric acid by the parietal cells of the stomach mucosa'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-134 (23.5: The Stomach): hormone table — gastrin 'Increases secretion by gastric glands'; text: 'gastrin … induces parietal cells to increase their production of HCl'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-34.4 (34.4: Digestive System Regulation): 'Gastrin stimulates the release of stomach acid, or hydrochloric acid (HCl) which aids in the digestion of the proteins'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "gastrin stimulates parietal-cell H+/K+-ATPase acid secretion (directly + via ECL-cell histamine)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gastric_acid_secretion> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gastrin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Agastric_acid_secretion%3Anegative%3Agastrin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gastric_acid_secretion> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gastrin> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; dcterms:source "Schubert 2020, Comprehensive Physiology 10(1):197-228, doi:10.1002/cphy.c180035 (low antral pH -> D-cell somatostatin -| gastrin); StatPearls 'Physiology, Gastrin' NBK534822 (pH<3 negative feedback on gastrin) | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-134 (23.5: The Stomach): 'Whenever pH levels drop too low, cells in the stomach react by suspending HCl secretion' — acid negative feedback on gastrin loop; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-34.4 (34.4: Digestive System Regulation): 'when the stomach is emptied, the acidic environment need not be maintained and a hormone called somatostatin stops the release of hydrochloric acid. This is controlled by a negative feedback mechanism.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "antral luminal acidification (low pH) releases somatostatin from antral D cells, which paracrine-inhibits G-cell gastrin secretion — the antral-pH negative feedback that makes the gastric-acid arm a regulated loop (gastrin -> acid -| gastrin)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gastric_emptying_rate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Agastric_emptying_rate%3Apositive%3Aplasma_glucose> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gastric_emptying_rate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "E-DES BioModels MODEL2403070001 (Maas 2015 J Diabetes Sci Technol): gut glucose-appearance term raises plasma glucose; Deane 2010 JCEM"
    ; pmo:mechanism "faster gastric emptying accelerates intestinal glucose appearance into plasma"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_insulin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_glucose%3Apositive%3Aplasma_insulin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_insulin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Bergman minimal model (1979/1981); Topp 2000 beta-cell model; HumMod pancreas; textbook | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-005 (1.6: Homeostasis): 'pancreatic beta cells respond to the increased level of blood glucose by releasing the hormone insulin'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-090 (17.3: Hormones): 'High blood glucose levels cause the release of insulin from the pancreas'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-097 (17.10: The Endocrine Pancreas): elevated blood glucose levels stimulate beta-cell insulin release (glucose-stimulated insulin secretion); OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-142 (24.6: Metabolic States of the Body): 'The ingestion of food and the rise of glucose concentrations in the bloodstream stimulate pancreatic beta cells to release insulin into the bloodstream.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-144 (24.8: Nutrition and Diet) — 'As blood glucose levels rise, the pancreas releases insulin to stimulate the uptake of glucose'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-33.3 (Homeostasis): 'When an animal has eaten, blood glucose levels rise...the hormone insulin is released by the endocrine system.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.1 (37.1: Types of Hormones): 'They are then released in response to stimuli such as high blood glucose levels in the case of insulin'; diagnostic context confirms pancreas stimulated by high glucose produces insulin to decrease blood glucose levels.; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.4 (37.4: Regulation of Hormone Production): rise in blood glucose triggers pancreatic release of insulin (humoral stimulus example); OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.5 (37.5: Endocrine Glands): 'When blood glucose levels rise, such as after a meal, beta cells release insulin to lower blood glucose levels.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "glucose-stimulated insulin secretion (beta-cell)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucagon> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_glucose%3Anegative%3Aplasma_glucagon> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucagon> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Sturis 1991; HumMod pancreas (alpha cell); standard endocrinology | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-097 (17.10: The Endocrine Pancreas): low blood glucose stimulates alpha-cell glucagon release; rising glucose inhibits further glucagon secretion (negative feedback); OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-142 (24.6: Metabolic States of the Body): 'In response to a drop in blood glucose concentration, the hormone glucagon is released from the alpha cells of the pancreas.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-144 (24.8: Nutrition and Diet) — 'Falling glucose levels trigger the pancreas to release glucagon'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-33.3 (Homeostasis): 'if an animal has not eaten and blood glucose levels decrease, this is sensed in another group of cells in the pancreas, and the hormone glucagon is released.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.5 (37.5: Endocrine Glands): 'As blood glucose levels decline, alpha cells release glucagon to raise the blood glucose levels.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "hyperglycaemia suppresses alpha-cell glucagon secretion"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_insulin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/peripheral_glucose_uptake> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_insulin%3Apositive%3Aperipheral_glucose_uptake> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_insulin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/peripheral_glucose_uptake> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"disruption of GLUT4 in muscle or adipose tissue causes an insulin-resistant phenotype\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 34, p 1356-1357  ||  \"Increases in insulin in response to rising blood glucose levels facilitate cellular glucose uptake\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 36, p 1428"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Insulin stimulates GLUT4 translocation to plasma membrane in muscle and adipose; disruption of GLUT4 causes insulin-resistant phenotype"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_insulin> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_glucose_production> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_insulin%3Anegative%3Ahepatic_glucose_production> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_insulin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_glucose_production> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Bergman minimal model (insulin suppresses net hepatic glucose output); HumMod liver | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-097 (17.10: The Endocrine Pancreas): insulin inhibits enzymes involved in glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, suppressing hepatic glucose output; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-138 (24.2: Overview of Metabolic Reactions): 'insulin... store it in the liver and muscle as glycogen; its effect is the opposite of glucagon'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "insulin suppresses hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucagon> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_glucose_production> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_glucagon%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_glucose_production> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucagon> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_glucose_production> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "HumMod liver; Sturis 1991; standard endocrinology | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-090 (17.3: Hormones): 'Glucagon: Increases blood glucose levels'; 'glucagon stimulates the breakdown of glycogen stores, increasing blood glucose'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-097 (17.10: The Endocrine Pancreas): glucagon stimulates hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, raising blood glucose; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-138 (24.2: Overview of Metabolic Reactions): 'glucagon... stimulates the breakdown of glycogen in the liver to increase blood glucose levels'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-144 (24.8: Nutrition and Diet) — glucagon released 'to turn off glycogen synthesis in the liver and stimulate its breakdown into glucose'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "glucagon drives hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_glucose_production> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahepatic_glucose_production%3Apositive%3Aplasma_glucose> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_glucose_production> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Bergman minimal model glucose mass balance (EGP appearance term)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "hepatic glucose output adds glucose to plasma pool"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/peripheral_glucose_uptake> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aperipheral_glucose_uptake%3Anegative%3Aplasma_glucose> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/peripheral_glucose_uptake> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Bergman minimal model glucose mass balance (disposal/clearance term) | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-005 (1.6: Homeostasis): 'As glucose concentration in the bloodstream drops, the decrease in concentration... is detected by pancreatic alpha cells, and insulin release stops'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "peripheral disposal removes glucose from plasma pool"
    .
