<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ghrh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aghrh%3Apositive%3Apituitary_gh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ghrh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Farhy 2007 BioModels MODEL0912096133 (PMID 17185408); MacGregor & Leng 2005 BioModels MODEL9811206584 (PMID 16280026); Williams Endocrinology | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-091 (17.4: The Pituitary Gland and Hypothalamus): 'GH levels are controlled by the release of GHRH and GHIH (also known as somatostatin) from the hypothalamus.' [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GHRH binds somatotroph GHRH receptor (Gs/cAMP), stimulating GH synthesis and pulsatile release — raising the pituitary's GH secretory output."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/somatostatin> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asomatostatin%3Anegative%3Apituitary_gh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/somatostatin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Farhy 2007 BioModels MODEL0912096133 (PMID 17185408); MacGregor & Leng 2005 BioModels MODEL9811206584 (PMID 16280026); Williams Endocrinology | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-090 (17.3: Hormones): 'when growth hormone-inhibiting hormone (GHIH), also known as somatostatin, binds to its receptors in the pituitary gland, the level of cAMP decreases, thereby inhibiting the secretion of human growth hormone'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-091 (17.4: The Pituitary Gland and Hypothalamus): 'GH levels are controlled by the release of GHRH and GHIH (also known as somatostatin) from the hypothalamus.' [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Somatostatin (SRIF) binds somatotroph SST receptors (Gi), inhibiting cAMP and suppressing the pituitary's GH secretory output."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ghrelin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aghrelin%3Apositive%3Apituitary_gh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ghrelin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Farhy 2007 BioModels MODEL0912096133 (PMID 17185408); Williams Endocrinology (ghrelin/GHS-R) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Ghrelin acts on somatotroph GHS-R1a to amplify GH secretory output, synergistic with GHRH."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ghrelin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ghrh> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aghrelin%3Apositive%3Aghrh> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ghrelin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ghrh> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Farhy 2007 BioModels MODEL0912096133 (PMID 17185408); Williams Endocrinology [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Ghrelin stimulates hypothalamic GHRH neurons, increasing GHRH output."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh_secretion> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apituitary_gh_secretion%3Apositive%3Apituitary_gh> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh_secretion> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Farhy 2007 BioModels MODEL0912096133 (PMID 17185408) and MacGregor & Leng 2005 BioModels MODEL9811206584 (PMID 16280026) both model plasma GH as the pituitary secretory output into the circulation (secretion vs clearance). Curated by Paul N. Schofield. *** DRAFT FOR REVIEW ***"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Anterior-pituitary GH secretory output sets the circulating GH concentration (mass balance: plasma [GH] = secretion − metabolic clearance)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ghrh> obo:RO_0002411 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/somatostatin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aghrh%3Aunknown%3Asomatostatin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ghrh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/somatostatin> ; pmo:sign "?"
    ; dcterms:source "Horvath et al. 1992 (intrahypothalamic GHRH/SRIF interactions, PMID 1358561) report GHRH terminals contacting periventricular SRIF neurons, but Kovacs et al. 2015 (Neuroscience, GHRH/SST juxtapositions in human hypothalamus, doi:10.1016/j.neuroscience.2015.04.006) find GHRH fibres avoid SRIF perikarya — direction of the forward arm is unsettled, sign '?'. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Forward arm of the reciprocal GHRH/SRIF hypothalamic coupling. Anatomical evidence is genuinely mixed: GHRH terminals have been reported to contact periventricular somatostatin neurons (rat), but in the human hypothalamus GHRH fibres commonly avoid somatostatinergic perikarya, arguing against a strong direct GHRH->SRIF drive. Sign undetermined; flagged '?' rather than guessing. The well-established, load-bearing arm of the coupling is the reverse edge somatostatin -| ghrh."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/somatostatin> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ghrh> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asomatostatin%3Anegative%3Aghrh> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/somatostatin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ghrh> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "Osterstock et al. 2016, Sci Rep 6:24394 'Somatostatin triggers rhythmic electrical firing in hypothalamic GHRH neurons' (PMID 27072430) — direct demonstration somatostatin suppresses GHRH neuron activity; MacGregor & Leng 2005 BioModels MODEL9811206584 (PMID 16280026, reciprocal coupling encoded as SRIF -| GHRH) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Somatostatin reciprocally inhibits GHRH neuron activity within the hypothalamic oscillator (initial K+-channel-mediated suppression of GHRH neuron firing)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/somatostatin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apituitary_gh%3Apositive%3Asomatostatin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/somatostatin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "MacGregor & Leng 2005 BioModels MODEL9811206584 (PMID 16280026); Farhy 2007 BioModels MODEL0912096133; Williams Endocrinology [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GH short-loop feedback: GH stimulates hypothalamic somatostatin release, restraining further GH secretion."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_igf1> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apituitary_gh%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_igf1> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_igf1> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Farhy 2007 BioModels MODEL0912096133; Williams Endocrinology; Boron & Boulpaep | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-091 (17.4: The Pituitary Gland and Hypothalamus): 'GH indirectly mediates growth and protein synthesis by triggering the liver and other tissues to produce a group of proteins called insulin-like growth factors (IGFs).'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.3 (37.3: Regulation of Body Processes): 'The indirect mechanism of GH action is mediated by insulin-like growth factors (IGFs) or somatomedins, which are a family of growth-promoting proteins produced by the liver.' [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GH binds hepatic GH receptor (JAK2/STAT5), inducing IGF-1 gene transcription and secretion of circulating IGF-1."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_igf1> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahepatic_igf1%3Anegative%3Apituitary_gh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_igf1> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Farhy 2007 BioModels MODEL0912096133; Williams Endocrinology (IGF-1 negative feedback) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "IGF-1 acts directly on somatotrophs to inhibit GH gene expression and secretory output (pituitary arm of long-loop feedback)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_igf1> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/somatostatin> .
