    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Semenza 2009; Haase 2013 Blood Reviews (HIF-driven EPO); HumMod renal O2 -> EPO | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-098 (17.11: Organs with Secondary Endocrine Functions): 'the kidneys produce the hormone erythropoietin (EPO) in response to low oxygen levels'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-101 (18.3: Production of the Formed Elements): 'Erythropoietin (EPO) is a glycoprotein hormone secreted by the interstitial fibroblast cells of the kidneys in response to low oxygen levels.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-102 (18.4: Erythrocytes): 'In a classic negative-feedback loop, as oxygen saturation rises, EPO secretion falls, and vice versa' — kidney interstitial fibroblasts secrete EPO in response to hypoxemia.; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-129 (22.7: Modifications in Respiratory Functions): 'the kidneys are stimulated to produce the hormone erythropoietin (EPO)' in response to low tissue O2 at high altitude; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-154 (25.11: The Urinary System and Homeostasis): 'If you move to a higher altitude, the partial pressure of oxygen is lower... One way the body compensates is to manufacture more red blood cells by increasing EPO production' (lines 27-29); 'Renal failure (loss of EPO production) is associated with anemia' (lines 32-33)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "hypoxia stabilizes HIF-2alpha (reduced PHD/VHL degradation), driving renal peritubular EPO transcription; higher tissue O2 suppresses EPO"
    .
