    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il1beta> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/core_temperature> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ail1beta%3Apositive%3Acore_temperature> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il1beta> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/core_temperature> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (endogenous pyrogens IL-1/IL-6/TNF -> hypothalamic PGE2 -> fever); Janeway's Immunobiology | perturbation: i.p. recombinant IL-1beta evokes fever in wild-type mice (opened PMC2192408) — controlled administration of the source raises core temperature | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-33.3 (Homeostasis): 'When a bacterium is destroyed by phagocytic leukocytes, chemicals called endogenous pyrogens are released into the blood. These pyrogens circulate to the hypothalamus and reset the thermostat. This allows the body's temperature to increase in what is commonly called a fever.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-42.1 (42.1: Innate Immune Response): 'Cytokines also increase the core body temperature, causing a fever' — IL-1beta is a principal pro-inflammatory cytokine driving pyrogenic fever."
    ; pmo:mechanism "IL-1beta is an endogenous pyrogen -> hypothalamic PGE2 -> raised thermoregulatory set point (fever)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/core_temperature> .
