    ; dcterms:source "StatPearls NBK541119 (Physiology, Gluconeogenesis): the liver uses blood lactate to produce glucose, so lactate supply supports gluconeogenic flux. Lehninger 7th ed. ch.14-15 (Cori cycle, lactate -> glucose) | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-7.5 (7.5: Metabolism without Oxygen): 'once the lactic acid has been removed from the muscle and circulated to the liver, it can be reconverted into pyruvic acid and further catabolized for energy'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "lactate is a primary gluconeogenic substrate (Cori cycle); increased blood-lactate supply drives hepatic gluconeogenic flux"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_free_fatty_acids> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_ketogenesis> .
