    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "StatPearls NBK493179 (Biochemistry, Ketogenesis): elevated FFA supply to the liver augments hepatic ketogenesis. Lehninger 7th ed. ch.17 (ketone bodies formed from fatty-acid-derived acetyl-CoA) | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-142 (24.6: Metabolic States of the Body): 'fatty acids and triglyceride stores are used to create ketones for the body' during starvation.; Wong, Cells: Molecules and Mechanisms §wong-5.7 (5.7 Starch and Glycogen Depolymerization Undeclared): 'a large part of the acetyl-CoA generated by oxidation of fatty acids does not enter the TCA cycle. Instead, it is converted into acetoacetate or D-beta-hydroxybutyrate.'; Wong, Cells: Molecules and Mechanisms §wong-5.7 (5.7 Starch and Glycogen Depolymerization Undeclared): 'in liver especially, a large part of the acetyl-CoA generated by oxidation of fatty acids... is converted into acetoacetate or D-β-hydroxybutyrate' — fatty acid beta-oxidation (from plasma FFA) is the substrate supply for hepatic ketogenesis.; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-7.6 (7.6: Connections of Carbohydrate, Protein, and Lipid Metabolic Pathways): 'The acetyl groups are picked up by CoA to form acetyl CoA that proceeds into the citric acid cycle.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "plasma FFA is transported to the liver where beta-oxidation generates acetyl-CoA directed into ketone-body synthesis; elevated FFA supply augments ketogenic flux"
    .
