    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger 7th ed. ch.15 & ch.23 (glucagon stimulates hepatic glycogenolysis); Boron & Boulpaep 3rd ed. ch.58 | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-097 (17.10: The Endocrine Pancreas): glucagon stimulates liver to convert glycogen back to glucose (glycogenolysis); OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-142 (24.6: Metabolic States of the Body): 'Glucagon acts upon the liver cells, where it inhibits the synthesis of glycogen and stimulates the breakdown of stored glycogen back into glucose.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "glucagon (cAMP/PKA) activates glycogen phosphorylase, inhibits glycogen synthase"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucagon> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_gluconeogenesis> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_glucagon%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_gluconeogenesis> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucagon> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_gluconeogenesis> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger 7th ed. ch.15 & ch.23; Boron & Boulpaep 3rd ed. ch.58 | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-097 (17.10: The Endocrine Pancreas): glucagon stimulates liver to take up amino acids and convert them to glucose (gluconeogenesis); OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-142 (24.6: Metabolic States of the Body): 'Gluconeogenesis will also begin in the liver to replace the glucose that has been used by the peripheral tissues.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "glucagon induces PEPCK, lowers fructose-2,6-bisphosphate, driving gluconeogenesis"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucagon> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_ketogenesis> .
