    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (iron-limited erythropoiesis); Ganz & Nemeth 2012 BBA review; Parmar2019 BioModels MODEL1805140003 [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "transferrin-bound iron is the obligatory substrate for hemoglobin synthesis in erythroid precursors; iron availability is rate-limiting for effective erythropoiesis and red-cell production"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_pyruvate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_organic_acids> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_pyruvate%3Apositive%3Aurinary_organic_acids> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_pyruvate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_organic_acids> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 16 (pyruvate as organic acid, renal filtration/excretion); pyruvate dehydrogenase complex deficiency (OMIM 312170): elevated plasma pyruvate and increased urinary organic acids are diagnostic; Scriver's OMMBID (organic aciduria overview: elevated organic acids in urine reflect elevated plasma precursors) | intervention: do(PDC deficiency / plasma pyruvate elevation)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Pyruvate is an organic acid; when plasma pyruvate is elevated (e.g., pyruvate dehydrogenase complex deficiency, PDC def), it spills into urine and directly elevates total urinary organic acid output — do(plasma pyruvate UP) => urinary organic acids UP"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_alpha_ketoglutarate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_ketoacids> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_alpha_ketoglutarate%3Apositive%3Aurinary_ketoacids> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_alpha_ketoglutarate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_ketoacids> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase complex deficiency / OGDH deficiency (OMIM 203740): elevated urine alpha-ketoglutarate is a hallmark (MedLink Neurology, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase deficiency review); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 16 (TCA cycle: alpha-ketoglutarate = 2-oxoglutarate, a keto acid; renal organic acid excretion) | intervention: do(OGDH/alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase deficiency)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Alpha-ketoglutarate (2-oxoglutarate) is a 2-oxo monocarboxylic acid (keto acid); elevated plasma alpha-KG exceeds the renal reabsorption threshold, spilling into urine and raising total urinary keto acid levels — do(plasma alpha-KG UP) => urinary keto acids UP"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aco2_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_citrate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aaco2_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_citrate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aco2_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_citrate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 16 (aconitase: citrate -> cis-aconitate -> isocitrate); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (renal citrate handling) | intervention: do(ACO2 loss-of-function) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Aconitase 2 (ACO2) catalyses the isomerisation of citrate to isocitrate via cis-aconitate in the TCA cycle; reduced ACO2 activity leaves citrate unconsumed, causing it to accumulate — do(ACO2 deficiency) => citrate UP. Citrate is excreted in urine (major urinary organic acid)."
    .
