    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Hartnup disease (SLC6A19, OMIM 234500): characterized by indoluria including elevated urinary indole-3-acetic acid due to excess tryptophan reaching gut bacteria; Saudubray, Inborn Metabolic Diseases — Hartnup disorder: excess colonic tryptophan fermented to indoles including indoleacetic acid; Blau, Physician's Guide to Inherited Metabolic Diseases | intervention: SLC6A19 loss-of-function (Hartnup disease)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "SLC6A19 (B0AT1) mediates intestinal and renal neutral amino acid (especially tryptophan) absorption; LoF (Hartnup disease) reduces intestinal tryptophan uptake, increasing luminal tryptophan available to gut bacteria, which ferment tryptophan via indole-3-pyruvate to indole-3-acetic acid (IAA); elevated IAA is then absorbed and excreted in urine — reduced transporter activity raises urinary IAA"
    .
