    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Hereditary tyrosinemia type 1 (FAH deficiency, OMIM 276700): succinylacetone is the pathognomonic biomarker — Saudubray, Inborn Metabolic Diseases; Scriver's OMMBID (tyrosinemia type I: elevated plasma and urine succinylacetone is the diagnostic hallmark); Blau, Physician's Guide | intervention: FAH loss-of-function (hereditary tyrosinemia type 1)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Fumarylacetoacetase (FAH) catalyzes the last step of tyrosine catabolism, cleaving fumarylacetoacetate to fumarate and acetoacetate; FAH deficiency causes fumarylacetoacetate to accumulate and be converted to succinylacetoacetate and then succinylacetone — reduced FAH activity raises plasma succinylacetone"
    .
