    ; dcterms:source "HIBCH deficiency (OMIM 250620): urinary metabolite investigations show increased 2,3-dihydroxy-2-methylbutyrate as a diagnostic marker indicating methacrylyl-CoA accumulation — Saudubray, Inborn Metabolic Diseases; Ferdinandusse et al., J Inherit Metab Dis 2015 (PMID 26163321: metabolite studies in HIBCH defects, increases in 2,3-dihydroxy-2-methylbutyrate) | intervention: HIBCH loss-of-function (3-hydroxyisobutyryl-CoA hydrolase deficiency) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "3-Hydroxyisobutyryl-CoA hydrolase (HIBCH) cleaves 3-hydroxyisobutyryl-CoA to free 3-hydroxyisobutyrate and CoA in valine catabolism; HIBCH deficiency causes 3-hydroxyisobutyryl-CoA to accumulate and back up to methacrylyl-CoA (a reactive electrophile), which undergoes conjugation reactions producing 2,3-dihydroxy-2-methylbutyrate as a urinary shunt metabolite — reduced HIBCH activity raises urinary 2,3-dihydroxy-2-methylbutyrate"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cftr_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sweat_chloride> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acftr_activity%3Anegative%3Asweat_chloride> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cftr_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sweat_chloride> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology, sweat gland physiology: CFTR mediates apical Cl− reabsorption in the duct; cystic fibrosis (CFTR LoF) is diagnosed by sweat chloride ≥60 mmol/L. Confirmed: elevated sweat chloride is the diagnostic criterion for CF (OMIM 219700). | intervention: CFTR loss-of-function (cystic fibrosis, OMIM 219700)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CFTR is the principal chloride channel in the sweat gland duct epithelium; it reabsorbs chloride from the sweat duct lumen back into cells. Loss of CFTR activity (cystic fibrosis, CFTR LoF) abolishes ductal chloride reabsorption, so sweat chloride rises. do(CFTR_activity down) → sweat_chloride up."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/slc26a3_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/stool_chloride> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aslc26a3_activity%3Anegative%3Astool_chloride> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/slc26a3_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/stool_chloride> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Frontiers Physiology 2015, PMID:34988036 (SLC26A3 LoF / congenital chloride diarrhea, OMIM 214700): elevated fecal chloride >90 mmol/L is the pathognomonic finding. Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology, intestinal electrolyte absorption. | intervention: SLC26A3 loss-of-function (congenital chloride diarrhea, OMIM 214700) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "SLC26A3 (DRA) mediates electroneutral Cl−/HCO3− exchange at the apical membrane of ileal and colonic enterocytes, absorbing luminal chloride. Loss-of-function mutation (congenital chloride diarrhea, CLD) abolishes intestinal chloride reabsorption, causing massive fecal chloride loss (>90 mmol/L stool chloride, pathognomonic for CLD)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/slc9a3_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fecal_sodium> .
