    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Bogdanic et al. Mol Genet Genomic Med 2022 (PMID:35775128); OMIM 616868 (DIAR8, SLC9A3 LoF): elevated fecal sodium, metabolic acidosis, watery diarrhea. Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology, Na+ absorption in the intestine. | intervention: SLC9A3/NHE3 loss-of-function (congenital secretory sodium diarrhea, OMIM 616868) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "SLC9A3/NHE3 is the main apical Na+/H+ exchanger in enterocytes, responsible for electroneutral intestinal sodium absorption. Loss-of-function mutation (congenital secretory sodium diarrhea, DIAR8) abolishes NHE3-mediated Na+ absorption, leading to excessive fecal sodium loss."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_chloride> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/serum_anion_gap> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_chloride%3Anegative%3Aserum_anion_gap> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_chloride> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/serum_anion_gap> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "NCBI Bookshelf StatPearls: Biochemistry, Anion Gap (NBK539757): anion gap = Na − (Cl + CO2); Wikipedia Anion gap. Harrison's PIM acid-base chapter: serum AG formula and clinical interpretation. | intervention: Algebraic definition: do(plasma_chloride) directly determines term in AG formula"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Serum anion gap is the algebraic identity AG = [Na+] − ([Cl−] + [HCO3−]). Because Cl− appears as a negative term, plasma chloride is inversely related to anion gap: do(plasma_chloride down) → serum_anion_gap up (hyperchloremic normal-AG acidosis; do(plasma_chloride up) → AG down)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intestinal_lactase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fecal_ph> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aintestinal_lactase_activity%3Apositive%3Afecal_ph> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intestinal_lactase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fecal_ph> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lancet (PMID:6691) ‘Intestinal pH and propulsion’: lactase deficiency → fecal pH <5.5. Children’s Hospital of Philadelphia, Carbohydrate Malabsorption: stool pH <5.5 diagnostic for carbohydrate malabsorption. Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (colonic fermentation of undigested carbohydrates). | intervention: Lactase (LCT) loss-of-function (primary hypolactasia, OMIM 223100) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Lactase (LCT) hydrolyses dietary lactose in the small intestinal brush border. Lactase deficiency (or any intestinal disaccharidase insufficiency) causes unabsorbed carbohydrate to reach the colon, where bacterial fermentation produces short-chain organic acids (lactate, acetate, succinate), lowering fecal pH (<5.5). More lactase activity → more lactose absorbed → less colonic fermentation → higher (less acidic) fecal pH."
    .
