<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_biliverdin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_biliverdin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_biliverdin%3Apositive%3Aurinary_biliverdin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_biliverdin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_biliverdin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "StatPearls NBK549781 (Biochemistry, Biliverdin: biliverdin is water-soluble and can be renally filtered). PMC1145080: ‘urinary excretion of isomers of biliverdin after destruction of haemoproteins in vivo’. Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (renal filtration depends on water-solubility). | intervention: do(plasma_biliverdin up) via heme oxygenase overactivation or impaired biliverdin reductase"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Biliverdin is water-soluble (unlike unconjugated bilirubin) and, when present in plasma at elevated concentrations, passes freely through the glomerular filter and appears in urine. Elevated plasma biliverdin (e.g., impaired biliverdin reductase) directly drives urinary biliverdin excretion."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_conjugated_bilirubin_excretion> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/stool_urobilinogen> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahepatic_conjugated_bilirubin_excretion%3Apositive%3Astool_urobilinogen> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_conjugated_bilirubin_excretion> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/stool_urobilinogen> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Nature Microbiology 2024 (PMC10769871): BilR is the gut microbial enzyme reducing bilirubin to urobilinogen; biliary supply is the rate-limiting substrate. Lehninger, heme catabolism (bile bilirubin → colonic bacteria → urobilinogen/stercobilinogen). Harrison's PIM, Jaundice (biliary obstruction drastically reduces fecal urobilinogen). | intervention: do(hepatic_conjugated_bilirubin_excretion) altered by MRP2/ABCC2 LoF (Dubin-Johnson, OMIM 237500) or bile duct obstruction"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Conjugated bilirubin secreted by hepatocytes via MRP2/ABCC2 into bile reaches the distal ileum and colon, where gut microbiota (e.g., BilR-expressing Firmicutes) reduce it to urobilinogen and stercobilinogen. Greater biliary bilirubin excretion supplies more substrate, increasing fecal urobilinogen."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/stool_urobilinogen> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_urobilinogen> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Astool_urobilinogen%3Apositive%3Aurinary_urobilinogen> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/stool_urobilinogen> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_urobilinogen> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "StatPearls (Physiology, Bilirubin NBK470290): urobilinogen enterohepatic cycle; a fraction reaches urine. Wikipedia Urobilinogen: ‘~10–20% is reabsorbed and re-excreted in urine’. Lehninger, heme catabolism. | intervention: do(biliary bilirubin) via hemolysis, hepatocellular disease; increased urobilinogen pool → both fecal and urinary urobilinogen rise"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Approximately 10–20% of intestinal urobilinogen is reabsorbed through the intestinal mucosa into portal blood (enterohepatic circulation); the fraction that escapes hepatic re-uptake is filtered by the kidney and excreted in urine. Elevated fecal/intestinal urobilinogen raises portal urobilinogen and hence urinary urobilinogen."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/stool_urobilinogen> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urine_urobilinogen> .
