    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger, heme catabolism; StatPearls (Physiology, Bilirubin NBK470290): obstructive jaundice reduces intestinal urobilinogen → low fecal AND low urinary urobilinogen. Harrison’s PIM, Jaundice. | intervention: do(biliary obstruction) → stool_urobilinogen falls → urine_urobilinogen falls"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Same enterohepatic mechanism as urinary_urobilinogen (the two nodes represent the same analyte in different HPO directional contexts). Intestinal urobilinogen pool determines both fecal concentration and the fraction reabsorbed and renally excreted. When biliary output falls (obstructive jaundice) both stool and urine urobilinogen decrease."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sulfite_oxidase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_thiosulfate> .
