    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Fernandez-Salguero et al. (Nicotine metabolite ratio as index of CYP2A6 activity). Benowitz et al. PMC3617060 (The ability of plasma cotinine to predict nicotine exposure is altered by CYP2A6 differences). | intervention: do(CYP2A6 LoF/GoF) — CYP2A6 activity level determines plasma cotinine from a fixed nicotine dose [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CYP2A6 is responsible for ~90% of hepatic nicotine metabolism to cotinine via C-oxidation; CYP2A6 poor metabolizers (LoF variants) produce significantly less cotinine from a given nicotine dose, while extensive metabolizers show higher cotinine from the same nicotine exposure."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glycerol_kinase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_glycerol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aglycerol_kinase_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_glycerol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glycerol_kinase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_glycerol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 'Inborn errors of lipid metabolism' (glycerol kinase deficiency: hyperglycerolemia and glyceroluria). OMIM 307030. PMC11540572 (In vivo glycerol metabolism in GKD patients). | intervention: do(GK LoF) — glycerol kinase deficiency causes urinary glycerol excretion"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Glycerol kinase (GK) phosphorylates glycerol to glycerol-3-phosphate for triglyceride/gluconeogenesis metabolism; deficiency (glycerol kinase deficiency, OMIM 307030) causes hyperglycerolemia and glyceroluria due to failure of glycerol clearance."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tissue_nonspecific_alp_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_phosphoethanolamine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atissue_nonspecific_alp_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_phosphoethanolamine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tissue_nonspecific_alp_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_phosphoethanolamine> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Whyte 2016 PMC4824800 'Alkaline Phosphatase and Hypophosphatasia' (phosphoethanolamine, PPi, and PLP accumulate in HPP; phosphoethanolamine is a diagnostic marker of hypophosphatasia). Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 'Hypophosphatasia'. | intervention: do(ALPL LoF) — genetic loss-of-function of tissue-nonspecific alkaline phosphatase"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Tissue-nonspecific alkaline phosphatase (TNAP/ALPL) hydrolyzes phosphoethanolamine as one of its natural substrates; ALPL deficiency (hypophosphatasia, OMIM 241500/241510/146300) causes phosphoethanolamine to accumulate and become elevated in plasma and urine."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tissue_nonspecific_alp_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_pyrophosphate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atissue_nonspecific_alp_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_pyrophosphate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tissue_nonspecific_alp_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_pyrophosphate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Whyte 2016 PMC4824800 (elevated plasma PPi in hypophosphatasia). PMC8684482 (reference range for plasma PPi in children). Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 'Hypophosphatasia' (PPi accumulation as hallmark). | intervention: do(ALPL LoF) — genetic loss-of-function of tissue-nonspecific alkaline phosphatase"
    ; pmo:mechanism "TNAP hydrolyzes extracellular inorganic pyrophosphate (PPi); deficiency (hypophosphatasia) causes PPi to accumulate in plasma, inhibiting hydroxyapatite crystal growth and causing soft bones and pseudo-gout."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tissue_nonspecific_alp_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_pyrophosphate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atissue_nonspecific_alp_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_pyrophosphate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tissue_nonspecific_alp_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_pyrophosphate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Whyte 2016 PMC4824800 (hypophosphatasia: elevated PPi in plasma and urine). Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 'Hypophosphatasia'. | intervention: do(ALPL LoF) — genetic loss-of-function of tissue-nonspecific alkaline phosphatase"
    ; pmo:mechanism "TNAP hydrolyzes PPi; ALPL deficiency (hypophosphatasia) elevates systemic PPi which is cleared renally, raising urinary PPi excretion."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hmg_coa_lyase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_acetoacetate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahmg_coa_lyase_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_acetoacetate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hmg_coa_lyase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_acetoacetate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, ketogenesis (HMG-CoA lyase produces acetoacetate from HMG-CoA). PMC9136170 (HMG-CoA lyase deficiency: hypoketotic hypoglycemia, OMIM 246450). | intervention: do(HMGCL LoF) — genetic loss-of-function of HMG-CoA lyase reduces plasma acetoacetate"
    ; pmo:mechanism "HMG-CoA lyase cleaves HMG-CoA to acetoacetate and acetyl-CoA, the final step of hepatic ketogenesis; deficiency (HMGCL deficiency, OMIM 246450) abolishes ketone body synthesis, causing hypoketotic hypoglycemia — plasma acetoacetate falls (reduced synthesis). Conversely, elevated HMG-CoA lyase activity drives more acetoacetate production."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/qdpr_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_biopterin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aqdpr_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_biopterin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/qdpr_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_biopterin> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Blau et al., Physician's Guide to Inherited Metabolic Diseases (DHPR/QDPR deficiency: elevated urinary biopterin, normal/near-normal neopterin — OMIM #261630); PreventionGenetics DHPR deficiency test note: 'biopterin is highly elevated' in DHPR deficiency. | intervention: loss-of-function of QDPR (DHPR deficiency)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Dihydropteridine reductase (QDPR) regenerates BH4 from quinonoid-BH2; QDPR deficiency (DHPR deficiency, OMIM #261630) blocks regeneration causing BH2/biopterin to accumulate. Biopterin (the oxidised pterin) spills into urine and is markedly elevated — the hallmark biochemical marker of DHPR deficiency."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/qdpr_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_7_biopterin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aqdpr_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_7_biopterin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/qdpr_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_7_biopterin> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Blau et al., Physician's Guide to Inherited Metabolic Diseases (DHPR deficiency: elevated urinary primapterin/7-biopterin as consequence of quinonoid-BH2 rearrangement); PMC11743392 (UAE BH4 disorder cohort: pterin profiling in DHPR deficiency). | intervention: loss-of-function of QDPR (DHPR deficiency)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "In QDPR/DHPR deficiency, quinonoid-BH2 accumulates and non-enzymatically rearranges to 7-biopterin (primapterin; CHEBI:74689). Elevated urinary 7-biopterin is a diagnostic marker of DHPR deficiency distinguishing it from GCH1/PTS deficiency."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_tryptophan> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_indican> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_tryptophan%3Apositive%3Aurinary_indican> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_tryptophan> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_indican> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Indoxyl sulfate — Wikipedia (pathway: L-tryptophan → indole by gut bacteria → absorbed, hepatically sulfated to indoxyl sulfate, renally excreted) (quantification of tryptophan, indole and indoxyl sulfate in urine — dose-dependent relationship with dietary tryptophan); AJCN study (PMID S0002916523307925) confirming tryptophan intake drives urinary indoxyl sulfate excretion. | intervention: raising plasma tryptophan (dietary increase or impaired conversion, e.g. tryptophan-loading test) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Urinary indican (indoxyl sulfate, CHEBI:43355) is formed from dietary L-tryptophan by intestinal bacterial tryptophanase (tryptophan → indole → indoxyl → indoxyl sulfate). Increased tryptophan availability raises intestinal substrate and thus indican production and urinary excretion."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fut3_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ca19_9> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afut3_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_ca19_9> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fut3_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ca19_9> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Wikipedia CA19-9 entry (Lewis-negative status: FUT3 deficiency → undetectable CA19-9 even in advanced cancer); ResearchGate PMID (FUT3 mutations → reduced Lewis enzyme activity → reduced sialyl-Lewis a / CA19-9 levels); J Hepatology 2013 (FUT3 genotype determines CA19-9 cut-offs in PSC/cholangiocarcinoma). | intervention: loss-of-function of FUT3 (Lewis-negative genotype)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CA19-9 (sialyl-Lewis a antigen) is synthesised by FUT3 (Lewis enzyme, alpha-(1,3/1,4)-fucosyltransferase): FUT3 adds the terminal fucose to create the sialyl-Lewis a epitope on epithelial cell-surface and secreted glycoproteins. FUT3 null individuals (Lewis-negative genotype, ~10% of whites) have unmeasurable CA19-9 regardless of tumour burden; conversely, high FUT3 activity supports elevated CA19-9 production."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pancreatic_amylase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_amylase> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apancreatic_amylase_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_amylase> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pancreatic_amylase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_amylase> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "NCBI StatPearls 'Analytical and Clinical Perspectives on Amylase' (NBK557738): P-type amylase is pancreas-specific; serum amylase rises 6–12 h after acinar injury (acute pancreatitis) as enzyme spills into circulation; Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (pancreatic exocrine secretion, acinar cell function). | intervention: pancreatic isoamylase secretion/acinar cell activity (elevated in pancreatitis; reduced in chronic pancreatitis/EPI)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Pancreatic isoamylase (AMY2A/B) is synthesised and secreted by acinar cells into the duodenum; a small fraction enters the circulation constitutively. Acinar cell injury, pancreatitis, or ductal obstruction markedly raises serum amylase by direct enzyme spillage into the bloodstream — a dose-dependent relationship between acinar secretory activity and circulating amylase."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gastrin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_pepsinogen_i> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Agastrin%3Apositive%3Aplasma_pepsinogen_i> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gastrin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_pepsinogen_i> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (gastrin and CCK stimulate chief cells to secrete pepsinogen); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology 'Pepsinogen Secretion' (gastrin stimulates pepsinogen secretion from chief cells; pepsinogen I spills into blood proportionally); ScienceDirect Pepsinogen I overview (gastrin is a major stimulant; HP infection raises PGI via gastrin elevation). | intervention: gastrin elevation (Zollinger–Ellison syndrome, H. pylori gastritis) raises plasma pepsinogen I; gastrin suppression (acid suppression, vagotomy) reduces it"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Gastrin (secreted by G cells) acts on gastric peptic chief cells (CL:0000155) to stimulate pepsinogen I secretion. Small amounts of pepsinogen I spill across the basal membrane into the circulation; elevated gastrin (as in Zollinger–Ellison syndrome or H. pylori infection) thus raises plasma pepsinogen I."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glucocerebrosidase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_acid_phosphatase> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aglucocerebrosidase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_acid_phosphatase> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glucocerebrosidase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_acid_phosphatase> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, Gaucher Disease section (elevated plasma acid phosphatase as a biomarker of Gaucher disease; reflects macrophage/Gaucher cell activation by lipid-laden lysosomes); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (lysosomal storage diseases: enzyme deficiency → substrate accumulation → macrophage activation). | intervention: loss-of-function of glucocerebrosidase (GBA, Gaucher disease)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Gaucher disease (glucocerebrosidase/GBA deficiency) causes glucosylceramide accumulation in macrophages → Gaucher cell activation → macrophage secretion of tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase (TRACP5b) into plasma. Low glucocerebrosidase activity leads to high plasma acid phosphatase, a well-known clinical marker of Gaucher disease activity."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mast_cell_degranulation> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_tryptase> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amast_cell_degranulation%3Apositive%3Aplasma_tryptase> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mast_cell_degranulation> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_tryptase> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "NEJM 1987 (Schwartz et al., 'Tryptase Levels as an Indicator of Mast-Cell Activation in Systemic Anaphylaxis and Mastocytosis'): serum tryptase reflects mast cell degranulation and burden; Mast cell tryptases and chymases PMC2275918 (tryptase stored in azurophilic granules, released on IgE-mediated and non-IgE-mediated activation); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (mast cell degranulation in allergy/anaphylaxis). | intervention: mast cell activation/degranulation (anaphylaxis, mastocytosis, IgE cross-linking)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Mast cell tryptase (beta-tryptase, TPSB2) is stored in mast cell secretory granules and released into circulation during mast cell degranulation. Plasma/serum total tryptase rises in proportion to mast cell activation (anaphylaxis, systemic mastocytosis). Tryptase is the dominant secretory protein of mast cell granules."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/t_cell_immune_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_gm_csf> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3At_cell_immune_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_gm_csf> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/t_cell_immune_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_gm_csf> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Holland-Frei Cancer Medicine NCBI NBK13400 (GM-CSF is produced by T cells, macrophages, mast cells, endothelial cells and fibroblasts in response to immune stimuli); Nature Cell Mol Immunol 2021 (GM-CSF: T-cell and innate immune source in sepsis/COVID-19); Frontiers in Immunology PMC7605919 (GM-CSF target for inflammation and autoimmunity: T cell and macrophage production). | intervention: T-cell activation (antigen stimulation, autoimmune disease, sepsis) raises circulating GM-CSF"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GM-CSF is produced by activated T lymphocytes (and to a lesser extent macrophages, endothelial cells and fibroblasts) upon antigen stimulation or inflammatory signalling. Circulating GM-CSF rises during inflammation and autoimmunity in proportion to T-cell activation."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/right_atrial_pressure> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/jugular_venous_pressure> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aright_atrial_pressure%3Apositive%3Ajugular_venous_pressure> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/right_atrial_pressure> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/jugular_venous_pressure> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "StatPearls NBK534125 (JVP reflects right atrial pressure and central venous pressure; normal CVP 1–8 mmHg); NCBI StatPearls 'Jugular Venous Distention' NBK553098 (elevated JVP indicates elevated RAP from right heart failure or volume overload); Guyton & Hall (venous pressure–right atrial pressure coupling). | intervention: volume expansion, right heart failure, or cardiac tamponade raise right atrial pressure and jugular venous pressure"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Jugular venous pressure (JVP) is a clinical surrogate for central venous / right atrial pressure (RAP). The internal jugular vein is a direct conduit to the right atrium with no intervening valves; JVP height mirrors RAP. Volume overload, right heart failure, and pericardial disease raise RAP and JVP in parallel."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/end_diastolic_volume> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lv_end_diastolic_diameter> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aend_diastolic_volume%3Apositive%3Alv_end_diastolic_diameter> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/end_diastolic_volume> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lv_end_diastolic_diameter> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Physiology Frank Starling Law StatPearls NBK470295 (increased EDV → increased fibre stretch → increased LV cavity dimensions; Frank-Starling preload relationship); Advances in Physiology Education (end-diastolic diameter as a measure of preload/EDV); Healthline 'End-Diastolic Volume' (dilated cardiomyopathy: overstretched ventricle raises EDV and end-diastolic diameter). | intervention: increasing venous return / volume loading raises end-diastolic volume and LV end-diastolic diameter; diuresis or ventriculoplasty reduces both"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Left-ventricular end-diastolic volume (EDV, preload) determines the degree of myocardial fibre stretch and LV cavity size at end-diastole; LV end-diastolic diameter is the echocardiographic linear dimension that directly indexes EDV. Dilated cardiomyopathy and volume overload increase both EDV and LV end-diastolic diameter simultaneously."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/stroke_volume> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aortic_transvalvular_gradient> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Astroke_volume%3Apositive%3Aaortic_transvalvular_gradient> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/stroke_volume> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aortic_transvalvular_gradient> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "MDPI J Clin Med 2025 (PMID PMC8457381, 'Understanding the Transvalvular Gradient in Aortic Stenosis': TVG rises with flow velocity, which depends on stroke volume at fixed orifice area; Bernoulli equation); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (pressure-flow relationships across cardiac valves); Thoracic Key 'Physiological Basis for Area and Gradient Assessment: Hemodynamic Principles of Aortic Stenosis' (gradient ∝ SV²/AVA²). | intervention: raising stroke volume (exercise, aortic regurgitation volume overload) raises the aortic transvalvular gradient; reduced stroke volume (low-flow state) reduces gradient despite unchanged stenosis severity"
    ; pmo:mechanism "The aortic transvalvular pressure gradient (TVG) arises when blood is ejected through a restrictive orifice; TVG = 4v² (simplified Bernoulli). At fixed valve area, a higher stroke volume per beat requires higher flow velocity, which squares to a higher gradient. This is the basis of 'flow-gradient discordance' in low-gradient severe aortic stenosis: low stroke volume produces low gradient despite severe stenosis."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lv_wall_thickness> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ecg_qrs_voltage> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alv_wall_thickness%3Apositive%3Aecg_qrs_voltage> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lv_wall_thickness> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ecg_qrs_voltage> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "StatPearls NBK557534 'Left Ventricular Hypertrophy' (increased wall thickness → higher QRS voltage; Sokolow–Lyon criterion SV1+RV5 >35 mm as LVH marker); LITFL ECG Library LVH (QRS voltage criteria for LVH reflecting wall thickness). | intervention: LVH due to pressure overload (hypertension, aortic stenosis) raises LV wall thickness and QRS voltage; concentric LVH reversal with antihypertensive treatment reduces both [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Greater left ventricular wall thickness (LVH) increases the total dipole produced by ventricular depolarisation, raising QRS voltage on the surface ECG. Standard Sokolow–Lyon and Cornell voltage criteria for LVH exploit this relationship. Conversely, myocardial fibrosis and dilated (thin-walled) cardiomyopathy reduce QRS voltage despite increased mass."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/metabolic_rate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/resting_energy_expenditure> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ametabolic_rate%3Apositive%3Aresting_energy_expenditure> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/metabolic_rate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/resting_energy_expenditure> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Physiological Reviews 2014 (PMID thyroid hormone regulation of metabolism: T3 is the principal determinant of BMR; BMR ≡ REE under standardised resting conditions); Frontiers in Physiology 2026 (associations between resting and total energy expenditure and thyroid hormone levels — REE tracks metabolic_rate). | intervention: hyperthyroidism raises metabolic_rate and REE; hypothyroidism lowers both; beta-adrenergic stimulation (epinephrine) raises REE acutely"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Resting energy expenditure (REE) is the principal operational measurement of basal/resting metabolic rate (BMR/RMR): both measure the rate of energy consumption at rest (O2 uptake / heat generation). Thyroid hormone, sympathetic tone, lean mass, and temperature set metabolic_rate; changes in metabolic_rate are reflected 1:1 in resting energy expenditure."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glyoxalase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_methylglyoxal> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aglyoxalase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_methylglyoxal> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glyoxalase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_methylglyoxal> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "PMC9152679 (methylglyoxal and glyoxalase 1 pathway: GLO1 deficiency → MG accumulation → AGE formation; CHEBI:17158); PMC2915835 (oxidative stress in IEM: glyoxalase activity inversely correlated with methylglyoxal); PMID 25818485 'Role of methylglyoxal and the glyoxalase system in diabetes and other age-related diseases' (reduced GLO1 → MG accumulation). | intervention: reduction in glyoxalase I activity (pharmacological GLO1 inhibitors; or hyperglycaemia-driven GLO1 downregulation) raises plasma methylglyoxal [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Glyoxalase I (GLO1) is the rate-limiting enzyme that converts methylglyoxal (MG) + glutathione to S-lactoylglutathione. Reduced GLO1 activity allows methylglyoxal (a reactive dicarbonyl produced as a glycolytic byproduct) to accumulate intracellularly and in plasma."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/catalase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_hydrogen_peroxide> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acatalase_activity%3Anegative%3Aerythrocyte_hydrogen_peroxide> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/catalase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_hydrogen_peroxide> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "PMC7016158 'Effect of hydrogen peroxide on normal and acatalasemic mouse erythrocytes' (acatalasaemia → elevated intracellular H2O2, increased methemoglobin); ScienceDirect 'Importance of Catalase in the Disposal of Hydrogen Peroxide Within Human Erythrocytes' (catalase handles ~50% of H2O2 generated in red cells); news-medical.net acatalasaemia review (CAT deficiency → H2O2 accumulation in erythrocytes). | intervention: loss-of-function of CAT (acatalasaemia) raises erythrocyte hydrogen peroxide"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Catalase catalyses the decomposition of H2O2 to H2O + O2 in erythrocytes; it is the principal defence against H2O2 in red cells (with glutathione peroxidase). Acatalasaemia (CAT LoF, OMIM #614097) causes H2O2 to accumulate in erythrocytes, leading to oxidative haemoglobin damage and methemoglobin formation."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glutathione_reductase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_oxidized_glutathione> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aglutathione_reductase_activity%3Anegative%3Aerythrocyte_oxidized_glutathione> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glutathione_reductase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_oxidized_glutathione> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "OMIM 618660 (congenital non-spherocytic haemolytic anaemia 10: GSR deficiency → near-complete absence of erythrocyte GSR → GSSG accumulation, low GSH, haemolytic anaemia); NORD 'Haemolytic Anaemia Due to Glutathione Reductase Deficiency' (GSR deficiency → GSSG elevated, GSH low in red cells); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (glutathione redox cycle: GSH/GSSG ratio maintained by GSR). | intervention: loss-of-function of GSR (congenital GSR deficiency) raises erythrocyte GSSG"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Glutathione reductase (GSR) reduces oxidised glutathione (GSSG) to GSH using NADPH. GSR deficiency (haemolytic anaemia due to GSR deficiency, OMIM #618660) impairs GSSG→GSH recycling; GSSG accumulates in erythrocytes, reducing the antioxidant GSH pool and increasing susceptibility to oxidative haemolysis."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/end_diastolic_volume> obo:RO_0002411 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lv_wall_thickness> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aend_diastolic_volume%3Aunknown%3Alv_wall_thickness> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/end_diastolic_volume> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lv_wall_thickness> ; pmo:sign "?"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (eccentric vs. concentric hypertrophy: volume overload causes cavity dilation with relatively normal wall thickness; pressure overload causes wall thickening); Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology (remodelling patterns in response to preload vs. afterload). | intervention: chronic volume overload (e.g. mitral regurgitation, high-output states) modifies LV wall thickness contextually"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Chronically elevated preload (end-diastolic volume) leads to eccentric LV remodelling: sarcomere addition in series elongates myocytes and dilates the LV cavity without proportional wall thickening. Conversely, pressure overload (elevated afterload) drives concentric LVH with increased wall thickness. The sign of EDV → wall thickness is therefore context-dependent ('?')."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/telomere_length> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_replicative_capacity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atelomere_length%3Apositive%3Acellular_replicative_capacity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/telomere_length> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_replicative_capacity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Wong, Cells: Molecules and Mechanisms §wong-7.7 (Telomeres): 'As cells divide, the telomeres shorten, and eventually signal the cell to stop dividing.' Dyskeratosis congenita (OMIM #305000): LoF mutations in TERT/DKC1 reduce telomerase activity and shorten telomeres, causing premature stem-cell exhaustion and tissue failure. Harley et al. 1990 Nature 345:458 (PMID 2342578) (telomere shortening limits replicative lifespan). | intervention: genetic LoF of TERT or DKC1 (dyskeratosis congenita) shortens telomeres and reduces replicative capacity; overexpression of telomerase in primary cells extends replicative lifespan (Bodnar et al. 1998 Science 279:349 PMID 9454332) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Each somatic cell division shortens telomeres by 50-200 bp; when telomeres reach a critically short length they trigger DNA-damage responses (p53/p21 pathway) that induce replicative senescence or apoptosis, limiting the total number of cell divisions (Hayflick limit). Longer initial telomere length allows more doublings before reaching the senescence threshold."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/neutrophil_extravasation> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/interstitial_fluid_volume> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aneutrophil_extravasation%3Apositive%3Ainterstitial_fluid_volume> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/neutrophil_extravasation> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/interstitial_fluid_volume> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-024 (4.7: Tissue Injury and Aging): 'The endothelium lining the local blood vessel becomes leaky under the influence of histamine and other inflammatory mediators allowing neutrophils, macrophages, and fluid to move from the blood into the interstitial tissue spaces.' | intervention: mechanistic model: neutrophil depletion in mouse peritonitis models (anti-Ly6G antibody do()) prevents inflammatory edema; neutrophil transfusion restores edema formation, confirming causal role [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Transmigrated neutrophils release proteases (elastase, MMP-9), reactive oxygen species, and histamine from degranulation that increase microvascular permeability and cause vasodilation; the resulting Starling-force imbalance drives net filtration of fluid into the interstitium, expanding interstitial fluid volume (inflammatory edema)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_contraction_rate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_tension> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Askeletal_muscle_contraction_rate%3Apositive%3Askeletal_muscle_tension> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_contraction_rate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_tension> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-056 (10.5: Nervous System Control of Muscle Tension): 'As the frequency of motor neuron signaling increases, additional APs stimulate repeated contractions before relaxation is complete… the contractions begin to combine, with the tension reaching greater and greater peak levels (wave summation).' Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology Ch. 6 (tetanus and summation). | intervention: mechanistic model: in vitro muscle preparations — increasing stimulation rate from single twitches to fused tetanus (do(stimulation_frequency)) produces proportional increase in isometric tension; neuromuscular blocking agents (curare do()) abolish contractions and tension"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Increasing stimulation frequency causes successive twitches to summate before relaxation; at sufficiently high frequencies, twitches fuse into smooth tetanus, producing maximal tension far exceeding a single twitch. Thus higher contraction frequency (up to the fusion frequency) increases tetanic tension produced by the muscle."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/faah_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_oleylethanolamide> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afaah_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_oleylethanolamide> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/faah_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_oleylethanolamide> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Rodriguez de Fonseca et al. 2001 Nature 414:209 (PMID 11700558): OEA is a FAAH substrate and satiety signal. Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 12 (lipid signaling molecules). | intervention: genetic LoF of FAAH (FAAH-null mouse) → tissue OEA levels significantly elevated alongside anandamide; pharmacological FAAH inhibition raises OEA in rodent brain and periphery [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "FAAH hydrolyzes N-acylethanolamines broadly, including oleoylethanolamide (OEA/oleylethanolamide, N-oleoylethanolamide); FAAH LoF therefore elevates OEA levels alongside anandamide. OEA is a PPAR-alpha agonist involved in satiety signaling."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sodium_calcium_exchanger_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cardiomyocyte_intracellular_sodium> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asodium_calcium_exchanger_activity%3Apositive%3Acardiomyocyte_intracellular_sodium> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sodium_calcium_exchanger_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cardiomyocyte_intracellular_sodium> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology: NCX stoichiometry 3Na+:1Ca2+. | intervention: mechanistic model: NCX overexpression in transgenic mice → elevated intracellular Na+ and abnormal Ca2+ homeostasis; NCX inhibitors (ORM-10103, KB-R7943 reverse-mode blocker) confirm NCX as the Na+-entry route in cardiomyocytes [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "The Na+/Ca2+ exchanger (NCX1/SLC8A1) in forward mode exports 1 Ca2+ out of the cardiomyocyte by importing 3 Na+ ions; greater NCX forward-mode activity therefore elevates intracellular [Na+]. This is particularly relevant in heart failure, where NCX is upregulated and contributes to Na+ loading and impaired Ca2+ handling."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skin_cholecalciferol_synthesis> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/calcitriol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Askin_cholecalciferol_synthesis%3Apositive%3Acalcitriol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skin_cholecalciferol_synthesis> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/calcitriol> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-035 (6.8: Calcium Homeostasis): skin synthesizes cholecalciferol from UV irradiation, which is converted to calcitriol via liver and kidney. Nutritional rickets (vitamin D deficiency) confirms the substrate-supply causal path. | intervention: mechanistic model: sunscreen use reducing UV exposure (do(skin_D3_synthesis=low)) → reduced 25(OH)D3 and calcitriol in randomized trials; vitamin D supplementation (do(cholecalciferol=high) bypassing skin step) raises calcitriol in a dose-dependent fashion [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Cutaneous photosynthesis of cholecalciferol (vitamin D3) from 7-dehydrocholesterol is the substrate step for the entire vitamin D activation cascade: D3 is 25-hydroxylated by hepatic CYP2R1 to 25(OH)D3, then 1α-hydroxylated by renal CYP27B1 to calcitriol (1,25-(OH)2D3). Reduced skin D3 synthesis (e.g. from limited UV exposure, pigmentation, or age) limits substrate availability and directly lowers circulating calcitriol."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/motor_neuron_firing_rate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/motor_unit_recruitment> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amotor_neuron_firing_rate%3Apositive%3Amotor_unit_recruitment> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/motor_neuron_firing_rate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/motor_unit_recruitment> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-056 (10.5: Nervous System Control of Muscle Tension): 'As the frequency of motor neuron signaling increases, additional APs stimulate repeated contractions' and motor unit recruitment is graded by nerve firing rate. Henneman size principle (Henneman et al. 1965 J Neurophysiol PMID 14328454): motoneuron firing threshold determines recruitment order. | intervention: mechanistic model: tetrodotoxin block of motor neurons (do(firing_rate=0)) abolishes recruitment; electrical stimulation of motor nerve at increasing frequencies progressively recruits additional motor units (confirmed by EMG recording) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Higher motor neuron action potential firing rates activate more motor units (size principle: small, low-threshold units fire at lower frequencies; as firing rate increases, larger motor units are progressively recruited). Each recruited unit adds its muscle fibers to the active pool, increasing the total number of recruited motor units."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_sarcoplasmic_calcium> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_tension> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Askeletal_muscle_sarcoplasmic_calcium%3Apositive%3Askeletal_muscle_tension> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_sarcoplasmic_calcium> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_tension> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-055 (10.4: Muscle Fiber Contraction and Relaxation): 'Ca2+ ions bind to troponin...tropomyosin moves off active sites of actin... A cross-bridge forms.' Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 5 (muscle contraction mechanism). | intervention: mechanistic model: caffeine (do(): opens RyR1, raises sarcoplasmic [Ca2+]) increases muscle tension at rest; dantrolene (RyR1 blocker, do(sarcoplasmic_Ca=low)) reduces muscle tension and is used to treat malignant hyperthermia [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Released Ca2+ from the SR binds troponin-C on thin filaments, causing tropomyosin to shift away from myosin-binding sites on actin, enabling cross-bridge cycling and force production. The relationship is sigmoidal: higher [Ca2+]i increases the proportion of troponin-C occupied and the number of force-generating cross-bridges, raising tension."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/motor_unit_recruitment> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_tone> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amotor_unit_recruitment%3Apositive%3Askeletal_muscle_tone> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/motor_unit_recruitment> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_tone> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-056 (10.5: Nervous System Control of Muscle Tension): motor unit recruitment and summation underlie gradation of muscle force including resting tone. Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology Ch. 55: spinal reflex arcs maintaining muscle tone; lower motor neuron lesions → flaccid paralysis (zero tone); upper motor neuron lesions → hypertonicity (increased recruitment). | intervention: mechanistic model: lower motor neuron block (do(motor_unit_recruitment=0)) causes flaccid paralysis (no tone); baclofen (GABA-B agonist reducing motor neuron firing, do(recruitment↓)) decreases spastic tone clinically"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Resting skeletal muscle tone is maintained by a low level of tonic motor unit activity (low-frequency, asynchronous firing of a small fraction of motor units). Higher motor unit recruitment increases the number of active fibers contributing to resting tone. Loss of lower motor neuron input (e.g. lower motor neuron lesion) abolishes tone (flaccid paralysis); increased input (e.g. upper motor neuron lesion) increases tonic recruitment and raises tone (spasticity)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_blood_flow> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_atp> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Askeletal_muscle_blood_flow%3Apositive%3Askeletal_muscle_atp> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_blood_flow> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_atp> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-055 (10.4: Muscle Fiber Contraction and Relaxation): 'The aerobic process of cellular respiration is the predominate route producing ATP.' Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology Ch. 60: muscle O2 delivery by blood flow determines aerobic capacity and ATP production. Ischemia → ATP depletion within 5-10 minutes confirmed clinically (tourniquet studies). | intervention: mechanistic model: tourniquet application (do(blood_flow=0) → ischemia) causes rapid ATP depletion in exercising muscle within minutes; exercise hyperemia (do(blood_flow↑) via vasodilation) sustains elevated muscle ATP during aerobic exercise"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Increased blood flow delivers more oxygen and glucose substrate to skeletal muscle mitochondria, fueling oxidative phosphorylation (ATP synthase) and increasing steady-state intracellular ATP concentration. Conversely, reduced blood flow (ischemia) causes a precipitous fall in muscle ATP as aerobic synthesis stops."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/norepinephrine> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pupil_diameter> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Anorepinephrine%3Apositive%3Apupil_diameter> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/norepinephrine> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pupil_diameter> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-080 (15.2: Divisions of the Autonomic Nervous System): 'The pupils dilate when the sympathetic system is activated.' Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology Ch. 14 (autonomic control of the eye). Horner syndrome (sympathetic interruption, do(NE=0)): miosis, ptosis, anhidrosis — confirms NE as pupil dilator drive. Topical phenylephrine (alpha-1 agonist, do(NE-equivalent=high)) → mydriasis. | intervention: pharmacological: topical phenylephrine (alpha-1 agonist, mimics NE) → pupil dilation (mydriasis); Horner syndrome (sympathetic chain lesion, do(NE=0)) → persistent miosis; norepinephrine reuptake inhibitors raise synaptic NE and cause mild mydriasis"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Sympathetic norepinephrine activates alpha-1 adrenoceptors on the dilator pupillae muscle of the iris, causing smooth-muscle contraction and pupil dilation (mydriasis). This is the sympathetic effector limb of pupillary control; bilateral sympathetic denervation (Horner syndrome) causes miosis (pupil constriction) due to loss of this dilator drive."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/net_filtration_pressure> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tissue_interstitial_fluid_volume> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Anet_filtration_pressure%3Apositive%3Atissue_interstitial_fluid_volume> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/net_filtration_pressure> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tissue_interstitial_fluid_volume> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-113 (20.4: Capillary Exchange): 'the NFP of 10 mm Hg drives a net movement of fluid out of the capillary at the arterial end'; the interstitial space accumulates fluid proportional to positive NFP. | intervention: Pharmacological elevation of capillary hydrostatic pressure (e.g., venous congestion in heart failure) increases net filtration and causes tissue edema"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Starling forces: positive net filtration pressure (capillary hydrostatic pressure exceeding colloid osmotic pressure) drives fluid from capillary lumen into the interstitial compartment, expanding local tissue interstitial fluid volume and producing edema when chronically elevated."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/helper_t_cell_activation> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/th2_cell_activation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahelper_t_cell_activation%3Apositive%3Ath2_cell_activation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/helper_t_cell_activation> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/th2_cell_activation> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-42.2 (42.2: Adaptive Immune Response): helper T cells release cytokines that 'activate B cells'; Th2 cytokines (IL-4, IL-5, IL-13) drive humoral responses; antigen-presenting cells plus IL-4 polarise naïve CD4+ T cells to Th2 fate. | intervention: Antigenic stimulation / allergen exposure (do(helper_t_cell_activation)) increases Th2 activation and type-2 cytokine output; verified by allergy immunotherapy studies showing antigen drives Th2 responses"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Activated CD4+ helper T cells differentiate into Th2 effector cells under IL-4 cytokine milieu; increased helper T cell activation (antigen stimulation) drives Th2 polarization, raising Th2 cell activation rate and IL-4/IL-5/IL-13 secretion."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/blood_viral_load> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cd4_t_cell_count> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ablood_viral_load%3Anegative%3Acd4_t_cell_count> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/blood_viral_load> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cd4_t_cell_count> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "HIV pathogenesis paradigm: untreated HIV (high viral load) reduces CD4 count from ~1000 to <200/μL over years — the criterion for AIDS (CD4 <200/μL; ACTG/CDC staging). OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-42.4 (42.4: Disruptions in the Immune System): 'The virus infects helper T cells and eventually kills them' — demonstrating that viral replication (blood_viral_load) causally reduces CD4 count. | intervention: HIV infection / do(blood_viral_load +): CD4 T cell count falls; antiretroviral therapy / do(blood_viral_load -): CD4 count recovers (ACTG 019/076 trials)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "High circulating viral load (especially HIV-1) directly infects and destroys CD4+ helper T cells via gp120-CD4 binding, leading to progressive CD4 lymphopenia; viral proteins also induce bystander apoptosis and immune exhaustion of uninfected CD4+ T cells."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_allergen_specific_ige> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mast_cell_degranulation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_allergen_specific_ige%3Apositive%3Amast_cell_degranulation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_allergen_specific_ige> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mast_cell_degranulation> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-120 (21.5: The Adaptive Immune Response - B Lymphocytes and Antibodies): 'if a person is allergic to peanuts, there will be peanut-specific IgE bound to their mast cells. In this person, eating peanuts will cause the mast cells to degranulate'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-42.4: 'IgE molecules on mast cells bind the antigen via their variable domains and stimulate the mast cell to release the modified amino acids histamine and serotonin'. | intervention: Allergen challenge in sensitised individuals (do(plasma_allergen_specific_ige +) or antigen administration) triggers mast cell degranulation — basis of passive sensitisation experiments and allergen skin-prick tests"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Allergen-specific IgE pre-bound to high-affinity FcεRI receptors on mast cells undergoes crosslinking when allergen re-exposure clusters adjacent receptor-bound IgE molecules; this triggers signal transduction (Lyn/Syk kinase activation, Ca2+ mobilization) culminating in degranulation and rapid release of preformed mediators (histamine, heparin, tryptase, serotonin)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bronchiole_smooth_muscle_tone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/airway_resistance> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abronchiole_smooth_muscle_tone%3Apositive%3Aairway_resistance> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bronchiole_smooth_muscle_tone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/airway_resistance> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-124 (22.2: Organs and Structures of the Respiratory System): 'This muscular wall can change the size of the tubing to increase or decrease airflow through the tube'; asthma section: 'bronchospasms (constriction of the bronchioles), which can inhibit air from entering the lungs' — the canonical do() is bronchial provocation (methacholine challenge), which increases smooth-muscle tone and raises measured airway resistance (PD20/PC20 endpoints in spirometry). | intervention: Methacholine challenge / do(bronchiole_smooth_muscle_tone +): FEV1 falls and airway resistance rises; β2-agonist bronchodilator / do(bronchiole_smooth_muscle_tone -): resistance falls (standard spirometric reversibility testing)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Bronchiole smooth muscle contraction (bronchoconstriction) reduces luminal radius; by the Hagen–Poiseuille relationship airway resistance scales as r⁻⁴, so even modest narrowing dramatically increases resistance to airflow. Conversely, bronchodilation reduces resistance."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bisphosphoglycerate_mutase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_2_3_bpg> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abisphosphoglycerate_mutase_activity%3Apositive%3Aerythrocyte_2_3_bpg> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bisphosphoglycerate_mutase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_2_3_bpg> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "BPGM deficiency (OMIM:222800): patients have essentially zero 2,3-BPG in red cells and erythrocytosis — direct genetic proof that BPGM activity controls erythrocyte 2,3-BPG. Jakubowski & Flatt, Fundamentals of Biochemistry Vol I §biochem-5.03.4 (Summary): 'bisphosphoglycerate, all allosteric ligands... shift the binding curves of Hb for oxygen to the right' — 2,3-BPG is the direct product of BPGM. | intervention: do(bisphosphoglycerate_mutase_activity LoF): BPGM deficiency (OMIM:222800) abolishes erythrocyte 2,3-BPG; altitude / hypoxia increases BPGM expression and raises 2,3-BPG"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Bisphosphoglycerate mutase (BPGM) converts 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate (a glycolytic intermediate) to 2,3-BPG within erythrocytes; BPGM is the sole synthetic enzyme for 2,3-BPG. Loss-of-function mutations in BPGM (bisphosphoglycerate mutase deficiency, OMIM:222800) abolish 2,3-BPG synthesis, causing a near-complete absence of erythrocyte 2,3-BPG. Gain of enzyme activity (e.g. high altitude acclimatization) raises 2,3-BPG, shifting the oxygen-dissociation curve rightward."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sympathetic_tone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_sympathetic_tone> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asympathetic_tone%3Apositive%3Agi_sympathetic_tone> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sympathetic_tone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_sympathetic_tone> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-080 (15.2: Divisions of the Autonomic Nervous System): sympathetic activation causes 'blood flow to the digestive system to decrease' and GI activity to shut down — demonstrating the causal pathway sympathetic_tone → gi_sympathetic_tone. Do() = systemic sympathetic activation (epinephrine infusion, stress response). | intervention: Epinephrine infusion / do(sympathetic_tone +): increases splanchnic vasoconstriction and GI sympathetic outflow (standard pharmacological test of splanchnic sympathetic activity)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Systemic sympathetic tone is carried to the gastrointestinal tract via the celiac and mesenteric ganglia; increased overall sympathetic outflow from the CNS increases efferent sympathetic discharge to enteric ganglia and the GI vasculature, directly elevating GI sympathetic tone."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/parasympathetic_tone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_parasympathetic_tone> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aparasympathetic_tone%3Apositive%3Agi_parasympathetic_tone> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/parasympathetic_tone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_parasympathetic_tone> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-080 (15.2: Divisions of the Autonomic Nervous System): parasympathetic division 'favors the resting functions' including increased GI activity. Do() = vagal nerve stimulation / atropine blockade (which abolishes GI parasympathetic effects). | intervention: Vagal nerve stimulation / do(parasympathetic_tone +): increases GI motility and secretion (standard parasympathetic pharmacology via muscarinic agonists/antagonists)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Parasympathetic (vagal) efferents project from the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus to the enteric nervous system, modulating GI motility and secretion. Increased systemic parasympathetic (vagal) tone increases the firing rate of vagal efferents to the GI tract, elevating GI parasympathetic tone."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_parasympathetic_tone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_tract_motility> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Agi_parasympathetic_tone%3Apositive%3Agi_tract_motility> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_parasympathetic_tone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_tract_motility> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-080 (15.2: Divisions of the Autonomic Nervous System): parasympathetic activity increases GI motility; muscarinic agonists (bethanechol) increase intestinal motility in clinical use. Atropine (muscarinic antagonist, do(gi_parasympathetic_tone -)) reduces GI motility. | intervention: Bethanechol (muscarinic agonist) / do(gi_parasympathetic_tone +): increases GI motility; atropine / do(gi_parasympathetic_tone -): decreases GI motility"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Vagal efferents release acetylcholine onto enteric neurons and smooth muscle cells in the GI wall; ACh binding to M2/M3 muscarinic receptors on smooth muscle cells promotes contraction and propagation of peristaltic waves, increasing overall GI motility."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_sympathetic_tone> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_tract_motility> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Agi_sympathetic_tone%3Anegative%3Agi_tract_motility> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_sympathetic_tone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_tract_motility> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-080 (15.2: Divisions of the Autonomic Nervous System): sympathetic activation causes 'The digestive system shuts down so that blood is not absorbing nutrients when it should be delivering oxygen to skeletal muscles' — directly describing GI motility inhibition. | intervention: Epinephrine infusion / do(gi_sympathetic_tone +): reduces GI motility (ileus induction); alpha-adrenergic blockade reduces this inhibition (clinical perioperative management)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Sympathetic nerve fibers release norepinephrine onto enteric neurons (presynaptically) and GI smooth muscle; this inhibits enteric neuron activity and smooth-muscle contraction, reducing peristaltic force and frequency. The physiological role is to arrest digestion during fight-or-flight."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_parasympathetic_tone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_tract_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Agi_parasympathetic_tone%3Apositive%3Agi_tract_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_parasympathetic_tone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_tract_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-080 (15.2: Divisions of the Autonomic Nervous System): parasympathetic system increases digestive secretions; muscarinic receptor activation on secretory cells drives fluid and enzyme secretion throughout the GI tract. Atropine (do(gi_parasympathetic_tone -)) dries secretions (dry mouth, reduced gastric acid — standard anticholinergic effect). | intervention: Pilocarpine (muscarinic agonist, do(gi_parasympathetic_tone +)): increases salivation and GI secretion; atropine (do(gi_parasympathetic_tone -)): dries all GI secretions"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Vagal efferents stimulate secretory cells throughout the GI tract: salivary glands (amylase), gastric chief/parietal cells (pepsin/HCl), small intestinal crypts (fluid), and pancreatic acini (enzyme juice) via ACh → M3 receptors. Increased parasympathetic tone globally elevates GI secretion rate."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cck> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pancreatic_enzyme_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_cck%3Apositive%3Apancreatic_enzyme_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cck> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pancreatic_enzyme_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-136 (23.7: Accessory Organs in Digestion): 'CCK stimulates the pancreas to produce and secrete more of its enzymes'; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 18: CCK is the principal hormonal stimulant of pancreatic enzyme secretion. | intervention: CCK infusion / do(plasma_cck +): increases pancreatic enzyme output into duodenal juice (standard pancreatic secretion test); CCK-A receptor blockade reduces enzyme secretion"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CCK (cholecystokinin) released from I-cells in the duodenal mucosa binds CCK-A receptors on pancreatic acinar cells, activating phospholipase C / IP3 / Ca2+ signaling, stimulating exocytosis of zymogen granules and secretion of pancreatic digestive enzymes (trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, proelastase, prolipase, amylase) into the duodenum."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cholecystokinin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gallbladder_bile_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_cholecystokinin%3Apositive%3Agallbladder_bile_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cholecystokinin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gallbladder_bile_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-136 (23.7: Accessory Organs in Digestion): 'The hormone CCK triggers the contractions of the gallbladder that push bile out through the bile duct'; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 21: CCK is the primary stimulant for gallbladder contraction. | intervention: CCK infusion / sincalide (CCK-8, synthetic CCK-A agonist, do(plasma_cholecystokinin +)): is the gold-standard cholescintigraphy provocation agent for gallbladder emptying fraction measurement — direct pharmacological proof"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CCK (plasma_cholecystokinin) binds CCK-A receptors on gallbladder smooth muscle, causing strong contraction of the gallbladder and relaxation of the sphincter of Oddi, expelling concentrated bile into the duodenum to aid fat digestion."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_secretin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pancreatic_exocrine_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_secretin%3Apositive%3Apancreatic_exocrine_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_secretin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pancreatic_exocrine_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-136 (23.7: Accessory Organs in Digestion): 'Secretin...stimulates the pancreas to produce and secrete a watery, bicarbonate-rich solution that acts to buffer the pH'; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 18: secretin is the principal stimulus for pancreatic ductal water and bicarbonate secretion. | intervention: Secretin stimulation test (do(plasma_secretin +)): intravenous secretin injection is the clinical standard for measuring pancreatic exocrine output — direct pharmacological intervention"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Secretin released from S-cells in the duodenal mucosa (triggered by acidic chyme) binds secretin receptors on pancreatic ductal cells, stimulating adenylyl cyclase and cAMP production, which drives CFTR-mediated bicarbonate and fluid secretion into the pancreatic duct — the major component of pancreatic exocrine juice."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pancreatic_enzyme_secretion> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/trypsin_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apancreatic_enzyme_secretion%3Apositive%3Atrypsin_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pancreatic_enzyme_secretion> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/trypsin_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-136 (23.7: Accessory Organs in Digestion): 'the pancreatic juice contains trypsin, chymotrypsin, and elastase'; Jakubowski & Flatt, Fundamentals of Biochemistry Vol I §biochem-6.6.3 (Zymogen Activation): enteropeptidase activates trypsinogen to trypsin in the duodenum — the rate-limiting step depends on substrate (trypsinogen) supply. | intervention: Chronic pancreatitis (LoF of pancreatic enzyme secretion): markedly reduced trypsin activity in duodenal juice — validated clinically by the secretin-CCK functional test"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Pancreatic enzyme secretion releases trypsinogen (the zymogen precursor of trypsin) into the duodenum; greater secretion delivers more trypsinogen substrate for enteropeptidase activation, increasing total luminal trypsin activity. Trypsin is the dominant serine protease in pancreatic digestive juice."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gallbladder_bile_secretion> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intestinal_lipid_emulsification> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Agallbladder_bile_secretion%3Apositive%3Aintestinal_lipid_emulsification> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gallbladder_bile_secretion> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intestinal_lipid_emulsification> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-136 (23.7: Accessory Organs in Digestion): 'Bile contains bile salts...that act as emulsifying agents needed to break down fats'; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 21: 'bile salts emulsify dietary fats in the small intestine, increasing the surface area for pancreatic lipase'. | intervention: Cholestyramine (bile-acid sequestrant, do(gallbladder_bile_secretion -)): reduces fat emulsification and absorption, causing steatorrhoea — pharmacological proof; cholecystectomy patients with impaired bile delivery show fat malabsorption"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Bile salts secreted from the gallbladder into the small intestine amphipathically surround dietary fat globules, coating them with a hydrophilic shell that prevents coalescence and dispersing them into small emulsion droplets. This emulsification dramatically increases the surface area available for pancreatic lipase-mediated hydrolysis of triglycerides."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cck> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gallbladder_bile_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_cck%3Apositive%3Agallbladder_bile_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cck> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gallbladder_bile_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-136 (23.7): 'The hormone CCK triggers the contractions of the gallbladder that push bile out through the bile duct'; sincalide (CCK-8) provocation during hepatobiliary scintigraphy is the clinical gold standard for gallbladder ejection fraction measurement. | intervention: Sincalide / CCK infusion (do(plasma_cck +)): gold-standard pharmacological stimulus for gallbladder contraction in hepatobiliary scintigraphy"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CCK binds CCK-A receptors on gallbladder smooth muscle and the sphincter of Oddi, causing synchronous gallbladder contraction and sphincter relaxation. This expels stored concentrated bile into the common bile duct and then into the duodenum."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_insulin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_lipogenesis> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_insulin%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_lipogenesis> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_insulin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_lipogenesis> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Studies in mice and humans with insulin receptor mutations show that reduced insulin signaling in the liver protects against hepatic steatosis\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 34, p 1358"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Insulin activates aPKC and mTORC1 to increase SREBP1c expression and processing, upregulating lipogenic gene expression"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_igf1> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/muscle_protein_synthesis_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahepatic_igf1%3Apositive%3Amuscle_protein_synthesis_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_igf1> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/muscle_protein_synthesis_rate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Moller & Jorgensen 2009, Endocr Rev 30(2):152-77 : IGF-1 increases muscle protein synthesis via PI3K/Akt/mTOR; exogenous IGF-1 infusion in GH-deficient adults restores muscle protein accretion. OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-058 (10.7: Exercise and Muscle Performance): 'Human growth hormone (hGH) and IGF-1...can facilitate building muscle mass'. | intervention: Recombinant IGF-1 infusion / do(hepatic_igf1 +): increases skeletal muscle protein synthesis rate measured by stable isotope tracer (leucine incorporation) — clinical pharmacological endpoint [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "IGF-1 binds the IGF-1 receptor (IGF1R) on skeletal muscle cells, activating the PI3K → Akt → mTORC1 signaling cascade. mTORC1 phosphorylates S6K1 and 4E-BP1, promoting ribosomal biogenesis and mRNA translation initiation, increasing rates of muscle protein synthesis. This is the canonical anabolic pathway for muscle hypertrophy."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/endometrial_thickness> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aestradiol%3Apositive%3Aendometrial_thickness> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/endometrial_thickness> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The exogenous administration of only estradiol and progesterone is sufficient to prepare the endometrium for implantation in the absence of ovarian function...Estradiol or synthetic estrogens such as ethinyl estradiol cause a striking thickening of endometrial tissue. Stromal and epithelial cells of the endometrium proliferate rapidly under the influence of estradiol.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 17, p 595"
    ; pmo:mechanism "estradiol drives endometrial epithelial and stromal proliferation, thickening the mucosa"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glycogen_phosphorylase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_glycogen_content> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aglycogen_phosphorylase_activity%3Anegative%3Ahepatic_glycogen_content> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glycogen_phosphorylase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_glycogen_content> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 15 (Glycogen Metabolism); glycogen storage disease type VI / Hers disease (hepatic PYGL deficiency, OMIM 232700): LoF of liver glycogen phosphorylase → hepatomegaly due to hepatic glycogen accumulation, demonstrating PYGL LoF → elevated hepatic glycogen content. | intervention: Genetic LoF of PYGL (liver glycogen phosphorylase; GSD type VI / Hers disease, OMIM 232700): reduced PYGL activity → hepatic glycogen accumulates."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Hepatic glycogen phosphorylase (PYGL) cleaves α-1,4-glucosidic bonds in glycogen via phosphorolysis, releasing glucose-1-phosphate; higher enzyme activity depletes glycogen stores, lower activity (LoF) causes glycogen to accumulate. Direction: enzyme activity → rate of glycogenolysis → glycogen store size (inverse)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pancreatic_lipase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/duodenal_lipase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apancreatic_lipase_activity%3Apositive%3Aduodenal_lipase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pancreatic_lipase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/duodenal_lipase_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Jakubowski & Flatt Fundamentals of Biochemistry Vol I §biochem-6.6.3 (Zymogen Activation): 'trypsin will cleave and activate other zymogen proteases and lipases in the duodenum … including … lipase.' Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (EPI) due to chronic pancreatitis or cystic fibrosis (CFTR LoF) → severely reduced pancreatic lipase secretion → steatorrhoea (fat malabsorption), demonstrating pancreatic cellular lipase activity drives duodenal luminal lipase activity. | intervention: Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (pharmacological model: cerulein-induced pancreatitis or cystic fibrosis CFTR LoF) → reduced pancreatic acinar cell lipase secretion → reduced duodenal lipase activity."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Pancreatic acinar cells synthesize and secrete pancreatic triacylglycerol lipase (PNLIP) into the pancreatic duct; the secreted zymogen (procolipase-dependent form) is activated in the duodenum by trypsin (and bile salts). More cellular secretory activity → higher luminal lipase activity in the duodenum."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/calcineurin_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/postsynaptic_ampa_receptor_density> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acalcineurin_activity%3Anegative%3Apostsynaptic_ampa_receptor_density> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/calcineurin_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/postsynaptic_ampa_receptor_density> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Jakubowski & Flatt Fundamentals of Biochemistry Vol I §biochem-4.8.4: 'calcineurin, an important protein (with phosphatase activity) in immune cells … required for T cell function'; demonstrates that FK506/cyclosporin inhibit calcineurin, elevating downstream targets — consistent with calcineurin driving AMPA endocytosis in neurons by the same dephosphorylation logic. | intervention: Pharmacological: FK506 (tacrolimus) or cyclosporin A inhibit calcineurin → prevent GluA1 dephosphorylation → higher synaptic AMPA receptor density; conversely, calcineurin activators (e.g., ionomycin-elevated Ca²⁺) → reduce AMPA density. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Calcineurin (protein phosphatase 2B/PP2B) dephosphorylates GluA1/GluR1 AMPA receptor subunit at Ser-845 (PKA phosphorylation site) and promotes GluA1 endocytosis via clathrin-mediated internalization; increased calcineurin activity therefore decreases the number of synaptic AMPA receptors (long-term depression, LTD). FKBP12:FK506 and cyclophilin:cyclosporin complexes that inhibit calcineurin elevate synaptic AMPA density, confirming the direction."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/presynaptic_neurotransmitter_release_rate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/synaptic_acetylcholine_concentration> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apresynaptic_neurotransmitter_release_rate%3Apositive%3Asynaptic_acetylcholine_concentration> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/presynaptic_neurotransmitter_release_rate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/synaptic_acetylcholine_concentration> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-35.2 (35.2 How Neurons Communicate): 'The signal arrives at the synapse and causes neurotransmitter molecules to be released from vesicles into the synaptic cleft.' Tetanus toxin (proteolytic cleavage of VAMP/synaptobrevin) blocks vesicle fusion → abolishes neurotransmitter release and synaptic ACh elevation; botulinum toxin similarly, confirming presynaptic release rate drives synaptic [ACh]. | intervention: Pharmacological: Ca²⁺ ionophore (ionomycin) or presynaptic voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channel agonists increase vesicle exocytosis and raise synaptic [ACh]; botulinum toxin/tetanus toxin LoF blocks exocytosis and abolishes the synaptic concentration rise."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Presynaptic vesicle exocytosis (Ca²⁺-triggered SNARE complex fusion) releases neurotransmitter into the synaptic cleft; greater vesicle exocytosis rate directly raises synaptic ACh concentration (and other neurotransmitters). Applies equivalently to cholinergic, GABAergic and aminergic synapses; modeled here for acetylcholine as the archetypical example."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_production_rate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intracranial_pressure> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acsf_production_rate%3Apositive%3Aintracranial_pressure> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_production_rate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intracranial_pressure> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (CSF production by choroid plexus, Monro–Kellie doctrine); acetazolamide (carbonic anhydrase inhibitor) reduces CSF secretion by choroid plexus epithelium and lowers ICP in idiopathic intracranial hypertension / pseudotumor cerebri — confirming that CSF production rate positively drives ICP. Davson H et al. Physiology of the CSF and Blood-Brain Barriers (1996) — choroid plexus secretion sets CSF turnover rate and basal ICP. | intervention: Pharmacological: acetazolamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase in choroid plexus epithelium, reducing CSF production rate by ~50%, with corresponding fall in ICP in idiopathic intracranial hypertension patients."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Cerebrospinal fluid is produced by the choroid plexus epithelium; at constant drainage, higher CSF production rate raises the total CSF volume and thereby intracranial pressure (Monro–Kellie doctrine: intracranial volume is fixed, so increased CSF volume raises ICP). The causal direction is confirmed by carbonic anhydrase inhibitors that reduce CSF production and lower ICP in idiopathic intracranial hypertension."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/monoamine_oxidase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/synaptic_dopamine_concentration> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amonoamine_oxidase_activity%3Anegative%3Asynaptic_dopamine_concentration> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/monoamine_oxidase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/synaptic_dopamine_concentration> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (catecholamine catabolism: dopamine → DOPAC via MAO); Parkinson's disease adjunct therapy with selegiline (MAO-B inhibitor) raises striatal dopamine by blocking MAO-B-mediated degradation — pharmacological confirmation that MAO activity negatively drives synaptic [dopamine]. OMIM 300615 (Norrie disease/MAOB deletion): MAOB LoF → elevated CSF HVA indicates reduced dopamine catabolism. | intervention: Pharmacological MAO inhibitors (selegiline / L-deprenyl for MAO-B; phenelzine, tranylcypromine for MAO-A+B) block dopamine catabolism → synaptic [dopamine] rises; confirmed across multiple clinical and preclinical studies."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Monoamine oxidase (MAO-B preferentially in dopaminergic neurons) oxidatively deaminates dopamine to 3,4-dihydroxyphenylacetaldehyde (then DOPAC/HVA); greater MAO activity consumes synaptic dopamine, lowering its concentration. MAO inhibitors (selegiline for MAO-B; phenelzine for both) block this catabolism and raise synaptic [dopamine]."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/monoamine_oxidase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/synaptic_serotonin_concentration> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amonoamine_oxidase_activity%3Anegative%3Asynaptic_serotonin_concentration> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/monoamine_oxidase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/synaptic_serotonin_concentration> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (serotonin catabolism: 5-HT → 5-HIAA via MAO-A); clinical pharmacology of moclobemide (reversible MAO-A inhibitor): raises synaptic serotonin → antidepressant and anxiolytic effects; OMIM 300615 (MAOB deletion, Norrie disease) and MAOA knockout animal models demonstrate MAO LoF → elevated tissue monoamine levels. | intervention: Pharmacological: moclobemide or phenelzine (MAO-A inhibitors) raise synaptic [serotonin]; MAO-A LoF (e.g., Brunner syndrome mutation p.Arg306Stop, OMIM 300615) → grossly elevated urinary serotonin metabolites confirming direction."
    ; pmo:mechanism "MAO-A (predominant form for serotonin) oxidatively deaminates serotonin (5-HT) to 5-hydroxyindoleacetaldehyde → 5-HIAA; greater MAO-A activity degrades synaptic serotonin, reducing its concentration. MAO-A inhibitors (clorgyline, moclobemide) block this and raise synaptic [5-HT], underpinning their antidepressant effect."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/scn_output_rate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_melatonin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ascn_output_rate%3Apositive%3Aplasma_melatonin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/scn_output_rate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_melatonin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "Reppert SM & Weaver DR Nature 2002;418:935–941 (PMID:12198538): SCN drives melatonin rhythm via sympathetic innervation of the pineal; SCN ablation abolishes plasma melatonin rhythm. OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-018 (3.5 The Cell Cycle): melatonin circadian rhythm is described; §anp-091: pineal melatonin as circadian regulator — 'the pineal gland secretes melatonin…regulated by the SCN'. | intervention: light-dark reversal shifts the SCN output phase and correspondingly shifts plasma melatonin peak. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "The suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) drives nocturnal melatonin synthesis via a multisynaptic pathway: SCN → paraventricular nucleus → superior cervical ganglion → sympathetic noradrenergic innervation of pinealocytes → β1-adrenoceptor/cAMP → AANAT (arylalkylamine N-acetyltransferase) activation → N-acetylserotonin → ASMT/HIOMT → melatonin secretion. Greater SCN nocturnal output (darkness-driven) produces higher plasma melatonin; SCN lesions abolish the melatonin rhythm."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/motor_unit_recruitment> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_force> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amotor_unit_recruitment%3Apositive%3Askeletal_muscle_force> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/motor_unit_recruitment> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_force> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-056 (10.5 Nervous System Control of Muscle Tension): 'As more motor units are recruited, the muscle contraction grows progressively stronger'; 'the largest motor units may generate a contractile force of 50 times more than the smallest motor units in the muscle.' The distinction between skeletal_muscle_force and skeletal_muscle_tension is quantitative (total force vs. normalised tension), both driven by the same motor-unit recruitment mechanism. | intervention: Perturbation: nerve stimulation at increasing frequencies recruits additional motor units (spatial summation), generating proportionally larger total contractile force; neuromuscular blockade (curare/vecuronium) prevents recruitment → force falls to zero."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Each recruited motor unit (one motor neuron + all its muscle fibers) adds its contractile force contribution to the total muscle output; activating more motor units in parallel increases total force production proportionally. Large motor units (fast-twitch, type IIb) contribute up to 50-fold more force than smallest type-I units."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cardiac_output> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pulmonary_perfusion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acardiac_output%3Apositive%3Apulmonary_perfusion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cardiac_output> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pulmonary_perfusion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "West JB Respiratory Physiology 9th ed (Fick principle: pulmonary blood flow = cardiac output in the absence of shunts). Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (pulmonary circulation: entire right heart output perfuses the lung). Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 13th ed Ch. 38: 'the quantity of blood flowing through the lungs is approximately equal to the cardiac output'. | intervention: Perturbation: exercise raises cardiac output 4–6 fold → proportional increase in pulmonary perfusion (confirmed by indicator-dilution pulmonary blood flow measurements at rest vs. exercise)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "In the normal pulmonary circulation, all cardiac output passes through the pulmonary capillary bed (assuming no right-to-left shunt); pulmonary capillary perfusion rate equals cardiac output (Fick principle). An increase in cardiac output directly raises the rate of blood flow through the pulmonary capillaries."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mean_arterial_pressure> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/coronary_blood_flow> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amean_arterial_pressure%3Apositive%3Acoronary_blood_flow> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mean_arterial_pressure> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/coronary_blood_flow> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 13th ed Ch. 21 (Coronary Circulation): coronary blood flow is proportional to aortic diastolic pressure; severe hypotension leads to coronary ischaemia confirming positive pressure-flow relationship. Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology Ch. 24 (coronary autoregulation and perfusion pressure). | intervention: Perturbation: aortic pressure clamping experiments demonstrate that lower MAP → reduced coronary flow and myocardial ischaemia; vasopressor infusion (phenylephrine) raises MAP → increases coronary perfusion."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Coronary perfusion pressure = mean aortic pressure − right atrial pressure (≈ MAP); coronary blood flow is primarily governed by perfusion pressure during diastole (when left coronary resistance is lowest). Raising MAP increases the driving pressure and, in the absence of compensatory vasoconstriction, raises coronary flow."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_glutaminase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_tubular_ammonia_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Arenal_glutaminase_activity%3Apositive%3Arenal_tubular_ammonia_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_glutaminase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_tubular_ammonia_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (renal ammoniagenesis: glutaminase hydrolyses glutamine in PCT mitochondria; NH₃ diffuses to lumen and is trapped as NH₄⁺, enabling net acid excretion). | intervention: Metabolic acidosis (ammonium chloride loading) increases renal GLS activity → elevated tubular NH₃ secretion. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Renal proximal tubule mitochondrial glutaminase (GLS/GLS1) hydrolyses glutamine → glutamate + NH₃; the NH₃ is secreted into the tubular lumen (and converted to NH₄⁺ for excretion) as the principal mechanism of renal acid excretion and bicarbonate regeneration. Greater glutaminase activity produces more NH₃ available for secretion; metabolic acidosis increases glutaminase expression/activity, boosting ammoniagenesis."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bisphosphoglycerate_mutase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_23_bpg> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abisphosphoglycerate_mutase_activity%3Apositive%3Aerythrocyte_23_bpg> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bisphosphoglycerate_mutase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_23_bpg> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 14 (Glycolysis and its regulation): BPGM is the exclusive source of erythrocyte 2,3-BPG. BPGM deficiency (OMIM 222800): LoF → erythrocyte 2,3-BPG dramatically reduced → polycythaemia due to high Hb-O₂ affinity, confirming BPGM activity positively determines erythrocyte [2,3-BPG]. | intervention: Genetic LoF: BPGM deficiency (OMIM 222800) → erythrocyte 2,3-BPG falls → high Hb-O₂ affinity → compensatory erythrocytosis."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Bisphosphoglycerate mutase (BPGM) catalyses the conversion of 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate → 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate (2,3-BPG) in erythrocytes; it is the sole enzyme producing 2,3-BPG in human RBCs. BPGM LoF abolishes 2,3-BPG synthesis; BPGM GoF raises erythrocyte 2,3-BPG levels."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_23_bpg> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hemoglobin_o2_saturation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aerythrocyte_23_bpg%3Anegative%3Ahemoglobin_o2_saturation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_23_bpg> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hemoglobin_o2_saturation> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Jakubowski & Flatt Fundamentals of Biochemistry Vol I §biochem-5.03.4: '2,3-bisphosphoglycerate … shift the binding curves of Hb for oxygen to the right, lowering the apparent affinity of Hb for oxygen.' BPGM deficiency (OMIM 222800): very low erythrocyte 2,3-BPG → left-shifted ODC → high SaO₂ at physiological PO₂ — genetic evidence for the negative sign. | intervention: Mechanistic model: BPGM LoF (OMIM 222800) demonstrates that low 2,3-BPG → high Hb-O₂ saturation (negative slope confirmed). [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "2,3-Bisphosphoglycerate binds preferentially to deoxyhaemoglobin (T-state) in a pocket between the β-chains, stabilising the low-affinity T-state and shifting the O₂–Hb dissociation curve rightward (lower P₅₀). Higher erythrocyte [2,3-BPG] therefore decreases Hb-O₂ affinity and reduces Hb-O₂ saturation at any given PO₂."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_sr_calcium_release> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_sarcoplasmic_calcium> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Askeletal_muscle_sr_calcium_release%3Apositive%3Askeletal_muscle_sarcoplasmic_calcium> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_sr_calcium_release> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_sarcoplasmic_calcium> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Franzini-Armstrong C & Protasi F Physiol Rev 1997;77(3):699–729 (PMID:9234963): DHPR-RyR1 orthograde coupling mediates SR Ca²⁺ release in skeletal muscle. Malignant hyperthermia (RYR1 GoF, OMIM 145600): excessive SR Ca²⁺ release → hypercalcaemia of sarcoplasm → sustained contraction, hyperthermia. Central core disease (RYR1 LoF, OMIM 117000): impaired SR Ca²⁺ release → muscle weakness. | intervention: Genetic GoF: RYR1 mutations causing malignant hyperthermia (OMIM 145600) → uncontrolled SR Ca²⁺ release → extreme sarcoplasmic Ca²⁺ elevation. Pharmacological: dantrolene blocks RyR1 → reduces SR Ca²⁺ release → lower sarcoplasmic Ca²⁺ (anti-malignant-hyperthermia therapy). [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Skeletal muscle EC-coupling: T-tubule membrane depolarisation triggers voltage-sensor DHPR (Cav1.1/CACNA1S) to directly gate RyR1 (ryanodine receptor 1) via physical coupling, releasing Ca²⁺ from the SR lumen into the sarcoplasm. Greater SR Ca²⁺ release flux raises free sarcoplasmic [Ca²⁺] (the trigger for cross-bridge cycling). RyR1 GoF mutations (malignant hyperthermia, OMIM 145600) cause uncontrolled SR Ca²⁺ release → dangerously elevated sarcoplasmic Ca²⁺."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pyruvate_carboxylase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_alanine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apyruvate_carboxylase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_alanine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pyruvate_carboxylase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_alanine> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Pyruvate carboxylase deficiency (OMIM 266150): GeneReviews NBK6852 — 'elevated levels of plasma lysine, citrulline, alanine, and proline'; Cureus PMC8202815 — 'elevated plasma alanine ... likely secondary to the increased level of pyruvate'; Merck Manual 'Pyruvate Metabolism Disorders' — hyperalaninemia is a consistent laboratory finding in PC deficiency; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 16 (pyruvate carboxylase and pyruvate disposal). | intervention: do(PC activity ↓) = pyruvate carboxylase deficiency (OMIM 266150)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Pyruvate carboxylase (PC) converts pyruvate to oxaloacetate, entering the TCA cycle. In PC deficiency, pyruvate accumulates and is redirected by alanine aminotransferase (ALT) to alanine; thus higher PC activity clears pyruvate and lowers alanine (∂[Ala]/∂(PC activity) < 0). LoF of PC → pyruvate up → alanine up (hyperalaninemia secondary to hyperpyruvataemia)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sds_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_threonine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asds_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_threonine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sds_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_threonine> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "PubMed 6434824 (J Inherit Metab Dis 1984): 'Threonine dehydratase deficiency: a probable cause of non-ketotic hyperglycinaemia' — threonine dehydratase was undetectable in liver from a patient, documenting the first inborn error of threonine catabolism with elevated threonine; biorxiv 2025.10.23.683531 — 'SDS deletion drastically increases systemic serine, glycine, and threonine levels'; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (serine/threonine dehydratases, Ch. 18). | intervention: do(SDS activity ↓) = serine dehydratase / threonine deaminase deficiency (PubMed 6434824) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "L-serine dehydratase / L-threonine deaminase (SDS, gene SDS) deaminates threonine to 2-ketobutyrate and ammonia; it is the principal hepatic enzyme catabolising threonine. Reduced SDS activity → threonine not cleared → plasma threonine accumulates (∂[Thr]/∂(SDS activity) < 0). SDS deletion in experimental models drastically increases systemic threonine levels."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hal_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_histidine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahal_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_histidine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hal_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_histidine> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Histidinemia (OMIM 235800); MedlinePlus Genetics — 'Histidinemia is a rare autosomal recessive metabolic disorder caused by deficiency of the enzyme histidase (HAL gene), which normally converts the amino acid histidine to urocanic acid'; NORD — 'Biochemical hallmarks are elevated histidine in blood, urine, and cerebrospinal fluid'; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 22 (histidine catabolism, HAL step). | intervention: do(HAL activity ↓) = histidinemia (OMIM 235800)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Histidine ammonia-lyase (HAL / histidase) irreversibly deaminates histidine to urocanate + ammonia; it is the sole enzyme catabolising the histidine carbon skeleton. Reduced HAL activity (histidinemia) → histidine not cleared → plasma histidine accumulates (∂[His]/∂(HAL activity) < 0)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/asns_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_asparagine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aasns_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_asparagine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/asns_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_asparagine> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Asparagine synthetase deficiency (OMIM 615574); PMC4486270 — 'asparagine synthetase deficiency: new inborn errors of metabolism; CSF asparagine concentration is typically low or not detected; plasma asparagine concentration is low in about half of affected individuals'; GeneReviews NBK525916 — 'low CSF asparagine supports the diagnosis'; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (asparagine biosynthesis via ASNS). | intervention: do(ASNS activity ↓) = asparagine synthetase deficiency (OMIM 615574)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Asparagine synthetase (ASNS) catalyses the ATP-dependent transamination of aspartate + glutamine → asparagine + glutamate; it is the principal biosynthetic route for asparagine. Reduced ASNS activity → less asparagine produced → plasma (and especially CSF) asparagine falls (∂[Asn]/∂(ASNS activity) > 0)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bckdh_enzyme_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_leucine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abckdh_enzyme_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_leucine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bckdh_enzyme_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_leucine> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Maple syrup urine disease / MSUD (OMIM 248600); NBK557773 — 'BCKD enzyme complex catalyzes the decarboxylation of the alpha-keto acids of leucine, isoleucine, and valine'; Wikipedia MSUD — 'large accumulation of the branched-chain amino acids leucine, isoleucine, and valine'; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 18 (BCAA catabolism, BCKDH complex). | intervention: do(BCKDH complex activity ↓) = maple syrup urine disease (OMIM 248600)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "The BCKDH complex (subunits BCKDHA/B) oxidatively decarboxylates the branched-chain alpha-keto acids derived from leucine, isoleucine, and valine; leucine is the most neurotoxic substrate. Reduced BCKDH activity → alpha-ketoisocaproate and leucine accumulate (∂[Leu]/∂(BCKDH activity) < 0). Maple syrup urine disease (MSUD) is the canonical LoF."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bckdh_enzyme_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_isoleucine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abckdh_enzyme_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_isoleucine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bckdh_enzyme_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_isoleucine> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "MSUD (OMIM 248600); NBK557773 — accumulation of leucine, isoleucine, and valine; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 18. | intervention: do(BCKDH complex activity ↓) = maple syrup urine disease (OMIM 248600)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "BCKDH decarboxylates the alpha-keto acid of isoleucine (alpha-keto-beta-methylvaleric acid); with BCKDH deficiency, isoleucine and its keto acid accumulate (∂[Ile]/∂(BCKDH activity) < 0)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bckdh_enzyme_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_valine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abckdh_enzyme_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_valine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bckdh_enzyme_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_valine> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "MSUD (OMIM 248600); NBK557773 — accumulation of leucine, isoleucine, and valine; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 18. | intervention: do(BCKDH complex activity ↓) = maple syrup urine disease (OMIM 248600)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "BCKDH decarboxylates the alpha-keto acid of valine (alpha-ketoisovaleric acid); with BCKDH deficiency, valine and its keto acid accumulate (∂[Val]/∂(BCKDH activity) < 0)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bckdh_enzyme_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_alloisoleucine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abckdh_enzyme_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_alloisoleucine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bckdh_enzyme_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_alloisoleucine> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "MSUD (OMIM 248600); NBK557773 — 'elevated plasma alloisoleucine is pathognomonic for classic MSUD; formed by keto-enol tautomerisation and amination of the 2-oxoacid derivative of isoleucine'; myadlm.org MSUD review — alloisoleucine elevation is a distinguishing characteristic of MSUD. | intervention: do(BCKDH complex activity ↓) = maple syrup urine disease (OMIM 248600)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "L-alloisoleucine is a non-protein stereoisomer of isoleucine formed by keto-enol tautomerisation and transamination of the BCKDH substrate alpha-keto-beta-methylvaleric acid; it accumulates whenever the BCKDH substrate pool is elevated (∂[alloisoleucine]/∂(BCKDH activity) < 0). Elevated plasma alloisoleucine is pathognomonic for MSUD."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dmgdh_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_dimethylglycine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Admgdh_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_dimethylglycine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dmgdh_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_dimethylglycine> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "DMGDH deficiency (OMIM 605850); PMC1275637 (AJHG 2001) — 'the concentration of N,N-dimethylglycine (DMG) was increased approximately 100-fold in the serum' in DMGDH deficiency; Clinical Chemistry PubMed 10102904 — NMR spectroscopy study confirming DMGDH deficiency as new inborn error of metabolism with markedly elevated DMG. | intervention: do(DMGDH activity ↓) = dimethylglycine dehydrogenase deficiency (OMIM 605850) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Dimethylglycine dehydrogenase (DMGDH) oxidatively demethylates N,N-dimethylglycine to sarcosine, using FAD as cofactor. Reduced DMGDH activity → dimethylglycine not cleared → plasma concentration rises approximately 100-fold (∂[DMG]/∂(DMGDH activity) < 0)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldh7a1_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_aminoadipic_semialdehyde> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aaldh7a1_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_aminoadipic_semialdehyde> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldh7a1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_aminoadipic_semialdehyde> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Pyridoxine-dependent epilepsy / ALDH7A1 deficiency (OMIM 266100); ScienceDirect — 'Clinical, biochemical, molecular and therapeutic aspects of 2 new cases of 2-aminoadipic semialdehyde synthase deficiency'; PMC1378037 — AASS gene identification with elevated alpha-AASA in familial hyperlysinemia; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 18 (lysine catabolism pathway). | intervention: do(ALDH7A1 activity ↓) = pyridoxine-dependent epilepsy / ALDH7A1 deficiency (OMIM 266100)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Alpha-aminoadipic semialdehyde dehydrogenase (ALDH7A1 / antiquitin) oxidises alpha-aminoadipic semialdehyde (alpha-AASA) to alpha-aminoadipate; alpha-AASA is in rapid equilibrium with Δ1-piperideine-6-carboxylate. ALDH7A1 deficiency (pyridoxine-dependent epilepsy) → alpha-AASA cannot be cleared → plasma level rises (∂[AASA]/∂(ALDH7A1 activity) < 0)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldh7a1_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_pipecolate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aaldh7a1_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_pipecolate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldh7a1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_pipecolate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Pyridoxine-dependent epilepsy (OMIM 266100): elevated pipecolic acid co-occurs with elevated alpha-AASA (Lehninger, lysine catabolism) — 'Identification of L-pipecolate oxidase in human liver and its deficiency in the Zellweger syndrome'; Nature Pediatric Research 1989 — 'Peroxisomal L-Pipecolic Acid Oxidation Is Deficient in Liver from Zellweger Syndrome Patients'; Mayo Clinic Neurology catalog — pipecolic acid elevated in peroxisomal biogenesis disorders. | intervention: do(ALDH7A1 activity ↓) = pyridoxine-dependent epilepsy (OMIM 266100); do(PIPOX absent) = Zellweger syndrome / peroxisomal biogenesis disorder [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Alpha-AASA (the ALDH7A1 substrate) equilibrates with Δ1-piperideine-6-carboxylate, which in turn equilibrates with pipecolic acid through the saccharopine pathway; when ALDH7A1 is deficient, the entire upstream pool (including pipecolate) accumulates (∂[pipecolate]/∂(ALDH7A1 activity) < 0). Additionally, peroxisomal L-pipecolate oxidase (PIPOX) degrades pipecolic acid directly; peroxisomal biogenesis disorders lacking functional PIPOX also accumulate pipecolate."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/suox_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_s_sulfocysteine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asuox_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_s_sulfocysteine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/suox_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_s_sulfocysteine> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Isolated sulfite oxidase deficiency (OMIM 272300) and molybdenum cofactor deficiency (OMIM 252150); PMC3509921 — 'Molybdenum Cofactor Deficiency: A New HPLC Method for Fast Quantification of S-Sulfocysteine'; PMC7371372 — 'elevated levels of urine and plasma sulfite, SSC, thiosulfate, and taurine' in ISOD and MoCD; mechanism: 'sulfite reacts with cystine forming S-sulfocysteine' (sulfite accumulation → SSC); Emedicine sulfite oxidase deficiency review. | intervention: do(SUOX activity ↓) = isolated sulfite oxidase deficiency (OMIM 272300) or molybdenum cofactor deficiency (OMIM 252150)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Sulfite oxidase (SUOX) oxidises sulfite (SO₃²⁻) to sulfate, the terminal step of cysteine/methionine sulfur catabolism. When SUOX is deficient, sulfite accumulates and spontaneously reacts with cystine to form S-sulfocysteine (SSC) — a non-enzymatic reaction. Thus higher SUOX activity lowers sulfite and thereby lowers SSC (∂[SSC]/∂(SUOX activity) < 0: LoF → SSC up)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dhtkd1_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_2_aminoadipate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Adhtkd1_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_2_aminoadipate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dhtkd1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_2_aminoadipate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Alpha-aminoadipic and alpha-ketoadipic aciduria (AAKAD, OMIM 204750); PMID 25860818 (J Inherit Metab Dis 2015) — 'mutations in DHTKD1 … elevated 2-aminoadipate in plasma'; PMC5059180 — 'New Cases of DHTKD1 Mutations in Patients with 2-Ketoadipic Aciduria'; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 18 (lysine catabolism: 2-aminoadipate → 2-oxoadipate → glutaryl-CoA step). | intervention: do(DHTKD1 activity ↓) = alpha-aminoadipic and alpha-ketoadipic aciduria (OMIM 204750) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "DHTKD1 encodes the E1 subunit of the 2-oxoadipate dehydrogenase complex, which oxidatively decarboxylates 2-oxoadipate to glutaryl-CoA in the final common step of lysine, tryptophan, and hydroxylysine catabolism. With DHTKD1 deficiency, 2-oxoadipate accumulates and equilibrates with 2-aminoadipate by transamination, raising plasma 2-aminoadipate (∂[2-AA]/∂(DHTKD1 activity) < 0)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/kynu_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_3_hydroxykynurenine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Akynu_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_3_hydroxykynurenine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/kynu_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_3_hydroxykynurenine> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "KYNU deficiency / hydroxykynureninuria (OMIM 236800 / VCRL2); PMC2019216 — 'Hydroxykynureninuria: a case of Abnormal Tryptophan Metabolism Probably Due to a Deficiency of Kynureninase'; OMIM 605197 — 'kynureninase deficiency; 3-hydroxykynurenine elevated in urine organic acids in KYNU-mutant patients'; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (kynurenine pathway: KYNU converts 3-hydroxykynurenine → 3-hydroxyanthranilate). | intervention: do(KYNU activity ↓) = kynureninase deficiency / hydroxykynureninuria (OMIM 236800)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Kynureninase (KYNU) is a pyridoxal-5-phosphate-dependent enzyme that cleaves 3-hydroxykynurenine to 3-hydroxyanthranilate + alanine (and separately cleaves kynurenine to anthranilate + alanine). With KYNU deficiency, 3-hydroxykynurenine cannot be converted and accumulates (∂[3-HK]/∂(KYNU activity) < 0). KYNU mutations also cause vertebral, cardiac, renal, and limb defects syndrome-2 (VCRL2)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cystathionine_gamma_lyase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cystine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acystathionine_gamma_lyase_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_cystine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cystathionine_gamma_lyase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cystine> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Cystathioninuria (OMIM 219500) — CTH deficiency; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 22 (transsulfuration: CBS → cystathionine → CTH → cysteine); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (sulfur amino acid metabolism); the cysteine/cystine equilibrium in plasma is governed by the CTH-driven cysteine supply. | intervention: do(CTH activity ↓) = cystathioninuria (OMIM 219500)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Cystathionine gamma-lyase (CTH) catalyses the beta-elimination of cystathionine to cysteine + 2-ketobutyrate + NH₃; cysteine is subsequently oxidised to cystine (the disulfide dimer) in plasma. Reduced CTH activity (cystathioninuria) → less cysteine produced → plasma cystine falls (∂[cystine]/∂(CTH activity) > 0: LoF → cystine decreases). Conversely, elevated CTH activity (e.g. under high-methionine flux) produces more cysteine and raises cystine."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cystathionine_gamma_lyase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_alpha_aminobutyrate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acystathionine_gamma_lyase_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_alpha_aminobutyrate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cystathionine_gamma_lyase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_alpha_aminobutyrate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Cystathioninuria (OMIM 219500); HealthMatters.io alpha-aminobutyrate entry — 'Alpha-aminobutyric acid is an intermediate formed during the catabolism of methionine and threonine; increases in AABA occur secondary to elevations of either methionine or threonine'; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 22 (transsulfuration: CTH releases 2-ketobutyrate → transamination to alpha-aminobutyrate); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology. | intervention: do(CTH activity ↓) = cystathioninuria (OMIM 219500)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CTH cleaves cystathionine to cysteine + 2-ketobutyrate + NH₃; 2-ketobutyrate is then transaminated by branched-chain aminotransferases to alpha-aminobutyrate (2-aminobutyrate). Higher CTH activity drives more 2-ketobutyrate and more alpha-aminobutyrate (∂[AABA]/∂(CTH activity) > 0). CTH deficiency → less 2-ketobutyrate → plasma alpha-aminobutyrate falls."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/agxt2_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_beta_aminoisobutyrate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aagxt2_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_beta_aminoisobutyrate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/agxt2_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_beta_aminoisobutyrate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Beta-aminoisobutyric aciduria / AGXT2 deficiency (OMIM 210100); Nature Scientific Reports PMC5067591 — 'liver-specific Hnf4a knockout mice exhibited elevated plasma BAIB; patients with AGXT2 SNPs develop hyper-D-beta-aminoisobutyric aciduria characterized by elevation of D-BAIBA levels in plasma and urine'; OMIM 210100 — 'AGXT2 deficiency causes elevation of BAIBA'; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (pyrimidine degradation pathway: thymine → beta-aminoisobutyrate → methylmalonate semialdehyde via AGXT2). | intervention: do(AGXT2 activity ↓) = beta-aminoisobutyric aciduria (OMIM 210100)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Alanine-glyoxylate aminotransferase 2 (AGXT2) transaminate R-3-amino-2-methylpropanoate (D-beta-aminoisobutyrate, D-BAIBA) to methylmalonate semialdehyde; AGXT2 is the principal route for D-BAIBA catabolism. Reduced AGXT2 activity → D-BAIBA not cleared → plasma beta-aminoisobutyrate rises (∂[BAIBA]/∂(AGXT2 activity) < 0). Patients with AGXT2 variants develop autosomal-recessive hyper-D-beta-aminoisobutyric aciduria."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/prolidase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glycylproline> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aprolidase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_glycylproline> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/prolidase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glycylproline> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Prolidase deficiency (OMIM 170100); Springer 'Disorders of Beta and Gamma Amino Acids' — prolidase deficiency causes iminodipeptiduria including glycylproline accumulation; Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine — prolidase (PEPD) deficiency results in accumulation of imidodipeptides containing C-terminal proline in urine and plasma; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (collagen-derived dipeptide catabolism; prolidase as the X-Pro dipeptidase). | intervention: do(prolidase/PEPD activity ↓) = prolidase deficiency (OMIM 170100)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Prolidase (PEPD, peptidase D) hydrolyses imidodipeptides with C-terminal proline or hydroxyproline, including glycylproline (Gly-Pro). Prolidase deficiency (PEPD deficiency) → glycylproline and other imidodipeptides cannot be cleaved → plasma and urine glycylproline accumulates (∂[Gly-Pro]/∂(prolidase activity) < 0)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/agat_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_homoarginine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aagat_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_homoarginine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/agat_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_homoarginine> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "AGAT (GATM) deficiency (OMIM 612718); Circulation 2013 — 'Homoarginine Levels Are Regulated by l-Arginine:Glycine Amidinotransferase … plasma homoarginine concentration was 0.17 µmol/L in wild-type mice whereas homoarginine was barely detectable in AGAT-deficient mice'; PMC9395972 — 'AGAT synthesizes homoarginine by catalyzing transfer of the guanidine group from arginine to lysine'; GeneReviews NBK3794 (Creatine Deficiency Disorders). | intervention: do(AGAT activity ↓) = AGAT deficiency (OMIM 612718)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "AGAT (GATM / arginine:glycine amidinotransferase) transfers the amidino group from arginine to both glycine (forming guanidinoacetate) and lysine (forming homoarginine). Reduced AGAT activity → less homoarginine synthesised → plasma homoarginine falls (∂[homoarginine]/∂(AGAT activity) > 0). In AGAT-deficient mice plasma homoarginine is barely detectable."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/haao_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_3_hydroxyanthranilate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahaao_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_3_hydroxyanthranilate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/haao_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_3_hydroxyanthranilate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Kynurenine pathway biochemistry; bioRxiv 2021.06.01.446651 — 'elevating physiological levels of 3-hydroxyanthranilic acid via inhibition of HAAO extends lifespan'; Nature Communications 2023 — HAAO inhibition raises 3-HAA; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (kynurenine pathway: KYNU → 3-hydroxyanthranilate → HAAO → ACMS); note: isolated HAAO deficiency as a clean human IEM is not fully characterised, but HAAO is the definitive enzymatic consumer of 3-HAA in the pathway. | intervention: do(HAAO activity ↓) = HAAO inhibition / kynurenine pathway block at HAAO step"
    ; pmo:mechanism "3-Hydroxyanthranilate 3,4-dioxygenase (HAAO) converts 3-hydroxyanthranilic acid (3-HAA) to 2-amino-3-carboxymuconate semialdehyde (the upstream precursor of quinolinate and NAD+) in the kynurenine pathway. Reduced HAAO activity → 3-HAA not consumed → plasma 3-hydroxyanthranilate accumulates (∂[3-HAA]/∂(HAAO activity) < 0). HAAO inhibition experimentally elevates systemic 3-HAA levels in worms and mice."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/abat_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_beta_alanine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aabat_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_beta_alanine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/abat_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_beta_alanine> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "GABA-transaminase deficiency (OMIM 613163); PMC4256671 — 'disorders of GABA metabolism: significantly elevated GABA and beta-alanine levels in plasma and CSF; beta-alanine is an alternative substrate for GABA-transaminase'; MedLink Neurology — 'GABA-transaminase deficiency: detection of elevated CSF GABA and beta-alanine; inhibition of ABAT results in accumulation of GABA, beta-alanine, homocarnosine, and 2-pyrrolidinone'; OMIM 613163 (GABATD). | intervention: do(ABAT activity ↓) = GABA-transaminase deficiency (OMIM 613163)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "4-Aminobutyrate aminotransferase (ABAT / GABA-transaminase) degrades both GABA and beta-alanine (an alternative substrate); beta-alanine is transaminated to malonate semialdehyde. Reduced ABAT activity → beta-alanine not cleared → plasma and CSF beta-alanine rises (∂[beta-Ala]/∂(ABAT activity) < 0). GABA-transaminase deficiency (GABATD) is the canonical LoF."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/prodh2_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_hydroxyproline> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aprodh2_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_hydroxyproline> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/prodh2_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_hydroxyproline> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Hydroxyprolinemia OMIM 237000; PRODH2 gene OMIM 616377; Jansen et al. (2016) J Inherit Metab Dis — biallelic PRODH2 mutations cause elevated plasma hydroxyproline; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (proline/hydroxyproline catabolism, PRODH2 as the entry step); confirmed benign condition on newborn screening. | intervention: do(PRODH2 loss-of-function) — homozygous PRODH2 mutations; OMIM 237000"
    ; pmo:mechanism "PRODH2 (hydroxyproline dehydrogenase) catalyzes FAD-dependent oxidation of trans-4-hydroxy-L-proline to delta-1-pyrroline-3-OH-5-carboxylate; hydroxyproline is the substrate. Loss of PRODH2 activity (hydroxyprolinemia) blocks catabolism so hydroxyproline accumulates: do(PRODH2 LoF) -> plasma hydroxyproline rises."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sardh_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_sarcosine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asardh_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_sarcosine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sardh_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_sarcosine> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Sarcosinemia OMIM 268900; SARDH gene OMIM 604455; PMID 22825317 (Wortmann et al.); Orphanet 3129; elevated plasma and urinary sarcosine in SARDH-deficient patients. | intervention: do(SARDH loss-of-function) — homozygous SARDH mutations; sarcosinemia OMIM 268900 [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Sarcosine dehydrogenase (SARDH) demethylates sarcosine (N-methylglycine) to glycine; sarcosine is the substrate. SARDH deficiency (sarcosinemia) blocks sarcosine catabolism causing accumulation in plasma."
    .
