    ; dcterms:source "Histidinemia OMIM 235800; Wikipedia Histidinemia (citing primary literature): 'Formation of Nτ-ribosylhistidine, a novel histidine derivative found in the urine in histidinemia'; Orphanet 2157 histidinemia; Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (histidine catabolism, histidinemia as benign inborn error); Lehninger (histidine catabolism, HAL first step). | intervention: do(HAL loss-of-function) — histidinemia OMIM 235800"
    ; pmo:mechanism "HAL (histidine ammonia-lyase/histidase) catalyzes the first step of histidine catabolism (histidine -> urocanate + NH3). HAL deficiency (histidinemia) causes histidine to accumulate; the accumulated histidine undergoes modified catabolism including formation of N-tau-ribosylhistidine (Nτ-ribosylhistidine), a novel histidine derivative found specifically in the urine of histidinemia patients. do(HAL LoF) -> urinary N-tau-ribosylhistidine rises."
    .
