<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aglutathione_synthetase_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_gamma_glutamylcysteine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glutathione_synthetase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_gamma_glutamylcysteine> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Glutathione synthetase deficiency OMIM 266130; PMC1852094 (Inborn errors in glutathione metabolism): 'elevated gamma-glutamylcysteine... blockade of glutathione synthesis causes functional loss of feedback inhibition on gamma-GCS, increasing flux'; PMID 4152248 (GSS deficiency original paper); Lehninger (gamma-glutamyl cycle, GSS step). | intervention: do(GSS loss-of-function) — glutathione synthetase deficiency OMIM 266130 [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Glutathione synthetase (GSS) converts gamma-glutamylcysteine + glycine -> glutathione. GSS deficiency blocks glutathione synthesis; gamma-glutamylcysteine (the GSS substrate) accumulates and is excreted in urine. Additionally, loss of glutathione feedback inhibition on gamma-GCS causes overproduction of gamma-glutamylcysteine. do(GSS LoF) -> urinary gamma-glutamylcysteine rises."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ggt1_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_gamma_glutamylphenylalanine> .
