    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Tyrosinemia type II GeneReviews NBK608431 — elevated urinary tyrosine metabolites including N-acetyltyrosine in TAT (tyrosine aminotransferase, TAT) deficiency; Tyrosinemia type III PMC5471395 — N-acetyltyrosine elevated in HPD deficiency; the connection is an overflow N-acetylation proportional to plasma tyrosine. | intervention: TAT deficiency (tyrosinemia type II, OMIM 276600): plasma tyrosine UP → urinary N-acetyltyrosine UP via overflow N-acetylation"
    ; pmo:mechanism "When plasma tyrosine is markedly elevated (e.g., in tyrosinemia type II due to TAT deficiency, or tyrosinemia type III / HPD deficiency), overflow N-acetylation by cytosolic N-acetyltransferases produces N-acetyltyrosine, which is excreted in urine. Elevated urinary N-acetyltyrosine is a secondary marker of hypertyrosinemia."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/prolidase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_imidodipeptides> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aprolidase_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_imidodipeptides> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/prolidase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_imidodipeptides> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "GeneReviews NBK299584 (prolidase deficiency): deficiency of PEPD leads to massive imidopeptiduria (imidodipeptides containing proline/hydroxyproline elevated in urine); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, collagen degradation (prolidase step); OMIM 170100 PEPD deficiency. | intervention: PEPD LoF (prolidase deficiency, OMIM 170100): prolidase activity DOWN → urinary imidodipeptides (X-Pro/X-Hyp) accumulate markedly"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Prolidase (PEPD/peptidase D) is an exopeptidase that cleaves imidodipeptides (X-Pro and X-Hyp dipeptides) at the C-terminal proline or hydroxyproline. PEPD LoF (prolidase deficiency) abolishes this cleavage, causing massive imidodipeptiduria — urinary accumulation of dipeptides containing C-terminal proline or hydroxyproline."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/agxt2_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_3_aminoisobutyrate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aagxt2_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_3_aminoisobutyrate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/agxt2_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_3_aminoisobutyrate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "OMIM 210100 beta-aminoisobutyric aciduria — autosomal recessive benign trait caused by deficient AGXT2 (hepatic beta-aminoisobutyrate transaminase); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, thymine catabolism (R-BAIB → methylmalonate semialdehyde). | intervention: AGXT2 LoF (beta-aminoisobutyric aciduria, OMIM 210100): AGXT2 activity DOWN → urinary 3-aminoisobutyric acid UP [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "AGXT2 (alanine-glyoxylate aminotransferase 2 / D-3-aminoisobutyrate-pyruvate transaminase) catalyzes the transamination of (R)-3-aminoisobutyrate → methylmalonate semialdehyde, which enters the propionate pathway. AGXT2 LoF (benign beta-aminoisobutyric aciduria) blocks this catabolism so 3-aminoisobutyric acid accumulates in plasma and urine."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alpha_aminoadipic_semialdehyde_synthase_activity> obo:RO_0002411 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_saccharopine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aalpha_aminoadipic_semialdehyde_synthase_activity%3Aunknown%3Aurinary_saccharopine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alpha_aminoadipic_semialdehyde_synthase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_saccharopine> ; pmo:sign "?"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "OMIM 238700 hyperlysinemia type I (AASS, LKR-predominant) — low saccharopine; OMIM 268700 saccharopinuria (AASS, SDH-predominant) — elevated saccharopine. Same reasoning as plasma_saccharopine '?' edge already in fragment. | intervention: AASS LoF (hyperlysinemia/saccharopinuria, OMIM 238700/268700): sign depends on which domain is more affected; flagged '?' per PhysioMap sign discipline"
    ; pmo:mechanism "AASS is bifunctional: the LKR (lysine-ketoglutarate reductase) domain forms saccharopine (substrate), and the SDH (saccharopine dehydrogenase) domain cleaves saccharopine. Dominant SDH impairment gives saccharopinuria (elevated saccharopine); dominant LKR impairment gives hyperlysinemia with low saccharopine. Net sign on urinary saccharopine is indeterminate from the global enzyme activity node alone."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pipox_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_pipecolic_acid> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apipox_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_pipecolic_acid> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pipox_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_pipecolic_acid> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Wanders RJ et al. PMID 2869967 — pipecolic acid is oxidized by renal and hepatic peroxisomes; implications for Zellweger syndrome; Mayo Clinic Labs PIPU test citation — elevated pipecolic acid in urine is a marker of peroxisomal biogenesis disorders (Zellweger, OMIM 214100) where PIPOX function is absent; PR:000012749 PIPOX verified in PR.obo. | intervention: PIPOX LoF / peroxisome biogenesis disorder (Zellweger, OMIM 214100): L-pipecolate oxidase activity DOWN → urinary pipecolic acid UP [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "PIPOX (peroxisomal sarcosine oxidase / L-pipecolate oxidase) catalyzes the peroxisomal oxidation of L-pipecolic acid → delta1-piperideine-6-carboxylic acid (entry into the saccharopine/lysine catabolism pathway). Loss of peroxisomal function (Zellweger spectrum disorders) or isolated PIPOX deficiency abolishes pipecolic acid catabolism, causing hyperpiperidinemia and elevated urinary pipecolic acid."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bckdh_enzyme_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_alloisoleucine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abckdh_enzyme_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_alloisoleucine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bckdh_enzyme_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_alloisoleucine> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Maple syrup urine disease GeneReviews NBK1319; OMIM 248600 MSUD — alloisoleucine pathognomonic, formed by keto-enol tautomerization of alpha-keto-beta-methylvalerate; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, BCAA catabolism (BCKDH); StatPearls NBK557773. | intervention: BCKDH LoF (maple syrup urine disease, OMIM 248600): BCKDH activity DOWN → alpha-keto-beta-methylvalerate accumulates → alloisoleucine produced by tautomerization/transamination → urinary alloisoleucine UP"
    ; pmo:mechanism "BCKDH (branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase complex) decarboxylates alpha-keto-beta-methylvalerate (from isoleucine). BCKDH LoF causes all BCKAs to accumulate; the BCKA of isoleucine undergoes keto-enol tautomerization and transamination back to produce alloisoleucine (the diastereomer of isoleucine), a pathognomonic MSUD biomarker. Elevated alloisoleucine >5 µM is diagnostic for MSUD."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/phosphoserine_phosphatase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_phosphoserine> .
