    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "OMMBID PSPH deficiency (serine biosynthesis defect) — O-phosphoserine accumulates, urinary phosphoserine elevated; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, serine biosynthesis (PSPH is the final phosphatase step). | intervention: PSPH LoF (PSPH deficiency, serine biosynthesis defect): phosphoserine phosphatase activity DOWN → O-phosphoserine accumulates → urinary phosphoserine UP"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Phosphoserine phosphatase (PSPH) catalyzes the final step of de novo serine synthesis: O-phosphoserine → serine. PSPH LoF blocks this dephosphorylation, causing O-phosphoserine to accumulate and spill into urine (phosphoserinuria) in addition to reducing plasma serine."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cystinosin_transport_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intracellular_cystine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acystinosin_transport_activity%3Anegative%3Aintracellular_cystine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cystinosin_transport_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intracellular_cystine> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Cystinosis GeneReviews NBK1400 — CTNS mutations cause lysosomal cystine accumulation due to absent cystinosin transporter function; Wikipedia Cystinosis; PR:000006014 cystinosin verified in PR.obo. | intervention: CTNS LoF (cystinosis, OMIM 219800): cystinosin transport activity DOWN → lysosomal cystine export abolished → intracellular (lysosomal) cystine concentration UP"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Cystinosin (encoded by CTNS) is the H(+)-driven lysosomal cystine transporter that exports free cystine from lysosomes to the cytosol. CTNS LoF (cystinosis) abolishes lysosomal cystine export, causing intralysosomal cystine crystals to accumulate throughout cells; intracellular (lysosomal) cystine concentration rises markedly."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cystinosin_transport_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/leukocyte_cystine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acystinosin_transport_activity%3Anegative%3Aleukocyte_cystine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cystinosin_transport_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/leukocyte_cystine> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Cystinosis GeneReviews NBK1400 — leukocyte cystine is the diagnostic test; LC-MS/MS measurement of leukocyte cystine is used for diagnosis and cysteamine treatment monitoring (ScienceDirect 2019); OMIM 219800. | intervention: CTNS LoF (cystinosis, OMIM 219800): cystinosin transport activity DOWN → lysosomal cystine not exported → leukocyte cystine concentration UP"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Cystinosin deficiency causes lysosomal cystine accumulation in all cells including leukocytes. Leukocyte cystine measurement is the standard diagnostic and monitoring assay for cystinosis; CTNS LoF → leukocyte cystine UP."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nags_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_citrulline> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Anags_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_citrulline> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nags_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_citrulline> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "NAGS deficiency overview (Medscape 941090) — biochemical blockade identical to CPS1 deficiency; low citrulline and high glutamine/ammonia; NAGS deficiency Nature Sci Rep 2021; OMIM 237310 NAGS deficiency. | intervention: NAGS LoF (NAGS deficiency, OMIM 237310): NAGS activity DOWN → no N-acetylglutamate → CPS1 inactive → urea cycle blocked → plasma citrulline DOWN"
    ; pmo:mechanism "N-acetylglutamate synthase (NAGS) produces N-acetylglutamate (NAG), the essential allosteric activator of CPS1. Without NAG, CPS1 is inactive and cannot make carbamoyl phosphate; the urea cycle is blocked at its first step. NAGS LoF → CPS1 inactive → no carbamoyl phosphate → OTC has no substrate → citrulline synthesis falls. Biochemically identical presentation to CPS1 deficiency: hyperammonemia, low plasma citrulline."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/carbamoylphosphate_synthetase_1_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_citrulline> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acarbamoylphosphate_synthetase_1_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_citrulline> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/carbamoylphosphate_synthetase_1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_citrulline> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "CPS1 deficiency ScienceDirect overview — CPS1 LoF causes hyperammonemia with low citrulline; PMC11513499 retrospective CPS1 cohort — low plasma citrulline consistently; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, urea cycle (CPS1 step 1, OTC step 2 → citrulline); OMIM 237300 CPS1 deficiency. | intervention: CPS1 LoF (CPS1 deficiency, OMIM 237300): carbamoylphosphate synthetase 1 activity DOWN → no carbamoyl phosphate → OTC substrate absent → plasma citrulline DOWN"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CPS1 catalyzes the first committed step of the urea cycle: ammonia + HCO3- + 2ATP → carbamoyl phosphate. Carbamoyl phosphate is the substrate for OTC, which produces citrulline. CPS1 LoF blocks carbamoyl phosphate production, depriving OTC of substrate, so citrulline synthesis falls."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cystathionine_gamma_lyase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_cystathionine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acystathionine_gamma_lyase_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_cystathionine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cystathionine_gamma_lyase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_cystathionine> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Cystathioninuria Wikipedia; Genomic basis of cystathioninuria — multiple CTH mutations cause cystathioninuria (PMID 12574942); Cystathionine gamma-lyase PMC2752209; OMIM 219500 cystathioninuria. | intervention: CTH LoF (cystathioninuria, OMIM 219500): cystathionine gamma-lyase activity DOWN → cystathionine not cleaved → urinary cystathionine UP [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Cystathionine gamma-lyase (CTH / cystathionase) cleaves cystathionine → cysteine + alpha-ketobutyrate (the second transsulfuration step). CTH LoF (cystathioninuria) causes cystathionine to accumulate in plasma and urine; it is a benign autosomal recessive trait."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cndp1_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_1_methylhistidine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acndp1_activity%3Apositive%3Aurinary_1_methylhistidine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cndp1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_1_methylhistidine> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Carnosinase deficiency PMID 7375183 — urinary 1-methylhistidine measurable and increased on high anserine diet; CNDP1 knockout PMC7402351 — elevated tissue anserine when carnosinase absent; the interventional interpretation: varying CNDP1 activity varies the 1-methylhistidine released from dietary anserine. | intervention: CNDP1 activity perturbation: CNDP1 activity UP → more anserine cleaved → urinary 1-methylhistidine UP; CNDP1 LoF (carnosinemia) → anserine accumulates → urinary 1-methylhistidine falls [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Carnosinase 1 (CNDP1) cleaves anserine (beta-alanyl-1-methylhistidine) → beta-alanine + 1-methylhistidine. CNDP1 activity is the rate-limiting determinant of how much 1-methylhistidine is released from anserine. Higher CNDP1 activity → more anserine hydrolyzed → more urinary 1-methylhistidine; CNDP1 deficiency → anserine accumulates, less free 1-methylhistidine."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pdh_complex_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_lactate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apdh_complex_activity%3Anegative%3Acsf_lactate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pdh_complex_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_lactate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (pyruvate dehydrogenase complex, pyruvate -> acetyl-CoA; LDH: pyruvate <-> lactate); PDH complex deficiency OMIM 312170 (lactic acidosis, elevated CSF lactate); Blau et al., Physician's Guide to Inherited Metabolic Diseases (PDH deficiency — hyperalaninemia, lactic acidosis, elevated CSF lactate and alanine). | intervention: do(PDHA1 LoF) — PDH complex deficiency"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex (PDH) converts pyruvate to acetyl-CoA; PDH deficiency blocks this step, pyruvate accumulates and lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) reduces it to lactate, causing lactic acidosis and elevated CSF lactate."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pdh_complex_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_alanine> .
