    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (blood-CSF barrier glucose transport; GLUT1 as main BBB glucose transporter); GLUT1 deficiency syndrome (De Vivo disease, OMIM 606777) — low CSF glucose with normal blood glucose is the diagnostic hallmark; Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (CSF analysis: normal CSF glucose is ~60-70% of plasma glucose, driven by GLUT1). | intervention: do(SLC2A1 LoF) — GLUT1 deficiency syndrome"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GLUT1 (SLC2A1) is the principal glucose transporter at the blood-brain barrier (endothelial cells) and choroid plexus; GLUT1 deficiency syndrome abolishes glucose transport into CSF, causing hypoglycorrhachia (low CSF glucose) with normal blood glucose."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urea_cycle_enzyme_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_ammonia> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aurea_cycle_enzyme_activity%3Anegative%3Acsf_ammonia> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urea_cycle_enzyme_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_ammonia> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (urea cycle; ammonia detoxification); OTC deficiency OMIM 311250 (hyperammonemia — plasma and CSF ammonia elevated); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (ammonia crosses BBB freely; CSF ammonia reflects blood levels); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (hyperammonemia in urea-cycle disorders; CSF ammonia elevation in hepatic encephalopathy). | intervention: do(OTC LoF) — OTC deficiency / urea-cycle disorders"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Urea cycle enzymes (OTC, CPS1, ASS1, ASL, ARG1) convert ammonia to urea; reduced urea-cycle enzyme activity (e.g., OTC deficiency) leads to hyperammonemia; ammonia is freely diffusible across the blood-brain barrier and equilibrates rapidly into CSF, raising CSF ammonia proportionally to plasma ammonia."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hgprt_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_urate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahgprt_activity%3Anegative%3Acsf_urate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hgprt_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_urate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (purine salvage pathway: HGPRT; hypoxanthine -> xanthine -> urate via xanthine oxidase when salvage fails); Lesch-Nyhan syndrome HPRT1 OMIM 300322 — elevated plasma and CSF uric acid (urate overproduction is the biochemical hallmark); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (Lesch-Nyhan: hyperuricemia and neurological features). | intervention: do(HPRT1 LoF) — Lesch-Nyhan syndrome"
    ; pmo:mechanism "HGPRT (hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase) salvages hypoxanthine and guanine back into the purine nucleotide pool; HGPRT deficiency (Lesch-Nyhan syndrome) routes unsalvaged hypoxanthine and guanine entirely through xanthine oxidase to urate, causing urate overproduction; urate is elevated in both plasma and CSF in Lesch-Nyhan patients."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_dopamine> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_norepinephrine> .
