<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_galactose%3Apositive%3Aplasma_galactitol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_galactose> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_galactitol> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Galactokinase deficiency (type II galactosemia) OMIM 230200; classic galactosemia GALT deficiency OMIM 230400; Saudubray 'Inborn Metabolic Diseases' (galactosemia chapter: galactitol formed by aldose reductase from excess galactose); ScienceDirect 'Galactitol and galactonate accumulation in heart and skeletal muscle of mice with deficiency of galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase' (PMID-based: Galactosemia.com mechanism-of-disease 2024); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (polyol pathway, aldose reductase substrate specificity) | intervention: do(GALK1 LoF) -> galactose UP -> galactitol UP; do(GALT LoF) -> galactose UP -> galactitol UP"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Excess galactose is reduced to galactitol by aldose reductase (AKR1B1/NADPH-dependent polyol pathway); substrate drives product flux. In galactokinase deficiency (GALK1 LoF, type II galactosemia) and classic galactosemia (GALT LoF), galactose accumulates, increasing AKR1B1 flux and plasma galactitol."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_galactose> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_galactitol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_galactose%3Apositive%3Aurinary_galactitol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_galactose> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_galactitol> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Galactokinase deficiency OMIM 230200; classic galactosemia OMIM 230400; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (galactose metabolism, polyol pathway); Saudubray 'Inborn Metabolic Diseases' (galactosemia; urinary galactitol as diagnostic marker); galactosemia.com HCP mechanism page (urinary galactitol elevated in galactosemia) | intervention: do(GALK1 LoF) -> galactose UP -> urinary galactitol UP; clinical diagnosis of galactosemia uses urinary galactitol measurement"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Galactitol formed by AKR1B1 from excess galactose is filtered at the glomerulus and excreted in urine (galactitol is not reabsorbed efficiently by renal tubules). Elevated plasma galactose in galactokinase or GALT deficiency drives urinary galactitol excretion."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/galt_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_galactitol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Agalt_activity%3Anegative%3Aerythrocyte_galactitol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/galt_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_galactitol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Classic galactosemia OMIM 230400 (erythrocyte galactitol elevated in GALT-deficient patients); Saudubray 'Inborn Metabolic Diseases' galactosemia chapter; ScienceDirect 'Galactitol and galactonate accumulation in heart and skeletal muscle of GALT-deficient mice'; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (Leloir pathway: GALT catalyses galactose-1-P + UDP-glucose -> glucose-1-P + UDP-galactose; loss blocks galactose clearance) | intervention: do(GALT LoF) -> erythrocyte galactitol UP (classic galactosemia diagnosis relies on erythrocyte galactitol measurement)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GALT (galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase) deficiency causes galactose-1-phosphate and free galactose to accumulate in erythrocytes; the excess galactose is reduced to galactitol by erythrocyte AKR1B1. More GALT activity -> galactose cleared via Leloir pathway -> less galactitol in RBCs."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/galt_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_galactonate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Agalt_activity%3Anegative%3Aerythrocyte_galactonate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/galt_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_galactonate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Classic galactosemia OMIM 230400; ScienceDirect 'Galactitol and galactonate accumulation in heart and skeletal muscle of mice with deficiency of galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase'; Longdom 'Towards an advanced understanding of pathogenesis of CNS complications in galactosemia — targeting galactitol' (galactonate noted as galactose oxidation product in GALT-deficient tissues); Saudubray 'Inborn Metabolic Diseases' galactosemia chapter | intervention: do(GALT LoF) -> erythrocyte galactonate UP (documented in classic galactosemia patients and GALT-knockout mice)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GALT deficiency causes galactose and galactose-1-phosphate to accumulate in erythrocytes; some galactose is oxidized to galactonate via an alternative non-Leloir oxidative pathway. Increased GALT activity clears galactose through the Leloir route, reducing diversion to galactonate. Galactonate accumulation in erythrocytes is well documented in classic galactosemia."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_sorbitol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_glucose%3Apositive%3Aplasma_sorbitol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_sorbitol> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Pharmacological: Kinoshita et al. (PMID 15531183) 'Erythrocytic sorbitol contents in diabetic patients correlate with blood aldose reductase protein contents and plasma glucose levels, and are normalized by the potent aldose reductase inhibitor fidarestat'; Yan 2018 (PMC5975374) 'Redox imbalance stress in diabetes mellitus: Role of the polyol pathway'; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (polyol pathway: glucose -> sorbitol by aldose reductase); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (diabetic polyol pathway complications) | intervention: do(hyperglycemia) -> plasma sorbitol UP; do(ARI fidarestat) -> plasma sorbitol DOWN (reversal confirms causal direction) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Aldose reductase (AKR1B1) reduces glucose to sorbitol using NADPH (first step of the polyol pathway). Hyperglycemia drives increased substrate flux through AKR1B1, raising plasma/erythrocyte sorbitol. The causal direction is confirmed by pharmacological intervention: ARI fidarestat (SNK-860) normalises erythrocyte sorbitol in diabetic patients in a dose-dependent manner."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_anhydroglucitol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_glucose%3Anegative%3Aplasma_anhydroglucitol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_anhydroglucitol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Kilpatrick et al. 1999 Diabetic Medicine (PMID 10391398) 'Plasma 1,5-anhydroglucitol concentrations are influenced by variations in the renal threshold for glucose'; Sechterberger et al. 2012 Diabetes Care (PMC3263896) 'Serum 1,5-anhydroglucitol concentrations are a reliable index of glycaemic control'; Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (renal tubular competitive reabsorption); 1,5-AG mass balance model ScienceDirect 2008 (SGLT4 competitive inhibition mechanism) | intervention: do(hyperglycemia -> glycosuria) -> plasma 1,5-AG DOWN; do(glucose normalisation) -> plasma 1,5-AG UP (reversibility confirmed in clinical studies) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "1,5-anhydroglucitol (1,5-AG) is reabsorbed at the renal tubule via SGLT4, competing with glucose. Hyperglycemia causes glycosuria; urinary glucose competitively inhibits 1,5-AG tubular reabsorption (SGLT4), reducing plasma 1,5-AG. When glycaemia normalises and glycosuria resolves, 1,5-AG rises. This pharmacological/physiological mechanism is the basis of 1,5-AG as a short-term glycaemic marker."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fructokinase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_fructose> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afructokinase_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_fructose> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fructokinase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_fructose> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Essential fructosuria OMIM 229700 (KHK/fructokinase deficiency; urinary fructose elevated as diagnostic marker); Saudubray 'Inborn Metabolic Diseases' (essential fructosuria chapter: fructokinase deficiency -> fructosuria); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (fructose metabolism: fructokinase, Ch. 14); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (fructose disorders) | intervention: do(KHK LoF) -> urinary fructose UP (essential fructosuria, OMIM 229700; benign disorder diagnosed by fructosuria on urine reducing substance screening)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Fructokinase (KHK, ketohexokinase) phosphorylates fructose to fructose-1-phosphate in the liver (first step of hepatic fructose metabolism). KHK deficiency (essential fructosuria) means fructose is not phosphorylated, circulates freely, and is excreted in urine. More KHK activity clears fructose, reducing urinary excretion."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alpha_l_fucosidase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_fucose> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aalpha_l_fucosidase_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_fucose> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alpha_l_fucosidase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_fucose> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Fucosidosis OMIM 230000 (FUCA1 deficiency; urinary fucose-containing oligosaccharides and free fucose elevated); MDPI Genes 2020 'Fucosidosis—Clinical Manifestation, Long-Term Outcomes, and Genetic Profile'; NORD (fucosidosis: accumulation of fucose-rich substrates in lysosomes and urine); Saudubray 'Inborn Metabolic Diseases' (oligosaccharidoses, fucosidosis) | intervention: do(FUCA1 LoF) -> urinary L-fucose and fucose-oligosaccharides UP (fucosidosis diagnostic biomarker)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Lysosomal alpha-L-fucosidase (FUCA1) cleaves terminal L-fucose residues from fucosylated glycoproteins and glycolipids during lysosomal catabolism. FUCA1 deficiency (fucosidosis) causes accumulation of fucose-containing oligosaccharides and free L-fucose in lysosomes, which are then excreted in urine. More fucosidase activity reduces the urinary fucose burden."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/neuraminidase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_n_acetylneuraminate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aneuraminidase_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_n_acetylneuraminate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/neuraminidase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_n_acetylneuraminate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Sialidosis OMIM 256550 (NEU1/neuraminidase deficiency; urinary sialylated oligosaccharides elevated); Salla disease OMIM 604369 (SLC17A5/sialin deficiency; free sialic acid in urine); Saudubray 'Inborn Metabolic Diseases' (sialuria, sialidosis, Salla disease chapters); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (sialic acid/N-acetylneuraminic acid in glycoprotein catabolism) | intervention: do(NEU1 LoF) -> urinary N-acetylneuraminic acid UP (sialidosis; confirmed diagnostic biomarker)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Lysosomal sialidase (NEU1, neuraminidase-1) cleaves N-acetylneuraminic acid (sialic acid/NANA) from sialoglycoconjugates. NEU1 deficiency (sialidosis, OMIM 256550) causes sialylated oligosaccharides to accumulate and spill into urine as NANA-containing species. Additionally, SLC17A5 (sialin) transporter deficiency (Salla disease, OMIM 604369; free sialic acid storage disease) causes free NANA to accumulate in lysosomes and be excreted in urine. More NEU1 activity -> more complete sialooligosaccharide degradation -> less urinary NANA."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dcxr_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_l_xylulose> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Adcxr_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_l_xylulose> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dcxr_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_l_xylulose> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Essential pentosuria OMIM 260800 (DCXR/L-xylulose reductase deficiency; characterised by L-xylulosuria); Springer link 'On the nature of l-xylulose reductase deficiency in essential pentosuria' Biochemical Genetics; MedlinePlus Genetics (essential pentosuria: DCXR gene, L-xylulose in urine); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (glucuronate-xylulose pathway; L-xylulose -> xylitol by L-xylulose reductase/DCXR) | intervention: do(DCXR LoF) -> urinary L-xylulose UP (essential pentosuria, OMIM 260800; benign autosomal recessive condition in Ashkenazi Jews)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "DCXR (dicarbonyl/L-xylulose reductase) reduces L-xylulose to xylitol (NADPH-dependent) as part of the glucuronate-xylulose pathway. DCXR deficiency (essential pentosuria, OMIM 260800) blocks this conversion, causing L-xylulose to accumulate and be excreted in urine (pentosuria). More DCXR activity clears L-xylulose; its loss-of-function raises urinary L-xylulose."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/transaldolase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ribitol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atransaldolase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_ribitol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/transaldolase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ribitol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Transaldolase deficiency OMIM 606003; Wamelink et al. 2001 AJHG (PMC1226089) 'Transaldolase deficiency: liver cirrhosis associated with a new inborn error in the pentose phosphate pathway' (PMID 11283793; elevated ribitol and arabitol in plasma); Mayo Clinic Labs TALDO panel (ribitol, arabitol key diagnostic markers) | intervention: do(TALDO1 LoF) -> plasma ribitol UP (primary diagnostic biomarker of transaldolase deficiency) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "TALDO1 deficiency causes ribose-5-phosphate to accumulate upstream; ribose-5-P is reduced to ribitol (by NADPH-dependent reductases), which accumulates in plasma. Elevated plasma ribitol is a hallmark of transaldolase deficiency."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/transaldolase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_ribitol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atransaldolase_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_ribitol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/transaldolase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_ribitol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Transaldolase deficiency OMIM 606003; Wamelink et al. 2001 AJHG (PMID 11283793); Williams et al. 2019 JIMD; Mayo Clinic Labs TALDO panel overview (ribitol elevated in urine); Saudubray 'Inborn Metabolic Diseases' (transaldolase deficiency) | intervention: do(TALDO1 LoF) -> urinary ribitol UP (primary diagnostic marker; Mayo Clinic TALDO polyol panel) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "TALDO1 deficiency -> ribose-5-P accumulation -> ribitol formed and excreted in urine. Urinary ribitol is elevated in all characterised patients with transaldolase deficiency."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/transaldolase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_ribitol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atransaldolase_activity%3Anegative%3Acsf_ribitol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/transaldolase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_ribitol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Transaldolase deficiency OMIM 606003; PMC4245499 (Clinical and molecular characteristics of two transaldolase-deficient patients, CSF polyol data); Saudubray 'Inborn Metabolic Diseases' (transaldolase deficiency CSF features) | intervention: do(TALDO1 LoF) -> CSF ribitol UP (confirmed in at least one patient with CSF polyol analysis; OMIM 606003) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "TALDO1 deficiency causes polyol accumulation in CNS compartments as well as peripheral fluids. CSF ribitol was measured as elevated (19 μmol/L, reference <6 μmol/L) in a TALDO1-deficient patient."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/transaldolase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_arabinitol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atransaldolase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_arabinitol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/transaldolase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_arabinitol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Transaldolase deficiency OMIM 606003; Wamelink et al. 2001 AJHG (PMID 11283793; elevated D-arabitol and ribitol in plasma); Williams et al. 2019 JIMD ('elevated arabitol, ribitol, and erythritol in urine and plasma in all patients'); Mayo Clinic Labs TALDO panel (arabitol and ribitol elevated) | intervention: do(TALDO1 LoF) -> plasma arabinitol UP (primary diagnostic metabolite) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "TALDO1 deficiency causes xylulose-5-P and related C5 intermediates to accumulate; xylulose-5-P is reduced to arabinitol (D-arabitol). Elevated plasma arabinitol (D-arabitol) is a hallmark metabolite of transaldolase deficiency."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/transaldolase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_arabitol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atransaldolase_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_arabitol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/transaldolase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_arabitol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Transaldolase deficiency OMIM 606003; Wamelink et al. 2001 AJHG (PMID 11283793); PMC8522900 (transaldolase deficiency clinical outcome pathogenesis diagnostic process — arabitol elevated in urine); Saudubray 'Inborn Metabolic Diseases' | intervention: do(TALDO1 LoF) -> urinary arabitol UP (well-established diagnostic marker) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "TALDO1 deficiency -> xylulose-5-P accumulates -> reduced to D-arabinitol -> excreted in urine. Urinary D-arabinitol is a primary diagnostic metabolite of transaldolase deficiency."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/transaldolase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_xylitol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atransaldolase_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_xylitol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/transaldolase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_xylitol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Transaldolase deficiency OMIM 606003; Frontiers Genetics 2021 (PMC8855097) 'Prenatal Diagnosis of Fetus With Transaldolase Deficiency: qualitative analysis showed xylitol + arabitol and ribitol significantly increased in neonatal urine'; Saudubray 'Inborn Metabolic Diseases' | intervention: do(TALDO1 LoF) -> urinary xylitol UP (confirmed in neonatal urinary polyol screening in OMIM 606003) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "TALDO1 deficiency causes accumulation of multiple pentose phosphate pathway intermediates including xylulose-5-P, which is reduced to the polyol mixture that includes xylitol and arabitol. The prenatal diagnosis report (Frontiers Genetics 2021) specifically documented 'xylitol + arabitol significantly increased in neonatal urine' of TALDO1-deficient patients."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/transaldolase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_erythritol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atransaldolase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_erythritol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/transaldolase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_erythritol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Transaldolase deficiency OMIM 606003; Wamelink et al. 2001 AJHG (PMID 11283793; 'elevated concentrations of ribitol, D-arabitol, and erythritol found in urine and plasma'); Williams et al. 2019 JIMD (erythritol elevated in all patients); Mayo Clinic TALDO polyol panel (erythritol is a key analyte) | intervention: do(TALDO1 LoF) -> plasma erythritol UP (primary diagnostic biomarker) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "TALDO1 deficiency causes erythrose-4-phosphate to accumulate upstream of the transaldolase reaction; erythrose-4-P is reduced to erythritol. Plasma erythritol is elevated in all documented transaldolase-deficient patients."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/transaldolase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_erythritol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atransaldolase_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_erythritol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/transaldolase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_erythritol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Transaldolase deficiency OMIM 606003; Wamelink et al. 2001 AJHG (PMID 11283793); Sedoheptulokinase deficiency PMC4551550 (erythritol also elevated in SHPK deficiency as secondary PPP marker); Saudubray 'Inborn Metabolic Diseases' | intervention: do(TALDO1 LoF) -> urinary erythritol UP (primary diagnostic marker; also elevated in SHPK deficiency OMIM/Wamelink 2015 JIMD) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "TALDO1 deficiency -> erythrose-4-P accumulates -> reduced to erythritol -> excreted in urine. Urinary erythritol is elevated in transaldolase deficiency (and also in sedoheptulokinase deficiency as a secondary marker)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta_hexosaminidase_b_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_glcnac_oligosaccharide> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abeta_hexosaminidase_b_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_glcnac_oligosaccharide> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta_hexosaminidase_b_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_glcnac_oligosaccharide> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Scriver's OMMBID / Saudubray Inborn Metabolic Diseases: Sandhoff disease (HEXB deficiency, OMIM 268800) — urinary GlcNAc-rich oligosaccharides are a diagnostic feature; Williams Hematology / lysosomal storage disease references confirm oligosacchariduria. | intervention: HEXB loss-of-function (Sandhoff disease): do(beta_hexosaminidase_b_activity↓) → urinary GlcNAc-oligosaccharide excretion↑"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Beta-hexosaminidase B (HEXB) cleaves N-acetylglucosamine residues from gangliosides, glycoproteins, and keratan sulfate in lysosomes. HEXB LoF (Sandhoff disease, OMIM 268800) causes both Hex A and Hex B enzymes to be deficient, leading to accumulation and urinary excretion of GlcNAc-rich GM2 ganglioside-derived oligosaccharides and glycoprotein fragments."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta_galactosidase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_galactosylated_oligosaccharide> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abeta_galactosidase_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_galactosylated_oligosaccharide> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta_galactosidase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_galactosylated_oligosaccharide> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Scriver's OMMBID: GM1 gangliosidosis (GLB1 deficiency, OMIM 230500) — galactosylated oligosacchariduria is a hallmark diagnostic finding; Saudubray Inborn Metabolic Diseases (galactosialidosis / GM1 chapter). | intervention: GLB1 loss-of-function (GM1 gangliosidosis): do(beta_galactosidase_activity↓) → urinary galactosylated oligosaccharide excretion↑"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Lysosomal acid beta-galactosidase (GLB1) cleaves terminal galactose from gangliosides (GM1), keratan sulfate, and galactose-containing glycoproteins. GLB1 LoF (GM1 gangliosidosis / Morquio B, OMIM 230500 / 253010) causes galactosylated oligosaccharides and galactose-rich glycoprotein fragments to accumulate and be excreted in urine."
    .
