<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aglycogen_debrancher_activity%3Anegative%3Amuscle_glycogen_content> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glycogen_debrancher_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/muscle_glycogen_content> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 15 (glycogen debranching: AGL enzymatic mechanism); Scriver's OMMBID (Cori disease / GSD-III, OMIM 232400: skeletal muscle glycogen accumulation with limit-dextrin structure); Harrison's Principles (glycogen-storage disease type III — myopathy from muscle glycogen accumulation). | intervention: AGL loss-of-function (Cori disease, GSD-III): do(glycogen_debrancher_activity↓) → muscle glycogen accumulates (limit dextrin) → muscle_glycogen_content↑"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Glycogen debranching enzyme (AGL) catalyzes two sequential reactions at glycogen branch points (glucan transferase + alpha-1,6-glucosidase), enabling phosphorylase to continue degrading the glycogen chain. AGL LoF (GSD-III / Cori disease, OMIM 232400) → glycogen degradation stalls at branch points → glycogen accumulates in both liver and skeletal muscle (GSD-IIIa). AGL activity ↑ → branch resolution → muscle glycogen content ↓."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glucose_6_phosphate_translocase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aglucose_6_phosphate_translocase_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_glucose> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glucose_6_phosphate_translocase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 15 (hepatic glucose-6-phosphatase system: G6P translocase + G6Pase share the ER-membrane complex); Scriver's OMMBID (GSD-Ib, SLC37A4 deficiency, OMIM 232220: fasting hypoglycemia, neutropenia); Harrison's Principles (glycogen-storage disease Ib — clinical hypoglycemia). | intervention: SLC37A4 loss-of-function (GSD-Ib): do(glucose_6_phosphate_translocase_activity↓) → plasma glucose↓ (fasting hypoglycemia)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "SLC37A4 (glucose-6-phosphate translocase) transports glucose-6-phosphate from the cytoplasm into the ER lumen, where glucose-6-phosphatase (G6PC) dephosphorylates it to free glucose for export into plasma. SLC37A4 LoF (GSD-Ib, OMIM 232220) → G6P cannot enter the ER → hepatic glucose output from both glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis is blocked → fasting hypoglycemia. SLC37A4 activity ↑ → more G6P translocated → more free glucose release → plasma_glucose ↑."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/galt_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_galactose> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Agalt_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_galactose> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/galt_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_galactose> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 15 (Leloir pathway: GALT is the galactose-1-P uridylyltransferase step); Scriver's OMMBID (classic galactosemia, OMIM 230400 — hypergalactosaemia, galactosuria); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (galactose metabolism in liver). | intervention: GALT loss-of-function (classic galactosemia, OMIM 230400): do(galt_activity↓) → plasma galactose accumulates (hypergalactosaemia)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GALT (galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase) catalyzes the exchange reaction: galactose-1-phosphate + UDP-glucose → glucose-1-phosphate + UDP-galactose (second step of the Leloir galactose pathway). GALT LoF (classic galactosemia, OMIM 230400) → galactose-1-P accumulates, galactose cannot be cleared → plasma galactose rises markedly on a galactose-containing diet. GALT activity ↑ → galactose-1-P converted to UDP-Gal → pathway flux increases → plasma galactose ↓."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/galt_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_galactitol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Agalt_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_galactitol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/galt_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_galactitol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Scriver's OMMBID (classic galactosemia, OMIM 230400: galactitol accumulation in lens, urine, plasma — mechanism is aldose reductase-mediated reduction of excess galactose); Saudubray Inborn Metabolic Diseases (galactosemia: galactitol as toxic byproduct). | intervention: GALT loss-of-function (classic galactosemia): do(galt_activity↓) → galactose↑ → aldose reductase → plasma galactitol↑"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GALT LoF → galactose accumulates → excess galactose is reduced to galactitol by NADPH-dependent aldose reductase (a non-specific polyol pathway enzyme). Galactitol cannot be further metabolized and accumulates in plasma, lens (cataracts), and brain. GALT activity ↑ → galactose cleared → less substrate for aldose reductase → plasma galactitol ↓."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/galactokinase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_galactose> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Agalactokinase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_galactose> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/galactokinase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_galactose> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 15 (Leloir pathway first step: galactokinase phosphorylates galactose); Scriver's OMMBID (GALK deficiency / galactosemia type II, OMIM 230200: hypergalactosaemia, cataracts from galactitol in lens); Harrison's (galactose metabolism disorders). | intervention: GALK1 loss-of-function (galactosemia type II, OMIM 230200): do(galactokinase_activity↓) → plasma galactose accumulates"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GALK1 (galactokinase) catalyzes the first step of the Leloir pathway: galactose + ATP → galactose-1-phosphate + ADP. GALK1 LoF (galactosemia type II / GALK deficiency, OMIM 230200) → galactose is not phosphorylated → plasma galactose accumulates. GALK activity ↑ → galactose rapidly phosphorylated → plasma galactose ↓."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/galactokinase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_galactitol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Agalactokinase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_galactitol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/galactokinase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_galactitol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Scriver's OMMBID (GALK deficiency / galactosemia type II, OMIM 230200: galactitol accumulation via aldose reductase is the cataract mechanism); Saudubray Inborn Metabolic Diseases (GALK deficiency: isolated cataract galactosemia from galactitol). | intervention: GALK1 loss-of-function (galactosemia type II): do(galactokinase_activity↓) → galactose↑ → aldose reductase → galactitol↑"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GALK1 LoF → galactose cannot be phosphorylated → free galactose accumulates → aldose reductase reduces excess galactose to galactitol. Galactitol accumulation is the primary cause of cataracts in GALK deficiency. GALK activity ↑ → galactose phosphorylated (entering the Leloir pathway rather than the aldose reductase route) → less galactitol formed."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/udp_glucose_4_epimerase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_galactose> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Audp_glucose_4_epimerase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_galactose> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/udp_glucose_4_epimerase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_galactose> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Scriver's OMMBID (GALE deficiency / galactosemia type III, OMIM 230350: biochemical phenotype includes elevated galactose metabolites); Saudubray Inborn Metabolic Diseases (generalized GALE deficiency resembles classic galactosemia: hypergalactosaemia on galactose intake); Lehninger Ch. 15 (GALE reaction: UDP-Gal ↔ UDP-Glc). | intervention: GALE loss-of-function (galactosemia type III, OMIM 230350): do(udp_glucose_4_epimerase_activity↓) → galactose/UDP-Gal accumulates → plasma galactose↑"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GALE (UDP-glucose 4-epimerase) interconverts UDP-galactose ↔ UDP-glucose at equilibrium. GALE LoF (galactosemia type III / GALE deficiency, OMIM 230350) → UDP-galactose cannot be converted to UDP-glucose → galactose-containing metabolites accumulate; in the severe generalized form, galactose itself rises in plasma because the downstream disposal of UDP-Gal (via its conversion to UDP-Glc) is impaired. GALE activity ↑ → UDP-Gal cleared → plasma galactose ↓."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hexokinase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/red_cell_mass> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahexokinase_activity%3Apositive%3Ared_cell_mass> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hexokinase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/red_cell_mass> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Williams Hematology (HK deficiency: non-spherocytic hemolytic anemia — glycolytic enzyme defect, OMIM 235700); Scriver's OMMBID (erythrocyte enzymopathies: HK1 deficiency causes chronic hemolytic anemia); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 14 (hexokinase: committed step of glycolysis). | intervention: HK1 loss-of-function (congenital hemolytic anemia, OMIM 235700): do(hexokinase_activity↓) → erythrocyte ATP depletion → hemolysis → red_cell_mass↓"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Erythrocyte hexokinase-1 (HK1) phosphorylates glucose to glucose-6-phosphate, the committed entry step into glycolysis in red cells. HK1 LoF (congenital non-spherocytic hemolytic anemia type I, OMIM 235700) → glycolytic failure in mature erythrocytes → ATP depletion → premature hemolysis → reduced red cell mass. HK activity ↑ → sustained glycolysis → RBC viability → red_cell_mass ↑."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glucose_phosphate_isomerase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/red_cell_mass> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aglucose_phosphate_isomerase_activity%3Apositive%3Ared_cell_mass> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glucose_phosphate_isomerase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/red_cell_mass> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Williams Hematology (GPI deficiency: hemolytic anemia, OMIM 172400 — second most common glycolytic enzymopathy after PK deficiency); Scriver's OMMBID (erythrocyte glycolytic enzyme deficiencies); Lehninger Ch. 14 (phosphoglucose isomerase: G6P ↔ F6P). | intervention: GPI loss-of-function (GPI deficiency, OMIM 172400): do(glucose_phosphate_isomerase_activity↓) → erythrocyte glycolytic failure → hemolysis → red_cell_mass↓"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GPI (glucose-6-phosphate isomerase) catalyzes the second step of glycolysis: G6P ↔ F6P. GPI LoF (congenital non-spherocytic hemolytic anemia / GPI deficiency, OMIM 172400) → glycolytic failure in mature RBCs → ATP depletion → premature hemolysis → reduced red cell mass. GPI activity ↑ → glycolytic flux maintained → RBC viability → red_cell_mass ↑."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldolase_b_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_fructose> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aaldolase_b_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_fructose> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldolase_b_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_fructose> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 15 (fructose catabolism: KHK → F1P; ALDOB cleaves F1P); Scriver's OMMBID (hereditary fructose intolerance, OMIM 229600 — fructosuria is a diagnostic feature after fructose/sucrose ingestion); Harrison's Principles (HFI: nausea, hypoglycemia, fructosuria after fructose intake). | intervention: ALDOB loss-of-function (HFI, OMIM 229600): do(aldolase_b_activity↓) → fructose-1-P accumulates → fructose backs up → urinary_fructose↑"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Hepatic aldolase B (ALDOB) cleaves fructose-1-phosphate (produced by ketohexokinase/KHK from dietary fructose) into dihydroxyacetone phosphate and glyceraldehyde. ALDOB LoF (hereditary fructose intolerance, HFI, OMIM 229600) → fructose-1-P accumulates in hepatocytes → fructose cannot be cleared (KHK product inhibition and direct fructose backup) → plasma fructose rises → fructosuria. ALDOB activity ↑ → fructose-1-P cleared → fructose catabolism proceeds → urinary fructose ↓."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pyruvate_kinase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_23_bpg> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apyruvate_kinase_activity%3Anegative%3Aerythrocyte_23_bpg> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pyruvate_kinase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_23_bpg> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 14 (pyruvate kinase, last step of glycolysis: PEP→pyruvate); Williams Hematology (PK deficiency, OMIM 266200: characteristic elevation of 2,3-BPG and upstream glycolytic intermediates — a well-established phenotypic marker used in diagnosis); Scriver's OMMBID (pyruvate kinase deficiency). | intervention: PKLR loss-of-function (PK deficiency, OMIM 266200): do(pyruvate_kinase_activity↓) → erythrocyte 2,3-BPG markedly elevated"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Pyruvate kinase (PKLR) catalyzes the last ATP-generating step of glycolysis: PEP + ADP → pyruvate + ATP. PKLR LoF in RBCs (PK deficiency, OMIM 266200) → glycolytic block → accumulation of all upstream glycolytic intermediates including 1,3-BPG → BPGM shunts excess 1,3-BPG to 2,3-BPG → erythrocyte 2,3-BPG markedly elevated (characteristic of PK deficiency). PKLR activity ↑ → PEP converted to pyruvate → 1,3-BPG drained → less substrate for BPGM → erythrocyte 2,3-BPG ↓."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/g6pd_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/red_cell_mass> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ag6pd_activity%3Apositive%3Ared_cell_mass> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/g6pd_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/red_cell_mass> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Williams Hematology (G6PD deficiency: X-linked, episodic or chronic hemolytic anemia, OMIM 305900 — most common enzymopathy worldwide); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 14 (pentose phosphate pathway: G6PD generates NADPH for GSH protection of RBCs); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (erythrocyte antioxidant defense via G6PD-NADPH-GSH). | intervention: G6PD loss-of-function (G6PD deficiency, OMIM 305900): do(g6pd_activity↓) → NADPH↓ → oxidative hemolysis → red_cell_mass↓"
    ; pmo:mechanism "G6PD (glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase) catalyzes the first step of the pentose phosphate pathway, generating NADPH. In mature erythrocytes (which lack mitochondria), G6PD is the sole source of NADPH, required to regenerate reduced glutathione (GSH) via glutathione reductase. GSH protects RBC membranes and Hb from oxidative damage. G6PD LoF (G6PD deficiency, OMIM 305900) → NADPH depletion → oxidative stress → Heinz body formation → hemolytic anemia → reduced red cell mass. G6PD activity ↑ → NADPH adequate → GSH maintained → RBC survival → red_cell_mass ↑."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fructose_1_6_bisphosphatase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afructose_1_6_bisphosphatase_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_glucose> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fructose_1_6_bisphosphatase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 15 (gluconeogenesis: FBP1 is the key regulatory step, hydrolyzes F1,6-BP → F6P; flux-controlling enzyme); Scriver's OMMBID (FBP1 deficiency, OMIM 229700: fasting hypoglycemia + lactic acidosis in neonates/infants); Harrison's Principles (gluconeogenic enzyme deficiencies — FBP1). | intervention: FBP1 loss-of-function (FBP1 deficiency, OMIM 229700): do(fructose_1_6_bisphosphatase_activity↓) → gluconeogenesis blocked → fasting plasma glucose↓"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase 1 (FBP1) catalyzes the irreversible hydrolysis of fructose-1,6-bisphosphate to fructose-6-phosphate in gluconeogenesis, a committed rate-controlling step. FBP1 LoF (FBP1 deficiency, OMIM 229700) → gluconeogenesis blocked → fasting hypoglycemia (plasma glucose falls) with lactic acidosis (lactate cannot be converted to glucose). FBP1 activity ↑ → gluconeogenic flux ↑ → hepatic glucose production ↑ → plasma glucose ↑."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pepck_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apepck_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_glucose> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pepck_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 15 (gluconeogenesis: PEPCK catalyzes the first flux-controlling step, OAA → PEP; required for amino acid and lactate entry into gluconeogenesis); Scriver's OMMBID (PEPCK1 deficiency, OMIM 261680: fasting hypoglycemia with lactic acidosis); Harrison's Principles (gluconeogenic defects). | intervention: PCK1 loss-of-function (PEPCK1 deficiency, OMIM 261680): do(pepck_activity↓) → gluconeogenesis blocked → fasting plasma glucose↓"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Cytosolic PEPCK (PCK1) catalyzes the first committed step of gluconeogenesis from oxaloacetate: OAA + GTP → phosphoenolpyruvate (PEP) + CO2 + GDP. PCK1 LoF (PEPCK1 deficiency, OMIM 261680) → the gluconeogenic pathway from amino acids, lactate, and glycerol is blocked at the PEP step → fasting hypoglycemia with lactic acidosis. PCK1 activity ↑ → more PEP generated → gluconeogenic flux ↑ → plasma glucose ↑."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/phosphomannomutase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_transferrin_glycoform_ratio> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aphosphomannomutase_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_transferrin_glycoform_ratio> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/phosphomannomutase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_transferrin_glycoform_ratio> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (N-glycosylation pathway: mannose-1-P → GDP-mannose is required for dolichol-PP-oligosaccharide assembly); Scriver's OMMBID / Saudubray Inborn Metabolic Diseases (PMM2-CDG, CDG-Ia, OMIM 212065: most common CDG — serum transferrin isoelectrofocusing shows type I CDG pattern, reduced normally glycosylated tetrasialo-transferrin); Harrison's Principles (CDG syndromes — PMM2 deficiency). | intervention: PMM2 loss-of-function (PMM2-CDG, OMIM 212065): do(phosphomannomutase_activity↓) → GDP-mannose↓ → hypoglycosylation of serotransferrin → plasma_transferrin_glycoform_ratio↓"
    ; pmo:mechanism "PMM2 converts mannose-6-phosphate to mannose-1-phosphate, which is the precursor for GDP-mannose synthesis. GDP-mannose is the essential sugar nucleotide donor for mannose incorporation into N-linked glycans in the ER. PMM2 LoF (PMM2-CDG / CDG-Ia, OMIM 212065) → GDP-mannose deficiency → incomplete N-glycosylation of secreted glycoproteins including serotransferrin → CDG type-I transferrin isoelectrofocusing pattern (elevated disialo- and asialo-transferrin) → proportion of correctly glycosylated transferrin (tetrasialo-form) falls. PMM2 activity ↑ → adequate GDP-mannose → complete N-glycosylation → normal transferrin glycoform ratio ↑."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/phosphomannose_isomerase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_transferrin_glycoform_ratio> .
