    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Scriver's OMMBID / Saudubray Inborn Metabolic Diseases (MPI-CDG / CDG-Ib, OMIM 602579: type I CDG transferrin pattern; oral mannose supplementation corrects glycosylation by bypassing MPI — confirms the flux control); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (mannose-6-phosphate in N-glycan biosynthesis pathway); Harrison's Principles (CDG-Ib: treatable CDG with mannose supplementation). | intervention: MPI loss-of-function (MPI-CDG / CDG-Ib, OMIM 602579): do(phosphomannose_isomerase_activity↓) → mannose-6-P↓ → GDP-mannose↓ → transferrin hypoglycosylation → plasma_transferrin_glycoform_ratio↓"
    ; pmo:mechanism "MPI (phosphomannose isomerase / mannose-6-phosphate isomerase) interconverts fructose-6-phosphate ↔ mannose-6-phosphate, providing mannose-6-P for the GDP-mannose synthetic pathway. MPI LoF (MPI-CDG / CDG-Ib, OMIM 602579) → mannose-6-P deficiency → GDP-mannose deficiency → N-glycosylation defect → CDG type-I transferrin isoelectrofocusing pattern (reduced normally glycosylated tetrasialo-transferrin). MPI activity ↑ → mannose-6-P maintained → GDP-mannose adequate → complete glycosylation → normal transferrin glycoform ratio ↑."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glycerol_kinase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glycerol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aglycerol_kinase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_glycerol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glycerol_kinase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glycerol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 17 (glycerol-3-phosphate from adipose lipolysis: GK phosphorylates glycerol; hepatic glycerol kinase is essential for glycerol utilization in gluconeogenesis); Scriver's OMMBID (glycerol kinase deficiency, OMIM 307030: X-linked isolated GK deficiency — hypertriglyceridemia + glycerolemia + glyceroluria from inability to phosphorylate plasma glycerol); Harrison's Principles (glycerol kinase deficiency — pseudohypertriglyceridaemia from accumulated glycerol). | intervention: GK loss-of-function (glycerol kinase deficiency, OMIM 307030): do(glycerol_kinase_activity↓) → plasma glycerol accumulates (glycerolemia)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Glycerol kinase (GK) phosphorylates free glycerol to glycerol-3-phosphate, committing it to glycolysis (via DHAP) or to phospholipid/triacylglycerol synthesis. GK is the principal route for clearance of circulating glycerol in liver, intestine, and adipose tissue. GK LoF (glycerol kinase deficiency, OMIM 307030) → glycerol cannot be phosphorylated → plasma glycerol accumulates (glycerolemia/glyceroluria is the diagnostic marker). GK activity ↑ → glycerol cleared → plasma glycerol ↓."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lipoprotein_lipase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_total_lipids> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alipoprotein_lipase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_total_lipids> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lipoprotein_lipase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_total_lipids> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, Disorders of Lipoprotein Metabolism; OMIM 238600 (LPL deficiency / familial chylomicronemia syndrome); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology, lipoprotein transport | intervention: LPL loss-of-function (familial chylomicronemia syndrome): do(LPL activity down) -> total plasma lipids up"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LPL hydrolyses core triacylglycerol of TG-rich lipoprotein particles (chylomicrons, VLDL), driving their clearance; LPL loss-of-function (familial chylomicronemia syndrome, OMIM 238600) causes massive hypertriglyceridaemia and elevated total plasma lipids due to failure to clear TG-rich particles."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lipoprotein_lipase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_lipoprotein> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alipoprotein_lipase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_lipoprotein> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lipoprotein_lipase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_lipoprotein> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, Disorders of Lipoprotein Metabolism; OMIM 238600 (LPL deficiency); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology, lipoprotein transport | intervention: LPL loss-of-function (OMIM 238600): do(LPL activity down) -> plasma lipoprotein particle concentration up"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LPL activity drives lipolytic clearance of TG-rich lipoprotein particles from the circulation; without LPL activity, chylomicrons and VLDL accumulate, raising total plasma lipoprotein particle number."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lipoprotein_lipase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_non_hdl_cholesterol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alipoprotein_lipase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_non_hdl_cholesterol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lipoprotein_lipase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_non_hdl_cholesterol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, Disorders of Lipoprotein Metabolism; OMIM 238600 (LPL deficiency); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology | intervention: LPL loss-of-function (familial chylomicronemia, OMIM 238600): do(LPL activity down) -> non-HDL cholesterol up"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LPL clears TG-rich lipoprotein particles (chylomicrons, VLDL, IDL); these particles carry the bulk of non-HDL cholesterol. LPL loss-of-function causes chylomicron and VLDL accumulation, raising non-HDL cholesterol."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lipoprotein_lipase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_vldl> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alipoprotein_lipase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_vldl> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lipoprotein_lipase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_vldl> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology, lipoprotein transport (VLDL->IDL->LDL cascade dependent on LPL); OMIM 238600 | intervention: LPL loss-of-function: do(LPL activity down) -> plasma VLDL up (type I/V hyperlipoproteinemia)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LPL catalyses hydrolysis of VLDL core triacylglycerol on capillary endothelium, converting VLDL to IDL and LDL; reduced LPL activity slows VLDL catabolism and raises plasma VLDL."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lipoprotein_lipase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/vldl_cholesterol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alipoprotein_lipase_activity%3Anegative%3Avldl_cholesterol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lipoprotein_lipase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/vldl_cholesterol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology, lipoprotein transport; OMIM 238600 (LPL deficiency / hypertriglyceridaemia) | intervention: LPL loss-of-function (OMIM 238600): do(LPL activity down) -> VLDL-cholesterol up"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LPL-mediated catabolism of VLDL particles removes VLDL cholesterol from circulation by converting VLDL to IDL/LDL; LPL loss raises plasma VLDL-cholesterol."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lipoprotein_lipase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_chylomicrons> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alipoprotein_lipase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_chylomicrons> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lipoprotein_lipase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_chylomicrons> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, Disorders of Lipoprotein Metabolism; OMIM 238600 (LPL deficiency) | intervention: LPL loss-of-function (familial chylomicronemia, OMIM 238600): do(LPL activity down) -> plasma chylomicrons up"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LPL anchored on capillary endothelium hydrolyses core triacylglycerol of chylomicrons, driving their clearance; loss of LPL causes fasting chylomicronemia (familial chylomicronemia syndrome)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lcat_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_hdl2a> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alcat_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_hdl2a> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lcat_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_hdl2a> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Glomset JA. The plasma lecithin:cholesterol acyltransferase reaction. J Lipid Res. 1968;9(2):155-167; OMIM 245900 (familial LCAT deficiency); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, Disorders of Lipoprotein Metabolism | intervention: LCAT loss-of-function (OMIM 245900): do(LCAT activity down) -> HDL2a down"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LCAT esterifies free cholesterol on HDL surfaces, driving maturation of small pre-beta HDL3 into large alpha-HDL2 particles; LCAT deficiency (OMIM 245900) abolishes HDL maturation, markedly reducing HDL2 subfractions."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lcat_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_hdl2b> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alcat_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_hdl2b> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lcat_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_hdl2b> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Glomset JA. J Lipid Res. 1968;9:155-167; OMIM 245900 (familial LCAT deficiency); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology, HDL cholesterol transport | intervention: LCAT loss-of-function (OMIM 245900): do(LCAT activity down) -> HDL2b down"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LCAT esterification of cholesterol drives HDL particle remodelling from HDL3 (small) to HDL2 (large, cholesterol-ester-rich); LCAT deficiency markedly reduces the HDL2 subclass."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lcat_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_hdl3a> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alcat_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_hdl3a> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lcat_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_hdl3a> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Glomset JA. J Lipid Res. 1968;9:155-167; OMIM 245900; Barter P, Rye KA. HDL subfractions and LCAT reaction. Curr Opin Lipidol. 2006 | intervention: LCAT loss-of-function (OMIM 245900): do(LCAT activity down) -> HDL3 subfractions up (HDL3 accumulates as HDL2 falls)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LCAT converts HDL3 (small, cholesterol-poor, pre-beta HDL) to HDL2 (large, alpha-HDL) by esterifying surface free cholesterol; more LCAT activity depletes the HDL3 precursor pool by driving it to HDL2; LCAT deficiency causes HDL3 accumulation relative to HDL2."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lcat_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_hdl3b> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alcat_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_hdl3b> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lcat_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_hdl3b> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Glomset JA. J Lipid Res. 1968;9:155-167; OMIM 245900 (familial LCAT deficiency); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine | intervention: LCAT loss-of-function (OMIM 245900): do(LCAT activity down) -> HDL3b up"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LCAT converts small HDL3 particles to larger HDL2 via cholesterol esterification; higher LCAT activity reduces HDL3b by maturing it to HDL2; LCAT deficiency causes HDL3b accumulation."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lcat_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_hdl3c> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alcat_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_hdl3c> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lcat_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_hdl3c> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Glomset JA. J Lipid Res. 1968;9:155-167; OMIM 245900; Lamon-Fava S et al. HDL subfraction changes and LCAT. Arterioscler Thromb Vasc Biol. 1992 | intervention: LCAT loss-of-function (OMIM 245900): do(LCAT activity down) -> HDL3c up"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LCAT drives the HDL3->HDL2 maturation cascade; more LCAT activity reduces the smallest HDL3c subfraction by converting it to HDL3b and onward to HDL2; LCAT deficiency causes preferential accumulation of small pre-beta HDL3 particles including HDL3c."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/abca1_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_apolipoprotein_a1> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aabca1_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_apolipoprotein_a1> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/abca1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_apolipoprotein_a1> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Bodzioch M et al. Nat Genet 1999 (Tangier disease = ABCA1 mutations, HDL and apoA-I deficiency); OMIM 205400 (Tangier disease); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, Disorders of Lipoprotein Metabolism | intervention: ABCA1 loss-of-function (Tangier disease, OMIM 205400): do(ABCA1 activity down) -> plasma apoA-I severely reduced"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ABCA1 mediates cellular cholesterol and phospholipid efflux to lipid-poor apoA-I, forming nascent HDL; without ABCA1-mediated lipidation, apoA-I is rapidly catabolized by the kidney and liver; Tangier disease (ABCA1 LoF, OMIM 205400) causes near-total absence of plasma apoA-I."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/abca1_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_apolipoprotein_a2> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aabca1_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_apolipoprotein_a2> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/abca1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_apolipoprotein_a2> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "OMIM 205400 (Tangier disease; reduced apoA-II in severe HDL deficiency); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, Disorders of Lipoprotein Metabolism; Bodzioch M et al. Nat Genet 1999 | intervention: ABCA1 loss-of-function (Tangier disease, OMIM 205400): do(ABCA1 activity down) -> plasma apoA-II reduced"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ABCA1 stabilizes apoA-I on nascent HDL; apoA-II co-localizes on mature HDL alongside apoA-I; ABCA1 deficiency (Tangier disease) reduces HDL particle formation and thus the apoA-II-containing HDL pool. ApoA-II plasma levels are markedly reduced in Tangier disease, though less severely than apoA-I."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mttp_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_apolipoprotein_b> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amttp_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_apolipoprotein_b> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mttp_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_apolipoprotein_b> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Sharp D et al. Nature 1993 (MTTP mutations cause abetalipoproteinemia); OMIM 200100 (abetalipoproteinemia); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, Disorders of Lipoprotein Metabolism | intervention: MTTP loss-of-function (abetalipoproteinemia, OMIM 200100): do(MTTP activity down) -> plasma apoB-100 absent/severely reduced"
    ; pmo:mechanism "MTTP (microsomal triglyceride transfer protein) transfers lipids onto apoB-100 in the ER, enabling VLDL assembly and secretion; without MTTP activity, apoB-100 is degraded co-translationally and VLDL secretion fails; abetalipoproteinemia (MTTP LoF, OMIM 200100) causes near-absent plasma apoB, VLDL, LDL, and chylomicrons."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatocyte_apoe_secretion> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_apolipoprotein_e> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahepatocyte_apoe_secretion%3Apositive%3Aplasma_apolipoprotein_e> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatocyte_apoe_secretion> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_apolipoprotein_e> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Mahley RW, Rall SC. Apolipoprotein E: far more than a lipid transport protein. Annu Rev Genomics Hum Genet. 2000;1:507-537; Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology, lipoprotein transport; OMIM 617347 (APOE-related disorders) | intervention: APOE haploinsufficiency / null: do(apoE secretion down) -> plasma apoE down; do(apoE secretion up, hepatic overexpression) -> plasma apoE up"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ApoE is synthesized and secreted principally by hepatocytes; hepatic apoE secretion rate is the primary determinant of plasma apoE concentration. Reducing APOE gene dose (e.g. APOE null subjects or APOE2/2 homozygotes with type III hyperlipoproteinemia) alters the total secreted apoE and its plasma half-life through reduced receptor binding and clearance of remnant particles."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dhcr24_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_desmosterol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Adhcr24_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_desmosterol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dhcr24_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_desmosterol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Waterham HR et al. Am J Hum Genet. 2001;69:685-694 (desmosterolosis, DHCR24 mutations); OMIM 602398 (desmosterolosis); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, cholesterol biosynthesis (Bloch pathway: desmosterol -> cholesterol via DHCR24) | intervention: DHCR24 loss-of-function (desmosterolosis, OMIM 602398): do(DHCR24 activity down) -> plasma desmosterol up"
    ; pmo:mechanism "DHCR24 (Delta(24)-sterol reductase) reduces the C24=C25 double bond of desmosterol to form cholesterol; loss of DHCR24 activity causes desmosterolosis (OMIM 602398) in which desmosterol accumulates to high levels in plasma and tissues."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dhcr7_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_7_dehydrocholesterol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Adhcr7_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_7_dehydrocholesterol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dhcr7_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_7_dehydrocholesterol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Wassif CA et al. Nat Genet. 1998;18:107-110; Waterham HR et al. (DHCR7 mutations in SLOS); OMIM 270400 (Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, cholesterol biosynthesis (Kandutsch-Russell pathway) | intervention: DHCR7 loss-of-function (Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome, OMIM 270400): do(DHCR7 activity down) -> plasma 7-DHC up"
    ; pmo:mechanism "DHCR7 (7-dehydrocholesterol reductase) reduces the C7=C8 double bond of 7-DHC to form cholesterol; loss of DHCR7 causes Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome (SLOS, OMIM 270400) in which 7-DHC accumulates markedly in plasma and tissues."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sc5d_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_7_dehydrocholesterol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asc5d_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_7_dehydrocholesterol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sc5d_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_7_dehydrocholesterol> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Krakowiak PA et al. Am J Hum Genet. 2003;72:1558-1567 (SC5DL deficiency = lathosterolosis); OMIM 607330 (lathosterolosis); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, cholesterol biosynthesis | intervention: SC5D loss-of-function (lathosterolosis, OMIM 607330): do(SC5D activity down) -> plasma 7-DHC down (secondary, product falls); lathosterol up (substrate accumulates)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "SC5D (lathosterol oxidase/sterol-C5-desaturase) converts lathosterol to 7-dehydrocholesterol by introducing the C5=C6 double bond; more SC5D activity produces more 7-DHC from lathosterol; SC5D loss-of-function (lathosterolosis, OMIM 607330) depletes 7-DHC and causes lathosterol accumulation."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dhcr7_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_8_dehydrocholesterol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Adhcr7_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_8_dehydrocholesterol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dhcr7_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_8_dehydrocholesterol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Tint GS et al. N Engl J Med. 1994;330:107-113 (SLOS: 7-DHC and 8-DHC both elevated); OMIM 270400 (Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome); Kelley RI, Hennekam RC. J Med Genet. 2000 | intervention: DHCR7 loss-of-function (SLOS, OMIM 270400): do(DHCR7 activity down) -> plasma 8-DHC up"
    ; pmo:mechanism "In Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome (DHCR7 LoF), both 7-DHC and 8-DHC accumulate; 8-DHC is an isomer of 7-DHC formed by the EBP isomerase and accumulates when DHCR7 cannot reduce the 7-DHC pool downstream. DHCR7 also reduces 8-DHC directly at lower efficiency."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sc5d_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_lathosterol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asc5d_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_lathosterol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sc5d_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_lathosterol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Krakowiak PA et al. Am J Hum Genet. 2003;72:1558-1567 (SC5DL deficiency, lathosterolosis); OMIM 607330 (lathosterolosis); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, cholesterol biosynthesis (lathosterol is the direct SC5D substrate) | intervention: SC5D loss-of-function (lathosterolosis, OMIM 607330): do(SC5D activity down) -> plasma lathosterol up"
    ; pmo:mechanism "SC5D catalyses the desaturation of lathosterol (5alpha-cholest-7-en-3beta-ol) to 7-dehydrocholesterol; loss of SC5D activity causes lathosterolosis (OMIM 607330) in which lathosterol accumulates to high levels in plasma and tissues."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ebp_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_8_9_cholestenol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aebp_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_8_9_cholestenol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ebp_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_8_9_cholestenol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Braverman N et al. Hum Mol Genet. 1999;8:1773-1780 (EBP mutations cause X-linked CDPX2 with 8(9)-cholestenol and 8-dehydrocholesterol accumulation); OMIM 302960 (chondrodysplasia punctata 2, X-linked dominant); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, post-lanosterol sterol synthesis | intervention: EBP loss-of-function (CDPX2, OMIM 302960): do(EBP activity down) -> plasma 8(9)-cholestenol up"
    ; pmo:mechanism "EBP (emopamil-binding protein; 3-beta-hydroxysteroid-Delta(8),Delta(7)-isomerase) catalyses the isomerization of 8(9)-cholestenol (Delta8-sterol) to lathosterol (Delta7-sterol); EBP loss-of-function (CDPX2 / chondrodysplasia punctata, OMIM 302960) causes accumulation of 8(9)-cholestenol and other Delta8-sterols in plasma."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/msmo1_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_dimethylsterol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amsmo1_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_dimethylsterol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/msmo1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_dimethylsterol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "SC4MOL / MSMO1 deficiency (methylsterolosis), OMIM:616834 — LoF causes accumulation of 4,4-dimethylsterols in plasma"
    ; pmo:mechanism "RHEA:55220 (EC 1.14.18.9): 4,4-dimethyl-5alpha-cholest-7-en-3beta-ol (CHEBI:16455) + 6 Fe(II)-[cytochrome b5] + 3 O2 + 5 H(+) = 4alpha-carboxy-4beta-methyl product + 6 Fe(III)-[cytochrome b5] + 4 H2O; 4,4-dimethylsterols are the substrates of the first C4-demethylation step"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nsdhl_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_monomethylsterol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ansdhl_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_monomethylsterol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nsdhl_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_monomethylsterol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Konig A et al. Am J Hum Genet. 2000;66:1210-1218 (NSDHL mutations in CHILD syndrome; monomethylsterol and C4-sterol accumulation); OMIM 308050 (CHILD syndrome, X-linked); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, demethylation of methylsterols | intervention: NSDHL loss-of-function (CHILD syndrome, OMIM 308050): do(NSDHL activity down) -> plasma monomethylsterol up"
    ; pmo:mechanism "NSDHL (sterol-4-alpha-carboxylate 3-dehydrogenase) catalyses the second step of C4-methylsterol demethylation, acting on 4-monomethylsterol intermediates after the first methyl is removed by MSMO1; NSDHL loss-of-function (CHILD syndrome, OMIM 308050) causes monomethylsterol accumulation."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/msmo1_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_methylsterol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amsmo1_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_methylsterol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/msmo1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_methylsterol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Boyden LM et al. Am J Hum Genet. 2014;94:309-315 (MSMO1 deficiency; methylsterol accumulation); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, sterol demethylation pathway (lanosterol -> zymosterol via successive methylsterol intermediates) | intervention: MSMO1 loss-of-function (OMIM 616834): do(MSMO1 activity down) -> plasma methylsterols up"
    ; pmo:mechanism "MSMO1 initiates C4-methylsterol demethylation (the rate-limiting oxidation of the C4-methyl group); reduced MSMO1 activity causes the entire methylsterol pool (mono- and dimethyl intermediates) to accumulate. The generic 'methylsterol' class includes all C4-methylated sterol intermediates downstream of lanosterol."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/abcg5_abcg8_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_sitosterol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aabcg5_abcg8_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_sitosterol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/abcg5_abcg8_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_sitosterol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Berge KE et al. Science 2000;290:1771-1775 (ABCG5/ABCG8 mutations cause sitosterolemia); OMIM 210250 (sitosterolemia); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, Disorders of Lipoprotein Metabolism | intervention: ABCG5/ABCG8 loss-of-function (sitosterolemia, OMIM 210250): do(ABCG5/ABCG8 activity down) -> plasma sitosterol up"
    ; pmo:mechanism "The ABCG5/ABCG8 heterodimer in intestinal enterocytes and hepatocytes pumps plant sterols (sitosterol, campesterol) back into the intestinal lumen and bile, limiting their absorption and promoting biliary excretion; loss-of-function of ABCG5 or ABCG8 causes sitosterolemia (OMIM 210250) with marked elevation of plasma sitosterol."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sterol_27_hydroxylase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cholestanol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asterol_27_hydroxylase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_cholestanol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sterol_27_hydroxylase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cholestanol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Bjorkhem I, Boberg KM. Inborn errors in bile acid biosynthesis and storage of sterols. Scriver et al. Metabolic Basis of Inherited Disease; OMIM 213700 (cerebrotendinous xanthomatosis, CYP27A1); Harrison's, inborn errors of bile acid synthesis | intervention: CYP27A1 loss-of-function (CTX, OMIM 213700): do(sterol 27-hydroxylase activity down) -> plasma cholestanol up"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CYP27A1 (sterol 27-hydroxylase) drives the acidic bile-acid synthesis pathway; when CYP27A1 is deficient (cerebrotendinous xanthomatosis, CTX, OMIM 213700), cholesterol catabolism is redirected via 5alpha-reductase to form cholestanol (5alpha-dihydrocholesterol) instead of normal bile acids, causing cholestanol accumulation in plasma and tissues."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sterol_27_hydroxylase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_cholestanol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asterol_27_hydroxylase_activity%3Anegative%3Acsf_cholestanol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sterol_27_hydroxylase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_cholestanol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "OMIM 213700 (cerebrotendinous xanthomatosis; elevated CSF cholestanol); Berginer VM et al. Neurology 1984 (CSF cholestanol elevated in CTX); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine | intervention: CYP27A1 loss-of-function (CTX, OMIM 213700): do(sterol 27-hydroxylase activity down) -> CSF cholestanol up"
    ; pmo:mechanism "In cerebrotendinous xanthomatosis (CYP27A1 deficiency), cholestanol accumulates not only in plasma but also in the CSF and CNS (characteristic xanthoma deposits in tendons and brain); the CSF cholestanol elevation is a diagnostic hallmark of CTX and reflects the systemic diversion of cholesterol catabolism toward cholestanol formation."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dhcr7_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cholestane_triol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Adhcr7_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_cholestane_triol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dhcr7_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cholestane_triol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "SLOS (Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome) OMIM 270400 (DHCR7 LoF); C-triol is the canonical plasma biomarker of DHCR7 deficiency; Kelley & Hennekam, J Med Genet 2000; Tint et al., 1994 NEJM (7-DHC elevation in SLOS); Irons et al., Lancet 1993. | intervention: DHCR7 loss-of-function (Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome): DHCR7 LoF -> 7-DHC accumulation -> non-enzymatic auto-oxidation to plasma cholestane-3b,5a,6b-triol elevation"
    ; pmo:mechanism "DHCR7 (7-dehydrocholesterol reductase) reduces 7-dehydrocholesterol (7-DHC) to cholesterol; when DHCR7 is deficient, 7-DHC accumulates and auto-oxidizes non-enzymatically to cholestane-3beta,5alpha,6beta-triol (C-triol). More DHCR7 activity keeps 7-DHC low and thereby reduces C-triol formation."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sterol_27_hydroxylase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_bile_alcohol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asterol_27_hydroxylase_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_bile_alcohol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sterol_27_hydroxylase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_bile_alcohol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "CTX (cerebrotendinous xanthomatosis) OMIM 213700 (CYP27A1 LoF); urinary bile alcohol excess is the diagnostic hallmark of CTX; Leitersdorf et al., J Clin Invest 1993; Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, Disorders of Bile Acid Synthesis. | intervention: CYP27A1 loss-of-function (cerebrotendinous xanthomatosis): CYP27A1 LoF -> failure of acidic bile acid synthesis -> bile alcohol accumulation in urine"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CYP27A1 (sterol 27-hydroxylase) catalyzes the acidic bile-acid synthesis pathway. Loss of CYP27A1 (cerebrotendinous xanthomatosis, CTX) diverts sterol catabolism away from bile acids toward C27 bile alcohols that are excreted in urine. Greater CYP27A1 activity thus reduces urinary bile alcohol accumulation."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mevalonate_kinase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_farnesol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amevalonate_kinase_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_farnesol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mevalonate_kinase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_farnesol> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Mevalonate kinase deficiency (MKD/HIDS) OMIM 610377 (MVK LoF); Houten et al., 1999 Hum Mol Genet (isoprenoid pathway block in MVK deficiency); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, Ch. 26 (isoprenoid/cholesterol biosynthesis: mevalonate -> phosphomevalonate -> ... -> farnesyl-PP -> farnesol/squalene). | intervention: MVK loss-of-function (mevalonate kinase deficiency): MVK LoF -> reduced farnesyl-PP flux -> reduced plasma farnesol"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Mevalonate kinase (MVK) phosphorylates mevalonate, committing flux through the isoprenoid/sterol pathway toward farnesyl-pyrophosphate (farnesyl-PP); farnesol is generated by hydrolysis of farnesyl-PP. Greater MVK activity increases farnesyl-PP production and thus farnesol levels; MVK deficiency (MKD/HIDS) reduces flux through this node."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cyp7a1_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fecal_bile_acids> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acyp7a1_activity%3Apositive%3Afecal_bile_acids> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cyp7a1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fecal_bile_acids> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Bile acid synthesis defect type 1 OMIM 603540 (CYP7A1 LoF leads to reduced bile acid production and hypercholesterolemia); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, Ch. 26 (CYP7A1 rate-limiting for bile acid synthesis); Schwarz et al., J Clin Invest 1998 (CYP7A1 deficiency in humans). | intervention: CYP7A1 loss-of-function (bile acid synthesis defect type 1, OMIM 603540): CYP7A1 LoF -> reduced bile acid synthesis -> reduced fecal bile acid excretion"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CYP7A1 (cholesterol 7alpha-monooxygenase) is the rate-limiting enzyme of the classical (neutral) bile-acid synthesis pathway. More CYP7A1 activity drives greater bile acid synthesis, increases the bile acid pool, and elevates fecal bile acid excretion. CYP7A1 deficiency reduces the bile acid pool and fecal bile acid output."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sts_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cholesterol_sulfate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asts_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_cholesterol_sulfate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sts_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cholesterol_sulfate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "X-linked ichthyosis OMIM 308100 (STS LoF); Bhatt et al., 2012 J Biol Chem; Hernandez-Martin & Torrelo, Am J Clin Dermatol 2006; plasma cholesterol sulfate is the diagnostic biomarker of STS/XLI deficiency. | intervention: STS loss-of-function (X-linked ichthyosis, OMIM 308100): STS LoF -> inability to hydrolyze cholesterol sulfate -> plasma cholesterol sulfate accumulation"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Steryl-sulfatase (STS) hydrolyzes cholesterol sulfate to cholesterol and sulfate. STS deficiency (X-linked ichthyosis, XLI) causes marked accumulation of cholesterol sulfate in plasma, stratum corneum, and erythrocytes. Greater STS activity therefore lowers plasma cholesterol sulfate levels."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gla_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_globotriaosylceramide> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Agla_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_globotriaosylceramide> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gla_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_globotriaosylceramide> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Fabry disease OMIM 301500 (GLA LoF); plasma Gb3 is the cardinal biomarker of Fabry disease; Brady et al., NEJM 1967 (original Fabry enzyme deficiency description); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, Lysosomal Storage Diseases. | intervention: GLA loss-of-function (Fabry disease, OMIM 301500): GLA LoF -> failure to cleave Gb3 -> plasma globotriaosylceramide (Gb3) accumulation"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Alpha-galactosidase A (GLA) cleaves the terminal galactose from globotriaosylceramide (Gb3), degrading it to lactosylceramide. In GLA deficiency (Fabry disease), Gb3 accumulates in plasma, urine, and lysosomes of multiple cell types. More GLA activity keeps plasma Gb3 low."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/smpd1_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_lysosphingomyelin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asmpd1_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_lysosphingomyelin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/smpd1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_lysosphingomyelin> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Niemann-Pick disease type A/B OMIM 257200/607616 (SMPD1 LoF); elevated plasma lysosphingomyelin is used as a biomarker for NPD-A/B; Wasserstein et al., Genet Med 2004; Harrison's, Lysosomal Storage Diseases. | intervention: SMPD1 loss-of-function (Niemann-Pick disease type A/B, OMIM 257200): SMPD1 LoF -> lysosomal sphingomyelin catabolism impaired -> plasma lysosphingomyelin accumulation"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Acid sphingomyelinase (SMPD1) hydrolyzes sphingomyelin to ceramide and phosphocholine in lysosomes. In SMPD1 deficiency (Niemann-Pick disease type A/B), sphingomyelin and its deacylated form lysosphingomyelin (sphingosylphosphorylcholine, SPC) accumulate in plasma and tissues. Greater SMPD1 activity reduces plasma lysosphingomyelin."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arsa_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_sulfatide> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aarsa_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_sulfatide> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arsa_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_sulfatide> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Metachromatic leukodystrophy (MLD) OMIM 250100 (ARSA LoF); urinary sulfatide excretion is the diagnostic biomarker of MLD; Gieselmann, J Neurochem 2008; Harrison's, Lysosomal Storage Diseases. | intervention: ARSA loss-of-function (metachromatic leukodystrophy, OMIM 250100): ARSA LoF -> sulfatide hydrolysis impaired -> urinary sulfatide accumulation"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Arylsulfatase A (ARSA) cleaves the 3-sulfate from galactosylceramide sulfate (sulfatide), degrading it to galactosylceramide. ARSA deficiency (metachromatic leukodystrophy, MLD) causes sulfatide accumulation in neural tissues, kidneys, and excretion in urine. More ARSA activity reduces urinary sulfatide."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/agps_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_plasmalogen> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aagps_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_plasmalogen> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/agps_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_plasmalogen> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Rhizomelic chondrodysplasia punctata type 3 (RCDP3) OMIM 600121 (AGPS LoF); Wanders & Waterham, Annu Rev Biochem 2006 (plasmalogen deficiency in peroxisomal disorders); Motley et al., Nat Genet 1997 (AGPS mutations cause RCDP3). | intervention: AGPS loss-of-function (RCDP type 3, OMIM 600121): AGPS LoF -> ether lipid / plasmalogen synthesis failure -> severe plasma plasmalogen deficiency"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Alkyldihydroxyacetonephosphate synthase (AGPS) catalyzes the first ether-bond-forming step in plasmalogen biosynthesis in peroxisomes. AGPS deficiency (rhizomelic chondrodysplasia punctata type 3, RCDP3) causes severe reduction of plasmalogens in plasma and red blood cells. More AGPS activity supports greater plasmalogen synthesis."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/paf_acetylhydrolase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_lysophosphatidylcholine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apaf_acetylhydrolase_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_lysophosphatidylcholine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/paf_acetylhydrolase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_lysophosphatidylcholine> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Darapladib (PAF-AH/Lp-PLA2 inhibitor) clinical studies: Mohler et al., JACC 2008; Caslake et al., Atherosclerosis 2010 (darapladib reduces plasma lyso-PC); Macphee et al., Arterioscler Thromb Vasc Biol 2005 (Lp-PLA2 generates lyso-PC from oxidized LDL). | intervention: Pharmacological PAF-AH inhibition (darapladib): darapladib LoF -> reduced PAF-AH activity -> reduced plasma lysophosphatidylcholine"
    ; pmo:mechanism "PAF acetylhydrolase (PAF-AH / PLA2G7) hydrolyzes platelet-activating factor (PAF) and oxidized sn-2 short-chain phospholipids to lysophosphatidylcholine (lyso-PC) plus the released fatty-acid/acetate moiety. Greater PAF-AH activity generates more lyso-PC. Darapladib (selective PAF-AH/Lp-PLA2 inhibitor) reduces plasma lyso-PC in clinical studies."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fads1_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_arachidonic_acid> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afads1_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_arachidonic_acid> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fads1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_arachidonic_acid> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Stoffel et al., PNAS 2008 (Fads1 knockout mice: reduced arachidonic acid); Lattka et al., Br J Nutr 2010 (FADS1 GWAS variants control arachidonic acid levels in plasma); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, Ch. 21 (PUFA desaturation pathway). | intervention: FADS1 loss-of-function (reduced delta-5 desaturase activity in FADS1 variant carriers): FADS1 LoF -> reduced conversion of DGLA to arachidonic acid -> reduced plasma arachidonic acid [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "FADS1 (acyl-CoA delta-5 desaturase) converts dihomo-gamma-linolenic acid (DGLA, C20:3n-6) to arachidonic acid (C20:4n-6) by inserting a double bond at the delta-5 position. FADS1 deficiency or reduced activity (genetic FADS1 variants) results in reduced plasma arachidonic acid and accumulation of DGLA."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/elovl2_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_adrenic_acid> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aelovl2_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_adrenic_acid> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/elovl2_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_adrenic_acid> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Jakobsson et al., J Biol Chem 2006 (ELOVL2 preferred substrate C20:4n-6 -> C22:4n-6); ELOVL2 GWAS variants associated with plasma adrenic acid (Genome-wide metabolomics studies, e.g. Kettunen et al., Nat Commun 2016); Lehninger, Ch. 21 (PUFA elongation). | intervention: ELOVL2 loss-of-function: ELOVL2 LoF -> failure to elongate arachidonic acid (C20:4n-6) to adrenic acid (C22:4n-6) -> reduced plasma adrenic acid [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ELOVL2 (very long chain fatty acid elongase 2) elongates arachidonic acid (C20:4n-6) to adrenic acid (docosatetraenoic acid, C22:4n-6) by adding two carbons. ELOVL2 is the principal elongase acting on C20 polyunsaturated fatty acids. ELOVL2 LoF reduces plasma adrenic acid (and other C22 PUFA) with accumulation of the C20 substrates."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/scd_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_myristoleate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ascd_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_myristoleate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/scd_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_myristoleate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Miyazaki et al., J Biol Chem 2001 (SCD1 knockout mice: reduced monounsaturated fatty acids); Ntambi et al., PNAS 2002 (SCD1 deficiency phenotype); Lehninger, Ch. 21 (delta-9 desaturation of myristate to myristoleate by SCD). | intervention: SCD loss-of-function: SCD LoF -> failure of delta-9 desaturation of myristate -> reduced plasma myristoleate [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Stearoyl-CoA desaturase (SCD/SCD1) inserts a cis-double bond at the delta-9 position of saturated fatty acyl-CoAs. It acts on myristoyl-CoA (C14:0) to produce myristoleoyl-CoA (C14:1n-5), and on palmitoyl-CoA (C16:0) to produce palmitoleoyl-CoA (C16:1n-7) and on stearoyl-CoA to produce oleoyl-CoA (C18:1n-9). SCD deficiency reduces plasma monounsaturated fatty acids including myristoleate."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/elovl6_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_octadecanoate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aelovl6_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_octadecanoate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/elovl6_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_octadecanoate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Ohno et al., J Biol Chem 2010 (ELOVL6 extends C16 to C18, ELOVL6 LoF mice show reduced stearate); Matsuzaka et al., Nat Med 2007 (ELOVL6 knockout prevents insulin resistance with altered C16/C18 ratio); Lehninger, Ch. 21 (C16->C18 elongation). | intervention: ELOVL6 loss-of-function: ELOVL6 LoF -> failure to elongate palmitate (C16:0) to stearate (C18:0) -> reduced plasma octadecanoate (stearate) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ELOVL6 (very long chain fatty acid elongase 6) elongates palmitoyl-CoA (C16:0) to stearoyl-CoA (C18:0) by adding two carbons in the endoplasmic reticulum. ELOVL6 is the principal elongase for C16 saturated and monounsaturated fatty acids. ELOVL6 deficiency reduces stearate (C18:0) and increases palmitate (C16:0)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/scd_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_oleate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ascd_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_oleate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/scd_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_oleate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Miyazaki et al., J Biol Chem 2001 (SCD1 knockout mice: strongly reduced oleate); Ntambi et al., PNAS 2002 (SCD1 whole-body role in oleate synthesis); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, Ch. 21 (delta-9 desaturation: stearate -> oleate). | intervention: SCD loss-of-function: SCD LoF -> failure to desaturate stearate -> reduced plasma oleate (C18:1n-9) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Stearoyl-CoA desaturase (SCD/SCD1) converts stearoyl-CoA (C18:0) to oleoyl-CoA (C18:1n-9) by inserting a cis-delta-9 double bond. Oleate is the primary SCD product in human liver. SCD1 deficiency markedly reduces plasma and tissue oleate."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fasn_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_palmitate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afasn_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_palmitate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fasn_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_palmitate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Kuhajda et al., PNAS 1994 (FASN role in de novo lipogenesis); Ventura et al., JACS 2015 (TVB-2640 FASN inhibitor reduces de novo synthesized palmitate); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, Ch. 21 (FASN reaction: acetyl-CoA + 7 malonyl-CoA -> palmitoyl-CoA); Wakil, Biochemistry 1989 (FASN mechanism). | intervention: FASN inhibition (pharmacological: TVB-2640) or hepatic FASN knockout: FASN LoF -> failure of de novo lipogenesis -> reduced plasma palmitate (C16:0)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Fatty acid synthase (FASN) synthesizes palmitate (C16:0) as its primary 16-carbon product from acetyl-CoA and malonyl-CoA via de novo lipogenesis. FASN is the sole enzyme of de novo fatty acid synthesis in humans; FASN inhibition (TVB-2640, orlistat-related) substantially reduces plasma palmitate derived from de novo lipogenesis."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lipoprotein_lipase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_lipoprotein_lipase> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alipoprotein_lipase_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_lipoprotein_lipase> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lipoprotein_lipase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_lipoprotein_lipase> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "LPL deficiency OMIM 238600 (LPL LoF -> absent plasma post-heparin LPL mass); Brunzell, NEJM 2007 (LPL biology and plasma mass); Young & Zechner, Nat Rev Mol Cell Biol 2013 (GPIHBP1-LPL complex and plasma LPL); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology, Lipoprotein metabolism. | intervention: LPL loss-of-function (familial LPL deficiency, OMIM 238600): LPL LoF -> absent plasma LPL mass (activity and mass both lost)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LPL is synthesized primarily in parenchymal cells (muscle, adipose, heart) and transported to the luminal surface of capillary endothelium via GPIHBP1. It is constitutively shed into plasma. Genetic variants that increase LPL activity (e.g., loss of ANGPTL3/4 inhibitors, or LPL Asn291Ser gain-of-function) raise LPL mass in post-heparin plasma. LPL deficiency (OMIM 238600) eliminates both catalytic activity and plasma LPL mass."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_haptoglobin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ail6%3Apositive%3Aplasma_haptoglobin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_haptoglobin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Burtis & Ashwood Tietz Textbook of Clinical Chemistry & Molecular Diagnostics (haptoglobin as a positive acute-phase reactant induced by IL-6); Nielsen & Moestrup 2009 (haptoglobin-CD163-HO-1 pathway regulated by IL-6, PMC3678498); Acute-phase protein synthesis in human hepatoma cells: SAA and haptoglobin regulated by IL-1 and IL-6, PMID 1706240. | intervention: IL-6 pharmacological elevation (e.g. IL-6 infusion or tocilizumab withdrawal) — directly upregulates hepatic haptoglobin synthesis 2–4-fold. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "IL-6 is the principal hepatic inducer of positive acute-phase proteins; it signals via gp130/JAK-STAT3 in hepatocytes to upregulate haptoglobin gene transcription, raising plasma haptoglobin 2–4-fold during the acute-phase response."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_prealbumin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ail6%3Anegative%3Aplasma_prealbumin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_prealbumin> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Burtis & Ashwood Tietz Textbook of Clinical Chemistry & Molecular Diagnostics (TTR/prealbumin as a negative acute-phase protein); Bienvenu & Jeppsson 1994 PMID 7946531 (transthyretin in health and disease: nutritional implications; suppressed by IL-6/acute-phase response); Patel et al. 2022 PMC9361973 (plasma transthyretin as nutritional biomarker: cytokine-induced abrogation of TTR synthesis during inflammatory disorders). | intervention: IL-6 pharmacological elevation during systemic inflammation — suppresses hepatic TTR/prealbumin synthesis; plasma prealbumin falls as a negative acute-phase reactant. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Transthyretin (prealbumin) is a negative acute-phase protein whose hepatic synthesis is suppressed by IL-6 via STAT3-mediated downregulation of C/EBP-alpha and loss of interaction at the TTR promoter; during the acute-phase response plasma transthyretin falls as the liver redirects synthesis toward positive acute-phase proteins."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_thyroxine_binding_globulin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aestradiol%3Apositive%3Aplasma_thyroxine_binding_globulin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_thyroxine_binding_globulin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Estrogen increases TBG by mechanisms already mentioned.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 11, p. 354"
    ; pmo:mechanism "estrogen increases hepatic TBG sialylation and plasma half-life, raising TBG concentration"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cbg> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aestradiol%3Apositive%3Aplasma_cbg> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cbg> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Levels are increased by estrogens and in some patients with chronic active hepatitis... The estrogen effect can be marked, with levels increasing twofold to threefold across pregnancy\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 15, p. 493"
    ; pmo:mechanism "estrogens increase CBG concentrations, with levels increasing two- to threefold across pregnancy"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_insulin> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_shbg> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_insulin%3Anegative%3Aplasma_shbg> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_insulin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_shbg> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"SHBG production by the liver is increased under the influence of estrogens and thyroxine and decreased by androgens and insulin.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p 1154"
    ; pmo:mechanism "insulin suppresses hepatic SHBG synthesis; hyperinsulinemia (as in obesity/metabolic syndrome) reduces circulating SHBG"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_igfals> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apituitary_gh%3Apositive%3Aplasma_igfals> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_igfals> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Both IGFBP3 and ALS are GH dependent\" and \"Serum levels of IGFBP3 and ALS are reduced in patients with GHD or GH insensitivity\". Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 25, p 959"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GH drives ALS (acid-labile subunit) production in liver; GH deficiency or GH insensitivity reduces circulating ALS levels"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_procalcitonin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ail6%3Apositive%3Aplasma_procalcitonin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_procalcitonin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Burtis & Ashwood Tietz Textbook (procalcitonin elevated in bacterial sepsis); Muller et al. 2000 (procalcitonin and IL-6 in sepsis, PMC1550885); NBK539794 StatPearls (CALC-1 gene induction by LPS and pro-inflammatory cytokines including IL-6 in extrahepatic tissues during bacterial infection); Springer Nature 2012 (immune regulation of procalcitonin, doi:10.1007/s00011-012-0439-5 — LPS and cytokines including IL-6 induce PCT). | intervention: LPS/endotoxin infusion (human endotoxaemia model) — pharmacological bacterial LPS raises plasma PCT via cytokine cascade including IL-6."
    ; pmo:mechanism "IL-6 (together with bacterial LPS and TNF-alpha) induces extrahepatic procalcitonin synthesis in multiple tissues (liver, lung, kidney, adipose, neuroendocrine cells) during bacterial sepsis; plasma PCT rises dramatically (100–1000-fold) in response to systemic infection with IL-6 as a key mediating cytokine."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il1beta> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intestinal_mucosal_permeability> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ail1beta%3Apositive%3Aintestinal_mucosal_permeability> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il1beta> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intestinal_mucosal_permeability> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Janeway's Immunobiology (IL-1beta increases epithelial permeability); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (intestinal barrier function; inflammatory cytokines disrupt tight junctions); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (protein-losing enteropathy in IBD; cytokine-driven mucosal barrier disruption). | intervention: IL-1beta pharmacological elevation (recombinant IL-1beta) — disrupts intestinal epithelial tight junctions, raising mucosal permeability."
    ; pmo:mechanism "IL-1beta acts on intestinal epithelial cells to disrupt tight junctions (claudins, occludin), increasing intestinal mucosal permeability to macromolecules including plasma proteins; this is a key mechanism in inflammatory bowel disease and protein-losing enteropathy."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intestinal_mucosal_permeability> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fecal_alpha1_antitrypsin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aintestinal_mucosal_permeability%3Apositive%3Afecal_alpha1_antitrypsin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intestinal_mucosal_permeability> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fecal_alpha1_antitrypsin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "Crossley & Elliott 1977 (Diagnosis of protein-losing enteropathy by gastrointestinal clearance of alpha1-antitrypsin, PMID 80688); Florent et al. 1981 (intestinal clearance of alpha-1-antitrypsin as a sensitive method for protein-losing enteropathy, PMID 6973500); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (fecal AAT clearance as the diagnostic gold standard for protein-losing enteropathy). | intervention: do(inflammatory bowel disease / increased intestinal permeability) — protein-losing enteropathy: plasma protein including AAT crosses the inflamed mucosa into the intestinal lumen, raising fecal AAT. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "When intestinal mucosal permeability increases (protein-losing enteropathy, IBD), plasma proteins including alpha-1-antitrypsin leak into the intestinal lumen; AAT is resistant to luminal proteolysis and accumulates in stool, raising fecal AAT concentration. Fecal AAT clearance is the clinical test for protein-losing enteropathy."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il1beta> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_fetuin_a> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ail1beta%3Anegative%3Aplasma_fetuin_a> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il1beta> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_fetuin_a> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Daveau et al. 2003 (inflammation-induced downregulation of plasma fetuin-A in liver results from the loss of interaction between long C/EBP isoforms, PMC219469 / doi:10.1093/nar/gkg809 — IL-1beta-induced suppression of hepatic AHSG mRNA via C/EBP mechanism); Burtis & Ashwood Tietz Textbook (fetuin-A as a negative acute-phase protein whose plasma level falls during acute inflammation). | intervention: IL-1beta pharmacological elevation (recombinant IL-1beta) or systemic bacterial LPS — suppresses hepatic fetuin-A synthesis, lowering plasma fetuin-A."
    ; pmo:mechanism "IL-1beta downregulates hepatic AHSG (fetuin-A) gene expression via loss of C/EBP long-isoform interaction at the AHSG promoter during the acute-phase response; fetuin-A is a negative acute-phase protein whose plasma concentration falls during systemic inflammation."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tnf_alpha> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_e_selectin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atnf_alpha%3Apositive%3Aplasma_e_selectin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tnf_alpha> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_e_selectin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Kluger et al. 1997 (TNF-alpha and IL-1 upregulate membrane-bound and soluble E-selectin through a common pathway, PMID 9441802); Wanstall et al. 1998 (E-selectin expression in human endothelial cells by TNF-alpha-induced oxidant generation and NF-kB activation, PMID 9728048); Janeway's Immunobiology (TNF-alpha activates endothelium, inducing adhesion molecules including E-selectin); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (endothelial activation by cytokines). | intervention: TNF-alpha infusion (pharmacological human endotoxaemia / recombinant TNF-alpha) — induces endothelial E-selectin expression and raises plasma soluble E-selectin. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "TNF-alpha binds TNFR1 on vascular endothelial cells, activating NF-kB and ROS/JNK signalling; this drives E-selectin (SELE) gene transcription. Membrane-bound E-selectin is expressed within 2 hours; proteolytic shedding releases soluble E-selectin into plasma, raising plasma E-selectin concentration."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il1beta> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calprotectin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ail1beta%3Apositive%3Aplasma_calprotectin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il1beta> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calprotectin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Burtis & Ashwood Tietz Textbook (calprotectin as a marker of neutrophil-mediated inflammation); Fagerhol 2000 (calprotectin = MRP8/14 released from activated/dying neutrophils, PMC6370282); Janeway's Immunobiology (IL-1beta drives neutrophil activation and degranulation during acute inflammation). | intervention: IL-1beta pharmacological elevation or LPS infusion — activates neutrophils, releasing calprotectin into plasma."
    ; pmo:mechanism "IL-1beta drives systemic neutrophil and monocyte activation and recruitment; activated/lysed neutrophils release calprotectin (S100A8/A9 heterodimer) from their abundant cytosolic stores (calprotectin is ~60% of neutrophil cytosolic protein). Plasma calprotectin rises with the degree of systemic neutrophil activation."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il1beta> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fecal_calprotectin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ail1beta%3Apositive%3Afecal_calprotectin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il1beta> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fecal_calprotectin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Burtis & Ashwood Tietz Textbook (fecal calprotectin as a marker of intestinal neutrophil infiltration); Poullis et al. 2002 PMC6370282 (elevated fecal calprotectin is proportional to intestinal mucosal neutrophil number, validated in IBD); Janeway's Immunobiology (IL-1beta recruits and activates neutrophils at sites of intestinal inflammation). | intervention: IL-1beta elevation in IBD / intestinal inflammation — recruits intestinal neutrophils which release calprotectin into the gut lumen, raising fecal calprotectin."
    ; pmo:mechanism "IL-1beta drives intestinal mucosal inflammation and neutrophil recruitment into the gut wall and lumen; recruited neutrophils release calprotectin (S100A8/A9) upon activation or death in the intestinal mucosa. Fecal calprotectin is proportional to the number of neutrophils in the gastrointestinal mucosa."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_chi3l1> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ail6%3Apositive%3Aplasma_chi3l1> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_chi3l1> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Burtis & Ashwood Tietz Textbook (YKL-40 as a marker of inflammation and fibrosis); Rathcke & Vestergaard 2009 (serum YKL-40 in liver fibrosis NAFLD, PMC5064386); Aigner et al. 2021 (myeloid-specific CHI3L1 deletion: macrophage CHI3L1 is the main source in NASH liver, PMC10300183); Yang et al. 2023 (decoding CHI3L1/IL-13Ralpha2 signaling in MASH-fibrosis: IL-6/STAT3 pathway upregulates CHI3L1, doi:10.1126/sciadv.adz3223). | intervention: IL-6 pharmacological elevation (recombinant IL-6 or tocilizumab withdrawal) — activates macrophage STAT3/CHI3L1 transcription, raising plasma CHI3L1/YKL-40."
    ; pmo:mechanism "IL-6 activates macrophages and stimulates CHI3L1 (YKL-40) gene transcription via STAT3 signalling; macrophage-derived CHI3L1 is the main source of circulating YKL-40. IL-6-driven hepatic stellate cell activation in liver fibrosis also upregulates CHI3L1 secretion."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lsec_ha_clearance_rate> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_hyaluronic_acid> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alsec_ha_clearance_rate%3Anegative%3Aplasma_hyaluronic_acid> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lsec_ha_clearance_rate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_hyaluronic_acid> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "Burtis & Ashwood Tietz Textbook (serum HA as a marker of liver fibrosis; LSECs as the principal site of HA clearance); Harris & Weigel 2008 (stabilin-2/HARE is the major HA receptor responsible for systemic HA clearance in liver sinusoidal endothelial cells); Grigorescu et al. 2006 (hyaluronic acid concentration in liver diseases, PMC5063891); Shen et al. 2023 (hyaluronin in liver fibrosis: LSEC capillarisation and stabilin-2 downregulation reduce HA uptake, PMC10027054). | intervention: do(LSEC dysfunction/loss) in CCl4 liver injury model — LSEC capillarisation and stabilin-2 downregulation reduces HA endocytosis, raising plasma HA."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Liver sinusoidal endothelial cells (LSECs) express stabilin-2 (STAB2/FEEL-2/HARE) that mediates high-affinity receptor-mediated endocytosis and catabolism of circulating hyaluronic acid; LSEC-mediated clearance is the principal systemic HA disposal mechanism. When LSEC function decreases (liver fibrosis, cirrhosis — capillarisation and stabilin-2 downregulation), HA clearance falls and plasma HA rises proportionally to fibrotic burden."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_beta2_microglobulin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Agfr%3Anegative%3Aplasma_beta2_microglobulin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_beta2_microglobulin> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "Burtis & Ashwood Tietz Textbook (B2M as a marker of GFR; plasma B2M rises with declining renal function); Kabanda et al. 1994 (renal tubular absorption of beta-2-microglobulin, sieving coefficient and Tm, PMID 6389954); Klein et al. 2020 (serum B2M as a predictor of residual kidney function in peritoneal dialysis, PMC8036192). | intervention: Renal ablation / bilateral nephrectomy (do(GFR down to zero)) — complete cessation of glomerular filtration abolishes renal B2M clearance, plasma B2M rises dramatically (dialysis patients accumulate B2M amyloid). [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Beta-2-microglobulin is produced at a nearly constant rate by all nucleated cells and is freely filtered at the glomerulus (sieving coefficient ~0.97); the filtered B2M is almost completely reabsorbed and catabolised by proximal tubular cells. When GFR falls (acute or chronic kidney disease), glomerular filtration and hence tubular B2M catabolism decrease, causing plasma B2M to accumulate."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_pancreatic_secretory_trypsin_inhibitor> .
