    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Hereditary pancreatitis (OMIM 167800): PRSS1 R122H and N29I GoF mutations cause elevated plasma trypsinogen and pancreatitis (PRSS1-Related Hereditary Pancreatitis, GeneReviews NBK84399; Human cationic trypsinogen PRSS1 variants and chronic pancreatitis PMC3949028; Trypsin activity governs increased susceptibility to pancreatitis in mice expressing PRSS1-R122H PMC6934224); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (pancreatic zymogen secretion and activation) | intervention: do(PRSS1 GoF) — PRSS1 R122H or N29I gain-of-function variant → hereditary pancreatitis → elevated plasma trypsinogen; also triplication of PRSS1 locus (gene-dosage GoF, Nature Genetics 2006)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Gain-of-function mutations in PRSS1 (encoding cationic trypsinogen, the predominant ~60% of pancreatic trypsinogen) accelerate trypsinogen autoactivation and/or inhibit chymotrypsin C-dependent degradation; intrapancreatic trypsin activation triggers pancreatitis with release of trypsinogen/trypsin into the bloodstream, elevating plasma trypsinogen concentration"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_prostate_specific_antigen> .
