<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abeta_glucuronidase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_heparan_sulfate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta_glucuronidase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_heparan_sulfate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 7 (GAG catabolism; beta-glucuronidase role); Sly syndrome / MPS VII OMIM 253220; Neufeld & Muenzer in Scriver's Metabolic Bases: GUSB deficiency causes storage of HS, DS, and CS; urinary HS is a sensitive marker; all three GAGs accumulate in MPS VII | intervention: do(GUSB activity ↓) = Sly syndrome / MPS VII (OMIM 253220): urinary heparan sulfate, dermatan sulfate, and chondroitin sulfate elevated"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GUSB (beta-glucuronidase) cleaves terminal glucuronate from heparan sulfate, dermatan sulfate, and chondroitin sulfate. Heparan sulfate is a primary substrate and accumulates when GUSB is deficient — Sly syndrome (MPS VII)"
    .
