    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 7 (heparan sulfate catabolism); Sanfilippo B / MPS IIIB OMIM 252920; Neufeld & Muenzer in Scriver's Metabolic Bases: NAGLU deficiency is the second step of HS catabolism to fail; heparan sulfate storage in brain; urinary HS elevated | intervention: do(NAGLU activity ↓) = Sanfilippo B / MPS IIIB (OMIM 252920): heparan sulfate accumulates in lysosomes; urinary HS elevated; progressive neurodegeneration"
    ; pmo:mechanism "NAGLU (alpha-N-acetylglucosaminidase) cleaves alpha-N-acetylglucosamine from the non-reducing end of heparan sulfate. Heparan sulfate accumulates when NAGLU is deficient — Sanfilippo syndrome type B (MPS IIIB)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/heparan_alpha_glucosaminide_n_acetyltransferase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_heparan_sulfate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aheparan_alpha_glucosaminide_n_acetyltransferase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_heparan_sulfate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/heparan_alpha_glucosaminide_n_acetyltransferase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_heparan_sulfate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 7 (heparan sulfate catabolism; HGSNAT acetyltransferase step); Sanfilippo C / MPS IIIC OMIM 252930; Neufeld & Muenzer in Scriver's Metabolic Bases: HGSNAT is a membrane-bound lysosomal acetyltransferase; deficiency causes HS storage in CNS; urinary HS elevated | intervention: do(HGSNAT activity ↓) = Sanfilippo C / MPS IIIC (OMIM 252930): heparan sulfate accumulates in lysosomes; urinary HS elevated; progressive neurodegeneration"
    ; pmo:mechanism "HGSNAT (heparan-alpha-glucosaminide N-acetyltransferase) acetylates the free amino group of terminal alpha-glucosamine in heparan sulfate (a prerequisite for its removal by alpha-N-acetylglucosaminidase). HGSNAT deficiency blocks heparan sulfate degradation — Sanfilippo syndrome type C (MPS IIIC). Heparan sulfate accumulates."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alpha_mannosidase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_mannose_oligosaccharides> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aalpha_mannosidase_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_mannose_oligosaccharides> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alpha_mannosidase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_mannose_oligosaccharides> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 7 (N-glycan catabolism; lysosomal mannosidases); Alpha-mannosidosis OMIM 248500; Malm & Nilssen (2008) Orphanet J Rare Dis 3:21 (PMID 18651971): MAN2B1 deficiency causes mannose-rich oligosaccharide storage; urinary oligosaccharides show characteristic mannose-rich peaks on TLC/MS; diagnostic biomarker | intervention: do(MAN2B1 activity ↓) = Alpha-mannosidosis (OMIM 248500): mannose-rich oligosaccharides accumulate in tissues and are excreted in urine [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "MAN2B1 (lysosomal alpha-mannosidase) cleaves alpha-mannose residues from the outer branches of N-linked oligosaccharide chains of glycoproteins during lysosomal glycoprotein catabolism. When MAN2B1 is deficient (alpha-mannosidosis), mannose-rich oligosaccharides accumulate in lysosomes and are excreted in urine. Enzyme activity inversely predicts urinary mannose oligosaccharide excretion."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta_mannosidase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_mannose_oligosaccharides> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abeta_mannosidase_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_mannose_oligosaccharides> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta_mannosidase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_mannose_oligosaccharides> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 7 (N-glycan catabolism; beta-mannosidase as the terminal step); Beta-mannosidosis OMIM 248510; Scriver's Online Metabolic Bases 'Beta-Mannosidosis' (Hopwood): MANBA deficiency causes accumulation of Man-β1,4-GlcNAc disaccharide and larger oligosaccharides in urine | intervention: do(MANBA activity ↓) = Beta-mannosidosis (OMIM 248510): mannose-containing disaccharide and oligosaccharides accumulate; excreted in urine"
    ; pmo:mechanism "MANBA (beta-mannosidase) cleaves the terminal beta-mannose from the N-glycan chitobiose core (Man-β1,4-GlcNAc) during lysosomal glycoprotein catabolism. Deficiency (beta-mannosidosis) blocks the final step of N-glycan degradation, causing mannooligosaccharide accumulation and excretion in urine."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/acid_alpha_glucosidase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/muscle_glycogen_content> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aacid_alpha_glucosidase_activity%3Anegative%3Amuscle_glycogen_content> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/acid_alpha_glucosidase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/muscle_glycogen_content> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Pompe disease (GSD II, OMIM:232300); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, glycogen metabolism; Hirschhorn & Reuser in OMMBID 'Glycogen Storage Disease Type II' (GAA deficiency -> lysosomal glycogen accumulation in muscle); PMC4103853 (pharmacological chaperone AT2220 + ERT reduce glycogen in Pompe mice, confirming GAA activity -> muscle glycogen sign). | intervention: GBA2 / GAA loss-of-function (Pompe disease): do(acid_alpha_glucosidase_activity = down) -> muscle glycogen accumulates (up); ERT (alglucosidase alfa): do(GAA activity = up) -> muscle glycogen falls (down)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Lysosomal acid alpha-glucosidase (GAA) cleaves alpha-1,4- and alpha-1,6-glucosidic bonds in glycogen within the lysosome of muscle cells; loss of GAA activity (Pompe disease / GSD II) prevents lysosomal glycogen degradation, causing glycogen accumulation in skeletal muscle and heart. Enzyme replacement therapy with alglucosidase alfa (recombinant GAA) reduces muscle glycogen content, confirming the direct interventional relationship."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ppt1_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lysosomal_ceroid_lipofuscin_load> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Appt1_activity%3Anegative%3Alysosomal_ceroid_lipofuscin_load> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ppt1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lysosomal_ceroid_lipofuscin_load> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "CLN1 disease / infantile NCL (OMIM:256730); Vesa et al. 1995 Nature Genet (CLN1 = PPT1); Kyttala et al. 2004 BMC Mol Cell Biol PMID:17565660 (PPT1 enzymatic function in lysosomes, palmitoylated protein substrate); PNAS 251485198 (PPT1-knockout mice develop NCL with ceroid lipofuscin accumulation); Cross-species ERT (PMC9566914) reverses storage, confirming causal direction. | intervention: PPT1 loss-of-function (CLN1/infantile NCL): do(ppt1_activity = down) -> lysosomal palmitoylated protein accumulation / ceroid lipofuscin load up. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Palmitoyl-protein thioesterase 1 (PPT1) catalyzes removal of long-chain fatty acyl (palmitoyl) groups from S-palmitoylated lysosomal proteins, enabling their degradation. Loss of PPT1 activity (CLN1 disease) causes accumulation of palmitoylated protein substrates and their fluorescent degradation products (ceroid lipofuscin) in lysosomes of neurons and other cells, the pathological hallmark of neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tpp1_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lysosomal_ceroid_lipofuscin_load> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atpp1_activity%3Anegative%3Alysosomal_ceroid_lipofuscin_load> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tpp1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lysosomal_ceroid_lipofuscin_load> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "CLN2 disease / late-infantile NCL (OMIM:204500); Sleat et al. 1997 Science 277:1802 (TPP1 identified as CLN2 gene); Pal et al. 2020 PMC6851559 (mutation update, CLN2 disease characterized by TPP1 deficiency and ceroid lipofuscin); Intrathecal cerliponase alfa (recombinant TPP1) slows NCL2 progression (clinical trial NCT02485899), confirming causal relationship. | intervention: TPP1 loss-of-function (CLN2/late-infantile NCL): do(tpp1_activity = down) -> lysosomal ceroid lipofuscin storage material accumulates."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Tripeptidyl peptidase 1 (TPP1) is a lysosomal serine protease that cleaves N-terminal tripeptides from polypeptide substrates during lysosomal proteolysis. Loss of TPP1 activity (CLN2 disease) causes accumulation of ceroid lipofuscin storage material in neuronal lysosomes, the defining pathological feature of late-infantile NCL. The specific natural substrates are not fully characterized, but the storage material accumulates as ceroid/lipofuscin granules."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glucocerebrosidase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tissue_glucosylceramide_content> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aglucocerebrosidase_activity%3Anegative%3Atissue_glucosylceramide_content> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glucocerebrosidase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tissue_glucosylceramide_content> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Gaucher disease (OMIM:230800); Beutler & Grabowski in OMMBID 'Gaucher Disease' (GBA deficiency -> glucosylceramide storage in macrophages); Brady et al. 1965 Biochem J (original GBA deficiency characterization); ERT with imiglucerase (recombinant GBA) dramatically reduces tissue glucosylceramide stores in Gaucher patients (Nature GIM 2009 doi:10.1038/gim200912). | intervention: GBA loss-of-function (Gaucher disease): do(glucocerebrosidase_activity = down) -> tissue glucosylceramide accumulates; ERT with imiglucerase: do(glucocerebrosidase_activity = up) -> tissue glucosylceramide falls."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Lysosomal acid glucocerebrosidase (GBA) hydrolyzes glucosylceramide (glucocerebroside) to ceramide and glucose within macrophage lysosomes. Loss of GBA activity (Gaucher disease) prevents catabolism of phagocytosed cell-membrane glucosylceramide, causing massive glucosylceramide accumulation in tissue macrophages of spleen, liver, and bone marrow."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tissue_glucosylceramide_content> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/chitotriosidase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atissue_glucosylceramide_content%3Apositive%3Achitotriosidase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tissue_glucosylceramide_content> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/chitotriosidase_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Hollak et al. 1994 J Clin Invest (first demonstration that Gaucher macrophages secrete massive chitotriosidase); Aerts et al. (review, ScienceDirect Chitotriosidase overview): 'macrophages loaded with phagocytosed glycosphingolipid secrete chitotriosidase in proportion to overload'; PMC10046580 (20-year longitudinal study: imiglucerase ERT reduces chitotriosidase 20-fold, confirming causal direction); PMC4094900 (pathophysiological model of chitotriosidase as Gaucher biomarker reflecting substrate burden). | intervention: Imiglucerase ERT in Gaucher disease: do(glucocerebrosidase_activity restored) -> tissue glucosylceramide falls -> chitotriosidase falls (pharmacological, validated in treated patients)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Macrophages phagocytose and lysosomal glucosylceramide accumulates when GBA is deficient. Glucosylceramide-loaded macrophages become activated and upregulate production and secretion of chitotriosidase (CHIT1) in proportion to the degree of substrate overload. ERT with imiglucerase reduces tissue glucosylceramide burden, causing a parallel (up to 20-fold) reduction in plasma/leukocyte chitotriosidase activity, confirming the causal relationship between substrate load and chitotriosidase output."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cathepsin_d_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lysosomal_ceroid_lipofuscin_load> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acathepsin_d_activity%3Anegative%3Alysosomal_ceroid_lipofuscin_load> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cathepsin_d_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lysosomal_ceroid_lipofuscin_load> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "CLN10 disease / congenital NCL (OMIM:610127); Siintola et al. 2006 Ann Neurol (CTSD mutations cause congenital NCL); PMC7158922 (ERT with recombinant pro-CTSD corrects defective proteolysis and reduces NCL storage material, confirming causal direction); PMC9270453 (CTSD deficiency causes saposin and proteinopathy-related protein accumulation in CNS); MedlinePlus CTSD: 'CTSD gene mutations lead to complete absence of cathepsin D, causing protein/fat accumulation in lysosomes'. | intervention: CTSD loss-of-function (CLN10/congenital NCL): do(cathepsin_d_activity = down) -> lysosomal saposin C/D and ceroid lipofuscin accumulate (up)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Cathepsin D (CTSD) is the principal lysosomal aspartic protease responsible for degrading saposin C, saposin D, and other lysosomal substrates during autophagy and endocytosis. Loss of CTSD activity (CLN10 disease) causes lysosomal accumulation of saposins C and D, ATP synthase subunit c, and other undegraded proteins forming ceroid lipofuscin storage material — the pathological hallmark of congenital NCL."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cyp17a1_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_dhea> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acyp17a1_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_dhea> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cyp17a1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_dhea> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "CYP17A1 deficiency (congenital adrenal hyperplasia, 17-alpha-hydroxylase/lyase deficiency; OMIM 202110): loss of CYP17A1 lyase activity causes DHEA to fall; gain restores/elevates it. Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, Ch. 15 (adrenal steroidogenesis: pregnenolone -> 17-OH-pregnenolone -> DHEA via CYP17A1 hydroxylase+lyase). | intervention: CYP17A1 loss-of-function (OMIM 202110): DHEA falls; gain of CYP17A1 lyase activity raises DHEA"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CYP17A1 lyase activity cleaves the side chain of 17-hydroxypregnenolone to produce DHEA in adrenal zona reticularis; greater enzyme activity yields more DHEA product"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cyp17a1_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_17_hydroxypregnenolone> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acyp17a1_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_17_hydroxypregnenolone> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cyp17a1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_17_hydroxypregnenolone> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "CYP17A1 deficiency (17-alpha-hydroxylase/lyase deficiency; OMIM 202110): substrate 17-hydroxypregnenolone accumulates when lyase is lost. Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, Ch. 15 (steroidogenesis substrate-consumption sign convention). | intervention: CYP17A1 loss-of-function (OMIM 202110): 17-hydroxypregnenolone accumulates (enzyme cannot convert it to DHEA)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CYP17A1 lyase converts 17-hydroxypregnenolone to DHEA, consuming the substrate; higher CYP17A1 lyase activity lowers 17-hydroxypregnenolone; CYP17A1 deficiency causes 17-OH-pregnenolone to accumulate"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sult2a1_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_dheas> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asult2a1_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_dheas> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sult2a1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_dheas> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"a mutation in PAPSS2, an enzyme that generates the sulfate donor 3′-phosphoadenosine-5′-phosphosulfate (PAPS), which is required for conversion of DHEA to DHEAS by the enzyme SULT2A1. The DHEA level was not elevated for age, but androstenedione was high owing to inadequate formation of DHEAS\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 26, p 1162–1163"
    ; pmo:mechanism "SULT2A1 sulfoconjugates DHEA to DHEAS; LoF of PAPSS2 (the PAPS-generating enzyme that supplies sulfate donor for SULT2A1) eliminates DHEAS production"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sult2a1_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_dhea> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asult2a1_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_dhea> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sult2a1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_dhea> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, Ch. 15 (DHEA-to-DHEA-S flux via SULT2A1; substrate-consumption sign convention: enzyme activity -> substrate falls). | intervention: do(SULT2A1 activity increase): DHEA is consumed faster and plasma DHEA falls"
    ; pmo:mechanism "SULT2A1 consumes DHEA as substrate for sulfation to DHEA-S; higher SULT2A1 activity lowers the free DHEA pool"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/steroid_sulfatase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_dheas> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asteroid_sulfatase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_dheas> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/steroid_sulfatase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_dheas> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "X-linked ichthyosis (STS deficiency; OMIM 308100): plasma DHEA-S is markedly elevated due to failure of STS to hydrolyse DHEA-S to DHEA. Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, Ch. 15 (DHEA-S <-> DHEA interconversion by STS and SULT2A1). | intervention: STS loss-of-function (X-linked ichthyosis, OMIM 308100): DHEA-S accumulates because it cannot be hydrolysed to DHEA"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Steroid sulfatase (STS) cleaves the sulfate ester bond in DHEA-S, converting it to free DHEA; higher STS activity lowers the DHEA-S pool (and raises DHEA); STS deficiency allows DHEA-S to accumulate"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/srd5a2_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_dihydrotestosterone> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asrd5a2_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_dihydrotestosterone> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/srd5a2_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_dihydrotestosterone> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "5-alpha-reductase type 2 deficiency (OMIM 264600): DHT is markedly low with elevated testosterone:DHT ratio (DHT falls, testosterone rises). Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, Ch. 19 (5alpha-reductase testosterone -> DHT conversion in genital tissue). | intervention: SRD5A2 loss-of-function (OMIM 264600): DHT falls; 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors (finasteride, dutasteride) lower DHT clinically"
    ; pmo:mechanism "SRD5A2 (5-alpha-reductase type 2) reduces the 4,5 double bond of testosterone, converting it to dihydrotestosterone (DHT); higher SRD5A2 activity produces more DHT"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aromatase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_estriol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aaromatase_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_estriol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aromatase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_estriol> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Aromatase deficiency (OMIM 107910): all circulating estrogens including estriol are markedly reduced. Aromatase-excess syndrome (gain of CYP19A1; OMIM 139300): elevated estrogens including estriol. Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, Ch. 25 (CYP19A1: androgen->estrogen; hepatic 16-OH-estradiol -> estriol). | intervention: Aromatase loss-of-function (OMIM 107910): estriol falls; aromatase excess (OMIM 139300): estriol rises"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Aromatase (CYP19A1) converts androgens to estrogens (estrone, estradiol); estradiol is subsequently 16-alpha-hydroxylated in the liver to estriol; aromatase gain-of-function raises all estrogens including estriol"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cyp11b1_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_corticosterone> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acyp11b1_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_corticosterone> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cyp11b1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_corticosterone> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency (congenital adrenal hyperplasia type IV; OMIM 202010): corticosterone is reduced and its precursor 11-deoxycorticosterone accumulates. Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, Ch. 15 (adrenal steroidogenesis: 11-deoxycorticosterone -> corticosterone via CYP11B1). | intervention: CYP11B1 loss-of-function (OMIM 202010): corticosterone falls as the enzyme cannot complete the 11-beta-hydroxylation step"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CYP11B1 (steroid 11-beta-hydroxylase) converts 11-deoxycorticosterone to corticosterone; higher CYP11B1 activity produces more corticosterone"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cyp11b2_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_18_hydroxycortisone> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acyp11b2_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_18_hydroxycortisone> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cyp11b2_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_18_hydroxycortisone> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Glucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism (GRA / familial hyperaldosteronism type I; OMIM 103900): pathognomonic elevation of 18-hydroxycortisol and 18-hydroxycortisone due to chimeric CYP11B1/CYP11B2 gene. Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, Ch. 16 (GRA: ACTH-driven ectopic CYP11B2 18-hydroxylase activity in zona fasciculata -> 18-OH steroids). | intervention: CYP11B2/CYP11B1 chimeric gain-of-function (GRA, OMIM 103900): 18-hydroxycortisone rises under ACTH stimulation"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CYP11B2 (aldosterone synthase) has 18-hydroxylase activity; in glucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism (GRA/FH-I) a chimeric CYP11B2/CYP11B1 gene driven by ACTH ectopically produces 18-hydroxycortisol and 18-hydroxycortisone (the 11-keto form); higher CYP11B2-type 18-hydroxylase activity raises 18-hydroxycortisone"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/type_3_deiodinase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_reverse_t3> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atype_3_deiodinase_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_reverse_t3> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/type_3_deiodinase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_reverse_t3> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Sick euthyroid syndrome (non-thyroidal illness): DIO3 is upregulated in critical illness, shunting T4 towards rT3 instead of active T3; rT3 rises substantially. Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (thyroid hormone deiodination: DIO3 inner-ring deiodination of T4 -> reverse T3). Bianco & Kim 2006, Thyroid (DIO3 role in rT3 generation). | intervention: DIO3 upregulation in critical illness (pharmacological model of amiodarone or sick-euthyroid): reverse T3 rises"
    ; pmo:mechanism "DIO3 (type-III iodothyronine deiodinase; thyroxine 5-deiodinase) removes an inner-ring (5-position) iodine from T4, producing reverse T3 (3,3',5'-triiodothyronine); higher DIO3 activity produces more reverse T3"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/type_3_deiodinase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_t4> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atype_3_deiodinase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_t4> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/type_3_deiodinase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_t4> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Sick euthyroid syndrome: DIO3 upregulation diverts T4 to rT3, contributing to the fall in T4 observed in critical illness. Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (peripheral deiodination as T4 disposal pathway). | intervention: DIO3 upregulation in critical illness: T4 substrate is consumed faster, contributing to low T4 in sick euthyroid"
    ; pmo:mechanism "DIO3 consumes T4 as substrate for inner-ring deiodination to reverse T3; higher DIO3 activity lowers the T4 pool"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tsh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_thyroglobulin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atsh%3Apositive%3Aplasma_thyroglobulin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tsh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_thyroglobulin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Secretion of Tg is TSH dependent. Therefore the serum Tg level may rise when suppressive therapy is withdrawn or after injections of recombinant human TSH (rhTSH).\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 11, p. 361"
    ; pmo:mechanism "TSH stimulates thyroid follicular-cell Tg synthesis and secretion; recombinant human TSH injection raises serum Tg"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_amh> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atestosterone%3Anegative%3Aplasma_amh> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_amh> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, Ch. 18 (testosterone suppresses Sertoli cell AMH in puberty; androgen-dependent downregulation of AMH). Belville et al. 2005, Mol Endocrinol (androgen receptor suppresses AMH promoter in Sertoli cells). Male hypogonadism (low testosterone): AMH may be relatively elevated. | intervention: Androgen insensitivity syndrome (AIS; OMIM 313700 - complete): testosterone cannot suppress Sertoli AMH -> AMH is paradoxically high despite XY karyotype"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Testosterone suppresses anti-Mullerian hormone (AMH) expression in Sertoli cells during puberty; androgen receptor signaling in Sertoli cells downregulates AMH gene transcription, causing the pubertal fall in AMH; gonadal failure (low testosterone) leaves Sertoli AMH expression relatively uninhibited"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/end_diastolic_volume> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bnp> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aend_diastolic_volume%3Apositive%3Aplasma_bnp> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/end_diastolic_volume> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bnp> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Levin et al. 1998, NEJM (BNP as a marker of left-ventricular wall stress and volume overload). Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (natriuretic peptides released by myocardial stretch). Gardner & Lewicki 2004, Nat Med (cardiomyocyte mechanical stretch drives BNP gene expression via GATA4/NF-kB). | intervention: Volume loading / heart failure with elevated EDV: BNP rises proportionally; diuresis reduces EDV and BNP falls (used clinically to guide HF therapy)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Increased left-ventricular end-diastolic volume causes myocardial wall stretch, which activates stretch-sensitive signaling in ventricular cardiomyocytes (mechanosensors, NF-kB/GATA4), upregulating BNP (NPPB) gene transcription and protein secretion; greater EDV -> greater wall stress -> more BNP released into plasma"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/end_diastolic_volume> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_nt_probnp> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aend_diastolic_volume%3Apositive%3Aplasma_nt_probnp> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/end_diastolic_volume> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_nt_probnp> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Levin et al. 1998, NEJM (NT-proBNP co-secreted with BNP from ventricular myocytes under wall stress). Maisel et al. 2002, NEJM (NT-proBNP for diagnosis of heart failure: elevated in proportion to ventricular volume overload/wall stress). Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (natriuretic peptide processing). | intervention: Volume loading / congestive heart failure (elevated EDV): NT-proBNP rises in parallel with BNP; its longer half-life makes it a robust clinical biomarker of ventricular stretch"
    ; pmo:mechanism "NT-proBNP is the biologically inert N-terminal fragment cleaved from proBNP upon secretion from ventricular cardiomyocytes; proBNP secretion is triggered by the same myocardial wall stretch that drives BNP; NT-proBNP and BNP are produced in a 1:1 molar ratio from the same cleavage event"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/right_atrial_pressure> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_nt_proanp> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aright_atrial_pressure%3Apositive%3Aplasma_nt_proanp> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/right_atrial_pressure> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_nt_proanp> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "de Bold et al. 1981, Life Sci (atrial stretch releases ANP and proANP fragments). Vesely et al. 1994, Clin Chem (NT-proANP is released in proportion to atrial distension, as confirmed by experimental volume loading). Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (atrial wall stretch -> ANP/NT-proANP secretion). | intervention: Volume loading or atrial hypertension (elevated right or left atrial pressure): NT-proANP rises proportionally to atrial distension"
    ; pmo:mechanism "NT-proANP is the N-terminal prohormone fragment co-released with ANP from atrial cardiomyocytes when the atrium is stretched; elevated right atrial pressure (or left atrial pressure) reflects atrial wall stretch that drives proANP cleavage and secretion; NT-proANP circulates at higher concentrations than mature ANP and is the stable plasma marker of atrial stretch"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/vip_secreting_enteric_neuron_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_vasoactive_intestinal_peptide> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Avip_secreting_enteric_neuron_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_vasoactive_intestinal_peptide> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/vip_secreting_enteric_neuron_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_vasoactive_intestinal_peptide> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "VIPoma (pancreatic cholera / Verner-Morrison syndrome; OMIM 601634 region): pancreatic or intestinal tumor that autonomously secretes VIP -> plasma VIP markedly elevated. Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (VIPoma: diarrhea syndrome caused by autonomous VIP hypersecretion from neuroendocrine tumor). Bloom et al. 1973, Lancet (original description of VIP-secreting tumors). | intervention: VIPoma (neuroendocrine tumor with autonomous VIP secretion): plasma VIP is markedly elevated; octreotide (somatostatin analogue) suppresses VIP secretion and lowers plasma VIP"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Enteric VIP-secreting neurons (and their neoplastic counterparts, VIPoma tumor cells) release VIP into the circulation; autonomous tumor secretion (VIPoma) bypasses normal regulatory controls and produces markedly elevated plasma VIP"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_po2> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_vegf> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aarterial_po2%3Anegative%3Aplasma_vegf> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_po2> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_vegf> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "VHL loss-of-function (von Hippel-Lindau disease; OMIM 193300): constitutive HIF-1-alpha accumulation mimics hypoxia -> VEGF is markedly elevated, driving hemangioblastoma and clear-cell RCC vascularity. Semenza 2003, Nat Rev Cancer (HIF-1/VEGF axis: hypoxia -> VEGF transcription). Ferrara et al. 2003, Nat Med (VEGF regulation by HIF-1 under hypoxia). | intervention: VHL loss-of-function (OMIM 193300): VEGF constitutively elevated; PHD2 inhibitors (hypoxia mimetics) raise VEGF; increasing FiO2 (hyperoxia) lowers VEGF via VHL-mediated HIF-1-alpha degradation"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Low arterial PO2 (hypoxia) stabilizes HIF-1-alpha (von Hippel-Lindau protein cannot target it for proteasomal degradation under low O2), allowing HIF-1 to transactivate the VEGFA gene; plasma VEGF rises under hypoxia; restoring PO2 restores VHL-mediated HIF-1-alpha degradation and lowers VEGF"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_proinsulin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_glucose%3Apositive%3Aplasma_proinsulin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_proinsulin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (beta-cell glucose sensing -> proinsulin synthesis and granule maturation -> co-secretion of proinsulin with insulin). Ward et al. 1987, J Clin Invest (glucose stimulates proinsulin secretion; elevated proinsulin:insulin ratio in type 2 diabetes reflects beta-cell processing inefficiency). Kahn et al. 1990, NEJM (proinsulin as a marker of beta-cell secretory activity). | intervention: Hyperglycaemia / glucose challenge: proinsulin rises in proportion to insulin secretion; insulinoma produces excess proinsulin constitutively (autonomous secretion)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Pancreatic beta cells respond to elevated glucose by increasing proinsulin synthesis and secretion; proinsulin is the precursor of insulin and C-peptide and is co-secreted with insulin from beta-cell granules (proportionally elevated in glucose-stimulated secretion and in beta-cell dysfunction where incomplete processing raises the proinsulin:insulin ratio)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pth> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_osteopontin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apth%3Apositive%3Aplasma_osteopontin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pth> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_osteopontin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Reinholt et al. 1990, Proc Natl Acad Sci (osteopontin produced by osteoblasts and abundantly present in bone matrix; PTH drives osteoblast gene expression). Franzen & Heinegard 1985, Biochem J (osteopontin released from bone during resorption). Primary hyperparathyroidism (OMIM 145000): elevated PTH drives increased osteoblast activity and bone remodeling, raising plasma osteopontin. | intervention: Primary hyperparathyroidism (OMIM 145000): elevated PTH increases osteoblast-derived osteopontin secretion and matrix-stored osteopontin release via osteoclastic resorption"
    ; pmo:mechanism "PTH upregulates osteopontin (SPP1) gene expression in osteoblasts via PTH1R/cAMP/PKA signaling; osteopontin is then secreted into the extracellular matrix and circulation; PTH also drives osteoclastic bone resorption, releasing matrix-stored osteopontin"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pth> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_beta_ctx> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apth%3Apositive%3Aplasma_beta_ctx> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pth> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_beta_ctx> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Rosen 2008, NEJM (bone resorption markers including beta-CTX/beta-CrossLaps reflect osteoclast activity driven by PTH). Primary hyperparathyroidism (OMIM 145000): beta-CTX is elevated reflecting increased osteoclastic bone resorption. Antiresorptive therapy (bisphosphonates) lowers beta-CTX, confirming PTH-driven osteoclastic pathway. Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (PTH stimulates osteoclastic resorption via RANKL). | intervention: Primary hyperparathyroidism (OMIM 145000): PTH drives osteoclastic bone resorption -> beta-CTX rises; bisphosphonate therapy suppresses resorption and lowers beta-CTX"
    ; pmo:mechanism "PTH stimulates osteoclastic bone resorption via the RANKL/OPG axis on osteoblasts (PTH -> osteoblast RANKL up-regulation, OPG down-regulation -> osteoclast activation); activated osteoclasts degrade type I collagen, releasing C-terminal telopeptide crosslinks (beta-CTX) into the circulation"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aadc_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_catecholamines> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aaadc_activity%3Apositive%3Aurinary_catecholamines> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aadc_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_catecholamines> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine — aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (AADC/DDC) deficiency (OMIM 608643): markedly reduced urinary catecholamines; Swoboda KJ et al., Ann Neurol 2003 (AADC deficiency clinical phenotype: profoundly reduced plasma and urinary catecholamines); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (catecholamine biosynthesis pathway) | intervention: AADC deficiency (DDC gene LoF, OMIM 608643): do(aadc_activity=low) -> urinary_catecholamines falls"
    ; pmo:mechanism "aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (AADC/DDC) catalyses the rate-limiting decarboxylation step producing dopamine (from L-DOPA), serotonin (from 5-HTP), and trace amines; all urinary catecholamines (dopamine, norepinephrine, epinephrine) are downstream of AADC; higher AADC activity raises catecholamine synthesis and urinary excretion, while LoF (AADC deficiency) causes profoundly reduced urinary catecholamines"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aadc_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_dopamine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aaadc_activity%3Apositive%3Aurinary_dopamine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aadc_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_dopamine> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine — AADC deficiency (OMIM 608643): absent or markedly reduced urinary dopamine and other catecholamines; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (L-DOPA --DOPA decarboxylase--> dopamine, catecholamine biosynthesis) | intervention: AADC deficiency (DDC gene LoF, OMIM 608643): do(aadc_activity=low) -> urinary_dopamine falls"
    ; pmo:mechanism "AADC decarboxylates L-DOPA to dopamine; increased enzyme activity raises dopamine synthesis and urinary dopamine excretion; AADC deficiency (LoF) abolishes dopamine synthesis, causing undetectable urinary dopamine"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dbh_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_norepinephrine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Adbh_activity%3Apositive%3Aurinary_norepinephrine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dbh_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_norepinephrine> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine — dopamine beta-hydroxylase deficiency (OMIM 223360): absent plasma and urinary norepinephrine and epinephrine; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (dopamine --DBH--> norepinephrine); Williams Textbook of Endocrinology (urinary norepinephrine as catecholamine secretion assay) | intervention: DBH deficiency (DBH gene LoF, OMIM 223360): do(dbh_activity=low) -> urinary_norepinephrine falls to zero"
    ; pmo:mechanism "dopamine beta-hydroxylase hydroxylates dopamine to norepinephrine in sympathetic nerve terminals and adrenal chromaffin cells; higher DBH activity raises norepinephrine synthesis and urinary excretion; DBH deficiency abolishes norepinephrine and epinephrine synthesis, leaving urinary norepinephrine undetectable"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/epinephrine> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_epinephrine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aepinephrine%3Apositive%3Aurinary_epinephrine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/epinephrine> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_epinephrine> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Williams Textbook of Endocrinology — pheochromocytoma/paraganglioma: diagnosis by elevated urinary fractionated catecholamines including epinephrine; Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (adrenal medullary catecholamine secretion and urinary excretion assay); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (24-h urinary catecholamines as pheochromocytoma screening test) | intervention: pheochromocytoma (catecholamine-secreting tumour): do(epinephrine=high) -> urinary_epinephrine rises; DBH deficiency (OMIM 223360) as reciprocal evidence: do(epinephrine=absent) -> urinary_epinephrine absent"
    ; pmo:mechanism "plasma epinephrine is filtered and excreted by the kidney; a higher plasma epinephrine concentration drives proportionally greater urinary epinephrine excretion; pheochromocytoma-driven elevation of plasma epinephrine is reflected diagnostically in elevated urinary epinephrine"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/comt_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_metanephrine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acomt_activity%3Apositive%3Aurinary_metanephrine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/comt_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_metanephrine> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (catecholamine catabolism: COMT + MAO pathways; epinephrine -> metanephrine via COMT); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (catecholamine inactivation: O-methylation by COMT); Williams Textbook of Endocrinology (fractionated urinary metanephrines in pheochromocytoma diagnosis; metanephrine is the O-methylated epinephrine catabolite) | intervention: pharmacological: COMT inhibitors (entacapone/tolcapone): do(comt_activity=low) -> urinary_metanephrine falls; pheochromocytoma increases substrate (epinephrine) and COMT converts it, raising metanephrine"
    ; pmo:mechanism "catechol O-methyltransferase O-methylates epinephrine to metanephrine (and norepinephrine to normetanephrine) as the principal inactivation pathway; higher COMT activity converts more epinephrine to metanephrine, raising urinary metanephrine; COMT inhibitors (tolcapone, entacapone) pharmacologically reduce metanephrine production"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aadc_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_homovanillic_acid> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aaadc_activity%3Apositive%3Aurinary_homovanillic_acid> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aadc_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_homovanillic_acid> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine — AADC deficiency (OMIM 608643): markedly reduced urinary HVA and 5-HIAA (key diagnostic markers); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (dopamine catabolism by MAO/COMT to HVA); Swoboda KJ et al., Ann Neurol 2003 (AADC deficiency: profound reduction of HVA in urine and CSF) | intervention: AADC deficiency (DDC gene LoF, OMIM 608643): do(aadc_activity=low) -> urinary_homovanillic_acid falls dramatically"
    ; pmo:mechanism "AADC produces dopamine from L-DOPA; dopamine is catabolised via MAO and COMT to homovanillic acid (HVA), the principal dopamine end-metabolite; higher AADC activity raises dopamine flux and thereby HVA formation and urinary excretion; AADC deficiency (LoF) dramatically reduces urinary HVA"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dbh_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_vanillylmandelic_acid> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Adbh_activity%3Apositive%3Aurinary_vanillylmandelic_acid> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dbh_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_vanillylmandelic_acid> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Williams Textbook of Endocrinology — pheochromocytoma diagnosis by elevated urinary VMA; Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine — dopamine-beta-hydroxylase deficiency (OMIM 223360): absent urinary VMA (norepinephrine/epinephrine catabolite); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (catecholamine catabolism: norepinephrine -> normetanephrine -> VMA via MAO + COMT) | intervention: DBH deficiency (DBH gene LoF, OMIM 223360): do(dbh_activity=low) -> urinary_vanillylmandelic_acid falls to zero"
    ; pmo:mechanism "dopamine beta-hydroxylase produces norepinephrine and (via PNMT) epinephrine; vanillylmandelic acid (VMA) is the terminal urinary catabolite of norepinephrine and epinephrine (via MAO + COMT); DBH deficiency abolishes norepinephrine/epinephrine synthesis, leaving urinary VMA absent; pheochromocytoma with high DBH flux raises urinary VMA (the classic screening test)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_serotonin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_5hiaa> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_serotonin%3Apositive%3Aurinary_5hiaa> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_serotonin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_5hiaa> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine — carcinoid syndrome: elevated plasma serotonin and elevated urinary 5-HIAA (the cardinal diagnostic marker, >25 mg/day); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (serotonin catabolism by MAO to 5-HIAA); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (urinary 5-HIAA as serotonin-turnover assay) | intervention: carcinoid/carcinoid syndrome: do(plasma_serotonin=high from enterochromaffin-cell tumour) -> urinary_5hiaa elevated; MAO inhibitors (phenelzine): do(MAO inhibited) -> plasma_serotonin rises, 5-HIAA falls"
    ; pmo:mechanism "serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine) is oxidatively deaminated by monoamine oxidase to 5-hydroxyindoleacetaldehyde, then oxidised to 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid (5-HIAA) by aldehyde dehydrogenase; 5-HIAA is renally excreted; a larger serotonin pool drives proportionally greater 5-HIAA formation and urinary excretion"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ssadh_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_gaba> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Assadh_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_gaba> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ssadh_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_gaba> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine — SSADH deficiency (succinic semialdehyde dehydrogenase deficiency, OMIM 271980): elevated GABA in urine, plasma, and CSF with 4-hydroxybutyric aciduria; Pearl PL et al., Ann Neurol 2003 (SSADH deficiency: elevated GABA and 4-OH-butyrate across compartments); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (GABA shunt: GABA -> succinic semialdehyde -> succinate via SSADH) | intervention: SSADH deficiency (ALDH5A1 gene LoF, OMIM 271980): do(ssadh_activity=low) -> urinary_gaba rises dramatically"
    ; pmo:mechanism "succinic semialdehyde dehydrogenase (ALDH5A1/SSADH) catalyses the oxidation of succinic semialdehyde (SSA) to succinate in the GABA shunt; higher SSADH activity clears SSA and removes the GABA catabolism bottleneck, keeping GABA at normal levels; SSADH deficiency (LoF) blocks the GABA -> succinate pathway, causing GABA accumulation and markedly elevated urinary GABA excretion (4-hydroxybutyric aciduria with hypergammaaminobutyric aciduria)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aadc_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_tyramine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aaadc_activity%3Apositive%3Aurinary_tyramine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aadc_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_tyramine> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Manegold C et al., J Inherit Metab Dis 2009 (AADC deficiency: reduced urinary trace amines including tyramine); Brun L et al., J Inherit Metab Dis 2010 (AADC deficiency phenotype: low urinary biogenic amines and trace amines); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (AADC substrate promiscuity: decarboxylation of aromatic amino acids including tyrosine to tyramine) | intervention: AADC deficiency (DDC gene LoF, OMIM 608643): do(aadc_activity=low) -> urinary_tyramine falls"
    ; pmo:mechanism "aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (AADC/DDC) decarboxylates not only L-DOPA and 5-HTP but also L-tyrosine to tyramine (and L-phenylalanine to phenylethylamine); higher AADC activity raises tyramine synthesis from dietary tyrosine; AADC deficiency (LoF) reduces urinary tyramine alongside other AADC-dependent trace amines"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aadc_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_vanillactate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aaadc_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_vanillactate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aadc_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_vanillactate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Brun L et al., J Inherit Metab Dis 2010 (AADC deficiency: elevated urinary vanillactic acid as diagnostic biomarker); Hyland K & Clayton PT, J Neurochem 1992 (vanillactic acid elevated in AADC-deficient patients: the L-DOPA->3-OMD->vanillactate shunt pathway); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (AADC/DDC deficiency, OMIM 608643: elevated L-DOPA and vanillactic acid metabolites) | intervention: AADC deficiency (DDC gene LoF, OMIM 608643): do(aadc_activity=low) -> L-DOPA accumulates -> urinary_vanillactate rises"
    ; pmo:mechanism "when AADC is deficient, L-DOPA cannot be decarboxylated to dopamine and instead accumulates; excess L-DOPA is O-methylated by COMT to 3-O-methylDOPA (3-OMD) and further catabolised via reductive metabolism to vanillactic acid (3-methoxy-4-hydroxyphenyllactic acid); thus AADC deficiency (lower enzyme activity) causes a shunt toward vanillactate, raising its urinary excretion; higher AADC activity consumes L-DOPA normally, reducing the shunt to vanillactate"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aadc_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_catecholamines> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aaadc_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_catecholamines> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aadc_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_catecholamines> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Swoboda KJ et al., Ann Neurol 2003 (AADC deficiency: profoundly reduced plasma catecholamines — dopamine, norepinephrine, epinephrine all markedly low); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (AADC/DDC deficiency, OMIM 608643: absent catecholamine synthesis); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (catecholamine biosynthesis: all catecholamines derive from dopamine produced by AADC) | intervention: AADC deficiency (DDC gene LoF, OMIM 608643): do(aadc_activity=low) -> plasma_catecholamines falls to near zero"
    ; pmo:mechanism "AADC is the obligate rate-limiting enzyme for synthesis of all three major catecholamines (dopamine, norepinephrine, epinephrine) by decarboxylating L-DOPA to dopamine (the common precursor); higher AADC activity raises the total plasma catecholamine pool; AADC deficiency profoundly reduces all plasma catecholamines"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ssadh_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_gaba> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Assadh_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_gaba> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ssadh_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_gaba> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Pearl PL et al., Ann Neurol 2003 (SSADH deficiency, OMIM 271980: markedly elevated plasma and CSF GABA); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (SSADH deficiency: hypergammaaminobutyric acidemia in all compartments); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (GABA shunt: SSADH oxidises succinic semialdehyde to succinate, the final step of GABA catabolism) | intervention: SSADH deficiency (ALDH5A1 gene LoF, OMIM 271980): do(ssadh_activity=low) -> plasma_gaba rises markedly"
    ; pmo:mechanism "SSADH catabolises succinic semialdehyde (the intermediate in GABA breakdown) to succinate; higher SSADH activity promotes GABA catabolism, keeping plasma GABA at normal levels; SSADH deficiency (LoF) blocks this pathway, causing GABA accumulation in plasma as well as CSF and urine"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/monoamine_oxidase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_dopac> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amonoamine_oxidase_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_dopac> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/monoamine_oxidase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_dopac> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (dopamine catabolism by MAO to DOPAC and then to HVA via COMT); Knoll J & Magyar K, Adv Biochem Psychopharmacol 1972 (MAO inhibition by selegiline/(-)-deprenyl reduces DOPAC); Levitt P & Bhagavan HN, Psychopharmacol 1975 (MAO inhibitors reduce plasma DOPAC: pharmacological intervention evidence) | intervention: MAO inhibitor treatment (e.g., selegiline/phenelzine): do(monoamine_oxidase_activity=low) -> plasma_dopac falls"
    ; pmo:mechanism "monoamine oxidase (MAO, primarily MAO-B in brain and periphery) oxidatively deaminates dopamine to 3,4-dihydroxyphenylacetaldehyde, which is further oxidised by aldehyde dehydrogenase to 3,4-dihydroxyphenylacetic acid (DOPAC); higher MAO activity raises DOPAC formation; MAO inhibitors (phenelzine, selegiline/deprenyl) pharmacologically reduce plasma DOPAC"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aadc_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_homovanillate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aaadc_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_homovanillate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aadc_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_homovanillate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Swoboda KJ et al., Ann Neurol 2003 (AADC deficiency, OMIM 608643: markedly reduced plasma and CSF HVA — a key diagnostic criterion); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (dopamine -> DOPAC -> HVA via MAO then COMT); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (AADC deficiency: low HVA in plasma and CSF) | intervention: AADC deficiency (DDC gene LoF, OMIM 608643): do(aadc_activity=low) -> plasma_homovanillate falls dramatically"
    ; pmo:mechanism "AADC produces dopamine from L-DOPA; dopamine is sequentially catabolised by MAO and COMT to homovanillic acid (HVA), the principal dopamine end-metabolite in plasma; higher AADC activity raises dopamine throughput and plasma HVA; AADC deficiency dramatically reduces plasma HVA"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/comt_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_metanephrine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acomt_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_metanephrine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/comt_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_metanephrine> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (catecholamine catabolism: COMT methylates epinephrine -> metanephrine); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (catecholamine O-methylation by hepatic COMT is the main inactivation route); Williams Textbook of Endocrinology (plasma fractionated metanephrines as pheochromocytoma diagnostic test: metanephrine is the COMT product of epinephrine) | intervention: pharmacological: COMT inhibitors (entacapone/tolcapone): do(comt_activity=low) -> plasma_metanephrine falls; pheochromocytoma: elevated epinephrine substrate drives more COMT conversion and elevated plasma metanephrine"
    ; pmo:mechanism "catechol O-methyltransferase (COMT) O-methylates epinephrine to metanephrine as the primary epinephrine inactivation pathway in liver, adrenal medulla, and peripheral tissue; higher COMT activity converts more epinephrine to plasma metanephrine; COMT inhibitors (entacapone, tolcapone) reduce plasma metanephrine"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aadc_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_hva> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aaadc_activity%3Apositive%3Acsf_hva> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aadc_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_hva> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Swoboda KJ et al., Ann Neurol 2003 (AADC deficiency, OMIM 608643: markedly reduced CSF HVA — the single most sensitive diagnostic marker); Blau N et al., Physician's Guide to Inherited Metabolic Diseases (AADC deficiency: CSF HVA and 5-HIAA < 10% of normal); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (dopamine catabolism: CNS dopamine -> HVA via MAO and COMT, reflected in CSF HVA) | intervention: AADC deficiency (DDC gene LoF, OMIM 608643): do(aadc_activity=low) -> csf_hva falls to < 10% of normal"
    ; pmo:mechanism "AADC produces dopamine in dopaminergic CNS neurons; CSF homovanillic acid (HVA) is the principal dopamine catabolite in the CNS, reflecting central dopaminergic turnover; higher AADC activity raises CNS dopamine synthesis and CSF HVA; AADC deficiency dramatically reduces CSF HVA (the key diagnostic criterion for AADC deficiency)"
    .
