<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dystrophin_level> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Adystrophin_level%3Anegative%3Asarcolemmal_permeability> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dystrophin_level> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Frontiers Physiology 2023 doi:10.3389/fphys.2023.1183101 (DMD disease mechanism, sarcolemmal leakage); Duchenne muscular dystrophy OMIM 310200; Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (skeletal muscle membrane physiology); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 'Muscular Dystrophies' (absent dystrophin → CK 50–300× ULN, sarcolemmal fragility). | intervention: do(dystrophin_level ↓) — Duchenne/Becker muscular dystrophy, DMD gene loss-of-function"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Dystrophin anchors the sarcolemma to the cytoskeleton via the dystrophin-glycoprotein complex; loss of dystrophin destabilizes sarcolemmal integrity, causing membrane tears during contraction and markedly increased membrane permeability (quality: dystrophin concentration → disposition: sarcolemmal stability → process: contraction-induced membrane tearing → quality: permeability)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/muscle_caveolin_3> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amuscle_caveolin_3%3Anegative%3Asarcolemmal_permeability> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/muscle_caveolin_3> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Caveolinopathies review, European Journal of Human Genetics 2009 (doi:10.1038/ejhg.2009.103); CAV3 OMIM *601253; LGMD1C/rippling muscle disease OMIM 606072; Nature Genetics 1998 (Minetti et al., CAV3 mutations in LGMD1C); Harrison's 'Muscular Dystrophies'. | intervention: do(muscle_caveolin_3 ↓) — LGMD1C / rippling muscle disease, CAV3 loss-of-function"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Caveolin-3 is the principal structural protein of caveolae at the sarcolemma; it stabilises the membrane and co-localises with the dystrophin complex. LoF mutations reduce CAV3 at the sarcolemma, disorganise the T-tubule network and increase sarcolemmal permeability (quality: CAV3 level → disposition: membrane scaffold integrity → process: sarcolemmal instability → quality: permeability)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lactate_dehydrogenase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alactate_dehydrogenase_activity%3Anegative%3Asarcolemmal_permeability> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lactate_dehydrogenase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Glycolytic metabolic myopathy with exercise-induced rhabdomyolysis/myoglobinuria. LDHA (LDH-M subunit) deficiency / GSD XI, OMIM 612933. Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, \"Metabolic Myopathies\" and \"Rhabdomyolysis\" (glycolytic-enzyme defects cause exertional myoglobinuria and CK elevation); the leak panel (sarcolemmal_permeability → CK/LDH/aldolase/myoglobin) is already curated above. *** DRAFT prototype: metabolic trigger into the existing muscle-damage hub ***"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LDHA (lactate dehydrogenase M-subunit) regenerates cytosolic NAD+ to sustain anaerobic glycolytic ATP under load; LoF stalls glycolytic flux during exertion, so the myocyte cannot meet contractile energy demand, ATP-dependent sarcolemmal ion pumps fail and Ca2+ overload breaks down the membrane (quality: LDHA activity → disposition: glycolytic ATP capacity under load → process: exertional energy failure / membrane injury → quality: sarcolemmal permeability). Exertion-gated: revealed by exercise, not at rest."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/myophosphorylase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amyophosphorylase_activity%3Anegative%3Asarcolemmal_permeability> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/myophosphorylase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "McArdle disease / GSD V, OMIM 232600 (PYGM). The paradigm exertional-rhabdomyolysis myopathy: cramps, myoglobinuria and high serum CK after brief intense exercise. Harrison's \"Metabolic Myopathies\"; DiMauro, muscle glycogenoses. Leak panel curated above. *** DRAFT prototype ***"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Myophosphorylase (PYGM) releases glucose-1-phosphate from glycogen, the dominant fuel for short, intense exertion; LoF blocks glycogenolysis so the contracting myocyte hits an energy crisis (\"second-wind\" defect), ATP-dependent pumps fail and the sarcolemma is damaged (quality: myophosphorylase activity → disposition: glycogenolytic ATP capacity → process: exertional energy failure / membrane injury → quality: sarcolemmal permeability). Exertion-gated."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/phosphofructokinase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aphosphofructokinase_activity%3Anegative%3Asarcolemmal_permeability> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/phosphofructokinase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Tarui disease / GSD VII, OMIM 232800 (PFKM). Exercise intolerance with cramps and exertional myoglobinuria/CK elevation. Harrison's \"Metabolic Myopathies\"; DiMauro, muscle glycogenoses. Leak panel curated above. *** DRAFT prototype ***"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Muscle phosphofructokinase (PFKM) catalyses the committed step of glycolysis; LoF blocks glycolytic ATP production during exertion, producing the same energy-failure membrane injury as the other glycolytic defects (quality: PFK activity → disposition: glycolytic ATP capacity → process: exertional energy failure / membrane injury → quality: sarcolemmal permeability). Exertion-gated."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cpt2_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acpt2_activity%3Anegative%3Asarcolemmal_permeability> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cpt2_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "CPT II deficiency (myopathic form), OMIM 255110. The commonest inherited cause of recurrent exertional rhabdomyolysis; episodic myoglobinuria and CK elevation provoked by prolonged exercise, fasting or intercurrent illness. Harrison's \"Metabolic Myopathies\" / \"Rhabdomyolysis\". Leak panel curated above. *** DRAFT prototype ***"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CPT II enables mitochondrial long-chain fatty-acid oxidation, the main fuel for sustained exercise and fasting; LoF starves the myocyte of ATP during prolonged exertion/fasting, failing the sarcolemmal pumps and damaging the membrane (quality: CPT2 activity → disposition: fatty-acid-oxidation ATP capacity → process: prolonged-exertion/fasting energy failure / membrane injury → quality: sarcolemmal permeability). Gated by prolonged exercise, fasting or fever."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_creatine_kinase> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asarcolemmal_permeability%3Apositive%3Aplasma_creatine_kinase> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_creatine_kinase> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 'Approach to the Patient with Muscle Disease' (serum CK 50–300× ULN in Duchenne MD); DMD warrior/PPMD (serum CK = diagnostic sign of DMD, mechanism: sarcolemmal leakiness); OMIM 310200; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (creatine kinase as abundant cytosolic muscle enzyme). | intervention: do(sarcolemmal_permeability ↑) — Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD LoF) as paradigm perturbation"
    ; pmo:mechanism "When sarcolemmal permeability increases (membrane tears/leakiness), cytosolic creatine kinase diffuses down its concentration gradient from the muscle fibre into the interstitium and then plasma. Total serum CK reflects the sum of all isoforms leaking from skeletal (and cardiac) muscle."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ck_mm> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asarcolemmal_permeability%3Apositive%3Aplasma_ck_mm> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ck_mm> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "ScienceDirect Diagnostic Accuracy of CK-MM in DMD Newborn Screening (CK-MM isoform validated as DMD skeletal-muscle biomarker); OMIM 310200; Harrison's 'Muscular Dystrophies' (CK-MM isoform predominantly elevated in skeletal myopathies). | intervention: do(sarcolemmal_permeability ↑) — Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD LoF)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CK-MM is the skeletal-muscle-predominant homodimer of creatine kinase M-type; it is the major isoform that leaks into plasma when sarcolemmal integrity is lost. CK-MM fraction is a more specific indicator of skeletal muscle damage than total CK."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/creatine_kinase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asarcolemmal_permeability%3Apositive%3Acreatine_kinase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/creatine_kinase_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (serum CK activity assay used to diagnose/monitor muscular dystrophies and rhabdomyolysis); OMIM 310200; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (CK catalytic mechanism). | intervention: do(sarcolemmal_permeability ↑) — Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD LoF)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Serum creatine kinase catalytic activity rises in parallel with total CK protein concentration when muscle CK leaks through a damaged sarcolemma; the enzyme retains activity in circulation, so serum CK activity faithfully tracks the leakage event."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ldh> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asarcolemmal_permeability%3Apositive%3Aplasma_ldh> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ldh> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 'Approach to Patient with Muscle Disease' (CK, LDH, aldolase all elevated in myopathic processes); UpToDate 'Rhabdomyolysis' (LDH among muscle enzymes elevated in rhabdomyolysis); OMIM 310200. | intervention: do(sarcolemmal_permeability ↑) — Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD LoF) / rhabdomyolysis paradigm"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LDH is an abundant cytosolic enzyme in skeletal muscle fibres; sarcolemmal damage allows LDH to leak into plasma. Elevated serum LDH is a well-established secondary marker of rhabdomyolysis and muscular dystrophy alongside CK."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lactate_dehydrogenase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asarcolemmal_permeability%3Apositive%3Alactate_dehydrogenase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lactate_dehydrogenase_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's (LDH activity as serum muscle-injury marker); UpToDate 'Rhabdomyolysis'; Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology; OMIM 310200. | intervention: do(sarcolemmal_permeability ↑) — muscular dystrophy / rhabdomyolysis paradigm"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Sarcolemmal leakage releases catalytically active LDH from muscle fibres into plasma; serum LDH enzymatic activity rises in parallel with concentration. Both 'reduced circulating LDH' (downward HPO term) and elevated LDH map to this node as the opposite poles of the same causal axis."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_aldolase> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asarcolemmal_permeability%3Apositive%3Aplasma_aldolase> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_aldolase> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Casciola-Rosen et al. PMC3786178 (isolated elevated serum aldolase in myositis patients; ALDOA leaks from damaged regenerating muscle fibres); UpToDate 'Rhabdomyolysis' (aldolase listed among muscle enzymes elevated in rhabdomyolysis); Harrison's 'Polymyositis and Dermatomyositis' (serum aldolase elevation); OMIM 310200. | intervention: do(sarcolemmal_permeability ↑) — Duchenne MD / inflammatory myopathy paradigm"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Aldolase A (ALDOA) is the muscle isoform of fructose-bisphosphate aldolase; it is a cytosolic glycolytic enzyme that leaks into plasma when the sarcolemma is damaged. Elevated serum aldolase is found in inflammatory myopathies, rhabdomyolysis and muscular dystrophies."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_myoglobin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asarcolemmal_permeability%3Apositive%3Aplasma_myoglobin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_myoglobin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Serum myoglobin in muscular dystrophy PubMed 7374673 (elevated serum myoglobin in 91.7% of DMD patients); ScienceDirect myoglobinuria in DMD (myoglobin released from fragile sarcolemma); OMIM 310200; Harrison's 'Rhabdomyolysis' (myoglobin as early plasma marker of muscle necrosis). | intervention: do(sarcolemmal_permeability ↑) — Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD LoF) / rhabdomyolysis [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Myoglobin is a small (17 kDa) heme-containing oxygen-storage protein of skeletal muscle fibres; its small size means it leaks rapidly into plasma when the sarcolemma is damaged, and then is filtered by the kidney. Elevated serum myoglobin is a sensitive early marker of rhabdomyolysis."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/enolase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aenolase_activity%3Anegative%3Asarcolemmal_permeability> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/enolase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "GSD XIII / glycogen storage disease due to muscle beta-enolase deficiency OMIM 612932; Wigley et al. JIMD Reports 2019 PMC6851005 (GSD XIII: exercise intolerance, myalgia, elevated serum CK, recurrent rhabdomyolysis); Harrison's 'Metabolic Myopathies'. | intervention: do(enolase_activity ↓) — GSD XIII, ENO3 loss-of-function (autosomal recessive)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Beta-enolase (ENO3) catalyses the dehydration of 2-phosphoglycerate to phosphoenolpyruvate in glycolysis; ENO3 deficiency impairs the distal glycolytic flux in skeletal muscle fibres, reducing ATP production during exercise. ATP depletion compromises Na+/K+-ATPase and membrane maintenance, leading to exercise-induced myofibre damage and increased sarcolemmal permeability (quality: ENO3 catalytic activity → disposition: glycolytic flux capacity → process: exercise-induced muscle energy failure → quality: sarcolemmal permeability)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/phosphoglycerate_kinase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aphosphoglycerate_kinase_activity%3Anegative%3Asarcolemmal_permeability> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/phosphoglycerate_kinase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "PGK1 deficiency OMIM 300653; NORD Phosphoglycerate Kinase Deficiency (myopathy, elevated CK, myoglobinuria); Suárez et al. Molecular Genetics and Metabolism 2012 (PGK1 deficiency: CK elevated, PGK1 activity 0.9% of control in muscle); Harrison's 'Metabolic Myopathies'. | intervention: do(phosphoglycerate_kinase_activity ↓) — PGK1 deficiency OMIM 300653 (X-linked)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Phosphoglycerate kinase 1 (PGK1) transfers the high-energy phosphate from 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate to ADP, generating ATP in glycolysis. PGK1 deficiency in muscle reduces ATP yield from glycolysis, causing exercise intolerance, myoglobinuria and elevated serum CK consistent with exercise-induced sarcolemmal permeabilisation and rhabdomyolysis."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/phosphoglycerate_mutase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aphosphoglycerate_mutase_activity%3Anegative%3Asarcolemmal_permeability> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/phosphoglycerate_mutase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "GSD X / phosphoglycerate mutase deficiency OMIM 261670; MedlinePlus Genetics 'phosphoglycerate mutase deficiency' (exercise-induced myoglobinuria, elevated CK between episodes); PubMed 27612597 (GSD X caused by novel PGAM2 variant, elevated serum CK); Harrison's 'Metabolic Myopathies'. | intervention: do(phosphoglycerate_mutase_activity ↓) — GSD X, PGAM2 loss-of-function (autosomal recessive) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Phosphoglycerate mutase 2 (PGAM2/muscle isoform) interconverts 3-phosphoglycerate and 2-phosphoglycerate; its deficiency blocks glycolysis in muscle, causing ATP deficit under exercise and episodic rhabdomyolysis with elevated serum CK and myoglobinuria, reflecting increased sarcolemmal permeability."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_pyruvate_kinase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/red_cell_mass> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aerythrocyte_pyruvate_kinase_activity%3Apositive%3Ared_cell_mass> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_pyruvate_kinase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/red_cell_mass> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Congenital nonspherocytic haemolytic anaemia-2 / PKLR deficiency OMIM 266200; Frontiers Pediatrics 2022 PMC9752143 (PKLR deficiency: chronic haemolysis ranging from mild to transfusion-dependent, ATP depletion → RBC destruction); Orphanet ORPHA766; Williams Hematology (PK deficiency). | intervention: do(erythrocyte_pyruvate_kinase_activity ↓) — PKLR deficiency (autosomal recessive) OMIM 266200"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Erythrocyte pyruvate kinase (PKLR) catalyses the final ATP-generating step of glycolysis in red cells; PKLR deficiency causes ATP depletion in erythrocytes, loss of membrane plasticity and premature RBC destruction (haemolysis). The consequent reduction in red-cell survival lowers total red-cell mass (hereditary nonspherocytic haemolytic anaemia)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_pyruvate_kinase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ldh> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aerythrocyte_pyruvate_kinase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_ldh> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_pyruvate_kinase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ldh> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "PKLR deficiency OMIM 266200; Williams Hematology 'Pyruvate Kinase Deficiency' (elevated serum LDH in haemolytic episodes); Harrison's 'Haemolytic Anaemia' (LDH released from lysing RBCs is a marker of haemolysis). | intervention: do(erythrocyte_pyruvate_kinase_activity ↓) — PKLR deficiency OMIM 266200"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Red blood cells contain high concentrations of LDH; haemolysis caused by PKLR deficiency releases intracellular LDH into plasma, elevating serum LDH. This haemolytic source of plasma LDH is independent of the muscle-damage leakage pathway."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_pyruvate_kinase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_unconjugated_bilirubin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aerythrocyte_pyruvate_kinase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_unconjugated_bilirubin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_pyruvate_kinase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_unconjugated_bilirubin> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "PKLR deficiency OMIM 266200; Frontiers Pediatrics PMC9752143 (unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, neonatal jaundice in severe PKLR deficiency); Harrison's 'Haemolytic Anaemia' (indirect bilirubin rises due to haem catabolism); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry Ch. 23 (haem degradation pathway). | intervention: do(erythrocyte_pyruvate_kinase_activity ↓) — PKLR deficiency OMIM 266200"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Haemolysis from PKLR deficiency releases haem from destroyed erythrocytes; haem is catabolised via haem oxygenase → biliverdin → unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin, which accumulates in plasma when haemolysis exceeds hepatic conjugation capacity, causing jaundice."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gamt_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_creatine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Agamt_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_creatine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gamt_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_creatine> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "GAMT deficiency / cerebral creatine deficiency syndrome 2 OMIM 612736; PubMed 9322805 (Schulze et al. 1997, 'Guanidino compounds in GAMT deficiency' — creatine concentrations low in plasma, CSF and urine); NORD GAMT deficiency (creatine supplementation restores plasma creatine); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (creatine biosynthesis: AGAT then GAMT). | intervention: do(gamt_activity ↓) — GAMT deficiency (autosomal recessive) OMIM 612736 [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Guanidinoacetate methyltransferase (GAMT) methylates guanidinoacetate to produce creatine (second and final step of creatine biosynthesis, occurring mainly in liver). LoF of GAMT abolishes this production, causing creatine depletion in plasma and tissues (∂[creatine]/∂GAMT_activity > 0)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_creatine> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_creatine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_creatine%3Apositive%3Aurinary_creatine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_creatine> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_creatine> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "GAMT deficiency OMIM 612736 (low plasma and urinary creatine/creatinine); AGAT deficiency (low urinary guanidinoacetate and low creatine); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (renal handling of creatine via SLC6A8 reabsorption; net urinary excretion proportional to filtered load); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (creatine → creatinine spontaneous conversion; urinary creatinine tracks creatine pool). | intervention: do(plasma_creatine ↑) — creatine supplementation in GAMT deficiency raises urinary creatine"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Creatine is freely filtered at the glomerulus and partially reabsorbed by tubular transporters (SLC6A8); higher plasma creatine concentration increases the filtered load and raises urinary creatine excretion. In GAMT deficiency, both plasma and urinary creatine are low; supplementation raises both."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_complex_iii_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_respiratory_chain_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amitochondrial_complex_iii_activity%3Apositive%3Amitochondrial_respiratory_chain_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_complex_iii_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_respiratory_chain_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "BCS1L deficiency / GRACILE syndrome (OMIM 614297): BCS1L mutations abolish Complex III assembly and reduce overall respiratory chain activity in muscle/liver; respiratory chain complex III deficiency due to BCS1L mutations (PMID 28427446; PMID 19162478). Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry ch. 19 (oxidative phosphorylation: complexes I-IV function in series). | intervention: do(BCS1L LoF) -> reduced Complex III activity -> reduced overall respiratory chain electron-transport flux [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Complex III (ubiquinol:cytochrome c oxidoreductase) transfers electrons from ubiquinol to cytochrome c; its activity is a rate-limiting step in overall mitochondrial electron transport chain flux. Loss-of-function of Complex III assembly factor BCS1L reduces Complex III activity and consequently the overall respiratory chain activity."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_complex_iv_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_respiratory_chain_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amitochondrial_complex_iv_activity%3Apositive%3Amitochondrial_respiratory_chain_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_complex_iv_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_respiratory_chain_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Mitochondrial Complex IV deficiency, nuclear type 1 (OMIM 220110 / SURF1 deficiency): biallelic SURF1 mutations abolish COX assembly and reduce Complex IV and overall respiratory chain activity in fibroblasts and muscle (OMIM 220110). Leigh syndrome phenotype. Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry ch. 19 (terminal oxidase of ETC). | intervention: do(SURF1/COX-assembly-gene LoF) -> reduced Complex IV activity -> reduced overall respiratory chain activity"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Complex IV (cytochrome c oxidase, COX) is the terminal electron acceptor of the respiratory chain, transferring electrons from cytochrome c to molecular oxygen. Its activity is essential for overall chain flux; COX deficiency directly reduces total respiratory chain electron transport activity."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pyruvate_carboxylase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_lactate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apyruvate_carboxylase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_lactate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pyruvate_carboxylase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_lactate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Pyruvate carboxylase deficiency (OMIM 266150): biallelic PC mutations cause severe lactic acidemia with elevated plasma lactate and pyruvate (OMIM 266150 clinical synopsis; PMC5471145 'Pyruvate carboxylase deficiency: An underestimated cause of lactic acidosis'). Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry ch. 16 (anaplerosis). | intervention: do(PC LoF) -> pyruvate carboxylase activity falls -> pyruvate accumulates and is reduced to lactate -> plasma lactate elevated"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Pyruvate carboxylase (PC) converts pyruvate to oxaloacetate (OAA) for anaplerotic entry into the TCA cycle and gluconeogenesis. PC deficiency blocks this conversion, causing pyruvate to accumulate and be shunted to lactate via lactate dehydrogenase, causing lactic acidosis. More PC activity means less pyruvate is diverted to lactate."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pyruvate_carboxylase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_pyruvate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apyruvate_carboxylase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_pyruvate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pyruvate_carboxylase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_pyruvate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Pyruvate carboxylase deficiency (OMIM 266150): elevated plasma pyruvate alongside lactic acidosis is a diagnostic feature (OMIM 266150; PMC2572257 'Molecular Basis of Pyruvate Carboxylase Deficiency'). Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry ch. 16. | intervention: do(PC LoF) -> pyruvate not converted to OAA -> plasma pyruvate accumulates"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Pyruvate carboxylase converts pyruvate to OAA; deficiency reduces clearance of pyruvate, causing pyruvate to accumulate in plasma. More enzyme lowers plasma pyruvate by channeling it into the TCA cycle."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/malic_enzyme_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_malic_acid> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amalic_enzyme_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_malic_acid> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/malic_enzyme_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_malic_acid> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "ME2 depletion by siRNA in human cells causes malate accumulation and inhibition of cellular respiration (PMC10202922: 'Suppression of the human malic enzyme 2 modifies energy metabolism and inhibits cellular respiration'). HPO HP:0003232 'Mitochondrial malic enzyme reduced' captures the clinical enzyme-activity phenotype. Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry ch. 16 (malate as TCA intermediate; malic enzyme cataplerotic role). | intervention: do(ME2 knockdown / deficiency) -> malic enzyme activity falls -> malate accumulates -> urinary malate elevated"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Mitochondrial malic enzyme (ME2) catalyzes the oxidative decarboxylation of malate to pyruvate and CO2 (with NADH or NADPH production). Reduced ME2 activity decreases conversion of malate to pyruvate, causing malate to accumulate and spill into urine. More enzyme clears malate faster, lowering urinary malate."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dhapat_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_plasmalogen> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Adhapat_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_plasmalogen> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dhapat_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_plasmalogen> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Rhizomelic chondrodysplasia punctata type 2 / RCDP2 (OMIM 222765): biallelic GNPAT mutations abolish DHAPAT activity, causing severely reduced plasmalogen levels in plasma and deficient plasmalogen synthesis in cultured skin fibroblasts (OMIM 222765; PMC10264737 neonatal RCDP type 2). Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry ch. 21 (ether-lipid/plasmalogen biosynthesis in peroxisomes). | intervention: do(GNPAT LoF) -> DHAPAT activity falls -> plasmalogen synthesis blocked -> plasma plasmalogen reduced"
    ; pmo:mechanism "DHAPAT (glyceronephosphate O-acyltransferase, GNPAT) catalyzes the first committed step of plasmalogen biosynthesis in peroxisomes: acylation of dihydroxyacetone phosphate (DHAP). GNPAT deficiency blocks plasmalogen synthesis, reducing plasma plasmalogen levels. More DHAPAT activity drives more plasmalogen production."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alkyl_dhap_synthase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_plasmalogen> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aalkyl_dhap_synthase_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_plasmalogen> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alkyl_dhap_synthase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_plasmalogen> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Rhizomelic chondrodysplasia punctata type 3 / RCDP3 (OMIM 600121): biallelic AGPS mutations abolish alkyl-DHAP synthase activity, causing severely reduced plasmalogen levels (OMIM 600121; PMC8765576 novel AGPS variant causing RCDP3). Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry ch. 21 (peroxisomal ether-lipid biosynthesis pathway). | intervention: do(AGPS LoF) -> alkyl-DHAP synthase activity falls -> plasmalogen synthesis blocked -> plasma plasmalogen reduced"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Alkyl-DHAP synthase (AGPS) catalyzes the second step of plasmalogen biosynthesis, converting the acyl-DHAP produced by DHAPAT to the 1-alkyl-DHAP backbone required for plasmalogen synthesis. AGPS deficiency blocks the pathway downstream of DHAPAT, equally abolishing plasmalogen production. More AGPS activity supports more plasmalogen synthesis."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hmg_coa_lyase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_3_hydroxy_3_methylglutarate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahmg_coa_lyase_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_3_hydroxy_3_methylglutarate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hmg_coa_lyase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_3_hydroxy_3_methylglutarate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "HMG-CoA lyase deficiency (HMGCLD; OMIM 246450): biallelic HMGCL mutations cause greatly increased urinary excretion of 3-hydroxy-3-methylglutaric acid as the cardinal biochemical finding (OMIM 246450; PMC9136170 'HMG-CoA Lyase Deficiency: A Retrospective Study of 62 Saudi Patients'; PMC3685558 neonatal HMGCL case). Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry ch. 18 (leucine catabolism; HMG-CoA lyase). | intervention: do(HMGCL LoF) -> HMG-CoA lyase activity falls -> 3-hydroxy-3-methylglutarate accumulates -> elevated urinary excretion"
    ; pmo:mechanism "HMG-CoA lyase cleaves 3-hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl-CoA (HMG-CoA) to acetoacetate and acetyl-CoA, the terminal step of leucine catabolism and hepatic ketogenesis. Deficiency causes HMG-CoA (and its hydrolysis product 3-hydroxy-3-methylglutaric acid) to accumulate and be excreted in urine. More lyase activity clears this substrate, lowering urinary 3-hydroxy-3-methylglutarate."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hmg_coa_lyase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ketone_bodies> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahmg_coa_lyase_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_ketone_bodies> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hmg_coa_lyase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ketone_bodies> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "HMG-CoA lyase deficiency (HMGCLD; OMIM 246450): hypoketotic hypoglycemia is the hallmark clinical presentation — patients have low plasma ketone bodies despite fasting and elevated FFA because HMG-CoA lyase is required for hepatic ketogenesis (OMIM 246450; PMC9136170; Frontiers in Genetics 2022 doi:10.3389/fgene.2022.880464). Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry ch. 17 (ketogenesis pathway; HMG-CoA lyase as the ketone-forming step). | intervention: do(HMGCL LoF) -> HMG-CoA lyase activity falls -> ketone body synthesis blocked -> plasma ketone bodies fall (hypoketotic)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "HMG-CoA lyase is the enzyme that produces acetoacetate (and thereby all ketone bodies) from HMG-CoA in the liver. Deficiency blocks ketone body production, causing hypoketotic hypoglycemia during catabolic crises: despite elevated free fatty acids (the usual ketogenic substrate), ketone bodies cannot be formed. More lyase activity generates more acetoacetate and ketone bodies."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/t2_thiolase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_acetoacetate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3At2_thiolase_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_acetoacetate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/t2_thiolase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_acetoacetate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Beta-ketothiolase / T2 deficiency (alpha-methylacetoacetic aciduria; OMIM 203750): elevated urinary ketone bodies including acetoacetate during metabolic crisis is a key marker (OMIM 203750; PMC6790690 'Mutation update on ACAT1 variants associated with T2 deficiency'; PMC8900828 case report). Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry ch. 17 (ketone body metabolism; T2 thiolase in acetoacetyl-CoA utilization). | intervention: do(ACAT1 LoF) -> T2 thiolase activity falls -> acetoacetyl-CoA not cleaved -> acetoacetate accumulates -> urinary acetoacetate elevated"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Mitochondrial acetoacetyl-CoA thiolase (T2/ACAT1) catalyzes the thiolytic cleavage of acetoacetyl-CoA to two acetyl-CoA molecules (ketone body utilization step in peripheral tissues) and also the cleavage of 2-methylacetoacetyl-CoA in isoleucine catabolism. T2 deficiency impairs ketone body utilization, causing acetoacetyl-CoA and acetoacetate to accumulate and be excreted. More T2 activity clears acetoacetate/acetoacetyl-CoA faster."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/t2_thiolase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_tiglylglycine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3At2_thiolase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_tiglylglycine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/t2_thiolase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_tiglylglycine> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "T2 deficiency (OMIM 203750): tiglylglycine (tiglyl-glycine, TIG) in plasma and urine is the characteristic isoleucine-catabolic marker — together with 2-methyl-3-hydroxybutyrate, it distinguishes T2 deficiency (OMIM 203750 clinical synopsis; PMC3509868 three Japanese patients — key biochemical findings; Aramaki 1991 J Inherit Metab Dis doi:10.1007/BF01804391). Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry ch. 18 (isoleucine catabolism). | intervention: do(ACAT1 LoF) -> T2 thiolase activity falls -> 2-methylacetoacetyl-CoA not cleaved -> tiglyl-CoA accumulates -> tiglylglycine elevated in plasma"
    ; pmo:mechanism "T2 thiolase also catalyzes the thiolytic cleavage of 2-methylacetoacetyl-CoA in isoleucine catabolism. Deficiency causes upstream accumulation of tiglyl-CoA (formed from 2-methylacetoacetyl-CoA), which is conjugated with glycine to form tiglylglycine for excretion. More T2 activity clears the isoleucine catabolic intermediate faster, lowering tiglylglycine."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sulfide_quinone_oxidoreductase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_thiosulfate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asulfide_quinone_oxidoreductase_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_thiosulfate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sulfide_quinone_oxidoreductase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_thiosulfate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "SQOR deficiency (SQORD; OMIM 619221): SQOR LoF impairs H2S oxidation; the pathway from H2S via persulfide to thiosulfate is blocked, reducing thiosulfate production (OMIM 619221; PMC7484123 'Pathogenic variants in SQOR are a potentially treatable cause of Leigh disease'; Ziosi et al. Frontiers in Physiology 2017 doi:10.3389/fphys.2017.00525). Thiosulfate is the stable biomarker of the H2S oxidation pathway output. | intervention: do(SQOR LoF) -> SQOR activity falls -> H2S oxidation to persulfide reduced -> thiosulfate production falls -> plasma thiosulfate reduced"
    ; pmo:mechanism "SQOR (sulfide:quinone oxidoreductase) is the first enzyme of the mitochondrial hydrogen sulfide oxidation pathway: it oxidizes H2S to a persulfide, transferring electrons to coenzyme Q. The persulfide is subsequently converted to thiosulfate by TST (rhodanese). SQOR deficiency reduces the flux through this pathway, decreasing thiosulfate production from H2S. More SQOR activity produces more thiosulfate."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_transcobalamin_1> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_vitamin_b12> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_transcobalamin_1%3Apositive%3Aplasma_vitamin_b12> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_transcobalamin_1> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_vitamin_b12> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "TCN1 deficiency OMIM 193090; PMID 19686235 (Nexo et al., Br J Haematol 2009: TCN1 mutations cause mildly to severely low serum cobalamin levels); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (plasma cobalamin transport: haptocorrin carries >70% of circulating B12). | intervention: TCN1 loss-of-function (OMIM 193090): serum cobalamin falls proportionally to degree of haptocorrin deficiency [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Transcobalamin I (haptocorrin) binds more than 70% of circulating cobalamin in plasma; TCN1 loss-of-function markedly reduces the total serum cobalamin concentration (while intracellular cobalamin status remains normal because cellular delivery depends on transcobalamin II). Therefore plasma_transcobalamin_1 concentration is a positive determinant of plasma_vitamin_b12 (total serum cobalamin)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bco1_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_beta_carotene> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abco1_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_beta_carotene> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bco1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_beta_carotene> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Hypercarotenemia and vitamin A deficiency (HCVAD) OMIM 115300, caused by heterozygous BCO1 (BCMO1) mutation T170M with ~90% reduced enzymatic activity leading to elevated serum beta-carotene; Molecular and dietary regulation of BCMO1 (von Lintig, Arch Biochem Biophys 2010); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (carotenoid metabolism, beta-carotene as provitamin A). | intervention: BCO1/BCMO1 loss-of-function (OMIM 115300, hypercarotenemia): plasma beta-carotene rises markedly"
    ; pmo:mechanism "BCO1 (beta,beta-carotene 15,15'-dioxygenase) catalyzes the oxidative cleavage of beta-carotene to two molecules of retinal, which is the major catabolic route for circulating beta-carotene. Reduced BCO1 activity impairs this cleavage, causing beta-carotene to accumulate in plasma (hypercarotenemia). Edge sign is negative: more enzyme activity lowers plasma beta-carotene."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ttpa_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tissue_tocopherol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Attpa_activity%3Apositive%3Atissue_tocopherol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ttpa_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tissue_tocopherol> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Ataxia with vitamin E deficiency (AVED) OMIM 277460; GeneReviews NBK1241 (TTPA LoF causes low plasma and tissue tocopherol); OMIM 600415 (TTPA gene); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (fat-soluble vitamin transport; alpha-TTP role in hepatic tocopherol secretion). | intervention: TTPA loss-of-function (AVED, OMIM 277460): plasma and adipose-tissue alpha-tocopherol falls to very low levels"
    ; pmo:mechanism "The alpha-tocopherol transfer protein (alpha-TTP, encoded by TTPA) selectively binds alpha-tocopherol in hepatocytes and facilitates its transfer to nascent VLDL for secretion into plasma, enabling systemic distribution to peripheral tissues including adipose. TTPA loss-of-function ablates this export step, causing progressive depletion of alpha-tocopherol in plasma and all tissues. More TTPA activity maintains higher tissue tocopherol levels."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nadsyn1_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_nad> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Anadsyn1_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_nad> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nadsyn1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_nad> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Congenital NAD deficiency disorder OMIM 618845 (NADSYN1 biallelic LoF); Shi et al. NEJM 2017 (NAD deficiency, congenital malformations, niacin supplementation); PMC10866660 metabolic signature for NADSYN1-dependent CNDD; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (NAD biosynthesis: de novo from tryptophan via kynurenine; Preiss-Handler pathway). | intervention: NADSYN1 biallelic loss-of-function (OMIM 618845): NAD+ levels fall in blood and tissues"
    ; pmo:mechanism "NADSYN1 encodes NAD(+) synthetase 1, the final enzymatic step converting nicotinic acid mononucleotide/NaMN to NAD+ via the Preiss-Handler pathway and the de novo kynurenine/tryptophan pathway. Biallelic NADSYN1 loss-of-function blocks both pathways simultaneously causing congenital NAD deficiency disorder (CNDD); NADSYN1 activity is the rate-limiting determinant of intracellular and circulating NAD pool size."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nadsyn1_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_nadh> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Anadsyn1_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_nadh> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nadsyn1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_nadh> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Congenital NAD deficiency disorder OMIM 618845; PMC10866660 metabolic signature for NADSYN1-CNDD (reduced total NAD pool including NADH fraction); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (NAD+/NADH as redox couple; pool size set by biosynthesis). | intervention: NADSYN1 biallelic loss-of-function (OMIM 618845): total NAD pool (NAD+ and NADH) falls"
    ; pmo:mechanism "NADH is the reduced form of NAD+; the total cellular NAD pool (NAD+ + NADH) is set by biosynthetic capacity. NADSYN1 LoF reduces total NAD pool, which necessarily reduces steady-state NADH as well. The sign is positive: more NADSYN1 activity supports a larger total NAD pool and therefore more NADH."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/coq2_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/muscle_coenzyme_q10> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acoq2_activity%3Apositive%3Amuscle_coenzyme_q10> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/coq2_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/muscle_coenzyme_q10> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Primary coenzyme Q10 deficiency type 1 OMIM 607426 (COQ2 mutation as founding cause); Quinzii et al. AJHG 2006 PMID 16400613 (COQ2 Y297C mutation causes primary CoQ10 deficiency); GeneReviews NBK410087 (primary CoQ10 deficiency overview); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (ubiquinone biosynthesis in mitochondria). | intervention: COQ2 loss-of-function (primary CoQ10 deficiency, OMIM 607426): skeletal-muscle CoQ10 concentration falls [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "COQ2 (4-hydroxybenzoate polyprenyltransferase) catalyzes an early committed step in the mitochondrial coenzyme Q10 (ubiquinone) biosynthetic pathway, transferring the polyprenyl tail onto 4-hydroxybenzoate. COQ2 loss-of-function causes primary CoQ10 deficiency, with reduced ubiquinone levels in skeletal muscle and other tissues. More COQ2 activity supports higher tissue CoQ10 content."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lias_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/muscle_lipoic_acid> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alias_activity%3Apositive%3Amuscle_lipoic_acid> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lias_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/muscle_lipoic_acid> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Hyperglycinemia, lactic acidosis, and seizures (HGCLAS) OMIM 614462 (LIAS biallelic LoF); PMC3234378 lipoic acid synthetase deficiency (neonatal epilepsy, defective mitochondrial energy metabolism); Mayr et al. JIMD 2014 PMID 24777537 (lipoic acid biosynthesis defects); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (lipoic acid as prosthetic group of pyruvate dehydrogenase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase). | intervention: LIAS biallelic loss-of-function (OMIM 614462): tissue lipoic acid content falls, mitochondrial enzyme complexes lose cofactor [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LIAS (lipoyl synthase, mitochondrial) catalyzes the final step of de novo lipoic acid biosynthesis in mitochondria, inserting two sulfur atoms into the octanoyl precursor chain attached to lipoyl carrier proteins. LIAS loss-of-function abolishes endogenous lipoic acid synthesis, causing hyperglycinemia, lactic acidosis, and seizures (HGCLAS) and severely reducing tissue lipoic acid content including in skeletal muscle."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pnpo_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_pyridoxal_phosphate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apnpo_activity%3Apositive%3Acsf_pyridoxal_phosphate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pnpo_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_pyridoxal_phosphate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "PNPO deficiency OMIM 610090; OMIM 603287 (PNPO gene); PMC7820968 phenotypic and molecular spectrum of PNPO deficiency (87 cases; low CSF PLP is diagnostic hallmark); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (vitamin B6 metabolism: PNP/PMP oxidation to PLP by PNPO). | intervention: PNPO biallelic loss-of-function (OMIM 610090): CSF pyridoxal-5'-phosphate concentration falls to very low levels; neonatal seizures respond to PLP but not pyridoxine [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "PNPO (pyridoxamine-5'-phosphate oxidase; pyridoxine-5'-phosphate oxidase) catalyzes the FMN-dependent oxidation of pyridoxamine-5'-phosphate (PMP) and pyridoxine-5'-phosphate (PNP) to pyridoxal-5'-phosphate (PLP), the active coenzyme form of vitamin B6. PNPO loss-of-function blocks PLP synthesis, causing markedly reduced CSF pyridoxal-5'-phosphate concentration, which is the biochemical hallmark of this neonatal epileptic encephalopathy."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/holocarboxylase_synthetase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/propionyl_coa_carboxylase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aholocarboxylase_synthetase_activity%3Apositive%3Apropionyl_coa_carboxylase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/holocarboxylase_synthetase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/propionyl_coa_carboxylase_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Holocarboxylase synthetase deficiency OMIM 253270; OMIM 609018 (HLCS gene); PMC2630166 management of HLCS deficiency (multiple carboxylase deficiency: impaired PCC, MCC, and pyruvate carboxylase activities with normal serum biotin); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (biotin as cofactor for carboxylases; HLCS biotinylates apo-carboxylases). | intervention: HLCS biallelic loss-of-function (OMIM 253270): propionyl-CoA carboxylase activity falls markedly despite normal plasma biotin; propionic acidemia-like metabolic pattern"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Holocarboxylase synthetase (HLCS) covalently attaches biotin to the biotin-carboxyl carrier protein subunits of all five biotin-dependent carboxylases, including propionyl-CoA carboxylase (PCC). Without HLCS activity, apo-PCC is not converted to holo-PCC and remains catalytically inactive. HLCS loss-of-function causes multiple carboxylase deficiency including severely impaired PCC activity, even when free biotin levels are normal."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ptps_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_tetrahydrobiopterin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aptps_activity%3Apositive%3Acsf_tetrahydrobiopterin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ptps_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_tetrahydrobiopterin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "PTPS deficiency OMIM 261640; GeneReviews NBK616086 (PTS-related BH4 deficiency); Consensus guideline BH4 deficiencies (Orphanet J Rare Dis 2020); high urinary/CSF neopterin with low biopterin/BH4 is the diagnostic hallmark of PTPS deficiency; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (tetrahydrobiopterin de novo biosynthesis). | intervention: PTS (PTPS) biallelic loss-of-function: CSF BH4 (tetrahydrobiopterin) falls to very low levels; the most common BH4 deficiency (60% of cases)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "PTPS (6-pyruvoyltetrahydropterin synthase) converts 7,8-dihydroneopterin triphosphate to 6-pyruvoyl-tetrahydropterin, the immediate precursor of tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4). PTPS is the rate-limiting step of de novo BH4 biosynthesis. PTPS loss-of-function causes severe BH4 deficiency with very low CSF tetrahydrobiopterin, impaired neurotransmitter synthesis, and hyperphenylalaninemia. More PTPS activity supports higher CSF BH4."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ptps_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_neopterin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aptps_activity%3Anegative%3Acsf_neopterin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ptps_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_neopterin> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "PTPS deficiency OMIM 261640; retrospective analysis 19 PTPS-deficiency patients PMC7749858 (high neopterin with low biopterin is pathognomonic); consensus guideline BH4 deficiencies 2020 (elevated neopterin, reduced biopterin ratio diagnostic of PTPSD); Blau et al. Physician's Guide to Inherited Metabolic Diseases (neopterin rises in PTPS deficiency because dihydroneopterin-TP substrate backs up). | intervention: PTS (PTPS) biallelic loss-of-function: CSF and urinary neopterin rises markedly while BH4 falls"
    ; pmo:mechanism "When PTPS activity is low, the substrate 7,8-dihydroneopterin triphosphate accumulates and is dephosphorylated and oxidized to neopterin, which accumulates in urine and CSF. More PTPS activity diverts this intermediate toward BH4 production, reducing neopterin accumulation. The comparative-static sign of PTPS activity on CSF neopterin is therefore negative (enzyme clears the substrate branch-point, reducing neopterin side-product)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sepiapterin_reductase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_sepiapterin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asepiapterin_reductase_activity%3Anegative%3Acsf_sepiapterin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sepiapterin_reductase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_sepiapterin> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Sepiapterin reductase deficiency OMIM 612716; OMIM 182125 (SPR gene); PMC10996339 sepiapterin reductase deficiency case review (pathognomonic CSF finding: elevated sepiapterin, low BH4, low HVA and 5-HIAA); Blau et al. Physician's Guide to Inherited Metabolic Diseases (sepiapterin accumulation in SPR deficiency); hpo_csf_markers edge csf_sepiapterin -> csf_dihydrobiopterin already encodes this step's product. | intervention: SPR biallelic loss-of-function (OMIM 612716): CSF sepiapterin rises markedly (pathognomonic finding); BH4 falls; dopa-responsive dystonia"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Sepiapterin reductase (SPR) reduces sepiapterin (an intermediate of the de novo BH4 pathway) to 7,8-dihydrobiopterin (BH2) as part of the salvage/de novo synthesis route to BH4. SPR loss-of-function causes sepiapterin to accumulate in CSF because it cannot be reduced. More SPR activity clears sepiapterin (negative sign: enzyme removes substrate)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sepiapterin_reductase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_tetrahydrobiopterin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asepiapterin_reductase_activity%3Apositive%3Acsf_tetrahydrobiopterin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sepiapterin_reductase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_tetrahydrobiopterin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Sepiapterin reductase deficiency OMIM 612716; PMC10996339 (SPR deficiency: low BH4, low HVA and 5-HIAA in CSF; dopa-responsive dystonia due to BH4 deficiency); hpo_csf_markers fragment edge csf_dihydrobiopterin -> csf_tetrahydrobiopterin encodes the next step; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (tetrahydrobiopterin biosynthesis and regeneration). | intervention: SPR biallelic loss-of-function (OMIM 612716): CSF BH4 (tetrahydrobiopterin) falls to very low levels"
    ; pmo:mechanism "SPR is required for the final reduction steps in de novo BH4 biosynthesis (sepiapterin -> BH2 -> BH4 via SPR and DHPR/DHFR). SPR loss-of-function causes severe CSF BH4 deficiency because the de novo route is blocked at this step, even though neopterin pathway flux upstream is normal. More SPR activity supports higher CSF BH4 production."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/slc30a10_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_manganese> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aslc30a10_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_manganese> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/slc30a10_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_manganese> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "OMIM 255800 (hypermanganesemia with dystonia, polycythemia and cirrhosis, HMNDYT1; SLC30A10 LoF); Tuschl et al. 2012 PMC3309187 (homozygous SLC30A10 mutations cause hepatic cirrhosis, dystonia, polycythemia, hypermanganesemia); Xia et al. 2017 PMC5524415 (SLC30A10 as Mn efflux transporter); Nature Communications 2025 (molecular mechanisms of SLC30A10-mediated Mn transport); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (manganese homeostasis: hepatobiliary excretion as principal elimination route). | intervention: SLC30A10 loss-of-function (autosomal-recessive, OMIM 255800)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "SLC30A10 (ZnT10) is a Mn efflux transporter expressed at the surface of hepatocytes and enterocytes that exports Mn from cells into bile (hepatic) and the intestinal lumen (gut), constituting the primary route of Mn elimination. LoF abolishes biliary and intestinal Mn export, trapping Mn in the body and causing blood hypermanganesemia. Intervention: do(SLC30A10 LoF) -> blood Mn rises."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/slc39a14_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_manganese> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aslc39a14_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_manganese> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/slc39a14_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_manganese> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "OMIM 617013 (hypermanganesemia with dystonia 2, HMNDYT2; SLC39A14 LoF); GeneReviews NBK431123 (SLC39A14 deficiency impairs hepatic Mn uptake and biliary excretion, resulting in Mn accumulation in circulation and brain; whole-blood Mn markedly elevated >1000 nmol/L); Rojas et al. 2018 PMC5791243 (SLC39A14 mutations cause hypermanganesemia); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (hepatic manganese clearance). | intervention: SLC39A14 loss-of-function (autosomal-recessive, OMIM 617013)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ZIP14 (SLC39A14) is a Mn uptake transporter expressed on hepatocytes that imports Mn from portal blood into liver cells, enabling hepatic Mn clearance via biliary excretion (through SLC30A10). When ZIP14 activity is lost, hepatocytes cannot take up plasma Mn for biliary excretion; Mn accumulates in the circulation and brain (hypermanganesemia). Intervention: do(SLC39A14 LoF) -> blood Mn rises."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/slc39a8_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_manganese> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aslc39a8_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_manganese> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/slc39a8_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_manganese> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "OMIM 616721 (SLC39A8-CDG, CDG type IIn; SLC39A8 LoF causing hypoManganesemia); Ng et al. 2015 PMID 26637979 (SLC39A8 deficiency: disorder of Mn transport and glycosylation; low serum Mn); PMC12698256 (intestinal ZIP8 regulates Mn distribution; impaired intestinal absorption of dietary Mn in Slc39a8-IEC knockout mice; 54Mn in blood 33% lower); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (SLC39A8-CDG: hypoManganesemia). | intervention: SLC39A8 loss-of-function (autosomal-recessive CDG, OMIM 616721) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ZIP8 (SLC39A8) is localized to the apical membrane of enterocytes and mediates dietary Mn absorption from the intestinal lumen into enterocytes, making it the principal intestinal Mn absorption transporter. LoF reduces intestinal Mn absorption so less dietary Mn enters the body, leading to blood hypoManganesemia. Radiotracer studies in Slc39a8 intestinal-epithelial knockout mice show 33% lower blood 54Mn than controls. Intervention: do(SLC39A8 LoF) -> blood Mn falls."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_manganese> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_manganese> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_manganese%3Apositive%3Aurinary_manganese> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_manganese> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_manganese> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "OMIM 255800 and OMIM 617013 (urinary Mn elevated in hypermanganesemia disorders — HPO HP:6000480 'Elevated urine manganese level'); Tuschl et al. 2012 PMC3664426 (SLC30A10 LoF hypermanganesemia: chelation therapy increases urinary Mn excretion and decreases plasma Mn — documents plasma->urine causal link); Frontera et al. 2021 PMC8633869 (SLC30A10 mutation: elevated urinary Mn under chelation); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (urinary Mn rises in manganese overload states). | intervention: Endogenous: SLC30A10/SLC39A14 LoF raising plasma Mn (OMIM 255800/617013); exogenous: chelation therapy mobilising Mn to urine"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Although the dominant route of Mn elimination is biliary/fecal, a fraction of plasma Mn is freely filtered at the glomerulus and excreted in urine; at pathologically elevated plasma Mn concentrations (hypermanganesemia) urinary Mn excretion increases proportionally. Under chelation therapy (which mobilises tissue and plasma Mn), urinary Mn excretion rises dramatically, documenting the plasma->urine causal link at elevated plasma loads. Intervention: do(SLC30A10 LoF) -> plasma Mn rises -> urinary Mn rises."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/secisbp2_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_selenium> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asecisbp2_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_selenium> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/secisbp2_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_selenium> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "OMIM 609698 (thyroid hormone metabolism abnormal 1, THMA1; SECISBP2 LoF; multisystem selenoprotein deficiency); Dumitrescu et al. 2005 (SECISBP2 deficiency reduces synthesis of most of the 25 human selenoproteins, including SELENOP; low circulating selenoproteins); PMC6154453 and PMC12411517 (SECISBP2 mutation: reduced plasma selenium levels with multiorgan selenoprotein deficiency); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (SECISBP2/SBP2: essential for selenocysteine insertion into selenoproteins; SELENOP is major plasma Se carrier). | intervention: SECISBP2 loss-of-function (autosomal-recessive, OMIM 609698) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "SECISBP2 (SBP2) is required for co-translational insertion of selenocysteine into all 25 human selenoproteins, including selenoprotein P (SELENOP/SEPP1), which is the principal plasma selenium transport protein secreted by hepatocytes. SECISBP2 LoF globally reduces selenoprotein synthesis; reduced SELENOP secretion lowers the amount of selenium delivered to and maintained in plasma. Intervention: do(SECISBP2 LoF) -> impaired selenoprotein synthesis -> low circulating SELENOP -> low plasma selenium."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_zinc> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_zinc> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_zinc%3Apositive%3Aurinary_zinc> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_zinc> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_zinc> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (renal handling of trace metals: glomerular filtration of the non-protein-bound fraction sets urinary excretion); King 2011 Journal of Nutrition (zinc homeostasis: urinary Zn excretion tracks plasma Zn and adjusts with intake changes, 100-fold range documented); Oxford NDT 2020 (renal handling of zinc: serum zinc level correlates with urinary zinc in clinical cohorts); analogous canonical renal mechanism to plasma_copper -> urinary_copper already encoded in this fragment. | intervention: Dietary/interventional zinc loading; hyperzincemia states; clinical urinary zinc monitoring"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Zinc not bound to large plasma proteins is filtered at the glomerulus and a fraction is excreted in urine; at elevated plasma Zn concentrations (e.g., in hyperzincemia states or after excess dietary intake), the filterable Zn load increases proportionally, raising urinary Zn excretion. This renal excretion pathway is the same mechanism as for copper (already encoded: plasma_copper -> urinary_copper). Urinary Zn tracks plasma Zn in clinical assessments of zinc status."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/slc30a2_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/breast_milk_zinc> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aslc30a2_activity%3Apositive%3Abreast_milk_zinc> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/slc30a2_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/breast_milk_zinc> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "OMIM 608118 (zinc deficiency, transient neonatal, TNZD; caused by SLC30A2 LoF mutations in the mother); Chowanadisai et al. 2006 / Itsumura et al. 2013 PMC3669329 (compound heterozygous SLC30A2/ZnT2 mutations result in low milk Zn concentrations; ZnT2 mobilises Zn into secretory vesicles in mammary epithelial cells, which is secreted into breast milk); PMC10030689 (novel SLC30A2 mutations in transient neonatal zinc deficiency: ZnT2 essential for mammary gland Zn secretion); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (transient neonatal zinc deficiency due to low breast milk zinc). | intervention: SLC30A2 loss-of-function in lactating mother (OMIM 608118)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ZnT2 (SLC30A2) is expressed in the secretory vesicles of mammary gland epithelial cells and transports Zn from the cytoplasm into secretory vesicles, which fuse with the apical membrane to release Zn into breast milk. ZnT2 LoF reduces Zn secretion into breast milk, causing transient neonatal zinc deficiency in the nursing infant. Intervention: do(SLC30A2 LoF heterozygous or homozygous in lactating mothers) -> breast milk Zn >75% reduced."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nis_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_iodine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Anis_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_iodine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nis_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_iodine> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "OMIM 274400 (thyroid dyshormonogenesis 1, TDH1; SLC5A5/NIS LoF causing congenital hypothyroidism with absent thyroidal radioiodine uptake); PMC11693440 and PMC8861941 (novel SLC5A5 mutations: NIS LoF causes dyshormonogenic congenital hypothyroidism with absent radioiodine uptake in thyroid); Wikipedia sodium/iodide cotransporter (NIS: 'The rest of the iodine which remains unlinked and free in the bloodstream is removed from the body through urine'); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (thyroidal iodide trapping by NIS is the first step of hormone synthesis; urinary iodine excretion reflects the iodide not captured by the thyroid); Williams Textbook of Endocrinology (NIS-mediated thyroid iodide uptake; urinary iodine as a measure of iodide bioavailability). | intervention: NIS (SLC5A5) loss-of-function (autosomal-recessive, OMIM 274400)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "NIS (sodium/iodide symporter, SLC5A5) on the apical membrane of thyroid follicular cells actively transports iodide from blood into thyroid follicular cells for incorporation into thyroid hormones; this is the first and rate-limiting step of thyroid hormone synthesis. When NIS activity is high, more iodide is cleared from blood into the thyroid, lowering the filtered iodide load and reducing urinary iodide excretion. When NIS is lost (LoF), the thyroid cannot trap iodide; radioiodine uptake is absent, plasma iodide remains elevated, and more iodide is excreted in urine. Intervention: do(NIS LoF) -> no thyroidal iodide trapping -> elevated urinary iodide."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nkcc2_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_chloride> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ankcc2_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_chloride> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nkcc2_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_chloride> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Bartter syndrome type 1 (OMIM 601678): biallelic SLC12A1 LoF mutations abolish NKCC2-mediated Cl reabsorption in the TAL, causing urinary salt (NaCl) wasting and polyuria. Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology (TAL NaCl reabsorption; NKCC2). PMID 12761241 (Karolyi et al.): all Bartter type I SLC12A1 mutations result in nonfunctional NKCC2 transporters. | intervention: do(NKCC2 activity DOWN) — Bartter syndrome type 1, SLC12A1 biallelic LoF [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb apically cotransports Na-K-2Cl from the filtrate into tubular cells, reabsorbing Cl from the lumen; higher NKCC2 activity increases tubular Cl reabsorption, reducing urinary Cl excretion. Loss-of-function mutations cause Bartter syndrome type 1 (salt-wasting hypercalciuria with urinary Cl loss)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_chloride> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_chloride> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_chloride%3Apositive%3Aurinary_chloride> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_chloride> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_chloride> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology (renal handling of chloride; filtered load principle). Rose & Post, Clinical Physiology of Acid-Base and Electrolyte Disorders (urinary Cl excretion). | intervention: do(plasma_chloride UP) — hyperchloremia raises filtered Cl load"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Urinary chloride excretion = filtered Cl load (plasma [Cl] x GFR) minus tubular reabsorption; raising plasma chloride raises the filtered load and thus urinary Cl excretion, all else equal."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/carbonic_anhydrase_ii_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urine_bicarbonate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acarbonic_anhydrase_ii_activity%3Anegative%3Aurine_bicarbonate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/carbonic_anhydrase_ii_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urine_bicarbonate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "CA2 deficiency (OMIM 259730, autosomal recessive osteopetrosis with RTA): CA2-knockout mice show bicarbonaturia and alkaline urine consistent with type III RTA. Sly et al., NEJM 1983 / PMID 6405388. Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology (proximal tubule bicarbonate reabsorption; role of CA2). | intervention: do(CA2 activity DOWN) — carbonic anhydrase II deficiency (OMIM 259730) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CA2 in proximal tubule cells catalyzes luminal CO2+H2O -> H2CO3 -> H+ + HCO3-; cytosolic CA2 also generates H+ for luminal NHE-3, driving bicarbonate reclamation. CA2 LoF impairs H+ secretion and bicarbonate reabsorption, causing proximal + distal bicarbonate wasting (bicarbonaturia). More CA2 activity = more bicarbonate reclaimed = less in urine."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bicarbonate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urine_bicarbonate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_bicarbonate%3Apositive%3Aurine_bicarbonate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bicarbonate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urine_bicarbonate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology (bicarbonate threshold; metabolic alkalosis -> bicarbonaturia). Rose & Post, Clinical Physiology of Acid-Base and Electrolyte Disorders (proximal RTA bicarbonate wasting). | intervention: do(plasma_bicarbonate UP) — metabolic alkalosis raises filtered HCO3- load above tubular threshold"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Urinary bicarbonate = filtered HCO3- load (plasma [HCO3-] x GFR) minus tubular reabsorption; above the tubular reabsorptive threshold (~24 mmol/L), excess filtered bicarbonate spills into the urine. Higher plasma bicarbonate (metabolic alkalosis) raises urinary bicarbonate excretion."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ketone_bodies> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/serum_osmolal_gap> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_ketone_bodies%3Apositive%3Aserum_osmolal_gap> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ketone_bodies> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/serum_osmolal_gap> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Rose & Post, Clinical Physiology of Acid-Base and Electrolyte Disorders (osmolal gap and unmeasured osmoles). UI Health Care Osmolal Gap reference: acetone listed as a cause of elevated osmolal gap. | intervention: do(plasma_ketone_bodies UP) — diabetic ketoacidosis or starvation ketosis raises acetone as an unmeasured osmole [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "The osmolal gap = measured serum osmolality (freezing-point depression) minus calculated osmolality (2x[Na] + glucose/18 + BUN/2.8). Acetone, produced by spontaneous decarboxylation of acetoacetate, is an electrically neutral, low-molecular-weight osmole not included in the standard calculation formula; it contributes to measured osmolality, raising the gap. Higher plasma ketone bodies produce more acetone and expand the osmolal gap."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_uromodulin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Agfr%3Apositive%3Aurinary_uromodulin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_uromodulin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Hojs et al. 2020 PMC10824655 (urinary uromodulin falls with CKD stage and correlates with GFR). Gast et al. 2018 NDT PMID 28605509 (UMOD mutations reduce urinary uromodulin in gene-dosage-dependent manner). Brenner & Rector's The Kidney (Tamm-Horsfall protein as marker of TAL tubular mass). | intervention: do(GFR DOWN) — CKD or experimental nephron ablation reduces urinary uromodulin [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Uromodulin is exclusively synthesized by cells of the thick ascending limb (TAL) and early distal tubule; urinary uromodulin excretion reflects the functional TAL nephron mass. As GFR declines in CKD (fewer functioning nephrons), the total mass of uromodulin-secreting TAL cells decreases, reducing urinary uromodulin. Higher GFR = more functioning TAL = more urinary uromodulin. Additionally, UMOD LoF mutations paradoxically reduce urinary uromodulin (protein retained in ER), consistent with the direction."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_ngal> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Agfr%3Anegative%3Aurinary_ngal> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_ngal> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Mori & Nakao, AJKD 2007 (NGAL as a marker of kidney damage). Paragas et al., Kidney Int 2011 PMID 32110797 (impaired tubular reabsorption is the main mechanism of elevated urinary NGAL after AKI). Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (NGAL as early AKI biomarker). | intervention: do(GFR DOWN via AKI) — cisplatin nephrotoxicity, ischemia-reperfusion injury, or contrast nephropathy consistently raise urinary NGAL [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GFR decline in acute kidney injury (AKI) reflects tubular cell injury; injured tubular cells in the thick ascending limb, distal tubule and collecting duct markedly upregulate LCN2 (NGAL) expression and secrete it into the urine. NGAL rises within 2 hours of ischemic or nephrotoxic AKI, before creatinine changes. Lower GFR from acute tubular injury reliably predicts higher urinary NGAL."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_orosomucoid> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Agfr%3Anegative%3Aurinary_orosomucoid> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_orosomucoid> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Wang et al., Nephrology 2020 (urinary orosomucoid 1 as biomarker of renal impairment in T2D, threshold 2.53 mg/L). Torffvit et al., Scand J Clin Lab Invest 2005 (monitoring urinary orosomucoid in acute inflammation with albuminuria). Brenner & Rector's The Kidney (tubular biomarkers in kidney disease). | intervention: do(GFR DOWN) — diabetic nephropathy or glomerulonephritis lowers GFR and raises urinary orosomucoid [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Orosomucoid (alpha-1-acid glycoprotein, MW ~41 kDa) is normally retained by the glomerular filtration barrier; glomerular injury accompanying GFR decline increases glomerular permeability, allowing orosomucoid to pass into the filtrate and appear in urine. Lower GFR indicates more glomerular dysfunction and more urinary orosomucoid. The relationship is well-documented in diabetic nephropathy and proteinuric CKD."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bile_acids> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ggt> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_bile_acids%3Apositive%3Aplasma_ggt> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bile_acids> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ggt> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "NCBI Bookshelf Clinical Methods, 'Alkaline Phosphatase and Gamma Glutamyltransferase' (Walker 1990) — increased plasma GGT in cholestasis attributed to bile acid-stimulated hepatobiliary GGT synthesis and membrane release. PMC3922898 (Ginguene et al., 'Gamma-Glutamyltransferase Fractions in Human Plasma and Bile', 2014) — GGT shed as membrane microvesicles; bile-acid micelle solubilization mechanism. eClinpath 'GGT' chapter — cholestasis causes membrane-associated GGT release into plasma. | intervention: do(plasma_bile_acids ↑) — pharmacological cholestasis model (bile duct ligation, bile shunting) reproducibly elevates plasma GGT 5–30-fold"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Elevated bile acid concentration induces hepatocyte and bile duct GGT1 membrane expression and solubilizes GGT from hepatocellular membranes into the systemic circulation; shunting bile into the systemic circulation of rats increases plasma GGT activity without structural cholestasis, demonstrating a bile-acid-specific induction independent of biliary pressure."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldehyde_dehydrogenase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_acetaldehyde> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aaldehyde_dehydrogenase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_acetaldehyde> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldehyde_dehydrogenase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_acetaldehyde> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "OMIM #610251 (Acute Alcohol Sensitivity) — ALDH2*2 (Glu487Lys) LoF variant causes elevated blood acetaldehyde and Asian flush syndrome; documented in PMC6804850 (Gene Therapy Correction of ALDH2 Deficiency, Mol Ther Methods Clin Dev 2019) and PMC6875787 (Aldehyde Dehydrogenase Deficiency, Alcohol Res 2019). Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, Ch. 17 — ethanol oxidation pathway: ethanol → (ADH) → acetaldehyde → (ALDH2) → acetate. | intervention: do(ALDH2 activity ↓) via ALDH2*2 homozygosity — blood acetaldehyde rises 5–20-fold after ethanol challenge compared to ALDH2*1/*1 individuals"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ALDH2 catalyzes the NAD+-dependent oxidation of acetaldehyde to acetate in hepatocyte mitochondria — the rate-limiting step in ethanol catabolism after ADH-mediated ethanol→acetaldehyde conversion. Reduced ALDH2 catalytic activity impairs acetaldehyde clearance, causing blood acetaldehyde to accumulate following ethanol ingestion."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_prekallikrein> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bradykinin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_prekallikrein%3Apositive%3Aplasma_bradykinin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_prekallikrein> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bradykinin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Inborn error: prekallikrein deficiency / Fletcher factor deficiency (KLKB1 LoF, OMIM 612423) — affected patients have severely reduced bradykinin generation and impaired contact-pathway activation; PMC8114252 (A Rare Cause of Isolated Prolonged aPTT: Prekallikrein Deficiency and the Contact System). Mechanism: Williams Hematology (contact activation: factor XII, prekallikrein, HMWK, bradykinin); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (Fletcher factor deficiency, prolonged aPTT without bleeding, impaired kallikrein-kinin pathway). | intervention: KLKB1 loss-of-function (prekallikrein deficiency, OMIM 612423): do(plasma_prekallikrein ↓) → plasma_bradykinin ↓, confirming sign '+'."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Plasma prekallikrein (KLKB1 gene product) is the zymogen of plasma kallikrein; factor XIIa activates prekallikrein to plasma kallikrein on negatively charged surfaces, and plasma kallikrein then cleaves the bradykinin nonapeptide from high-molecular-weight kininogen. A larger circulating prekallikrein pool supports proportionally more kallikrein activity and bradykinin generation at steady state."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_kininogen> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bradykinin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_kininogen%3Apositive%3Aplasma_bradykinin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_kininogen> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bradykinin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Inborn error: HMWK deficiency / Fitzgerald trait (KNG1 LoF, OMIM 228960) — patients have severely reduced bradykinin generation and abnormal surface-mediated fibrinolysis; PMID 1245795 (Kininogen deficiency in Fitzgerald trait: role of HMWK in clotting and fibrinolysis). Mechanism: Williams Hematology (HMWK as bradykinin precursor and contact-pathway cofactor); JACI review (Pathways for bradykinin formation and inflammatory disease). | intervention: KNG1 loss-of-function (HMWK deficiency / Fitzgerald trait, OMIM 228960): do(plasma_kininogen ↓) → plasma_bradykinin ↓, confirming sign '+'. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "High-molecular-weight kininogen (HMWK, kininogen-1; KNG1 gene product) is the direct substrate from which plasma kallikrein cleaves the bradykinin nonapeptide; it also acts as the non-enzymatic cofactor chaperoning both prekallikrein and factor XI onto activated surfaces. A larger circulating HMWK pool supports proportionally more bradykinin release at steady state."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adamts13_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/platelet_activation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aadamts13_activity%3Anegative%3Aplatelet_activation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adamts13_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/platelet_activation> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Inborn error: Upshaw-Schulman syndrome / congenital TTP (ADAMTS13 biallelic LoF mutations, OMIM 274150) — severe thrombocytopenia and microvascular platelet activation due to UL-VWF-driven platelet aggregation; Zheng et al. 2004 Blood 107:3161 (complete ADAMTS13 deficiency is prothrombotic); ASH Hematology Education 2012 (Role of ADAMTS13 in TTP pathogenesis). Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (TTP: ADAMTS13 deficiency, UL-VWF, platelet consumption). Williams Hematology (ADAMTS13 and VWF multimer size regulation). | intervention: ADAMTS13 biallelic loss-of-function (Upshaw-Schulman syndrome, OMIM 274150): do(adamts13_activity ↓) → UL-VWF accumulation → platelet_activation ↑, confirming sign '-'."
    ; pmo:mechanism "ADAMTS13 (a metalloprotease secreted mainly by hepatic stellate cells and endothelial cells) cleaves ultralarge VWF multimers into smaller, less reactive forms that cannot spontaneously capture platelets under arterial shear stress. When ADAMTS13 activity is high, UL-VWF strings are rapidly trimmed, limiting spontaneous VWF-platelet binding and platelet activation. When ADAMTS13 activity is low, UL-VWF accumulates on endothelial surfaces and in plasma, recruiting and activating platelets to form microvascular platelet thrombi."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_vitamin_k> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_pivka_ii> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_vitamin_k%3Anegative%3Aplasma_pivka_ii> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_vitamin_k> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_pivka_ii> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Pharmacological do: warfarin (vitamin K antagonist, blocks VKORC1) raises plasma PIVKA-II; vitamin K supplementation reverses PIVKA-II elevation in neonates and anticoagulated patients — PMID 22280352 (PIVKA-II as marker of subclinical vitamin K deficiency in term infants); PMID 2381266 (PIVKA-II and coagulation factors in warfarin-treated patients); PMID 3735800 (liver and plasma PIVKA-II in vitamin K deficiency in rats). Inborn error corroboration: VKCFD1 (GGCX biallelic mutations) → PIVKA-II accumulation, PMC2643352. Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (vitamin K-dependent gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors). | intervention: Pharmacological: warfarin do(plasma_vitamin_k effective ↓) → plasma_pivka_ii ↑, confirming sign '-'. Also: dietary vitamin K restriction or neonatal deficiency → PIVKA-II rises; vitamin K repletion → PIVKA-II falls. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Vitamin K is the obligatory cofactor and electron-donor substrate for the hepatic endoplasmic-reticulum enzyme gamma-glutamyl carboxylase (GGCX), which gamma-carboxylates glutamate residues on prothrombin (and factors VII, IX, X, protein C/S). When plasma vitamin K is sufficient, GGCX fully carboxylates prothrombin to its functional form; when vitamin K is low or pharmacologically blocked (warfarin inhibits the vitamin K epoxide reductase VKORC1, depleting reduced vitamin K), GGCX substrate is absent, carboxylation stalls, and under-carboxylated prothrombin (PIVKA-II / des-gamma-carboxy prothrombin) accumulates in plasma. Raising plasma vitamin K → restores GGCX activity → PIVKA-II falls."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_fibrinogen> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ail6%3Apositive%3Aplasma_fibrinogen> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_fibrinogen> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Mackiewicz et al. 1989 FEBS Lett 246(1-2):15 (ScienceDirect article pii/0014579389804764) confirms IL-6 as the major regulator of acute-phase protein synthesis in adult human hepatocytes; Dalmon et al. 1993 Mol Cell Biol 13(2):1183 (PMC359003) identifies the IL-6-responsive element in the human beta-fibrinogen promoter; Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 'The Acute Phase Response' | intervention: do(il6 high): systemic IL-6 elevation (e.g. experimental recombinant IL-6 infusion, sepsis) raises plasma fibrinogen within 24-48 h; do(il6 low): IL-6 blockade (tocilizumab) suppresses acute-phase fibrinogen rise [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "IL-6 signals through hepatocyte gp130/JAK-STAT3 and NF-IL6 to transcriptionally upregulate the FGA/FGB/FGG fibrinogen genes; fibrinogen is an archetypal positive acute-phase protein whose hepatic synthesis rises proportionally to IL-6 concentration"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_fibrinogen> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_sedimentation_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_fibrinogen%3Apositive%3Aerythrocyte_sedimentation_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_fibrinogen> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_sedimentation_rate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Afibrinogenemia (OMIM #202400; loss-of-function mutations in FGA/FGB/FGG): ESR is characteristically near-zero because fibrinogen is the dominant rouleaux promoter — well established in clinical haematology textbooks (Williams Hematology; Harrison's, 'Disorders of Fibrinogen'); StatPearls 'Erythrocyte Sedimentation Rate' (NBK557485): 'Any process that elevates fibrinogen levels may also increase the ESR'; quantitative log-linear relationship between [fibrinogen] and ESR demonstrated experimentally (Springer et al. 1997 Med Biol Eng Comput 35:57, doi:10.1007/BF02530035); hypofibrinogenemia causes markedly depressed ESR even during active inflammation (Cancer Therapy Advisor, Fibrinogen Deficiency — Congenital) | intervention: Genetic LoF: congenital afibrinogenemia (OMIM #202400) — ESR approaches zero mm/h; GoF: acute-phase fibrinogen elevation in infection/malignancy/pregnancy → elevated ESR"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Fibrinogen (an elongated, asymmetric plasma protein) reduces the zeta-potential between erythrocytes and promotes rouleaux formation by bridging adjacent red-cell surfaces; larger rouleaux settle faster by Stokes' law, accelerating ESR. Conversely, absence of fibrinogen abolishes rouleaux and ESR falls to near zero."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_iron> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/total_iron_binding_capacity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_iron%3Anegative%3Atotal_iron_binding_capacity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_iron> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/total_iron_binding_capacity> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Iron deficiency anaemia (IDA) — TIBC elevated (>360 µg/dL) with low serum iron is pathognomonic: Harrison's, 'Iron-Deficiency and Other Hypoproliferative Anaemias'; Bothwell et al. classical iron physiology; StatPearls 'Iron-Binding Capacity' (NBK559119); HFE hereditary haemochromatosis (OMIM #235200) — loss-of-function in HFE causes iron overload, plasma iron high, TIBC characteristically low-normal or below normal; Muckenthaler et al. 2017 Cell 168:344 (IRP/IRE system) confirms IRP1/2 regulation of transferrin synthesis in iron deficiency; Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology, 'Iron Metabolism' | intervention: do(plasma_iron low): iron deficiency anaemia — TIBC rises; do(plasma_iron high): HFE haemochromatosis or iron-dextran loading — TIBC falls"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Low plasma iron activates iron regulatory proteins (IRP1/IRP2) in hepatocytes, which bind the 3'-IRE of transferrin receptor mRNA (stabilising it) and the 5'-IRE of transferrin mRNA; hepatic transferrin synthesis is upregulated when iron is scarce, raising plasma transferrin and therefore TIBC. Conversely iron excess (high plasma iron / high transferrin saturation) suppresses IRP-mediated stabilisation, lowering transferrin synthesis and TIBC."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cytochrome_b5_reductase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_methemoglobin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acytochrome_b5_reductase_activity%3Anegative%3Aerythrocyte_methemoglobin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cytochrome_b5_reductase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythrocyte_methemoglobin> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Hereditary methemoglobinemia types I and II (OMIM #250800): autosomal-recessive LoF mutations in CYB5R3 cause metHb to rise to 10-70% of total haemoglobin depending on type (MedlinePlus Genetics CYB5R3); PMC3075332 (Moura et al. 2011) — two novel CYB5R3 mutations in type I; Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry, 'Hemoglobin: An Allosteric Protein' (metHb reduction by NADH-metHb reductase); Harrison's, 'Methaemoglobinaemia' | intervention: Genetic LoF: homozygous CYB5R3 mutation (OMIM #250800, type I/II recessive congenital methemoglobinemia) — erythrocyte metHb rises to 10-70%; pharmacological inhibition of the NADH pathway by dapsone/nitrites also raises metHb (phenocopies LoF) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "In erythrocytes, the soluble isoform of CYB5R3 (NADH-cytochrome b5 reductase) transfers electrons from NADH via cytochrome b5 to methaemoglobin (Fe3+ haem), reducing it back to functional oxyhaemoglobin (Fe2+ haem). This is the principal (>99%) pathway for disposal of spontaneously-oxidised metHb. When CYB5R3 activity is reduced, the metHb-to-Hb reduction is impaired and erythrocyte methemoglobin accumulates."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bicarbonate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/blood_base_excess> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_bicarbonate%3Apositive%3Ablood_base_excess> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bicarbonate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/blood_base_excess> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Siggaard-Andersen O. The pH-log pCO2 blood acid-base nomogram revised. Scand J Clin Lab Invest. 1962;14:598-604 (base excess definition). Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology, 3rd ed., 'Acid-Base Physiology' (base excess as metabolic acid-base index, primary determinant is [HCO3⁻]). Clinical confirmation: sodium bicarbonate infusion (metabolic alkalosis) raises both plasma [HCO3⁻] and base excess in parallel — a canonical pharmacological intervention. | intervention: Sodium bicarbonate infusion (pharmacological do()): raises plasma [HCO3⁻], which raises base excess — confirmed in clinical management of metabolic acidosis and in experimental metabolic alkalosis induction."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Base excess is a near-linear function of plasma bicarbonate concentration via the Siggaard-Andersen titration formula: BE ≈ 0.93 × ([HCO3⁻] − 24.4 + 14.8 × (pH − 7.4)). Plasma bicarbonate is the dominant buffer base contributing to base excess; a 1 mEq/L rise in [HCO3⁻] raises BE by ~0.93 mEq/L at fixed pH/pCO2. This is a curated algebraic-mechanistic relationship, not a stochastic signalling chain."
    .
