    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The LDL receptor is a large (160 kDa) glycoprotein expressed on most cells. Because it recognizes apoB100 as well as apoE, it is involved in the uptake of LDL, chylomicron remnants, VLDL, and IDL.\" and \"This relatively common cause of severe hypercholesterolemia results from one of many defects in production of the LDL receptor leading to impaired function or production of the LDL receptor protein.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 41, p. 1593–1603"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Hepatic LDL receptor binds apoB100-containing LDL and mediates endocytosis/lysosomal degradation → reduces circulating LDL; receptor LoF (FH mutations) causes hypercholesterolaemia"
    .
