    ; pmo:mechanism "renal tissue O2 tension is set by convective O2 delivery to the kidney (renal blood flow x arterial O2 content); higher systemic DO2 raises renal O2 supply -> closes the renal-O2 -> EPO loop"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adipose_tissue_mass> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_25oh_vitamin_d> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aadipose_tissue_mass%3Anegative%3Aplasma_25oh_vitamin_d> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adipose_tissue_mass> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_25oh_vitamin_d> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "do(increase adipose mass)/high-fat-diet perturbation lowers hepatic 25-hydroxylase activity and serum 25(OH)D. Roizen JD et al., Obesity Decreases Hepatic 25-Hydroxylase Activity Causing Low Serum 25-Hydroxyvitamin D. J Bone Miner Res 2019;34(6):1068-1073 (PMID:30790351, verified via PubMed/PMC6663580). Corroborated by Wu et al. PMID:34448353 (HFD reduces hepatic vitamin-D-25-hydroxylase). Textbook anchor: Holick MF, Vitamin D Deficiency, NEJM 2007;357:266-281 (PMID:17634462, verified)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Increased adiposity/obesity downregulates hepatic CYP2R1 (the principal vitamin D 25-hydroxylase), reducing conversion of cholecalciferol (D3) to 25-hydroxyvitamin D. High-fat-diet feeding lowers liver Cyp2r1 expression dose-dependently and obese livers generate less 25(OH)D, so serum 25(OH)D is low in obesity and rises subnormally after supplementation (volumetric dilution of 25(OH)D into expanded adipose adds in the same negative direction). Within-scale ORGAN_SYSTEM -> ORGAN_SYSTEM; analyte is a downstream sink, so no new path is created through the homeostatic feedback core."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adipose_tissue_mass> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_adiponectin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aadipose_tissue_mass%3Anegative%3Aplasma_adiponectin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adipose_tissue_mass> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_adiponectin> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Diez JJ, Iglesias P. The role of the novel adipocyte-derived hormone adiponectin in human disease. Eur J Endocrinol. 2003;148(3):293-300. PMID:12611609 (PMID verified via PubMed). Inverse relation corroborated by Kern 2003 (PMID:12829646). NOTE: partially mediated by the adipose_tissue_mass -> tnf_alpha -> plasma_adiponectin path; this edge captures the additional TNF-independent hypoxia/hypertrophy component. Both endpoints ORGAN_SYSTEM."
    ; pmo:mechanism "The adipose paradox: although adiponectin is secreted only by adipocytes, plasma adiponectin FALLS as adipose-tissue mass rises. Hypertrophic/hypoxic adipocytes down-regulate per-cell ADIPOQ (local hypoxia, adipocyte hypertrophy, increased local TNF-alpha/IL-6), and this per-cell suppression dominates the increase in producer-cell number, so net plasma adiponectin decreases. Interventional support: weight-loss / fat-mass reduction raises plasma adiponectin (do(decrease adipose mass) -> increase adiponectin)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/amyloid_protein_aggregate_amount> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_amyloid_beta_42> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aamyloid_protein_aggregate_amount%3Anegative%3Acsf_amyloid_beta_42> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/amyloid_protein_aggregate_amount> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_amyloid_beta_42> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Mechanism: plaque sequestration of soluble Abeta42 ('sink' hypothesis). Citation VERIFIED: Fagan et al., 'Inverse relation between in vivo amyloid imaging load and CSF Abeta42 in humans', Ann Neurol 2006;59:512-519, PMID 16372280 (confirmed real via PubMed). Review anchor: Blennow & Zetterberg, 'Biomarkers for Alzheimer's disease: current status and prospects', J Intern Med 2018;284:643."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Abeta42 is the most aggregation-prone Abeta species; as fibrillar plaque burden rises it acts as a 'sink', sequestering soluble Abeta42 into insoluble plaque and lowering the soluble CSF pool. do(more aggregation) -> lower soluble CSF Abeta42 (the classic inverse CSF-Abeta42 vs amyloid-PET signature of Alzheimer's disease). Captures the HPO 'Decreased CSF Abeta42' term mechanistically."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bace1_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_amyloid_beta_40> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abace1_activity%3Apositive%3Acsf_amyloid_beta_40> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bace1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_amyloid_beta_40> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Mechanism: BACE1 is the rate-limiting beta-secretase generating Abeta40; BACE1 inhibitors dose-dependently lower CSF Abeta (pharmacological do()). Same sign/structure as the curated edge bace1_activity -> csf_amyloid_beta (+) (present in all_edges.json). Anchor: O'Brien & Wong, 'Amyloid precursor protein processing and Alzheimer's disease', Annu Rev Neurosci 2011;34:185 (verified review of BACE1/gamma-secretase APP cleavage to Abeta40/42)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Amyloid-beta 40 is generated by sequential cleavage of APP by beta-secretase (BACE1) then gamma-secretase. Abeta40 is the dominant, relatively soluble, less aggregation-prone Abeta species; its CSF concentration tracks amyloidogenic production, and pharmacological BACE1 inhibition lowers CSF Abeta40 dose-dependently. Higher BACE1 activity -> more Abeta40 production -> higher CSF Abeta40. Mirrors the curated edge bace1_activity -> csf_amyloid_beta (+)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bckdh_enzyme_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_alloisoleucine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abckdh_enzyme_activity%3Anegative%3Acsf_alloisoleucine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bckdh_enzyme_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_alloisoleucine> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Mechanism: BCKDH-complex deficiency backs up branched-chain keto-acids; alloisoleucine is the pathognomonic MSUD metabolite. Same sign and structure as the curated map edges bckdh_enzyme_activity -> plasma_alloisoleucine (-) and bckdh_enzyme_activity -> csf_isoleucine (-) (both present in all_edges.json). Anchor: Guyton & Hall, Medical Physiology, 11th ed. (branched-chain amino-acid catabolism); MSUD/alloisoleucine reviewed in Chuang & Shih, OMMBID 'Maple Syrup Urine Disease (Branched-Chain Ketoaciduria)'."
    ; pmo:mechanism "L-alloisoleucine is the non-physiological D-allo epimer formed by transamination/racemization of the isoleucine branched-chain keto-acid (2-oxo-3-methylvalerate). When BCKDH is deficient (maple syrup urine disease, a clean genetic LoF do()), the keto-acid pool backs up and alloisoleucine accumulates (pathognomonic MSUD marker). Higher BCKDH activity drains the keto-acid pool and lowers alloisoleucine. Mirrors the curated edges bckdh_enzyme_activity -> plasma_alloisoleucine (-) and bckdh_enzyme_activity -> csf_isoleucine/leucine/valine (-)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/capillary_permeability> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_surfactant_protein> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acapillary_permeability%3Apositive%3Aplasma_surfactant_protein> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/capillary_permeability> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_surfactant_protein> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "SP-D traverses disrupted alveolar-capillary barriers proportionally to surface-area loss, mirroring albumin leakage; serum SP-D rises in ALI/ARDS due to barrier injury and leakage into circulation. PMC4122417 (Lung SP-D response and regulation during acute and chronic lung injury); Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology (alveolar-capillary barrier). Uses systemic capillary_permeability as the within-scale proxy for alveolar barrier permeability (no lung-specific barrier node exists)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Surfactant proteins (SP-A/SP-D) are synthesized by type-II alveolar epithelium and normally confined to the alveolar lining. When the alveolar-capillary barrier loses integrity (increased microvascular/epithelial permeability in lung injury/edema), SPs spill into the circulation in proportion to barrier disruption (mirroring albumin leak). do(barrier permeability up) -> alveolar-to-blood SP spillover up -> plasma surfactant protein up."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_adp_level> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/acetyl_coa_carboxylase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acellular_adp_level%3Anegative%3Aacetyl_coa_carboxylase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_adp_level> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/acetyl_coa_carboxylase_activity> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Mechanism: ADP/AMP rise -> AMPK activation -> ACC1/ACC2 Ser80/Ser221 phosphorylation -> ACC inactivation. Hardie DG, AMP-activated protein kinase: a key regulator of energy balance with many roles in human disease, J Intern Med 2014;276(6):543-559, PMID 24824502 (verified: confirms AMPK phosphorylates both ACC isoforms at equivalent Ser sites, inactivating them and thereby lowering malonyl-CoA, enhancing fatty-acid oxidation). Guyton & Hall, Medical Physiology, 11th ed., Ch. 68 (energy metabolism / fat utilization). ACC is an established direct AMPK substrate; AMPK activation by AMP/ADP and the resulting inhibitory ACC Ser-phosphorylation are demonstrated by perturbation (AMP titration, AMPK activators, non-phosphorylatable ACC mutants) in the cited mechanistic literature."
    ; pmo:mechanism "ACC catalytic activity is acutely controlled by cellular energy charge, on top of its ACACA/ACACB gene-dose floor. A rise in cellular ADP/AMP (energy deficit: fasting, exercise, hypoxia) allosterically activates AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK), which phosphorylates ACC1/ACC2 at the equivalent Ser80/Ser221 sites and inactivates them. So do(increase) in cellular ADP lowers ACC activity. This is a genuine within-CELLULAR allosteric/phosphorylation controller, parallel to the already-encoded cellular_adp_level -> phosphofructokinase_activity energy-charge edge."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_cobalamin_uptake> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/methionine_synthase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acellular_cobalamin_uptake%3Apositive%3Amethionine_synthase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_cobalamin_uptake> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/methionine_synthase_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Mechanism: textbook cobalamin biochemistry — methylcobalamin is the obligate cofactor of cytosolic methionine synthase; cofactor availability (set by cellular uptake) determines holoenzyme activity. Textbook anchors: Guyton & Hall, Medical Physiology, 11th ed., Ch. 32 (vitamin B12 coenzyme function); Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology (cobalamin-dependent methionine synthase / methylcobalamin cofactor). Sign chain checks: cellular_cobalamin_uptake -> plasma_methylcobalamin already (+); methylcobalamin is the MTR cofactor."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Methionine synthase (MTR) is a cobalamin-dependent enzyme requiring methylcobalamin as prosthetic cofactor to remethylate homocysteine to methionine. Intracellular methylcobalamin supply depends on transcobalamin-II-mediated cellular cobalamin uptake and downstream processing (MMACHC/MTRR); when cellular cobalamin uptake falls (transcobalamin-II deficiency, cblE/cblG, B12 deficiency), holo-MTR activity drops. do(increase cellular cobalamin uptake) -> increased functional methionine synthase activity. Consistent with the existing downstream edge methionine_synthase_activity -> plasma_homocysteine (-)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_cobalamin_uptake> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/methylmalonyl_coa_mutase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acellular_cobalamin_uptake%3Apositive%3Amethylmalonyl_coa_mutase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_cobalamin_uptake> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/methylmalonyl_coa_mutase_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Mechanism: textbook cobalamin biochemistry — adenosylcobalamin is the obligate cofactor of mitochondrial methylmalonyl-CoA mutase; cofactor availability (set by cellular uptake) determines holoenzyme activity. Textbook anchors: Guyton & Hall, Medical Physiology, 11th ed., Ch. 32 (vitamin B12 coenzyme functions, methylmalonyl-CoA); Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology (adenosylcobalamin-dependent methylmalonyl-CoA mutase). Sign chain checks: cellular_cobalamin_uptake -> plasma_adenosylcobalamin already (+); AdoCbl is the MMUT cofactor."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase (MMUT) is an adenosylcobalamin (AdoCbl)-dependent mitochondrial enzyme; its functional holoenzyme activity requires AdoCbl generated from cobalamin delivered by transcobalamin-II-mediated cellular uptake and processed via MMAA/MMAB. When cellular cobalamin uptake/AdoCbl supply falls (transcobalamin-II deficiency, cblA/cblB, B12 deficiency), measured MMUT holoenzyme activity drops, raising methylmalonate. do(increase cellular cobalamin uptake) -> increased functional MMUT activity. Consistent with existing downstream edges MMUT -> plasma_lactate (-) and -> urinary_methylmalonylcarnitine (-)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_fibronectin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acortisol%3Apositive%3Aplasma_fibronectin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_fibronectin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Dexamethasone/corticosterone raise secreted plasma fibronectin ~2.8-fold in hepatocyte culture, with 6-fold FN1 mRNA induction blocked by transcription inhibitors and abolished by a GR-competing antagonist (GR-mediated). PMID:3611064 (Nimmer et al., 'Regulation of plasma fibronectin biosynthesis by glucocorticoids in chick hepatocyte cultures', J Biol Chem 1987) — verified real."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Plasma fibronectin (FN1) is synthesized by hepatocytes. Glucocorticoids acting via the glucocorticoid receptor induce FN1 transcription (~6-fold mRNA rise) and improve secretion efficiency, raising secreted plasma fibronectin ~2.8-fold; GR antagonist abolishes the effect dose-dependently. do(cortisol up) -> hepatic fibronectin synthesis/secretion up -> plasma fibronectin up."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_plasminogen> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acortisol%3Apositive%3Aplasma_plasminogen> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_plasminogen> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Mechanism: cortisol -> hepatic glucocorticoid receptor -> increased/permissive PLG transcription within the IL-6 acute-phase program -> higher plasma plasminogen. Citation: Jenkins GR, Seiffert D, Parmer RJ, Miles LA. Blood 1997;89(7):2394-2403 (PMID 9116283; hydrocortisone separately increased plasminogen mRNA in primary hepatocytes). Standard glucocorticoid-permissiveness for hepatic acute-phase proteins: Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology; Guyton & Hall, Medical Physiology (cortisol and the acute-phase response). Hydrocortisone addition (pharmacological do()) to primary hepatocytes increases plasminogen mRNA."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Glucocorticoid (cortisol/hydrocortisone) independently increases hepatic plasminogen mRNA and is permissive/potentiating for the hepatic acute-phase response (synergy with IL-6 on C/EBPbeta-class acute-phase genes), raising plasma plasminogen."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_tryptophan> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acortisol%3Anegative%3Aplasma_tryptophan> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_tryptophan> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Glucocorticoid de-novo induction of hepatic TDO lowering systemic tryptophan is established physiology. do() support: Gibney et al., Int J Neuropsychopharmacol 2014;17(6):917-928 (PMID 24472498, verified) shows stress/corticosterone induces hepatic TDO and TDO inhibition raises tryptophan; in humans a hydrocortisone RCT (Hydrocortisone Affects Fatigue and Physical Functioning Through Metabolism of Tryptophan, J Clin Endocrinol Metab 2018;103(9):3411) confirms cortisol lowers tryptophan availability via this pathway. Textbook anchor: Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology (glucocorticoid induction of hepatic TDO)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Cortisol (glucocorticoid) transcriptionally induces hepatic tryptophan 2,3-dioxygenase (TDO/TDO2) de novo, the rate-limiting, hormone-inducible enzyme of the dominant (>90%) hepatic kynurenine pathway. Increased TDO flux catabolises tryptophan to N-formylkynurenine, depleting the circulating (total) tryptophan pool. The TDO-induction effect dominates net plasma tryptophan, so the sign is determinately negative. Within-scale ORGAN_SYSTEM -> ORGAN_SYSTEM; cortisol is an upstream core variable, plasma_tryptophan is a downstream sink (no new path through the feedback core)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cystathionine_gamma_lyase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_alpha_aminobutyrate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acystathionine_gamma_lyase_activity%3Apositive%3Acsf_alpha_aminobutyrate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cystathionine_gamma_lyase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_alpha_aminobutyrate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Mechanism: CTH cleaves cystathionine to cysteine + 2-oxobutanoate + NH3; 2-oxobutanoate is transaminated to 2-aminobutyrate. Identical sign/structure to the curated map edge cystathionine_gamma_lyase_activity -> plasma_alpha_aminobutyrate (+) (present in all_edges.json). Anchor: Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology (sulfur amino-acid / trans-sulfuration metabolism)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Alpha-aminobutyrate (2-aminobutyrate) is produced by transamination of 2-oxobutyrate. The major source of 2-oxobutyrate is the cystathionine gamma-lyase (CTH/CSE) step of trans-sulfuration, which cleaves cystathionine to cysteine + 2-oxobutyrate + NH3 (also from threonine catabolism). Greater CTH flux raises the 2-oxobutyrate/2-aminobutyrate pool, so alpha-aminobutyrate rises with CTH activity. Directly mirrors the curated edge cystathionine_gamma_lyase_activity -> plasma_alpha_aminobutyrate (+)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_mbl> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t3%3Apositive%3Aplasma_mbl> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_mbl> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Sorensen, Hansen, Steffensen, Jensenius, Thiel. Hormonal regulation of mannan-binding lectin synthesis in hepatocytes. Clin Exp Immunol 2006;145(1):173-182, PMID 16792688 (DOI 10.1111/j.1365-2249.2006.03101.x) - VERIFIED; T3 4-fold at 1 nM, >8-fold at 100 nM, dose-dependent, P<0.001."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Thyroid hormone is a strong positive transcriptional regulator of hepatic mannan-binding lectin (MBL2). In human hepatocyte (HuH-7) culture, T3 gave a ~4-fold increase in MBL at 1 nM and >8-fold at 100 nM (dose-dependent, P<0.001); T4 similar, less potent. core->analyte downstream sink (free T3 -> circulating MBL via hepatocyte production), no new feedback-core path. Within-scale ORGAN_SYSTEM->ORGAN_SYSTEM."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatocellular_injury> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_alt> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahepatocellular_injury%3Apositive%3Aplasma_alt> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatocellular_injury> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_alt> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Hepatocellular necrosis releases cytosolic ALT into plasma (passive membrane-permeability leak), proportional to injury. Smith AK et al., 'Contrasting model mechanisms of alanine aminotransferase (ALT) release from damaged and necrotic hepatocytes', PLoS Comput Biol 2020;16(6):e1007622 (PMID:32484845, VERIFIED real, exactly on point); Guyton & Hall, Medical Physiology, 11th ed., liver function tests."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Loss of hepatocyte plasma-membrane integrity / hepatocellular necrosis passively releases cytosolic alanine aminotransferase (ALT, the most liver-specific aminotransferase) into the circulation in proportion to the degree/extent of injury, raising plasma ALT. do(increase hepatocellular injury) -> Delta plasma ALT up."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatocellular_injury> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ast> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahepatocellular_injury%3Apositive%3Aplasma_ast> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatocellular_injury> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ast> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "AST released from injured/necrotic hepatocytes (cytosolic + mitochondrial isoforms) by passive membrane leak. Smith AK et al., PLoS Comput Biol 2020;16(6):e1007622 (PMID:32484845, VERIFIED, ALT/AST biomarker-release modelling); Guyton & Hall, Medical Physiology, 11th ed., liver function tests."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Hepatocellular necrosis / loss of hepatocyte membrane integrity releases both cytosolic and mitochondrial aspartate aminotransferase (AST) into plasma, raising plasma AST. Same membrane-leak mechanism as ALT; reuses the ORGAN_SYSTEM hepatocellular_injury node."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatocellular_injury> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cholinesterase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahepatocellular_injury%3Anegative%3Aplasma_cholinesterase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatocellular_injury> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cholinesterase_activity> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Serum cholinesterase is a hepatocyte-synthesis-dependent biomarker that falls with impaired hepatic synthetic capacity (cirrhosis: median 1590 vs 7886 IU/L in controls). Ramachandran J et al., 'Serum cholinesterase is an excellent biomarker of liver cirrhosis', Trop Gastroenterol 2014;35(1):15-20 (PMID:25276901, VERIFIED real); Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology (hepatic synthetic function)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Serum butyrylcholinesterase (BCHE / pseudocholinesterase) is synthesized almost exclusively by hepatocytes and continuously secreted into plasma; hepatocellular injury/necrosis (and the cirrhosis it drives) reduces functional hepatic synthetic mass, lowering BCHE production, so serum cholinesterase activity falls. A recognised marker of hepatic synthetic reserve (parallels falling albumin). do(increase hepatocellular injury) -> Delta serum cholinesterase down."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hoga1_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_2_4_dihydroxyglutarate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahoga1_activity%3Anegative%3Aurinary_2_4_dihydroxyglutarate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hoga1_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_2_4_dihydroxyglutarate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Belostotsky R et al., The enzyme 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate aldolase is deficient in primary hyperoxaluria type III (PMID 22391140); Clifford-Mobley/Pelle et al., 4-Hydroxyglutamate Is a Biomarker for Primary Hyperoxaluria Type 3 (PMC4270872) — reports significantly increased 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate, 4-hydroxyglutamate and 2,4-dihydroxyglutarate in PH3 patients. PMID and PMC verified real via PubMed."
    ; pmo:mechanism "HOGA1 (mitochondrial 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate aldolase) cleaves 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate (HOG) in the hydroxyproline catabolic pathway. When HOGA1 activity falls (primary hyperoxaluria type 3, HOGA1 LoF), its substrate HOG accumulates and is reduced to 2,4-dihydroxyglutarate, which is excreted — so lower HOGA1 activity raises urinary 2,4-dihydroxyglutarate (negative sign). Mirrors the existing hoga1_activity -> urinary_4_hydroxy_2_oxoglutarate (-) edge (parallel accumulation product). HOGA1 genetic LoF is a clean do() that raises this metabolite."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il1beta> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_tenascin_c> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ail1beta%3Apositive%3Aplasma_tenascin_c> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il1beta> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_tenascin_c> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Tenascin-C expression is induced by inflammatory cytokines including IL-1, signalling through NF-kB/c-Jun/Sp1 to activate TNC transcription. PMID:25793574 (Chiovaro, Chiquet-Ehrismann & Chiquet, 'Transcriptional regulation of tenascin genes', Cell Adh Migr 2015) — verified consistent with literature. This closes a real positive-feedback loop with the existing plasma_tenascin_c -> tnf_alpha (+) and tnf_alpha -> il1beta (+) edges (TNC amplifies inflammation via TLR4)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Tenascin-C (TNC) is an inducible matricellular protein at low baseline; pro-inflammatory IL-1alpha/IL-1beta (with TGF-beta, PDGF, mechanical stress) induce TNC transcription (NF-kB/c-Jun) in fibroblasts/myofibroblasts, raising tissue and circulating TNC. do(IL-1beta up) -> fibroblast TNC transcription up -> plasma tenascin-C up."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_alpha1_antitrypsin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ail6%3Apositive%3Aplasma_alpha1_antitrypsin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_alpha1_antitrypsin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "AAT is a prototype liver-derived acute-phase protein whose plasma concentration increases 3-4 fold during inflammation; IL-6 is the primary inducer of hepatocyte acute-phase protein synthesis. StatPearls 'Physiology, Acute Phase Reactants' (NCBI Bookshelf NBK519570); Guyton & Hall, Medical Physiology, 11th ed., acute-phase response. Consistent with existing il6 -> c_reactive_protein/haptoglobin/fibrinogen (+) edges in the map."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Alpha-1-antitrypsin (SERPINA1) is the prototypical hepatic positive acute-phase reactant; plasma AAT rises 3-4 fold during inflammation. IL-6 (JAK1/STAT3 in hepatocytes, the same axis driving CRP/haptoglobin/fibrinogen already in this model) up-regulates hepatic SERPINA1 synthesis. do(IL-6 up) -> hepatic AAT synthesis up -> plasma AAT up."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ca125> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ail6%3Apositive%3Aplasma_ca125> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ca125> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Mechanical stretch and inflammatory cytokines (IL-1beta, IL-6, IL-8) directly upregulate MUC16/CA125 expression and shedding from mesothelial cells; serum CA125 correlates positively with serum IL-6/TNF-alpha in heart failure. PMID:22743023 (Kosar/Huang et al., 'New mechanism of elevated CA125 in heart failure: the mechanical stress and inflammatory stimuli initiate CA125 synthesis', Med Hypotheses 2012) — verified real."
    ; pmo:mechanism "CA125 (MUC16) is produced by mesothelial cells lining the serous cavities. In non-malignant states inflammatory cytokines (IL-6, IL-1beta) via NF-kB induce MUC16 synthesis and ectodomain shedding from mesothelial cells, raising serum CA125. do(IL-6 up) -> mesothelial MUC16 synthesis/shedding up -> plasma CA125 up."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_plasminogen> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ail6%3Apositive%3Aplasma_plasminogen> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_plasminogen> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Mechanism: IL-6 -> STAT3/C/EBPbeta -> increased hepatic PLG transcription -> higher plasma plasminogen (modest positive acute-phase reactant). Citation: Jenkins GR, Seiffert D, Parmer RJ, Miles LA. Regulation of plasminogen gene expression by interleukin-6. Blood 1997;89(7):2394-2403 (PMID 9116283 — title/authors/journal/volume/pages verified). C/EBPbeta mechanism corroborated by Bannach FG et al., J Thromb Haemost 2004;2(12):2205-2212. Textbook anchor: Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology (hepatic IL-6-driven acute-phase response). Recombinant IL-6 do() (cytokine addition to hepatocytes / IL-6 injection in vivo) raises plasminogen mRNA and a plasminogen-promoter luciferase reporter — pharmacological/perturbational interventional evidence."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Plasminogen is a (modest) positive hepatic acute-phase reactant. IL-6, acting on hepatocytes via JAK/STAT3 and the transcription factor C/EBPbeta, transcriptionally up-regulates the plasminogen (PLG) gene, raising plasminogen mRNA and plasma plasminogen concentration. Demonstrated in primary murine hepatocytes, Hep3B human hepatoma cells, and in vivo after recombinant IL-6 injection in mice."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/interferon_gamma> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/xanthine_oxidase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ainterferon_gamma%3Apositive%3Axanthine_oxidase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/interferon_gamma> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/xanthine_oxidase_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "Interventional do(IFN): in-vivo IFN/IFN-inducer administration increases XO activity (Ghezzi P et al., Biochem Biophys Res Commun 1984, PMID:6584105); IFN-gamma treatment of cultured pulmonary endothelial cells raises XDH/XO mRNA and activity chiefly via increased transcription rate (Dupont GP et al., J Clin Invest 1992, PMID:1370294, verified via WebSearch/JCI). Net effect is induction (+); a separate post-translational NO-mediated inactivation occurs in IFN-gamma-stimulated macrophages (PMID:7524568) but does not reverse the dominant transcriptional induction. Interventional: do(IFN) administration in vivo and IFN-gamma treatment of endothelial cells increase XDH/XO transcription and activity."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Interferon-gamma is a potent transcriptional inducer of xanthine oxidoreductase (XDH/XO): in cultured endothelial cells IFN-gamma markedly raises the XDH/XO mRNA and activity, with the major site of upregulation at the level of transcription; in vivo IFN/IFN-inducer administration increases XO activity across organs. Raising the inflammatory IFN-gamma signal therefore increases tissue/circulating XO catalytic activity. The XO-activity node is a downstream sink that drives hepatic urate production and lowers plasma xanthine, so connecting it adds no new path into the cardiovascular/renal feedback core."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/myocardial_contractility> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/left_atrial_pressure> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amyocardial_contractility%3Anegative%3Aleft_atrial_pressure> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/myocardial_contractility> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/left_atrial_pressure> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Depressed contractility raises atrial/filling pressures (congestive backward failure). Guyton & Hall, Medical Physiology, 11th ed., Ch.22 (Cardiac Failure); Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology (ventricular function and congestion). Directly parallels the existing myocardial_contractility -> right_atrial_pressure (-) edge in this map (same sign convention)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Left atrial pressure equals LV filling pressure. Reduced LV contractility (systolic dysfunction) impairs LV emptying, raising LV end-diastolic/filling pressure, which transmits backward to elevate left atrial pressure (backward/congestive failure). do(contractility down) -> LV filling pressure up -> left atrial pressure up; contractility and LA pressure move oppositely (sign -)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_2_hydroxybutyrate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_2_hydroxybutyric_acid> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_2_hydroxybutyrate%3Apositive%3Aurinary_2_hydroxybutyric_acid> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_2_hydroxybutyrate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_2_hydroxybutyric_acid> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Standard renal handling of filtered low-molecular-weight organic acids: Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, 11th ed., Ch. 27 (excretion of freely filtered organic solutes scales with plasma concentration). Follows the model's existing plasma->urinary excretion convention."
    ; pmo:mechanism "2-hydroxybutyrate is a small freely-filtered organic acid with limited tubular reabsorption; urinary excretion is a monotone-increasing function of the filtered (plasma) load. Raising plasma 2-HB raises filtered 2-HB and thus urinary 2-HB."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_alpha_globulin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_globulin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_alpha_globulin%3Apositive%3Aplasma_globulin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_alpha_globulin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_globulin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Total serum globulin is the sum of alpha, beta and gamma fractions; alpha-globulin is an acute-phase constituent. Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology (plasma proteins). Existing edge il6 -> plasma_alpha_globulin (+) makes globulin transitively inflammation-predictable."
    ; pmo:mechanism "The alpha-globulin acute-phase fraction is a constituent of total globulin. Raising the alpha-globulin fraction (already il6-driven in this model) raises total globulin. do(alpha-globulin up) -> total globulin up (constituent of an additive identity, same plasma scale)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_apolipoprotein_b> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_apolipoprotein_b> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_apolipoprotein_b%3Apositive%3Acsf_apolipoprotein_b> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_apolipoprotein_b> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_apolipoprotein_b> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Mechanism: absence of CNS ApoB synthesis means CSF ApoB is plasma-derived (barrier transfer/leak), monotonic positive. Identical structure to the curated edge plasma_albumin -> csf_albumin (+) (present in all_edges.json). Anchor: Roheim et al., 'Apolipoproteins in human cerebrospinal fluid', PNAS 1979;76:4646 (apolipoprotein profile of CSF; no ApoB synthesis in CNS)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "ApoB is not synthesized in the CNS (brain makes ApoE/ApoA-I, no ApoB), so any CSF ApoB derives from plasma ApoB crossing/leaking the blood-CSF/blood-brain barrier (CSF ApoB is used as a blood-contamination/BBB-leak index). Higher plasma ApoB -> more transfer into CSF -> higher CSF ApoB. Directly analogous to the curated edge plasma_albumin -> csf_albumin (+)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cck> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fecal_pancreatic_elastase> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_cck%3Apositive%3Afecal_pancreatic_elastase> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cck> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fecal_pancreatic_elastase> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Mechanism: CCK is the dominant acinar enzyme-secretagogue; fecal elastase-1 is a stable, pancreas-specific marker of acinar secretory/functional capacity that falls in exocrine pancreatic insufficiency and rises with secretory output. CITATION (verified): Walkowiak J et al., 'Fecal elastase-1 concentration: an indirect test of exocrine pancreatic function and a marker of an enteropathy regardless of cause', PMID:12604968 (title/PMID confirmed via PubMed). Textbook anchor: Guyton & Hall, Medical Physiology, 11th ed., Ch. 64 (pancreatic secretion regulated by CCK and secretin). CAVEAT: fecal elastase is a chronic integrated acinar-capacity marker, relatively insensitive to acute single CCK pulses; the '+' is a steady-state directional claim (acinar drive up -> fecal elastase up; acinar loss -> down)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Cholecystokinin (CCK) from duodenal I-cells is the principal physiological secretagogue for pancreatic acinar cells, driving exocytosis of the zymogen complement including proelastase (CELA). Greater acinar secretory drive delivers more elastase-1 to the gut lumen; elastase-1 resists intestinal degradation and is concentrated several-fold in stool, so steady-state fecal elastase-1 rises with acinar secretory output and falls with acinar functional loss (exocrine pancreatic insufficiency). do(sustained increase in CCK / acinar secretory drive) -> increased luminal then fecal elastase-1."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_free_fatty_acids> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_2_hydroxybutyrate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_free_fatty_acids%3Apositive%3Aplasma_2_hydroxybutyrate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_free_fatty_acids> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_2_hydroxybutyrate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Gall WE et al., alpha-Hydroxybutyrate Is an Early Biomarker of Insulin Resistance and Glucose Intolerance in a Nondiabetic Population, PLoS One 2010;5(5):e10883 (PMID 20526369; doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0010883) — alpha-HB rises with increased lipid oxidation/oxidative stress. Verified real via PubMed (PMID 20526369) and PMC2878333."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Increased delivery of free fatty acids to the liver drives hepatic fatty-acid beta-oxidation, raising the hepatic NADH/NAD+ ratio and increasing the oxidative-stress demand for glutathione synthesis. 2-hydroxybutyrate is generated from alpha-ketobutyrate (a by-product of the cystathionine/glutathione-synthesis pathway and of Met/Thr catabolism) by NADH-dependent reduction (LDH / alpha-hydroxybutyrate dehydrogenase). Higher hepatic lipid oxidation therefore raises plasma 2-HB. Parallels the existing plasma_free_fatty_acids -> hepatic_ketogenesis (+) edge (same FFA -> hepatic lipid-oxidation driver). do(): raising FFA flux (lipid infusion / fasting / insulin resistance) raises plasma 2-HB."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_free_fatty_acids> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_acetylcarnitine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_free_fatty_acids%3Apositive%3Aplasma_acetylcarnitine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_free_fatty_acids> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_acetylcarnitine> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "Mechanism: FFA up -> FAO up -> matrix acetyl-CoA up -> CrAT buffering -> acetylcarnitine export -> plasma C2 up. Xu G, Hansen JS, Zhao XJ, et al., Liver and Muscle Contribute Differently to the Plasma Acylcarnitine Pool During Fasting and Exercise in Humans, J Clin Endocrinol Metab 2016;101(12):5044-5052, PMID 27648961 (verified: fasting/exercise increase plasma short-chain acylcarnitines including C2, with pronounced hepato-splanchnic C2 release). Guyton & Hall, Medical Physiology, 11th ed., Ch. 68 (fat utilization in fasting). Curated mechanistic: CrAT-mediated acetyl-CoA-to-acetylcarnitine buffering is biochemically established; the FFA->FAO->acetyl-CoA->C2 directionality is corroborated by fasting/exercise (do()-like) human flux studies showing concordant rises."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Plasma acetylcarnitine (C2) reflects the mitochondrial acetyl-CoA/CoA ratio: when beta-oxidation flux outpaces TCA-cycle capacity, excess acetyl-CoA is buffered by carnitine acetyltransferase (CrAT) to acetylcarnitine, which is exported to plasma. A do(increase) in plasma free fatty acids (fasting, lipolysis, exercise) drives hepatic and muscle FAO, raises matrix acetyl-CoA, and increases plasma C2. Human fasting/exercise studies show concordant FFA and plasma C2 rises with hepato-splanchnic C2 release."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_erythritol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_glucose%3Apositive%3Acsf_erythritol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_erythritol> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Hootman KC et al., 'Erythritol is a pentose-phosphate pathway metabolite and associated with adiposity gain in young adults', PNAS 2017;114(21):E4233-E4240 (PMID 28484010; DOI 10.1073/pnas.1620079114). Verified via PubMed/PNAS. Demonstrates endogenous glucose->erythritol conversion by 13C-glucose stable-isotope tracing (ex vivo blood and in vivo dried-blood-spot), with blood erythritol markedly elevated with adiposity gain / elevated HbA1c. Both endpoints ORGAN_SYSTEM scale (same-scale, within-scale edge). Note: the HPO 'decreased CSF erythritol' direction in transaldolase deficiency is set by the PPP enzyme block, a separate enzyme-determined axis not claimed by this core->sink edge."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Erythritol is synthesized endogenously from glucose via the pentose-phosphate pathway: glucose-6-phosphate -> PPP -> erythrose-4-phosphate -> erythrose -> (reduction) -> erythritol. Higher plasma glucose raises PPP flux and substrate availability, increasing the circulating erythritol pool; CSF erythritol tracks the systemic pool of this small, freely-diffusing polyol. do(plasma glucose up) -> CSF erythritol up."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_apolipoprotein_c3> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_glucose%3Apositive%3Aplasma_apolipoprotein_c3> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_apolipoprotein_c3> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Glucose directly activates apoCIII transcription in hepatocytes via ChREBP and HNF-4alpha; plasma apoCIII correlates more strongly with fasting glucose than with insulin. Caron S et al., 'Transcriptional activation of apolipoprotein CIII expression by glucose may contribute to diabetic dyslipidemia,' Arterioscler Thromb Vasc Biol 2011;31(3):513-519, PMID 21183731 (verified via PubMed)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Glucose transactivates the hepatic APOC3 gene. Elevated glucose activates carbohydrate-response-element-binding protein (ChREBP) and HNF-4alpha, which bind the APOC3 promoter and increase apoC-III transcription and secretion. In vivo, plasma apoC-III tracks fasting glucose more closely than insulin, identifying glucose as the dominant driver in hyperglycemia. do(raise plasma glucose) -> increased hepatic APOC3 transcription -> increased plasma apoC-III. Within-scale ORGAN_SYSTEM -> ORGAN_SYSTEM (circulating glucose to circulating apoC-III)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_hippuric_acid> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_hippuric_acid> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_hippuric_acid%3Apositive%3Aurinary_hippuric_acid> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_hippuric_acid> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_hippuric_acid> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Proximal-tubule organic-anion secretion (OAT1/OAT3): Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, 11th ed., Ch. 27. Hippurate as the renally-excreted gut-microbial benzoate–glycine conjugate: Nutrients 2023;15(5):1138 (PMC10005077). Plasma hippuric_acid itself remains an abstained exogenous (diet/microbiome) root; this edge only makes the urinary reading forward-predictable."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Hippuric acid is cleared almost entirely by the kidney — freely filtered plus efficient proximal-tubule organic-anion secretion (OAT1/OAT3, PAH-like) — so urinary hippurate excretion is a monotone-increasing function of plasma hippurate. Raising plasma hippurate raises its renal (filtered + secreted) load and thus urinary hippurate. do(): any rise in plasma hippurate (benzoate / dietary polyphenol load) raises urinary hippurate."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ige> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_allergen_specific_ige> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_ige%3Apositive%3Aplasma_allergen_specific_ige> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ige> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_allergen_specific_ige> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Th2 two-signal model of IgE class switching: IL-4/IL-13 + CD40L drive Cepsilon switching (verified: Punnonen et al., PNAS 1993;90(8):3730-3734, PMID 8097323, IL-13/IL-4-induced human IgE synthesis). Total and antigen-specific IgE share these drivers (Janeway's Immunobiology 9th ed., Ch.10)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Allergen-specific IgE is the atopy-driven subset of the total circulating IgE pool. Both fractions are produced by the same Th2 program: IL-4/IL-13 -> STAT6 -> germline Cepsilon transcription, plus CD40-CD40L co-stimulation, driving deletional class-switch recombination in B cells. Raising the systemic IgE-production drive (reflected in total plasma IgE, itself fed in the core by aicda/cd40l/btk) raises the allergen-specific clones. core->analyte downstream sink; adds no new path through the feedback core. Within-scale ORGAN_SYSTEM->ORGAN_SYSTEM."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_igg> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_precipitin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_igg%3Apositive%3Aplasma_precipitin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_igg> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_precipitin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, 11th ed., Ch.34 (Immunity and Allergy - antibody mechanisms including precipitation); Janeway's Immunobiology 9th ed., Ch.10 (precipitating IgG/IgM immune complexes)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "A precipitin is a precipitating (immune-complex-forming) antibody, predominantly high-avidity IgG (also IgM), assayed by precipitation reactions (e.g. anti-avian/anti-fungal precipitins in hypersensitivity pneumonitis). Precipitin titre scales with the antigen-specific IgG produced by the humoral response, so it tracks the circulating IgG pool (fed in the core by helper-T/CD40L/AICDA/BTK). core->analyte downstream sink. Within-scale ORGAN_SYSTEM->ORGAN_SYSTEM."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_immunoglobulin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_globulin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_immunoglobulin%3Apositive%3Aplasma_globulin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_immunoglobulin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_globulin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Total serum globulin = alpha + beta + gamma globulin fractions, with gamma globulin = immunoglobulins; this is a definitional additive relationship. Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology (plasma proteins); NCBI Clinical Methods Ch.101 (Serum Albumin and Globulin)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Total plasma globulin is the mereological sum of its sub-fractions; the gamma-globulin fraction IS the immunoglobulins. Raising total immunoglobulin necessarily raises measured total globulin. do(immunoglobulin up) -> total globulin up (constituent of an additive identity, same plasma scale)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_immunoglobulin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_specific_antibody> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_immunoglobulin%3Apositive%3Aplasma_specific_antibody> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_immunoglobulin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_specific_antibody> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, 11th ed., Ch.34 (Immunity and Allergy - B lymphocytes, plasma cells and antibody production); Janeway's Immunobiology 9th ed., Ch.10-11 (T-dependent B-cell responses generate antigen-specific antibody)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Antigen-specific antibody titre is the subset of total circulating immunoglobulin generated by the antigen-driven B-cell/helper-T humoral response (germinal-center proliferation, AICDA-mediated class switch and affinity maturation, plasma-cell secretion). Systemic Ig-production capacity drives specific-antibody titre: when total Ig production fails (agammaglobulinemia/CVID - the HPO 'Decreased specific antibody' context), specific antibody falls in step. plasma_immunoglobulin is fed in the core by btk_kinase_activity. core->analyte downstream sink. Within-scale ORGAN_SYSTEM->ORGAN_SYSTEM."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_insulin> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_apolipoprotein_c3> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_insulin%3Anegative%3Aplasma_apolipoprotein_c3> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_insulin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_apolipoprotein_c3> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "APOC3 hepatic expression is negatively regulated by insulin via FOXO1; FOXO1 binds the APOC3 promoter insulin-response element and insulin phosphorylates/excludes FOXO1 from the nucleus, downregulating apoC-III. Altomonte J et al., 'Foxo1 mediates insulin action on apoC-III and triglyceride metabolism,' J Clin Invest 2004;114(10):1493-1503, PMID 15546000 (verified via PubMed/JCI). Reviewed in Caron et al. ATVB 2011, PMID 21183731."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Insulin suppresses hepatic APOC3 transcription. Nuclear FOXO1 binds an insulin-response element in the APOC3 promoter to activate transcription; insulin signaling phosphorylates FOXO1 and excludes it from the nucleus, de-repressing (lowering) apoC-III synthesis. Insulin replacement in insulin-deficient states reduces hepatic APOC3 mRNA and normalizes hypertriglyceridemia (a do()-like manipulation). do(raise insulin) -> decreased APOC3 transcription -> decreased plasma apoC-III. Within-scale ORGAN_SYSTEM -> ORGAN_SYSTEM. Note: glucose and insulin act through distinct, opposing transcriptional arms (ChREBP/HNF-4alpha vs FOXO1); each is captured by a separate edge, so the net of a mixed glucose+insulin load is the resolved sum of both edges."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_phosphate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/prpp_synthetase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_phosphate%3Apositive%3Aprpp_synthetase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_phosphate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/prpp_synthetase_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "Pi is a defined allosteric activator/cofactor of PRPS demonstrated by enzyme-kinetic studies in which adding/removing Pi changes activity (a do(Pi) manipulation on the purified/cellular enzyme), and intracellular Pi is rate-limiting for PRPP production. Becker MA, 'Phosphoribosylpyrophosphate synthetase and the regulation of phosphoribosylpyrophosphate production in human cells', Prog Nucleic Acid Res Mol Biol 2001, PMID:11550793 (verified); enzyme requirement for Pi/Mg2+ also in classic PRS kinetic literature. Textbook anchor: Guyton & Hall, Medical Physiology, 11th ed. (purine synthesis). Pi shown to be a defined allosteric activator by enzyme-kinetic do(Pi) studies; intracellular Pi rate-limiting for PRPP production."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Inorganic phosphate (Pi) is an obligate activator and cofactor of PRPP synthetase (PRPS1): Pi binds the regulatory/allosteric site (the site that also binds the inhibitory nucleotides ADP/GDP), stabilising the active hexameric conformation, and enzyme activity is strictly dependent on Pi (with Mg2+). Intracellular Pi tracks plasma phosphate and is rate-limiting for PRPP production in human cells. Raising plasma (hence intracellular) phosphate therefore increases realised PRPS catalytic activity. The activity node is a downstream sink (it then drives de-novo purine synthesis), so no new path through the homeostatic feedback core is created."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_riboflavin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glutathione_reductase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_riboflavin%3Apositive%3Aglutathione_reductase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_riboflavin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glutathione_reductase_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Mechanism: cofactor (FAD) saturation of apo-GSR sets the holoenzyme activity; this is the physiological basis of the EGRAC riboflavin-status assay (basal activity low + large FAD activation = deficiency). CITATIONS (verified): Hill MHE et al., 'Optimal and stable conditions for the determination of erythrocyte glutathione reductase activation coefficient to evaluate riboflavin status', PMID:7883462 (confirmed; AC ~1.3 in healthy subjects, rising to ~1.6 on 0.5 mg/d riboflavin, falling after supplementation); EGRAC protocol PMID:37988268 (confirmed). New node plasma_riboflavin grounded CHEBI:17015 (riboflavin, OBO-verified)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Erythrocyte glutathione reductase (GSR) is an FAD-flavoprotein whose measured (basal, non-FAD-stimulated) catalytic activity depends on saturation of the apoenzyme with its FAD cofactor, derived from dietary riboflavin (vitamin B2). In riboflavin deficiency FAD supply falls, apo-GSR accumulates and basal GSR activity drops (the erythrocyte glutathione reductase activation coefficient, EGRAC, rises). do(raise riboflavin/FAD availability) -> increased holo-GSR activity."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/platelet_count> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_thrombopoietin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplatelet_count%3Anegative%3Aplasma_thrombopoietin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/platelet_count> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_thrombopoietin> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Fielder PJ, Gurney AL, Stefanich E, Marian M, Moore MW, Carver-Moore K, de Sauvage FJ. Regulation of thrombopoietin levels by c-mpl-mediated binding to platelets. Blood. 1996;87(6):2154-2161. PMID:8630374 (PMID verified via PubMed; c-mpl-/- mice have fewer platelets and higher plasma TPO with unchanged TPO mRNA, and platelet-rich plasma binds/internalises 125I-TPO). Both endpoints ORGAN_SYSTEM; existing TPO->platelet_count(+) edge present in textbook_extracted.yaml."
    ; pmo:mechanism "TPO is produced near-constitutively (mainly hepatic); its plasma level is set by clearance, not production. Circulating platelets (and megakaryocytes) bear c-Mpl receptors that bind, internalise and degrade TPO, so plasma TPO is inversely related to total platelet mass: high platelet count sponges/removes more TPO (level falls); thrombocytopenia leaves more free TPO (level rises). do(raise platelet count) -> decrease TPO. This closes the autoregulatory negative-feedback loop with the existing plasma_thrombopoietin -> platelet_count (+) edge."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ast> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asarcolemmal_permeability%3Apositive%3Aplasma_ast> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sarcolemmal_permeability> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ast> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Skeletal-muscle necrosis releases AST (and ALT) into plasma in parallel with CK and LDH. Nathwani RA, Pais S, Reynolds TB, Kaplowitz N, 'Serum alanine aminotransferase in skeletal muscle diseases', Hepatology 2005;41(2):380-2 (PMID:15660433, VERIFIED real); mirrors the curated sarcolemmal_permeability -> CK/LDH/aldolase/myoglobin leak edges. Guyton & Hall, Medical Physiology, 11th ed."
    ; pmo:mechanism "AST is abundant in skeletal muscle; loss of sarcolemmal membrane integrity (rhabdomyolysis/myonecrosis) releases muscle AST into plasma, producing a non-hepatic aminotransferase rise. Exactly parallels the existing muscle-leak panel edges sarcolemmal_permeability -> plasma_creatine_kinase / plasma_ldh / plasma_aldolase / plasma_myoglobin already in the graph."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tnf_alpha> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_adiponectin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atnf_alpha%3Anegative%3Aplasma_adiponectin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tnf_alpha> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_adiponectin> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Kern PA, Di Gregorio GB, Lu T, Rassouli N, Ranganathan G. Adiponectin expression from human adipose tissue: relation to obesity, insulin resistance, and tumor necrosis factor-alpha expression. Diabetes. 2003;52(7):1779-1785. PMID:12829646 (PMID verified via PubMed). Both endpoints ORGAN_SYSTEM scale; analyte is a downstream sink, no new path through the feedback core."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Adipose/circulating TNF-alpha suppresses adipocyte ADIPOQ transcription and secretion (NF-kB activation, down-regulation of the PPAR-gamma-driven ADIPOQ promoter), lowering plasma adiponectin. do(raise TNF-alpha) -> decrease adiponectin. Verified: plasma adiponectin and adipose ADIPOQ mRNA are inversely related to adipose TNF-alpha expression; TNF-alpha is a principal driver of the low-adiponectin state of obesity/inflammation."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fgf23> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_phosphate_reabsorption> .
