    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "BioModels BIOMD0000000494 (Roblitz2013, LH->E2); Boron & Boulpaep two-cell two-gonadotropin model | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-161 (27.3: Anatomy and Physiology of the Female Reproductive System): 'LH also stimulates the granulosa and theca cells of the follicles to produce the sex steroid hormone estradiol'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-162 (27.4: Development of the Male and Female Reproductive Systems): 'During puberty, the release of LH and FSH from the anterior pituitary stimulates the gonads to produce sex hormones in both male and female adolescents'; 'the dominant follicle produces more estrogen, triggering positive feedback and the LH surge'.; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.3 (37.3: Regulation of Body Processes): 'LH also plays a role in the development of ova, induction of ovulation, and stimulation of estradiol and progesterone production by the ovaries.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-43.3 (43.3: Human Reproductive Anatomy and Gametogenesis): 'LH also plays a role in the development of ova, induction of ovulation, and stimulation of estradiol...and progesterone production by the ovaries.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LH drives thecal androgen production aromatised to estradiol; granulosa/Leydig steroidogenesis"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fsh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afsh%3Apositive%3Aestradiol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fsh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "BioModels BIOMD0000000494 (Roblitz2013, FSH->follicle/E2); Boron & Boulpaep folliculogenesis | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-161 (27.3: Anatomy and Physiology of the Female Reproductive System): 'FSH stimulates the follicles to grow'; 'FSH stimulates the growth of a tertiary follicle, and LH stimulates the production of estrogen by granulosa and theca cells'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-162 (27.4: Development of the Male and Female Reproductive Systems): 'Early tertiary follicles...will be stimulated by an increase in FSH...to grow'; 'Supporting granulosa and theca cells in the growing follicles produce estrogens'.; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-163 (28.2: Fertilization): FSH analogs support development of multiple follicles in IVF, consistent with FSH driving folliculogenesis and granulosa-cell estradiol production.; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-43.3 (43.3: Human Reproductive Anatomy and Gametogenesis): 'In females, FSH stimulates development of egg cells...Follicle cells produce the hormone inhibin...LH also plays a role in...stimulation of estradiol'; 'As the follicles grow, they begin releasing estrogens.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "FSH induces granulosa-cell aromatase converting androgens to estradiol (follicular growth)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/progesterone> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alh%3Apositive%3Aprogesterone> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/progesterone> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "BioModels BIOMD0000000494 (Roblitz2013, LH->corpus luteum P4); Boron & Boulpaep luteal phase | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-161 (27.3: Anatomy and Physiology of the Female Reproductive System): 'The surge of LH also stimulates a change in the granulosa and theca cells...luteinized granulosa and theca cells of the corpus luteum begin to produce large amounts of the sex steroid hormone progesterone'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-163 (28.2: Fertilization): LH analog triggers ovulation in IVF protocol, consistent with the LH surge triggering follicle rupture and subsequent luteal progesterone secretion.; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-43.3 (43.3: Human Reproductive Anatomy and Gametogenesis): 'LH also plays a role in...stimulation of estradiol...and progesterone production by the ovaries.' Also: 'The corpus luteum produces estrogen and progesterone.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LH maintains corpus luteum steroidogenesis raising progesterone (luteal phase)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> .
