    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"treatment with LH-like activity (hCG) stimulates Leydig cell testosterone production and ameliorates androgen deficiency\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p. 730"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LH (via hCG as analog) stimulates testicular Leydig cell testosterone synthesis; hCG administration raises testosterone in men with LH receptor mutations and stimulates Leydig cell testosterone production in fertile eunuch syndrome"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fsh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/inhibin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afsh%3Apositive%3Ainhibin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fsh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/inhibin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "BioModels BIOMD0000000494 (Roblitz2013) — inhibin synthesis driven by gonadotropin/follicle dynamics; Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology, 3rd ed., Ch. 55 'The Female Reproductive System' (FSH-driven granulosa inhibin secretion) | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-160 (27.2: Anatomy and Physiology of the Male Reproductive System): 'FSH also stimulates the Sertoli cells to produce hormones called inhibins.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.3 (37.3: Regulation of Body Processes): 'Follicle cells produce the hormone inhibin, which inhibits FSH production.' (from ovarian follicles stimulated by FSH); 'FSH stimulates the maturation of sperm cells. FSH production is inhibited by the hormone inhibin, which is released by the testes.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-43.3 (43.3: Human Reproductive Anatomy and Gametogenesis): 'FSH enters the testes and stimulates the Sertoli cells to begin facilitating spermatogenesis...The Sertoli cells produce the hormone inhibin.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "FSH stimulates granulosa/Sertoli inhibin-B production"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aestradiol%3Anegative%3Agnrh> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"both testosterone and estradiol (derived from aromatization of testosterone) exert negative feedback effects at the hypothalamus to suppress pulsatile GnRH secretion.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p 683"
    ; pmo:mechanism "estradiol acts via ERα in hypothalamic kisspeptin neurons to suppress GnRH pulse secretion"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_fsh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aestradiol%3Anegative%3Apituitary_fsh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_fsh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "BioModels BIOMD0000000494 (Roblitz2013, E2->FSH feedback); Boron & Boulpaep | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-161 (27.3: Anatomy and Physiology of the Female Reproductive System): 'high concentrations of estrogen will stimulate the hypothalamus and pituitary to reduce the production of GnRH, LH, and FSH' (negative feedback); OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-162 (27.4: Development of the Male and Female Reproductive Systems): 'the level of estrogen...triggers negative feedback at the hypothalamus and pituitary. This results in a reduction of FSH and LH'; 'Birth control pills provide constant levels of estrogen and progesterone to negatively feed back on the hypothalamus and pituitary, and suppress the release of FSH and LH'.; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-166 (28.5: Changes During Pregnancy, Labor, and Birth): 'They suppress FSH and LH production, effectively preventing ovulation.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "estradiol negative feedback suppresses pituitary FSH secretion"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/progesterone> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aprogesterone%3Anegative%3Agnrh> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/progesterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "BioModels BIOMD0000000494 (Roblitz2013, P4 feedback); Boron & Boulpaep luteal feedback | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-161 (27.3: Anatomy and Physiology of the Female Reproductive System): 'Progesterone triggers negative feedback at the hypothalamus and pituitary, which keeps GnRH, LH, and FSH secretions low'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-162 (27.4: Development of the Male and Female Reproductive Systems): 'Birth control pills provide constant levels of estrogen and progesterone to negatively feed back on the hypothalamus and pituitary, and suppress the release of FSH and LH, which inhibits ovulation'.; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-166 (28.5: Changes During Pregnancy, Labor, and Birth): 'Like estrogen, progesterone suppresses FSH and LH.' (via GnRH pulse suppression)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "progesterone (with estradiol) slows the GnRH pulse generator (luteal-phase negative feedback)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/progesterone> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_lh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aprogesterone%3Anegative%3Apituitary_lh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/progesterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_lh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "BioModels BIOMD0000000494 (Roblitz2013) — P4 negative feedback term on gonadotropin secretion; Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology, 3rd ed., Ch. 55 'The Female Reproductive System' (progesterone slowing of GnRH/LH pulse frequency) | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-162 (27.4: Development of the Male and Female Reproductive Systems): 'Birth control pills provide constant levels of estrogen and progesterone to negatively feed back on the hypothalamus and pituitary, and suppress the release of FSH and LH, which inhibits ovulation'.; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-166 (28.5: Changes During Pregnancy, Labor, and Birth): 'Like estrogen, progesterone suppresses FSH and LH.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-43.3 (43.3: Human Reproductive Anatomy and Gametogenesis): 'Progesterone assists in endometrial regrowth and inhibition of FSH and LH release.' Also: 'The progesterone...inhibits the release of further FSH and LH.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-43.5 (43.5: Human Pregnancy and Birth): 'Hormonal methods use synthetic progesterone (sometimes in combination with estrogen), to inhibit the hypothalamus from releasing FSH or LH, and thus prevent an egg from being available for fertilization.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "progesterone reduces LH pulse frequency/secretion (luteal-phase negative feedback)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atestosterone%3Anegative%3Agnrh> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"normal men and GnRH-deficient men with CHH treated with physiologic doses of pulsatile GnRH (i.e., a GnRH clamp) underwent medical castration induced by high-dose ketoconazole and treatment with physiologic doses of testosterone or estradiol. The effects of these interventions on production of LH and FSH were measured… both testosterone and estradiol (derived from aromatization of testosterone) exert negative feedback effects at the hypothalamus to suppress pulsatile GnRH secretion.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p 683  ||  \"Administration of sex steroids—androgens, progestins, or estrogens—suppresses gonadotropin secretion by negative feedback mechanisms at the hypothalamus or the pituitary gland or both\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p. 732"
    ; pmo:mechanism "testosterone (via aromatization to estradiol) acts at hypothalamic GnRH neurons (via kisspeptin interneurons bearing ERα/AR) to suppress pulsatile GnRH secretion"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_lh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atestosterone%3Anegative%3Apituitary_lh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_lh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "\"During chronic administration of high-dose androgenic anabolic steroids, serum concentrations of testosterone, LH, and FSH are very low\" and (from Fig 19.24 caption) \"high testosterone concentrations but reduced androgen action…elevated luteinizing hormone (LH)…due to impaired androgen-mediated negative feedback regulation of gonadotropins\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p. 716 and p. 732  ||  orchiectomy (but not oophorectomy) in the ovine fetus leads to increased pulsatile secretion of LH and, to a lesser degree, of FSH. Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 26"
    ; pmo:mechanism "androgens exert negative feedback on pituitary gonadotrophs to suppress LH secretion; chronic administration of androgenic anabolic steroids causes LH suppression; androgen receptor mutations impair this feedback causing LH elevation"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/inhibin> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_fsh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ainhibin%3Anegative%3Apituitary_fsh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/inhibin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_fsh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Sertoli cell products, inhibin B, and AMH decline to very low or undetectable serum concentrations…and FSH concentrations are disproportionately elevated relative to LH concentrations\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p. 714  ||  administration of an inhibin-rich extract inhibits fetal FSH but not LH secretion, evidence of the functional capacity of the FSH-fetal gonadal inhibin feedback system. Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 26"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Sertoli-cell-derived inhibin B selectively suppresses pituitary FSH secretion; Sertoli cell damage (e.g., Klinefelter) causes inhibin B decline with disproportionate FSH elevation"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> obo:RO_0002411 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_lh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aestradiol%3Aunknown%3Apituitary_lh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_lh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "?"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "BioModels BIOMD0000000494 (Roblitz2013, E2->LH tonic feedback AND estradiol-triggered mid-cycle LH surge); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology, Ch. 55 'The Female Reproductive System' (mid-cycle estradiol positive-feedback LH surge) | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-161 (27.3: Anatomy and Physiology of the Female Reproductive System): both negative feedback ('reduce...LH') and positive surge ('extremely high concentrations of systemic plasma estrogen trigger...secreting large amounts of LH and FSH') — dual-mode ? edge; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-162 (27.4: Development of the Male and Female Reproductive Systems): dual-mode described — 'the level of estrogen...triggers negative feedback at the hypothalamus and pituitary. This results in a reduction of FSH and LH' (tonic negative) AND 'the dominant follicle produces more estrogen, triggering positive feedback and the LH surge that will induce ovulation' (positive feedback surge).; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-166 (28.5: Changes During Pregnancy, Labor, and Birth): 'They suppress FSH and LH production, effectively preventing ovulation.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "DUAL-MODE: tonic/moderate estradiol exerts NEGATIVE feedback suppressing pituitary LH; sustained high estradiol SWITCHES to POSITIVE feedback at the hypothalamus/pituitary triggering the mid-cycle LH surge. Net steady-state sign of E2->LH is therefore threshold/context-dependent (negative at low/moderate E2, positive at sustained high E2), hence \"?\"."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acortisol%3Anegative%3Agnrh> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Glucocorticoids also act centrally to inhibit gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) pulsatility and release of luteinizing hormone (LH) and follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)\"; \"Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism occurs because of a direct inhibitory effect of cortisol on GnRH pulsatility\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 15, p. 497, 499  ||  \"Glucocorticoids act primarily to suppress gonadotropins via inhibition of hypothalamic GnRH secretion\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p. 734"
    ; pmo:mechanism "glucocorticoids act centrally to inhibit GnRH pulsatility and release, causing hypogonadotropic hypogonadism"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_lh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acortisol%3Anegative%3Apituitary_lh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_lh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; dcterms:source "Endocrine Society FHA Clinical Practice Guideline, Gordon CM et al., J Clin Endocrinol Metab. 2017;102(5):1413-1439. (glucocorticoid suppression of gonadotropin secretion in functional hypothalamic amenorrhea) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "glucocorticoid excess blunts pituitary gonadotropin (LH) secretion"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/leptin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aleptin%3Apositive%3Agnrh> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/leptin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Administration of recombinant leptin to hypogonadal ob/ob mice and to rats experiencing pubertal delay associated with food restriction in the rat partially reverses the hypogonadism. Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 26"
    ; pmo:mechanism "leptin acts on ventral premammillary neurons to stimulate glutamate release which activates GnRH neurons; leptin-deficient ob/ob mice have hypogonadotropic hypogonadism reversed by leptin administration"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alveolar_ventilation> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_pco2> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aalveolar_ventilation%3Anegative%3Aarterial_pco2> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alveolar_ventilation> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_pco2> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"If the alveolar ventilation is halved (and CO2 production remains unchanged) ... the alveolar and arterial PCO2 will double\" (West's Respiratory Physiology, 11th ed., ch 2)  ||  The resulting hyperventilation reduces the PCO2 in the blood and therefore in the CSF"
    ; pmo:mechanism "alveolar and arterial PCO2 are virtually identical, so the inverse alveolar-ventilation relation carries through; halving alveolar ventilation doubles arterial PCO2"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alveolar_ventilation> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_po2> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aalveolar_ventilation%3Apositive%3Aarterial_po2> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alveolar_ventilation> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_po2> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Grodins 1967 alveolar O2 mass balance; HumMod respiratory; alveolar gas equation | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-127 (22.5: Gas Exchange): increased ventilation raises alveolar O2, driving diffusion into blood; 'Both deep and forced breathing cause the alveolar air composition to be changed more rapidly … oxygen to enter … the blood more quickly'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-39.2 (39.2: Gas Exchange across Respiratory Surfaces): residual air in lungs mixes with inspired air, affecting alveolar PO2; increased ventilation raises alveolar PO2 (alveolar gas equation), driving O2 into blood"
    ; pmo:mechanism "increased ventilation raises alveolar/arterial O2 (alveolar gas equation)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_pco2> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/chemoreceptor_drive> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aarterial_pco2%3Apositive%3Achemoreceptor_drive> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_pco2> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/chemoreceptor_drive> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"the chemoreceptors sense any elevation of arterial P 2 and respond by increasing ventilation.\" — West's Respiratory Physiology, 11th ed., ch 5"
    ; pmo:mechanism "chemoreceptors sense a rise in arterial PCO2 and increase the integrated drive to breathe"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_po2> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/chemoreceptor_drive> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aarterial_po2%3Anegative%3Achemoreceptor_drive> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_po2> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/chemoreceptor_drive> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"in some patients with a shunt, the arterial P 2 is low because the hypoxemia increases respiratory drive\" — West's Respiratory Physiology, 11th ed., ch 5"
    ; pmo:mechanism "hypoxemia (low arterial PO2) is sensed by peripheral chemoreceptors and increases respiratory drive"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_ph> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/chemoreceptor_drive> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aarterial_ph%3Anegative%3Achemoreceptor_drive> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_ph> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/chemoreceptor_drive> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "The stimulus to raise the ventilation is chiefly the action of H+ ions on the peripheral chemoreceptors"
    ; pmo:mechanism "H+ ions (lower pH) stimulate the peripheral chemoreceptors, raising ventilatory drive (respiratory compensation for metabolic acidosis)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/chemoreceptor_drive> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alveolar_ventilation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Achemoreceptor_drive%3Apositive%3Aalveolar_ventilation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/chemoreceptor_drive> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alveolar_ventilation> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"whenever the chemoreceptors sense a rising P 2, there is an increase in ventilatory drive ... The consequent increase in ventilation to the alveoli is usually effective in returning the arterial P 2 to normal.\" — West's Respiratory Physiology, 11th ed., ch 5"
    ; pmo:mechanism "increased chemoreceptor/ventilatory drive increases ventilation to the alveoli"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_ph> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bicarbonate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aarterial_ph%3Anegative%3Aplasma_bicarbonate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_ph> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bicarbonate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; dcterms:source "Renal acid-base handling (slow); HumMod kidney acid-base; standard physiology | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-158 (26.5: Acid-Base Balance): 'the renal system controls the blood levels of bicarbonate'; renal tubular H+ secretion and HCO3- conservation increase in acidosis to restore blood pH.; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-159 (26.6: Disorders of Acid-Base Balance): 'In cases of respiratory acidosis, the kidney increases the conservation of bicarbonate and secretion of H+...These processes increase the concentration of bicarbonate in the blood'; 'In cases of respiratory alkalosis, the kidneys decrease the production of bicarbonate and reabsorb H+ from the tubular fluid.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "renal compensation — acidosis (low pH) increases tubular H+ secretion / HCO3- regeneration, raising [HCO3-]"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/metabolic_rate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_pco2> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ametabolic_rate%3Apositive%3Aarterial_pco2> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/metabolic_rate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_pco2> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "respiratory mass balance: PaCO2 ~ VCO2 / alveolar ventilation"
    ; pmo:mechanism "higher oxidative metabolism raises CO2 production (all else equal, before ventilatory compensation)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/chemoreceptor_drive> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sympathetic_tone> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Achemoreceptor_drive%3Apositive%3Asympathetic_tone> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/chemoreceptor_drive> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sympathetic_tone> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Ursino & Magosso 2003; chemoreflex-mediated sympathoexcitation; standard physiology | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-114 (20.5: Homeostatic Regulation of the Vascular System): 'The chemoreceptors respond to increasing carbon dioxide and hydrogen ion levels (falling pH) by stimulating the cardioaccelerator and vasomotor centers, increasing cardiac output and constricting peripheral vessels.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "peripheral chemoreflex increases central sympathetic outflow (chemoreflex-sympathetic activation)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intracellular_atp> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/phosphofructokinase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aintracellular_atp%3Anegative%3Aphosphofructokinase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intracellular_atp> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/phosphofructokinase_activity> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "Wong, Cells: Molecules and Mechanisms §wong-3.4 (Regulation of Enzyme Activity): 'if there are high enough levels of ATP in the cell that other cellular processes aren't using, then it can bind to phosphofructokinase outside of its active site... and turn it off. This blocks glycolysis and production of excess ATP when the cell does not need it.' | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-7.2 (7.2: Glycolysis): 'it is less active when ADP levels are low and the concentration of ATP is high. Thus, if there is 'sufficient' ATP in the system, the pathway slows down. This is a type of end product inhibition, since ATP is the end product of glucose catabolism.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Allosteric negative feedback: excess ATP binds to an allosteric site on PFK (outside the fructose-6-phosphate active site), conformationally inhibiting the enzyme and blocking glycolysis"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/na_k_atpase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cardiomyocyte_intracellular_sodium> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ana_k_atpase_activity%3Anegative%3Acardiomyocyte_intracellular_sodium> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/na_k_atpase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cardiomyocyte_intracellular_sodium> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Wong, Cells: Molecules and Mechanisms §wong-4.4 (The Action Potential in Neurons): 'the Na+/K+ ATPase...utilizes the energy from ATP hydrolysis to power the conformational changes needed to move both Na+ and K+ ions against the gradient'; cardiac glycosides 'inhibit the Na+/K+ ATPase...causes a rise in [Na+]in'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Na+/K+-ATPase continuously exports 3 Na+ per cycle, keeping intracellular [Na+] low; greater pump activity lowers cytosolic [Na+] (quality: pump activity -> process: electrogenic Na+ extrusion -> quality: intracellular [Na+])"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ketone_bodies> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_ph> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_ketone_bodies%3Anegative%3Aarterial_ph> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ketone_bodies> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_ph> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Wong, Cells: Molecules and Mechanisms §wong-5.7 (5.7 Starch and Glycogen Depolymerization Undeclared): 'This leads to a buildup of the molecules in the bloodstream, which lowers the pH, since the molecules are acidic.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Ketone bodies (acetoacetate, 3-hydroxybutyrate) are acidic; their accumulation in blood raises [H+] and lowers arterial pH (ketoacidosis)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/norepinephrine> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_free_fatty_acids> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Anorepinephrine%3Apositive%3Aplasma_free_fatty_acids> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/norepinephrine> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_free_fatty_acids> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Wong, Cells: Molecules and Mechanisms §wong-5.9 (Amino Acid Degradation - Undeclared): 'The hormone norepinephrine increases production of free fatty acids, which open the thermogenin channel' (section 5.5, Uncoupling Electron Transport from ATP Synthesis)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "norepinephrine activates beta-adrenergic receptors on brown (and white) adipocytes, stimulating hormone-sensitive lipase via cAMP/PKA, mobilising free fatty acids from stored triacylglycerol"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_free_fatty_acids> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/metabolic_heat_production> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_free_fatty_acids%3Apositive%3Ametabolic_heat_production> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_free_fatty_acids> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/metabolic_heat_production> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "Wong, Cells: Molecules and Mechanisms §wong-5.9 (Amino Acid Degradation - Undeclared): free fatty acids open the thermogenin channel, allowing protons to flow back into the matrix without ATP synthase, and 'the excess energy not being used in ATP production is released as heat' (section 5.5, Uncoupling Electron Transport from ATP Synthesis)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "free fatty acids open the UCP1/thermogenin proton channel in brown adipose tissue inner mitochondrial membrane, uncoupling electron transport from ATP synthesis so the proton gradient dissipates as heat (non-shivering thermogenesis)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tert_expression> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/telomerase_activity> .
