    ; dcterms:source "Wong, Cells: Molecules and Mechanisms §wong-16.8 (The Immune System - Undeclared): 'The activated helper T-cell binds to and activates a B cell that has also bound to the antigen of interest, leading to massive B-cell proliferation'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Activated helper T cell binds to and activates a B cell that has also bound to the same antigen, leading to massive B-cell proliferation"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nmj_ache_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/synaptic_acetylcholine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Anmj_ache_activity%3Anegative%3Asynaptic_acetylcholine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nmj_ache_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/synaptic_acetylcholine> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Jakubowski & Flatt, Fundamentals of Biochemistry Vol I §biochem-5.7 (Binding - Enzyme Linked Immunosorbant): 'The transmitter must not reside too long in the synapse; otherwise, muscle contraction will continue uncontrolled. To prevent this, a hydrolytic enzyme, acetylcholinesterase, a serine esterase found in the synapse, cleaves the transmitter at rates close to diffusion-controlled.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Acetylcholinesterase hydrolyzes acetylcholine in the synapse, reducing its concentration"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/core_temperature> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/metabolic_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acore_temperature%3Apositive%3Ametabolic_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/core_temperature> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/metabolic_rate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-004 (1.5: Requirements for Human Life): 'controlled hypothermia... decreases the metabolic needs of the brain, heart, and other organs' and 'slows the patient's metabolic rate'; also: 'chemical reactions upon which the body depends can only take place within a narrow range of body temperature'."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Temperature sets the rate of enzyme-catalysed metabolic reactions; controlled hypothermia (reduced core temperature) decreases whole-body metabolic rate, while hyperthermia raises it (Q10 effect on cellular chemistry)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/uterine_contraction_rate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cervical_distension> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Auterine_contraction_rate%3Apositive%3Acervical_distension> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/uterine_contraction_rate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cervical_distension> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-005 (1.6: Homeostasis): 'This causes even greater stretching of the cervix' — parturition positive-feedback loop"
    ; pmo:mechanism "stronger uterine contractions push the fetus against and through the cervix, increasing cervical mechanical stretch"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_histamine> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/capillary_permeability> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_histamine%3Apositive%3Acapillary_permeability> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_histamine> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/capillary_permeability> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-024 (4.7: Tissue Injury and Aging): 'The endothelium lining the local blood vessel becomes leaky under the influence of histamine and other inflammatory mediators allowing neutrophils, macrophages, and fluid to move from the blood into the interstitial tissue spaces.' | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-42.1 (42.1: Innate Immune Response): 'In response to a cut, mast cells secrete histamines that cause nearby capillaries to dilate' (Figure 42.5 caption) — histamine from mast cells/basophils increases microvascular permeability during inflammation."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Histamine released by mast cells acts on vascular endothelium to loosen interendothelial junctions, making the vessel wall 'leaky' and increasing fluid/protein extravasation into interstitial tissue"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_histamine> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/total_peripheral_resistance> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_histamine%3Anegative%3Atotal_peripheral_resistance> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_histamine> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/total_peripheral_resistance> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-024 (4.7: Tissue Injury and Aging): 'mast cells present in tissue degranulate, releasing the potent vasodilator histamine. Increased blood flow results in apparent redness and heat' — vasodilation implies decreased peripheral resistance. | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-42.1 (42.1: Innate Immune Response): 'mast cells secrete histamines that cause nearby capillaries to dilate' (Figure 42.5 caption) — histamine-mediated arteriolar vasodilation reduces peripheral resistance.; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-42.4 (42.4: Disruptions in the Immune System): 'effects of an allergic reaction range from...plummeting blood pressure' in anaphylactic shock — histamine-driven vasodilation causes the observed MAP/TPR drop"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Histamine is a potent vasodilator: it binds H1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle and endothelium to cause arteriolar dilation, reducing total peripheral resistance"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcitonin> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcium> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_calcitonin%3Anegative%3Aplasma_calcium> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcitonin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcium> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-034 (Exercise, Nutrition, Hormones, and Bone Tissue): 'Calcitonin inhibits osteoclast activity and stimulates calcium uptake by the bones, thus reducing the concentration of calcium ions in the blood.' | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.3 (37.3: Regulation of Body Processes): 'Calcitonin decreases blood calcium levels by inhibiting osteoclasts, stimulating osteoblasts, and stimulating calcium excretion by the kidneys.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.5 (37.5: Endocrine Glands): 'The combination of these two events [inhibit osteoclast activity; stimulate renal calcium excretion] lowers body fluid levels of calcium.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Calcitonin stimulates calcium uptake by bones and inhibits osteoclasts, thereby lowering plasma calcium concentration"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcium> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcitonin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_calcium%3Apositive%3Aplasma_calcitonin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcium> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcitonin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-035 (6.8: Calcium Homeostasis - Interactions of the Skeletal System and Other Organ): 'When blood levels of calcium get too high, the thyroid gland is stimulated to release calcitonin.' | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.3 (37.3: Regulation of Body Processes): 'The hormone calcitonin...has the opposite effect on blood calcium levels as does PTH.' — implied: calcitonin is released in response to high blood calcium.; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.5 (37.5: Endocrine Glands): 'calcitonin is produced by parafollicular cells of the thyroid... Calcitonin release is not controlled by TSH, but instead is released when calcium ion concentrations in the blood rise.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Elevated blood calcium stimulates the thyroid gland to release calcitonin"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_creatine_phosphate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_atp> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Askeletal_muscle_creatine_phosphate%3Apositive%3Askeletal_muscle_atp> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_creatine_phosphate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_atp> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-055 (10.4: Muscle Fiber Contraction and Relaxation): 'creatine phosphate transfers its phosphate back to ADP to form ATP and creatine. This reaction is catalyzed by the enzyme creatine kinase and occurs very quickly; thus, creatine phosphate-derived ATP powers the first few seconds of muscle contraction'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Creatine kinase transfers the phosphate group from creatine phosphate to ADP, rapidly regenerating ATP during the first seconds of muscle contraction"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/motor_unit_recruitment> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_tension> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amotor_unit_recruitment%3Apositive%3Askeletal_muscle_tension> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/motor_unit_recruitment> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_tension> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-056 (10.5: Nervous System Control of Muscle Tension): 'As more motor units are recruited, the muscle contraction grows progressively stronger'; 'the largest motor units may generate a contractile force of 50 times more than the smallest motor units in the muscle'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Activating more motor units (each comprising a motor neuron and its innervated muscle fibers) adds their contractile forces; larger motor units contribute disproportionately more force (up to 50x that of smallest units)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lean_body_mass> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atestosterone%3Apositive%3Alean_body_mass> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lean_body_mass> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Decreases in lean body mass, muscle size, and strength were induced by androgen deficiency\" (in a study where healthy young-to-middle-age men were treated with GnRH agonist ± testosterone ± aromatase inhibitor). Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p 1339–1341  ||  \"testosterone treatment for 1 year that raised testosterone concentrations to the mid-normal range for young men improved…anemia and hemoglobin, volumetric BMD…slightly improved walking distance\" and \"In a preliminary double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled 12-month trial in men with hepatic cirrhosis found that testosterone improved lean body and bone mass\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p. 724–725"
    ; pmo:mechanism "androgen (AR-mediated) increases muscle protein synthesis and lean mass; experimental androgen deficiency by GnRH agonist decreases lean mass"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lean_body_mass> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apituitary_gh%3Apositive%3Alean_body_mass> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lean_body_mass> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-058 (10.7: Exercise and Muscle Performance): 'Human growth hormone (hGH)...can facilitate building muscle mass, its main role is to promote the healing of muscle and other tissues after strenuous exercise'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "growth hormone facilitates anabolic building of skeletal muscle mass and promotes healing/repair of muscle tissue after exercise-induced damage, supporting net accretion of lean mass"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sympathetic_tone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/respiratory_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asympathetic_tone%3Apositive%3Arespiratory_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sympathetic_tone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/respiratory_rate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-080 (15.2: Divisions of the Autonomic Nervous System): 'As a response to a threat, the sympathetic system would increase heart rate and breathing rate and cause blood flow to the skeletal muscle to increase.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Sympathetic activation increases breathing rate to deliver more oxygen to activated skeletal muscle during fight-or-flight response."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sympathetic_tone> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/splanchnic_blood_flow> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asympathetic_tone%3Anegative%3Asplanchnic_blood_flow> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sympathetic_tone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/splanchnic_blood_flow> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-080 (15.2: Divisions of the Autonomic Nervous System): 'blood flow to the digestive system to decrease' as part of sympathetic fight-or-flight; also 'The digestive system shuts down so that blood is not absorbing nutrients when it should be delivering oxygen to skeletal muscles.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Sympathetic activation constricts splanchnic vasculature, diverting blood away from the digestive system toward skeletal muscle during fight-or-flight."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/epinephrine> obo:RO_0002411 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/total_peripheral_resistance> .
