    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-091 (17.4: The Pituitary Gland and Hypothalamus): 'ADH is also known as vasopressin because, in very high concentrations, it causes constriction of blood vessels, which increases blood pressure by increasing peripheral resistance.' | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-41.5 (41.5: Hormonal Control of Osmoregulatory Functions): 'ADH also acts as a vasoconstrictor and increases blood pressure during hemorrhaging.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "At high concentrations ADH (vasopressin) causes constriction of blood vessels, increasing peripheral resistance and thereby blood pressure."
