    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-104 (18.6: Hemostasis): 'there is a fairly complicated series of reactions that involves factor XII ... bradykinin, a vasodilator, is released, reversing the effects of the serotonin and prostaglandins from the platelets'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "during fibrinolysis, factor XII and the kallikrein-kinin contact pathway generate bradykinin from kininogen"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intrapericardial_pressure> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/end_diastolic_volume> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aintrapericardial_pressure%3Anegative%3Aend_diastolic_volume> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intrapericardial_pressure> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/end_diastolic_volume> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-106 (19.2: Heart Anatomy): 'excess fluid in the pericardial cavity puts pressure on the heart and prevents full relaxation, so the chambers within the heart contain slightly less blood as they begin each heart cycle' (cardiac tamponade description)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Elevated pericardial fluid pressure externally compresses the heart, preventing full diastolic relaxation and reducing the volume of blood that can fill the ventricular chambers before each contraction."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/left_atrial_pressure> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/end_diastolic_volume> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aleft_atrial_pressure%3Apositive%3Aend_diastolic_volume> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/left_atrial_pressure> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/end_diastolic_volume> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-108 (19.4: Cardiac Cycle): 'Atrial contraction, also referred to as the \"atrial kick,\" contributes the remaining 20–30 percent of filling'; 'As the atrial muscles contract... pressure rises within the atria and blood is pumped into the ventricles through the open atrioventricular valves'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "atrial systole raises pressure within the atria, pumping blood through the open atrioventricular valves into the ventricles (the 'atrial kick'), contributing the remaining 20–30% of ventricular filling"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/heart_rate> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/end_diastolic_volume> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aheart_rate%3Anegative%3Aend_diastolic_volume> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/heart_rate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/end_diastolic_volume> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-109 (19.5: Cardiac Physiology): 'as the HR rises, there is less time spent in diastole and consequently less time for the ventricles to fill with blood … SV gradually decreases due to decreased filling time.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Higher heart rate shortens diastolic filling time, reducing ventricular filling and EDV (preload)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/venous_return> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/capillary_hydrostatic_pressure> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Avenous_return%3Apositive%3Acapillary_hydrostatic_pressure> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/venous_return> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/capillary_hydrostatic_pressure> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-111 (20.2: Structure and Function of Blood Vessels): 'Any blood that accumulates in a vein will increase the pressure within it, which can then be reflected back into the smaller veins, venules, and eventually even the capillaries.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Elevated venous pressure is reflected retrograde into venules and capillaries, raising capillary hydrostatic pressure"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/capillary_hydrostatic_pressure> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/interstitial_fluid_volume> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acapillary_hydrostatic_pressure%3Apositive%3Ainterstitial_fluid_volume> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/capillary_hydrostatic_pressure> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/interstitial_fluid_volume> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-111 (20.2: Structure and Function of Blood Vessels): 'Increased pressure will promote the flow of fluids out of the capillaries and into the interstitial fluid. The presence of excess tissue fluid around the cells leads to a condition called edema.' | curated_mechanistic: NCBI Bookshelf NBK53445 'Pathophysiology of Edema - Capillary Fluid Exchange' (opened) — even modest capillary-pressure increases substantially increase filtration, raising interstitial fluid"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Elevated capillary hydrostatic pressure raises the outward Starling filtration force (Jv), driving fluid from capillary lumen into the interstitial space, increasing interstitial fluid volume and causing edema"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_compliance> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pulse_pressure> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aarterial_compliance%3Anegative%3Apulse_pressure> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_compliance> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pulse_pressure> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-112 (20.3: Blood Flow, Blood Pressure, and Resistance): 'A persistently high pulse pressure at or above 100 mm Hg may indicate excessive resistance in the arteries'; arterial stiffening reduces the ability to buffer systolic pressure surges."
    ; pmo:mechanism "A compliant artery absorbs the stroke volume ejected per beat, dampening the systolic pressure rise; reduced compliance causes the same stroke volume to produce a larger systolic spike and thus wider pulse pressure"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/capillary_hydrostatic_pressure> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/net_filtration_pressure> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acapillary_hydrostatic_pressure%3Apositive%3Anet_filtration_pressure> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/capillary_hydrostatic_pressure> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/net_filtration_pressure> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-113 (20.4: Capillary Exchange): 'The net filtration pressure (NFP) represents the interaction of the hydrostatic and osmotic pressures, driving fluid out of the capillary. It is equal to the difference between the CHP and the BCOP.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "NFP = CHP - BCOP; capillary hydrostatic pressure is the positive driving component that pushes fluid out of capillaries into the interstitium (filtration)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/blood_colloidal_osmotic_pressure> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/net_filtration_pressure> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ablood_colloidal_osmotic_pressure%3Anegative%3Anet_filtration_pressure> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/blood_colloidal_osmotic_pressure> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/net_filtration_pressure> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-113 (20.4: Capillary Exchange): 'whereas hydrostatic pressure forces fluid out of the capillary, osmotic pressure draws fluid back in... NFP = CHP - BCOP.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "NFP = CHP - BCOP; blood colloidal osmotic pressure generated by plasma proteins opposes filtration and drives reabsorption, reducing NFP"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/net_filtration_pressure> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/interstitial_fluid_volume> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Anet_filtration_pressure%3Apositive%3Ainterstitial_fluid_volume> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/net_filtration_pressure> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/interstitial_fluid_volume> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-113 (20.4: Capillary Exchange): 'Thus, the NFP of 10 mm Hg drives a net movement of fluid out of the capillary at the arterial end.' and 'when reabsorption is occurring, the NFP is a negative number.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Positive NFP drives net filtration of fluid out of capillaries into the interstitial space, increasing interstitial fluid volume; negative NFP (reabsorption) decreases it"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_albumin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/blood_colloidal_osmotic_pressure> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_albumin%3Apositive%3Ablood_colloidal_osmotic_pressure> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_albumin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/blood_colloidal_osmotic_pressure> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-113 (20.4: Capillary Exchange): 'it is the plasma proteins that play the key role... The pressure created by the concentration of colloidal proteins in the blood is called the blood colloidal osmotic pressure (BCOP). Its effect on capillary exchange accounts for the reabsorption of water.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Plasma proteins (chiefly albumin) are too large to cross the capillary wall; their colloidal suspension in blood generates the blood colloidal osmotic pressure (oncotic pressure) that drives water reabsorption from interstitium into capillaries"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lymphatic_flow> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/interstitial_fluid_volume> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alymphatic_flow%3Anegative%3Ainterstitial_fluid_volume> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lymphatic_flow> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/interstitial_fluid_volume> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-113 (20.4: Capillary Exchange): 'This excess fluid is picked up by capillaries of the lymphatic system... An important function of the lymphatic system is to return the fluid (lymph) to the blood. Approximately 24 liters per day are filtered, whereas 20.4 liters are reabsorbed [by capillaries].'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Lymphatic capillaries continuously drain excess interstitial fluid that was not reabsorbed at the venous end of capillaries, returning it to the venous circulation and thereby reducing interstitial fluid volume"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/anp> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldosterone> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aanp%3Anegative%3Aaldosterone> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/anp> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldosterone> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Other factors, notably somatostatin, heparin, atrial natriuretic factor, and dopamine, can directly inhibit aldosterone synthesis\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 15, p. 489"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ANP directly inhibits aldosterone synthesis in zona glomerulosa"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/anp> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adh> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aanp%3Anegative%3Aadh> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/anp> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adh> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-114 (20.5: Homeostatic Regulation of the Vascular System): 'Natriuretic hormones are antagonists to angiotensin II. They...suppress renin, aldosterone, and ADH production and release.' | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-41.5 (41.5: Hormonal Control of Osmoregulatory Functions): 'Its [ANP's] actions suppress the actions of aldosterone, ADH, and renin.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ANP suppresses hypothalamic/posterior-pituitary ADH (vasopressin) production and release"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythropoietin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/total_peripheral_resistance> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aerythropoietin%3Apositive%3Atotal_peripheral_resistance> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythropoietin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/total_peripheral_resistance> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-114 (20.5: Homeostatic Regulation of the Vascular System): 'In addition, EPO is a vasoconstrictor. Overproduction of EPO...will increase viscosity, resistance, and pressure.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "EPO directly causes vasoconstriction, raising peripheral resistance independently of hematocrit/viscosity changes"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mean_arterial_pressure> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/capillary_filtration_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amean_arterial_pressure%3Apositive%3Acapillary_filtration_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mean_arterial_pressure> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/capillary_filtration_rate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-117 (21.2: Anatomy of the Lymphatic and Immune Systems): 'Blood pressure causes leakage of fluid from the capillaries, resulting in the accumulation of fluid in the interstitial space'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Elevated arterial blood pressure raises capillary hydrostatic pressure, increasing the net outward Starling force and driving more fluid across the capillary wall into interstitial space"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/capillary_filtration_rate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/interstitial_fluid_volume> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acapillary_filtration_rate%3Apositive%3Ainterstitial_fluid_volume> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/capillary_filtration_rate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/interstitial_fluid_volume> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-117 (21.2: Anatomy of the Lymphatic and Immune Systems): 'Blood pressure causes leakage of fluid from the capillaries, resulting in the accumulation of fluid in the interstitial space'; '20 liters of plasma is released into the interstitial space of the tissues each day due to capillary filtration'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Greater capillary filtration delivers more fluid to the interstitial compartment, expanding its volume"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/interstitial_fluid_volume> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lymph_drainage_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ainterstitial_fluid_volume%3Apositive%3Alymph_drainage_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/interstitial_fluid_volume> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lymph_drainage_rate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-117 (21.2: Anatomy of the Lymphatic and Immune Systems): 'As interstitial pressure increases, the spaces between the cells open up, allowing the fluid to enter. Entry of fluid...is also enabled by the collagen filaments that anchor the capillaries to surrounding structures. As interstitial pressure increases, the filaments pull on the endothelial cell flaps, opening up them even further' | curated_mechanistic: NCBI Bookshelf NBK53445 'Pathophysiology of Edema' (opened) — increased interstitial fluid pressure increases lymph flow"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Raised interstitial fluid volume/pressure provides the driving pressure into initial lymphatics and creates radial tension on anchoring filaments that widens initial-lymphatic diameter and opens inter-endothelial flap junctions, increasing lymph flow (Guyton's interstitial-pressure/lymph-flow relationship)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_leukotriene_c4> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/capillary_permeability> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_leukotriene_c4%3Apositive%3Acapillary_permeability> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_leukotriene_c4> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/capillary_permeability> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-118 (21.3: Barrier Defenses and the Innate Immune Response): 'Leukotrienes attract neutrophils from the blood by chemotaxis and increase vascular permeability.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Leukotrienes act on endothelial cells to increase vascular permeability, promoting fluid and protein extravasation at sites of inflammation"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_allergen_specific_ige> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_histamine> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_allergen_specific_ige%3Apositive%3Aplasma_histamine> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_allergen_specific_ige> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_histamine> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-120 (21.5: The Adaptive Immune Response - B Lymphocytes and Antibodies): 'if a person is allergic to peanuts, there will be peanut-specific IgE bound to their mast cells. In this person, eating peanuts will cause the mast cells to degranulate, sometimes causing severe allergic reactions' | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-42.4 (42.4: Disruptions in the Immune System): 'IgE molecules on mast cells bind the antigen via their variable domains and stimulate the mast cell to release the modified amino acids histamine and serotonin' — confirms IgE-triggered mast cell degranulation releases histamine"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Allergen-specific IgE bound to mast-cell Fc receptors; allergen cross-linking triggers mast cell degranulation and histamine release"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_specific_antibody> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/blood_viral_load> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_specific_antibody%3Anegative%3Ablood_viral_load> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_specific_antibody> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/blood_viral_load> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-121 (21.6: The Immune Response Against Pathogens): 'As the antibody levels rise, the virus levels decline, and this is a sign that the immune response is being at least partially effective' — seroconversion demonstrates the reciprocal causal relationship."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Antibody-mediated neutralization coats virions, blocking receptor binding and preventing infection of new cells, reducing circulating virus titre (seroconversion)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/epinephrine> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mean_arterial_pressure> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aepinephrine%3Apositive%3Amean_arterial_pressure> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/epinephrine> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mean_arterial_pressure> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-122 (21.7: Diseases Associated with Depressed or Overactive Immune Responses): 'epinephrine raises blood pressure and relaxes bronchial smooth muscle, it is routinely used to counteract the effects of anaphylaxis' | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.5 (37.5: Endocrine Glands): 'Epinephrine and norepinephrine increase heart rate, breathing rate, cardiac muscle contractions, blood pressure, and blood glucose levels.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-42.4 (42.4: Disruptions in the Immune System): 'If not treated with epinephrine to counter the blood pressure and breathing effects, this condition can be fatal' — epinephrine raises blood pressure in anaphylaxis"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Epinephrine acts on adrenergic receptors to increase heart rate, cardiac output, and peripheral vasoconstriction, raising blood pressure"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/t_cell_immune_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acortisol%3Anegative%3At_cell_immune_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/t_cell_immune_activity> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-123 (21.8: Transplantation and Cancer Immunology): 'hormones such as cortisol (naturally produced by the adrenal cortex) and prednisone (synthetic) are well known for their abilities to suppress T cell immune mechanisms'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Glucocorticoids (cortisol) suppress T cell activation, proliferation, and effector function via glucocorticoid receptor-mediated repression of cytokine genes and T cell signaling pathways"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bronchiole_smooth_muscle_tone> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bronchiole_airflow> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abronchiole_smooth_muscle_tone%3Anegative%3Abronchiole_airflow> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bronchiole_smooth_muscle_tone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bronchiole_airflow> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-124 (22.2: Organs and Structures of the Respiratory System): 'This muscular wall can change the size of the tubing to increase or decrease airflow through the tube' (bronchioles); asthma section: 'bronchospasms (constriction of the bronchioles), which can inhibit air from entering the lungs'."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Increased bronchiole smooth muscle contraction narrows the bronchiole lumen, reducing airflow through the tube; relaxation widens lumen and increases airflow."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alveolar_surfactant_concentration> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alveolar_surface_tension> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aalveolar_surfactant_concentration%3Anegative%3Aalveolar_surface_tension> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alveolar_surfactant_concentration> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alveolar_surface_tension> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"some of the cells lining the alveoli secrete a material called surfactant that dramatically lowers the surface tension of the alveolar lining layer\" (West's Respiratory Physiology, 11th ed., ch 1)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "secreted surfactant lowers the surface tension of the alveolar lining layer"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/core_temperature> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alveolar_ventilation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acore_temperature%3Apositive%3Aalveolar_ventilation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/core_temperature> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alveolar_ventilation> ; pmo:sign "+"
