    ; pmo:mechanism "low alveolar PO2 acts directly on the small pulmonary arteriole smooth muscle, causing contraction and narrowing; high PO2 dilates"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alveolar_pco2> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bronchiole_diameter> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aalveolar_pco2%3Apositive%3Abronchiole_diameter> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alveolar_pco2> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bronchiole_diameter> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-127 (22.5: Gas Exchange): 'A greater partial pressure of carbon dioxide in the alveoli causes the bronchioles to increase their diameter … allowing carbon dioxide to be exhaled from the body at a greater rate'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "higher alveolar PCO2 stimulates bronchodilation, increasing airway diameter to allow more CO2-laden air to be exhaled"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_ph> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hemoglobin_o2_saturation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aarterial_ph%3Apositive%3Ahemoglobin_o2_saturation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_ph> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hemoglobin_o2_saturation> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-128 (22.6: Transport of Gases): 'The Bohr effect is a phenomenon that arises from the relationship between pH and oxygen's affinity for hemoglobin: A lower, more acidic pH promotes oxygen dissociation from hemoglobin. In contrast, a higher, or more basic, pH inhibits oxygen dissociation from hemoglobin.' | textbook corroboration: Jakubowski & Flatt, Fundamentals of Biochemistry Vol I §biochem-5.03.4 (Summary); Jakubowski & Flatt, Fundamentals of Biochemistry Vol I §biochem-5.03.7 (Summary): 'The Bohr effect, where a decrease in pH shifts the binding curve to favor oxygen release, is highlighted as a critical regulatory mechanism in oxygen delivery to tissues.' Corroborates arterial_ph → hemoglobin_o2_saturation (+).; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-39.4 (39.4: Transport of Gases in Human Bodily Fluids): 'This increase in carbon dioxide and subsequent decrease in pH reduce the affinity of hemoglobin for oxygen. The oxygen dissociates from the Hb molecule, shifting the oxygen dissociation curve to the right.' Lower pH reduces Hb-O2 affinity and saturation (Bohr effect)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Bohr effect: higher (more alkaline) blood pH increases hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen, raising Hb-O2 saturation; conversely, lower (more acidic) pH promotes O2 dissociation from heme, reducing saturation"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/core_temperature> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hemoglobin_o2_saturation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acore_temperature%3Anegative%3Ahemoglobin_o2_saturation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/core_temperature> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hemoglobin_o2_saturation> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-128 (22.6: Transport of Gases): 'a higher temperature promotes hemoglobin and oxygen to dissociate faster, whereas a lower temperature inhibits dissociation' | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-39.4 (39.4: Transport of Gases in Human Bodily Fluids): 'A similar shift in the curve also results from an increase in body temperature. Increased temperature, such as from increased activity of skeletal muscle, causes the affinity of hemoglobin for oxygen to be reduced.' Higher temperature reduces Hb-O2 saturation."
    ; pmo:mechanism "higher body temperature promotes faster dissociation of oxygen from hemoglobin, reducing Hb-O2 saturation (right-shift of the O2-Hb dissociation curve)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/esophageal_luminal_pressure> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lower_esophageal_sphincter_tone> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aesophageal_luminal_pressure%3Anegative%3Alower_esophageal_sphincter_tone> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/esophageal_luminal_pressure> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lower_esophageal_sphincter_tone> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-133 (23.4: The Mouth, Pharynx, and Esophagus): 'distention of the esophagus initiates a short reflex relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter that allows the bolus to pass into the stomach'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Distension of the esophagus by a bolus triggers a reflex relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter (receptive relaxation), allowing the bolus to pass into the stomach"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pancreatic_bicarbonate_secretion> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/duodenal_chyme_acidity> .
