    ; pmo:mechanism "skeletal muscle (lean mass) is the dominant site of resting energy expenditure; loss of muscle mass directly reduces whole-body basal metabolic rate"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cck> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_pancreatic_lipase> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_cck%3Apositive%3Aplasma_pancreatic_lipase> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cck> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_pancreatic_lipase> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-140 (24.4: Lipid Metabolism): 'CCK stimulates the release of pancreatic lipase from the pancreas'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CCK released by intestinal mucosa in response to chyme stimulates release of pancreatic lipase from the pancreas"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_motilin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/small_intestine_motility> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_motilin%3Apositive%3Asmall_intestine_motility> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_motilin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/small_intestine_motility> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "PMID:456236 / DOI:10.1007/BF01489315 (Vantrappen et al., Motilin and the interdigestive migrating myoelectric complex in man): intravenous infusion of pure motilin induced a premature phase-III activity front in 12/16 fasting normal volunteers (endpoint: intestinal migrating-motor-complex activity). Pharmacological corroboration: the motilin-receptor agonist erythromycin induces phase-III MMC-like activity in the human GI tract (PMID:3943442)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Plasma motilin acts on the smooth-muscle/enteric motilin receptor (MLNR/GPR38) to trigger a premature phase-III migrating-motor-complex contractile front that originates gastroduodenally and propagates into the proximal small intestine during the fasting (interdigestive) state; the intrinsic distal-small-intestinal MMC front is motilin-independent, so the sign holds for the motilin-driven proximal front, not for fed-state intestinal motility."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urine_osmolality> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aadh%3Apositive%3Aurine_osmolality> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urine_osmolality> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-145 (25.2: Physical Characteristics of Urine): 'insufficient pituitary antidiuretic hormone (ADH) release or insufficient numbers of ADH receptors in the collecting ducts means that too few water channels are inserted... Insufficient numbers of water channels (aquaporins) reduce water absorption, resulting in high volumes of very dilute urine.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ADH (vasopressin) inserts aquaporin-2 water channels into the apical membrane of collecting-duct principal cells, increasing water reabsorption from the tubular lumen; higher ADH concentrates urine (raises urine osmolality), while insufficient ADH release or receptor deficiency yields high volumes of very dilute urine (diabetes insipidus)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_glucose> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_glucose%3Apositive%3Aurinary_glucose> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_glucose> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"When serum glucose concentrations exceed the renal threshold of approximately 180 mg/dL (10 mmol/L), osmotic diuresis leads to dehydration and loss of electrolytes\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 36, p 1436"
    ; pmo:mechanism "hyperglycemia exceeds renal threshold → tubular glucose overflow → glycosuria"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_osmolality> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldosterone> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_osmolality%3Anegative%3Aaldosterone> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_osmolality> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldosterone> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-157 (26.4: Electrolyte Balance): 'In a negative feedback loop, increased osmolality of the ECF (which follows aldosterone-stimulated sodium absorption) inhibits the release of the hormone.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "elevated ECF osmolality (following aldosterone-driven Na reabsorption) inhibits further aldosterone release as negative feedback"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mean_arterial_pressure> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldosterone> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amean_arterial_pressure%3Anegative%3Aaldosterone> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mean_arterial_pressure> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldosterone> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-157 (26.4: Electrolyte Balance): 'Aldosterone is released if blood levels of potassium increase, if blood levels of sodium severely decrease, or if blood pressure decreases.' | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.5 (37.5: Endocrine Glands): 'Aldosterone release from the adrenal cortex is stimulated by a decrease in blood concentrations of sodium ions, blood volume, or blood pressure, or by an increase in blood potassium levels.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "decreased blood pressure is a direct stimulus for aldosterone release"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_potassium> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bicarbonate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_potassium%3Anegative%3Aplasma_bicarbonate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_potassium> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bicarbonate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-158 (26.5: Acid-Base Balance): 'If more potassium is present than normal, potassium, rather than the hydrogen ions, will be exchanged, and...less bicarbonate is conserved.' | curated_mechanistic: Deranged Physiology 'Type 4 (hyperkalaemic) renal tubular acidosis' (opened) — hyperkalemia impairs ammoniagenesis/NH4+ excretion; correcting hyperkalemia corrects the acidosis"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Hyperkalemia impairs renal proximal-tubule ammoniagenesis and ammonium reabsorption (K+ competes at the Na-K-2Cl cotransporter), reducing ammonia-dependent acid excretion and bicarbonate regeneration -> metabolic acidosis (type-4 RTA), lowering plasma [HCO3-]. (The older 'K+/H+ competition at Na+/H+ exchange' account is a sign-preserving oversimplification.)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_chloride> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bicarbonate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_chloride%3Anegative%3Aplasma_bicarbonate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_chloride> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bicarbonate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-158 (26.5: Acid-Base Balance): 'lost chloride results in an increased reabsorption of bicarbonate by the renal system.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Chloride loss (hypochloremia) prompts the kidney to reabsorb bicarbonate in place of the lost chloride ions to maintain electrochemical neutrality, raising plasma [HCO3-]; thus lower plasma [Cl-] causes higher [HCO3-] — i.e. plasma_chloride negatively affects plasma_bicarbonate."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ketone_bodies> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bicarbonate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_ketone_bodies%3Anegative%3Aplasma_bicarbonate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ketone_bodies> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_bicarbonate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-158 (26.5: Acid-Base Balance): 'elevated levels of ketones (common in unmanaged diabetes mellitus), which bind bicarbonate in the filtrate and prevent its conservation.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Elevated ketone bodies in the renal filtrate bind bicarbonate ions, preventing their conservation and return to blood, thereby lowering plasma [HCO3-]."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/acth> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldosterone> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aacth%3Apositive%3Aaldosterone> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/acth> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldosterone> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"GRA is a form of hyperaldosteronism in which the hypersecretion of aldosterone can be reversed with physiologic doses of glucocorticoid...Mineralocorticoid production is regulated by ACTH instead of by the normal secretagogue, angiotensin II.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 16, p. 567"
    ; pmo:mechanism "In GRA (CYP11B1/CYP11B2 chimeric gene GoF), ACTH drives aldosterone synthase → aldosterone excess; glucocorticoid suppresses ACTH → suppresses aldosterone"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/epinephrine> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alveolar_ventilation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aepinephrine%3Apositive%3Aalveolar_ventilation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/epinephrine> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alveolar_ventilation> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-159 (26.6: Disorders of Acid-Base Balance): 'An elevated respiratory rate leading to hyperventilation can be due to extreme emotional upset or fear, fever, infections, hypoxia, or abnormally high levels of catecholamines, such as epinephrine and norepinephrine.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Elevated catecholamines (epinephrine, norepinephrine) stimulate respiratory rate and depth, producing hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ldl_cholesterol> .
