    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-165 (28.4: Fetal Development): 'The lungs begin producing surfactant, a substance that reduces surface tension in the lungs and assists proper lung expansion after birth.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Pulmonary surfactant adsorbs at the air-liquid interface of alveoli and reduces surface tension, lowering the collapsing pressure and enabling alveolar expansion."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/progesterone> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_fsh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aprogesterone%3Anegative%3Apituitary_fsh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/progesterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_fsh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-166 (28.5: Changes During Pregnancy, Labor, and Birth): 'Like estrogen, progesterone suppresses FSH and LH.' | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-43.3 (43.3: Human Reproductive Anatomy and Gametogenesis): 'Progesterone assists in endometrial regrowth and inhibition of FSH and LH release.' Also: 'The progesterone facilitates the regrowth of the uterine lining and inhibits the release of further FSH and LH.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-43.5 (43.5: Human Pregnancy and Birth): 'Hormonal methods use synthetic progesterone (sometimes in combination with estrogen), to inhibit the hypothalamus from releasing FSH or LH, and thus prevent an egg from being available for fertilization.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Progesterone directly suppresses pituitary FSH secretion (parallel to estradiol), preventing ovulation during pregnancy"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_oxytocin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_prostaglandin_e2> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_oxytocin%3Apositive%3Aplasma_prostaglandin_e2> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_oxytocin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_prostaglandin_e2> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-166 (28.5: Changes During Pregnancy, Labor, and Birth): 'uterine contractions...stimulate the secretion of prostaglandins from fetal membranes. Like oxytocin, prostaglandins also enhance uterine contractile strength.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Uterine contractions triggered by oxytocin stimulate prostaglandin secretion from fetal membranes, further amplifying contractile activity"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mean_arterial_pressure> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_protein> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amean_arterial_pressure%3Apositive%3Aurinary_protein> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mean_arterial_pressure> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_protein> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-5.2 (5.2: Passive Transport): 'One of the effects of high blood pressure is the appearance of protein in the urine, which abnormally high pressure squeezes through.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Elevated blood pressure increases the hydrostatic pressure across the glomerular filtration barrier, forcing protein that would normally be retained into the renal tubular filtrate and thence into urine."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_albumin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_oncotic_pressure> .
