    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-5.2 (5.2: Passive Transport): 'Animals also have high albumin concentrations, which the liver produces, in their blood. This protein is too large to pass easily through plasma membranes and is a major factor in controlling the osmotic pressures applied to tissues.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Albumin, produced by the liver and too large to cross plasma membranes easily, is the principal determinant of plasma colloid osmotic (oncotic) pressure, which opposes capillary fluid filtration into tissues."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_complex_i_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_respiratory_chain_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amitochondrial_complex_i_activity%3Apositive%3Amitochondrial_respiratory_chain_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_complex_i_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_respiratory_chain_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-7.1 (7.1: Energy in Living Systems): 'Most of the ATP generated during glucose catabolism... is derived from... chemiosmosis, which takes place in mitochondria'; NAD+ accepts electrons from organic molecules and passes them into the respiratory chain; in type 2 diabetes 'the oxidation efficiency of NADH is reduced, impacting oxidative phosphorylation'."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Complex I (NADH:ubiquinone oxidoreductase) oxidizes NADH and feeds electrons into the mitochondrial electron-transport chain; greater complex I activity drives greater overall respiratory chain electron flux."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_respiratory_chain_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_atp_synthase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amitochondrial_respiratory_chain_activity%3Apositive%3Amitochondrial_atp_synthase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_respiratory_chain_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_atp_synthase_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-7.1 (7.1: Energy in Living Systems): 'chemiosmosis... is used to generate 90 percent of the ATP made during glucose catabolism'; 'The production of ATP using the process of chemiosmosis is called oxidative phosphorylation'; reduced NADH oxidation 'impact[s] oxidative phosphorylation'."
    ; pmo:mechanism "The respiratory chain generates a proton electrochemical gradient across the inner mitochondrial membrane; this proton-motive force drives ATP synthase (complex V) to phosphorylate ADP to ATP via chemiosmosis (oxidative phosphorylation)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/citrate_synthase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acellular_atp_level%3Anegative%3Acitrate_synthase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/citrate_synthase_activity> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-7.3 (7.3: Oxidation of Pyruvate and the Citric Acid Cycle): 'The rate of this reaction is controlled by negative feedback and the amount of ATP available. If ATP levels increase, the rate of this reaction decreases.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Allosteric product-inhibition: high ATP negatively regulates citrate synthase (step 1 of citric acid cycle) by feedback inhibition, slowing entry of acetyl-CoA into the cycle"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_adp_level> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/isocitrate_dehydrogenase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acellular_adp_level%3Apositive%3Aisocitrate_dehydrogenase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_adp_level> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/isocitrate_dehydrogenase_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-7.3 (7.3: Oxidation of Pyruvate and the Citric Acid Cycle): 'This step is also regulated by negative feedback from ATP and NADH and a positive effect of ADP.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Allosteric activation: ADP signals low energy status and positively activates isocitrate dehydrogenase (step 3 of TCA cycle), accelerating NADH production and ATP regeneration"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/isocitrate_dehydrogenase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acellular_atp_level%3Anegative%3Aisocitrate_dehydrogenase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/isocitrate_dehydrogenase_activity> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-7.3 (7.3: Oxidation of Pyruvate and the Citric Acid Cycle): 'This step is also regulated by negative feedback from ATP and NADH and a positive effect of ADP.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Allosteric feedback inhibition: high ATP suppresses isocitrate dehydrogenase activity (step 3 of TCA cycle), slowing the cycle when energy is abundant"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alpha_ketoglutarate_dehydrogenase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acellular_atp_level%3Anegative%3Aalpha_ketoglutarate_dehydrogenase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_atp_level> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alpha_ketoglutarate_dehydrogenase_activity> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-7.3 (7.3: Oxidation of Pyruvate and the Citric Acid Cycle): 'The enzyme that catalyzes step four is regulated by feedback inhibition of ATP, succinyl CoA, and NADH.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Feedback inhibition: ATP inhibits alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase complex (step 4 of TCA cycle), reducing conversion of alpha-ketoglutarate to succinyl-CoA when cellular energy is high"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_nadh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_complex_i_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acellular_nadh%3Apositive%3Amitochondrial_complex_i_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_nadh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mitochondrial_complex_i_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-7.4 (7.4: Oxidative Phosphorylation): 'two electrons are carried to the first complex via NADH'; NADH provides the electron input that sustains Complex I catalytic activity"
    ; pmo:mechanism "NADH donates two electrons to Complex I (NADH dehydrogenase), driving its redox activity and proton-pumping function"
    .
