    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-34.2 (34.2: Nutrition and Energy Production): 'The pyruvate produced during glycolysis is used to synthesize fatty acids. When there is more glucose in the body than required, the resulting excess pyruvate is converted into molecules that eventually result in the synthesis of fatty acids within the body. These fatty acids are stored in adipose cells.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "excess plasma glucose enters glycolysis yielding pyruvate, which is used for de novo fatty acid synthesis; these fatty acids are stored in adipose cells, increasing fat mass"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/acetylcholinesterase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/synaptic_acetylcholine_concentration> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aacetylcholinesterase_activity%3Anegative%3Asynaptic_acetylcholine_concentration> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/acetylcholinesterase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/synaptic_acetylcholine_concentration> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-35.2 (35.2: How Neurons Communicate): 'some drugs that are given to Alzheimer's patients work by inhibiting acetylcholinesterase, the enzyme that degrades acetylcholine. This inhibition of the enzyme essentially increases neurotransmission at synapses that release acetylcholine.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Acetylcholinesterase hydrolyzes acetylcholine in the synaptic cleft into choline and acetate, reducing the available ACh concentration; inhibiting this enzyme prolongs ACh presence (basis of AChE-inhibitor drugs used in Alzheimer's disease)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/synaptic_acetylcholine_concentration> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/postsynaptic_membrane_potential> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asynaptic_acetylcholine_concentration%3Apositive%3Apostsynaptic_membrane_potential> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/synaptic_acetylcholine_concentration> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/postsynaptic_membrane_potential> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-35.2 (35.2: How Neurons Communicate): 'when acetylcholine is released at the synapse between a nerve and muscle (called the neuromuscular junction) by a presynaptic neuron, it causes postsynaptic Na+ channels to open. Na+ enters the postsynaptic cell and causes the postsynaptic membrane to depolarize. This depolarization is called an excitatory postsynaptic potential (EPSP).'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Acetylcholine binds to ligand-gated Na+ channels on the postsynaptic membrane (neuromuscular junction), opening them and allowing Na+ to enter the postsynaptic cell, causing depolarization (excitatory postsynaptic potential, EPSP), i.e. a rise in membrane potential toward threshold."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/synaptic_gaba_concentration> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/postsynaptic_membrane_potential> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asynaptic_gaba_concentration%3Anegative%3Apostsynaptic_membrane_potential> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/synaptic_gaba_concentration> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/postsynaptic_membrane_potential> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-35.2 (35.2: How Neurons Communicate): 'when the neurotransmitter GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid) is released from a presynaptic neuron, it binds to and opens Cl- channels. Cl- ions enter the cell and hyperpolarizes the membrane, making the neuron less likely to fire an action potential.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GABA binds to and opens Cl- channels on the postsynaptic membrane; Cl- ions enter the cell and hyperpolarize the membrane (inhibitory postsynaptic potential, IPSP), reducing the membrane potential and making the neuron less likely to fire."
    .
