    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-40.2 (Components of the Blood): 'Many of the clotting factors require vitamin K to work, and vitamin K deficiency can lead to problems with blood clotting.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Vitamin K is required for hepatic gamma-carboxylation of glutamate residues on clotting factors II (prothrombin), VII, IX, and X; without sufficient vitamin K these zymogens are non-functional (PIVKA-II forms instead), reducing thrombin generation in the coagulation cascade."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_allergen_specific_ige> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_serotonin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_allergen_specific_ige%3Apositive%3Aplasma_serotonin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_allergen_specific_ige> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_serotonin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-42.4 (42.4: Disruptions in the Immune System): 'IgE molecules on mast cells bind the antigen via their variable domains and stimulate the mast cell to release the modified amino acids histamine and serotonin'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Allergen-specific IgE bound to mast-cell Fc receptors; allergen cross-linking triggers mast cell degranulation, releasing both histamine and serotonin as inflammatory mediators"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcium> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aestradiol%3Apositive%3Aplasma_calcium> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcium> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-43.3 (43.3: Human Reproductive Anatomy and Gametogenesis): 'Estrogen is involved in calcium metabolism and, without it, blood levels of calcium decrease. To replenish the blood, calcium is lost from bone which may decrease the bone density and lead to osteoporosis.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Estrogen promotes intestinal calcium absorption and renal calcium conservation; its withdrawal at menopause decreases blood calcium levels, causing compensatory mobilisation of calcium from bone"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/calcineurin_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/t_cell_activation_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acalcineurin_activity%3Apositive%3At_cell_activation_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/calcineurin_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/t_cell_activation_rate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Jakubowski & Flatt, Fundamentals of Biochemistry Vol I §biochem-4.8.4 (Protein Folding In Vivo): 'calcineurin, an important protein (with phosphatase activity) in immune cells (T cells) required for T cell function'; the FKBP:FK506 and cyclophilin:cyclosporin complexes inhibit calcineurin and thereby prevent T cell activation."
    ; pmo:mechanism "Calcineurin (protein phosphatase 3 catalytic subunit alpha) is required for T cell activation; its phosphatase activity dephosphorylates NFAT, enabling nuclear translocation and T cell function."
    .
