<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/metabolic_heat_production> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/core_temperature> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ametabolic_heat_production%3Apositive%3Acore_temperature> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/metabolic_heat_production> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/core_temperature> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Stolwijk 1971 heat-balance equations; HumMod (heat production -> body temperature) | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-054 (10.3: Skeletal Muscle): 'when sustained muscle movement causes body temperature to rise' — ATP breakdown during muscle contraction produces heat that raises core temperature.; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-143 (24.7: Energy and Heat Balance): approximately 60% of ATP energy is in the form of heat used to maintain body temperature; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-167 (28.6: Adjustments of the Infant at Birth and Postnatal Stages): brown adipose tissue mitochondria 'engage in cellular respiration reactions that produce less ATP and more heat than standard cellular respiration reactions', raising body temperature."
    ; pmo:mechanism "heat generated by oxidative metabolism and shivering raises body heat content, hence core temperature"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/metabolic_rate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/metabolic_heat_production> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ametabolic_rate%3Apositive%3Ametabolic_heat_production> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/metabolic_rate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/metabolic_heat_production> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; dcterms:source "thermoregulation: metabolic rate is the source term for metabolic heat production | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-143 (24.7: Energy and Heat Balance): 10% of daily energy expenditure is for body thermoregulation/temperature control; metabolic rate links to heat generation"
    ; pmo:mechanism "increased oxidative metabolism dissipates energy as heat (obligatory thermogenesis)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/core_temperature> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sweating> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acore_temperature%3Apositive%3Asweating> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/core_temperature> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sweating> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Stolwijk 1971 (sweat command ~ positive core error); HumMod sweat rate control | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-003 (1.4: Functions of Human Life): 'increased body temperature can cause the responses of sweating … in order to decrease body temperature'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-004 (1.5: Requirements for Human Life): 'One of the body's responses to heat is, of course, sweating.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-005 (1.6: Homeostasis): 'As blood flow to the skin increases, sweat glands are activated to increase their output'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-026 (5.3: Accessory Structures of the Skin): 'When the body becomes warm, sudoriferous glands produce sweat to cool the body' — body warmth drives eccrine sweat secretion.; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-027 (5.4: Functions of the Integumentary System): 'If the body becomes excessively warm... sweat glands will be stimulated by the sympathetic nervous system to produce large amounts of sweat'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-143 (24.7: Energy and Heat Balance): hypothalamus initiates sweating to allow evaporation of water on the skin when temperature is too high; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-33.3 (Homeostasis): 'The hypothalamus maintains the set point for body temperature through reflexes that cause vasodilation and sweating when the body is too warm.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "hypothalamic warm signal drives eccrine sweat secretion above the sweating threshold"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sweating> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/core_temperature> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asweating%3Anegative%3Acore_temperature> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sweating> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/core_temperature> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep: evaporative heat loss; Stolwijk evaporative cooling term | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-004 (1.5: Requirements for Human Life): 'As sweat evaporates from skin, it removes some thermal energy from the body, cooling it.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-026 (5.3: Accessory Structures of the Skin): 'Eccrine glands are a primary component of thermoregulation in humans and thus help to maintain homeostasis' — sweat production lowers core temperature.; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-027 (5.4: Functions of the Integumentary System): 'When the sweat evaporates from the skin surface, the body is cooled as body heat is dissipated'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-068 (12.2: Basic Structures and Function of the Nervous System): 'When you are hot, sweating helps cool your body down. That is a homeostatic mechanism.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-143 (24.7: Energy and Heat Balance): evaporation of water (sweat) takes heat from the skin, cooling it"
    ; pmo:mechanism "evaporation of sweat removes latent heat from the skin, lowering body heat content"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/core_temperature> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skin_blood_flow> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acore_temperature%3Apositive%3Askin_blood_flow> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/core_temperature> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skin_blood_flow> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Stolwijk 1971 vasomotor control; HumMod (core temperature -> skin blood flow) | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-003 (1.4: Functions of Human Life): 'increased body temperature can cause … the dilation of blood vessels in the skin in order to decrease body temperature'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-004 (1.5: Requirements for Human Life): 'The body responds to cold by reducing blood circulation to the extremities... in order to prevent blood from cooling there and so that the body's core can stay warm.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-005 (1.6: Homeostasis): 'Blood vessels in the skin begin to dilate allowing more blood from the body core to flow to the surface of the skin allowing the heat to radiate into the environment'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-027 (5.4: Functions of the Integumentary System): 'arterioles in the dermis dilate so that excess heat carried by the blood can dissipate through the skin and into the surrounding environment'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-115 (20.6: Circulatory Pathways): 'When the ambient temperature is warm, more blood is diverted to the superficial veins where heat can be more easily dissipated to the environment. In colder weather, there is more constriction of the superficial veins and blood is diverted deeper where the body can retain more of the heat.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-143 (24.7: Energy and Heat Balance): hypothalamus initiates increasing circulation to surface to allow dissipation of heat through skin when temperature too high; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-33.3 (Homeostasis): 'Vasodilation brings more blood and heat to the body surface, facilitating radiation and evaporative heat loss'; 'Vasoconstriction reduces blood flow in peripheral blood vessels.'"
    ; pmo:mechanism "warm signal causes active cutaneous vasodilation, increasing skin perfusion"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skin_blood_flow> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/core_temperature> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Askin_blood_flow%3Anegative%3Acore_temperature> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skin_blood_flow> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/core_temperature> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep: skin blood flow sets dry heat loss; Stolwijk core->skin conduction | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-027 (5.4: Functions of the Integumentary System): 'the arterioles constrict to minimize heat loss... passive heat loss is prevented, and internal organs and structures remain warm'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-143 (24.7: Energy and Heat Balance): increased blood circulation to skin surface dissipates heat, lowering core temperature"
    ; pmo:mechanism "higher skin perfusion carries core heat to the surface for radiative/convective dissipation"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/core_temperature> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/shivering> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acore_temperature%3Anegative%3Ashivering> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/core_temperature> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/shivering> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Stolwijk 1971 (shivering command ~ negative core error); HumMod shivering control | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-004 (1.5: Requirements for Human Life): 'One response to cold is shivering, which is random muscle movement that generates heat.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-005 (1.6: Homeostasis): 'activation of the brain's heat-gain center by exposure to cold reduces blood flow to the skin... the brain triggers an increase in random signals to skeletal muscles, causing them to contract and producing shivering'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-022 (4.5: Muscle Tissue and Motion): shivering is an involuntary contraction of skeletal muscles in response to perceived lower than normal body temperature; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-143 (24.7: Energy and Heat Balance): if temperature falls below set core temperature, hypothalamus initiates shivering to generate heat; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-33.3 (Homeostasis): 'vasoconstriction and shivering when the body is too cold' — hypothalamic thermoregulatory reflex."
    ; pmo:mechanism "shivering is engaged by the cold (low-temperature) signal; below the threshold, lower core temperature raises shivering"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/shivering> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/metabolic_heat_production> .
