    ; dcterms:source "Guyton & Hall: shivering can raise heat production several-fold; HumMod shivering thermogenesis | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-004 (1.5: Requirements for Human Life): 'shivering, which is random muscle movement that generates heat.'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-005 (1.6: Homeostasis): 'The muscle contractions of shivering release heat while using up ATP'; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-022 (4.5: Muscle Tissue and Motion): skeletal muscles generate heat as a byproduct of their contraction, thus participating in thermal homeostasis via shivering; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-054 (10.3: Skeletal Muscle): 'in cases of extreme cold, when shivering produces random skeletal muscle contractions to generate heat' — shivering thermogenesis raises metabolic heat production.; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-143 (24.7: Energy and Heat Balance): shivering uses more energy and generates more heat"
    ; pmo:mechanism "involuntary skeletal-muscle tremor performs no external work, so all energy appears as heat"
