    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (ceruloplasmin as major Cu carrier, ~90% of serum copper); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology; Harrison's"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ceruloplasmin carries roughly 90% of circulating copper; measured (total) serum copper tracks holo-ceruloplasmin, so lower ceruloplasmin lowers the dominant ceruloplasmin-bound serum copper pool"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/atp7a_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_copper> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aatp7a_activity%3Apositive%3Aplasma_copper> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/atp7a_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_copper> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "OMIM #309400 Menkes disease (ATP7A); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (intestinal copper absorption/efflux); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"
    ; pmo:mechanism "enterocyte ATP7A effluxes absorbed dietary copper across the basolateral membrane into portal blood; ATP7A loss-of-function traps copper in enterocytes and blocks copper entry into the circulation, lowering plasma copper"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_copper> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ceruloplasmin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_copper%3Apositive%3Aplasma_ceruloplasmin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_copper> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ceruloplasmin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "OMIM #309400 Menkes disease (low ceruloplasmin secondary to copper deficiency); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry; Harrison's | opened authoritative source: StatPearls NBK554422 (Biochemistry, Ceruloplasmin): copper binding is essential for correct folding of ceruloplasmin; without sufficient copper, apo-ceruloplasmin is degraded, so copper deficiency lowers circulating holo-ceruloplasmin (copper-repletion reversibility and Menkes are interventional corroborations; held to curated_mechanistic on the opened primary)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "circulating copper is the substrate for hepatic copper loading of apoceruloplasmin; copper-deficient states (e.g. Menkes) provide insufficient copper for holo-ceruloplasmin formation, lowering secreted ceruloplasmin"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/atp7a_activity> obo:RO_0002411 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_zinc> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aatp7a_activity%3Aunknown%3Aplasma_zinc> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/atp7a_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_zinc> ; pmo:sign "?"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (copper–zinc antagonism in intestinal absorption); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology"
    ; pmo:mechanism "copper deficiency derepresses enterocyte metallothionein, which can secondarily alter zinc handling; the direction of the effect on plasma zinc is not well established, so the sign is flagged ambiguous"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/trpm6_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_magnesium_reabsorption> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atrpm6_activity%3Apositive%3Arenal_magnesium_reabsorption> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/trpm6_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_magnesium_reabsorption> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "OMIM #602014 hypomagnesemia 1, intestinal (TRPM6); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (DCT magnesium reabsorption); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"
    ; pmo:mechanism "TRPM6 is the apical Mg2+ entry channel of the distal convoluted tubule that mediates active transcellular magnesium reabsorption; TRPM6 loss-of-function causes renal magnesium wasting (and impairs intestinal Mg2+ absorption)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_magnesium_reabsorption> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_magnesium> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Arenal_magnesium_reabsorption%3Apositive%3Aplasma_magnesium> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_magnesium_reabsorption> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_magnesium> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (renal control of plasma magnesium); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (disorders of magnesium balance) | opened authoritative review: PMC7816272 (Mechanisms coupling Na and Mg reabsorption in the DCT): the DCT is the final determinant of urinary Mg2+ (later segments cannot reabsorb Mg2+), so greater TRPM6-mediated DCT reabsorption raises plasma Mg while reduced reabsorption causes renal wasting/hypomagnesemia (TRPM6 LoF / diuretic renal-wasting are interventional corroborations; held to curated_mechanistic on the opened primary)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "the distal convoluted tubule sets final urinary magnesium excretion; greater tubular Mg2+ reabsorption retains magnesium and raises plasma magnesium, whereas renal wasting lowers it"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_magnesium> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pth> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_magnesium%3Apositive%3Apth> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_magnesium> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pth> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (severe hypomagnesemia impairs PTH secretion/action -> hypocalcaemia, reversible with Mg repletion); OMIM #602014 (secondary hypocalcaemia); Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology"
    ; pmo:mechanism "moderate hypomagnesemia impairs PTH secretion and induces end-organ PTH resistance (low intracellular Mg2+ disrupts the Gs/adenylyl-cyclase signalling of the parathyroid CaSR), so falling plasma magnesium lowers effective PTH — the basis of secondary hypocalcaemia in severe Mg2+ depletion; magnesium-replacement therapy in symptomatic hypomagnesemic hypoparathyroidism reverses the PTH suppression, demonstrating the source-directed intervention"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ketone_bodies> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/skeletal_muscle_protein_catabolism_rate> .
