    ; dcterms:source "Methionine synthase deficiency / homocystinuria-megaloblastic anemia cblG (OMIM:250940) — MTR LoF causes LOW plasma methionine with elevated homocysteine"
    ; pmo:mechanism "RHEA:11172 (EC 2.1.1.13): 5-methyltetrahydrofolate + L-homocysteine = tetrahydrofolate + L-methionine; L-methionine is the PRODUCT, so LoF lowers methionine (corrects the prior homocysteine->methionine-only path that mis-predicted methionine up under MTR LoF)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cardiomyocyte_intracellular_sodium> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/na_k_atpase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acardiomyocyte_intracellular_sodium%3Apositive%3Ana_k_atpase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cardiomyocyte_intracellular_sodium> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/na_k_atpase_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"A special feature of the Na+-K+ ATPase pump is that its degree of activity is strongly stimulated when excess sodium ions accumulate inside the cell membrane. In fact, the pumping activity increases approximately in proportion to the third power of this intracellular sodium concentration. That is, as the internal sodium concentration rises from 10 to 20 mEq/L, the activity of the pump does not merely double but increases about eightfold.\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch w02, p 66"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Elevated intracellular [Na+] allosterically stimulates Na+/K+-ATPase activity (approximately cubic relationship: doubling intracellular Na+ from 10 to 20 mEq/L raises pump activity ~8-fold), restoring the transmembrane sodium gradient"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/interstitial_fluid_protein> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/interstitial_fluid_colloid_osmotic_pressure> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ainterstitial_fluid_protein%3Apositive%3Ainterstitial_fluid_colloid_osmotic_pressure> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/interstitial_fluid_protein> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/interstitial_fluid_colloid_osmotic_pressure> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"it is the proteins of the plasma and interstitial fluids that are responsible for the osmotic pressures on the two sides of the capillary membrane\" and \"the average protein concentration of the interstitial fluid is usually only 40 per cent of that in plasma...the average interstitial fluid colloid osmotic pressure for this concentration of proteins is about 8 mm Hg\" — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch w05"
    ; pmo:mechanism "plasma proteins that leak into interstitium are the solutes responsible for the interstitial colloid osmotic (oncotic) pressure; more protein → higher oncotic pressure"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lymph_drainage_rate> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/interstitial_fluid_protein> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alymph_drainage_rate%3Anegative%3Ainterstitial_fluid_protein> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lymph_drainage_rate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/interstitial_fluid_protein> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Third, the increasing interstitial fluid pressure greatly increases the rate of lymph flow...This in turn carries away the excess interstitial fluid volume and excess protein that has accumulated in the spaces\" — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch w05"
    ; pmo:mechanism "lymphatic drainage removes protein from interstitium; increased lymph flow lowers interstitial protein concentration toward steady state"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/interstitial_fluid_colloid_osmotic_pressure> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/capillary_filtration_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ainterstitial_fluid_colloid_osmotic_pressure%3Apositive%3Acapillary_filtration_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/interstitial_fluid_colloid_osmotic_pressure> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/capillary_filtration_rate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The interstitial fluid colloid osmotic pressure (Pif), which tends to cause osmosis of fluid outward through the capillary membrane\" listed as one of four Starling forces; and at the arterial end: interstitial fluid colloid osmotic pressure = 8 mm Hg contributes to 41 mm Hg total outward force — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch w05"
    ; pmo:mechanism "higher interstitial oncotic pressure creates an osmotic force pulling fluid out of capillaries into the interstitium, adding to net filtration pressure"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/capillary_hydrostatic_pressure> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/capillary_filtration_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acapillary_hydrostatic_pressure%3Apositive%3Acapillary_filtration_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/capillary_hydrostatic_pressure> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/capillary_filtration_rate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The increased cardiac output increases the capillary pressure so that fluid begins to transude out of the capillaries into the tissues\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch w07, p 242"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Higher capillary hydrostatic pressure increases the net filtration pressure driving fluid from capillary lumen into interstitial space (Starling forces)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aqueous_humor_formation_rate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intraocular_pressure> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aaqueous_humor_formation_rate%3Apositive%3Aintraocular_pressure> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aqueous_humor_formation_rate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intraocular_pressure> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The balance between formation and reabsorption of aqueous humor regulates the total volume and pressure of the intraocular fluid.\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch w16, p. 623"
    ; pmo:mechanism "increased ciliary-body secretion raises volume and pressure of anterior-chamber fluid against fixed outflow resistance"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aqueous_humor_outflow_resistance> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intraocular_pressure> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aaqueous_humor_outflow_resistance%3Apositive%3Aintraocular_pressure> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aqueous_humor_outflow_resistance> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intraocular_pressure> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The level of this pressure is determined mainly by the resistance to outflow of aqueous humor from the anterior chamber into the canal of Schlemm. This outflow resistance results from the meshwork of trabeculae.\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch w16, p. 624"
    ; pmo:mechanism "reduced drainage through trabecular meshwork into canal of Schlemm raises steady-state fluid pressure in anterior chamber"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/retinal_rhodopsin_concentration> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/retinal_photosensitivity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aretinal_rhodopsin_concentration%3Apositive%3Aretinal_photosensitivity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/retinal_rhodopsin_concentration> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/retinal_photosensitivity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "\"If a person has been in bright light for hours, large portions of the photochemicals in both the rods and the cones will have been reduced to retinal and opsins... the concentrations of the photosensitive chemicals remaining in the rods and cones are considerably reduced, and the sensitivity of the eye to light is correspondingly reduced. This is called light adaptation.\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch w16, p. 631"
    ; pmo:mechanism "rhodopsin is the light-sensitive photochemical of rods; higher rhodopsin concentration enables detection of lower light intensities (dark adaptation increases both rhodopsin and sensitivity in parallel)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_sympathetic_tone> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_tract_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Agi_sympathetic_tone%3Anegative%3Agi_tract_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_sympathetic_tone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_tract_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "In general, stimulation of the sympathetic nervous system inhibits activity of the gastrointestinal tract... It exerts its effects in two ways: (1) to a slight extent by direct effect of secreted norepinephrine to inhibit intestinal tract smooth muscle... and (2) to a major extent by an inhibitory effect of norepinephrine on the neurons of the entire enteric nervous system.  ||  \"Brunner's glands are inhibited by sympathetic stimulation; therefore, such stimulation in very excitable persons is likely to leave the duodenal bulb unprotected and is perhaps one of the factors that cause this area of the gastrointestinal tract to be the site of peptic ulcers\" — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch w21, p. 806"
    ; pmo:mechanism "sympathetic inhibition of enteric neurons decreases GI secretion"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_sympathetic_tone> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/splanchnic_blood_flow> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Agi_sympathetic_tone%3Anegative%3Asplanchnic_blood_flow> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_sympathetic_tone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/splanchnic_blood_flow> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "Sympathetic stimulation, by contrast, has a direct effect on essentially all the gastrointestinal tract to cause intense vasoconstriction of the arterioles with greatly decreased blood flow."
    ; pmo:mechanism "sympathetic norepinephrine causes arteriolar vasoconstriction in splanchnic bed → decreased blood flow"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/delta_cell_somatostatin_secretion> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_tract_motility> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Adelta_cell_somatostatin_secretion%3Anegative%3Agi_tract_motility> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/delta_cell_somatostatin_secretion> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_tract_motility> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Somatostatin decreases the motility of the stomach, duodenum, and gallbladder. Somatostatin decreases both secretion and absorption in the gastrointestinal tract.\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch w25, p 972"
    ; pmo:mechanism "somatostatin decreases motility of stomach, duodenum, and gallbladder to extend digestion period"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcium> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/myocardial_contractility> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_calcium%3Apositive%3Amyocardial_contractility> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcium> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/myocardial_contractility> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"An excess of calcium ions causes ... the heart to go toward spastic contraction. This is caused by a direct effect of calcium ions to initiate the cardiac contractile process ... Conversely, deficiency of calcium ions causes cardiac flaccidity\" -- Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 9 (Heart Muscle), p ~114"
    ; pmo:mechanism "extracellular Ca2+ availability directly initiates the cardiac actin-myosin contractile process; excess -> spastic contraction (raised inotropy), deficiency -> cardiac flaccidity"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_perfusion_pressure> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/afferent_arteriolar_resistance> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Arenal_perfusion_pressure%3Apositive%3Aafferent_arteriolar_resistance> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_perfusion_pressure> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/afferent_arteriolar_resistance> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"respond to increased wall tension or wall stretch by contraction of the vascular smooth muscle ... by raising vascular resistance, helps prevent excessive increases in renal blood flow and GFR when arterial pressure increases\" -- Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 26 (Urine Formation I), p 324"
    ; pmo:mechanism "increased renal arterial/wall pressure -> afferent smooth-muscle stretch, Ca2+ entry, contraction -> raised afferent resistance (myogenic autoregulation buffering RBF/GFR)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sympathetic_tone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/afferent_arteriolar_resistance> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asympathetic_tone%3Apositive%3Aafferent_arteriolar_resistance> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sympathetic_tone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/afferent_arteriolar_resistance> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Strong activation of the renal sympathetic nerves can constrict the renal arterioles and decrease renal blood flow and GFR\" -- Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 26 (Urine Formation I), p 322"
    ; pmo:mechanism "renal sympathetic nerve activity -> alpha-adrenergic afferent (and efferent) arteriolar constriction -> raised afferent resistance -> reduced glomerular hydrostatic pressure and GFR"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/macula_densa_nacl_signal> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/afferent_arteriolar_resistance> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amacula_densa_nacl_signal%3Apositive%3Aafferent_arteriolar_resistance> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/macula_densa_nacl_signal> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/afferent_arteriolar_resistance> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Decreased Macula Densa Sodium Chloride Causes Dilation of Afferent Arterioles ... it decreases resistance to blood flow in the afferent arterioles, which raises glomerular hydrostatic pressure\" -- Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 26 (Urine Formation I), Fig 26-18, p 324"
    ; pmo:mechanism "increased distal NaCl at macula densa -> ATP/adenosine release -> afferent arteriolar constriction -> raised afferent resistance (tubuloglomerular feedback); decreased NaCl dilates the afferent arteriole"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/right_atrial_pressure> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/afferent_arteriolar_resistance> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aright_atrial_pressure%3Anegative%3Aafferent_arteriolar_resistance> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/right_atrial_pressure> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/afferent_arteriolar_resistance> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Stretch of the atria also causes significant reflex dilation of the afferent arterioles in the kidneys ... The decreased afferent arteriolar resistance in the kidneys causes the glomerular capillary pressure to rise\" -- Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 18 (Nervous Regulation of the Circulation), p 212"
    ; pmo:mechanism "atrial stretch (raised right atrial/filling pressure) -> reflex dilation of renal afferent arterioles -> lowered afferent resistance -> raised glomerular capillary pressure and filtration (volume reflex)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/endothelial_no_production> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_vascular_resistance> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aendothelial_no_production%3Anegative%3Arenal_vascular_resistance> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/endothelial_no_production> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_vascular_resistance> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"administration of drugs that inhibit this normal formation of nitric oxide increases renal vascular resistance and decreases GFR and urinary sodium excretion\" -- Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 26 (Urine Formation I), p 322"
    ; pmo:mechanism "endothelial NO -> vascular smooth-muscle cGMP -> renal arteriolar relaxation -> lowered renal vascular resistance; NOS inhibitors raise RVR and lower GFR/Na excretion"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_prostaglandin_e2> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_prostaglandin_e2%3Apositive%3Agfr> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_prostaglandin_e2> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) and bradykinin ... cause vasodilation and increased renal blood flow and GFR\" -- Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 26 (Urine Formation I), p 322-323"
    ; pmo:mechanism "PGE2/PGI2 -> afferent arteriolar vasodilation -> increased renal blood flow and glomerular hydrostatic pressure -> increased GFR; NSAID (aspirin) inhibition reduces GFR, especially under stress"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bowmans_capsule_pressure> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abowmans_capsule_pressure%3Anegative%3Agfr> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bowmans_capsule_pressure> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gfr> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Increasing the hydrostatic pressure in Bowman's capsule reduces GFR, whereas decreasing this pressure raises GFR\" -- Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 26 (Urine Formation I), p 318"
    ; pmo:mechanism "increased hydrostatic pressure in Bowman's capsule lowers net filtration pressure -> reduced GFR (e.g. ureteral obstruction/stones); micropuncture-measured Starling force"
    .
