    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"inhibition of phosphate reabsorption by the proximal tubule\" -- Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 27 (Urine Formation II) Table 27-3, p 343"
    ; pmo:mechanism "PTH -> decreased NaPi-IIa/IIc transport maximum in proximal tubule -> reduced phosphate reabsorption -> phosphaturia"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_perfusion_pressure> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/peritubular_capillary_hydrostatic_pressure> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Arenal_perfusion_pressure%3Apositive%3Aperitubular_capillary_hydrostatic_pressure> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_perfusion_pressure> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/peritubular_capillary_hydrostatic_pressure> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Increases in arterial pressure tend to raise peritubular capillary hydrostatic pressure\" -- Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 27 (Urine Formation II) Table 27-2, p 341"
    ; pmo:mechanism "increased renal arterial pressure -> raised peritubular (and vasa recta) capillary hydrostatic pressure (buffered by autoregulation) -> feeds pressure-natriuresis reduction in reabsorption (Table 27-2: up Arterial Pressure -> up Pc)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/efferent_arteriolar_resistance> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/peritubular_capillary_hydrostatic_pressure> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aefferent_arteriolar_resistance%3Anegative%3Aperitubular_capillary_hydrostatic_pressure> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/efferent_arteriolar_resistance> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/peritubular_capillary_hydrostatic_pressure> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Increase in resistance of either the afferent or the efferent arterioles reduces peritubular capillary hydrostatic pressure ... constriction of the efferent arterioles ... lowers peritubular capillary hydrostatic pressure\" -- Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 27 (Urine Formation II) Table 27-2, p 341"
    ; pmo:mechanism "increased efferent arteriolar resistance -> reduced downstream peritubular capillary hydrostatic pressure -> raised net reabsorptive force -> increased Na/water reabsorption (angiotensin II mechanism; Table 27-2: down RE -> up Pc)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_potassium> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_potassium_excretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_potassium%3Apositive%3Arenal_potassium_excretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_potassium> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_potassium_excretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"increased plasma potassium concentration directly raises potassium secretion by the cortical collecting tubules and indirectly increases potassium secretion by raising plasma aldosterone concentration\" -- Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 29 (Renal Regulation), Fig 29-4/29-7, p 369-370"
    ; pmo:mechanism "raised plasma K+ -> stimulated basolateral Na/K-ATPase and increased cell-to-lumen K+ gradient in principal cells -> increased distal K+ secretion/excretion (direct, aldosterone-independent)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_ph> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_potassium_excretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aarterial_ph%3Apositive%3Arenal_potassium_excretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_ph> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_potassium_excretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Acute increases in hydrogen ion concentration of the extracellular fluid (acidosis) reduce potassium secretion, whereas decreased hydrogen ion concentration (alkalosis) increases potassium secretion\" -- Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 29 (Renal Regulation), p 372"
    ; pmo:mechanism "increased H+ (low pH/acidosis) reduces principal-cell Na/K-ATPase activity and K+ secretion; alkalosis (high pH) increases distal K+ secretion"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldosterone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_potassium_uptake> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aaldosterone%3Apositive%3Acellular_potassium_uptake> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldosterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cellular_potassium_uptake> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Aldosterone Increases Potassium Uptake into Cells. Increased potassium intake also stimulates secretion of aldosterone, which increases cell potassium uptake\" -- Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 29 (Renal Regulation) Table 29-1, p 366"
    ; pmo:mechanism "aldosterone -> increased Na/K-ATPase-mediated cellular K+ uptake -> lowered plasma K+ (extrarenal component of aldosterone's hypokalaemic action)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_ph> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_glutaminase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aarterial_ph%3Anegative%3Arenal_glutaminase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_ph> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_glutaminase_activity> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"An increase in extracellular fluid H+ concentration stimulates renal glutamine metabolism and, therefore, increases the formation of NH4+ and new HCO3- ... a decrease in H+ concentration has the opposite effect\" -- Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 30 (Regulation of Acid-Base Balance), p 394"
    ; pmo:mechanism "decreased pH (increased H+) -> stimulated renal glutamine metabolism/glutaminase -> increased NH4+ and new HCO3- for H+ buffering; alkalosis has the opposite effect"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcium> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/casr_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_calcium%3Apositive%3Acasr_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcium> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/casr_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Even the slightest decrease in calcium ion concentration in the extracellular fluid causes the parathyroid glands to increase their rate of secretion within minutes.\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 79 (chw36), p. 988. The CaSR is the established extracellular Ca2+ sensor mediating this control (named in the chapter reference list: Hofer AM, Brown EM: Extracellular calcium sensing; Chen RA, Goodman WG: Role of the calcium-sensing receptor)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "extracellular Ca2+ binds and activates the parathyroid chief-cell calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR), raising CaSR signalling activity; this supplies the upstream input to the existing casr_activity -(-)-> parathyroid_chief_pth_secretion edge (net: high Ca2+ -> CaSR active -> PTH secretion falls)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcium> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/parathyroid_chief_cell_proliferation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_calcium%3Anegative%3Aparathyroid_chief_cell_proliferation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcium> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/parathyroid_chief_cell_proliferation> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Even the slightest decrease in calcium ion concentration ... causes the parathyroid glands to increase their rate of secretion within minutes; if the decreased calcium concentration persists, the glands will hypertrophy, sometimes fivefold or more ... Conversely, conditions that increase the calcium ion [concentration reduce gland size].\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 79 (chw36), p. 988."
    ; pmo:mechanism "sustained low extracellular Ca2+ drives parathyroid chief-cell hypertrophy/proliferation (glands enlarge fivefold or more in rickets, pregnancy, lactation); conditions that raise Ca2+ decrease gland size -> inverse (negative) control"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pth> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/proximal_tubule_calcitriol_production> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apth%3Apositive%3Aproximal_tubule_calcitriol_production> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pth> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/proximal_tubule_calcitriol_production> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Note also in Figure 79-6 that the conversion of 25-hydroxycholecalciferol to 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol requires PTH. In the absence of PTH, almost none of the 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol is formed.\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 79 (chw36), p. 985."
    ; pmo:mechanism "PTH (via cAMP in renal proximal-tubule cells) stimulates 1-alpha-hydroxylase, driving conversion of 25-OH-D to 1,25(OH)2D3 (calcitriol); without PTH almost no calcitriol is formed"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcium> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/proximal_tubule_calcitriol_production> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_calcium%3Anegative%3Aproximal_tubule_calcitriol_production> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcium> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/proximal_tubule_calcitriol_production> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Figure 79-8 demonstrates that the plasma concentration of 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol is inversely affected by the concentration of calcium in the plasma. ... First, the calcium ion itself has a slight effect in preventing the conversion of 25-hydroxycholecalciferol to 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol.\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 79 (chw36), p. 985."
    ; pmo:mechanism "raised plasma Ca2+ inversely controls calcitriol formation: a direct inhibitory effect of the Ca2+ ion on the 25-OH-D -> 1,25(OH)2D3 conversion, plus suppression of PTH (which is required for the conversion)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tsh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thyroid_follicular_t4_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atsh%3Apositive%3Athyroid_follicular_t4_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tsh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thyroid_follicular_t4_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"1. Increased proteolysis of the thyroglobulin that has already been stored in the follicles, with resultant release of the thyroid hormones into the circulating blood ... In summary, TSH increases all the known secretory activities of the thyroid glandular cells.\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 76 (chw34), p. 938."
    ; pmo:mechanism "TSH increases proteolysis of stored thyroglobulin in the follicular cells, releasing T4/T3 into the blood; TSH increases all known secretory activities of the thyroid glandular cells"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tsh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nis_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atsh%3Apositive%3Anis_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tsh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nis_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"2. Increased activity of the iodide pump, which increases the rate of “iodide trapping” in the glandular cells, sometimes increasing the ratio of intracellular to extracellular iodide concentration ... to as much as eight times normal.\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 76 (chw34), p. 938."
    ; pmo:mechanism "TSH increases activity of the follicular-cell iodide pump (NIS/SLC5A5), raising iodide trapping; hypophysectomy greatly diminishes iodide-pump activity"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/trh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thyrotroph_tsh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atrh%3Apositive%3Athyrotroph_tsh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/trh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thyrotroph_tsh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"TRH directly affects the anterior pituitary gland cells to increase their output of TSH.\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 76 (chw34), p. 938-939."
    ; pmo:mechanism "hypothalamic TRH binds TRH receptors on anterior-pituitary thyrotrophs, activating phospholipase C / Ca2+ / DAG second messengers to increase TSH release"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thyrotroph_tsh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t3%3Anegative%3Athyrotroph_tsh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thyrotroph_tsh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Increased thyroid hormone in the body fluids decreases secretion of TSH by the anterior pituitary. When the rate of thyroid hormone secretion rises to about 1.75 times normal, the rate of TSH secretion falls essentially to zero ... increased thyroid hormone inhibits anterior pituitary secretion of TSH mainly by a direct effect on the [anterior pituitary].\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 76 (chw34), p. 939."
    ; pmo:mechanism "circulating thyroid hormone (T3 is the active form) exerts direct negative feedback on anterior-pituitary thyrotrophs to decrease TSH secretion; a rise to ~1.75x normal drops TSH essentially to zero"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thyrotroph_tsh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t4%3Anegative%3Athyrotroph_tsh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thyrotroph_tsh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Increased thyroid hormone in the body fluids decreases secretion of TSH by the anterior pituitary. When the rate of thyroid hormone secretion rises to about 1.75 times normal, the rate of TSH secretion falls essentially to zero.\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 76 (chw34), p. 939."
    ; pmo:mechanism "circulating free T4 (converted to T3 within the pituitary) exerts direct negative feedback on thyrotrophs to decrease TSH secretion; the feedback maintains near-constant free thyroid hormone"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/metabolic_heat_production> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t3%3Apositive%3Ametabolic_heat_production> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/metabolic_heat_production> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Both of these hormones profoundly increase the metabolic rate of the body. Complete lack of thyroid secretion usually causes the basal metabolic rate to fall 40 to 50 per cent below normal, and extreme excesses of thyroid secretion can increase the basal metabolic rate to 60 to 100 per cent above normal.\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 76 (chw34), p. 934."
    ; pmo:mechanism "thyroid hormone (T3) activates transcription of metabolic enzymes and Na-K-ATPase in nearly all tissues, raising O2 consumption and heat production; BMR falls 40-50% without thyroid and rises 60-100% in excess"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/food_intake> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t3%3Apositive%3Afood_intake> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/food_intake> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"...thyroid hormone also increases the appetite, and this may counterbalance the change in the metabolic rate.\" and \"In addition to increased appetite and food intake, which has been discussed...\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 76 (chw34), p. 937-938."
    ; pmo:mechanism "thyroid hormone increases appetite and food intake, partly counterbalancing the raised metabolic rate"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/angiotensin_II> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/zona_glomerulosa_cell_aldosterone_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aangiotensin_II%3Apositive%3Azona_glomerulosa_cell_aldosterone_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/angiotensin_II> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/zona_glomerulosa_cell_aldosterone_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The secretion of these [zona glomerulosa] cells is controlled mainly by the extracellular fluid concentrations of angiotensin II and potassium, both of which stimulate aldosterone secretion.\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 77 (chw34), p. 944-945."
    ; pmo:mechanism "angiotensin II acts directly on adrenal zona glomerulosa cells, increasing cholesterol->pregnenolone conversion and aldosterone-synthase output to stimulate aldosterone secretion"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_potassium> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/zona_glomerulosa_cell_aldosterone_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_potassium%3Apositive%3Azona_glomerulosa_cell_aldosterone_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_potassium> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/zona_glomerulosa_cell_aldosterone_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The secretion of these [zona glomerulosa] cells is controlled mainly by the extracellular fluid concentrations of angiotensin II and potassium, both of which stimulate aldosterone secretion.\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 77 (chw34), p. 944-945."
    ; pmo:mechanism "raised extracellular K+ depolarizes zona glomerulosa cells, triggering Ca2+ entry and increased aldosterone synthesis/secretion"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/acth> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/zona_fasciculata_cortisol_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aacth%3Apositive%3Azona_fasciculata_cortisol_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/acth> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/zona_fasciculata_cortisol_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"...secretion of cortisol is controlled almost entirely by ACTH secreted by the anterior pituitary gland.\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 77 (chw35), p. 955."
    ; pmo:mechanism "ACTH -> adenylyl cyclase/cAMP -> protein kinase A -> cholesterol desmolase (rate-limiting cholesterol->pregnenolone) drives cortisol secretion by zona fasciculata cells; cortisol secretion is controlled almost entirely by ACTH"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/crh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/corticotroph_acth_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acrh%3Apositive%3Acorticotroph_acth_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/crh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/corticotroph_acth_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"...corticotropin-releasing factor (CRF). It is secreted into the primary capillary plexus of the hypophysial portal system in the median eminence of the hypothalamus and then carried to the anterior pituitary gland, where it induces ACTH secretion. ... The anterior pituitary gland can secrete only minute quantities of ACTH in the absence of CRF.\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 77 (chw35), p. 955."
    ; pmo:mechanism "hypothalamic CRF/CRH secreted into the hypophysial portal system induces ACTH secretion from anterior-pituitary corticotrophs; the pituitary secretes only minute ACTH without CRF"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/parvocellular_crh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acortisol%3Anegative%3Aparvocellular_crh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/parvocellular_crh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Cortisol has direct negative feedback effects on (1) the hypothalamus to decrease the formation of CRF and (2) the anterior pituitary gland to decrease the formation of ACTH.\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 77 (chw35), p. 955-956."
    ; pmo:mechanism "cortisol exerts direct negative feedback on the hypothalamus (paraventricular parvocellular neurosecretory cells) to decrease CRF/CRH formation"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/corticotroph_acth_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acortisol%3Anegative%3Acorticotroph_acth_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/corticotroph_acth_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Cortisol has direct negative feedback effects on (1) the hypothalamus to decrease the formation of CRF and (2) the anterior pituitary gland to decrease the formation of ACTH.\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 77 (chw35), p. 955-956."
    ; pmo:mechanism "cortisol acts directly on anterior-pituitary corticotrophs to decrease ACTH formation (the basis of dexamethasone suppression)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_amino_acids> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acortisol%3Apositive%3Aplasma_amino_acids> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_amino_acids> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"2. Cortisol causes mobilization of amino acids from the extrahepatic tissues mainly from muscle. As a result, more amino acids become available in the plasma to enter into the gluconeogenesis process of the liver...\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 77 (chw35), p. 951."
    ; pmo:mechanism "cortisol mobilizes amino acids from extrahepatic (mainly muscle) protein stores, raising plasma amino acids available as substrate for hepatic gluconeogenesis"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alpha_cell_glucagon_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_glucose%3Anegative%3Aalpha_cell_glucagon_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alpha_cell_glucagon_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Increased Blood Glucose Inhibits Glucagon Secretion. The blood glucose concentration is by far the most potent factor that controls glucagon secretion ... increasing the blood glucose to hyperglycemic levels decreases plasma glucagon.\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 78 (chw35), p. 971."
    ; pmo:mechanism "raised blood glucose suppresses pancreatic alpha-cell glucagon secretion (opposite direction to its effect on insulin); hypoglycemia raises glucagon severalfold"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_amino_acids> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alpha_cell_glucagon_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_amino_acids%3Apositive%3Aalpha_cell_glucagon_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_amino_acids> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alpha_cell_glucagon_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Increased Blood Amino Acids Stimulate Glucagon Secretion. High concentrations of amino acids, as occur in the blood after a protein meal (especially the amino acids alanine and arginine), stimulate the secretion of glucagon.\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 78 (chw35), p. 971."
    ; pmo:mechanism "high plasma amino acids (especially alanine and arginine, after a protein meal) stimulate alpha-cell glucagon secretion, promoting conversion of amino acids to glucose"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_insulin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_glycogen_synthase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_insulin%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_glycogen_synthase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_insulin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_glycogen_synthase_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"3. Insulin also increases the activities of the enzymes that promote glycogen synthesis, including especially glycogen synthase, which is responsible for polymerization of the monosaccharide units to form the glycogen molecules.\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 78 (chw35), p. 964."
    ; pmo:mechanism "insulin increases the activity of hepatic glycogen synthase (via dephosphorylation), promoting polymerization of glucose into liver glycogen"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_insulin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lipoprotein_lipase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_insulin%3Apositive%3Alipoprotein_lipase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_insulin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lipoprotein_lipase_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Insulin activates lipoprotein lipase in the capillary walls of the adipose tissue, which splits the triglycerides again into fatty acids, a requirement for them to be absorbed into the adipose cells...\" Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 78 (chw35), p. 965."
    ; pmo:mechanism "insulin activates lipoprotein lipase in the adipose-capillary endothelium, hydrolyzing VLDL/chylomicron triglycerides to fatty acids for uptake and storage in adipose"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_insulin> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adipose_hsl_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_insulin%3Anegative%3Aadipose_hsl_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_insulin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adipose_hsl_activity> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Insulin inhibits the action of hormone-sensitive lipase. This is the enzyme that causes hydrolysis of the triglycerides already stored in the fat cells.\" — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, Ch. 78, p. 965"
    ; pmo:mechanism "insulin inhibits hormone-sensitive lipase in adipocytes, reducing hydrolysis of stored triglyceride and free-fatty-acid release"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/norepinephrine> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta_cell_insulin_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Anorepinephrine%3Anegative%3Abeta_cell_insulin_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/norepinephrine> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta_cell_insulin_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Other hormones, including somatostatin and norepinephrine (by activating a-adrenergic receptors), inhibit exocytosis of insulin.\" — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, Ch. 78, p. 968"
    ; pmo:mechanism "norepinephrine via alpha-adrenergic receptors inhibits exocytosis of insulin from beta cells"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ghrh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/somatotroph_gh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aghrh%3Apositive%3Asomatotroph_gh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ghrh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/somatotroph_gh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"GHRH stimulates growth hormone secretion by attaching to specific cell membrane receptors on the outer surfaces of the growth hormone cells in the pituitary gland. The receptors activate the adenylyl cyclase system inside the cell membrane, increasing the intracellular level of cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP).\" — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, Ch. 75, p. 925-926"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GHRH binds somatotroph membrane receptors -> adenylyl-cyclase/cAMP -> Ca2+ influx and GH vesicle fusion; also increases GH gene transcription"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/somatostatin> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/somatotroph_gh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asomatostatin%3Anegative%3Asomatotroph_gh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/somatostatin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/somatotroph_gh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Growth hormone inhibitory hormone (somatostatin)\" — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, Ch. 75, p. 925"
    ; pmo:mechanism "hypothalamic somatostatin (growth-hormone inhibitory hormone) suppresses somatotroph GH secretion"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_igf1> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/somatotroph_gh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahepatic_igf1%3Anegative%3Asomatotroph_gh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_igf1> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/somatotroph_gh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Somatomedins (insulin-like growth factors)\" — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, Ch. 75, p. 925"
    ; pmo:mechanism "circulating IGF-1/somatomedin exerts negative feedback on somatotroph GH secretion, closing the GH-IGF-1 loop"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/somatotroph_gh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_glucose%3Anegative%3Asomatotroph_gh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/somatotroph_gh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Decreased blood glucose [stimulates] ... Increased blood glucose [inhibits growth hormone secretion]\" — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, Ch. 75, p. 925"
    ; pmo:mechanism "increased blood glucose inhibits, decreased blood glucose (hypoglycaemia) stimulates GH secretion (sensed via ventromedial hypothalamus)"
    .
