    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Decreased blood free fatty acids [stimulates] ... Increased blood free fatty acids [inhibits growth hormone secretion]\" — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, Ch. 75, p. 925"
    ; pmo:mechanism "increased blood free fatty acids inhibit, decreased FFA stimulate GH secretion"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythropoietin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/epo_receptor_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aerythropoietin%3Apositive%3Aepo_receptor_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythropoietin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/epo_receptor_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The principal stimulus for red blood cell production in low oxygen states is a circulating hormone called erythropoietin ... In the absence of erythropoietin, hypoxia has little or no effect in stimulating red blood cell production.\" — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, Ch. 32, p. 422-423"
    ; pmo:mechanism "EPO binds and dimerizes the erythropoietin receptor (EPOR) on erythroid progenitors -> receptor activation"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/epo_receptor_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythroid_progenitor_proliferation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aepo_receptor_activity%3Apositive%3Aerythroid_progenitor_proliferation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/epo_receptor_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythroid_progenitor_proliferation> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"the important effect of erythropoietin is to stimulate the production of proerythroblasts from hematopoietic stem cells in the bone marrow ... the erythropoietin causes these cells to pass more rapidly through the different erythroblastic stages\" — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, Ch. 32, p. 423"
    ; pmo:mechanism "activated EPOR stimulates production of proerythroblasts from hematopoietic stem cells and accelerates passage through erythroblast stages"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/leptin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lepr_signaling> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aleptin%3Apositive%3Alepr_signaling> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/leptin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lepr_signaling> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Leptin then circulates to the brain, where it moves across the blood-brain barrier by facilitated diffusion and occupies leptin receptors at multiple sites in the hypothalamus, especially the POMC neurons of the arcuate nuclei.\" — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, Ch. 71, p. 868"
    ; pmo:mechanism "leptin binds LEPR on arcuate POMC/AGRP neurons -> LEPR signalling activation"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lepr_signaling> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hypothalamic_appetite_drive> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alepr_signaling%3Anegative%3Ahypothalamic_appetite_drive> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lepr_signaling> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hypothalamic_appetite_drive> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Stimulation of leptin receptors in these hypothalamic nuclei initiates multiple actions that decrease fat storage, including (1) decreased production in the hypothalamus of appetite stimulators, such as NPY and AGRP\" — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, Ch. 71, p. 868-870"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LEPR signalling stimulates POMC/alpha-MSH (anorexigenic) and inhibits AGRP-NPY, reducing orexigenic appetite drive"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adipose_tissue_mass> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adipocyte_leptin_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aadipose_tissue_mass%3Apositive%3Aadipocyte_leptin_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adipose_tissue_mass> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adipocyte_leptin_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"When the amount of adipose tissue increases (signaling excess energy storage), the adipocytes produce increased amounts of leptin, which is released into the blood.\" — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, Ch. 71, p. 867-868"
    ; pmo:mechanism "adipocytes secrete leptin in proportion to fat mass/size, the adiposity signal to the hypothalamus"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gonadotroph_lh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Agnrh%3Apositive%3Agonadotroph_lh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gonadotroph_lh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The secretion of LH by the anterior pituitary gland is also cyclical, with LH following fairly faithfully the pulsatile release of GnRH.\" — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, Ch. 80, p. 1007-1008"
    ; pmo:mechanism "pulsatile GnRH -> gonadotroph cAMP -> LH secretion; LH follows GnRH pulses faithfully"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gonadotroph_fsh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Agnrh%3Apositive%3Agonadotroph_fsh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gonadotroph_fsh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"FSH secretion ... changes more slowly over a period of many hours in response to longer-term changes in GnRH.\" — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, Ch. 80, p. 1007-1008"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GnRH stimulates gonadotroph FSH secretion (responds to longer-term GnRH changes rather than each pulse)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/leydig_testosterone_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alh%3Apositive%3Aleydig_testosterone_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/leydig_testosterone_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Testosterone is secreted by the interstitial cells of Leydig in the testes, but only when they are stimulated by LH ... the quantity of testosterone secreted increases approximately in direct proportion to the amount of LH available.\" — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, Ch. 80, p. 1007-1008"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LH stimulates Leydig cells (cAMP) -> testosterone secretion; output roughly proportional to LH"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh_neuron_gnrh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atestosterone%3Anegative%3Agnrh_neuron_gnrh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh_neuron_gnrh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Most of this inhibition probably results from a direct effect of testosterone on the hypothalamus to decrease the secretion of GnRH.\" — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, Ch. 80, p. 1007-1008"
    ; pmo:mechanism "testosterone acts mainly on the hypothalamus to decrease GnRH secretion (primary HPG negative-feedback arm)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/inhibin> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gonadotroph_fsh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ainhibin%3Anegative%3Agonadotroph_fsh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/inhibin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gonadotroph_fsh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"This hormone [inhibin] has a strong direct effect on the anterior pituitary gland to inhibit the secretion of FSH.\" — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, Ch. 80, p. 1008"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Sertoli-cell inhibin has a strong direct effect on the anterior pituitary to selectively inhibit FSH secretion (spermatogenesis feedback)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sympathetic_tone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adrenal_chromaffin_cell_epinephrine_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asympathetic_tone%3Apositive%3Aadrenal_chromaffin_cell_epinephrine_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sympathetic_tone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adrenal_chromaffin_cell_epinephrine_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Stimulation of the sympathetic nerves to the adrenal medullae causes large quantities of epinephrine and norepinephrine to be released into the circulating blood.\" — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, Ch. 60, p. 754-755"
    ; pmo:mechanism "sympathetic preganglionic (cholinergic) drive stimulates adrenal-medulla chromaffin cells to release large quantities of epinephrine (and norepinephrine)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/epinephrine> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/metabolic_heat_production> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aepinephrine%3Apositive%3Ametabolic_heat_production> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/epinephrine> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/metabolic_heat_production> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"the epinephrine secreted by the adrenal medullae can increase the metabolic rate of the whole body often to as much as 100 per cent above normal\" — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, Ch. 60, p. 754-755"
    ; pmo:mechanism "adrenal epinephrine calorigenic effect: raises whole-body metabolic rate (up to ~100% above normal) and basal metabolic rate"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_fibrinogen> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fibrin_clot> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_fibrinogen%3Apositive%3Afibrin_clot> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_fibrinogen> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fibrin_clot> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"It acts on fibrinogen to remove four low-molecular-weight peptides from each molecule of fibrinogen ... fibrinogen is one of the essential factors in the coagulation process\" — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, Ch. 36, p. 459-460"
    ; pmo:mechanism "thrombin polymerizes fibrinogen into fibrin fibers; fibrinogen is the essential substrate, so higher fibrinogen supply increases fibrin-clot formation"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucagon> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adipose_hsl_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_glucagon%3Apositive%3Aadipose_hsl_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucagon> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adipose_hsl_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"glucagon activates adipose cell lipase, making [increased quantities of fatty acids available]\" — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology (insulin/glucagon; glucagon lipolytic action)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "glucagon (via cAMP/PKA) activates adipocyte hormone-sensitive lipase, increasing lipolysis and free-fatty-acid mobilization"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_vitamin_k> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/vkgc_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_vitamin_k%3Apositive%3Avkgc_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_vitamin_k> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/vkgc_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Warfarin causes this effect by competing with vitamin K for reactive sites in the enzymatic processes for formation of prothrombin and the other three clotting factors, thereby blocking the action of vitamin K\" — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, ch 36 (Hemostasis)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "vitamin K is the obligate cofactor for gamma-glutamyl carboxylase (GGCX) carboxylating clotting factors; warfarin (coumarin) antagonizes the vitamin-K axis, lowering carboxylase output — so raising vitamin K raises GGCX activity"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il1beta> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_prostaglandin_e2> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ail1beta%3Apositive%3Aplasma_prostaglandin_e2> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il1beta> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_prostaglandin_e2> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"interleukin-1 causes fever by first inducing the formation of one of the prostaglandins, mainly prostaglandin E2... When prostaglandin formation is blocked by drugs, the fever is... reduced\" — Guyton & Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology (thermoregulation/fever)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "IL-1 (endogenous pyrogen) induces COX-mediated prostaglandin E2 synthesis; PGE2 acts on the hypothalamus to raise the thermoregulatory set-point (COX-blocking drugs abrogate the fever, corroborating the induction)"
