    ; dcterms:source "\"lungs inflated with saline have a much larger compliance (are easier to distend) than do air-filled lungs ... the saline abolished the surface tension forces but presumably did not affect the tissue forces\"; \"a low surface tension in the alveoli increases the compliance of the lung\" (West's Respiratory Physiology, 11th ed., ch 7)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "surface tension of the alveolar lining film adds a recoil/collapsing force; lowering it (e.g. saline-filling abolishes surface tension) makes the lung easier to distend, raising compliance"
    .
