    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The cause of the shift is an increase in concentration of 2,3-diphosphoglycerate, which develops primarily because of the respiratory alkalosis.\" (West's Respiratory Physiology, 11th ed., ch 9, Other Physiological Changes at High Altitude)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "respiratory alkalosis (raised arterial pH) stimulates red-cell glycolysis and bisphosphoglycerate-mutase flux -> increased 2,3-BPG (2,3-DPG) synthesis"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_pco2> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_bicarbonate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aarterial_pco2%3Apositive%3Acsf_bicarbonate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_pco2> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_bicarbonate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"If the CSF pH is displaced over a prolonged period, a compensatory change in [bicarbonate] occurs as a result of transport across the blood-brain barrier.\" (West's Respiratory Physiology, 11th ed., ch 8, Central Chemoreceptors)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "sustained rise in PaCO2 -> raised CSF PCO2 and lowered CSF pH -> compensatory bicarbonate transport across the blood-brain barrier raising CSF HCO3- toward restoring CSF pH (slow, days)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pulmonary_interstitial_fluid_volume> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pulmonary_gas_diffusion_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apulmonary_interstitial_fluid_volume%3Anegative%3Apulmonary_gas_diffusion_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pulmonary_interstitial_fluid_volume> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pulmonary_gas_diffusion_rate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"the amount of gas that moves across a sheet of tissue is ... inversely proportional to its thickness\" and \"when the diffusion properties of the lung are impaired, for example, because of thickening of the blood-gas barrier, the blood PO2 does not reach the alveolar value ...\" (West's Respiratory Physiology, 11th ed., ch 3 Diffusion / ch 4 Water Balance in the Lung)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "accumulated interstitial/alveolar fluid (edema) widens the effective blood-gas barrier -> longer diffusion path -> slower O2/CO2 diffusion (Fick: transfer inversely proportional to barrier thickness)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_pco2> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/blood_co2_content> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aarterial_pco2%3Apositive%3Ablood_co2_content> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/arterial_pco2> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/blood_co2_content> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The relationship between the PCO2 and the total CO2 concentration of blood is shown in Figure 6.5 ... the CO2 dissociation curve, and it is much more linear than is the O2 dissociation curve.\" (West's Respiratory Physiology, 11th ed., ch 6, CO2 Dissociation Curve)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "higher PCO2 -> more dissolved CO2, more carbonic-anhydrase-catalyzed bicarbonate formation, and more carbaminohemoglobin -> higher total blood CO2 content (CO2 dissociation curve, near-linear in-range)"
    .
