    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Large infantile hepatic hemangiomas express high D3 levels, causing 'consumptive hypothyroidism,' because thyroid hormone is inactivated at a more rapid rate than it can be produced\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 1, p 11"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Type 3 iodothyronine deiodinase (DIO3) removes an inner-ring iodine from T3, inactivating it; overexpression (GoF) in infantile hepatic hemangiomas consumes T3 faster than the thyroid can produce it, causing consumptive hypothyroidism with low free T3"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_myostatin> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lean_body_mass> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_myostatin%3Anegative%3Alean_body_mass> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_myostatin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lean_body_mass> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"A deficiency of myostatin is responsible for the 'double-muscled' phenotype of Belgian Blue and Piedmontese cattle, and deletion of its gene in mice and humans leads to massive muscle hypertrophy and hyperplasia.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 2, p 25"
    ; pmo:mechanism "myostatin (GDF8) negatively regulates skeletal muscle growth; absence of myostatin leads to muscle hypertrophy and hyperplasia (quality: myostatin concentration → disposition: ActRIIB/ALK4/5 signalling → process: SMAD2/3 inhibition of myogenesis → quality: lean body mass)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_activin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_fsh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_activin%3Apositive%3Apituitary_fsh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_activin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_fsh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"the hormone activin, which promotes the release of FSH, is formed by the assembly of homodimers of the β-subunit\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 2, p 24"
    ; pmo:mechanism "activin binds type II / type I receptors on pituitary gonadotrophs → SMAD2/3 signalling → FSH secretion (quality: activin concentration → disposition: receptor complex assembly → process: SMAD-mediated FSHβ transcription + secretion → quality: plasma FSH)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/collecting_duct_water_permeability> .
