    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Estrogen stimulates PRL gene transcription and PRL secretion, explaining why women have higher PRL levels, particularly during the periovulatory menstrual phase and in pregnancy.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 8, p 191"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Estrogen binds ERα on lactotroph PRL gene promoter → transcriptional activation of PRL gene → increased PRL synthesis and secretion"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/trh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_prolactin_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atrh%3Apositive%3Apituitary_prolactin_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/trh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_prolactin_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"In response to TRH, the proportion of the monomeric form increases... Neuropeptides acting through G protein-coupled receptors to stimulate PRL include vasoactive intestinal polypeptide (VIP), prolactin-releasing peptide (PrRP), opioid peptides, and TRH.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 8, p 191  ||  \"Patients with severe hypothyroidism may have increased serum prolactin levels, stimulated by the elevation in thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH) and proportional to the level of serum TSH elevation... Treatment with thyroid hormone normalizes the serum prolactin and TSH levels.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 13  ||  \"PRL release increases in response to TRH and decreases in response to dopamine.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 23, p 835"
    ; pmo:mechanism "TRH binds TRHR on lactotrophs → IP3/DAG signalling → PRL exocytosis; TRH is a PRL-releasing factor and increases monomeric (bioactive) PRL fraction"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/c_reactive_protein> .
