    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Proinflammatory factors such as C-reactive protein and IL6, strongly implicated in the pathogenesis of vascular disease, fall significantly with GH treatment\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 8, p. 205"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GH replacement reduces pro-inflammatory adipokine secretion → lower CRP"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bone_mineral_density> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apituitary_gh%3Apositive%3Abone_mineral_density> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bone_mineral_density> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "\"GH replacement activates bone formation and bone resorption, resulting in a transient decline before a net increase in bone mass by 12 months\" and \"Fifteen years of GH replacement increases bone mineral densities\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 8, p. 206  ||  \"reinstitution of treatment for 2 years in the transition period prevents deterioration in bone mineral density as experienced by untreated young adults with persistent GHD\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 25"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GH → IGF-1 → osteoblast stimulation → net positive bone mineral accretion"
