    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"significant shift in lipoprotein metabolism to a less atherogenic profile with improvements in the cholesterol and in HDL-cholesterol ratio\" and Fig 8.20 shows HDL-C rises from 1.21 to 1.36 mmol/L over 10 years of GH therapy. Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 8, p. 205"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GH improves lipoprotein lipase activity and hepatic lipid metabolism → higher HDL"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adh> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urine_volume> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aadh%3Anegative%3Aurine_volume> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urine_volume> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"When vasopressin is absent, 18 to 20 L of urine are excreted daily; with an increase of vasopressin by as little as 0.5 to 1 pg/mL, however, urine volume is reduced to less than 4 L/day. This illustrates the important point that at low plasma levels of vasopressin small changes of vasopressin are much larger determinants of polyuria than are greater changes at higher plasma levels.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 10, p 307"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ADH binds V2 receptors on renal collecting-duct principal cells, activating adenylate cyclase → cAMP → PKA → aquaporin-2 insertion into apical membrane, increasing water reabsorption and reducing urine volume"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urine_volume> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acortisol%3Apositive%3Aurine_volume> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urine_volume> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Cortisol deficiency alone decreases the ability to excrete water even in the absence of vasopressin.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 10, p 310"
    ; pmo:mechanism "cortisol (glucocorticoid) is required for normal renal free-water excretion; cortisol deficiency (clinical/pharmacological withdrawal) impairs water excretion independent of vasopressin, decreasing urine volume"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_oxytocin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mammary_milk_production_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_oxytocin%3Apositive%3Amammary_milk_production_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_oxytocin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mammary_milk_production_rate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The importance of oxytocin in maintaining milk secretion is demonstrated by transgenic mice with a knockout that inhibits oxytocin synthesis. These animals deliver their young normally and have normal milk production, but there is no milk release in spite of normal suckling. The pups die of dehydration with no milk in the stomach.377 Administration of oxytocin to these oxytocin-deficient mice rescues the ability to secrete milk and allows the pups to survive.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 10, p. 328"
    ; pmo:mechanism "oxytocin binds receptors on mammary gland myoepithelial cells -> myoepithelial contraction -> expulsion of milk from alveoli into ducts and toward nipple (milk ejection/letdown)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/progesterone> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/uterine_contractility> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aprogesterone%3Anegative%3Auterine_contractility> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/progesterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/uterine_contractility> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"during pregnancy the uterus is maintained in a quiescent state by the actions of progesterone and relaxin (produced by the corpus luteum and decidual tissue). The initiation of labor is accomplished by a relative increase in estrogen activation and a decrease in progesterone activation.\" and \"The role of progesterone in maintaining uterine quiescence is not only the action on oxytocin receptors but also the antagonizing of the inflammatory response that softens the lower uterus and cervix.384\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 10, p. 329"
    ; pmo:mechanism "progesterone binds uterine progesterone receptors -> suppresses oxytocin-receptor upregulation and antagonizes uterine inflammatory responses -> maintained myometrial quiescence"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_iodide> obo:RO_0002411 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_iodide%3Aunknown%3Afree_t4> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_iodide> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> ; pmo:sign "?"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The quantity of iodine organified in Tg...displays a biphasic response to increasing doses of iodide, at first increasing and then decreasing as a result of a relative blockade of organic binding. This decreasing yield of organic iodine from increasing doses of iodide, the Wolff-Chaikoff effect, results from a high concentration of inorganic iodide within the thyroid cell.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 11, p 350–351. \"T4 release from the thyroid cells is inhibited by several agents, the most important of which is iodide.\" p 337."
    ; pmo:mechanism "High intracellular iodide (Wolff-Chaikoff effect) inhibits TPO-catalysed organification and DUOX2 glycosylation, reducing T4 synthesis; pharmacological doses also promptly inhibit T4 release from the thyroid"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/type_1_deiodinase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_reverse_t3> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atype_1_deiodinase_activity%3Anegative%3Aplasma_reverse_t3> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/type_1_deiodinase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_reverse_t3> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The preferred substrates of D1 are rT3 (5′ deiodination) and T3SO4 (5 deiodination)...D1 is inhibited by PTU, unlike D2 or D3.\" Table 11.2. \"During fasting, there is a reduction of 50% or more in serum T3 and an increase in serum rT3...decreases in peripheral T4-to-T3 conversion by both D1 and D2 and a reduced clearance of rT3 by D1 play a role in this process.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 11, p 343 (Table 11.2), p 353."
    ; pmo:mechanism "D1 catalyses 5′-deiodination of rT3 (the preferred substrate of D1, Km 10−7); reduced D1 activity allows rT3 to accumulate"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_reverse_t3> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acortisol%3Apositive%3Aplasma_reverse_t3> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_reverse_t3> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The latter finding and the accompanying increase in rT3 production suggest that glucocorticoids may increase D3 activity.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 11, p. 354"
    ; pmo:mechanism "glucocorticoid promotes T4→rT3 conversion (likely via increased D3), increasing rT3 production"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_thyroxine_binding_globulin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atestosterone%3Anegative%3Aplasma_thyroxine_binding_globulin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_thyroxine_binding_globulin> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"In contrast, administration of androgens to women decreases TBG and decreases T4 turnover and levothyroxine requirements in patients with primary hypothyroidism.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 11, p. 354"
    ; pmo:mechanism "androgen administration decreases hepatic TBG synthesis/secretion, lowering circulating TBG"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/resting_energy_expenditure> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t4%3Apositive%3Aresting_energy_expenditure> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/resting_energy_expenditure> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Resting energy expenditure correlates very well with the free T4 and TSH in hypothyroid patients given varying doses of exogenous levothyroxine.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 11, p. 360  ||  \"An excess of thyroid hormone as seen in thyrotoxicosis leads to increased resting energy expenditure that in many individuals will lead to significant weight loss\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 40, p 1570"
    ; pmo:mechanism "thyroid hormone sets metabolic rate; dose-graded exogenous levothyroxine in hypothyroid patients raises resting energy expenditure proportionally"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_shbg> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t3%3Apositive%3Aplasma_shbg> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_shbg> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Thyrotoxicosis, whether spontaneous or induced by exogenous hormone, is accompanied by an increase in the concentration of sex hormone–binding globulin (SHBG) in plasma.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 12, p. 368  ||  \"The levels of SHBG are controlled by a balance of hormonal influences on its synthesis in the liver. Testosterone and insulin inhibit, and estrogen and thyroid hormone stimulate, SHBG formation.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 17, p 617"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Thyrotoxicosis (excess T3/T4) increases hepatic synthesis and secretion of sex hormone-binding globulin"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/red_cell_mass> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t3%3Apositive%3Ared_cell_mass> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/red_cell_mass> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"red blood cell mass is increased. The increase in erythropoiesis is due to the direct effect of thyroid hormones on the erythroid marrow, as patients that harbor mutations in the thyroid hormone receptor alpha (TRα) have mild anemia secondary to defects in erythropoiesis.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 12, p. 368"
    ; pmo:mechanism "T3 stimulates erythropoiesis via TRα-dependent effects on erythroid marrow, increasing total red cell mass"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_tract_motility> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t3%3Apositive%3Agi_tract_motility> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_tract_motility> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The increased gastric emptying and intestinal motility in thyrotoxicosis appear to be responsible for slight malabsorption of fat, and these functions return to normal when a normal metabolic state has been restored.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 12, p. 367"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Thyroid hormones increase gastric emptying rate and intestinal motility, with normalization upon restoration of euthyroid state confirming causal direction"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/endothelial_no_production> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/total_peripheral_resistance> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aendothelial_no_production%3Anegative%3Atotal_peripheral_resistance> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/endothelial_no_production> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/total_peripheral_resistance> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"peripheral vascular resistance is decreased...The decreased resistance is due to increased nitric oxide production via the PI3K/AKT signaling pathway.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 12, p. 365"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Endothelial nitric oxide diffuses to vascular smooth muscle, causing vasodilation and reducing total peripheral (systemic vascular) resistance"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_triglycerides> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t3%3Anegative%3Aplasma_triglycerides> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_triglycerides> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"triglyceride levels are usually slightly decreased.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 12, p. 366"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Thyroid hormones increase triglyceride clearance and hepatic lipase activity, raising the net rate of triglyceride catabolism and lowering plasma levels"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_total_cholesterol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t3%3Anegative%3Aplasma_total_cholesterol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_total_cholesterol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"a decrease in serum cholesterol level\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 12, p. 366"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Thyroid hormones increase hepatic LDL-receptor expression and bile acid synthesis from cholesterol, reducing plasma total cholesterol"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcium> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t3%3Apositive%3Aplasma_calcium> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcium> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"For all the same reasons, hypercalcemia may occur in patients with severe thyrotoxicosis. The total serum calcium concentration is increased in as many as 27% of patients, and the ionized serum calcium is elevated in 47%.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 12, p. 367"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Excess T3 accelerates osteoclastic bone resorption via nuclear TRα, releasing calcium from bone matrix into the circulation"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tshr_activation> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thyroid_follicular_t4_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atshr_activation%3Apositive%3Athyroid_follicular_t4_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tshr_activation> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/thyroid_follicular_t4_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Stimulating TSHRAb isolated from patients with Graves hyperthyroidism bind to the TSHR and activate Gαs and Gq signaling pathways, thereby inducing thyroid growth, increased vascularity, and increased production and secretion of thyroid hormones.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 12, p. 372"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Stimulating TSH-receptor antibodies (acting as TSH agonists) activate Gαs and Gq signaling via TSHR, inducing increased thyroid hormone production and secretion"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/systolic_blood_pressure> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t3%3Apositive%3Asystolic_blood_pressure> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/systolic_blood_pressure> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Widening of the pulse pressure is due to the increase in systolic pressure and decrease in diastolic pressure caused by reduced resistance.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 12, p. 365  ||  \"Thyrotoxic patients usually have tachycardia, high cardiac output, increased stroke volume, decreased peripheral vascular resistance, and increased systolic blood pressure.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 16, p. 572"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Thyroid hormones increase myocardial contractility and heart rate, raising systolic ejection force and systolic blood pressure"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/diastolic_blood_pressure> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t3%3Anegative%3Adiastolic_blood_pressure> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/diastolic_blood_pressure> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Widening of the pulse pressure is due to the increase in systolic pressure and decrease in diastolic pressure caused by reduced resistance.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 12, p. 365"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Thyroid-hormone-driven vasodilation reduces total peripheral resistance, lowering diastolic blood pressure"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/parasympathetic_tone> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t3%3Anegative%3Aparasympathetic_tone> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/parasympathetic_tone> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Tachycardia is virtually always present and is due to a combination of increased sympathetic and decreased vagal tone.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 12, p. 365"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Excess thyroid hormones decrease cardiac vagal (parasympathetic) tone, contributing to tachycardia in thyrotoxicosis"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/total_peripheral_resistance> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t4%3Anegative%3Atotal_peripheral_resistance> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/total_peripheral_resistance> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Peripheral vascular resistance at rest is increased, and blood volume is reduced... treatment with thyroid hormone corrects the hemodynamic... alterations of myxedema heart.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 13  ||  \"The mechanisms for the elevation in blood pressure include increased systemic vascular resistance and extracellular volume expansion. Treatment of thyroid hormone deficiency decreases blood pressure in most patients with hypertension.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 16, p. 572"
    ; pmo:mechanism "thyroid hormone promotes vasodilation / reduces vascular smooth muscle tone; loss of T4 raises TPR; T4 replacement normalises hemodynamics"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/metabolic_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t4%3Apositive%3Ametabolic_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/metabolic_rate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The decrease in energy metabolism and heat production is reflected in a low basal metabolic rate, decreased appetite, cold intolerance, and slightly low basal body temperature.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 13"
    ; pmo:mechanism "thyroid hormones drive mitochondrial thermogenesis and oxidative metabolism; loss of T4 lowers BMR; T4 replacement reverses"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/core_temperature> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t4%3Apositive%3Acore_temperature> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/core_temperature> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The decrease in energy metabolism and heat production is reflected in a low basal metabolic rate, decreased appetite, cold intolerance, and slightly low basal body temperature. All of these abnormalities are relieved by treatment.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 13"
    ; pmo:mechanism "thyroid hormone drives thermogenesis; loss of T4 lowers core temperature and causes cold intolerance; T4 replacement corrects"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ldl_cholesterol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t4%3Anegative%3Aldl_cholesterol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ldl_cholesterol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Hypothyroidism is consistently associated with elevations of total and low-density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol, which improve with T4 replacement. The higher the original serum TSH concentration and elevation of serum LDL, the greater the magnitude of reduction in LDL cholesterol after T4 therapy.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 13  ||  \"All patients with significant hyperlipidemia should be screened for hypothyroidism, because thyroid hormone deficiency causes hypercholesterolemia\" (Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 41, p. 1605); \"Subclinical hypothyroidism is a cause of hypercholesterolemia that sometimes responds to thyroid hormone replacement\" (ibid., p. 1605)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "thyroid hormone upregulates hepatic LDL receptors and promotes LDL clearance; T4 replacement lowers LDL; degree of reduction proportional to initial TSH and LDL levels"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_ldl_receptor_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t4%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_ldl_receptor_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_ldl_receptor_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The decrease in the lipid degradation rate may reflect the decrease in postheparin lipolytic activity as well as reduced LDL receptors.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 13"
    ; pmo:mechanism "thyroid hormone transcriptionally upregulates LDLR expression on hepatocytes; loss of T4 reduces LDL receptor density, impairing LDL clearance"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythropoietin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t4%3Apositive%3Aerythropoietin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythropoietin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"In response to the diminished oxygen requirements and decreased production of erythropoietin, the red blood cell mass is decreased; this is evident in the mild normocytic, normochromic anemia that often occurs.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 13"
    ; pmo:mechanism "thyroid hormone drives O2 consumption; reduced O2 demand in hypothyroidism leads to reduced EPO production; T4 supports EPO generation by maintaining high metabolic demand"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_shbg> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t4%3Apositive%3Aplasma_shbg> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_shbg> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The sex hormone–binding globulin in plasma is decreased... The alterations in steroid metabolism are corrected by restoration of the euthyroid state.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 13  ||  \"SHBG production by the liver is increased under the influence of estrogens and thyroxine and decreased by androgens and insulin.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p 1154"
    ; pmo:mechanism "thyroid hormone induces hepatic SHBG synthesis; loss of T4 decreases plasma SHBG; alterations corrected by restoration of euthyroid state"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t4%3Apositive%3Arenin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Plasma renin activity is decreased, and sensitivity to angiotensin II is increased, which may contribute to the association of hypertension with hypothyroidism.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 13"
    ; pmo:mechanism "thyroid hormone maintains renin secretion by juxtaglomerular apparatus; hypothyroidism reduces plasma renin activity"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_tract_motility> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t4%3Apositive%3Agi_tract_motility> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gi_tract_motility> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Peristaltic activity is decreased and, together with the decreased food intake, is responsible for the frequent complaint of constipation.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 13"
    ; pmo:mechanism "thyroid hormone supports GI smooth muscle peristaltic activity; loss of T4 decreases peristalsis causing constipation"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> obo:RO_0002411 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_triglycerides> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t4%3Aunknown%3Aplasma_triglycerides> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_triglycerides> ; pmo:sign "?"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Both the synthesis and the degradation of lipid are depressed in hypothyroidism. Degradation, however, is reduced to a greater extent, with a net effect of accumulation of LDL and triglycerides.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 13"
    ; pmo:mechanism "thyroid hormone promotes lipid degradation; loss of T4 impairs triglyceride clearance leading to accumulation; T4 replacement reverses"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bone_mineral_density> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t4%3Anegative%3Abone_mineral_density> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t4> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bone_mineral_density> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Although the administration of excessive doses of levothyroxine causes accelerated bone loss in postmenopausal patients, most authorities believe that returning thyroid status to normal does not have adverse effects on bone density.232\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 13, p. 428."
    ; pmo:mechanism "supraphysiological free T4 (via conversion to T3) increases osteoclast-mediated bone resorption, exceeding osteoblast formation and causing net bone loss; well documented in postmenopausal women on excess levothyroxine"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hdl_cholesterol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acortisol%3Anegative%3Ahdl_cholesterol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hdl_cholesterol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"An increase in total circulating cholesterol and triglycerides is observed, but HDL cholesterol levels fall\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 15, p. 496"
    ; pmo:mechanism "glucocorticoid excess → HDL cholesterol levels fall (mechanism not fully specified)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_calcium> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acortisol%3Apositive%3Aurinary_calcium> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/urinary_calcium> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Glucocorticoids also induce negative calcium balance by inhibiting intestinal calcium absorption and increasing renal calcium excretion\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 15, p. 496"
    ; pmo:mechanism "glucocorticoids increase renal calcium excretion"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> obo:RO_0002411 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/somatostatin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acortisol%3Aunknown%3Asomatostatin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/somatostatin> ; pmo:sign "?"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"GH secretion is reduced, possibly mediated through an increase in somatostatinergic tone\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 15, p. 502  ||  \"Ongoing glucocorticoid excess then causes ongoing suppression of GH secretion. This decrease in GH secretion is due to an increase in somatostatin tone.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 25, p 961"
    ; pmo:mechanism "glucocorticoid excess increases somatostatinergic tone → suppression of GH secretion"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mean_arterial_pressure> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acortisol%3Apositive%3Amean_arterial_pressure> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mean_arterial_pressure> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Glucocorticoids increase blood pressure by a variety of mechanisms involving actions on the kidney and vasculature. In vascular smooth muscle, they increase sensitivity to pressor agents such as catecholamines and angiotensin II while reducing nitric oxide–mediated endothelial dilatation\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 15, p. 496"
    ; pmo:mechanism "glucocorticoids increase vascular sensitivity to catecholamines/angiotensin II, reduce NO-mediated vasodilation, increase angiotensinogen synthesis, and promote sodium retention → hypertension"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_angiotensinogen> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acortisol%3Apositive%3Aplasma_angiotensinogen> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_angiotensinogen> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Angiotensinogen synthesis is increased by glucocorticoids\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 15, p. 496"
    ; pmo:mechanism "glucocorticoids increase hepatic angiotensinogen synthesis → elevated plasma angiotensinogen"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/muscle_protein_synthesis_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acortisol%3Anegative%3Amuscle_protein_synthesis_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/muscle_protein_synthesis_rate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"In muscle, glucocorticoids cause atrophy (but not necrosis)... Muscle protein synthesis is reduced\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 15, p. 496  ||  \"cortisol regulates muscle protein content by inhibiting muscle protein synthesis; and stimulating protein degradation likely via activation of the ubiquitin pathway.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 26, p.1168"
    ; pmo:mechanism "glucocorticoids cause catabolic changes in muscle → protein catabolism and muscle protein synthesis reduction"
    .
