<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/macula_densa_nacl_signal> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/jg_granular_renin_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amacula_densa_nacl_signal%3Anegative%3Ajg_granular_renin_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/macula_densa_nacl_signal> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/jg_granular_renin_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "A1 adenosine receptor (ADORA1) knockout mice show significantly increased plasma renin activity with abolished tubuloglomerular feedback (Brown, Ollerstam, Persson, Skott, Persson et al. 2001, Am J Physiol Regul Integr Comp Physiol, PMID:11641103, opened): germline loss-of-function of the required receptor disinhibits renin, demonstrating that the adenosine/ADORA1 arm of the macula-densa signal tonically suppresses renin secretion (a natural do() supporting the negative sign). Adenosine/A1 is a required constituent of JGA/tubuloglomerular-feedback signaling (Sun, Samuelson, Yang, Huang, Paliege, Saunders, Briggs & Schnermann, PNAS 2001;98(17):9983-9988, PMID:11504952, opened; note: Sun 2001 establishes the adenosine/A1-dependence of TGF, the elevated-plasma-renin result is Brown 2001). Boron & Boulpaep Medical Physiology (high distal NaCl suppresses renin); OmniPath ADORA1 Gi-coupled is_inhibition. | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-148 (25.5: Microscopic Anatomy of the Kidney): 'A second function of the macula densa cells is to regulate renin release from the juxtaglomerular cells of the afferent arteriole.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-41.2 (41.2: The Kidneys and Osmoregulatory Organs): 'The JGC is an endocrine structure that secretes the enzyme renin and the hormone erythropoietin. When hormones trigger the macula densa cells in the DCT due to variations in blood volume, blood pressure, or electrolyte balance, these cells can immediately communicate the problem to the capillaries in the afferent and efferent arterioles.' Macula densa senses luminal NaCl and signals JG granular cells to regulate renin secretion. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "macula densa basolateral ATP release (pannexin-1/P2) -> ecto-5'-nucleotidase -> adenosine -> ADORA1 (A1 adenosine receptor, Gi) on JG granular cell -> down-regulates cAMP/PKA -> inhibits renin exocytosis (GO:0002001 renin secretion into blood, negatively regulated)"
    .
