<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hypothalamic_opioid_tone> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahypothalamic_opioid_tone%3Anegative%3Agnrh> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hypothalamic_opioid_tone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"β-Endorphin and other opioids may suppress the hypothalamic release of GnRH. It was proposed that sex steroids enhance the activity of endogenous opioids that exert an inhibitory effect on GnRH secretion. The negative effect of opioids on GnRH secretion is clinically explicable because the reduced GnRH secretion associated with hypothalamic amenorrhea may be mediated by an increase in endogenous opioid inhibitory tone.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 17, p 577"
    ; pmo:mechanism "endogenous opioids (beta-endorphin) suppress hypothalamic GnRH secretion; mediates amenorrhea under stress/exercise"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hypothalamic_kisspeptin_signal> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahypothalamic_kisspeptin_signal%3Apositive%3Agnrh> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hypothalamic_kisspeptin_signal> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Mutations or knockout of KISS1R produces isolated hypogonadotropic hypogonadism in humans and mice, indicating that signaling through this receptor is essential for sexual development and function.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 17, p 577"
    ; pmo:mechanism "kisspeptin binds KISS1R on GnRH neurons, depolarises them and stimulates GnRH release; LoF of KISS1R causes isolated hypogonadotropic hypogonadism"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_activin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_fsh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apituitary_activin%3Apositive%3Apituitary_fsh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_activin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_fsh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Activin can directly stimulate FSH biosynthesis and release from the gonadotroph cells of the pituitary gland. Follistatin can negatively regulate biologic functions of activin via binding and prevent it from interacting with the activin receptor at the cell membrane.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 17, p 580"
    ; pmo:mechanism "pituitary-local activin (autocrine/paracrine) directly stimulates FSH biosynthesis and secretion from gonadotrophs"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_follistatin> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_activin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apituitary_follistatin%3Anegative%3Apituitary_activin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_follistatin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_activin> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Follistatin can negatively regulate biologic functions of activin via binding and prevent it from interacting with the activin receptor at the cell membrane. activin and follistatin are produced in the ovary and the pituitary. They appear to regulate FSH release from the pituitary via autocrine or paracrine but not endocrine pathways. Activin stimulates FSH production, whereas follistatin suppresses this action of activin.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 17, p 576"
    ; pmo:mechanism "follistatin binds and neutralises activin, preventing it from stimulating FSH"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/progesterone> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/endometrial_thickness> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aprogesterone%3Anegative%3Aendometrial_thickness> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/progesterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/endometrial_thickness> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Progesterone can inhibit and even reverse the proliferative action of estrogen on epithelial cells in the functionalis layer...treatment with and then withdrawal of a progestin causes menstruation, even with continued estrogen treatment.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 17, p 595"
    ; pmo:mechanism "progesterone inhibits and reverses estradiol-driven endometrial epithelial proliferation, promoting differentiation"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/crh> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acrh%3Anegative%3Agnrh> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/crh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"In an animal model, CRH seems to be an important factor in the inhibition of GnRH pulsatility. This inhibitory effect can be prevented by coadministration of a CRH antagonist or reversed by the opiate antagonist naloxone\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 17, p 604"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CRH inhibits hypothalamic GnRH pulse generator; inhibition prevented by CRH antagonist and reversed by opioid antagonist naloxone (animal model with pharmacological cross-validation)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_shbg> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atestosterone%3Anegative%3Aplasma_shbg> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_shbg> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The levels of SHBG are controlled by a balance of hormonal influences on its synthesis in the liver. Testosterone and insulin inhibit, and estrogen and thyroid hormone stimulate, SHBG formation.\" and \"Testosterone decreases serum SHBG levels, giving rise to a vicious feedback circle favoring low SHBG and high bioavailable testosterone levels\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 17, p 617  ||  \"SHBG production by the liver is increased under the influence of estrogens and thyroxine and decreased by androgens and insulin.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p 1154  ||  \"The concentration of sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG), which is suppressed by androgens and enhanced by estrogens, is similar to that found in 46,XX females\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 23, p 912  ||  \"as a consequence of testosterone, there is a greater decrease in boys, although the drop observed in normal boys is attenuated by treatment with tamoxifen\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 26, p 1053"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Testosterone inhibits hepatic SHBG synthesis, lowering plasma SHBG concentration and creating a positive-feedback loop of increasing free testosterone"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_shbg> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aestradiol%3Apositive%3Aplasma_shbg> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_shbg> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The levels of SHBG are controlled by a balance of hormonal influences on its synthesis in the liver. Testosterone and insulin inhibit, and estrogen and thyroid hormone stimulate, SHBG formation.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 17, p 617  ||  \"SHBG production by the liver is increased under the influence of estrogens and thyroxine and decreased by androgens and insulin.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p 1154  ||  \"The concentration of sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG), which is suppressed by androgens and enhanced by estrogens, is similar to that found in 46,XX females\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 23, p 912"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Estrogen stimulates hepatic SHBG gene transcription, increasing plasma SHBG concentration"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_androstenedione> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alh%3Apositive%3Aplasma_androstenedione> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_androstenedione> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"In patients with persistent anovulation, the average daily production of estrogen and androgens is increased and depends on LH stimulation\" and \"Androstenedione, secreted by polycystic ovaries, are particularly high\" in the context of elevated LH; \"Functioning stromal tissue secretes significant amounts of androstenedione under the influence of increased LH\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 17, p 617  ||  \"increased LH is a major factor that maintains significant quantities of androstenedione and testosterone secretion from the ovary, although the total production rates of both steroids decline after menopause\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 17, p. 633"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LH stimulates ovarian theca-cell steroidogenesis, increasing secretion of androstenedione as the dominant androgen precursor; circulating androstenedione levels in PCOS depend on sustained LH stimulation"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_insulin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_insulin%3Apositive%3Atestosterone> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_insulin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"metformin inhibits the output of hepatic glucose, necessitating a lower insulin concentration and thereby probably reducing androgen production by theca cells\" and \"Metformin at a dose of 500 mg three times daily reduced hyperinsulinemia, basal and stimulated levels of LH, and free testosterone concentrations in overweight women with PCOS\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 17, p. 620"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Insulin acts on ovarian theca cells to stimulate CYP17A1-mediated androgen biosynthesis; reducing insulin with metformin decreases theca-cell androgen output, lowering circulating testosterone"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bone_mineral_density> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aestradiol%3Apositive%3Abone_mineral_density> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bone_mineral_density> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Seventy-five percent or more of the bone loss in women during the first 15 years after menopause is attributed to estrogen deficiency rather than to aging\" and \"The WHI results...revealed the most conclusive evidence for a decreased number of vertebral and hip fractures in the group of postmenopausal women receiving estrogen plus progestin (E+P) or estrogen (E) only\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 17, p. 635  ||  \"The few men reported with inactivating mutations of the aromatase gene (CYP19A1) have demonstrated… delayed bone age, osteopenia or osteoporosis… Furthermore, estradiol treatment results in closure of the epiphyses, increased BMD, and increased bone age.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p 1358–1368  ||  \"demonstrating that estrogens are essential for normal skeletal maturation and proportions and for the prevention of osteoporosis in males and females\" (from study of aromatase-deficient males and aromatase-knockout mice) — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 23 [ch 24], p 923"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Estradiol suppresses osteoclast-mediated bone resorption; estrogen deficiency (menopause) accelerates trabecular and cortical bone loss, while hormone therapy (CEE ± MPA) restores BMD and reduces fracture incidence"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intratesticular_testosterone> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alh%3Apositive%3Aintratesticular_testosterone> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intratesticular_testosterone> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"In normal men, LH stimulates intratesticular testosterone concentrations that are approximately 100- to 200-fold higher than serum testosterone concentrations and correlate with circulating LH concentrations. Administration of various combinations of exogenous testosterone, progestin, and GnRH antagonist to induce gonadotropin deficiency in male contraception trials suppressed intratesticular testosterone by 98%… Short-term administration of hCG (LH-like activity) in normal men with experimental gonadotropin deficiency resulted in a dose-dependent increase in intratesticular testosterone.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p 683"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LH binds LHCGR on Leydig cells → Gs/cAMP/PKA → StAR + CYP11A1 + CYP17A1 → testosterone biosynthesis and secretion into testicular interstitium"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intratesticular_testosterone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/spermatogenesis_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aintratesticular_testosterone%3Apositive%3Aspermatogenesis_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intratesticular_testosterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/spermatogenesis_rate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Administration of various combinations of exogenous testosterone, progestin, and GnRH antagonist to induce gonadotropin deficiency in male contraception trials suppressed intratesticular testosterone by 98%… and reduced sperm production, producing severe oligozoospermia or azoospermia.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p 683"
    ; pmo:mechanism "intratesticular testosterone binds AR on Sertoli cells and peritubular myoid cells, supporting spermatocyte meiosis completion, spermiogenesis, and spermiation"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adipose_tissue_mass> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aestradiol%3Anegative%3Aadipose_tissue_mass> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adipose_tissue_mass> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "\"fat mass accumulation and bone loss was induced by estrogen deficiency\" (GnRH agonist + aromatase inhibitor study in healthy men). Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p 1341–1342"
    ; pmo:mechanism "estradiol restrains adipose tissue accumulation; experimental estrogen deficiency (GnRH agonist + aromatase inhibitor) leads to fat mass accumulation"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythropoietin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atestosterone%3Apositive%3Aerythropoietin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythropoietin> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Stimulation of erythropoiesis resulting in an increase in hematocrit, primarily by direct bone marrow induction of erythroid differentiation and stimulation of erythropoietin secretion.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p 1562–1563  ||  \"A greater erythropoietic response to testosterone is associated with stimulation of erythropoietin and suppression of ferritin and hepcidin\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p 751"
    ; pmo:mechanism "testosterone stimulates erythropoiesis partly by increasing renal EPO secretion"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hematocrit> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atestosterone%3Apositive%3Ahematocrit> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hematocrit> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"maintenance of body function changes such as… hematocrit in the male range (higher than the female range)\" and \"Stimulation of erythropoiesis resulting in an increase in hematocrit.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p 1579–1581  ||  \"testosterone treatment caused erythrocytosis (hemoglobin concentration >17.5 g/dL)\" and \"The only adverse effect found in these studies was excessive erythrocytosis in some men\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p. 725  ||  \"Testosterone replacement stimulates erythropoiesis in hypogonadal men, increasing the hemoglobin concentration and hematocrit from the female range into the normal adult male range. Occasionally, testosterone treatment causes excessive erythrocytosis (e.g., hematocrit >54%)\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p 751"
    ; pmo:mechanism "testosterone increases erythropoiesis via EPO and direct bone marrow effects, raising hematocrit to the male range"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> obo:RO_0002411 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hdl_cholesterol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atestosterone%3Aunknown%3Ahdl_cholesterol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hdl_cholesterol> ; pmo:sign "?"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"testosterone and DHT, but not estradiol, increase and maintain muscle mass and strength; testosterone, DHT, and estradiol play a role in normal male sexual function; and testosterone and DHT have opposing effects to estradiol on high-density lipoprotein cholesterol concentrations.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p 1309–1311  ||  \"In hypogonadal men, testosterone replacement results in no change or only a slight decrease in HDL cholesterol...The reduction in HDL cholesterol is greater in men with more severe androgen deficiency and in those treated with supraphysiologic testosterone doses or with nonaromatizable, oral 17alpha-alkylated androgens.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p 753  ||  \"testosterone increases serum levels of LDL cholesterol and decreases HDL cholesterol concentrations ... Postheparin hepatic lipase activity is increased by exogenous androgens (and decreased by estrogens), accounting for the decrease in HDL after androgen treatment or after a rise in endogenous androgen secretion.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 26, p. 1599–1600  ||  \"Androgens, by activating the androgen receptor, decrease HDL. Changes in HDL with estrogens and androgens correlate with changes in hepatic triglyceride lipase, an enzyme that, like LPL, is released into the bloodstream after heparin injection.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 41, p. 1587"
    ; pmo:mechanism "androgens reduce hepatic apoA-I synthesis and HDL, while estrogens increase HDL; testosterone and DHT have opposing effects to estradiol on HDL"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_dihydrotestosterone> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atestosterone%3Apositive%3Aplasma_dihydrotestosterone> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_dihydrotestosterone> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Men with inactivating mutations of SRD5A2 are born with severe 46,XY DSD… Serum DHT concentrations are low, testosterone concentrations are normal to slightly elevated.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p 1329–1351"
    ; pmo:mechanism "testosterone is converted to DHT by 5α-reductase (SRD5A1/SRD5A2) in peripheral tissues; SRD5A2 LoF mutations produce low serum DHT with normal-to-elevated testosterone"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atestosterone%3Apositive%3Aestradiol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The few men reported with inactivating mutations of the aromatase gene (CYP19A1) have demonstrated… undetectable estradiol concentrations with normal to elevated serum testosterone and gonadotropin concentrations.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p 1365–1366"
    ; pmo:mechanism "testosterone is aromatized to estradiol by CYP19A1 (aromatase) primarily in adipose tissue and testes; aromatase LoF → undetectable estradiol with normal/elevated testosterone"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/red_cell_mass> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atestosterone%3Apositive%3Ared_cell_mass> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/red_cell_mass> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"the hematocrit remains in the female range due to inadequate androgen stimulation of erythropoiesis\" and \"Men with severe androgen deficiency may have a mild hypoproliferative normocytic, normochromic anemia within the female range in the absence of androgen stimulation of erythropoiesis\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p 692–693"
    ; pmo:mechanism "androgen receptor stimulation in erythroid progenitors and kidney (EPO induction) increases erythropoiesis; testosterone deprivation yields female-range hematocrit; testosterone replacement restores it"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adipose_tissue_mass> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atestosterone%3Anegative%3Aadipose_tissue_mass> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adipose_tissue_mass> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"After testosterone treatment, there was an increase in penis size; an increase in chest, pubic, and facial hair … an increase in muscle mass, particularly in the upper body; and loss of fat in the face, chest, and hips\" and \"increases in body fat\" following GnRH agonist treatment of prostate cancer — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p 692 (Fig.19.14 caption) and p 997  ||  \"Most demonstrated beneficial effects of testosterone treatment on body composition, with increasing lean or muscle mass and decreasing fat mass\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p. 724  ||  \"testosterone treatment for one year...significantly improved...body composition (increasing lean mass and decreasing fat mass)\" and \"testosterone treatment plus a very low energy diet improved symptoms and reduced fat mass while maintaining lean mass\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, pp 737, 742"
    ; pmo:mechanism "androgen receptor activation promotes lipolysis and inhibits lipogenesis in adipose tissue; testosterone treatment reduces fat depots; castration/GnRH-agonist therapy causes fat accumulation"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bone_mineral_density> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atestosterone%3Apositive%3Abone_mineral_density> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bone_mineral_density> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"testosterone and its active metabolite, estradiol, have an important role in the maintenance of bone mass, men with chronic androgen deficiency may present with osteopenia or osteoporosis on BMD measurement\" and \"decreases in … BMD (associated with an increase in fracture risk)\" with GnRH agonist treatment — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p 693 and p 995  ||  \"Compared with placebo, testosterone treatment for 1 year…improved…volumetric BMD, and estimated bone strength\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p. 725  ||  \"Compared to placebo, testosterone treatment for one year...significantly improved...volumetric BMD and estimated bone strength\" (Testosterone Trials, double-blind, placebo-controlled) — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p 737"
    ; pmo:mechanism "testosterone (and its metabolite estradiol) maintains bone mineral density by stimulating osteoblast activity and inhibiting osteoclast-mediated resorption; testosterone deprivation (e.g., GnRH agonist, orchidectomy) leads to osteopenia/osteoporosis"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/synaptic_dopamine_concentration> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_prolactin_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asynaptic_dopamine_concentration%3Anegative%3Apituitary_prolactin_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/synaptic_dopamine_concentration> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_prolactin_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Dopamine antagonism (e.g., by neuroleptic or antipsychotic agents) results in elevated prolactin concentrations that suppress endogenous gonadotropin and testosterone secretion\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p 697"
    ; pmo:mechanism "hypothalamic dopamine (acting at D2 receptors on lactotrophs) tonically inhibits pituitary prolactin secretion; dopamine receptor antagonists (neuroleptics/antipsychotics) remove this inhibition, causing hyperprolactinemia"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/vsm_cgmp_level> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cavernosal_smooth_muscle_tone> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Avsm_cgmp_level%3Anegative%3Acavernosal_smooth_muscle_tone> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/vsm_cgmp_level> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cavernosal_smooth_muscle_tone> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"cGMP activates cGMP-dependent protein kinase, which phosphorylates many proteins, including myosin light chains and ion channels that ultimately decrease intracellular calcium concentrations, causing smooth muscle relaxation, increase in penile blood flow, and erection. cGMP is primarily hydrolyzed and inactivated by the enzyme phosphodiesterase type 5 (PDE5). … oral PDE5 inhibitors, such as sildenafil, vardenafil, and tadalafil, which act to inhibit the breakdown of cGMP, resulting in more sustained smooth muscle relaxation and improved penile erection after erotic stimulation\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p 697–698"
    ; pmo:mechanism "cGMP activates cGMP-dependent protein kinase → phosphorylates myosin light chains and ion channels → decreases intracellular calcium → cavernosal smooth muscle relaxation and engorgement; PDE5 inhibitors (sildenafil, vardenafil, tadalafil) maintain high cGMP by inhibiting its hydrolysis, producing sustained relaxation and erection"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/opioid_concentration> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aopioid_concentration%3Anegative%3Agnrh> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/opioid_concentration> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Administration of opioid antagonists such as naloxone or naltrexone results in an increase in LH pulse frequency in normal men, suggesting that endogenous opioid neuronal systems within the hypothalamus exert a negative regulation on pulsatile GnRH secretion. Therefore, exogenous administration of opioids most likely causes reduced gonadotropin secretion by suppressing hypothalamic GnRH secretion.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p. 732"
    ; pmo:mechanism "exogenous opioids suppress hypothalamic GnRH secretion via mu-opioid receptors; opioid antagonist naloxone/naltrexone increases LH pulse frequency, confirming endogenous opioid inhibitory tone on GnRH"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/spermatogenesis_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alh%3Apositive%3Aspermatogenesis_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/spermatogenesis_rate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"In men with acquired gonadotropin deficiency, normal testicular volumes and no coexisting testicular disease, hCG treatment alone (without addition of FSH therapy) often stimulates spermatogenesis to concentrations sufficient to restore fertility.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p. 733"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LH-driven intratesticular testosterone production (via Leydig cells) is required for spermatogenesis; hCG treatment alone (without FSH) restores spermatogenesis in men with acquired gonadotropin deficiency"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/neurokinin_b> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aneurokinin_b%3Apositive%3Agnrh> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/neurokinin_b> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"TAC3, which encodes neurokinin B, another important GnRH stimulatory neuropeptide, and the gene for its receptor, TAC3R\" [mutations causing normosmic CHH] — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p. 729"
    ; pmo:mechanism "neurokinin B (TAC3) stimulates GnRH neuronal activity; inactivating TAC3/TAC3R mutations cause normosmic CHH with absent GnRH-driven LH pulsatility"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepcidin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atestosterone%3Anegative%3Ahepcidin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepcidin> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"A greater erythropoietic response to testosterone is associated with stimulation of erythropoietin and suppression of ferritin and hepcidin (a liver-derived peptide that is the main negative regulator of iron bioavailability), possibly resetting the relationship between erythropoietin and hemoglobin and increasing the availability and use of iron for red blood cell production.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19, p 751"
    ; pmo:mechanism "testosterone suppresses hepatic hepcidin expression, increasing ferroportin-mediated iron efflux and iron availability for erythropoiesis"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tuberoinfundibular_dopamine_tone> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_prolactin_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atuberoinfundibular_dopamine_tone%3Anegative%3Apituitary_prolactin_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tuberoinfundibular_dopamine_tone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_prolactin_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"PRL release increases in response to TRH and decreases in response to dopamine.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 23, p 835"
    ; pmo:mechanism "tuberoinfundibular dopamine binds D2 receptor on lactotrophs -> inhibits PRL secretion"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/acth> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_dheas> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aacth%3Apositive%3Aplasma_dheas> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/acth> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_dheas> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"In vitro ACTH directly stimulates DHEAS and cortisol production, and in vivo excess ACTH is clearly involved in driving the high androgen concentrations seen in fetuses affected with CAH.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 23, p 839"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ACTH binds MC2R on fetal adrenal fetal zone cells -> activates StAR -> increases DHEA sulfation to DHEAS"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_dheas> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aestradiol%3Anegative%3Aplasma_dheas> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_dheas> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "\"In pregnant baboons with placental estrogen production suppressed by administration of an aromatase inhibitor, the volume of the FZ of the fetal adrenal increased markedly. This effect was reversed by administration of inhibitor plus estrogen, suggesting that estrogen selectively suppresses FZ growth and development during the second half of primate pregnancy.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 23, p 839"
    ; pmo:mechanism "estrogen suppresses fetal adrenal fetal-zone growth/development -> reduced DHEAS production"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/norepinephrine> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/metabolic_heat_production> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Anorepinephrine%3Apositive%3Ametabolic_heat_production> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/norepinephrine> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/metabolic_heat_production> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "\"NE, through β-adrenergic receptors, stimulates thermogenesis by brown adipose tissue\" and \"Basal brown adipose tissue thermogenesis and NE-stimulated and dibutyryl cAMP-stimulated thermogenesis are decreased by fetal thyroidectomy.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 23, p 864"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Norepinephrine activates beta-adrenergic receptors on brown adipose tissue, stimulating UCP1/thermogenin-mediated uncoupled respiration and heat production; fetal thyroidectomy (which reduces NE responsiveness) reduces NE-stimulated and basal BAT thermogenesis"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/casr_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/parathyroid_chief_pth_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acasr_activity%3Anegative%3Aparathyroid_chief_pth_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/casr_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/parathyroid_chief_pth_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Inactivating mutations lead to switching off of PTH secretion at a lower calcium concentration than usual and hypercalcemia ensues.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 23, p 857  ||  \"Inactivating mutations cause familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia (FHH), a disease of defective calcium sensing... whereas activating mutations cause familial hypoparathyroidism with hypercalciuria. Furthermore, mice genetically engineered to have only one functioning copy of the CASR gene also have the expected defects in parathyroid calcium sensing.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 29, p. 1199–1200  ||  \"Calcium sensors in the parathyroid glands control PTH secretion by monitoring the blood concentration of ionized calcium\" and \"the ability of the normal parathyroid cell to suppress PTH secretion in response to hypercalcemia\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 29, p 1218, 1224"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CaSR detects extracellular ionized calcium; active CaSR suppresses PTH secretion; inactivating CaSR mutations (LoF) reduce the calcium set-point for PTH suppression, causing hypercalcemia from inadequate PTH suppression"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hsd17b3_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahsd17b3_activity%3Apositive%3Atestosterone> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hsd17b3_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The typical biochemical profile in 17βHSD3 deficiency is an elevated androstenedione level relative to testosterone... Expressed as a ratio of testosterone to androstenedione (after an hCG stimulation test before puberty), values less than 0.8 are typically found in these patients\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 23, p 909"
    ; pmo:mechanism "HSD17B3 catalyzes the reduction of androstenedione to testosterone in testicular Leydig cells; loss of function causes elevated androstenedione with reduced testosterone (elevated androstenedione:testosterone ratio)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_11_deoxycorticosterone> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_11_deoxycorticosterone%3Anegative%3Arenin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_11_deoxycorticosterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renin> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"prolonged accumulation of 11-deoxycorticosterone, which has some mineralocorticoid activity, leads to salt and fluid retention, and plasma renin is suppressed\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 23 [ch 24], p 922"
    ; pmo:mechanism "11-deoxycorticosterone (mineralocorticoid activity) causes salt and fluid retention, expanding blood volume and renal perfusion pressure, thereby suppressing juxtaglomerular renin release"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_11_deoxycorticosterone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mean_arterial_pressure> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_11_deoxycorticosterone%3Apositive%3Amean_arterial_pressure> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_11_deoxycorticosterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mean_arterial_pressure> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Hypertension occurs in two-thirds of cases as prolonged accumulation of 11-deoxycorticosterone, which has some mineralocorticoid activity, leads to salt and fluid retention\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 23 [ch 24], p 922"
    ; pmo:mechanism "11-deoxycorticosterone has mineralocorticoid activity causing sodium and fluid retention, expanding blood volume and raising arterial pressure; CYP11B1 LoF causes DOC accumulation and clinical hypertension"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ghrelin> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/somatostatin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aghrelin%3Anegative%3Asomatostatin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ghrelin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/somatostatin> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "\"At the hypothalamic level, it increases GHRH secretion and inhibits somatostatin neurons.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 25, p. 948"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Ghrelin at the hypothalamic level inhibits somatostatin-secreting neurons, reducing SRIF tone and thereby disinhibiting GH release"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_free_fatty_acids> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_free_fatty_acids%3Anegative%3Apituitary_gh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_free_fatty_acids> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Free fatty acids (FFAs) inhibit GHRH-stimulated GH secretion by suppressing GHRH and/or stimulating SST secretion, or a direct effect on somatotropes.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 25, p. 951"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Elevated plasma free fatty acids suppress GH secretion by inhibiting hypothalamic GHRH release and/or stimulating somatostatin secretion, or acting directly on somatotropes"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_igf1> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aestradiol%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_igf1> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_igf1> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Estrogen also regulates IGF1 transcription, as its administration to a Ghr−/− mouse can stimulate hepatic Igf1 synthesis and growth.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 25, p 952  ||  \"Gonadal steroids increase plasma IGF1 concentrations in CPP, as in normal puberty.\" and \"Treatment with GnRH agonist reduces the level of IGF1 to the normal range for bone age but not for chronologic age.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 26, p. 1134"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Estrogen directly stimulates hepatic IGF1 gene transcription independent of GHR signaling, as shown by rescue of hepatic Igf1 synthesis in GHR-null mice by estrogen administration"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_igfbp3> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apituitary_gh%3Apositive%3Aplasma_igfbp3> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_igfbp3> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Serum levels of IGFBP3 and ALS are reduced in patients with GHD or GH insensitivity\" and \"IGFBP3 is increased in states of GH excess and acromegaly.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 25, p 959  ||  \"When patients with GH insensitivity are treated with GH for several days, the levels of IGF1 and IGFBP do not increase as they would in normal individuals\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 25"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GH directly or via IGF1 upregulates IGFBP3 expression; loss of GH action (GHD, GH insensitivity) reduces IGFBP3 levels; GH excess (acromegaly) increases IGFBP3"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_igf1> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ail6%3Anegative%3Ahepatic_igf1> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_igf1> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "\"IL6 has been demonstrated to activate SOCS3; this provides a pathway for inflammation to inhibit IGF1 production because SOCS3 is a negative regulator of the JAK2/STAT5 GH signal transduction pathway\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 25, p. 979. Study: De Benedetti et al., transgenic murine model expressing excessive IL6 demonstrated IL6-mediated decrease in IGF1 production."
    ; pmo:mechanism "IL-6 activates SOCS3, which is a negative regulator of the JAK2/STAT5 GH signal transduction pathway in hepatocytes, thereby reducing IGF1 production; additionally IL-6 reduces IGFBP3 levels increasing IGF1 clearance"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_igfbp3> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ail6%3Anegative%3Aplasma_igfbp3> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_igfbp3> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"IL6 may also cause a decrease in serum IGF1 levels by increasing its clearance through a decrease in IGFBP3 levels\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 25, p. 979"
    ; pmo:mechanism "IL-6 decreases IGFBP3 levels, thereby increasing clearance of IGF1 from the circulation"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_insulin> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_igfbp1> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_insulin%3Anegative%3Aplasma_igfbp1> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_insulin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_igfbp1> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"IGFBP1 is normally suppressed by insulin, and hypoinsulinemia results in elevated serum IGFBP1 levels that may inhibit IGF action\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 25, p. 980"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Insulin suppresses hepatic IGFBP1 production; hypoinsulinemia disinhibits IGFBP1 secretion, raising circulating IGFBP1 which sequesters IGF1"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_fgf21> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_igf1> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_fgf21%3Anegative%3Ahepatic_igf1> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_fgf21> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_igf1> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"fasting increases expression of FGF21, which may cause GH insensitivity by inhibiting STAT5\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 25, p. 974"
    ; pmo:mechanism "FGF21 inhibits STAT5 activity in the hepatocyte GH receptor signalling pathway, causing GH insensitivity and reduced IGF1 production"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aestradiol%3Apositive%3Apituitary_gh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"GH secretion stimulated by the rise in estrogen produced from the ovary or from aromatization of testosterone...after 2 days of treatment with estrogen, 95% of these children had a peak GH level higher than 7 µg/L\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 25  ||  \"Gonadal steroids exert direct effects on the bone by stimulating the generation of insulin-like growth factor 1 (IGF1) and other growth factors locally, and they exert indirect effects by stimulating increased GH secretion, which exerts its own effects on bone and stimulates the production of IGF1. In the female, the major gonadal steroid involved in the pubertal growth spurt is estradiol\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 26, p. 1329–1331  ||  \"treatment of late pubertal boys with tamoxifen, an estrogen receptor blocker, causes smaller GH secretory peaks and fewer GH secretory episodes\" and \"Exogenous estrogen increases the peak GH reached after insulin-induced hypoglycemia, exercise, and arginine\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 26, p 1059  ||  \"The rapid growth is associated with the increased GH secretion and elevation of serum IGF1 levels resulting from stimulation by estradiol.\" and \"Treatment with GnRH agonist usually results in decreased secretion of GH, most strikingly during sleep, and in a decreased GH response to provocative stimuli.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 26, p. 1121, 1134"
    ; pmo:mechanism "estrogen (from ovary or testosterone aromatization) acts on somatotrophs to increase GH secretion capacity"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atestosterone%3Apositive%3Apituitary_gh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Testosterone enhances growth velocity by direct actions, increases GH production, and may have a direct effect on IGF1 secretion\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 25"
    ; pmo:mechanism "testosterone (pharmacological intramuscular injection) increases GH production from pituitary somatotrophs"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acortisol%3Anegative%3Apituitary_gh_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Testing should not be performed if the patient is taking supraphysiologic doses of glucocorticoid (i.e., >15 mg/m2 per day of hydrocortisone or the equivalent of a synthetic glucocorticoid) because these drugs can suppress the GH response\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 25"
    ; pmo:mechanism "supraphysiologic glucocorticoid (hydrocortisone or synthetic equivalent) suppresses pituitary GH secretory response"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/linear_growth_velocity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apituitary_gh%3Apositive%3Alinear_growth_velocity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pituitary_gh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/linear_growth_velocity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "\"the general regimen of daily GH at the recommended doses typically accelerates growth in a GH-deficient child from a pretreatment rate of 3 to 4 cm/year to 10 to 12 cm/year in year 1 of therapy\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 25"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GH stimulates hepatic IGF1 production and direct epiphyseal growth-plate effects, accelerating longitudinal bone growth"
    .
