<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_kisspeptin%3Apositive%3Agnrh> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_kisspeptin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gnrh> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"A 1-year-old boy with CPP had a heterozygous missense mutation in KISS1 ... His KISS1 product was thought to be more resistant to degradation, leading to greater kisspeptin bioavailability and leading to a greater capacity to stimulate signal transduction compared to the wild-type kisspeptin\" and \"A heterozygous activating mutation in the KISS1R (p.Arg386Pro), which led to prolonged activation of intracellular signaling pathways in response to kisspeptin.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 26, p. 1123"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Kisspeptin binds KISS1R on GnRH neurons, stimulating pulsatile GnRH secretion; gain-of-function mutations in KISS1R (prolonged signaling) or KISS1 (resistance to degradation, increased bioavailability) cause CPP via excess GnRH pulse-generator activation"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/testosterone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_igf1> .
