<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aglp1%3Anegative%3Afood_intake> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glp1> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/food_intake> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"GLP1 is an incretin hormone produced in the gut that has been shown to boost glucose-dependent insulin secretion, suppress glucagon secretion, delay gastric emptying, and promote satiety\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 36, p 1434  ||  \"In humans, peripheral GLP1 administration to normal and diabetic subjects induces satiety and reduces food intake in short-term studies. Chronic continuous GLP1 administration to human diabetic subjects is associated with weight loss\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 39, p 1565  ||  \"GLP1, when infused directly into the rat ventricle, opposes the actions of orexigenic peptides including MCH and NPY\" and GLP1R agonists \"found to have meaningful effects in reducing appetite and body weight\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 40, p 1570"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GLP-1 → hypothalamic/vagal GLP-1R signaling disposition → enhanced satiety and reduced food intake"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta_cell_insulin_secretion> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alpha_cell_glucagon_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abeta_cell_insulin_secretion%3Anegative%3Aalpha_cell_glucagon_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta_cell_insulin_secretion> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alpha_cell_glucagon_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; dcterms:source "OmniPath INS->INSR (P01308->P06213, consensus_stimulation); Unger & Cherrington 2012 J Clin Invest PMID:22214853 [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "co-secreted insulin + Zn2+ -> alpha-cell INSR / Zn2+ action -> suppression of glucagon exocytosis (GO:0070091 glucagon secretion, negative regulation)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ethanol> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_gluconeogenesis> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_ethanol%3Anegative%3Ahepatic_gluconeogenesis> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ethanol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_gluconeogenesis> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Ethanol inhibits gluconeogenesis. Clinical alcohol-induced hypoglycemia typically follows a binge of alcohol consumption during which the person eats little food (i.e., in the setting of glycogen depletion).\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 38, p. 1288"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ethanol inhibits hepatic gluconeogenesis, causing hypoglycemia when glycogen is depleted; clinical alcohol-induced hypoglycemia follows binge drinking with glycogen depletion"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_glycogenolysis> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_glucose_production> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahepatic_glycogenolysis%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_glucose_production> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_glycogenolysis> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_glucose_production> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The liver is the major source of net endogenous glucose production (through glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis).\" and \"stimulates hepatic glucose production, largely by stimulating glycogenolysis.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 38, p. 1527"
    ; pmo:mechanism "hepatic glycogenolysis converts glycogen to glucose-6-phosphate and then to free glucose (via glucose-6-phosphatase), directly contributing to hepatic glucose output"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_gluconeogenesis> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_glucose_production> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahepatic_gluconeogenesis%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_glucose_production> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_gluconeogenesis> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_glucose_production> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis are important for maintenance of the plasma glucose concentration.\" and \"In liver: glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis\" listed as sources of endogenous glucose influx (Table 38.2). Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 38, p. 1527"
    ; pmo:mechanism "hepatic gluconeogenesis synthesizes new glucose from lactate, amino acids, and glycerol, contributing directly to hepatic glucose output released into the circulation"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta_cell_glucokinase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta_cell_insulin_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abeta_cell_glucokinase_activity%3Apositive%3Abeta_cell_insulin_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta_cell_glucokinase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta_cell_insulin_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Activating, dominantly inherited mutations of the glucokinase gene cause varying degrees of hypoglycemia that may respond to diazoxide but may require pancreatectomy.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 38, p 1550"
    ; pmo:mechanism "beta-cell glucokinase (GCK) phosphorylates glucose as the rate-limiting step for glycolytic ATP generation, raising the ATP/ADP ratio that drives KATP channel closure, membrane depolarisation, Ca2+ influx and insulin exocytosis; activating GCK mutations increase this flux, causing hyperinsulinism"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glutamate_dehydrogenase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta_cell_insulin_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aglutamate_dehydrogenase_activity%3Apositive%3Abeta_cell_insulin_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glutamate_dehydrogenase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta_cell_insulin_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Activating, dominantly inherited mutations of the glutamate dehydrogenase gene cause hyperinsulinemia and hyperammonemia syndrome, the second most common form of congenital hyperinsulinism. Hypoglycemia typically develops after several months of life and is responsive to diazoxide.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 38, p 1550"
    ; pmo:mechanism "activating mutations of glutamate dehydrogenase (GLUD1) increase oxidative glutamate deamination in beta-cell mitochondria, raising the ATP/ADP ratio via alpha-ketoglutarate entry into the TCA cycle, stimulating insulin secretion; leucine also activates this pathway"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/leptin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/resting_energy_expenditure> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aleptin%3Apositive%3Aresting_energy_expenditure> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/leptin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/resting_energy_expenditure> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Decreased leptin following caloric restriction initiates the neuroendocrine starvation response, increasing food-seeking and appetite and suppressing the expenditure of energy by neuroendocrine systems\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 39, p 1554"
    ; pmo:mechanism "leptin activates neuroendocrine and autonomic systems that increase energy expenditure; its absence suppresses expenditure"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alpha_msh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mc4r_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aalpha_msh%3Apositive%3Amc4r_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alpha_msh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mc4r_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "INTERVENTIONAL (pharmacological). Setmelanotide, an MC4R agonist, activates MC4R and reduces food intake in POMC/LEPR deficiency. DRAFT for domain review."
    ; pmo:mechanism "alpha-MSH is the endogenous MC4R agonist (quality: alpha-MSH level -> disposition: MC4R agonist occupancy -> process: MC4R Gs activation -> quality: MC4R signalling)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mc4r_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/food_intake> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amc4r_activity%3Anegative%3Afood_intake> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mc4r_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/food_intake> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Intracerebroventricular administration of αMSH or other melanocortin receptor agonists decreases food intake and body weight; overexpression of ASIP in the brain antagonizes the anorectic action of αMSH signaling and blunts endogenous melanocortin receptor activity resulting in hyperphagia\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 39, p 1554  ||  \"MC4R deletion in mice and mutations of MC4R in humans are associated with obesity ... MC4R mutations are the most common cause of monogenic obesity, accounting for about 5% of early-onset familial obesity\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 40, p 1570"
    ; pmo:mechanism "MC4R signalling in paraventricular nucleus neurons suppresses food intake via projections to lateral parabrachial nucleus"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mc4r_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/metabolic_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amc4r_activity%3Apositive%3Ametabolic_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mc4r_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/metabolic_rate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"MC4R blockade in mice prevents diet-induced thermogenesis and blocks the upregulation of brown adipose tissue activity\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 39, p 1561"
    ; pmo:mechanism "MC4R signalling activates sympathetic drive to brown/skeletal adipose tissue increasing thermogenesis and overall metabolic rate"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mc4r_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sympathetic_tone> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amc4r_activity%3Apositive%3Asympathetic_tone> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mc4r_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sympathetic_tone> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Deleting MC4Rs in autonomic (cholinergic) neurons lowers energy expenditure and increases adiposity\" and \"Selective deletion or re-expression of MC4R expression in cholinergic neurons produces...failure to increase adipose tissue UCP1, an impairment of diet-induced thermogenesis\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 39, p 1236/1561"
    ; pmo:mechanism "MC4R in sympathetic preganglionic neurons (intermediolateral cell column) directly drives sympathetic outflow; MC4R deletion in cholinergic neurons impairs diet-induced thermogenesis"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mc4r_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta_cell_insulin_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amc4r_activity%3Anegative%3Abeta_cell_insulin_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mc4r_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta_cell_insulin_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Deleting MC4Rs in parasympathetic preganglionic neurons (vagal motor neurons) causes hyperinsulinemia and insulin resistance\" and \"MC4R agonists decrease plasma insulin levels in lean and obese mice\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 39, p 1235/1565"
    ; pmo:mechanism "MC4R in the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus regulates parasympathetic tone to the pancreas, suppressing insulin secretion"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/agrp_npy_tone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/food_intake> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aagrp_npy_tone%3Apositive%3Afood_intake> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/agrp_npy_tone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/food_intake> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "\"ablation of GABA signaling by NPY/AGRP cells abrogates their orexigenic action\" and \"Not only does activating NPY/AGRP neurons increase food intake and body weight, but ablating NPY/AGRP neurons in adult animals results in anorexia\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 39, p 1558  ||  \"Administration of both NPY and AgRP peptides into rodent brain markedly increased food intake, even in satiated rats. Direct stimulation of these neurons also induces feeding.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 40, p 1569"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GABA and neuropeptide signalling from NPY/AGRP neurons inhibits anorexigenic circuits (POMC neurons and lateral parabrachial nucleus CGRP cells) to drive food intake"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/agrp_npy_tone> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alpha_msh> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aagrp_npy_tone%3Anegative%3Aalpha_msh> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/agrp_npy_tone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alpha_msh> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "\"GABA signaling by NPY/AGRP cells not only provides direct inhibitory input to arcuate nucleus POMC neurons but also is required to promote food intake\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 39, p 1558"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GABA released by NPY/AGRP neurons provides direct inhibitory synaptic input to arcuate POMC neurons, reducing alphaMS production/release"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_pyy> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/food_intake> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_pyy%3Anegative%3Afood_intake> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_pyy> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/food_intake> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Peripheral administration of PYY(3-36) in pharmacologic doses suppresses feeding in rodents as well as humans, thus suggesting a potential function as a satiety factor\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 39, p 1565"
    ; pmo:mechanism "PYY(3-36) activates Y2 receptors in the brainstem and inhibits NPY/AGRP neurons to suppress food intake"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_pyy> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gastric_emptying_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_pyy%3Anegative%3Agastric_emptying_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_pyy> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gastric_emptying_rate> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Infusion of PYY within the physiologic range has numerous effects, including inhibition of gastric emptying\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 39, p 1565"
    ; pmo:mechanism "PYY acts on vagal afferents and NTS/DMV to inhibit gastric motility and slow gastric emptying"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_pyy> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gastric_acid_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_pyy%3Anegative%3Agastric_acid_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_pyy> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gastric_acid_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"PYY appears to inhibit gastric acid secretion primarily through vagal innervation of the gastric fundus\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 39, p 1565"
    ; pmo:mechanism "PYY inhibits gastric acid secretion primarily through vagal innervation of the gastric fundus via Y2 receptors"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cck> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/food_intake> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_cck%3Anegative%3Afood_intake> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_cck> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/food_intake> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Studies using CCK receptor–specific antagonists as well as surgical or chemical vagotomy demonstrated that the satiety effects of CCK are specifically mediated via CCKA receptors on afferent vagal nerves\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 39, p 1566"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CCK acts via CCKA receptors on vagal afferent nerves to promote meal termination and satiety"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_amylin> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/food_intake> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_amylin%3Anegative%3Afood_intake> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_amylin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/food_intake> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Both peripheral and intracerebroventricular infusions of amylin inhibit food intake acutely and, during chronic infusion, lead to a sustained reduction in body weight\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 39, p 1566"
    ; pmo:mechanism "amylin acts on area postrema and NTS to inhibit food intake; also impairs gastric motility"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_gdf15> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/food_intake> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_gdf15%3Anegative%3Afood_intake> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_gdf15> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/food_intake> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Exogenous GDF15 suppresses feeding (including at levels observed in the aforementioned disease states), and interference with GDF15 action provides protection from the anorexia and weight loss associated with these disease states\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 39, p 1566"
    ; pmo:mechanism "GDF15 acts via GFRAL receptor on area postrema neurons to suppress food intake in response to cellular stress"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/resting_energy_expenditure> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aestradiol%3Apositive%3Aresting_energy_expenditure> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/resting_energy_expenditure> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Ovariectomy causes rats to gain weight to a similar extent when they are pair-fed to estradiol-treated rats, suggesting that endogenous estrogens regulate body weight homeostasis primarily by modulating energy expenditure\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 39, p 1560"
    ; pmo:mechanism "estradiol via ERalpha in ventromedial nucleus neurons increases sympathetic drive and diet-induced thermogenesis; ovariectomy causes obesity mainly via decreased energy expenditure"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/leptin> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aleptin%3Anegative%3Aplasma_glucose> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/leptin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Direct central nervous system infusion of leptin increases glucose disposal and rapidly normalizes blood glucose in Lepob/ob mice\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 39, p 1561"
    ; pmo:mechanism "central leptin action increases glucose disposal and normalizes blood glucose via hypothalamic circuits"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/leptin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/peripheral_glucose_uptake> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aleptin%3Apositive%3Aperipheral_glucose_uptake> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/leptin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/peripheral_glucose_uptake> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Central nervous system infusion of leptin also increases glucose disposal into muscle and brown adipose tissue and normalizes blood glucose in insulin-deficient rodents\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 39, p 1561"
    ; pmo:mechanism "central leptin signalling via hypothalamic circuits increases peripheral glucose disposal into muscle and brown adipose tissue"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/agrp_npy_tone> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mc4r_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aagrp_npy_tone%3Anegative%3Amc4r_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/agrp_npy_tone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/mc4r_activity> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "INTERVENTIONAL (genetic). AgRP overexpression / leptin-deficient disinhibition lowers MC4R signalling and drives hyperphagia. DRAFT for domain review."
    ; pmo:mechanism "AgRP is an MC4R inverse agonist/antagonist (quality: AgRP tone -> disposition: competitive MC4R blockade -> process: reduced MC4R signalling -> quality: MC4R signalling)"
    .
