<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atnf_alpha%3Anegative%3Aperipheral_glucose_uptake> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tnf_alpha> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/peripheral_glucose_uptake> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"TNFα expression increases with obesity and leads to localized inflammation that is associated with decreased insulin sensitivity. Likewise, blocking TNFα action improves insulin sensitivity.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 40, p 1573–1574"
    ; pmo:mechanism "TNF-alpha impairs insulin signalling (IRS-1 phosphorylation) in adipocytes/muscle, reducing insulin-mediated glucose uptake"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ail6%3Apositive%3Aplasma_glucose> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Administration of IL6 induces dose-dependent increases in fasting blood glucose in humans, probably by stimulating release of glucagon and other counterregulatory hormones, by inducing peripheral resistance to insulin action, or both.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 40, p 1574"
    ; pmo:mechanism "IL-6 stimulates glucagon secretion and induces peripheral insulin resistance, raising fasting blood glucose"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/peripheral_glucose_uptake> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ail6%3Anegative%3Aperipheral_glucose_uptake> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/peripheral_glucose_uptake> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "\"IL6 directly impairs insulin signaling\" and IL-6 administration raises blood glucose \"by inducing peripheral resistance to insulin action\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 40, p 1574"
    ; pmo:mechanism "IL-6 induces peripheral insulin resistance, impairing insulin-mediated glucose uptake"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_resistin> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/peripheral_glucose_uptake> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_resistin%3Anegative%3Aperipheral_glucose_uptake> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_resistin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/peripheral_glucose_uptake> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Administration of recombinant resistin to normal mice leads to impaired glucose tolerance and attenuated insulin action\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 40, p 1573"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Resistin impairs insulin signalling, attenuating insulin-mediated glucose uptake"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hypothalamic_mch_level> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/food_intake> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahypothalamic_mch_level%3Apositive%3Afood_intake> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hypothalamic_mch_level> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/food_intake> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "\"MCH administration into the brain leads to an acute, robust increase in feeding, whereas genetic deletion of the neuropeptide leads to leanness. MCH antagonists inhibit feeding in several species\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 40, p 1569"
    ; pmo:mechanism "MCH neurons in lateral hypothalamus signal to increase feeding; MCH peptide administration acutely increases food intake"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pka_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adipose_hsl_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apka_activity%3Apositive%3Aadipose_hsl_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pka_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/adipose_hsl_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"This process cannot occur unless perilipin 1, a protein that coats small lipid droplets, is phosphorylated by protein kinase A.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 41, p. 1585"
    ; pmo:mechanism "PKA phosphorylates perilipin-1 (required) and hormone-sensitive lipase, activating the diglyceride hydrolysis step of adipose lipolysis"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/malonyl_coa_level> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cpt1_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Amalonyl_coa_level%3Anegative%3Acpt1_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/malonyl_coa_level> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cpt1_activity> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Malonyl CoA inhibits carnitine palmitoyltransferase 1 (CPT1), which transports fatty acids into mitochondria, and thereby malonyl CoA prevents catabolism of fats under physiologic conditions in which energy is being stored as fat through fatty acid biosynthesis.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 41, p. 1583"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Malonyl-CoA (product of ACC/de novo lipogenesis) allosterically inhibits CPT1, blocking fatty-acid translocation into mitochondria and thus β-oxidation"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/acetyl_coa_carboxylase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/malonyl_coa_level> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aacetyl_coa_carboxylase_activity%3Apositive%3Amalonyl_coa_level> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/acetyl_coa_carboxylase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/malonyl_coa_level> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Acetyl CoA is then converted to malonyl CoA by acetyl CoA carboxylase (ACC)\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 41, p. 1583"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ACC catalyses acetyl-CoA → malonyl-CoA (first committed step of fatty acid synthesis); ACC activity determines malonyl-CoA steady-state level"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ldl_cholesterol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afree_t3%3Anegative%3Aldl_cholesterol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/free_t3> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ldl_cholesterol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Thyroid hormone reduces circulating cholesterol concentrations via modulation of LDL receptor expression, or through reduced hepatic synthesis of apoB lipoproteins and greater excretion of cholesterol into the bile. Thus, lipid levels tend to be high in hypothyroid patients and low with hyperthyroidism.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 41, p. 1587"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Thyroid hormone upregulates hepatic LDL receptor expression → increased LDL uptake and clearance → reduced plasma LDL; also reduces apoB lipoprotein synthesis and increases biliary cholesterol excretion"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ldl_cholesterol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acortisol%3Apositive%3Aldl_cholesterol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ldl_cholesterol> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Glucocorticoids have robust effects on multiple aspects of lipid metabolism, inducing expression of HMG-CoA reductase to promote cholesterol synthesis ... hyperlipidemia is commonly seen in the setting of glucocorticoid treatment, and insulin resistance induced by glucocorticoids amplifies the hyperlipidemia.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 41, p. 1587"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Glucocorticoids induce HMG-CoA reductase (↑cholesterol synthesis), increase FASN (↑lipogenesis), decrease LPL (↓TG clearance) → net hyperlipidemia including elevated LDL; insulin resistance amplifies effect"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_triglycerides> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aestradiol%3Apositive%3Aplasma_triglycerides> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_triglycerides> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Oral estrogen therapy increases plasma triglyceride levels as a result of greater liver production of VLDL\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 41, p. 1480 [section: Secondary Causes, Drugs]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Oral estrogens increase hepatic VLDL production → elevated plasma triglycerides (transdermal route spares liver first-pass and does not raise TG)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/er_cholesterol_level> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nuclear_srebp2_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aer_cholesterol_level%3Anegative%3Anuclear_srebp2_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/er_cholesterol_level> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nuclear_srebp2_activity> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "INTERVENTIONAL (pharmacological). Statins inhibit HMG-CoA reductase and deplete hepatic sterol, which ACTIVATES SREBP-2 and raises LDLR/HMGCR transcription (the feedback that statins exploit). DRAFT for domain review."
    ; pmo:mechanism "low ER cholesterol releases SCAP/SREBP-2 from INSIG, permitting Golgi cleavage and nuclear SREBP-2 accumulation (quality: ER sterol level -> disposition: SCAP escort competence -> process: regulated intramembrane proteolysis -> quality: nuclear SREBP-2 activity)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nuclear_srebp2_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_ldl_receptor_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Anuclear_srebp2_activity%3Apositive%3Ahepatic_ldl_receptor_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/nuclear_srebp2_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_ldl_receptor_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"SREBP2 is most important for LDL receptor transcription; its NH2-terminus contains a leucine zipper–type transcription factor structure that binds to a sterol regulatory element in the promoter of the LDL receptor gene.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 41, p. 1594"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Active nuclear SREBP-2 binds sterol regulatory element in LDL receptor gene promoter → increased LDL receptor transcription → more surface LDL receptors"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pcsk9_concentration> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_ldl_receptor_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apcsk9_concentration%3Anegative%3Ahepatic_ldl_receptor_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pcsk9_concentration> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_ldl_receptor_activity> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "\"PCSK9 is a secreted protease that promotes the degradation of the LDL receptor by interacting with the receptor so that it is targeted to the lysosome ... Human loss-of-function mutations in PCSK9 are associated with low LDL concentrations and decreased risk of vascular disease.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 41, p. 1596"
    ; pmo:mechanism "PCSK9 binds LDL receptor extracellularly and redirects it to lysosomal degradation rather than cell-surface recycling → fewer surface LDL receptors → less LDL clearance → higher plasma LDL"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_lipase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hdl_cholesterol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahepatic_lipase_activity%3Anegative%3Ahdl_cholesterol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepatic_lipase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hdl_cholesterol> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "\"High levels of hepatic lipase decrease HDL concentrations, whereas high levels of LPL increase HDL.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 41, p. 1595"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Hepatic lipase hydrolyses HDL phospholipids and triglycerides → converts HDL2 to smaller HDL3 and accelerates HDL catabolism → lower plasma HDL"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_free_fatty_acids> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/vldl_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_free_fatty_acids%3Apositive%3Avldl_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_free_fatty_acids> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/vldl_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Lipid derived from remnants and from lipolysis of adipose tissue is reassembled in the liver as VLDL particles, which are secreted into the plasma. Abnormal lipid metabolism in insulin resistance is mediated in large part by overproduction of VLDL, an event that occurs through disruption of signaling downstream of the insulin receptor\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 41, p. 1596"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Free fatty acids released from adipose lipolysis are transported to liver and reassembled as VLDL particles; increased FFA flux → increased hepatic VLDL triglyceride production and secretion"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_insulin> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/vldl_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_insulin%3Anegative%3Avldl_secretion%3Adirect-hepatic-insulin-signaling> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_insulin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/vldl_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Insulin reduces fatty acid flux from adipose tissue, reduces liver apoB production, inhibits de novo triglyceride synthesis, and optimizes LPL production. Kinetic studies in humans show that both increased triglyceride secretion and reduced clearance of triglyceride from the bloodstream often occur in concert in patients with diabetes.\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 41, p. 1431"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Insulin (via IRS/PI3K signalling) suppresses hepatic apoB production and de novo triglyceride synthesis, reduces FA flux from adipose → lower VLDL secretion; loss of insulin signalling in diabetes causes VLDL overproduction"
    ; pmo:contextId "direct-hepatic-insulin-signaling" ; pmo:contextLabel "direct hepatic insulin signaling and suppression of fatty-acid flux"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lipoprotein_lipase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hdl_cholesterol> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alipoprotein_lipase_activity%3Apositive%3Ahdl_cholesterol> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lipoprotein_lipase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hdl_cholesterol> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "\"high levels of LPL increase HDL\" Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 41, p. 1595"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LPL-mediated lipolysis of VLDL/chylomicrons generates excess surface components (phospholipids, apolipoproteins) that transfer to HDL particles, expanding the HDL pool; high LPL→ high HDL"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fgf23> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/proximal_tubule_calcitriol_production> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afgf23%3Anegative%3Aproximal_tubule_calcitriol_production> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fgf23> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/proximal_tubule_calcitriol_production> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "\"FGF23 decreases circulating levels of 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, both by decreasing mRNA levels for the renal 25(OH)D 1α-hydroxylase as well as by increasing expression of the 24-hydroxylase ... Klotho knockout mice exhibit the same hyperphosphatemia and high 1,25(OH)2D3 levels seen in the FGF23 knockout mice\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 29 (Actions of FGF23), p 1216"
    ; pmo:mechanism "FGF23 activates FGFR1 + α-Klotho on proximal-tubule cells (quality: plasma FGF23 → disposition: FGFR1/Klotho signalling → process: decreased CYP27B1/1α-hydroxylase mRNA (and increased 24-hydroxylase) → quality: reduced proximal-tubule calcitriol production rate); FGF23- and Klotho-null mice have high 1,25(OH)2D3"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fgf23> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/parathyroid_chief_pth_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afgf23%3Anegative%3Aparathyroid_chief_pth_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fgf23> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/parathyroid_chief_pth_secretion> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"FGF23, an important phosphate-regulating hormone ... activates FGF receptor 1 and its coreceptor, Klotho, on parathyroid cells and thereby suppresses PTH synthesis\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 29 (PTH Biosynthesis), p 1202"
    ; pmo:mechanism "FGF23 activates FGFR1 + Klotho on parathyroid chief cells (quality: plasma FGF23 → disposition: FGFR1/Klotho signalling → process: suppression of PTH synthesis/secretion → quality: chief-cell PTH secretion rate)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pth> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoblast_rankl> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apth%3Apositive%3Aosteoblast_rankl> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pth> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoblast_rankl> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"RANKL—also named osteoprotegerin ligand (OPGL) ... its synthesis is also increased by PTH.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 29 (Actions of PTH on Bone; RANKL/OPG), p 1205"
    ; pmo:mechanism "PTH acts on osteoblast/stromal PTH1R (quality: plasma PTH → disposition: PTH1R/cAMP signalling → process: increased RANKL (TNFSF11) transcription/secretion → quality: osteoblast RANKL secretion), driving osteoclastogenesis"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pth> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoblast_opg> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apth%3Anegative%3Aosteoblast_opg> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pth> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoblast_opg> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"PTH decreases the synthesis and secretion of OPG from these cells. Thus PTH, by increasing RANK and decreasing OPG locally in bone, serves to increase bone resorption.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 29 (Actions of PTH on Bone; RANKL/OPG), p 1205"
    ; pmo:mechanism "PTH acts on osteoblast-lineage PTH1R (quality: plasma PTH → disposition: PTH1R signalling → process: decreased OPG (TNFRSF11B) synthesis/secretion → quality: osteoblast OPG secretion), reducing RANKL sequestration and raising resorption"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcitonin> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoclast_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_calcitonin%3Anegative%3Aosteoclast_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_calcitonin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoclast_activity> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Calcitonin, which directly inhibits osteoclast function, may be used with other antiresorptive agents to achieve more rapid control of severe hypercalcemia.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 29 (Calcitonin / treatment of hypercalcemia)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "calcitonin binds the calcitonin receptor on mature osteoclasts (quality: plasma calcitonin → disposition: calcitonin-receptor signalling → process: osteoclast retraction/quiescence → quality: reduced osteoclast bone-resorption activity); salmon calcitonin is a clinical antiresorptive"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoclast_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aestradiol%3Anegative%3Aosteoclast_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoclast_activity> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "\"RANKL ... increases ... likely contribute to the rapid increase in osteoclastogenesis after estrogen withdrawal ... Osteoclasts express estrogen receptors, and some evidence suggests that direct actions of estrogen on osteoclasts are important as well.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 30 (postmenopausal bone loss / estrogen and bone)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "estradiol acts on ER of osteoclast lineage (and via reduced RANKL/increased OPG) (quality: plasma estradiol → disposition: ER signalling → process: reduced osteoclastogenesis / promotion of osteoclast apoptosis → quality: reduced osteoclast bone-resorption activity); estrogen withdrawal raises osteoclastogenesis, aromatase/ER mutations cause osteoporosis"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoblast_rankl> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aestradiol%3Anegative%3Aosteoblast_rankl> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/estradiol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoblast_rankl> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"RANKL has been identified as a major regulator of osteoclast differentiation, and increases (both locally and systemically) likely contribute to the rapid increase in osteoclastogenesis after estrogen withdrawal.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 30 (estrogen deprivation and bone)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "estradiol acts on osteoblast/stromal ER (quality: plasma estradiol → disposition: ER signalling → process: decreased RANKL (TNFSF11) production → quality: osteoblast/stromal RANKL secretion); RANKL rises on estrogen withdrawal"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/casr_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/parathyroid_chief_cell_proliferation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acasr_activity%3Anegative%3Aparathyroid_chief_cell_proliferation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/casr_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/parathyroid_chief_cell_proliferation> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Calcium, acting through the parathyroid CaSR, restrains parathyroid proliferation ... administration of the calcimimetic compound NPS R-568, which activates the CaSR directly, prevents parathyroid cell proliferation in experimental uremia.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 29 (Regulation of Parathyroid Cell Number), p 1202"
    ; pmo:mechanism "CaSR activation on chief cells (quality: chief-cell CaSR signalling activity → disposition: CaSR downstream signalling → process: restraint of parathyroid cell replication → quality: chief-cell proliferation rate); calcimimetic NPS R-568 prevents proliferation in uremia"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/calcitriol> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/parathyroid_chief_cell_proliferation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acalcitriol%3Anegative%3Aparathyroid_chief_cell_proliferation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/calcitriol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/parathyroid_chief_cell_proliferation> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The suppression of proliferation of cultured parathyroid cells by 1,25(OH)2D3 suggests that 1,25(OH)2D3 can directly inhibit parathyroid cell replication.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 29 (Regulation of Parathyroid Cell Number), p 1202"
    ; pmo:mechanism "calcitriol acts on parathyroid VDR (quality: plasma calcitriol → disposition: VDR signalling → process: suppression of parathyroid cell replication (and TGF-α/EGFR expression) → quality: chief-cell proliferation rate)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_phosphate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/parathyroid_chief_cell_proliferation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_phosphate%3Apositive%3Aparathyroid_chief_cell_proliferation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_phosphate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/parathyroid_chief_cell_proliferation> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Parathyroid cell number can dramatically increase, however, in the setting of hypocalcemia, low levels of 1,25(OH)2D3, hyperphosphatemia, or uremia, or during neoplastic growth.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 29 (Regulation of Parathyroid Cell Number), p 1202"
    ; pmo:mechanism "hyperphosphatemia drives parathyroid hyperplasia (quality: plasma phosphate → disposition: phosphate sensing → process: chief-cell proliferation → quality: chief-cell proliferation rate); the secondary hyperparathyroidism of CKD"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/calcitriol> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoblast_rankl> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acalcitriol%3Apositive%3Aosteoblast_rankl> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/calcitriol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoblast_rankl> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"[1,25(OH)2D3] increases osteoclastic bone resorption in high doses in vivo by stimulating production of RANKL (also called osteoclast differentiating factor) by osteoblasts. Notably, osteoblast-specific knockout of the vitamin D receptor leads to increased bone mass associated with impaired bone resorption due to decreased RANKL expression.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 29 (vitamin D actions on bone)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "calcitriol acts on osteoblast VDR (quality: plasma calcitriol → disposition: VDR signalling → process: increased RANKL (TNFSF11) production by osteoblasts → quality: osteoblast RANKL secretion), promoting osteoclastogenesis; osteoblast-specific VDR KO lowers RANKL"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pth> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteocyte_sclerostin> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apth%3Anegative%3Aosteocyte_sclerostin> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pth> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteocyte_sclerostin> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"Mechanical loading on bone and PTH therapy stimulates bone formation by downregulating sclerostin.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 30 (PTH/teriparatide anabolic action; sclerostin)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "PTH acts on osteocyte PTH1R (quality: plasma PTH → disposition: PTH1R signalling → process: decreased sclerostin (SOST) secretion → quality: osteocyte sclerostin secretion), disinhibiting Wnt/β-catenin and osteoblast activity (basis of teriparatide anabolism)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoblast_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acortisol%3Anegative%3Aosteoblast_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cortisol> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoblast_activity> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"glucocorticoids also have a strong negative effect on bone formation by suppressing expression of IGF1 in bone cells and by shifting marrow stromal cells into the fat lineage rather than down the osteoblast differentiation pathway.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 30 (glucocorticoid-induced bone loss)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "glucocorticoid acts on osteoblast GR (quality: plasma cortisol → disposition: GR signalling → process: suppressed IGF1/osteoblast differentiation, stromal-to-adipocyte shift, osteoblast apoptosis → quality: reduced osteoblast bone-formation activity); basis of glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tsh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tpo_nis_organification_capacity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atsh%3Apositive%3Atpo_nis_organification_capacity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tsh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tpo_nis_organification_capacity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"TSH increases NIS, Tg, and TPO synthesis and iodine organification and Tg turnover.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 11 (thyroid hormone synthesis / organification)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "TSH acts on follicular-cell TSHR (quality: plasma TSH → disposition: TSHR/cAMP signalling → process: increased NIS/Tg/TPO synthesis and iodide organification → quality: TPO/NIS organification capacity)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta_cell_insulin_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_glucose%3Apositive%3Abeta_cell_insulin_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_glucose> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta_cell_insulin_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The main stimulus for insulin secretion is glucose. ... glucose enters beta cells via the isoform 2 of the glucose transporter (GLUT2) and is metabolized\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 33 (Physiology of Insulin Secretion)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "glucose-stimulated insulin secretion (quality: plasma glucose → disposition: GLUT2 uptake + glucokinase/glycolysis → process: raised ATP/ADP -> K-ATP closure -> depolarization/Ca2+ influx -> exocytosis → quality: beta-cell insulin secretion rate); confirmed by glucokinase/K-ATP MODY/CHI mutations"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ghrelin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/agrp_npy_tone> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aghrelin%3Apositive%3Aagrp_npy_tone> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/ghrelin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/agrp_npy_tone> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "\"re-expression of the ghrelin receptor only in NPY/AGRP neurons in ghrelin receptor-null mice suffices to restore the orexigenic effect of exogenous ghrelin.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 39 (Neuroendocrine Control of Energy Stores; ghrelin / arcuate NPY-AgRP)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "ghrelin acts on GHSR of arcuate NPY/AgRP neurons (quality: plasma ghrelin → disposition: GHSR signalling → process: activation of NPY/AgRP neurons → quality: orexigenic AgRP/NPY neuron tone); GHSR re-expression restricted to NPY/AgRP neurons restores orexigenic effect"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fsh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sertoli_inhibin_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afsh%3Apositive%3Asertoli_inhibin_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fsh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sertoli_inhibin_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"FSH stimulates Sertoli cells to produce inhibin B, a peptide hormone that causes negative feedback inhibition of FSH secretion by the anterior pituitary.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 19 (Sertoli cell function / inhibin B)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "FSH acts on Sertoli-cell FSHR (quality: plasma FSH → disposition: FSHR/cAMP signalling → process: increased inhibin B secretion → quality: Sertoli-cell inhibin-B secretion rate), closing the FSH-inhibin B feedback loop"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fsh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aromatase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afsh%3Apositive%3Aaromatase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fsh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aromatase_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The rate-limiting step for granulosa cell estradiol production is regulated by the FSH-dependent activity of the aromatase enzyme in a cyclic fashion.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 17 (granulosa cell aromatase / follicular phase)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "FSH acts on granulosa-cell FSHR (quality: plasma FSH → disposition: FSHR/cAMP signalling → process: CYP19A1 (aromatase) induction → quality: aromatase catalytic activity), the rate-limiting step of granulosa estradiol synthesis (two-cell, two-gonadotropin model)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fsh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/granulosa_estradiol_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Afsh%3Apositive%3Agranulosa_estradiol_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/fsh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/granulosa_estradiol_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"cultured granulosa cells obtained from the small follicles of polycystic ovaries produce negligible amounts of estradiol but show a dramatic increase in estrogen production when FSH or IGF1 is added to the culture medium.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 17 (follicular phase / granulosa estradiol)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "FSH acts on granulosa-cell FSHR (quality: plasma FSH → disposition: FSHR/cAMP signalling → process: aromatase induction -> androgen-to-estradiol conversion and secretion → quality: granulosa-cell estradiol secretion rate); recombinant FSH raises endogenous estradiol clinically"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lh> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/luteal_progesterone_secretion> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Alh%3Apositive%3Aluteal_progesterone_secretion> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/lh> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/luteal_progesterone_secretion> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "\"The hCG secreted by the trophoblast acts as a surrogate LH and prevents regression of the corpus luteum that provides a continuous supply of progesterone until the placental tissue starts to produce sufficient quantities of progesterone.\" — Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 14th ed., ch 17 (luteal phase / corpus luteum maintenance)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "LH/hCG acts on luteal-cell LHCGR (quality: plasma LH → disposition: LHCGR/cAMP signalling → process: corpus luteum maintenance + steroidogenesis → quality: luteal-cell progesterone secretion rate); hCG (surrogate LH) rescues the corpus luteum in early pregnancy"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glut4_expression> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/peripheral_glucose_uptake> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aglut4_expression%3Apositive%3Aperipheral_glucose_uptake> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glut4_expression> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/peripheral_glucose_uptake> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "CB-MET-008. Intervention: two tissue-specific Slc2a4/GLUT4 knockouts in mouse — muscle-specific KO (Zisman A et al., Nat Med 2000;6:924-928, PMID:10932232) and adipose-specific KO (Abel ED et al., Nature 2001;409:729-733, PMID:11217863). Measured endpoint: insulin-stimulated glucose uptake — muscle GLUT4 KO nearly abolishes insulin-stimulated muscle glucose uptake with glucose intolerance/insulin resistance; adipose GLUT4 KO impairs adipocyte uptake and induces secondary muscle/liver insulin resistance. Context: mouse skeletal muscle/adipose, chronic total-GLUT4-protein manipulation. do(GLUT4 expression down) => peripheral glucose uptake down, sign +."
    ; pmo:mechanism "GLUT4 (SLC2A4) is the insulin-responsive glucose transporter; total GLUT4 protein sets the translocatable pool available for insulin-stimulated peripheral glucose disposal (quality: GLUT4 expression level -> disposition: insulin-responsive facilitated glucose transport capacity -> process: insulin-stimulated GLUT4 translocation and glucose transport -> quality: peripheral glucose uptake rate). Distinct do() from the mechanistic-model glut4_membrane_translocation -> peripheral_glucose_uptake edge (chronic total expression vs acute translocated fraction)."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_enac_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sodium_water_reabsorption> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Arenal_enac_activity%3Apositive%3Asodium_water_reabsorption> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_enac_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sodium_water_reabsorption> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "CB-PHY-001. Intervention: human monogenic gain/loss of ENaC function — SCNN1B/SCNN1G cytoplasmic PY-motif truncations (Liddle syndrome) increase surface ENaC (Shimkets RA et al., Cell 1994, PMID:7954808), recessive SCNN1A/B/G loss-of-function (systemic pseudohypoaldosteronism type 1) abolishes it (Chang SS et al., Nat Genet 1996, PMID:8589714); pharmacological corroboration amiloride/triamterene block ENaC (Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology, ISBN:9781455770052). Measured endpoint: distal-nephron Na+/water reabsorption — rises with ENaC GoF (volume expansion, hypertension), falls with ENaC LoF (renal salt wasting), amiloride produces natriuresis. Context: human kidney collecting duct; chronic Mendelian and acute amiloride manipulations converge. Direction +."
    ; pmo:mechanism "collecting-duct principal-cell apical ENaC sets the rate-limiting step of aldosterone-sensitive distal Na+ (and osmotically coupled water) reabsorption (quality: ENaC channel activity -> disposition: apical Na+ conductance -> process: electrogenic distal Na+ and coupled water reabsorption -> quality: renal sodium/water reabsorption rate)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldosterone> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_enac_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aaldosterone%3Apositive%3Arenal_enac_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/aldosterone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_enac_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "CB-PHY-002. Intervention: mineralocorticoid-receptor agonist (aldosterone/fludrocortisone) vs antagonist (spironolactone/eplerenone) on collecting-duct epithelia, with SGK1 as the aldosterone-induced mediator (Chen SY et al., PNAS 1999;96:2514-2519, PMID:10051674; SGK/Nedd4-2 control of ENaC surface expression, Snyder PM et al., J Biol Chem 2002, PMC125341). Measured endpoint: amiloride-sensitive Na+ current / surface ENaC number and open probability rise with MR agonism, abolished by MR antagonists. Context: human/rodent cortical collecting duct (A6, mpkCCD); pools rapid non-genomic + slower genomic components (same sign). Direction +."
    ; pmo:mechanism "aldosterone-bound mineralocorticoid receptor transcriptionally induces SGK1, which inhibits Nedd4-2, raising ENaC surface density and open probability (quality: plasma aldosterone -> disposition: MR/SGK1/Nedd4-2 signalling -> process: reduced ENaC ubiquitin-mediated retrieval -> quality: collecting-duct ENaC activity)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta1_adrenergic_activation> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gs_adenylyl_cyclase_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abeta1_adrenergic_activation%3Apositive%3Ags_adenylyl_cyclase_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/beta1_adrenergic_activation> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gs_adenylyl_cyclase_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "BioModels MODEL1006230118 Saucerman et al. 2003 J Biol Chem 278:47997 (PubMed 12972422): receptor -> Gs -> adenylyl cyclase module; Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology. | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.2 (37.2: How Hormones Work): 'The activated G-protein in turn activates a membrane-bound enzyme called adenylyl cyclase.' (Figure 37.6 caption: 'Hormone binding to receptor activates a G-protein, which in turn activates adenylyl cyclase') [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "active beta-1 receptor catalyses GDP/GTP exchange on Gs-alpha (disposition: GEF activity -> process: G-protein activation), and Gs-alpha-GTP stimulates adenylyl cyclase -> quality: AC catalytic rate"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gs_adenylyl_cyclase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/camp_level> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ags_adenylyl_cyclase_activity%3Apositive%3Acamp_level> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/gs_adenylyl_cyclase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/camp_level> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "BioModels MODEL1006230118 Saucerman et al. 2003 J Biol Chem 278:47997 (PubMed 12972422): AC production flux of cAMP; Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology. | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.2 (37.2: How Hormones Work): 'Adenylyl cyclase catalyzes the conversion of ATP to cAMP.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-9.2 (9.2: Propagation of the Signal): 'Cyclic AMP is synthesized by the enzyme adenylyl cyclase from ATP' [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "adenylyl cyclase (disposition: cyclase catalytic activity) runs process ATP -> 3',5'-cyclic AMP + PPi, raising quality: cytosolic cAMP concentration"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/camp_level> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pka_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acamp_level%3Apositive%3Apka_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/camp_level> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pka_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "BioModels BIOMD0000000165 Saucerman et al. 2006 PNAS 103:12923 (PubMed 16905651): cAMP-dependent PKA activation; also MODEL1006230118 Saucerman et al. 2003 (PubMed 12972422); Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology. | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.2 (37.2: How Hormones Work): 'cAMP, in turn, activates a group of proteins called protein kinases, which transfer a phosphate group from ATP to a substrate molecule in a process called phosphorylation.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-9.2 (9.2: Propagation of the Signal): 'The main role of cAMP in cells is to bind to and activate an enzyme called cAMP-dependent kinase (A-kinase)'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-9.3 (9.3: Response to the Signal): 'Cyclic AMP activates PKA (protein kinase A)' [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "cAMP (quality: concentration) binds PKA regulatory subunits (disposition: allosteric release -> process: dissociation of catalytic subunits) -> quality: free PKA-C catalytic activity"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/camp_level> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pde_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acamp_level%3Apositive%3Apde_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/camp_level> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pde_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "BioModels MODEL1006230118 Saucerman et al. 2003 J Biol Chem 278:47997 — PDE hydrolysis of cAMP; Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology (PDE3/PDE4 as cAMP-degrading enzymes underlying inotrope pharmacology). | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.2 (37.2: How Hormones Work): 'An enzyme called phosphodiesterase breaks down cAMP, terminating the signal.' (substrate-driven PDE activation implied by cAMP level); OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-9.2 (9.2: Propagation of the Signal): 'Termination of the signal occurs when an enzyme called phosphodiesterase converts cAMP into AMP' (cAMP drives PDE activity) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "rising cAMP (quality: substrate concentration) increases occupancy of phosphodiesterase (disposition: hydrolase activity) -> process: cAMP hydrolysis flux -> quality: PDE turnover rate (substrate-driven; PKA also feed-forward activates PDE3/PDE4)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pde_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/camp_level> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apde_activity%3Anegative%3Acamp_level> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pde_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/camp_level> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "BioModels MODEL1006230118 Saucerman et al. 2003 J Biol Chem 278:47997 — PDE degradation flux reduces cAMP; Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology. | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-37.2 (37.2: How Hormones Work): 'cAMP is deactivated by the cytoplasmic enzyme phosphodiesterase, or PDE. PDE is always present in the cell and breaks down cAMP to control hormone activity, preventing overproduction of cellular products.'; OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-9.2 (9.2: Propagation of the Signal): 'Termination of the signal occurs when an enzyme called phosphodiesterase converts cAMP into AMP' (PDE degrades cAMP, reducing its level); OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-9.3 (9.3: Response to the Signal): 'Cyclic AMP (cAMP) is degraded into AMP by phosphodiesterase' [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "phosphodiesterase (disposition: 3',5'-cyclic-nucleotide hydrolase) runs process cAMP -> 5'-AMP, lowering quality: cytosolic cAMP concentration"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pka_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/heart_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Apka_activity%3Apositive%3Aheart_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/pka_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/heart_rate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Saucerman et al. 2003 J Biol Chem 278:47997 (PMID 12972422); Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology (beta-adrenergic cAMP/PKA control of chronotropy)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "PKA phosphorylation of pacemaker and calcium-handling targets increases sinoatrial firing rate and positive chronotropy"
    .
