    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "\"cells release PTH, which stimulates osteoclast proliferation and resorption of bone by osteoclasts\" — OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e, section anp-035 (Calcium Homeostasis)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "PTH (via osteoblast/osteocyte RANKL signalling to osteoclast precursors) stimulates osteoclast proliferation and activation, increasing bone-resorption activity and calcium release"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteocyte_sclerostin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoblast_rankl> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aosteocyte_sclerostin%3Apositive%3Aosteoblast_rankl> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteocyte_sclerostin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoblast_rankl> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "Wijenayaka 2011 PLoS One PMID:21991382 [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Sclerostin stimulates RANKL expression by osteocytes/osteoblasts (raises RANKL:OPG ratio), promoting osteoclast formation (GO:0045672)\n"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoclast_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoblast_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aosteoclast_activity%3Apositive%3Aosteoblast_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoclast_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoblast_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Sims & Martin 2014 Bonekey Rep PMID:24466412; BioModels Komarova2003 BIOMD0000000148 | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-031 (6.4: Bone Structure): 'Osteoclasts are continually breaking down old bone while osteoblasts are continually forming new bone. The ongoing balance between osteoblasts and osteoclasts is responsible for the constant but subtle reshaping of bone.' Paget's disease example: overactive osteoclasts → osteoblasts try to compensate (coupling). [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Bone resorption releases matrix-bound TGF-beta/IGF-1 and osteoclasts secrete coupling factors (e.g. sphingosine-1-phosphate, CTHRC1) that recruit and stimulate osteoblasts (GO:0045669 positive regulation of osteoblast differentiation)\n"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoblast_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoblast_opg> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aosteoblast_activity%3Apositive%3Aosteoblast_opg> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoblast_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoblast_opg> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Boyce & Xing 2008 PMID:18395508 (Arch Biochem Biophys — RANKL/RANK/OPG review; PubMed-verified) [citations PubMed-verified] | curated_mechanistic: Wnt/beta-catenin-driven OPG production by mature osteoblasts is established functional biology (osteoblast-specific beta-catenin perturbation evidence not directly opened this pass)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Mature, Wnt/beta-catenin-active osteoblasts upregulate OPG (TNFRSF11B), favoring the anti-resorptive arm as bone formation proceeds\n"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/l_type_calcium_current> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sr_calcium_release> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Al_type_calcium_current%3Apositive%3Asr_calcium_release> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/l_type_calcium_current> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sr_calcium_release> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Bers (2001) Excitation-Contraction Coupling, ch. on CICR: I_CaL is the physiological trigger that opens RyR2; BioModels MODEL7914464799 (Shannon2004_VentricularMyocyte, PubMed 15347581) and MODEL0393108880 (tenTusscher2004, PubMed 14656705). [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "sarcolemmal Ca2+ entry through Cav1.2 raises dyadic-cleft Ca2+ (quality: trigger Ca2+ -> disposition: RyR2 Ca2+-sensitivity -> process: calcium-induced calcium release -> quality: SR Ca2+ release flux)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/l_type_calcium_current> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cytosolic_calcium_transient> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Al_type_calcium_current%3Apositive%3Acytosolic_calcium_transient> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/l_type_calcium_current> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cytosolic_calcium_transient> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "BioModels MODEL7914464799 (Shannon2004_VentricularMyocyte, PubMed 15347581): cytosolic Ca2+ balance includes the I_CaL flux term; Bers (2001). | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-059 (10.8: Cardiac Muscle Tissue): Ca++ entry through voltage-gated calcium channels in sarcolemma provides sustained depolarization plateau and longer contraction in cardiac muscle; OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-107 (19.3: Cardiac Muscle and Electrical Activity): approximately 20% of the calcium required for contraction is supplied by the influx of Ca2+ through slow calcium channels during the plateau phase of the cardiac action potential [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "L-type Ca2+ influx contributes a direct trans-sarcolemmal component to the rise in cytosolic free Ca2+ in addition to triggering SR release (quality: Ca2+ influx -> process: diffusion into cytosol -> quality: cytosolic [Ca2+])"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sr_calcium_release> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cytosolic_calcium_transient> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asr_calcium_release%3Apositive%3Acytosolic_calcium_transient> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sr_calcium_release> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cytosolic_calcium_transient> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Bers (2001) Excitation-Contraction Coupling: ~70-90% of the systolic Ca2+ transient derives from SR release; BioModels MODEL7914464799 (Shannon2004, PubMed 15347581). | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-107 (19.3: Cardiac Muscle and Electrical Activity): the remaining Ca2+ for contraction is released from storage in the sarcoplasmic reticulum, constituting the major source of systolic calcium; Wong, Cells: Molecules and Mechanisms §wong-12.9 (Cell Motility - Undeclared): 'When the t-tubule is depolarized... allows [Ca2+ channels on the SR] to open. Ca2+ rushes out of the SR where it is available to troponin-c.' [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "RyR2 release delivers the majority of activator Ca2+ (quality: SR release flux -> process: Ca2+ efflux from SR into cytosol -> quality: systolic cytosolic [Ca2+])"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cytosolic_calcium_transient> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/troponin_c_calcium_binding> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acytosolic_calcium_transient%3Apositive%3Atroponin_c_calcium_binding> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cytosolic_calcium_transient> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/troponin_c_calcium_binding> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Bers (2001) Excitation-Contraction Coupling: cytosolic Ca2+ binds troponin C to activate the myofilaments; BioModels MODEL7914464799 (Shannon2004, PubMed 15347581) includes cTnC as the dominant cytosolic Ca2+ buffer / activation site. | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-107 (19.3: Cardiac Muscle and Electrical Activity): calcium ions combine with the regulatory protein troponin in the troponin-tropomyosin complex, removing inhibition of myosin cross-bridge formation with actin active sites to produce contraction; Wong, Cells: Molecules and Mechanisms §wong-12.9 (Cell Motility - Undeclared): 'When Ca2+ is available, it can bind to troponin-C, leading to a conformational change that shifts the position of tropomyosin to reveal the myosin binding site.'; Jakubowski & Flatt, Fundamentals of Biochemistry Vol I §biochem-5.5.7 (Summary): 'Ca²⁺ binding to troponin-C triggers conformational changes that displace tropomyosin, thereby initiating contraction by allowing myosin heads to interact with actin.' [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "rising cytosolic free Ca2+ binds the regulatory N-lobe of cardiac troponin C (quality: cytosolic [Ca2+] -> disposition: cTnC Ca2+ affinity -> process: Ca2+ binding to cTnC -> quality: fractional Ca2+ occupancy of cTnC / thin-filament activation)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cytosolic_calcium_transient> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/serca_uptake_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acytosolic_calcium_transient%3Apositive%3Aserca_uptake_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cytosolic_calcium_transient> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/serca_uptake_rate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Bers (2001) Excitation-Contraction Coupling: SERCA2a uptake is steeply Ca2+ dependent and removes the bulk of cytosolic Ca2+ during relaxation; BioModels MODEL7914464799 (Shannon2004, PubMed 15347581). | textbook corroboration: Jakubowski & Flatt, Fundamentals of Biochemistry Vol I §biochem-11.5.3 (Membrane ATPases): elevated cytoplasmic Ca2+ activates SERCA-mediated Ca2+ pumping back into the SR after muscle excitation.; Wong, Cells: Molecules and Mechanisms §wong-12.9 (Cell Motility - Undeclared): 'as cytoplasmic Ca2+ levels rise, the phospholamban releases from the Ca2+ pump and allows it to function.'; Wong, Cells: Molecules and Mechanisms §wong-4.4 (The Action Potential in Neurons): 'The increased [Ca2+]cytoplasm is taken up by the sarcoplasmic reticulum' and Ca2+-calmodulin relieves inhibition of the Ca2+ pump, increasing Ca2+ removal when [Ca2+] rises. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "elevated cytosolic Ca2+ raises the SERCA2a turnover (quality: cytosolic [Ca2+] -> disposition: SERCA2a Ca2+ affinity on the cytosolic face -> process: ATP-driven Ca2+ pumping into the SR -> quality: SR Ca2+ uptake rate)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/serca_uptake_rate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sr_calcium_load> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aserca_uptake_rate%3Apositive%3Asr_calcium_load> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/serca_uptake_rate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sr_calcium_load> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Bers (2001) Excitation-Contraction Coupling: SERCA2a sets steady-state SR Ca2+ content; BioModels MODEL7914464799 (Shannon2004, PubMed 15347581). | textbook corroboration: Jakubowski & Flatt, Fundamentals of Biochemistry Vol I §biochem-11.5.3 (Membrane ATPases): SERCA transports Ca2+ from cytoplasm into the SR lumen, replenishing the SR store (actual free [Ca2+] in SR ~390 µM vs 0.1–0.2 µM in cytoplasm).; Wong, Cells: Molecules and Mechanisms §wong-4.4 (The Action Potential in Neurons): increased cytoplasmic Ca2+ is taken up by the sarcoplasmic reticulum, increasing its Ca2+ load. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "SERCA2a pumps Ca2+ into the SR lumen (quality: uptake rate -> process: net Ca2+ accumulation in the SR -> quality: diastolic SR Ca2+ load)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sr_calcium_load> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sr_calcium_release> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asr_calcium_load%3Apositive%3Asr_calcium_release> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sr_calcium_load> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sr_calcium_release> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Bers (2001) Excitation-Contraction Coupling: SR Ca2+ release is a steep function of SR Ca2+ load (load-dependence of release gain); BioModels MODEL7914464799 (Shannon2004, PubMed 15347581) models load-dependent RyR release. | textbook corroboration: Wong, Cells: Molecules and Mechanisms §wong-4.4 (The Action Potential in Neurons): extra SR Ca2+ load 'leading to extra Ca2+ when it is released to trigger muscle contraction, causing stronger contractions.' [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "RyR2 release flux is steeply dependent on luminal SR Ca2+ load (quality: SR Ca2+ load -> disposition: luminal Ca2+ regulation of RyR2 gating -> process: greater fractional release per trigger -> quality: SR release flux)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sr_calcium_release> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sr_calcium_load> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asr_calcium_release%3Anegative%3Asr_calcium_load> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sr_calcium_release> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sr_calcium_load> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Cannell, Kong, Imtiaz & Laver (2013) \"Control of SR Ca2+ Release by Stochastic RyR Gating... and Importance of Induction Decay for CICR Termination\", Biophys J 104:2149-2159 (PubMed 23708355, PMC3660628): release-driven local SR depletion terminates the Ca2+ spark; Bers (2001) Excitation-Contraction Coupling: release flux depletes SR Ca2+ content during systole. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "RyR2 release flux empties the SR lumen: efflux of Ca2+ out of the store during systole depletes the SR content (quality: SR release flux -> process: net Ca2+ loss from the SR lumen -> quality: SR Ca2+ load). Local junctional-SR depletion (\"induction decay\") is the dominant beat-to-beat CICR-termination mechanism."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cytosolic_calcium_transient> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sr_calcium_release> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acytosolic_calcium_transient%3Anegative%3Asr_calcium_release> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cytosolic_calcium_transient> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sr_calcium_release> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Bers (2001) Excitation-Contraction Coupling: Ca2+-dependent inactivation of RyR2 contributes to making CICR self-limiting (graded release); BioModels MODEL7914464799 (Shannon2004, PubMed 15347581) includes Ca-dependent RyR/release inactivation gating. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "rising cytosolic/dyadic Ca2+ inactivates the RyR2 release channels (quality: local cytosolic [Ca2+] -> disposition: Ca2+-dependent inactivation of RyR2 -> process: closure of release units -> quality: reduction of SR release flux). Secondary, cytosolic-side contributor to release termination; the dominant self-limiting term is the load-depletion edge sr_calcium_release -| sr_calcium_load above."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/troponin_c_calcium_binding> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cardiac_output> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atroponin_c_calcium_binding%3Apositive%3Acardiac_output> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/troponin_c_calcium_binding> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cardiac_output> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "mechanistic_model"
    ; dcterms:source "Bers DM. Excitation-Contraction Coupling and Cardiac Contractile Force (2001); Shannon et al. 2004 (PMID 15347581); OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §19.3."
    ; pmo:mechanism "calcium-bound cardiac troponin C displaces tropomyosin, increases actomyosin cross-bridge recruitment and ventricular force, and thereby increases cardiac output at a fixed loading condition"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_lysine> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/endothelial_no_production> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_lysine%3Anegative%3Aendothelial_no_production> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_lysine> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/endothelial_no_production> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Hyperlysinemia OMIM 238700 (AASS deficiency); Closs EI et al., Arterioscler Thromb Vasc Biol 2004 (CAT1/SLC7A1 mediates arginine and lysine transport into endothelial cells; competition between cationic amino acids); Bhatta A et al., Physiol Res 2011 (lysine competes with arginine at the cationic transporter affecting endothelial NOS). | intervention: do(AASS loss-of-function) — hyperlysinemia OMIM 238700; do(high-lysine diet/supplementation) — pharmacological increase in plasma lysine [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "L-lysine and L-arginine are both cationic amino acids that compete for the cationic amino acid transporter CAT1 (SLC7A1), which mediates arginine uptake into endothelial cells to supply eNOS substrate. Elevated plasma lysine competitively inhibits arginine transport into endothelium, reducing the intracellular arginine available for eNOS → reduced NO production. This is the mechanism underlying the 'arginine-lysine competition'. In hyperlysinemia (AASS/LKRSDH LoF), elevated plasma lysine reduces endothelial arginine bioavailability and NO production. do(AASS LoF) → plasma lysine ↑ → CAT1 arginine transport ↓ → endothelial NO ↓."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glutathione_synthetase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/endothelial_no_production> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aglutathione_synthetase_activity%3Apositive%3Aendothelial_no_production> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glutathione_synthetase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/endothelial_no_production> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Glutathione synthetase deficiency OMIM 266130; Gao L et al., Arterioscler Thromb Vasc Biol 2009 (GSH maintains eNOS coupling via BH4 protection); Zweier JL et al., Circ Res 2011 (eNOS uncoupling mechanism in oxidative stress — BH4 and zinc thiolate); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (glutathione as antioxidant; reduction of disulfides). | intervention: do(GSS loss-of-function) — glutathione synthetase deficiency OMIM 266130; do(GCLC loss-of-function) — gamma-glutamylcysteine synthetase deficiency OMIM 230450 [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Glutathione synthetase (GSS) produces glutathione (GSH), the primary intracellular antioxidant. GSH maintains the eNOS cofactor tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4) in its reduced, active form by reducing BH4 radicals generated during the eNOS catalytic cycle; GSH also prevents oxidation of the eNOS zinc-thiolate cluster required for enzyme dimerization. When GSH is depleted (GSS LoF), BH4 is oxidized to BH2, causing eNOS uncoupling (eNOS generates superoxide instead of NO), markedly reducing endothelial NO production. do(GSS LoF) → glutathione ↓ → BH4 oxidized → eNOS uncoupling → endothelial NO ↓."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_arginine> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/endothelial_no_production> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_arginine%3Apositive%3Aendothelial_no_production> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_arginine> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/endothelial_no_production> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Hyperargininemia (argininemia) OMIM 207800 (ARG1 deficiency — elevated plasma and CSF arginine); Tousoulis D et al., Int J Cardiol 2012 (L-arginine supplementation increases endothelial NO production); Closs EI et al., Arterioscler Thromb Vasc Biol 2004 (arginine transport into endothelium via CAT1/SLC7A1 is rate-limiting for eNOS; arginine paradox); Bode-Boger SM et al., Pharmacol Ther 2007 (arginine as eNOS substrate; elevated arginine augments NO synthesis); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (arginine → citrulline + NO by NOS). | intervention: do(ARG1 loss-of-function) — hyperargininemia / argininemia OMIM 207800 [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "L-arginine is the obligate substrate for endothelial NO synthase (eNOS/NOS3); eNOS oxidizes arginine to citrulline + NO. Under conditions where plasma arginine is elevated (as in hyperargininemia, ARG1 LoF), endothelial CAT1-mediated arginine uptake is enhanced, increasing the intracellular arginine pool available to eNOS, and NO production rises. The 'arginine paradox' demonstrates that even at normal plasma concentrations eNOS is substrate-limited due to intracellular compartmentation, so elevated plasma arginine (hyperargininemia) further augments eNOS activity. do(ARG1 LoF) → plasma arginine ↑ → endothelial arginine uptake ↑ → eNOS substrate ↑ → NO production ↑."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cystathionine_gamma_lyase_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/endothelial_no_production> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acystathionine_gamma_lyase_activity%3Apositive%3Aendothelial_no_production> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cystathionine_gamma_lyase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/endothelial_no_production> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Yang G et al., Science 2008 (CTH is the major vascular H2S synthase; CTH−/− mice show reduced NO production and hypertension — PMID 18948540); Coletta C et al., Proc Natl Acad Sci 2012 (H2S activates eNOS via PI3K/Akt-Ser1177 phosphorylation); Citi V et al., Br J Pharmacol 2018 (CTH-derived H2S maintains eNOS coupling); Cystathioninuria OMIM 219500 (CTH LoF); Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (transsulfuration: cystathionine → CTH → cysteine + alpha-ketobutyrate + H2S/NH3). | intervention: do(CTH loss-of-function) — cystathioninuria OMIM 219500; pharmacological CTH inhibition [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Cystathionine gamma-lyase (CTH / cystathionase) is the principal vascular enzyme for endogenous hydrogen sulfide (H2S) production from cystathionine and cysteine. Vascular H2S activates eNOS (NOS3) by (i) S-sulfhydrating eNOS at Cys443, stabilizing its active conformation; (ii) activating the PI3K/Akt pathway that phosphorylates eNOS at Ser1177; and (iii) preventing eNOS S-glutathionylation-induced uncoupling. Multiple genetic and pharmacological studies show that CTH LoF reduces vascular H2S and impairs eNOS-derived NO production. do(CTH LoF) → H2S ↓ → eNOS activation ↓ → endothelial NO production ↓."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/asl_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/endothelial_no_production> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aasl_activity%3Apositive%3Aendothelial_no_production> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/asl_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/endothelial_no_production> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Erez et al., Nature Medicine 2011 (Requirement of argininosuccinate lyase for systemic nitric oxide production — ASL LoF in mice and humans causes reduced NO); Brunetti-Pierri et al., Am J Hum Genet 2018 / PMC6080833: 'Argininosuccinate Lyase Deficiency Causes an Endothelial-Dependent Form of Hypertension'; GeneReviews NBK51784 (ASL deficiency, OMIM 207900); Lehninger, urea cycle step 4. | intervention: do(ASL loss-of-function) — argininosuccinic aciduria OMIM 207900: ASL activity DOWN -> arginine synthesis impaired -> eNOS substrate deficiency -> endothelial NO production DOWN [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Argininosuccinate lyase (ASL) cleaves argininosuccinate -> arginine + fumarate (urea cycle step 4). ASL is the sole route of endogenous arginine de novo synthesis. Arginine is the obligate substrate for all NOS isoforms including eNOS/NOS3. ASL deficiency (argininosuccinic aciduria, OMIM 207900) causes systemic arginine deficiency AND disrupts the ASL-ASS1-eNOS multi-protein complex, impairing eNOS-dependent NO production. do(ASL LoF) -> endothelial NO production falls; do(ASL GOF / higher activity) -> more arginine -> more eNOS substrate -> more NO."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tissue_galactosylceramide> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/macrophage_activation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atissue_galactosylceramide%3Apositive%3Amacrophage_activation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tissue_galactosylceramide> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/macrophage_activation> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Neuroimmune mechanisms in Krabbe disease, PMC5129482 (macrophage activation, TNF-alpha/IL-6/iNOS upregulation); StatPearls NBK562315; Galactosylceramidase deficiency and pathological abnormalities PMC10474820; Macrophages Expressing GALC Improve Peripheral Krabbe Disease Neuron 2020 (PMC7924901): GALC deficiency leads to galactosylceramide-laden globoid macrophages that drive inflammation. Disease: Krabbe disease / globoid-cell leukodystrophy (OMIM 245200). | intervention: GALC LoF (Krabbe disease): tissue galactosylceramide rises; macrophages phagocytose galactosylceramide -> M1 activation / inflammatory cytokine secretion increases"
    ; pmo:mechanism "In Krabbe disease (GALC deficiency), galactosylceramide accumulates in tissue macrophages/microglia, converting them into multinucleated globoid cells. Galactosylceramide-laden macrophages become classically activated (M1), secreting TNF-alpha, IL-6, and iNOS-derived NO, which drives the neuroinflammatory cascade. Elevated tissue galactosylceramide (due to GALC LoF) directly triggers macrophage pro-inflammatory activation."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/telomere_length> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythroblast_proliferation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Atelomere_length%3Apositive%3Aerythroblast_proliferation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/telomere_length> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythroblast_proliferation> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Alter BP et al 2009 Ann Intern Med 150:529-537 — dyskeratosis congenita (telomere disease) causes bone marrow failure due to exhausted haematopoietic progenitor replicative capacity; Ball SE et al 1998 Br J Haematol 103:990-996 — erythroid progenitors from DC patients show shortened telomeres and reduced colony formation; Armanios M & Blackburn EH 2012 Nat Rev Genet 13:693-704 — telomere length is a limiting factor for haematopoietic stem/progenitor proliferative reserve. | intervention: do(telomere_length -) — dyskeratosis congenita (TERT/TERC/DKC1 LoF) causes pancytopenia including aplastic anaemia from failed erythroblast proliferation (OMIM 127550); do(telomere_length +) — danazol-mediated TERT upregulation in telomeropathy patients partially rescues haematopoietic progenitor proliferation (Townsley DM et al 2016 NEJM 374:1922). [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Critically short telomeres in haematopoietic progenitors activate the ATM/ATR-p53-p21 DNA-damage checkpoint, inducing replicative senescence or apoptosis and halting cell division. Longer telomeres allow more cell divisions before this checkpoint is engaged, supporting sustained erythroblast proliferation in bone marrow. Telomeropathies (dyskeratosis congenita: DKC1, TERT, TERC mutations) manifest as aplastic anaemia precisely because shortened telomeres exhaust erythroblast and other haematopoietic progenitor replicative capacity."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cardiomyocyte_intracellular_sodium> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cytosolic_calcium_transient> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acardiomyocyte_intracellular_sodium%3Apositive%3Acytosolic_calcium_transient> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cardiomyocyte_intracellular_sodium> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/cytosolic_calcium_transient> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "Bers DM 2002 Nature 415:198-205 — quantitative analysis of NCX-mediated Ca2+ extrusion: rising [Na+]i slows NCX forward mode, increasing SR Ca2+ load and systolic Ca2+ transient; Bhatt DL et al and Digitalis Investigation Group 1997 NEJM 336:525 — digoxin (Na/K-ATPase inhibitor) raises [Na+]i, increases Ca2+ transient, positive inotropy; Sipido KR et al 1997 Circ Res 81:1174 — elevated [Na+]i in heart failure directly linked to reduced NCX efflux and elevated systolic Ca2+ transient amplitude. | intervention: do(cardiomyocyte_intracellular_sodium +) — digoxin (Na+/K+-ATPase inhibitor) raises [Na+]i, reducing NCX Ca2+ efflux, increasing cytosolic Ca2+ transient and contractility; do(cardiomyocyte_intracellular_sodium -) — ranolazine (late Na+ current inhibitor) lowers [Na+]i, reducing Ca2+ overload and Ca2+ transient amplitude. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "The sarcolemmal Na+/Ca2+ exchanger (NCX1) operates electrogenically: in forward mode it extrudes 1 Ca2+ per cycle by importing 3 Na+. When cardiomyocyte intracellular [Na+] rises, the inward Na+ driving force for NCX is reduced, impairing Ca2+ efflux; this raises SR Ca2+ load and increases the amplitude of the cytosolic Ca2+ transient during systole (positive inotropy). This is the molecular mechanism of cardiac glycosides (digoxin): inhibiting Na+/K+-ATPase → [Na+]i rises → NCX Ca2+ efflux decreases → Ca2+ transient increases → stronger contraction."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hgd_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoclast_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahgd_activity%3Anegative%3Aosteoclast_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hgd_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoclast_activity> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Alkaptonuria (HGD; OMIM 203500); Taylor et al., Am J Pathol 2017 (Mechanisms of Enhanced Osteoclastogenesis in Alkaptonuria); Ranganath et al., Ann Rheum Dis 2016 (PMC5188995): 'alkaptonuria — severe osteoclastic resorption and altered bone-remodelling biomarkers'; Alkaptonuria — GeneReviews NBK1454: 'resorption of subchondral bone and severe osteoarthropathy'; Saudubray, Inborn Metabolic Diseases (alkaptonuria, HGD). Intervention: do(HGD loss-of-function, alkaptonuria, OMIM 203500). | intervention: genetic LoF of HGD (homogentisate 1,2-dioxygenase; alkaptonuria, OMIM 203500) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Homogentisate 1,2-dioxygenase (HGD) cleaves homogentisic acid (HGA) in the tyrosine catabolism pathway. HGD deficiency (alkaptonuria, OMIM 203500) causes 100-fold elevation of plasma HGA, which polymerizes and deposits as a melanin-like pigment in cartilage, subchondral bone, and tendon (ochronosis). The ochronotic pigment deposits in bone microenvironment activate osteoclastogenesis through enhanced RANKL signalling, oxidative stress, and altered Wnt/beta-catenin signalling in osteoblasts, leading to enhanced osteoclast bone-resorption activity (osteolytic lesions, osteoporosis observed in AKU patients). Do(HGD LoF) -> HGA accumulates -> ochronotic deposits -> enhanced osteoclastogenesis -> osteoclast_activity increases. HGD active -> HGA cleared -> no ochronotic deposits -> normal osteoclast activity."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/acid_sphingomyelinase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/macrophage_activation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aacid_sphingomyelinase_activity%3Anegative%3Amacrophage_activation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/acid_sphingomyelinase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/macrophage_activation> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Niemann-Pick disease type A/B OMIM 257200/607616 (SMPD1 LoF); Wasserstein et al., Genet Med 2004 (NPD-B: macrophage foam cells, hepatosplenomegaly, elevated pro-inflammatory markers). McGovern et al., J Lipid Res 2004 (NPD-B: elevated TNF-alpha and IL-6, indicating macrophage activation). Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, Lysosomal Storage Diseases (Niemann-Pick: foam macrophages, reticuloendothelial system involvement). | intervention: SMPD1 loss-of-function (Niemann-Pick disease type A/B, OMIM 257200)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Acid sphingomyelinase (SMPD1) deficiency (Niemann-Pick disease types A/B, OMIM 257200/607616) causes sphingomyelin to accumulate in lysosomes, predominantly in mononuclear phagocytes. Macrophages and Kupffer cells become engorged 'foam cells' with lysosomal sphingomyelin storage, triggering chronic macrophage activation, pro-inflammatory cytokine secretion (TNF-alpha, IL-1beta, IL-6), and hepatosplenomegaly. do(SMPD1 LoF) → sphingomyelin storage in macrophages → macrophage foam cell transformation → macrophage classical activation rises."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/umps_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythroid_progenitor_proliferation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aumps_activity%3Apositive%3Aerythroid_progenitor_proliferation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/umps_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythroid_progenitor_proliferation> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Suchi et al., Am J Hum Genet 1997 (UMPS LoF: hereditary orotic aciduria with megaloblastic anemia); OMIM 258900 (hereditary orotic aciduria: UMPS deficiency -> orotic acid accumulation + megaloblastic anemia); Ng et al., Front Genet 2023 (UMPS deficiency: megaloblastic anemia, neutropenia, failure to thrive in infancy); Lecturio Orotic Aciduria (megaloblastic anemia from impaired nucleic acid synthesis in erythroid precursors). Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (de novo pyrimidine synthesis: UMPS produces UMP for pyrimidine nucleotide pool; essential for DNA replication). | intervention: do(UMPS LoF, OMIM 258900): de novo pyrimidine synthesis blocked at UMP; pyrimidine nucleotide pool depleted; erythroid progenitor DNA synthesis impaired; megaloblastic anemia. | intervention: do(UMPS LoF, hereditary orotic aciduria, OMIM 258900) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "UMPS (UMP synthase) catalyses the last two steps of de novo pyrimidine biosynthesis (orotate -> OMP -> UMP), providing the UMP precursor for all pyrimidine nucleotides (UTP, CTP, dTTP). UMP is essential for DNA synthesis in rapidly dividing erythroid progenitors. UMPS deficiency (hereditary orotic aciduria, OMIM 258900) blocks de novo pyrimidine synthesis, causing UMP/pyrimidine nucleotide deficiency in erythroid progenitors; impaired DNA synthesis leads to megaloblastic changes, nuclear-cytoplasmic asynchrony, intramedullary apoptosis, and megaloblastic anemia (severely impaired erythroid progenitor proliferation and maturation). Higher UMPS activity ensures adequate UMP supply for erythroid progenitor DNA synthesis."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alpha_l_fucosidase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/macrophage_activation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aalpha_l_fucosidase_activity%3Anegative%3Amacrophage_activation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/alpha_l_fucosidase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/macrophage_activation> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Stepien KM et al. Int J Mol Sci 2025 'Fucosidosis: A Review of a Rare Disease' (microglial activation, lysosomal dysfunction causing neuroinflammation and macrophage involvement; blood chitotriosidase moderately elevated in fucosidosis, indicating macrophage activation); Ashwell G 1974 (alpha-fucosidase deficiency causes fucosylated glycolipid/glycoprotein accumulation in macrophage-rich tissues); Fucosidosis mouse model PMC5047687 (macrophage infiltration, lysosomal storage pathology in FUCA1-KO mice). | intervention: do(FUCA1 LoF = fucosidosis OMIM:230000) -> alpha_l_fucosidase_activity falls -> fucosylated substrate accumulation -> macrophage_activation increases. | intervention: do(FUCA1 LoF = fucosidosis OMIM:230000) -> alpha_l_fucosidase_activity falls -> macrophage_activation increases [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "In fucosidosis (FUCA1/tissue alpha-L-fucosidase deficiency), fucosylated glycosphingolipids and glycoproteins accumulate in lysosomes of macrophages and other cells. This lysosomal storage drives microglial and macrophage activation in the CNS and peripheral tissues, similar to other glycoprotein storage disorders. Reduced alpha-L-fucosidase activity (LoF) leads to accumulation of fucosylated substrates -> macrophage lysosomal dysfunction -> macrophage activation. Sign is negative: lower enzyme activity -> higher macrophage activation."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glcnac_phosphotransferase_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoclast_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aglcnac_phosphotransferase_activity%3Anegative%3Aosteoclast_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/glcnac_phosphotransferase_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoclast_activity> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Kollmann K et al. J Bone Miner Res 2013 PMID:24127423 PMC3914524 'Decreased bone formation and increased osteoclastogenesis cause bone loss in mucolipidosis II' (GNPTAB knock-in mice: dysfunctional osteoblasts produce >5-fold elevated IL-6 driving RANKL-independent osteoclastogenesis; human ML-II bone biopsy confirms elevated osteoclast numbers; storage material accumulation in chondrocytes/osteoblasts confirmed); Mucolipidosis II alpha/beta OMIM:252500. | intervention: do(GNPTAB LoF = ML-II/I-cell disease OMIM:252500) -> GlcNAc-phosphotransferase activity falls -> osteoblast IL-6 up -> RANKL-independent osteoclastogenesis -> osteoclast_activity increases. | intervention: do(GNPTAB LoF = mucolipidosis II OMIM:252500) -> glcnac_phosphotransferase_activity falls -> osteoclast_activity increases [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "In mucolipidosis II / I-cell disease (GNPTAB/GlcNAc-1-phosphotransferase deficiency), lysosomal hydrolases are misdirected to the extracellular space due to failure of mannose-6-phosphate tagging. Lysosome-deficient osteoblasts accumulate storage material and produce markedly elevated IL-6 (>5-fold) via a RANKL-independent mechanism, which drives excessive osteoclastogenesis. Reduced GlcNAc-phosphotransferase activity (LoF) -> osteoblast lysosomal dysfunction + IL-6 -> RANKL-independent osteoclast recruitment -> osteoclast activity up. Sign is negative: lower enzyme activity -> higher osteoclast activity."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_methylcobalamin> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythroblast_proliferation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_methylcobalamin%3Apositive%3Aerythroblast_proliferation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_methylcobalamin> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythroblast_proliferation> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 'Megaloblastic Anemias'; Scott JM & Weir DG Blood 1981; Stover PJ PNAS 2017 (folate rescues B12-depletion-induced thymidylate synthesis inhibition); Lahner E et al. Ann Hematol 2025 (MTHFR/B12); OMIM 275350 (TCN2 deficiency — functional intracellular B12 deficiency despite normal serum B12 → megaloblastic anemia). | intervention: do(methylcobalamin deficiency, as in TCN2 LoF or nutritional B12 deficiency) [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Methylcobalamin is the obligatory cofactor for methionine synthase (MTR). In cobalamin deficiency (or TCN2/cblC/cblF LoF), folate becomes trapped as 5-methyltetrahydrofolate ('methyl-folate trap'), blocking de novo thymidylate and purine synthesis. Erythroblasts, with their rapid proliferation and high DNA-synthesis demand, are the principal victims: impaired thymidylate synthesis leads to uracil misincorporation, DNA double-strand breaks, and erythroblast apoptosis — producing megaloblastic anemia (ineffective erythropoiesis). do(methylcobalamin deficiency) → erythroblast proliferation rate decreases."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_5_methyltetrahydrofolate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythroblast_proliferation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_5_methyltetrahydrofolate%3Apositive%3Aerythroblast_proliferation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_5_methyltetrahydrofolate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythroblast_proliferation> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry (folate coenzymes in one-carbon metabolism and thymidylate synthesis); Harrison's 'Folate deficiency and megaloblastic anemia'; GeneReviews FOLR1 / OMIM 613068 (cerebral folate transport deficiency); Lucock M Mol Genet Metab 2000 (folate-dependent thymidylate synthesis in proliferating cells). | intervention: do(plasma folate / 5-MTHF deficiency, as in dietary folate deficiency or FOLR1 LoF)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "5-methyltetrahydrofolate (5-MTHF) is both the circulating folate form and the methyl donor for homocysteine remethylation (via methionine synthase). Folate deficiency — or FOLR1-mediated cerebral folate transport deficiency causing isolated CSF/tissue folate depletion — depletes the THF pool required for de novo thymidylate synthesis (thymidylate synthase) and purine synthesis. Erythroblasts depend on this for DNA replication; deficiency produces megaloblastic bone-marrow changes and impaired erythroblast proliferation. do(plasma 5-MTHF deficiency) → erythroblast proliferation decreases."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ficolin_3> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/macrophage_activation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_ficolin_3%3Apositive%3Amacrophage_activation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_ficolin_3> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/macrophage_activation> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Munthe-Fog et al. N Engl J Med 2009;360:1283 — first reported homozygous ficolin-3 (FCN3) deficiency in a patient with recurrent Aspergillus and Pseudomonas infections; absent lectin-pathway activity and abolished macrophage opsonophagocytosis. Janeway's Immunobiology 10th ed. Ch.2 (lectin pathway: ficolin/MBL → MASP → C3b opsonisation → macrophage CR3 phagocytosis). OMIM 613860 (Ficolin-3 deficiency, autosomal recessive) | intervention: do(FCN3 deficiency / plasma ficolin-3 absent) → macrophage_activation decreases | intervention: do(FCN3 deficiency, OMIM 613860 — plasma ficolin-3 absent) → macrophage_activation decreases"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Ficolin-3 (FCN3/Hakata antigen) is a lectin-pathway pattern-recognition molecule synthesised by hepatocytes. It binds acetylated polysaccharides on pathogen surfaces and activates MASP-1/MASP-2 serine proteases → C4b/C2a C3 convertase → C3b opsonisation → macrophage CR3-mediated phagocytosis and classical M1 activation. Higher plasma ficolin-3 → more lectin-pathway complement deposition on pathogens → greater macrophage phagocytic/inflammatory activation. FCN3 deficiency (OMIM 613860) causes recurrent severe bacterial infections due to absent complement opsonisation."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_mbl> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/macrophage_activation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_mbl%3Apositive%3Amacrophage_activation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_mbl> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/macrophage_activation> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Turner et al. Clin Exp Immunol 2000;119:383 — MBL-deficient adults have impaired opsonisation and neutrophil/macrophage killing of Staphylococcus aureus. Garred et al. Lancet Infect Dis 2006;6:132 — meta-analysis confirming MBL2 null alleles increase infectious susceptibility via defective complement-opsonin-mediated macrophage phagocytosis. OMIM 614372 (Mannose-binding lectin deficiency). Janeway's Immunobiology 10th ed. Ch.2 (MBL lectin complement pathway → macrophage opsonophagocytosis) | intervention: do(MBL2 null alleles / plasma MBL absent) → macrophage_activation decreases | intervention: do(MBL2 null alleles, OMIM 614372 — plasma MBL severely reduced) → macrophage_activation decreases"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Mannose-binding lectin (MBL, encoded by MBL2) is a hepatocyte-derived collectin that recognises mannose/N-acetylglucosamine arrays on pathogen surfaces and activates MASP-1/MASP-2 → C3 convertase → C3b opsonisation → macrophage CR1/CR3-mediated phagocytosis and M1 activation. MBL-deficient individuals (MBL2 functional allele polymorphisms; severe deficiency OMIM 614372) show impaired opsonin-mediated macrophage activation and increased susceptibility to infections. Higher plasma MBL → more lectin-pathway opsonisation → greater macrophage activation."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sod3_ecm_anchoring> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/endothelial_no_production> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Asod3_ecm_anchoring%3Apositive%3Aendothelial_no_production> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/sod3_ecm_anchoring> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/endothelial_no_production> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "Circulation Research 2004 (10.1161/01.res.0000092140.81594.a8, Extracellular Superoxide Dismutase Is a Major Determinant of Nitric Oxide Bioavailability): EC-SOD is the principal superoxide scavenger protecting endothelial NO; PMC3376381 (Role of Vascular Extracellular Superoxide Dismutase in Hypertension): SOD3 deletion increases vascular O2- and reduces NO in vessels; PMC3757697 (Extracellular but not cytosolic SOD protects against oxidant-mediated endothelial dysfunction): ecSOD specifically prevents endothelial dysfunction; PMC8058336 (SOD3 p.R231G reduces heparan sulfate affinity and redistributes SOD3 from ECM to plasma). intervention: do(sod3_ecm_anchoring decreased via p.R231G or ECM-binding LoF) -> vascular-wall SOD3 depleted -> superoxide accumulates at endothelium -> eNOS uncoupling and reduced NO production. | intervention: do(SOD3 ECM-anchoring loss-of-function, e.g. p.R231G variant reducing heparan sulfate binding)"
    ; pmo:mechanism "SOD3/EC-SOD is anchored in the vascular extracellular matrix and pericellular space via its C-terminal heparin-binding domain; this localization positions SOD3 at the endothelial surface where it scavenges superoxide before it can react with NO (forming peroxynitrite) or impair eNOS activity. When ECM anchoring is intact (sod3_ecm_anchoring high), SOD3 protects endothelial NO from superoxide-mediated destruction, sustaining endothelial NO production. Reduced ECM anchoring (e.g. SOD3 p.R231G LoF variant) releases SOD3 into plasma but depletes vascular-wall-bound SOD3, increasing local O2-radical levels that quench NO and impair shear-stress-induced eNOS activation. do(sod3_ecm_anchoring decreased) -> less vascular SOD3 -> more endothelial superoxide -> lower NO bioavailability and endothelial_no_production falls."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_homocysteine> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/endothelial_no_production> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aplasma_homocysteine%3Anegative%3Aendothelial_no_production> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/plasma_homocysteine> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/endothelial_no_production> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "PubMed 10997917 (Tyagi et al. Am J Physiol Renal Physiol 2000): 'concentrations of homocysteine >20 µM produce a significant indirect suppression of eNOS activity'; homocysteine-induced superoxide and peroxynitrite reversed by SOD mimetic. PMC4400833 (Tawfik et al. 2015, 'Hyperhomocystinemia Impairs Endothelial Function and eNOS Activity via PKC Activation'): decreased eNOS protein expression and increased Thr495 phosphorylation. PubMed 17622691 (Stühlinger et al. 2007, 'Homocysteine induces endothelial dysfunction via inhibition of arginine transport'). Frontiers Cardiovasc Med 2022, doi:10.3389/fcvm.2022.1109445 ('Mechanism of homocysteine-mediated endothelial injury and its consequences for atherosclerosis'). CBS deficiency OMIM 236200. | intervention: do(CBS LoF, homocystinuria OMIM 236200) -> plasma_homocysteine UP -> endothelial_no_production DOWN -> endothelial dysfunction, thromboembolism [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Elevated plasma homocysteine (hyperhomocysteinaemia) inhibits endothelial nitric oxide production via three convergent mechanisms: (1) oxidative stress — homocysteine auto-oxidises to generate superoxide and peroxynitrite that scavenge NO and oxidise eNOS sulfhydryl groups, uncoupling the enzyme; (2) ADMA accumulation — homocysteine inhibits DDAH (dimethylarginine dimethylaminohydrolase), allowing ADMA (an endogenous eNOS inhibitor) to accumulate; (3) arginine transport impairment — homocysteine downregulates CAT-1 transporter expression, limiting L-arginine supply to eNOS. All three mechanisms converge on reduced eNOS activity and NO production. In CBS deficiency (classic homocystinuria, OMIM 236200), severe hyperhomocysteinaemia causes premature endothelial dysfunction and thromboembolism via NO deficiency."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoblast_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tissue_nonspecific_alp_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aosteoblast_activity%3Apositive%3Atissue_nonspecific_alp_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/osteoblast_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/tissue_nonspecific_alp_activity> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Curated osteoblast-differentiation mechanism (evidence grade B). Endpoint: osteoblast-expressed tissue-nonspecific alkaline phosphatase (TNAP/ALPL) catalytic activity, bone fraction. Intervention/support: anabolic osteoblast stimulation — teriparatide / PTH raises osteoblast number and activity and bone-specific TNAP (StatPearls NBK559248; teriparatide marked-ALP-rise case reports PMC7333273); RUNX2/BMP2 transcriptional induction of ALPL as a canonical osteoblast-differentiation gene, with RUNX2 loss abolishing ALPL expression (Komori RUNX2 reviews); antiresorptives lower bone turnover and bone ALP — mechanistically distinct interventions moving TNAP the same direction. Context: human bone anabolic/antiresorptive therapy and osteoblast-differentiation biology; ALPL (TNAP) isozyme, bone fraction. TYPING NOTE: defensibly a production/expression relation (osteoblast expresses its own TNAP); authored as causal + because osteoblast_activity is a rate node — flagged for maintainer. | intervention: osteoblast differentiation / anabolic stimulation, do(osteoblast_activity up) -> tissue-nonspecific ALP (ALPL/TNAP) activity up"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Osteoblast differentiation and bone-forming (anabolic) activity upregulates ALPL transcription and the membrane-anchored tissue-nonspecific alkaline phosphatase (TNAP) that osteoblasts express as a differentiation / bone-formation marker; this is the osteoblast SOURCE of the bone TNAP enzyme-activity fraction. It is NOT a generic total-serum-ALP biomarker association — the target tissue_nonspecific_alp_activity is the ALPL/TNAP isozyme (PR:000003968), so the liver/intestinal/placental total-ALP confound does not apply. RUNX2/BMP2 induce ALPL during osteoblast differentiation (RUNX2 loss abolishes ALPL expression). The effect feeds the existing island edge tissue_nonspecific_alp_activity -(+)-> alkaline_phosphatase."
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_glutamate> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intracellular_calcium> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Acsf_glutamate%3Apositive%3Aintracellular_calcium> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/csf_glutamate> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/intracellular_calcium> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "Basic Neurochemistry: Molecular, Cellular and Medical Aspects, 6th edition (Siegel et al.); (Chapter: Excitotoxic injury in Hypoxia-Ischemia); “Experiments with cortical or hippocampal cell cultures suggest that much of the neuronal death associated with brief, intense glutamate exposure is mediated by NMDA receptor activation, probably because this can induce lethal amounts of Ca2+ influx more rapidly than can AMPA/KA receptor stimulation.” | Elevated extracellular glutamate activates NMDA receptors that drive Ca2+ influx into neurons, so do(raise CSF glutamate) raises intracellular Ca2+ (sign +)."
    ; pmo:mechanism "extracellular/CSF glutamate concentration -> NMDA (and AMPA/KA) receptor overstimulation disposition -> excitotoxic cation-channel opening process -> cytosolic free Ca2+ (intracellular calcium) influx"
    .
