    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "INTERVENTIONAL (genetic LoF). HJV/HFE2 loss-of-function -> juvenile hemochromatosis with LOW hepcidin and iron overload [Papanikolaou et al. 2004, PMID 14647275]; HJV identified as a BMP co-receptor that amplifies SMAD signalling and hepcidin. DRAFT for domain review. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "membrane HJV presents BMP6 to BMP type I/II receptors, amplifying SMAD1/5/8 phosphorylation (quality: HJV co-receptor activity -> disposition: BMP-receptor complex assembly -> process: SMAD1/5/8 phosphorylation -> quality: BMP-SMAD signalling)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hfe_tfr2_iron_sensing> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bmp_smad_signaling> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ahfe_tfr2_iron_sensing%3Apositive%3Abmp_smad_signaling> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hfe_tfr2_iron_sensing> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bmp_smad_signaling> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "INTERVENTIONAL (genetic LoF). HFE (C282Y) loss-of-function and TFR2 loss-of-function each cause hereditary hemochromatosis with inappropriately LOW hepcidin and iron overload — establishing that HFE/TFR2 sensing is required for normal BMP-SMAD-driven hepcidin induction. DRAFT for domain review."
    ; pmo:mechanism "HFE (with beta-2-microglobulin) and TFR2 sense diferric-transferrin saturation and potentiate BMP-SMAD signal amplitude (quality: HFE/TFR2 sensing activity -> disposition: co-receptor potentiation -> process: increased SMAD1/5/8 phosphorylation -> quality: BMP-SMAD signalling)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bmp6_ligand> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bmp_smad_signaling> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abmp6_ligand%3Apositive%3Abmp_smad_signaling> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bmp6_ligand> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bmp_smad_signaling> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "Ganz & Nemeth 2012 (Biochim Biophys Acta 1823:1434-1443, PMID 22306005); BioModels BIOMD0000000734 (Parmar et al. 2017, BMC Syst Biol 11:57). Opened primary knockout: Bmp6-deficient mice have low phospho-SMAD1/5/8 and markedly reduced hepcidin with massive iron overload -- do(BMP6 down) lowers BMP-SMAD signaling (Meynard et al. 2009, Nat Genet, PMID 19252488). [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "BMP6 binds BMP type-I/II receptors with the co-receptor hemojuvelin on the hepatocyte; the activated receptor disposition phosphorylates SMAD1/5/8, which complex with SMAD4 (process), raising nuclear SMAD signaling activity.\n"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bmp_smad_signaling> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepcidin_transcription> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Abmp_smad_signaling%3Apositive%3Ahepcidin_transcription> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bmp_smad_signaling> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepcidin_transcription> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "Ganz & Nemeth 2011 (Annu Rev Med 62:347-360); Boron & Boulpaep, Medical Physiology (hepcidin/BMP-SMAD regulation). Opened primary conditional knockout: liver-specific Smad4 disruption markedly decreases hepcidin and abrogates its transcriptional activation by BMP/iron/IL-6 -- do(SMAD signaling down) lowers hepcidin transcription (Wang et al. 2005, Cell Metab, PMID 16330325). [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Phospho-SMAD1/5/8-SMAD4 complexes bind BMP-responsive elements in the HAMP promoter (disposition -> transcriptional activation process), increasing hepcidin mRNA synthesis rate.\n"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6_stat3_signaling> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepcidin_transcription> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Ail6_stat3_signaling%3Apositive%3Ahepcidin_transcription> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/il6_stat3_signaling> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/hepcidin_transcription> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "Sobotta et al. 2017 (Front Physiol 8:775, PMID 29062282; BioModels MODEL2307050001); Ganz & Nemeth 2011 (Annu Rev Med 62:347-360). Opened primary: STAT3 binds a conserved hepcidin-promoter element and is necessary and sufficient for IL-6 responsiveness; disrupting STAT3 abolishes induction (loss-of-function), corroborated by human IL-6 infusion (controlled stimulation) rapidly raising hepcidin -- do(IL-6/STAT3 signaling up) raises hepcidin transcription (Wrighting & Andrews 2006, Blood, PMID 16835372; Nemeth 2004). [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "IL-6 binds IL6R/gp130, activating JAK1; phospho-STAT3 dimerizes and binds the STAT3 element of the HAMP promoter (disposition -> transcriptional activation process), raising hepcidin mRNA synthesis rate (basis of anemia of inflammation).\n"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythroferrone> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bmp_smad_signaling> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Aerythroferrone%3Anegative%3Abmp_smad_signaling> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/erythroferrone> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/bmp_smad_signaling> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "perturbation"
    ; dcterms:source "Arezes et al. 2018, \"Erythroferrone inhibits the induction of hepcidin by BMP6\" (Blood 132:1473-1477, PMID 30097509) — recombinant ERFE suppresses the hepatic BMP/SMAD pathway; Kautz et al. 2014 (Nat Genet 46:678-684, PMID 24880340). [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Erythroblast-derived ERFE acts as a BMP ligand trap: it sequesters BMP6 (and the BMP2/6 heterodimer), preventing the ligand from binding the hepatocyte BMP type-I receptor ALK3 (disposition), which lowers SMAD1/5/8 phosphorylation and hepatic BMP-SMAD signaling activity.\n"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_phd2_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_hif2a_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Arenal_phd2_activity%3Anegative%3Arenal_hif2a_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_phd2_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_hif2a_activity> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "pharmacological"
    ; dcterms:source "INTERVENTIONAL (pharmacological + genetic). HIF prolyl-hydroxylase inhibitors (roxadustat) inhibit PHD and RAISE endogenous EPO/Hb in humans — Chen et al. 2019 NEJM 381:1011-1022, PubMed 31340089. EGLN1/PHD2 loss-of-function -> familial erythrocytosis type 3 with inappropriately high EPO — Percy et al. 2006 PNAS 103:654-659, PubMed 16407130; OMIM 609820. Mechanism: Kaelin & Ratcliffe 2008 Mol Cell 30:393-402, PubMed 18498744. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "O2/2-oxoglutarate-dependent PHD2 hydroxylates HIF-2alpha Pro residues, creating the VHL recognition degron (quality: PHD2 catalytic activity -> disposition: HIF-2alpha prolyl-hydroxylation / VHL-targeting -> process: CRL2-VHL ubiquitination + proteasomal degradation -> quality: reduced HIF-2alpha activity)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_vhl_activity> obo:RO_0002305 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_hif2a_activity> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Arenal_vhl_activity%3Anegative%3Arenal_hif2a_activity> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_vhl_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_hif2a_activity> ; pmo:sign "-"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "INTERVENTIONAL (genetic LoF). VHL loss-of-function (Chuvash polycythemia, VHL p.Arg200Trp) impairs HIF degradation and RAISES EPO/red-cell mass -> familial erythrocytosis type 2 — OMIM 263400. Mechanism: Kaelin & Ratcliffe 2008 Mol Cell 30:393-402, PubMed 18498744. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "VHL is the substrate-recognition subunit of the CRL2 E3 ubiquitin ligase that binds hydroxylated HIF-2alpha (quality: VHL activity -> disposition: binding of prolyl- hydroxylated HIF-2alpha -> process: HIF-2alpha polyubiquitination + degradation -> quality: reduced HIF-2alpha activity)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_hif2a_activity> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_epo_transcription_rate> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Arenal_hif2a_activity%3Apositive%3Arenal_epo_transcription_rate> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_hif2a_activity> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/renal_epo_transcription_rate> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "genetic_lof_gof"
    ; dcterms:source "INTERVENTIONAL (genetic GoF). EPAS1/HIF2A gain-of-function -> familial erythrocytosis type 4 with elevated/inappropriate EPO — Percy et al. 2008 NEJM 358:162-168, PubMed 18184961; OMIM 611783. HIF-2alpha (not HIF-1alpha) is the EPO-driving isoform — Haase 2013 Blood Rev 27:41-53, PubMed 23291219; Kaelin & Ratcliffe 2008. [citations PubMed-verified]"
    ; pmo:mechanism "stabilised HIF-2alpha dimerises with HIF-1beta/ARNT and binds the EPO 3' hypoxia- response element (quality: HIF-2alpha activity -> disposition: HRE occupancy at the EPO locus -> process: EPO gene transcription -> quality: EPO transcription rate)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dendritic_cell_activation> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/th1_cell_activation> .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/edge/causal/influence%3Adendritic_cell_activation%3Apositive%3Ath1_cell_activation> a pmo:CausalRelation ; pmo:hasSource <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/dendritic_cell_activation> ; pmo:hasTarget <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/th1_cell_activation> ; pmo:sign "+"
    ; pmo:causalEvidence "curated_mechanistic"
    ; dcterms:source "OmniPath ligrec IL12A->IL12RB1/IL12RB2 consensus_stimulation=True (refs SIGNOR:12023369; Guide2Pharma); Janeway's Immunobiology 9th ed. | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Biology 2e §bio-42.2 (42.2: Adaptive Immune Response): 'Whether a TH1 or a TH2 immune response develops depends on the specific types of cytokines secreted by cells of the innate immune system' — dendritic cells are the principal innate APCs driving TH1 polarisation. Opened primary confirms IL-12 initiates differentiation of naive CD4 T cells into Th1 cells via selective STAT4 activation (Jacobson et al. 1995, J Exp Med, PMID 7722452 / PMC2191986). NB the YAML-cited SIGNOR:12023369 identifier was not verified"
    ; pmo:mechanism "dendritic-cell IL-12 (IL12A/IL12B heterodimer) -> IL-12 receptor (IL12RB1/IL12RB2) on naive CD4 T cell -> STAT4 -> T-bet -> Th1 differentiation/activation (GO:0035722 IL-12-mediated signaling)"
    .
<https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/th1_cell_activation> obo:RO_0002304 <https://w3id.org/physiomap/node/macrophage_activation> .
