    ; dcterms:source "GO:0004687 MLCK activity; textbook | textbook corroboration: OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e §anp-060 (10.9: Smooth Muscle): 'Ca++-calmodulin complex then activates an enzyme called myosin (light chain) kinase, which, in turn, activates the myosin heads by phosphorylating them'; Wong, Cells: Molecules and Mechanisms §wong-12.7 (Actin - Myosin Structures in Muscle): 'G-protein signaling cascade...can lead to activation of PLC and subsequent cleavage of PIP2 and increase in cytosolic Ca2+. These changes...can also activate myosin II' (p. 193-194).; Wong, Cells: Molecules and Mechanisms §wong-14.5 (Calcium Ion Signaling): 'Ca2+ release in muscle cells is required to allow contraction of each sarcomere'; calmodulin conformational change upon Ca2+ binding allows interaction with CaMKII and downstream kinases including MLCK.; Jakubowski & Flatt, Fundamentals of Biochemistry Vol I §biochem-11.3.4 (Lipid-Gated Ion Channels): Ca2+ influx via TRPC3/TRPC6 channels in vascular smooth muscle cells leads to smooth muscle contraction and vasoconstriction, consistent with the Ca2+/calmodulin-MLCK activation mechanism"
    ; pmo:mechanism "Ca2+/calmodulin activates myosin light-chain kinase (MLCK)"
